<!DOCTYPE tmx SYSTEM "tmx11.dtd">
<tmx version="1.1">
  <header creationtool="OmegaT" o-tmf="OmegaT TMX" adminlang="EN-US" datatype="plaintext" creationtoolversion="6.0.1_0_42ef0143" segtype="sentence" srclang="la"/>
  <body>
<!-- Default translations -->
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; ad proportionem distributarum afflictionum, pretiosos illos gratiarum acervos inter præsentes animas attentissime divisit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052807Z">
        <seg>And in proportion to the afflictions distributed, he most attentively divided those precious heaps of grace among the present souls.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; confestim ingressa, vidit copiosas sudoris guttulas, instar minutissimorum unionum, tota sacratissimæ imaginis facie conspicuas lucere ac prominere: subnascebantur aliæ aliis, &amp; mutuo attritu resolvebantur in fluxum, diversisque lineis per pictos capillos, &amp; barbam stillatim decurrebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053747Z">
        <seg>And immediately upon entering, she saw abundant drops of sweat, like tiny pearls, shining and protruding all over the face of the most sacred image: new ones sprang up beneath the others, and by their mutual friction they dissolved into a flow, trickling in various lines through the painted hair and beard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; coram illamet divæ Virginis imagine Rosariana, ubi contractum fuerat sanctum connubii fœdus, cordialem Rosæ digitum inclusit, non sine novo prodigio: nam ceremonia tam secreta fuit, quod nec proxime Virgini adgeniculans mater, quidquam vel minimum adverterit, quamquam ad omnia Filiæ intentissima.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044514Z">
        <seg>And before the image of the Virgin of the Rosary, where the sacred bond of matrimony had been contracted, he slipped on the Rosary ring, not without a new miracle: for the ceremony was so secret that even the mother, kneeling close to the Virgin, did not notice the slightest thing, though she was most attentive to everything concerning her Daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; ecce cochlear propalam in mensa cellulæ, ubi decies fuerat quæsitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035657Z">
        <seg>And behold, the spoon was openly on the table in the cell, where it had been searched ten times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; ego parando mihi cibo occupabor?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045035Z">
        <seg>And will I be busy preparing food for myself?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; ego, quæ jugiter &amp; ubique Sponsi mei præsentia munior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062058Z">
        <seg>and I, who am constantly and everywhere fortified by the presence of my Spouse;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; hunc in convexi meditullio conspicue exornabat gloriosa crux nostri Salvatoris, purpurata, madida, locisque clavorum perforata, &amp; Crucifixi titulo triumphali insignita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052730Z">
        <seg>And in the center of this convexion, the glorious cross of our Savior was conspicuously adorned: it was purple, blood-stained, pierced by the nails, and inscribed with the triumphal title of the Crucified One.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; in Peruano sanctæ Inquisitionis tribunali censor, quem vocant qualificatorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050148Z">
        <seg>and in the tribunal of the Holy Inquisition in Peru, a censor, whom they call a qualificator;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; infans amor, quod eloqui non poterat, balbutire gestiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044402Z">
        <seg>And the infant love, which could not speak, longed to stammer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; ita erat: nam archithalassus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; morbo correptus, receptui cecinerat, ac paulo post a suis in rupe portui opposita sepultus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060257Z">
        <seg>And so it was: for the archithalassus, stricken by disease, had sounded the retreat, and a little later was buried by his men on a cliff opposite the harbor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; non confirmabit me pariter confidentia in Deo salutari meo, ut nil umbrosum deinceps expavescam”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062108Z">
        <seg>And confidence in God my Savior will not likewise confirm me, so that I will no longer be afraid of anything shadowy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; qui facit, quod nemo, mirantur omnes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055715Z">
        <seg>And he who does what no one else does, is admired by all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; quæ in ceteris conflictibus generose steterat, in solo isto (quia de castitate erat) a nobili fuga victoriam mendicavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052212Z">
        <seg>And she who had stood bravely in other conflicts, in this one alone (because it was a matter of chastity) begged for victory through a noble flight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; si ad absentem litterulæ sibi formandæ erant, has nudo solo ingeniculata scribebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050718Z">
        <seg>And if he needed to form little letters for himself in his absence, he would write them on the bare ground on his knees.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; si cubiculum everrendum esset, hæc ligna suo loco vel immota relinqueret, vel actutum reponeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042558Z">
        <seg>And if a room were to be torn apart, it would either leave these beams in place, or promptly replace them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; tamen sibimet necessaria subtrahens dispersit, dedit pauperibus, ac memor cælestis Lapicidæ, qui (ut relatum supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061020Z">
        <seg>And yet, depriving himself of what was necessary, he distributed it to the poor, and, mindful of the heavenly Lapicida, who (as related above in chapter…</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; ut scias me tuum esse basilicum, ego alterum illum his manibus eradicavi, fregi, projeci.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051236Z">
        <seg>And so you know that I am yours, basil, I uprooted that other one with these hands, broke it, and threw it away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&amp; “Absit (inquit) quod a mortali manu palmam accipiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044306Z">
        <seg>"Far be it," he said, "that I should receive the palm from mortal hands."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>(ut supra) in reverentiam altissimi gaudii, dum pariebat Filium suum Dominum meum Jesum Christum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054617Z">
        <seg>(as above) in reverence of the highest joy, while she was giving birth to her Son, my Lord Jesus Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>* &lt;e0&gt;f.&lt;/e0&gt; stato</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055625Z">
        <seg>* f.&lt;/e0&gt; state</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>* &lt;e0&gt;forte&lt;/e0&gt; pulpas</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054208Z">
        <seg>* perhaps&lt;/e0&gt; pulps</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>* &lt;e0&gt;immo&lt;/e0&gt; proprius hic</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053638Z">
        <seg>Rather, this is more appropriate here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>1 &amp; 2 originem, situm, &amp; variam fortunam hujus insulæ ac urbis fuse describit.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T232212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035145Z">
        <seg>1 &amp; 2 describe in detail the origin, location, and varied fortunes of this island and city.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>1 &amp; sequentibus ampliorem hujus regionis descriptionem exhibet, ei mappam geographicam præfigens.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060917Z">
        <seg>1 &amp; following provides a more extensive description of this region, prefacing it with a geographical map.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>1 Indiæ Occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T232206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035142Z">
        <seg>1 on the Indies of the West ch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>10 Indiæ Occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035852Z">
        <seg>Chapter 10, West Indies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>10 Indiæ occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035055Z">
        <seg>Chapter 10, West Indies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>10 ejusdem Operis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043147Z">
        <seg>Chapter 10 of the same work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>10 occidentalis Indiæ cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054710Z">
        <seg>10. Of the western Indies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>10.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062508Z">
        <seg>Ten.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>10: “Non rapiet eas quisquam de manu mea;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061922Z">
        <seg>10: "No one will snatch them from my hand."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>11 Indiæ Occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T073557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T073557Z">
        <seg>Chapter 11, West Indies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>11 sic scribit:&lt;/e1&gt; His proxima amplitudine mala, quæ vocamus COTONEA &amp; Græci &lt;b2&gt;Cydonia&lt;/b2&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052909Z">
        <seg>11 thus writes:&lt;/e1&gt; Of these, the next in evil size is that which we call COTTON, and the Greeks Cydonia&lt;/b2&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>11, est quoddam subtilius chartæ genus, in sacris voluminibus scribendis usitatum ab&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἱερὸς&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt; sacer.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050814Z">
        <seg>11. It is a finer kind of paper, used for writing sacred scrolls by hieros, that is, sacred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>12 Indiæ Occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060914Z">
        <seg>12. West Indies, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>12 “Novit justus jumentorum suorum animas, viscera autem impiorum crudelia”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061522Z">
        <seg>12 The righteous knows the soul of his animals, but the entrails of the wicked are cruel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>12.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051543Z">
        <seg>twelve</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>13, cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050811Z">
        <seg>13, chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>15 Indiæ Occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T073622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T073622Z">
        <seg>Chapter 15, West Indies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>15 cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052906Z">
        <seg>Chapter 15</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>157 &amp; sequentibus hanc Francisci Losæ testis oculati lucubrationem Gallice interpretatus est.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042021Z">
        <seg>He translated this eyewitness treatise by Francis Losea, pages 157 and following, into French.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>16 Indiæ occidentalis cap.&lt;/e0&gt; XI Pekey &lt;e1&gt;appellat, &amp; de cujus trunco sic scribit:&lt;/e1&gt; Truncus interdum ita crassus est, ut vix trium quatuorve hominum complexu ambiri possit &lt;e2&gt;&amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043141Z">
        <seg>Book 16 of the West Indies, chapter 11, Pekey, concerning whose trunk he writes as follows: The trunk is sometimes so thick that it can scarcely be encircled by the arms of three or four men, &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>164, qui ortum ait in oppido Linhares Beiræ provinciæ) cum ex Hispania in Septemtrionalem Americæ plagam, Mexici nempe regnum transfretasset, insigni apud eas gentes vixit morum innocentiæ ac sanctitatis fama &lt;e2&gt;&amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042012Z">
        <seg>164, who says he was born in the town of Linhares in the province of Beira, when he crossed from Spain to the northern part of America, namely the kingdom of Mexico, he lived among those peoples with a remarkable reputation for innocence and sanctity of character, &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>174 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042449Z">
        <seg>174 can be read.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>177 elogium ejus repetitur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045400Z">
        <seg>177 His praise is repeated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>18 descripsimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054602Z">
        <seg>We described 18.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>19 legimus.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060329Z">
        <seg>We read nineteen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>198 fusius hunc morbum explicat.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041420Z">
        <seg>198 explains this disease in greater detail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 Operum medicorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045927Z">
        <seg>2 pages of medical works</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 agens de eutrapelia, videtur hanc Aristotelicam interpretationem secutus esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050842Z">
        <seg>2. Acting on eutrapelia, he appears to have followed this Aristotelian interpretation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 agens de fructu Indico, qui vulgo&lt;/e0&gt; cacao &lt;e1&gt;vocatur, ejusque figuram æri incisam repræsentans, de usu illius addit sequentia:&lt;/e1&gt; Sed principalis usus est in potu, quem ex illis conficiunt (CHOCOLATEN vocant) insanum in modum ab incolis harum regionum dilectum, &amp; in summo pretio habitum, cum iis, qui non assueverunt, spuma supernatans &amp; fæcum instar ebulliens merito nauseam moveat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052345Z">
        <seg>2. Concerning the fruit of the Indian tree, which is commonly called cacao, and representing its figure engraved on copper, he adds the following about its use:But its principal use is in a drink which they make from them (which they call CHOCOLATE), insanely beloved by the inhabitants of these regions, and held in the highest esteem, since for those who are not accustomed to it, the foam that rises to the top and the sediment, like a boil, rightly provoke nausea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T073613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T073613Z">
        <seg>Chapter 2</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 de illo Peruviæ fructu post alia sic scribit:&lt;/e0&gt; Pulpa illius candida, &amp; insipida.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041445Z">
        <seg>2. Concerning that fruit of Peru, he writes the following after other things: "Its pulp is white and insipid."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 eleg.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040217Z">
        <seg>two elegies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2 meminit de alia similis fere nominis planta, de qua tradit sequentia:&lt;/e2&gt; Est &amp; altera herba Peruviæ familiaris, quam indigenæ vocant &lt;b3&gt;Payco&lt;/b3&gt;, cujus folia (inquit Monardes) plantaginis foliis forma &amp; colore simillima sunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043150Z">
        <seg>2 he mentions another plant of a nearly similar name, about which he relates the following:&lt;/e2&gt; There is also another herb familiar in Peru, which the natives call Payco&lt;/b3&gt;, whose leaves (says Monardes) are very similar in shape and color to those of plantain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>2.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050833Z">
        <seg>two.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>214 asserit.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061652Z">
        <seg>214 asserts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>215, est medicamentum, quod non diluitur, sed ita, ut est, devoratur aut deglutitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045943Z">
        <seg>215, is a medicine that is not dissolved, but is swallowed or gulphed down as it is.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>219 cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045930Z">
        <seg>219 chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>22 fusius illam urbem describit, &amp; Franciscus de Echave in Opere de S. Turibio archiepiscopo Limensi, quod Antverpiæ anno 1688 Hispanice impressum est, novamejusdem emporii ichnographiam repræsentat.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035059Z">
        <seg>22 He describes that city in greater detail, and Francis de Echave, in his work on St. Turibius, Archbishop of Lima, which was printed in Spanish in Antwerp in 1688, presents a new ichnography of the same emporium.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>227 legitur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044005Z">
        <seg>227 is read.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>23 distincte describit&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054156Z">
        <seg>23 describes distinctly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>231 affirmat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045410Z">
        <seg>231 affirms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>246 &amp; sequentibus, ubi habet&lt;/e1&gt; Parergon de Sanctarum exstaticarum secundum species naturaliter præhabitas durante raptu quandoque motarum dictis factisque ad historicarum quæstronum decisiones non transferendis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051511Z">
        <seg>246 and following, where he has an appendix on the ecstatic saints, according to the naturally pre-existing species, who during the rapture were sometimes moved by words and deeds not to be transferred to the decisions of historical questions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>253 &amp; sequente reperiet&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052931Z">
        <seg>He will find 253 and the following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>28 &amp; 29 pluribus hanc urbem describit, &amp; circa distantiam ejus a Lima &amp; Cusco nonnihil aliter disserit,&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053635Z">
        <seg>He describes this city in more detail in 28 &amp; 29, and discusses its distance from Lima and Cusco somewhat differently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>28 meminit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035855Z">
        <seg>28 remembers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>285 &amp; sequentibus gesta &amp; scripta piissimi Ludovici Granatensis enumerat.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045419Z">
        <seg>285 &amp; following enumerates the deeds and writings of the most pious Louis of Granada.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>29 asserit&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035901Z">
        <seg>29 asserts</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>29 sic scribit:&lt;/e0&gt; Hinc transitur ad Limatambo, ubi plurima rudera visuntur regalium ædificiorum: distat novem leucis a Cusco.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054714Z">
        <seg>29 writes as follows: From here one goes on to Limatambo, where the remains of many royal buildings can be seen; it is nine leagues from Cusco.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>299 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T070835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T070835Z">
        <seg>299 can be read.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>3 Regum cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060326Z">
        <seg>3 Kings, chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>3 circa punctum, an voluntas ferri possit in incognitum, suppresso nomine (siquidem adhuc in vivis erat) allegat, velut orationis ac solidæ perfectionis magistrum, tantumque digito intento non demonstrat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050101Z">
        <seg>At around the third point, he argues that the will can be carried into the unknown, suppressing the name (if indeed he was still alive), presenting him as a master of oratory and solid perfection, and merely points with an outstretched finger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>30 antiquum &amp; modernum hujus civitatis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;e1&gt;statum pluribus describit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050805Z">
        <seg>30. Describes the state of this city in antiquity and modern times in several ways.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>307 tradit&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054205Z">
        <seg>307 reports</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>31 &lt;e5&gt;mentionem faciens de Raab meretrice, quæ exploratores exceperat, eos&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;κατυσκόπους&lt;/s6&gt; &lt;e7&gt;appellat&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040946Z">
        <seg>31 mentioning Rahab the prostitute, who had received the spies, he calls them&lt;/e5&gt; spies&lt;/s6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>31 de epilepsia, hunc morbum cognominat&lt;/e0&gt; sacrum, magnum, puerilem, Herculeum, caducum, comitialem, lunaticum &lt;e1&gt;ac denique&lt;/e1&gt; sonticum, &lt;e2&gt;quæ omnia epitheta affirmat esse synonyma, &amp; de quibus obiter rationem reddit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045934Z">
        <seg>31. On epilepsy, he nicknames this disease sacred, great, childish, Herculean, falling, comitial, lunatic, and finally sontic, all of which he asserts are synonymous, and of which he incidentally gives an account.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>322 sic disserit:&lt;/e0&gt; Membrana dura, inviolata, crassa, cuticularis (idem enim significant) est, quæ primum calvariæ ossibus exemptis, aut saltem apertis, cerebrum in orbem contegens apparet (quæ vulgo DURA MATER appellatur) cui altera tenuis mollisque subnectitur, quæ PIA MATER nominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042443Z">
        <seg>322 thus states: There is a tough, inviolable, thick, cuticular membrane (for these terms signify the same), which first appears, the bones of the skull having been removed or at least opened, covering the brain in a sphere (commonly called the dura mater), to which another, thin and soft membrane is attached, called the pia mater.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>347 ex Galeno tradit&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035241Z">
        <seg>347 from Galen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>352 § 16&lt;/e1&gt;, Præcepit, ut nulla prædictarum mulierum, quas BEATAS vocant, habitum alicujus religionis approbatæ deferat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040431Z">
        <seg>352 § 16, He decreed that none of the aforementioned women, whom they call BEATAS, should wear the habit of any approved religious order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>355 &amp; sequentibus virtutes &amp; lucubrationes hujus pii viri in prolixo ipsius elogio recensentur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050740Z">
        <seg>In 355 and the following, the virtues and writings of this pious man are recounted in his lengthy eulogy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>373 consuli potest&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043116Z">
        <seg>373 can be consulted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>378 apud Gorræum proxime citatum, si lubet.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052947Z">
        <seg>378 in Gorræus, cited above, if you please.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>4 pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044728Z">
        <seg>four pages</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>404 Acta hujus Sancti illustrarunt.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043205Z">
        <seg>The acts of this saint have been illustrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>417 de illo scribat sequentia:&lt;/e1&gt; Gregorius Lopez, cujus patria in obscuro est (alii Matritensem existimant, natumque anno MDXXXII, alii Lusitanum, ut Georgius Cardosus in Hagiologio suo XIII Martii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042009Z">
        <seg>417 write the following about him: Gregory Lopez, whose nationality is obscure (some consider him a native of Madrid, born in 1532, others a Portuguese, as George Cardoso in his Hagiology on March 13, page).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>417 referuntur, &amp; alia occasione hic infra num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045357Z">
        <seg>417 are referred to, and on another occasion here below, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>423 &amp; sequentibusfuse disputavimus, quis fuerit primus Rosarii institutor.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045425Z">
        <seg>We have discussed at length in 423 and following who was the first institutor of the Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>45 verbis Echardi retulimus.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051456Z">
        <seg>We have recounted Echard's words in 45 words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>46 se ipsam&lt;/e0&gt; abominabile generis humani carcinoma &lt;e1&gt;appellavit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061631Z">
        <seg>46 called herself an abominable carcinoma of the human race.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>464 refertur his verbis:&lt;/e1&gt; Canthari ac scarabæi &amp; hujus generis insecta plurima ita fœtoribus &amp; sordibus, ex quibus originem duxerunt, delectantur &amp; illis sustentantur, ut contra odorum suavitate plane exanimentur &amp; pereant, ideoque rosis insidentes scarabæi vel cantharides torpescere vel tandem emori dicuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044731Z">
        <seg>464 is referred to by these words: Cantharids and scarabs and many insects of this kind are so delighted by the foul odors and filth from which they originated and by which they subsist, that they are completely enraged and perish at the sweetness of scents; therefore, scarabs or cantharids that alight on roses are said to become torpid or ultimately die.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>469 id auctoritate veterum confirmat in hunc modum:&lt;/e2&gt; Mirum, quod tanta vivendi scarabæis insit facultas, ut etiam divisi viovant, rosarum tamen odore moriantur, ut tradidit Aristoteles, itemque Plinius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044737Z">
        <seg>469 He confirms this by the authority of the ancients in the following manner: It is remarkable that scarabs have such a capacity for life that they even survive being cut in two, yet they die from the scent of roses, as Aristotle and Pliny relate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>48 testatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055526Z">
        <seg>48 testifies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>5 &lt;e0&gt;§&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000859Z">
        <seg>5.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>5 &lt;e3&gt;§&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035626Z">
        <seg>5. Section.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>5, apparet Virgini, invitat ad ludum, utrimque victoriæ fructus relinquitur victoris arbitrio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051012Z">
        <seg>5. It appears to the Virgin, invites to the game, and leaves the fruits of victory on both sides at the victor's discretion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>50 fusius explicatur&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055548Z">
        <seg>It is explained in more detail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>50 referetur.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035914Z">
        <seg>50 is referred to.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>520 Lexici medici de meliceride habet sequentia:&lt;/e0&gt; Meliceris, &lt;s1&gt;μελικηρὶς&lt;/s1&gt;, humorem melli similem membrana conclusum habens, species est apostematis, ut &amp; &lt;s2&gt;ἀθέρωμα&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; &lt;s3&gt;στεάτωμα&lt;/s3&gt;, quorum hoc ab adipe, illud a pulticula intra membranam conclusa nomen habet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055603Z">
        <seg>520 A medical lexicon has the following concerning meliceris: Meliceris, &lt;s1&gt;μελικηρὶς&lt;/s1&gt;, having a honey-like humor enclosed in a membrane, is a species of apostema, like &amp; &lt;s2&gt;ἀθέρωμα&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; &lt;s3&gt;στεάτωμα&lt;/s3&gt;, of which this one takes its name from fat, that one from a porridge-like substance enclosed within the membrane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>54, quod appellavit pusillanimitatem spiritus &amp; tempestatem, vel quod Paulus emphatice vocat anathema a Christo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045813Z">
        <seg>54, which he called the cowardice of the spirit and the tempest, or what Paul emphatically calls anathema from Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>578 &amp; sequentibus videre est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035210Z">
        <seg>See 578 and following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>58 docet&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052937Z">
        <seg>58 teaches</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>6 &amp; 8 fuse exponit&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061702Z">
        <seg>6 &amp; 8 fuse exponent</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>6 cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T081128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T081128Z">
        <seg>Chapter 6</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>6 retulimus.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042003Z">
        <seg>We reported six.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>6, fas erit inde conjicere, quam sublimem unionis divinæ gradum Rosa attigerit, quæ orationis familiaritate jugiter soli Deo inhærebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044756Z">
        <seg>6, it will be permissible to infer from this how sublime a degree of divine union Rose had attained, who by the familiarity of prayer was continually clinging to God alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>651 tantum dicit, Christum nocte sub forma lusoris ei apparuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051502Z">
        <seg>651 only says that Christ appeared to him at night in the form of a player.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>652 Acta illius venerabilisHenrici Susonis illustravit.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045958Z">
        <seg>He illustrated the Acts of that venerable Henry of Suession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>69 “Deus in adjutorium meum intende, Domine ad adjuvandum me festina”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061727Z">
        <seg>69 "God, come to my aid; Lord, make haste to help me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>6: At laurus bona signa dedit, gaudete.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040221Z">
        <seg>6: The laurel has given good omens; rejoice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>7 Indiæ Occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T070311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T070311Z">
        <seg>Chapter 7, West Indies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>7 Indiæ occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052342Z">
        <seg>Chapter 7 of the West Indies</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>7, adaptat missioni angelorum ad hominis custodiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051555Z">
        <seg>7, adapts the mission of angels to the custody of man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>72 art.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050839Z">
        <seg>Article 72</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>796 miraculum de floribus in loco orationis ejus cælitus inventis refertur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035117Z">
        <seg>796 A miracle concerning flowers found there from heaven at the site of his prayer is recorded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>8 &amp; sequente singillatim describit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052407Z">
        <seg>8 &amp; sequente describes each one in detail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>8 Operis proxime citati de hac specie tumoris ita scribit:&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Ἀθέρωμα&lt;/s1&gt; est tumor concolor, doloris expers, in quo aliquid pulticulæ, quæ &lt;s2&gt;ἀθήρα&lt;/s2&gt; vocatur, simile: tunica quadam membranosa concluditur, cum quo simul aliquando lapidosa quædam duraque corpuscula, &amp; alia instar sulphuris ramenta reperiuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055554Z">
        <seg>8 In the work cited above, he writes thus about this type of tumor: "An atheroma is a tumor of a uniform color, free from pain, in which is found something like a pulp called *athera*:" is enclosed by a certain membranous tunica, with which at times are found certain stony and hard particles, and other fragments like sulphur.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>8 differentiam in istud ac præcedens apostema assignat his verbis:&lt;/e4&gt; Est autem &lt;s5&gt;ἀθέρωμα&lt;/s5&gt; oblongum, eminens, durumque, quod, ut digitis non facile cedit propter humoris crassitiem, sic, ubi cesserit, haud prompte revertitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055613Z">
        <seg>8 assigns the difference between this and the preceding abscess in these words:&lt;/e4&gt; It is, however, an oblong, prominent, and hard &lt;s5&gt;atheroma&lt;/s5&gt;, which, since it does not yield easily to the fingers because of the thickness of the humor, so, when it has yielded, it does not readily return;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>8, cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055523Z">
        <seg>8, chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>847 licet videre&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041957Z">
        <seg>847 may be seen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>851 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040419Z">
        <seg>851 can be read.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>870 olim monuimus&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050746Z">
        <seg>We once warned 870.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>894 in Actis S. Catharinæ Senensis narratur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051518Z">
        <seg>It is narrated in the Acts of St. Catherine of Siena, chapter 894.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>95 breviter relatum est&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044743Z">
        <seg>95 was briefly reported.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>96 habet sequentia:&lt;/e0&gt; Intentum meum hoc loco tantum est, ut demonstrem monachos sancti Benedicti primos, &amp; primates archiepiscopos &amp; vicarios sanctissimi domini Papæ fuisse in totam Americam (de quibus in hunc usque diem soli &amp; ambitiose nimis aliqui gloriantur) &amp; ibidem episcopos, præpositos, decanos, parœcianos novi orbis consecrasse, ordinasse, &amp; instituisse ecclesias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054655Z">
        <seg>96 has the following:&lt;/e0&gt; My intent in this place is only to demonstrate that the monks of Saint Benedict were the first, and the primates, archbishops, and vicars of the most holy Lord Pope in all of America (of whom to this day some alone and with excessive ambition boast) and that there they established bishops, preposits, deans, parishes of the New World consecrated, ordained, and established churches;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[100]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; matrem hæc observantem]&lt;/s2&gt; Cæterum lapilli sparsim ac mobiliter dispositi facile cedebant moli, quoties in aliud latus vertendum erat corpusculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042542Z">
        <seg>[100] [Observing her mother] Moreover, the pebbles, scattered and movably arranged, gave way easily to the effort whenever the little body had to be turned to the other side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[101]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[pie falleret;]&lt;/s2&gt; Subiit ergo nova ac postrema lectuli dirissime asperandi cogitatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042604Z">
        <seg>[101] [pie would deceive;] Then a new and final thought occurred to him for making the little bed most horribly harsh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[102]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliquando fassa est Rosa, in lectuli sui aditu hoc felleum libamen sibi non adeo cum sumeret, &lt;s2&gt;[quam lecti duritiem]&lt;/s2&gt; quam si citius evigilabat, fuisse molestissimum: tunc enim sentiebat palatum usque ad tonsillas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; &amp; epiglottidem&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; aruisse penitus, linguam præ siccitate vix non immobilem, gutturis bronchos&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;c&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; undique accensos, ita quod siccatis faucibus supplicium esset ipsa respiratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042635Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[102]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; At one point, Rosa confessed that, in the bedchamber, this bitter draught was no less unpleasant to her when she took it than if she had woken up sooner: for then she felt her palate all the way to her tonsils&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; &amp; epiglottis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; had completely burned, her tongue almost motionless from dryness, the bronchi of her throat&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;c&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; inflamed on all sides, so that breathing itself was a torment for her parched throat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[103]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Contigit subinde, Rosam velut justo pavori pene imparem, &lt;s2&gt;[Christus ei apparens approbavit.]&lt;/s2&gt; diutius hærere in ancipiti, lectumne tot pœnarum fertilem inscenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042648Z">
        <seg>[103] It happened that Rosa, as if scarcely equal to the just terror, hesitated longer on the horns of a dilemma, whether she should climb into the bed so fertile in so many torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[104]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Unde pulvinar, quod capiti subjicere jussa erat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Dicendum nunc de cervicali.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042719Z">
        <seg>[104] [From which the pillow that had been ordered to be placed under the head] Now we must speak of the cervical.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[105]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[nova industria identidem durius reddidit,]&lt;/s2&gt; Exinde mater in virtute sanctæ obedientiæ præcepit Filiæ, rursum vacuaret suum cervical, lanamque reponeret, at lanam solam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042809Z">
        <seg>[105] [new industry made it repeatedly harsher,] From then on the mother, in the virtue of holy obedience, commanded her daughter to empty her pillowcase again and to put away only the wool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[106]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ita ut mater totum ejus lectulum dejecerit,]&lt;/s2&gt; Demum advenit matri tempus optatissimum, quo sibi de Patrum confessariorum permissu licuit pœnosum Rosæ lectulum manibus avidissimis dissipare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042846Z">
        <seg>[106] [so that the mother had thrown down her entire little bed,] At last the mother's most eagerly awaited moment arrived, when, by the permission of the confessors, she was able to tear apart with her most avaricious hands the penitential little bed of Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[107]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ergo aliter post hæc Rosæ jacendum, &amp; dormiendum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042905Z">
        <seg>Therefore, after these things, Rose had to lie down and sleep differently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[108]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Interim prioris sui lectuli memor &amp; cupida, assidue tundebat confessariorum aures, &lt;s2&gt;[At illa solitam cubandi duritiem repetens,]&lt;/s2&gt; querebatur, se vitam suam otiose perdere, dum a pristinis pœnitentiæ exercitiis in vita cogebatur feriari, omni se patientiæ merito vacuam ex tanta Patrum indulgentia remanere, S. Patri suo Dominico indies occantari titulum &lt;b3&gt;Rosa patientiæ&lt;/b3&gt;, se vero, adempta sibi patientia, nec Rosam esse, nec tanti Patris Filiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042933Z">
        <seg>[108] Meanwhile, remembering and longing for her prior's bed, she constantly pounded the ears of the confessors,[But she, recalling the usual hardness of her bed,]&lt;/s2&gt; complained that she was idly wasting her life, since she was forced to take a holiday from the former exercises of penance, and that, deprived of all merit of patience by such great indulgence of the Fathers, she remained neither a Rose of Patience nor a Daughter of so great a Father; She would daily sing to her holy Father Dominic the title &lt;b3&gt;Rose of Patience&lt;/b3&gt;, but that she, patience being taken from her, was neither a Rose, nor a Daughter of so great a Father;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[109]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Sed nec Rosam morti jam vicinam deseruerat hoc pene coævum durissime cubandi desiderium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042952Z">
        <seg>[109] But neither had he abandoned Rosa, now close to death, because of this almost contemporary, most severe desire to sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[10]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; tantum quinquennis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosæ quinquenni &amp; adhuc inter pueritiæ infantiæque confinia constitutæ, notitia ac timor Dei sese insinuavit modo prorsus admirabili;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034934Z">
        <seg>[10] [Only five years old,] to Rose, who was five and still standing on the border between childhood and infancy, the knowledge and fear of God insinuated itself in a truly marvelous way;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[110]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[longas vigilias protrahebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Porro quisque ex tanta usualis strati duritie haud difficulter intelliget, quantum Rosa vigiliis dederit, cui ipsemet lectus soporem adimebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043013Z">
        <seg>[110] [He would stretch out long vigils.] Moreover, anyone accustomed to the usual hardness of a bed will not find it difficult to understand how much time Rosa devoted to her vigils, for her very bed was robbing her of sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[111]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; dæmoni somnolentiam immittenti]&lt;/s2&gt; Nec tamen suas artes partesve omisit versutus hostis, quin Virginem, tunc maxime, quando ad nocturnam orationem surrexerat, molestissime niteretur revocare ad somnum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043032Z">
        <seg>[111] Nor did the cunning enemy, however, abandon his arts or his schemes, but most persistently strove to draw the Virgin back to sleep, especially when she had risen for her night prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[112]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[acriter resistebat.]&lt;/s2&gt; Invenit &amp; alium pugnandi modum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043054Z">
        <seg>[112] He was resisting fiercely. He found another way to fight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[114]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[a tenerit annis amabat solitudinem,]&lt;/s2&gt; Cum annis crescebat latendi studium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043240Z">
        <seg>[114] From his earliest years he loved solitude, and as he grew older his desire to hide grew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[115]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ad quam obtinendam]&lt;/s2&gt; Paulatim eo statura &amp; ætas Rosam provexerat, ut idonea esset honorario comitatu stipare matrem foras egressuram, maxime cum invisendæ erant digniores matronæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043311Z">
        <seg>[115] Gradually, by her stature and age, Rosa had come to be fit to accompany her mother on honorable visits, especially when more distinguished matrons were to be called upon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[116]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Porro &amp; aliud Virgo excogitavit stratagema, ut solitudinis amans, &lt;s2&gt;[sæpius utebatur astuto stratagemate,]&lt;/s2&gt; redimeret vexam prodeundi in publicum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043333Z">
        <seg>[116] Moreover, the Virgin devised another stratagem, so that, as a lover of solitude, she might redeem herself from the vexation of going out in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[117]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Condicto mutuo domina Ludovica Vargas Carvajal cum Maria de Oliva statuerant, &lt;s2&gt;[quod tandem detexit mater,]&lt;/s2&gt; assumptis utrimque filialibus, vehi ad celebrem divæ Virginis stationem, cui a Monte Serrato&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; nomen est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043351Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[117] By a mutual agreement, Lady Ludovica Vargas Carvajal and Maria de Oliva had arranged, [which the mother eventually discovered,] taking their respective daughters, to travel to the famous shrine of the Virgin, which is named from Monte Serrato.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[118]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quis enarret scommata, clamores, dicteria, quæ tunc in Filiam detonuit mater?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043419Z">
        <seg>[118] Who can recount the scoffs, the shouts, the taunts that the mother then hurled at her daughter?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[119]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Cum cuperet in angusto tugurio domi sola degere,]&lt;/s2&gt; Usque adeo Rosæ placebat desertum, displicebat publicum, ut non solum privata palatia, communiaque urbis spectacula mordicus devitarit, verum &amp; generales celebrium processionum circuitus, ac populosas supplicationes curiose spectare defugerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043441Z">
        <seg>[119][Since she wished to live alone at home in a narrow hut,] To such an extent did Rosæ prefer solitude to public life that she not only scrupulously avoided private palaces and the city's public spectacles, but also shunned the general circuits of celebratory processions and the crowded religious processions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[11]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hinc subito in se reducta cœpit odisse suosmet capillos, &lt;s2&gt;[accepta singulari divinitatis cognitione,]&lt;/s2&gt; firmare animum adversus omnes offensas Dei, detestari occasiones, quæcumque inferno militarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035006Z">
        <seg>[11] From this, suddenly turning inward, she began to hate her own hair, [having received a singular knowledge of divinity,] to steel her mind against all offenses against God, to detest the occasions that served the inferno;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[120]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[matre id recusante,]&lt;/s2&gt; Igitur, aspirante Numine, sublimiorem speculata est viam, qua sese ab iis impedimentis viriliter explicaret: parentes verecunde supplex oravit, permitti sibi in extremo horti angulo locum solitarium pro architectanda cellula, quam cupiebat unicæ dumtaxat staturæ suæ capacem, cum exigua fenestella, quæ lumen exciperet, ostioli clavem sola (si vellet) mater teneret: ibi se labori manuum, orandi meditandique studio, &amp; collectioni spiritus absque ullo minimi temporis dispendio utilissime vacaturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043503Z">
        <seg>[120] [with her mother refusing,] Therefore, with the Deity favoring her, she discerned a nobler way by which she might bravely free herself from those impediments: she humbly besought her parents, asking that she be permitted a solitary spot in the farthest corner of the garden for the purpose of building a cell, which she desired to be just large enough for her single stature, with a small window to admit light, the key to the little door to be held by her mother alone (if she wished): there she would most usefully devote herself to manual labor, to the study of prayer and meditation, and to the collection of her spirit without the slightest waste of time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[121]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[confugit ad intercessionem Deiparæ,]&lt;/s2&gt; Postridie ad sacellum Virgo rediit Missam auditura, cumque notasset suam imagini coronam deesse, magnis precibus majorem (quem vocant) sacristanum exoravit, ut tandem ipse coronam imaginis collo circumdaret, sua interesse plurimum, ut id fiat, velle se pretioso hoc vinculo suaviter captivare Matrem Virginem, ut ita Filiolus vadem seu fidejussorem se interponat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043531Z">
        <seg>[121] [She fled to the intercession of the Mother of God,] The next day the Virgin returned to the chapel to hear Mass, and when she noticed that her image was missing a crown, she earnestly implored the senior sacristan to finally place the crown around the image's neck, insisting that it was of the utmost importance. She wanted to sweetly captivate the Mother and Virgin with this precious bond, so that the Son would thus interpose Himself as a surety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[122]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Rosa ex hoc mirabili ostento jam sui voti secura, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; per amicos ad matrem missos fit voti compos,]&lt;/s2&gt; ac pene compos, gravem ac honorificam adornavit legationem ad matrem: hanc obierunt magister &amp; cathedraticus P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043605Z">
        <seg>[122] Rosa, now secure in her vow by this marvelous sign, and almost having fulfilled it through friends sent to her mother, appointed a solemn and honorable embassy to her mother: this was undertaken by the master and the chancellor, P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[123]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Porro obtenta hac permissione, quam putas festivo dulcique gaudio diem illum transegit Rosa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043621Z">
        <seg>[123] Moreover, having obtained this permission, how do you suppose Rosa spent that day in festive, sweet joy?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[124]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nempe, ut dicebam, thalamus erat, quem Virgo filia Sion adornarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043649Z">
        <seg>[124] Indeed, as I was saying, there was a bridal chamber that the Virgin, daughter of Zion, had adorned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[125]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut nempe Missis procul celebratis interesset,]&lt;/s2&gt; Qui Virginis in templa &amp; res sacras ardentem affectum noverant, mirabantur ipsam jugiter suo reclusoriolo sic adhærescere, ut ferialibus diebus vix aliquando ecclesiam adiret, proinde a nonnemine interrogata fuit, quomodo cor ejus pientissimum sufferre posset, quod non saltem ad interveniendum Missæ sacrificio, tantisper omni mane tuguriolum suum relinqueret mox repetendum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043723Z">
        <seg>[125] [so that she might attend the Missas procul celebrated,] Who knew the Virgin's ardent affection for temples and sacred things, those who knew of her ardent devotion to the Virgin in temples and sacred things were amazed that she so constantly clung to her little retreat that on weekdays she would scarcely ever go to church; consequently, she was asked by no few how her most devout heart could bear it that she did not at least leave her little hut each morning to attend Mass, to be sure to return soon after?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[126]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; culices,]&lt;/s2&gt; Aliud Rosæ jam solitariæ privilegium a culicibus fuit, imo obsequium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043742Z">
        <seg>[126] [and mosquitoes,] Another privilege of the now solitary Rose was freedom from mosquitoes; indeed, they were deferential.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[127]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Accidit, quod vel mater, vel ex permissione confessarii nonnullæ religiosiores personæ, &lt;s2&gt;[quamvis aliis adventantibus infesti,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosam in angusto suo habitaculo, de divinis collocuturæ, inviserent: has vero, mox ubi ad januam aut fenestellam consedissent, hostiliter invadebat culicum exercitus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043804Z">
        <seg>[127] It happened that either the mother, or by the confessor's permission some more devout persons, [although hostile to other visitors,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosam would visit in her narrow dwelling to converse about divine matters: but these, as soon as they sat down at the door or the little window, were hostilely invaded by an army of gnats;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[128]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Plane sic erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043826Z">
        <seg>It was indeed so.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[129]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Soror Catharina de S. Maria tertii Ordinis S. P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043844Z">
        <seg>[129] Sister Catherine of St. Mary, of the Third Order of St. Francis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[12]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Demum sub hac tam præmatura ac sublimi Dei notitia quinquennis Rosa sensit, &lt;s2&gt;[perpetuam virginitatem]&lt;/s2&gt; apertius trahi se ac præparari ad S. Catharinæ Senensis excelsa tirocinia: siquidem hujus exemplo confestim &amp; ipsa virginitatis votum emisit inter hæc primula tenellæ ætatis crepuscula, quinto nimirum ætatis anno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035027Z">
        <seg>[12] Finally, under this so early and sublime knowledge of God, five-year-old Rosa felt,[perpetual virginity]&lt;/s2&gt; was more clearly drawn and prepared for the exalted disciplines of St. Catherine of Siena: for by her example she too immediately made a vow of virginity in the very dawn of her tender years, namely in the fifth year of her age;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[130]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[dum illa usque ad mortem in simili vitæ genere perseveravit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Ultimo vitæ suæ triennio, in Gundisalvi quæstoris domo, paris fere solitudinis desertum sibi Virgo diffinierat, totosque cum noctibus dies, vel in domestico oratorio latebat abdita, vel in ultimo ædium cubiculo arctis sese cancellis jugiter cohibebat, omnia præstruens, omnia interponens, ut inutiles visitationes devitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043943Z">
        <seg>[130] [while she persevered in a similar way of life until death.] In the last three years of her life, in the house of the treasurer Gundisalvus, the Virgin had appointed for herself a desert of almost equal solitude, and for the entire day and night, she would either hide herself in the domestic oratory, or keep herself constantly confined within the innermost chamber of the house behind barred doors, arranging everything and interposing every obstacle to avoid useless visits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[131]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Pater F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T074729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T074729Z">
        <seg>Father F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[132]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Humilitatem Rosæ jam cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044036Z">
        <seg>[132] The humility of the Rose is already in the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[133]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Primum (ex iis, quæ innotuerunt) hoc fuit: &lt;s2&gt;[ut post obscura quædam præludia,]&lt;/s2&gt; quando bicolor ille papilio (de quo sup.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044108Z">
        <seg>[133] The first (of those that became known) was this: [that after certain obscure preludes,] when that two-colored butterfly (of which above</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[134]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Paulo apertius alterum, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044136Z">
        <seg>[134] The other one, which follows, a little more openly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[135]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[mirabilem]&lt;/s2&gt; Visus deinde ex itinere rediisse Sponsus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044201Z">
        <seg>[135] Then the groom was seen to have returned from his journey;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[136]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[mysticamque visionem,]&lt;/s2&gt; Mirabatur Virgo virgines operi tam insueto implicitas, at significabatur virtus occupata in arduis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044219Z">
        <seg>[136] [and a mystical vision,] The Virgin marveled at the virgins engaged in such an unfamiliar task, but it signified virtue occupied with arduous deeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[137]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quæ in sacello Rosariano]&lt;/s2&gt; Jamque post ejusmodi præambula supererat ultimum, quo demum hic Sponsus manifeste ac palam se insinuaret vigili Rosæ, &amp;, exuto latomi schemate, Virginem ad connubialem thalamum apertius invitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044232Z">
        <seg>[137] [which is in the Rosary chapel] And now, after such preliminaries, the final one remained, by which this Spouse would at last manifestly and openly make himself known to the watcher of the Rose, and, having laid aside the guise of the gardener, would more openly invite the Virgin to the nuptial chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[138]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hac igitur novitate confusa Virgo, ut assolent teneriores conscientiæ, &lt;s2&gt;[coram imagine Deiparæ]&lt;/s2&gt; extimuit, ne aliquo forsan obliquo animi motu indignam se reddidisset illius ramiferæ processionis consortio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044250Z">
        <seg>[138] Confused by this novelty, the Virgin—as is the way with the more tender consciences—dreaded, before the image of the Mother of God, lest by some perhaps oblique movement of the mind she might have rendered herself unworthy of the company of that branch-bearing procession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[139]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ad hæc verba, mox vidit cæli Reginam, vultu exporrecto &amp; hilari, &lt;s2&gt;[explicata est,]&lt;/s2&gt; converti in parvulum (quem ulnis gestabat) Filium, ac inde velut a fausto oraculo suavius respicere in Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044312Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[139] At these words, she soon saw the Queen of Heaven, with a beaming and cheerful countenance, [turned,] transformed into the little Son (whom she was carrying in her arms), and from him, as from a favorable oracle, she cast a more gracious look upon the Rose;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[13]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Unum hic prætereundum non est, quod, qui deinde Virginis conscientiam moderati sunt, &lt;s2&gt;[Deo vovit.]&lt;/s2&gt; prudenter conjecere, superna hæc gratiæ allectamenta ipsi obtigisse sub illo primo usus rationis diluculo, quo juxta veriorem theologiam quilibet in Deum finem ultimum sese tenetur convertere pro suæ capacitatis modulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035037Z">
        <seg>[13] One thing here must not be overlooked, that those who have since moderated the Virgin's conscience, [Vowed to God.]&lt;/s2&gt; prudently conjectured that these heavenly allurements of grace had been granted to her at that first dawn of the use of reason, when, according to the more accurate theology, every person is bound to turn himself to God as his ultimate end, in proportion to his capacity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[140]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quid multa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044334Z">
        <seg>[140] What more?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[141]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Sed quæ olim in Canæ Galileæ nuptiis interlocuta virgo Mater, &lt;s2&gt;[eique magnos gratiæ thesauros dederit.]&lt;/s2&gt; hic minime tacuit, eo vel maxime, quod sola ipsa hujus sponsalitii paranympha aderat, sola Rosam promoverat, Filiumque pronuba exorarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044408Z">
        <seg>[141] But the virgin Mother, who once spoke at the wedding in Cana of Galilee, [and to whom great treasures of grace were given.]&lt;/s2&gt; here she was by no means silent, all the more so because she alone was the bridesmaid at this betrothal, alone had presented the Rose, and had implored the Son as sponsor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[142]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Rosa in memoriam anti beneficii]&lt;/s2&gt; Porro ut jugiter tanti beneficii mnemosynon versaretur ante oculos, domum reversa, cœpit extemplo cogitare de conflando connubiali anulo, quem cordis digito, in nuptiarum suarum gratam memoriam, gestaret perpetim ac palam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044436Z">
        <seg>[142] Furthermore, so that the memorial of so great a benefit might always be before her eyes, she, on returning home, immediately began to think of casting a marriage-ring.[Rose, in memory of a benefit] Furthermore, so that the memento of so great a benefit might be ever before her eyes, upon her return home, she at once began to think of having a wedding ring cast, which she would wear on her ring finger, as a pleasant memory of her nuptials, forever and openly;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[143]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[jussit fieri annulum connubialem]&lt;/s2&gt; Annulum deinde ex hoc jam chariorem, ejusdem majoris hebdomadæ feria quinta in ecclesiam detulit, magnisque precibus a patre sacrista impetravit eidem locum in arcula, quæ illo triduo SS. Eucharistiæ repositorium est, &amp; sepulti Redemptoris monumentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044504Z">
        <seg>[143] [He ordered a wedding ring to be made.] He then brought the ring, now made more precious by this, on the Thursday of the same week of Lent to the church, and by great prayers obtained from the sacristan a place for it in the little reliquary, which for that three-day period of the Saints' It is a repository for the Eucharist and a monument to the buried Redeemer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[144]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Post Virginis obitum una cum reliqua ipsius permodica supellectile anulus remansit penes uxorem Gundisalvi quæstoris, &lt;s2&gt;[cujus attactus post obitum Virginis]&lt;/s2&gt; istudque memorabile accidit anno MDCXVIII, mense Februario, in dicti quæstoris ædibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044517Z">
        <seg>After the Virgin's death, a ring remained with her very modest personal belongings, in the possession of the wife of Gundisalvus the treasurer.[144] After the Virgin's death, a ring remained with the wife of Gundisalvus the treasurer, [whose touch after the Virgin's death] and this remarkable event occurred in the year 1618, in the month of February, in the house of the said treasurer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[145]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Paulo post, hic servus Dei, velut internis stimulis erupit in voces: &lt;s2&gt;[quemdam Dei servum ita affecit,]&lt;/s2&gt; “Benedictus Deus, qui tam mirabilis est in Rosa sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044545Z">
        <seg>[145] A little later, this servant of God, as if spurred on by inner impulses, broke out in words: "Blessed be God, who is so marvelous in His Rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[146]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quæstoris uxor, devoto spectaculo jam diu oblectata, &lt;s2&gt;[ut præspirituali gaudio se non caperet,]&lt;/s2&gt; maritum advocat, quasi ad perfunctoriæ salutationis debitum officium: nam hospes, qui consederat, illis in ædibus notissimus &amp; charus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044616Z">
        <seg>The Questor's wife, long entertained by the devoted spectacle, [could not contain herself for pre-spiritual joy,][146] The quæstor's wife, long entertained by the devoted spectacle, [as she could not contain herself for pre-spiritual joy,] calls her husband, as if to the perfunctory duty of a greeting: for the guest, who had taken his seat, was very well-known and dear in that household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[147]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Mirum dictu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044656Z">
        <seg>[147] Strange to say!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[149]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Porro duplici assueverat orandi modo: prior, isque præcipuus erat, quando ex occupato simul tum corpore, &lt;s2&gt;[duplici orationum generi,]&lt;/s2&gt; tum animo, quietissime se totam collegerat ad loquendum cum Deo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044817Z">
        <seg>[149] Furthermore, she was accustomed to two modes of prayer: the first, and chief, was when, with a body then at rest, [of two kinds of prayer,] and a mind at peace, she had most quietly collected herself to speak with God;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[150]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Rarum hoc &amp; singulariter admirandum in Virgine donum fuit omnipotentis dexteræ, &lt;s2&gt;[quarum unum erat assiduum]&lt;/s2&gt; quod sive dormiret Rosa, sive vigilaret, numquam &amp; nusquam mentis suæ oculo elabebatur cælestis Sponsi præsentia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044830Z">
        <seg>[150] This was a rare and singularly admirable gift of the omnipotent right hand in the Virgin, [one aspect of which was its constancy], that whether Rose slept or was awake, the presence of the heavenly Spouse never and nowhere slipped from the eye of her mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[151]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nempe in S. Catharina Senensi simillimum gratiæ paradigma præcesserat, &lt;s2&gt;[quo sic cum Deo conjungebatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; quando streperis culinæ officiis damnata, construxit in intimo animæ recessu felicissimum illud imperturbabilis quietis conclave, quo nulla exteriorum tumultuum importunitas penetrabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044842Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[151] Indeed, in St. Catherine of Siena a very similar paradigm of grace had preceded, [by which she was thus united with God,] when, condemned to the noisy duties of the kitchen, she built in the innermost recess of her soul that most blessed chamber of unperturbed quiet, into which no importunity of external tumults could penetrate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[152]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliud æque mirabile huic dono accessit in cumulum, &lt;s2&gt;[ut exteriora objecta eam non distraherent,]&lt;/s2&gt; quod inter orandum non facile movebatur objectis, quæ ad ipsam minus pertinebant, tamquam si ad hæc sola obtorpuissent Rosæ externi sensus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044852Z">
        <seg>[152] Another equally marvelous gift was added to this, [so that external objects would not distract her,] namely, that while praying she was not easily diverted by things that had little to do with her, as if Rose's external senses had grown numb to everything else.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[153]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hinc &amp; illa Virginei sui corpusculi rupea immobilitas, &lt;s2&gt;[eique diu velut immobilis inhæreret.]&lt;/s2&gt; dum in templo, in sacello Rosarii, in domestico oratorio fundendis precibus vacabat: nam quo loco, situque, ad orandum initio se composuerat, eodem post horas, post diem integrum, post solidum cum die binoctium, reperiebatur omnino invariata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044904Z">
        <seg>[153] Hence that rocky immobility of her Virgin body, [and that she would long remain as if motionless.]&lt;/s2&gt; while she was occupied with prayer in the church, in the chapel of the Rosary, in the domestic oratory: for whatever place and position she had assumed at the beginning of her prayer, she was found in exactly the same spot after hours, after a full day, after a solid day and night of prayer, completely unchanged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[154]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Divina attributa contemplans,]&lt;/s2&gt; Tres dietim horas selegerat, quas sigillatim mane, meridie, vespere solis gratiis Deo reddendis insumebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044920Z">
        <seg>[Contemplating the divine attributes,] he had set aside three hours each day, which he devoted separately to giving thanks to God in the morning, at noon, and in the evening.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[155]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; cum Deo vel de Deo semper loquens,]&lt;/s2&gt; Insuper notatum fuit, adeo Virginem orationi adhæsisse, ut familiares sermones, quibus erga alios utebatur, simul vim orationis plerumque continerent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045003Z">
        <seg>[155] [&amp; always speaking with or about God,] Furthermore, it was noted that the Virgin was so devoted to prayer that her familiar conversations with others usually contained the power of prayer as well.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[156]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Accidit, quod die quadam ab ecclesia domum rediens, &lt;s2&gt;[cantu aviculæ in exstasim rapitur,]&lt;/s2&gt; ut sensit male sibi esse, pulmentum sibi ex aqua tritoque pane obiter parare constituerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045016Z">
        <seg>[156] It happened that one day, returning home from church, [rapt in ecstasy by the song of a little bird,] he felt unwell and had decided to prepare for himself a porridge of water and crushed bread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[157]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Paradoxum foret, si Rosa quod aliis tanto conatu suadebat, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alios tum per se,]&lt;/s2&gt; sibimet ipsi non æque serio persuasisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045053Z">
        <seg>It would have been paradoxical if Rosa had not persuaded herself as seriously as she urged it upon others with such great effort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[158]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nec minore cura Patres confessarios, quotquot adire poterat, &lt;s2&gt;[tum per suos confessarios]&lt;/s2&gt; monebat, rogabat, obsecrabat, ut suos pœnitentes omni suadela &amp; arte inducerent, ad mentalis orationis subeunda gymnasmata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045115Z">
        <seg>[158] Nor with less care did he admonish, beg, and implore the confessors, as many as he could approach, [then through his own confessors], to induce their penitents by every counsel and art to undertake the exercises of mental prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[159]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Peculiariter afficiebat Rosam parthenici Rosarii devota recitario;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045124Z">
        <seg>[159] It particularly moved Rosa, a devotee of the Virgin Mary's Rosary, to recite it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[15]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Accidit, quod coram honestis matronis Rosa, &lt;s2&gt;[corpus ornare]&lt;/s2&gt; præsente genitrice, importunius rogaretur suo capiti circumdare textum e floribus sertum, quod illic forte odoris gratia in medio positum cunctis arridebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035300Z">
        <seg>[15] It happened that in the presence of honorable matrons, Rosa, with her mother present, was more importunately asked to place the wreath of flowers on her head, which by chance, for the sake of its fragrance, had been set in the middle and pleased everyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[160]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quin imo plantas arboresque,]&lt;/s2&gt; Quin imo, hic orandi affectus in Rosa tantæ energiæ fuit, vel ipsas insensibiles plantas, stupida ligna, mutosque stipites, ad laudem, reverentiam, adorationem Deo exhibendam mirabiliter inclinarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045140Z">
        <seg>[160] Indeed, this fervor for prayer in Rosa was of such great energy that it miraculously inclined even the most insensitive plants, the dullest wood, and the mute trunks to offer praise, reverence, and adoration to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[161]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[non sine prodigioso miraculo,]&lt;/s2&gt; Accidit, quod mane quodam probatæ fidei persona se Rosæ hortum adeunti comitem adjunxerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045156Z">
        <seg>[161] [not without a prodigious miracle,] It happened that one morning a person of proven faith had joined Rosa as a companion on her way to the garden;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[162]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[excitat ad laudes divinas,]&lt;/s2&gt; Aliud suppar, &amp; finio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045214Z">
        <seg>[162] [stirs up to divine praises,] Another suppar, and I finish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[162]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliud.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T080139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T080139Z">
        <seg>[162] Another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[163]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Confestim avicula tractu vocis lenissimo inchoabat argutulam cantilenam, &lt;s2&gt;[quas diu cum avicula]&lt;/s2&gt; ac sensim vocalior assurgebat in epiphonema;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045307Z">
        <seg>[163] Immediately the little bird would begin its witty little song with the softest drawl, and gradually, growing more vocal, would rise into an epiphone;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[164]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ita in Dei laudibus alternabat Rosa &amp; avicula: &lt;s2&gt;[alternatim decantat.]&lt;/s2&gt; idque non perfunctorie, sed integro labentis horæ spatio, tanta lege ac ordine, ut canente avicula, ne quidem mutiret Rosa, &amp; vicissim Rosa modulante, avicula attentissima nec pipiret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045323Z">
        <seg>[164] Thus Rose and the little bird took turns in praising God, alternating their songs, and not perfunctorily, but for the entire span of the passing hour, with such discipline and order that when the bird was singing, Rose would not even move, and when Rose was singing, the bird, most attentive, would not chirp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[166]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[sic Sancta internæ tribulationis igne probatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Jacebat sub tenebrarum immani pondere gemens Rosa, nec ad objecta supernaturalia, imo neque ad naturalia sese valebat attollere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045506Z">
        <seg>[166] Thus the Holy One is tested by the fire of inner tribulation, Rose lay groaning under the immense weight of darkness, unable to lift herself to supernatural things, nor even to natural ones.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[167]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ac tantis terroribus angebatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Verum præcipua dolorum pars erat, quod hi faciem præferebant, quasi æternum duraturi, nec finem ullum promittebat vasta miseria, nusquam patebat hujus labyrinthi exitus, videbatur quasi muro adamantino obstructum effugium: unde perplexa Rosa, non reperiebat, quo ab infernali pœna damni hanc suam infelicitatem secerneret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045552Z">
        <seg>[167] [and was tormented by such terrors,]But the chief part of her sorrows was that they wore a face as if they would last forever, and the vast misery promised no end; nowhere was there an exit from this labyrinth, and the escape seemed blocked by an adamantine wall. Hence perplexed Rosa could not find by what means to separate this her own misfortune from the infernal punishment of loss.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[168]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Per ipsos quindecim continuos annos quotidie, minimum semel, &lt;s2&gt;[ut alioquin ad patiendum promptissima]&lt;/s2&gt; in hanc desolationis caliginem demergebatur pavens ac tremens Rosa, nec minus hora integra, quandoque diutius, palpitabat sub hoc agone;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045608Z">
        <seg>For fifteen consecutive years, every day at least once, Rosa—already most ready to suffer—was plunged into this darkness of desolation, trembling and quaking, and for a full hour, sometimes longer, she palpitated under this torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[169]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Denique tantus fuit istarum figurarum horror, &lt;s2&gt;[hunc amaritudinis calicem a se transferri peteret.]&lt;/s2&gt; tanta horroris acerbitas, quod Rosa, tametsi ad quidvis sufferendum a teneris impavida, unicum hunc amaritudinis calicem a se transferri enixe petierit, tamquam suis viribus omnino superiorem, ac simpliciter intolerabilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045636Z">
        <seg>[169] Finally, the horror of these figures was so great, [that she would earnestly ask for this cup of bitterness to be removed from her.]&lt;/s2&gt; such was the acerbity of the horror, that Rosa, though from her tenderest years impassive to all things, earnestly besought that this single cup of bitterness might be removed from her, as though it were altogether beyond her strength and simply intolerable;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[16]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliud.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035325Z">
        <seg>[16] Another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[170]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Talis erat hæc mentis afflictio,]&lt;/s2&gt; Tam abstrusa, monstrifica, ignota erat harum desolationum figura &amp; natura, ut vix demum reperti fuerint theologi perspicaces &amp; gnari, qui de illarum crasi&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; quidpiam certi præsumerent diffinire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045652Z">
        <seg>[170][Such was this affliction of the mind,] so abstruse, monstrous, and unknown was the form and nature of these desolations, that only with difficulty have there been found perceptive and knowledgeable theologians who could confidently presume to define anything certain about their crasi&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[171]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut quidam putarent, esse morbum corporis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Unum supererat exile solatium, quod mater, sicut auxiliari non poterat, ita hos Filiæ terribiles agones penitus ignorabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045726Z">
        <seg>[171] [as some thought, it was a bodily disease,] One solitary, meager consolation remained: that the mother, since she could not help, was completely unaware of her daughter's terrible sufferings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[172]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quem ipsa explicare non posset,]&lt;/s2&gt; Nitebatur subinde Rosa, ubi de hac suæ desolationis fornace fidentius loquendum erat, adinvenire similitudines, quibus utcumque rem adumbraret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045800Z">
        <seg>[172] [which she herself could not explain,] Rosa would then strive, when it was necessary to speak more confidently of this furnace of her desolation, to find similitudes by which she might in some way shadow it forth;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[173]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Fuit, qui putarit Virginem deduci a Deo per scrupeas vias S. Antonii eremitæ, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alii illam, sicut S. Antonium eremitam,]&lt;/s2&gt; quem inferni monstra formis terrificis agitarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045825Z">
        <seg>[173] There was one who thought that the Virgin was led by God along the scarped paths of St. Anthony the Hermit, [and others, like St. Anthony the Hermit,] whom the monsters of hell agitated with terrifying forms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[174]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Bis Virgo compulsa fuit edicere alteram hujus scenæ partem, &lt;s2&gt;[visibiliter a dæmonibus terreri suspicarentur.]&lt;/s2&gt; videlicet, qualis sibi soleret post hujusmodi nubila illucescere Phœbus: fieri enim non posse, quin Sponsus ille dulcissimus, post tam diram ac tenebricosam eclipsim, insigniter recreet, ac reficiat animam, tot doloribus humiliatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045850Z">
        <seg>[174] The Virgin was twice compelled to reveal the other part of this scene, [that they might be seen visibly terrified by demons.]&lt;/s2&gt; namely, what sort of Phoebus was wont to shine upon her after such clouds: for it could not be otherwise, but that that most sweet Spouse, after so dire and gloomy an eclipse, would notably refresh and restore the soul, so humbled by so many pains.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[176]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[duo ex præcipuis]&lt;/s2&gt; Doctor Joannes de Castillo, professione medicus, statu quidem secularis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050036Z">
        <seg>[176] [two of the principal] Doctor John de Castillo, a physician by profession, indeed a secular;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[177]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Pater magister F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050119Z">
        <seg>[177] Father Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[178]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Modo ad rem pressius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050228Z">
        <seg>[178] Now, to the matter more closely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[179]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[interrogavit eam de modo contemplationis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Instabat doctor, an non suæ imaginativæ vim aliquam adhiberet, dum reliquæ internæ potentiæ sic inhærebant sapori Dei, an, &amp; quo, quantove connixu, labore, fulcro, in hac suspensione dulcissima se sustineret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050256Z">
        <seg>[He asked her about the method of contemplation,] The doctor pressed her, whether she had not applied some force to her own imagination.[179] [He questioned her about the manner of contemplation,] The doctor pressed her, whether she was not applying some force of her own imagination while the other internal faculties thus clung to the savor of God, and whether, and by what, and with what effort, labor, and support, she sustained herself in this most sweet suspension?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[17]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[filia subtiliter excogitavit modum,]&lt;/s2&gt; Verumtamen induciæ fuerunt istæ, non sincera pax: nam mater ad alias artes brevi conversa, nunc tentare Filiam, ut puellari usu se comeret, ut collo monilia, brachiis armillas, vultu cerussam pro more regionis admitteret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035409Z">
        <seg>[17] However, there were truces, not a sincere peace.[17] [However, these were only a truce, not a sincere peace: for the mother, having soon resorted to other arts, now tried to persuade her daughter to adopt a girlish style, to wear necklaces around her neck, bracelets on her arms, and ceruse on her face according to the custom of the region;]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[180]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[de oratione unionis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Tunc Castillus, ut erat in hac schola versatissimus, aggressus est propriis notionibus explicare Virgini secretam hujus excelsæ illustrationis hieraticam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, nempe asceticæ disciplinæ magistris hoc genus contemplandi ORATIONEM UNIONIS dici: hic intellectum formari specie, non adquisita, sed superne infusa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050321Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050321Z">
        <seg>[180] [on the speech of union,]&lt;/s2&gt; Then Castille, being most versed in this school, undertook to explain to the Virgin the secret of this exalted hieratic illumination&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, namely, that among the masters of ascetic discipline this mode of contemplating is called the PRAYER OF UNION: here the intellect is formed by a kind of vision, not acquired, but poured down from above;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[181]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Perrexit doctor, &amp; studiose in viam purgativam resiliens quæsivit, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; gradibus, quibus ad hanc ascenderat.]&lt;/s2&gt; quam diu, quibus industriis, quanto sudore pugnandum sibi fuerit in pravas animi inclinationes, in vitiorum armata latibula, in morbos indomitarum effræniumque passionum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050333Z">
        <seg>[181] The doctor proceeded, and, diligently turning back onto the purgative path, he inquired, [and by the steps by which he had ascended to it.]&lt;/s2&gt; how long, by what labors, with how much sweat he had to fight against the evil inclinations of his soul, against the armed ambushes of vices, against the diseases of untamed and unbridled passions?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[182]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Verum ab iis opportuna digressione incidit in horrendas illas visiones, &lt;s2&gt;[Inde dilapsus ad memoratos animi angores,]&lt;/s2&gt; figuras, pavores, de quibus capite superiori actum est, magnisque precibus exoravit doctorem, ut sibi illius desolationis ac supplicii naturam, originem, critica, propriamque interpretationem docte elucidaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050358Z">
        <seg>[182] But from them, by a timely digression, he fell into those horrendous visions,[Then, having lapsed back into the aforementioned torments of the mind,] visions, and terrors, which have been treated in the preceding chapter, he earnestly implored the doctor to learnedly elucidate for him the nature, origin, critique, and proper interpretation of that desolation and punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[183]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[jussit illam revelare divinas illuminationes,]&lt;/s2&gt; His cursim explicatis, vicissim rogavit Virginem doctor, post illas inferni tenebras &amp; horrores qualiter sibi fuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050439Z">
        <seg>[183] [He commanded her to reveal the divine illuminations,] Having briefly explained these, the doctor in turn asked the Virgin how she had fared after those infernal darkness and horrors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[184]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ac cælestes visiones,]&lt;/s2&gt; Jussa ulterius dicere, novo pudore hæsit, rubuit, titubavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050522Z">
        <seg>[184] [and heavenly visions,] When ordered to say more, she was seized by a new modesty, blushed, and faltered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[185]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Unde subintulit Castillus, imaginarias esse visiones;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050554Z">
        <seg>[185] Hence Castille concluded that the visions were imaginary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[186]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Videbat sibi doctor satisfactum in eo, quod maxime scrutabatur, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; has omnes a Deo provenisse judicat,]&lt;/s2&gt; haud ignarus, in materia unionis quo plura dicuntur, minus dici.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050609Z">
        <seg>[186] He saw that the doctor had satisfied him in what he was most closely examining, [and judges that all of these came from God,] not unaware that the more one says about the subject of union, the less is said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[187]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Idem Patri magistro de Lorenzana sensus fuit, &lt;s2&gt;[cui judicio consenserunt Joannes de Lorenzana]&lt;/s2&gt; postquam crebriori &amp; per partes interciso examine spiritum Virginis diligentissime exploravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050628Z">
        <seg>[187] He held the same view as Father Master of Lorenzana, [to whose judgment John of Lorenzana agreed] after he had most diligently explored the spirit of the Virgin by a more frequent and piecemeal examination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[188]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; Ludovicus de Bilbao Dominicani,]&lt;/s2&gt; Lorenzanæ suppar magister, &amp; primarius cathedraticus F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050656Z">
        <seg>[188] [&amp; Louis of Bilbao, Dominican,] Master of the province of Lorenzana, and First Chairholder, Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[189]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[qui sanctitatem Virginis semper magni fecerunt.]&lt;/s2&gt; Præcipue vero Castillus &amp; Lorenzana, de Rosæ viis perfectissimis aliquoties mutua collatione instituta, concordes mirabantur hæc duo singularissima: primum, quod brevissimo compendio, &amp; quasi transsultu, gradum illuminativum, mox &amp; unitivum possederat, absque eo, quod gradum purgativum vix obiter attigisset, quia nimirum ab infantia benedictionibus dulcedinis præventam, ii semper comitati erant purissimi affectus, in quibus nil terrenum sorduerat operosius depurgandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050721Z">
        <seg>[189] [who always held the Virgin's sanctity in high esteem.] Especially, however, Castillon and Lorenzana, having on several occasions, through mutual consultation, established the most perfect ways of the Rose, marveled in unison at these two most singular things: first, that by the briefest compendium, and as it were by a single leap, she had possessed the illuminative, and soon the unitive, degree, without having scarcely touched the purgative degree in passing, because, indeed, from infancy having been preempted by blessings of sweetness, she had always been accompanied by the purest affections, in which nothing earthly had ever soiled itself as to require more diligent purging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[18]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quo matri obediret,]&lt;/s2&gt; Visum hoc matri opportunum compendium, quidquid vellet, extorquendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035421Z">
        <seg>[18] [by which he would obey his mother,] She saw this as a convenient way to extort whatever she wanted from her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[191]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[manibus operanti,]&lt;/s2&gt; Familiarius est, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050913Z">
        <seg>[191] [working with the hands,] What follows is more familiar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[192]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; sæpius ægrotanti]&lt;/s2&gt; Accidit, Rosam infirmari ex dolore gutturis, &amp; en totus ille Desiderabilis, cujus guttur suavissimum prædicatur Cant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051009Z">
        <seg>[192] It happened that Rosa fell ill with a sore throat, and behold that entire Desirable One, whose throat is proclaimed the sweetest in the Song of Songs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[193]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Jesus sub varia forma]&lt;/s2&gt; Alia vice, cum diutius ac fere ad mediam usque noctem in solitaria horti cellula substitisset Rosa, invasit fatigatam subitaneus languor ac virium defectio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051050Z">
        <seg>[193] [Jesus in various forms] On another occasion, when Rosa had been standing for a long time—almost until midnight—in the solitary cell of the garden, she was suddenly overcome by fatigue and a collapse of strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[194]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; In domo cujusdam illustris feminæ, Rosa de more post sancta colloquia veniam petiit secedendi tantisper ad orationem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051133Z">
        <seg>In the house of a certain illustrious woman, Rosa, as was her custom after holy conversations, asked permission to withdraw for a while to pray.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[195]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Zelus, ut intensi amoris præco simul &amp; explorator est, &lt;s2&gt;[apparuisse dicitur.]&lt;/s2&gt; ita vel ipsummet Sponsum cælestem curis sollicitat, rivalem non patitur, ne flosculum quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051204Z">
        <seg>Zeal, as the herald and scout of intense love, is said to have appeared, and so it even troubles the heavenly Bridegroom himself with its cares, allowing no rival, not even a tiny blossom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[196]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Etiam cum Deipara,]&lt;/s2&gt; Porro quam affabili, serena, frequenti apparitione Rosam suam dignata fuerit augustissima cælorum Imperatrix, sat inde conjicere fas erit, quod ab undecimo suæ ætatis anno ad extremum usque non defuerit umquam Virgini hoc pretiosum assiduæ familiaritatis solatium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051254Z">
        <seg>[196][Even with the Mother of God,] Furthermore, since the most august Empress of heaven was pleased to dignify her Rose with such affable, serene, and frequent appearances, it is fitting to infer from this that she never failed the Virgin this precious comfort of constant familiarity, from the eleventh year of her age until the very end.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[197]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quæ illam e somno]&lt;/s2&gt; Conabatur obedire infirma, sed prævalente serotinarum vigiliarum assuetudine, non obediebat somnus, seu veniendum, seu abeundum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051322Z">
        <seg>[197] She tried to obey feebly, but the habit of late-night vigils prevailed, and sleep would not obey, whether it was to come or to go.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[198]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ad orationem excitabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Semel evenit, quod veniente Domina, profundius dormiebat Rosa: primus hic somnus erat, ac nonnisi paulo ante cœperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051347Z">
        <seg>[198] [She was rousing him to speak.] Once, when the Lady arrived, Rosa was sleeping more deeply: it was her first sleep, and it had only begun a little while before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[199]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ex quo semel a Christo (ut compertum postea) Catharina Senensis cælitus Rosæ missa, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; S. Catharina Senensi amice conversabatur.]&lt;/s2&gt; ac data fuit magistra, frequenter hæc charam Discipulam visibiliter convenit in terris, præsertim dum occupabatur in perlegenda seraphicæ magistræ regula, quam sibi e Cusco (procurante Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051425Z">
        <seg>[199] From the time that Catherine of Siena was once sent by Christ from heaven to Rose (as was later learned), [&amp; She conversed with St. Catherine of Siena as a friend.]&lt;/s2&gt; and having been appointed a teacher, she frequently met this beloved Disciple visibly on earth, especially while she was occupied with reading the rule of the seraphic teacher, which had been sent to her from Cusco (by the procuration of Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[19]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Excogitavit tandem ingeniosa Virgo rationem idoneam, &lt;s2&gt;[ac Deum non offenderet.]&lt;/s2&gt; qua deinceps &amp; matris substaret imperio, &amp; nihilominus reliquam de corporis sui cultu vexam redimeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035440Z">
        <seg>[19] The ingenious Virgin at last devised a suitable plan, [and one that would not offend God,] by which she could henceforth both submit to her mother's authority and nevertheless free herself from the rest of the vexations concerning the care of her person.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[200]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Sancta tam familiariter utebatur obsequio angelorum,]&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;b3&gt;Sanctos&lt;/b3&gt; angelos esse ipsissimum cor Dei, sensit Bernardus serm.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051539Z">
        <seg>[200] [The Holy One made such familiar use of the angels' obedience,] Bernard, in his sermon, understood that the holy angels are the very heart of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[201]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut animi deliquium passa,]&lt;/s2&gt; Nocte quadam occlusis horti januis, Rosa de more vigilabat in angusta sui eremi cellula, sensitque se viribus repente destitui, adeo ut gravem timeret syncopen: unde in domum sese recipere ad matrem constituit, collato prius consilio cum angelo suo tutelari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051639Z">
        <seg>[201][as if she had fainted,] One night, with the garden gates locked, Rosa, as was her custom, was awake in her narrow cell in her solitude, and she felt herself suddenly deprived of strength, so much so that she feared a serious fainting spell. Therefore, she decided to return to the house to her mother, after first consulting with her guardian angel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[202]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[per eos potum sibi]&lt;/s2&gt; Filia, ne sui causa is sumptus fieret, obnixe rogavit matrem, affirmans, sibi non defuturam chocolatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051645Z">
        <seg>[202] [by them to drink for herself] The daughter earnestly begged her mother not to incur expenses for her own sake, assuring her that she would not be without chocolate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[203]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hic Mater confusa multiplici prodigio, sciscitari, &lt;s2&gt;[opportunum acceperit,]&lt;/s2&gt; quid Gundisalvi uxorem moverit, ut tali profundæ noctis hora vernam ædibus emitteret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051728Z">
        <seg>[203] Here the Mother, bewildered by the manifold prodigy, inquired, [she had taken it as an opportune moment,] what had moved Gundisalvus' wife to send a maidservant out of the house at such a late hour of the night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[204]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Alia vice in eadem horti cellula ultra mediam noctem exspectabat, &lt;s2&gt;[januam clausam aperiri jusserit,]&lt;/s2&gt; dum in ædes vocaretur pro more ad nocturnam quietem: solebat enim circa horam, quæ medium noctis antecedit, descendere mater ad aperiendum horti ostium, ut filia inde abducta, ad suum cubiculum sese reciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051803Z">
        <seg>[204] Another time, she was waiting in the same garden cell beyond midnight, [when she had ordered the closed gate to be opened,]&lt;/s2&gt; while she was being called into the house as was the custom for the night's rest: for the mother used to come down about the hour that precedes midnight to open the garden gate, so that her daughter, once led out, might retire to her own chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[205]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Occasio angelicæ custodiæ exigit, sequentem historiolam hic subnecti, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; cuidam iter agenti custodiam angelicam procuraverit.]&lt;/s2&gt; qua forte patebit, non proprium dumtaxat, verum &amp; alienos tutelares angelos sibi ad nutum habuisse obsequiosos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051831Z">
        <seg>[205] The occasion of angelic guardianship requires that the following little story be appended here, [&amp; which may have procured an angelic guard for a certain traveler.] by which it will perhaps be revealed that he had not only his own, but also the guardian angels of others, obedient to his will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[206]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Invidi demones variis modis, ac sub diversis formis]&lt;/s2&gt; Quantum vero Angelicam hanc puellam amabant, ambiebant, mulcebant sancti angeli, tantumdem ei clam, palam, vi ac fraude nocere conabantur furiæ infernales.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051911Z">
        <seg>[206] [I envied the demons in various ways, and in different forms] As much as the holy angels loved, surrounded, and caressed this angelic girl, so much the infernal furies tried to harm her, secretly and openly, by force and by fraud.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[207]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[hanc Virginem impugnarunt;]&lt;/s2&gt; Semel a domestico Gundisalvi quæstoris oratorio, ad interius cubile se Virgo recipiebat: per viam adrepens ex insidiis cacodæmon, quanta vi potuit, improvisum ei colaphum impegit, majori tamen sonitu, quam nocumento: subrisit enim intrepida Virago, insuper alteram maxillam dare parata, si hostem conspicuum palam habuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051946Z">
        <seg>[207] [They attacked this Virgin;] Once, as the Virgin was retreating from the oratory of Gundisalvus the treasurer to her inner chamber, As the evil spirit crept up from an ambush along the path, it struck her an unexpected slap with all the force it could muster, causing more noise than harm: for the intrepid maiden smiled, ready even to offer her other cheek, had she been facing an open enemy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[208]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Alia vice, postquam in eodem oratorio preces absolverat, &lt;s2&gt;[sed illis semper impavida restitit,]&lt;/s2&gt; incumbente vespere, secretius in altissima ædium parte cubiculum adiit, solitaria meditatione dulcedinem ruminatura, quam in oratorio gustarat inter preces.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052014Z">
        <seg>Another time, after she had finished her prayers in the same oratory, [but she always resisted them with unshaken courage,] as evening fell she went to a more secluded room in the highest part of the building, to savor in solitary meditation the sweetness she had tasted in the oratory while praying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[209]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Abierat ancilla, &amp; Rosa vix januam clauserat, cum repente audivit, &lt;s2&gt;[ita ut eam numquam terrere,]&lt;/s2&gt; simul a foris inducto pessulo, sese a cacodæmone velut captivam obserari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052045Z">
        <seg>The maid had gone, and Rosa had barely closed the door, when suddenly she heard, [so that she was never to be terrified,] as the latch was drawn from the outside, that she was being locked up like a captive by a demon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[20]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Canta&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; in regno Peruano locus est metalli fodinis percelebris, &lt;s4&gt;[In aliis autem rebus]&lt;/s4&gt; at putris soli exhalationibus, &amp; algore tetro admodum insalubris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035458Z">
        <seg>[20] Canta, in the Peruvian kingdom, is a place celebrated for its metal mines, but in other respects it is very unhealthy on account of the exhalations from its putrid soil and its bitter cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[210]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Interea quæstoris uxor, familiam de Rosa interrogans, &lt;s2&gt;[multo minus vincere,]&lt;/s2&gt; ab ancillula didicit, quo ipsam loco reliquerat cum candela, quodque diserte vetuerat evocari ad cœnam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052131Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the quaestor's wife, asking a servant girl about the family of Rosa, learned from her that she had left the girl herself in that very spot with a candle, and had expressly forbidden her to be summoned to dinner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[211]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[vel ad impudicitiam excitare potuerint,]&lt;/s2&gt; Aliquando in hortum oratura processerat, cum de vicinarum arborum opaco vidit obvium sibi antagonistam, non jam terrificum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052153Z">
        <seg>[211] [or if they could have aroused to impudence,] She had at one time stepped out into the garden to spit, when from the shady grove of the neighboring trees she saw an adversary coming toward her, no longer terrifying;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[212]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[cum inspirationes diabolicas a divinis statim distingueret.]&lt;/s2&gt; Eminebat in Rosa (confessariis attestantibus) mirabile donum internoscendi sinceras visiones ab illusionibus sathanæ, &amp; distinguendi sanctas inspirationes a versutis suggestionibus antiqui serpentis, quod Apostolus discretionem spirituum vocat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052231Z">
        <seg>[212] [since he immediately distinguished diabolical inspirations from divine ones.][with the ability to immediately distinguish diabolical inspirations from divine ones.] She excelled in the Rose (as her confessors attested) in the marvelous gift of discerning true visions from Satan's illusions, and of distinguishing holy inspirations from the cunning suggestions of the ancient serpent, which the Apostle calls the discretion of spirits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[214]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ab austeris domesticis;]&lt;/s2&gt; Quam suavis erat indoles Filiolæ, tam aspera conditio matris, ut pene videretur sola patientiæ biga par illud dissimillimum conjunxisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052500Z">
        <seg>[214] [from the household servants;] As sweet was the disposition of the little daughter, so harsh was her mother's temper, so that it almost seemed that only the chariot of patience had joined that most dissimilar pair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[215]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Accessit in persecutionis cumulum incredula quorumdam confessariorum seu oscitantia, seu ruditas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052522Z">
        <seg>[215] Added to the sum of the persecution was the unbelief of certain confessors, whether through negligence or ignorance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[216]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Et sane, aut numquam, aut raro deerat grandi ejus patientiæ grandis materia, &lt;s2&gt;[quibus successerunt morbi]&lt;/s2&gt; siquidem vacantem a cruce externa, gravior occupabat interna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052559Z">
        <seg>And indeed, either never or rarely was there a lack of ample material for his great patience, [by which were succeeded illnesses], for when he was free from external affliction, a graver one would occupy him internally.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[217]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Febres tum ephimeras&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;h&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt;, tum reliquas contumaciores, &lt;s4&gt;[omnis ferme generis,]&lt;/s4&gt; ipsa assuetudo pene in jocum verterat, non item articulares illas manuum, pedumque fidiculas, quibus notissima (attamen pavenda) chiragræ, podagræ, arthritidis nomina insederunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052624Z">
        <seg>[217] Fevers then, sometimes ephemeral, sometimes the more stubborn ones, [of almost every kind,]&lt;/s4&gt; the very habit had almost turned it into a joke, not so with those jointed little fidgets of the hands and feet, to which the well-known (yet dreadful) names of chiragra, podagra, and arthritis have clung.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[218]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quos vocabat cælestes favores,]&lt;/s2&gt; De his aliquando familiarius devotæ cuidam personæ collocuta, universam hanc morborum seriem inter pretiosos ac illustriores sibi a Deo collatos favores recensuit, subjecitque, nisi hos ipsamet experta fuisset, numquam persuadere sibi potuisse, unicum miserabile corpus tantorum ac tam multiplicium dolorum capax fore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052702Z">
        <seg>[218] [which he called heavenly favors,]&lt;/s2&gt; Speaking of these more familiarly at one time to a certain devout person, she reviewed this entire series of illnesses among the precious and more illustrious favors granted to her by God, and added that unless she herself had experienced them, she could never have been persuaded that a single miserable body could be capable of so many and such varied pains;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[219]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quosque ex mira quadam visione præsciebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Huc plane referenda videtur celebris illa visio, quam inter diviniores Rosæ excessus tanti fecere graves mysticæ theologiæ professores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052717Z">
        <seg>[219] [whom he had foreseen by a certain marvelous vision,] This seems plainly to refer to that celebrated vision which, among the more divine excesses of the Rose, was so highly esteemed by the grave professors of mystical theology.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[21]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliquando mater exploratura tam accuratæ obedientiæ pronitatem, &lt;s2&gt;[mira simplicitate &amp; reverentia]&lt;/s2&gt; mandavit Filiæ, quod sericos flores, quos tunc forte pingebat acu, præpostero filorum ductu formaret contra omnem artis ideam, rectumque ordinem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035529Z">
        <seg>[21] Once, the mother, to test her daughter's readiness for such exacting obedience, commanded her, with [wonderful simplicity and reverence], to form the silk flowers she was then painting with a needle using the threads in reverse, contrary to all artistic convention and proper order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[220]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Interrupit hic Virginem doctor, sciscitando, cujus coloris fuerint illi arcus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052739Z">
        <seg>[220] The doctor here interrupted the Virgin, asking what color those arches had been?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[221]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Angeli, correpta bilance ac ponderibus, cœperunt onerare trutinam, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; uberioris gratiæ noverat præcursores,]&lt;/s2&gt; afflictiones afflictionibus exaggerantes in cumulum, velut earum molestiam ad amussim exploraturi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052755Z">
        <seg>[221] The angels, seizing the balance and weights, began to load the scales, [&amp; knew the forerunners of a more abundant grace,] piling affliction upon affliction into a heap, as if to probe their annoyance to the very limit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[222]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; His peractis, sonoram decentissime extulit vocem Salvator: &lt;s2&gt;[sicut ipsa paucis ante obitum diebus]&lt;/s2&gt; “Noverint (inquit) universi, quod tribulationem subsequitur gratia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052816Z">
        <seg>[222] When these things were done, Salvator most fittingly raised a sonorous voice: [as she herself had done in the few days before her death] "Let all know," he said, "that grace follows tribulation;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[223]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[doctori Castillo explicuit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Interpellavit doctor, cupiens explorare magis, sub quali specie aut figura Virgini apparuisset gratia?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052841Z">
        <seg>[223] [He explained it to Doctor Castillo.] The doctor interrupted, wishing to probe further, under what form or figure the Virgin had appeared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[225]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut hic foras erumperet]&lt;/s2&gt; Hoc affectu calescens Rosa, pro impetrando divino amore, ex ipsa divini amoris ignita pharetra nonnullas jaculatorias collegit, in formam precatiunculæ, quam lubet hic verbatim transcribere, quia cunctis ea placuit, multis usu profuit, amandique tum signis, tum stimulis scatet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053055Z">
        <seg>[225][so that it might break forth here] Warmed by this affection, Rosa, in order to obtain divine love, gathered from the very fiery quiver of divine love several short prayers, in the form of a little prayer, which I am inclined to transcribe here verbatim, because it pleased everyone, was of use to many, and is full of both signs and incentives to love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[226]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[in omni publica conversatione;]&lt;/s2&gt; Noverat ingeniosa Virgo ceteras in se virtutes abscondere: sola charitas, quia flamma est, premi ac celari nequaquam poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053127Z">
        <seg>[226] In all public conversation, the ingenuous virgin knew that she could hide all her other virtues; only charity, because it is a flame, could in no way be restrained or concealed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[227]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[dum autem se solam esse existimabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; At vero cum sola erat in recessu tugurioli, aut cum putabat a nemine se observari, tenerius simul atque liberius prosa, versu, sermone, cantu ructabat solemnissima quæque seraphici amoris axiomata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053210Z">
        <seg>[227] [while she thought herself alone,] But when she was truly alone in the recess of her little hut, or when she believed no one was watching her, she would more tenderly and freely—in prose, verse, speech, and song—utter the most solemn axioms of seraphic love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[228]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Subinde non inveniens modum, quo sibi inter hos fervores aut temperaret aut satisfaceret, &lt;s2&gt;[liberius affectui suo indulgebat.]&lt;/s2&gt; citharam (quam pulsare numquam didicerat) arripiebat de proximo pariete forte pendulam, tactuque chordarum moderabatur impetum vocis, suaviter cantillando saucii querimonias amoris, ut omni ritu gestuque sese Dilecto suo ostentaret amantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053226Z">
        <seg>[228] Soon finding no way in which he could either temper or satisfy himself amid these passions, [he indulged his affections more freely.]&lt;/s2&gt; she would seize the harp (which she had never learned to play), perhaps hanging on a nearby wall, and with the touch of its strings she would moderate the impetus of her voice, sweetly singing the wounded complaints of love, so that with every gesture and rite she might display herself as a lover to her Beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[229]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Vehementissimæ dilectionis comes &amp; testis est zelus: &lt;s2&gt;[Etiam vehementer ideo dolebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; nec hujus in se ardores Virgo celare poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053300Z">
        <seg>Zeal is the companion and witness of the most ardent love: [She also grieved all the more because of it,] nor could the Virgin conceal its ardors within herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[22]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[obtemperabat matri]&lt;/s2&gt; Triennio circiter ante felicem Rosæ obitum contigit, eam ab illustri matrona D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035607Z">
        <seg>[22] [Obeyed his mother] About three years before the happy death of Rosa, it happened that she was taken in by the illustrious matron D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[230]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Deum peccatis offendi,]&lt;/s2&gt; Mendacia, quæ puerorum assiduis jocis &amp; loquacitati abesse vix umquam solent, tam dire execrabatur, ut proprium suum apophthegma esset: “Nec pro cælo, nec pro terra mentiendum, quia Deus veritas est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053328Z">
        <seg>[230] [That God is offended by sins,] He so execrated lies, which are seldom absent from the constant jesting and loquacity of boys, that it became his own apophthegm: "Lie not for heaven's sake, nor for earth's sake, for God is truth."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[231]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; maxime lætabatur ob conversionem cujusdam peccatricis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Ejusdem amicissimi zeli genuina soboles erat immensum illud gaudium, quo vernabat Rosa, quotiescumque Dei honorem &amp; obsequium insigni aliquo successu videbat promoveri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053412Z">
        <seg>[231] [&amp; was especially delighted by the conversion of a certain sinner,] That immense joy, which the Rose experienced, was the genuine offspring of her most ardent zeal whenever she saw the honor and worship of God promoted by some notable success.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[232]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nil erat, quod satiaret Rosam, amores suos Sponso testificari cupientem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053437Z">
        <seg>[232] Nothing could satisfy Rosa, who longed to testify to her love for her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[233]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Inquietissima vis amoris nihil non comminiscebatur, quo serviret unice dilecto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053526Z">
        <seg>The most restless force of love devised nothing that might not serve its one and only beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[235]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Summa vaporantis alloquii hæc erat: “Domine, &lt;s2&gt;[ferventer orans coram picta Christi imagine,]&lt;/s2&gt; o quando te omnes diligent, prout diligi mereris?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053706Z">
        <seg>[235] The gist of the vaporizing discourse was this: "Lord, fervently praying before the painted image of Christ, oh, when will all love you as you deserve to be loved?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[236]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Una tamen filiarum, non abnuente matre, irrepsit, &lt;s2&gt;[precibus ex ea elicit sudorem,]&lt;/s2&gt; quasi candelas emunctorio in altari purgatura: statimque velut subitaneo pavore Rosam interpellans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053734Z">
        <seg>[236] However, one of the daughters, with her mother's consent, crept in, [her pleas drew a sweat from her,] as if she were going to clean the candles on the altar with a cleaning tool; and immediately, as if seized by a sudden terror, she interrupted Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[237]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Obest miraculorum fidei præceps credulitas, prodest circumspecta dubitatio, &lt;s2&gt;[quem multi testes oculati]&lt;/s2&gt; ut severitate examinis, ceu polituræ defrictu simul exploretur soliditas, &amp; clarescat veritas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053805Z">
        <seg>[237] Precipitate credulity is detrimental to the faith of miracles; circumspect doubt is beneficial, [as many eyewitnesses testify], so that by the severity of examination the solidity may be explored, as it were, by the abrasion of polishing, and the truth may be made clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[238]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; His minime contentus quæstor fidum hominem Andream Lopes ablegat, jubetque e vicino Societatis Jesu collegio accersiri nominatim Patres Didacum Martinez, &amp; Didacum de Peñalosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053821Z">
        <seg>[238] Not at all satisfied with this, the quaestor sends his trusted man, Andrew Lopes, and orders that the Fathers Didacus Martinez and Didacus de Peñalosa be summoned by name from the nearby Jesuit college.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[239]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Hoc prodigio territam familiam metu liberat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Interea Gundisalvum, uxorem, familiam gravissimus invasit timor, ne forte ob aliquod suum, aut domesticorum occultum scelus, hoc in suis ædibus portentum accidisset, aut impendentem aliquam divinæ ultionis cladem miseris comminaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053908Z">
        <seg>[239] Meanwhile, Gundisalvus, his wife, and family were seized by the gravest fear, lest perhaps on account of some fault of their own, or of a household member, a calamity should befall them.[This prodigy frees the terrified family from fear,] Meanwhile, Gundisalvus, his wife, and their household were seized by the gravest fear, lest perhaps because of some hidden crime of his own or of his household this portent had occurred in their own home, or was threatening the miserable with some impending calamity of divine vengeance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[23]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[aliisque superioribus]&lt;/s2&gt; Sed longe mirabilior obedientia ultra mortem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035635Z">
        <seg>[23] [and other superiors] But far more marvelous is obedience beyond death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[240]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[suumque brachium læsum eo sudore liniri jubens,]&lt;/s2&gt; Neque hic stetit miraculum, quin partu novi miraculi &amp; suam certitudinem assereret, &amp; Rosæ brachium fidemque liberaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053939Z">
        <seg>[240] [and ordering his wounded arm to be anointed with that sweat,] Nor did the miracle stop there, but with the birth of a new miracle it confirmed its own certainty and delivered Rosa's arm and faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[241]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Meridiei punctus erat, quando D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054010Z">
        <seg>It was noon when D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[242]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Exinde gravior tempestas formidinis Gundisalvo incubuit, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; hospiti timenti, ne ideo imago transferatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; sed novo Rosæ solatio mox dissipanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054035Z">
        <seg>[242] From then on, a more severe storm of dread descended upon Gundisalvus, [and upon the fearful guest, lest the image be transferred because of it,] but was soon dispelled by a new consolation of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[243]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[affirmate prædicit, illam in domo ejus mansuram.]&lt;/s2&gt; Ita esse pridem conjecerant ex affectu, devotione, reverentia, quam peculiariter modo huic, modo illi ibidem imagini, Rosa impendebat, velut inde favoris divini copiosior eam aura afflasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054106Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[243] [He confidently predicts that she will remain in his house.] They had long since surmised this from the affection, devotion, and reverence that Rosa showed in a particularly peculiar way to this image and to that one there, as if a more abundant breeze of divine favor had blown upon her from them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[245]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Anno MDLIII juxta Caxaguanam&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; in Cusco ac finitimis provinciis, &lt;s4&gt;[quæ quondam in aëre apparens, Indos terruerat,]&lt;/s4&gt; gentilium Indorum plusquam ducenta millia sub signis erant, ut Catholicum exercitum, sexcentis non amplius capitibus numerosum, proculcarent potius, quam profligarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054236Z">
        <seg>[245] In the year 1553, near Caxaguana in Cusco and the neighboring provinces, [which, once appearing in the air, had terrified the Indians,]&lt;/s4&gt; more than two hundred thousand pagan Indians were under arms, so that they would trample rather than defeat the Catholic army, which numbered no more than six hundred men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[246]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Demum anno MDCXLIII sexto Idus Maii, rex Catholicus regnum suum Peruanum cælesti Virginis matris præsidio firmius contra impendentia pericula communiri exoptans, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; quæ regni Peruani protectrix electa]&lt;/s2&gt; mandavit, hanc ab universis incolis, publico ritu, eligi Protectricem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054249Z">
        <seg>Finally, in the year 1643, on May 7, the Catholic king, desiring to more firmly fortify his Peruvian kingdom against impending dangers by the heavenly protection of the Virgin Mother,[&amp; chosen as protectress of the Peruvian kingdom]&lt;/s2&gt; commanded that she be chosen by all the inhabitants, by public rite, as Protectress;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[247]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[multis beneficiis S. Rosam cumulavit,]&lt;/s2&gt; A teneris hic Rosæ (quamquam nil minus quam ferreæ) magnes fuit ac siderites&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054314Z">
        <seg>[247] [He showered St. Rose with many benefits,] From her earliest years, Rose was a magnet and siderite (though nothing less than iron).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[248]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut ipsa ingenue fatebatur.]&lt;/s2&gt; Inter alia de modo, quo Deiparam in hac imagine sibi loquentem percipiebat, interrogata, simplicissime respondit, mirabile hoc genus allocutionis sine idiomate, sonitu, motuque labiorum pure sympatheticum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;h&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; esse, ac solum iconis vultum varia serenissimæ frontis eradiatione, velut quibusdam notis ac nutibus, tam apposite, dextre, &amp; perspicue exprimere sensa &amp; affectus pro re nata, ut nulla exquisitissimorum verborum facundia melius præstare id possit: eamdem vultui divini Filioli vim inesse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054354Z">
        <seg>[248][as she herself ingenuously confessed.] When, among other things, she was asked about the way in which she perceived the Mother of God speaking to her in this image, she answered most simply that this marvelous kind of address was purely sympathetic, without idiom, sound, or movement of the lips, and that only the icon's face, by various radiations of its most serene brow, as it were by certain signs and nods, so aptly, skillfully, and clearly expresses the feelings and affections appropriate to the occasion, that the most eloquent language of the most exquisite words could not do it better: that the same power resides in the face of the divine Son;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[249]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Accidit permissu Dei, quod invidus sathan cuidam regulari communitati Limæ inseverat discordiarum zizania.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054413Z">
        <seg>[249] It happened by God's permission that the envious Satan had sown the tares of discord among a certain community of regulars in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[24]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ac confessariis suis.]&lt;/s2&gt; Ex his quilibet facillime æstimarit, quam accurate suis confessariis obtemperarit Rosa ad huc mortalis: nam ex quo vestem S. Patris Dominici assumpserat, ab ore suorum patrum spiritualium pendebat tota, &amp; quidquid hi vel obiter innuissent, id Virgini non mandatum, sed cæleste oraculum videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035703Z">
        <seg>[24] [and to her confessors.]From this, anyone could easily see how precisely Rosa had obeyed her confessors up to this point: for ever since she had taken the habit of St. Dominic, she hung entirely on the words of her spiritual fathers, and whatever they even hinted at seemed not a command to the Virgin but a heavenly oracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[250]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Verum paulo post a confessario solicitius examinata, &lt;s2&gt;[in Religioso cœtu exortam, precibus dissipavit]&lt;/s2&gt; nec ausa quidquam reticere, totam ingenue seriem exposuit, ut pridie inter simplices preces utrumque tum Deiparæ, tum Filioli vultum invenerat non modo chalybis instar prædurum, asperum, inexorabilem, sed &amp; severe minacem, austerum, ac manifesta indignatione caperatum: ut frustra conata fuerat Filium emollire per Matrem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054438Z">
        <seg>[250] But a little later, more carefully examined by her confessor, [having arisen in a religious community, she dissipated it with prayers] and not daring to conceal anything, she ingenuously laid out the entire sequence, that the day before, during simple prayers to both the Mother of God, she had found the face of the Son not only as hard as steel, rough, inexorable, but also sternly threatening, austere, and seized with manifest indignation: so that she had tried in vain to soften the Son through the Mother;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[251]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[(ab alia picta &amp; domestica Deiparæ effigie]&lt;/s2&gt; Non abs re fuerit paucis subnectere, quæ Rosæ cum alia Deiparentis imagine, non sculpta sed picta, in Gundisalvi oratorio contigerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054506Z">
        <seg>[251] [From another painted and domestic image of the Virgin Mary] It will not be out of place to briefly relate what happened to Rosa with another image of the Virgin Mary, not carved but painted, in Gundisalvi's oratory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[252]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[etiam varios faveres obtinuit)]&lt;/s2&gt; Accidit, quod in oratorio Maria de Usategui, coram Rosa &amp; duobus familiaribus instituta de beneficiis beatissimæ Virginis spirituali collatione, incideret in magnalia, quæ ad imaginem Deiparæ in Atocha&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, vico Matritensibus vicino, manus superna dignatur operari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054521Z">
        <seg>[252] [also obtained various favors]It happened that, in the oratory of Maria de Usategui, during a spiritual conference on the benefits of the most blessed Virgin held before Rosa and two family members, she was led to the great miracles that the divine hand deigns to work at the image of the Mother of God at Atocha, a village near Madrid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[253]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[cumque ad illam ornandam flores deessent,]&lt;/s2&gt; Sed redeundum tantisper ad sacellum Rosarii, cui Rosa toto anno per omne Sabbatum flores, quos ipsa coluerat, fasciculatim aut mittebat aut deferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054549Z">
        <seg>[253] [And since there were no flowers to adorn it,] But for the time being she had to return to the chapel of the Rosary, to which Rosa, every Sabbath throughout the year, would send or bring in bunches the flowers she herself had cultivated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[254]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Unius idea reperta fuit in Virginis pugillaribus, hoc tenore: “Memoria pro vestimento, quod ego Rosa de S. Maria, &lt;s2&gt;[ei spirituale vestimentum contexuit.]&lt;/s2&gt; indignum mancipium Reginæ angelorum, ordior contexere Virgini Dei Matri, auxiliante Domino.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054605Z">
        <seg>One idea was found in the Virgin's pamphlets, to the following effect: "A memorial for the vestment which I, Rosa of St. Mary, [wove for her a spiritual vestment,] an unworthy handmaid of the Queen of angels, have begun to weave for the Virgin Mother of God, with the help of the Lord."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[256]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Subinde Rosam ad sacras ædes processuram comitabatur honestatis causa germanus frater, &lt;s2&gt;[ubicumque positum erat,]&lt;/s2&gt; doluitque toties sibi cum ea subsistendum esse, quoties Soror ibidem ad stipulas de terra sublevandas sese inclinabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054850Z">
        <seg>[256] A brother would accompany Rosa to the sacred buildings for the sake of propriety, [wherever it was set,] and he grieved that he had to stop with her so often whenever his sister bent down there to lift the stakes from the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[257]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; At graviter modesteque replicuit Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054912Z">
        <seg>[257] But the Virgin replied gravely and modestly;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[258]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quarum unam, quæ in horto proreginæ exaruerat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosa in suo hortulo plantaverat libanotim&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, (rosmarinus vulgo dicitur) ejusque tres arbusculas distinctis cespitibus ita in crucis formam coëgerat, ut singulæ glebæ seorsum accuratam montis Calvariæ figuram undequaque exprimerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054937Z">
        <seg>[258] [of which one, which had withered in the queen's garden,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosa had planted in her little garden a libanothim&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;(commonly called rosemary), and had arranged its three little shrubs in distinct clumps in the shape of a cross, so that each clump, taken separately, would accurately reproduce the form of Mount Calvary on every side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[259]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[mirabiliter pristino virori restituit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Vix in quartum diem exsucca plantula penes Rosam fuit, cum speciosior, quam ante fuerat, undique reviruit, novis ramusculorum comata pullulis, novo radicularum ac medullarum succo rediviva, plexilique virgularum obedientia in pristinam crucis figuram reformata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055002Z">
        <seg>[259] [miraculously restored to its former green.]The little shriveled plant by Rosamunde had scarcely been revived for a fourth day when, more beautiful than before, it burst forth anew from every side, with new, bushy shoots on its branches, revived by the fresh sap of its roots and pith, and the obedience of its boughs reforming it into its original cross-shape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[25]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Sed redeamus ad matrem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035722Z">
        <seg>But let us return to the mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[260]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Ad imaginem S. Catharinæ Senensis ornandam]&lt;/s2&gt; Digrediendum nunc ad hujus seraphicæ magistræ imaginem, crucis ac stigmatum characteribus insignitam, quacum Rosæ plurimum fuit commercii, nec sine prodigiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055011Z">
        <seg>[260] [To adorn the image of St. Catherine of Siena] We must now turn to the image of this seraphic teacher, marked with the characters of the cross and of her stigmata, with which Rose had the greatest dealings, and not without miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[261]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[prodigiose pecunia subministratur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Sed dum circuibat charam iconem, dum vestiebat, dum plicas distinguebat, temperare sibi a lachrymis, ab osculis, ab alloquiis præ ardore non poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055036Z">
        <seg>[261] [A prodigious sum of money is provided,] But while she was circling the precious icon, while she was dressing it, while she was arranging its folds, she could not restrain herself from tears, from kisses, from addresses, for her ardor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[262]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Alio tempore optabat Rosa, caryophylli&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;e&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; copiam ad manum esse, &lt;s4&gt;[&amp; flores, quos illa tempestas anni non ferebat,]&lt;/s4&gt; qua sacram imaginem condecoraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055120Z">
        <seg>At another time, Rosa wished to have an abundance of carnations&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;e&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; on hand, &lt;s4&gt;[&amp; flowers, which that season of the year did not produce,]&lt;/s4&gt; with which to adorn the sacred image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[263]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Postridie summo mane (siquidem is processioni decretus erat dies) properarunt ad Rosam, &lt;s2&gt;[in horto sanctæ Virginis inveniuntur.]&lt;/s2&gt; ut quod in ornanda imagine reliquum erat, cum ea perficerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055148Z">
        <seg>The next morning at dawn (for the day had been appointed for the procession) they hurried to Rose, [they are found in the garden of the Holy Virgin,] so that they might complete with her whatever remained to be done in adorning the image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[264]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Deinde B Catharina cuidam ornatus adjutrici]&lt;/s2&gt; Alia vice Rosa ad vestiendam sanctæ Catharinæ iconem cum aliis adjutricibus vocarat Mariam Euphemiam de Parejas viduam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055216Z">
        <seg>[264] Then B. Catherine, to a certain attendant for adornment. On another occasion, Rosa had summoned Mary Euphemia de Parejas, a widow, to dress the icon of St. Catherine along with the other helpers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[265]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[duplex beneficium contulit,]&lt;/s2&gt; Aliquando eadem, quæ supra, Francisca de Montoya Rosam in exornanda S. Catharinæ Senensis icone per totam noctem adjuverat, completoque opere, dum nonnihil quietura discedebat, ut post aliquot horas festivæ processioni interveniret, Rosa digressam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055302Z">
        <seg>[265][265] [She conferred a twofold benefit,] At one time the same Francisca de Montoya had helped Rosa adorn the icon of St. Catherine of Siena all night, and when the work was completed, as she was departing to rest a little so that she could take part in the festive procession after a few hours, Rosa, having strayed, encountered the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[266]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ac sanctam Rosam arthritide,]&lt;/s2&gt; Beata Catharina in reliquas imaginis suæ vestiarias &amp; excultrices tam munifica, utique in suam Rosam illiberalis non fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055321Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055321Z">
        <seg>[266] [and Saint Rose, who suffered from arthritis,] Blessed Catherine was so munificent toward the other vestment-makers and sculptors of her image that she was by no means stingy toward her own Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[267]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Dies erat S. Laurentii martyris, quando sub vesperam medicus, &lt;s2&gt;[quæ manum ejus invaserat,]&lt;/s2&gt; inspecta manu, purulentam meliceridem&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, aut aliud quodcumque genus apostematis subesse ratus, lenta cataplasmata&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;k&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; decrevit applicanda, jussitque in diem posterum distrahendo sanguini, venam in lævo brachio aperiri scalpello.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055349Z">
        <seg>[267] It was the day of St. Lawrence the martyr, when toward evening the physician, [which had invaded his hand,]&lt;/s2&gt; having inspected the hand, suspected that a purulent melicerides&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, or some other kind of abscess, was present, and ordered that slow poultices&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;k&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; be applied; and he directed that the next day, by drawing blood, a vein in the left arm be opened with a scalpel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[268]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Post horulam supervenit quæstor, vidensque Rosam utraque manu expeditissime operantem, &lt;s2&gt;[confestim liberavit,]&lt;/s2&gt; adstupuit, ac leniter interpellans, “Itane (inquit) dextra tua valet, ut ad ista sufficiat?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055424Z">
        <seg>[268] An hour later the quæstor arrived, and seeing Rosa working with both hands as swiftly as possible, [he immediately released her,] he was astonished and, gently interrupting her, said, "Is your right hand really strong enough for this?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[269]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[sicut ipsa Virgo candide fassa est.]&lt;/s2&gt; Accersitus postridie medicus examinavit manum, deinde Virginem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055442Z">
        <seg>[269] [as the Virgin herself candidly confessed.] The next day, the doctor was summoned, examined the hand, and then the Virgin;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[26]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quæstor Gundisalvus (de quo supra, &amp; frequentius infra) tacitus mirabatur exhaustam viribus Rosam, &lt;s2&gt;[indefesso manuum labore,]&lt;/s2&gt; ac diurnis operibus extreme lassam, nihilominus in mediam usque noctem labori manuum insistere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035740Z">
        <seg>[26] Quaestor Gundisalvus (of whom mention is made above, and more frequently below) marveled in silence that Rosa, though drained of strength, [with tireless labor of her hands,] and utterly weary from her daily tasks, nevertheless persisted in manual work until the middle of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[271]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Neque id satis erat: nam siqua interveniebat aut festæ diei celebritas aut solemnis statio, &lt;s2&gt;[ex consilio confessariorum suorum,]&lt;/s2&gt; etiam quater, imo quinquies intra septimanam divino hoc Edulio avidissime fruebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055702Z">
        <seg>[271] Nor was that enough: for if any festival or solemn station intervened, [at the advice of his confessors,] he would even partake of this divine Edule four, nay, five times within a week, with the greatest eagerness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[272]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[idque semper faciebat cum tanto fervore,]&lt;/s2&gt; Hic vero notatu dignum est, quod Virgo in tanta communicandi frequentia, simulque tanta immaculatæ suæ conscientiæ serenitate, nihilominus, quotiescumque ad divinum epulum accedendum erat, toties sacramentalem confessionem præmittebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055718Z">
        <seg>[272] [and she always did it with such fervor,] It is truly worthy of note, however, that the Virgin, in such frequent communion,[272] [and she always did this with such fervor,] It is truly worthy of note that the Virgin, in such frequent communion and at the same time with such serenity of her immaculate conscience, nevertheless, whenever she was to approach the divine banquet, she would always precede it with sacramental confession;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[273]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut sacerdotes ei sacram Hostiam porrigentes,]&lt;/s2&gt; Quadam feria secunda Pentecostes in sacello Rosarii sacris operante P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055746Z">
        <seg>[273] [as the priests were presenting him with the sacred Host,] On a certain Tuesday after Pentecost, in the chapel of the Rosary, while the Mass was being celebrated by Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[274]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[faciem ipsius coruscantem viderent,]&lt;/s2&gt; Magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055808Z">
        <seg>[274] [if they saw his face shining,] Master Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[275]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055833Z">
        <seg>[275] P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[276]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Voluit Deus per extima hæc &amp; sensibilia, utcumque manifestari foris, &lt;s2&gt;[Ipsa Virgo explicare non poterat]&lt;/s2&gt; quanta devotionis pyra flagrabat intus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055842Z">
        <seg>[276] God willed that through these outward and sensible things, however manifest they were on the outside, [the Virgin herself could not explain] how great a pyre of devotion was burning within.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[277]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ex ore Virginis hæc audierat confessarius P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055910Z">
        <seg>[277] The confessor, Father, had heard these words from the Virgin's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[278]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quo sic confortabatur ac satiebatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Ne isti solares effectus omnino laterent abditi, providit Christus, dum ex ipsismet foras redundare fecit usque in Rosæ corpusculum duo prodigia, robur &amp; saturitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055932Z">
        <seg>[278] [by which she was thus comforted and satisfied,] So that these solar effects might not at all remain hidden and secret, Christ provided, while he caused two prodigies—strength and fullness—to overflow from them themselves right into Rose's little body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[279]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut diu alimento corporali non indigeret.]&lt;/s2&gt; Rogata cibum sumere, maxime quod pridiano exhausta jejunio videretur refectione indigere, meminissetque diem Domincum a lege jejunii exsortem esse, respondebat, præ ingenti satietate a Mensa Domini, alium se cibum admittere nondum posse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055947Z">
        <seg>[279] [so that she would not need to take food for a long time.]When asked to take food, especially since she appeared, from the previous day, to be exhausted by fasting and in need of refreshment, and would remember that Sunday was exempt from the law of fasting, she replied that, on account of her great satiety from the Lord's Table, she could not yet admit any other food;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[27]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Mirabilius est, quod Ludovica Urtado de Bustamante, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; solerti industria]&lt;/s2&gt; vidua Bartholomæi Alphonsi de Lombrera vexilliferi, asseverat in Processu, Rosam tot sanctis exercitiis interdiu noctuque districtam, tot affatim morbis impeditam, tot supernis raptibus crebro absorptam, unico tamen die tot laborum pensa solitam absolvere, quot alia quævis etiam celerrima, peritissima, vix integro quatriduo absolvisset: &amp; ne putemus ob tantam festinæ Rosæ velocitatem, (ut fit) hiulca, manca, imperfecta prodiisse opera;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035750Z">
        <seg>[27] More marvelous is the fact that Ludovica Urtado de Bustamante, [and with diligent industry] widow of Bartholomew Alphonsus de Lombrera, standard-bearer, affirms in the Process that Rosa was so occupied with holy exercises day and night, so impeded by a multitude of illnesses, so often absorbed in heavenly raptures, was accustomed to complete in a single day so many tasks that any other, even the swiftest and most skilled, would have scarcely finished in four days: and lest we think that on account of Rose's great haste, her works were (as is often the case) produced hollow, imperfect, and lacking in completion;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[280]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Summa cum reverentia]&lt;/s2&gt; Ceterum quanta religione solita fuerit Rosa venerari hoc Sacramentum, sicubi aut immolabatur in Missæ sacrificio, aut prostabat adorandum in altari, utcumque supra attigimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060009Z">
        <seg>[280] [With the utmost reverence] Moreover, how religiously Rose was accustomed to venerate this Sacrament, whether it was being offered in the sacrifice of the Mass or exposed for adoration on the altar, as we have noted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[281]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Idem Virgini erat immotæ attentionis tenor, quando in hierotheca expositum venerabile Sacramentum adorabatur a populo, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; immotæ mentis attentione]&lt;/s2&gt; ut fit in publicis XL horarum supplicationibus: illic, a mane usque ad vesperam continua &amp; prorsus immota geniculatione hærebat Rosa oblita prandii, neque vel guttulam acceptabat in refrigerium, contenta jubilo atque deliciis, quas percipiebat ex corporali Sponsi præsentia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060031Z">
        <seg>[281] The same was the Virgin's unmoving tenor of attention, when the venerable Sacrament was exposed in the oratory and adored by the people,[&amp; with an unmoving attention of mind]&lt;/s2&gt; as is done in public Forty Hours' devotions: there, from morning until evening, she remained in continuous and utterly motionless kneeling, Rose, having forgotten her lunch, nor did she accept even a drop for refreshment, content with the joy and delights she received from the bodily presence of the Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[282]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Neque segnior erat ad cultum hujus adorandi mysterii, &lt;s2&gt;[adorabat hoc venerabile Sacramentum]&lt;/s2&gt; etiam quando extra conspectum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060050Z">
        <seg>Nor was he any less diligent in the observance of this adored mystery, [he adored this venerable Sacrament] even when he was out of sight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[283]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nullus ei manuum labor jucundior erat, quam quo altare, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; ad illius ornatum strenue laborabat;]&lt;/s2&gt; tabernaculum, aut in majori hebdomada S. Sepulchrum veniebat exornandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060105Z">
        <seg>[283] No manual labor was more pleasing to him than decorating the altar, the tabernacle, or, during Holy Week, the Holy Sepulchre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[284]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Imo, cum classis Calvinistarum]&lt;/s2&gt; Denique erga SS. Eucharistiæ mysterium is Rosæ affectus erat, quo maluit pro illo sanguinem vitamque expendere, quam flores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060133Z">
        <seg>[284] Indeed, with the Calvinist class, he was so attached to the mystery of the Holy Eucharist that he preferred to shed his blood and life for it rather than flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[285]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ad littora Peruana appulisset,]&lt;/s2&gt; Tristius interea nuntium supervenit usque ad Rosam, nempe inimicos fidei, ex alto navium in lembos, inde in arenam prosultare manipulatim, &amp; ad portas civitatis globatim procedere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060158Z">
        <seg>[285] [had landed on the Peruvian shores,] Meanwhile a more distressing report reached Rosa: namely, that the enemies of the faith, from the ships, were jumping into boats, then onto the sand in squads, and advancing in masses toward the city gates.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[286]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; His facie rutilante prolatis, exemit vagina forfices, &lt;s2&gt;[pro defensione ac veritate sacræ Eucharistiæ]&lt;/s2&gt; sibique vestem, qua longior in terram defluebat, alacriter præcidit, manicisque in cubitum usque replicatis, quod reliquum erat tunicæ, intra cingulum, usque at talos attraxit, cothurnos subere sublevatos exuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060217Z">
        <seg>[286] With his face flushed by these words, he drew scissors from his sheath,[in defense of and for the truth of the sacred Eucharist] he eagerly cut off the part of his garment that hung longer on the ground, and, rolling up his sleeves to the elbow, drew the rest of his tunic inside his belt down to his ankles, and took off his cork-soled sandals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[287]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hæc ita pronuntiabat, ut ex oculorum scintillis, &lt;s2&gt;[sanguinem fundere parabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; ex impavidi vultus constantia, ex vivaci tono heroicæ vocis, ceteroque gestu animosæ Viraginis facile conjiceretur, Rosam pro Eucharistico altaris Agno leænam induisse: nec circumstantes feminæ suismet credebant oculis, videntes Amazonem, quæ hactenus non nisi viva mansuetudinis idea fuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060242Z">
        <seg>She pronounced these words in such a way that from the sparks in her eyes, [she was preparing to shed blood,]&lt;/s2&gt; from the steadfastness of her fearless countenance, from the lively tone of her heroic voice, and from the entire bearing of the spirited Virgin, it was easy to infer that Rosa had donned the lioness's guise for the Eucharistic Lamb of the altar: nor did the women standing by believe their own eyes, seeing an Amazon who until then had been nothing but the living idea of meekness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[288]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Defuit ergo non martyrio animus, sed animo martyrium, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; sæpe pro eadem martyrium subire cupiebat.]&lt;/s2&gt; toties &amp; ante, &amp; postea votis ardentissimis exoptatum: nam crebro deflebat sortem suam, quod pro conditione sexus infidelium regiones adire non poterat, pro Christo millies mortem quæsitura: dolebat, se non illis temporibus locisque natam, ubi tyrannica persecutio Christianorum sanguine orchestras, fora, amphitheatra purpurabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060304Z">
        <seg>[288] Thus it was not a lack of martyrdom that was lacking, but a lack of spirit for martyrdom, [and often he desired to undergo martyrdom for the same cause.][288] Thus it was not a lack of martyrdom that failed her, but a lack of spirit for it, [and she often longed to undergo martyrdom for the same cause.] For she frequently lamented her lot, that by reason of her sex she could not go into the regions of the infidels, where she would have sought death a thousand times for Christ's sake; she grieved that she was not born in those times and places where the tyrannical persecution was staining the orchestras, the forums, and the amphitheaters with the blood of Christians;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[28]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Neque hic stetit anxius amor ac solicitudo filiæ, &lt;s2&gt;[egestatem parentum suorum sublevare conabatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; conantis omni ingenio parentum egestatem sublevare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035756Z">
        <seg>[28] Nor did the anxious love and concern for his daughter stop here; he was trying with all his ingenuity to relieve his parents' poverty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[290]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[unum ex confessariis suis hortabatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Accidit, unum ex Rosæ confessariis tractare de sua missione ad annuntiandum vicinis barbaris Euangelium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060409Z">
        <seg>[290] [He was exhorting one of his confessors,] It happened that he was speaking with one of Rose's confessors about his mission to announce the Gospel to the neighboring barbarians.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[291]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hic Virgo, tametsi de suis piis operibus humillime sentiret, &lt;s2&gt;[ut impavide missionem apud Indos institueret,]&lt;/s2&gt; nihilominus, ut iturienti quantacumque poterat calcaria figeret, concepta ingenti fiducia promisit, universo exercitiorum suorum merito se illi in sancta expeditione succursuram, dummodo is vicissim pro rata ipsam participem vellet totius spiritualis lucri, quod ex infidelium conversione erat reportaturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060428Z">
        <seg>[291] Here the Virgin, although she felt most humbly about her own pious works, [as she fearlessly established the mission among the Indians,]&lt;/s2&gt; nevertheless, to spur him on as much as she could, she, with great confidence, promised that she would assist him in the holy expedition, provided that he, in turn, would let her share in proportion her part of all the spiritual gain he was to receive from the conversion of the infidels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[292]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Eodem fervore spiritus nitebatur pia Virgo religiosos viros (quotquot aptos noverat) ad gentilium conversionem succedere: &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alios viros aptos excitabat ad idem opus,]&lt;/s2&gt; præsertim sui Ordinis Fratres rogabat, monebat, obtestabatur, huc huc suos in litteris profectus intentione tempestiva destinarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060437Z">
        <seg>[292] With the same fervor of spirit, the pious Virgin urged religious men (whichever she knew to be suitable) to succeed in the conversion of the Gentiles: [and she also stirred other suitable men to the same work,] especially she begged, admonished, and implored the Brothers of her Order to send their men, well-versed in the letters, to this work with timely dispatch;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[293]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Asseverabat gravi sensu &amp; impetu, se nisi feminam natura finxisset, &lt;s2&gt;[illud per se factura, nisi sexus obstitisset.]&lt;/s2&gt; hoc primum in votis fuisse habituram, ut decursa studiorum litterariorum encyclopedia, se totam missioni euangelicæ devoveret, cupida adire gentes ferocissimas, belluinas, anthropophagas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, dummodo suis Indis, adhuc gentilismo implicitis, opem ac remedium sua catechesi, sudore, sanguine impertiret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060459Z">
        <seg>[293] She asserted with great earnestness and force that, had nature not made her a woman, she herself would have accomplished that, had not her sex been an obstacle.&lt;/s2&gt; this would have been the first thing she would have wished for, that, having completed the encyclopedia of literary studies, she would devote herself entirely to the evangelical mission, eager to go to the fiercest, most savage, man-eating peoples&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, provided she could reach her own Indians, still entangled in paganism, bring help and remedy through her catechesis, sweat, and blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[294]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Etiam malos Christianos monebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Nec tepidior ei zelus &amp; cura fuit erga periclitantes Christianos, si quos noverat aut per culpam mortalem a statu divinæ amicitiæ infeliciter excidisse, aut capitali aliquo vitio ad certum interitum illaqueari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060515Z">
        <seg>He also admonished wicked Christians, and his zeal and concern for those in peril were no less fervent.[294] [He also admonished wicked Christians,] Nor was his zeal and concern any less fervent toward Christian souls in peril, whether he knew any who had, by mortal sin, wretchedly fallen from the state of divine friendship, or who were entangled by some capital vice and thus drawn into certain ruin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[295]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[aut moneri curabat, ut resipiscerent,]&lt;/s2&gt; Hæc plerumque misericors Virgo tam luculento offerebat pectoris incendio, tantoque inflammati cordis affectu, ut non raro audientes compunxerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060539Z">
        <seg>[295] [or she would see to it that they were warned to repent,] This the merciful Virgin usually offered with such a brilliant display of ardor in her breast, and with such a fervent affection of her heart, that she would not seldom move those who heard her to compunction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[296]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Evenit, dum adhuc matri cohabitabat Rosa, juvenem genere, quam moribus nobiliorem Vincentium Montesium Venegas in eadem platea haud procul a Rosæ ædibus sortiri domicilium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060604Z">
        <seg>[296] It happened, while Rosa was still living with her mother, that a young man of noble birth, even more distinguished by his manners, Vincent Montes Venegas, took up residence on the same street not far from Rosa's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[297]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; At Virgo divinitus edocta secretum, quod corde premebat Vincentius, &lt;s2&gt;[divinitus edocta redarguit,]&lt;/s2&gt; efflictim misereri cœpit fœtentis animæ, in qua stagnabant lubricæ juventutis impura desideria;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060623Z">
        <seg>[297] But the Virgin, divinely instructed in the secret that weighed on Vincent's heart, [divinely instructed, she rebuked him,] began to pity with all her heart the foul-smelling soul in which the impure desires of a slippery youth were stagnating.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[298]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hac improvisa Virginis concione ictus Vincentius, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; ad pœnitentiam adducit.]&lt;/s2&gt; dejecit oculos, seseque introspiciens, aliquantisper obmutuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060704Z">
        <seg>Struck by the Virgin's sudden sermon, Vincent lowered his eyes and, looking inward, fell silent for a while.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[299]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Prodigium erat, siqua persona cuicumque vitio obnoxia Rosam accessisset, &lt;s2&gt;[Præterea mulierem iracundam corrigit,]&lt;/s2&gt; nec ab ea melior discessisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060716Z">
        <seg>It was a miracle if anyone, no matter how flawed, approached Rosa and did not come away better.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[29]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[eos ægrotantes obsequio juvabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Si quando patrem aut matrem dejecisset corporis ægritudo, nil Filiæ antiquius, nil sanctius, nil solemnius erat, quam, sepositis omnibus, decumbenti servire, medicinas conquirere, suis manibus parare, inferre, bolatimque porrigere esculenta, refrigeria adaptare, applicare lenitiva.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035808Z">
        <seg>[29] [she helped the sick with care,]If at any time her father or mother was laid low by illness, nothing was more important, more sacred, or more solemn for the daughter than, setting all else aside, to serve the invalid, to seek out medicines, to prepare them with her own hands, to administer them, to bring food in small portions, to provide cooling remedies, and to apply soothing lotions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[2]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; in Baptismo accipit nomen Elizabethæ]&lt;/s2&gt; Nascenti Rosæ paterna domus obtigit in vico S. Dominici, ac pone quidem juxta Ædem S. Spiritus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, ut vel ipse locus videatur futuræ tum sanctimoniæ tum instituti signasse præsagium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T193950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211814Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[2]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; at Baptism she receives the name Elizabeth]&lt;/s2&gt; When Rose was born, the paternal house on St. Dominic Street fell to her lot, and indeed right next to the Church of the Holy Spirit&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, so that even the place itself seems to have foretold both her future sanctity and her institute.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[300]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Religiosi cujusdam vilem passionem]&lt;/s2&gt; Virum quemdam professionem Religiosum illa non minus sordida, quam fatua ac monstruosa fumantis tabaci&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; ingluvies usque ad præsentissimam corporis animæque perniciem deduxerat, ita ut de utriusque remedio conclamatum fere putaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060744Z">
        <seg>[300][The vile passion of a certain religious man] A certain man whose religious profession had been led by that no less sordid than foolish and monstrous gulf of smoking tobacco to the very brink of the destruction of both body and soul, so that a remedy for both was almost universally cried out for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[301]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ac inveteratam consuetudinem,]&lt;/s2&gt; Facile annuit Virgo, miserta periclitantis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060756Z">
        <seg>[301] [and the long-established custom,] The Virgin readily agreed, taking pity on the one in peril;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[302]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[morbosque inde ortos oratione sua tollit,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosa in unico sanavit hanc insaniam, curatura plurimos, ubi rite fuerit implorata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060806Z">
        <seg>[302] [and with her speech she removes the diseases that sprang from it,] The rose in a single instance cured this madness, and will cure many more, if it is properly invoked.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[303]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Unum addidero &amp; finiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060818Z">
        <seg>I will add one more thing and I will finish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[304]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Intime hæc sauciabant cor Virginis, prompto compassionis affectui tam flexile ac morigerum: &lt;s2&gt;[qui ob imminens Dei judicium nimis anxiabatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; unde acturum injunxit Petro, infirmum adiret, suoque ex nomine juberet fidentius sperare de immensa optimi &amp; benignissimi Numinis misericordia: se quidem precaturam pro felici ipsius transitu, at simul ex nunc offerre se de suorum piorum exercitiorum qualicumque censu, quidquid moribundo ad supplendam meritorum vacuitatem opus sit, imo totum is sibi vendicet, &amp;, si quod bonum opus a Rosa umquam per totam illius vitam hactenus prodierat, integre a se in ægrum dono liberrimo translatum velle, hoc ipse, velut suum, Judici exhibeat, desinatque interim abjecte &amp; humiliter sentire de magnifica liberalitate Altissimi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060837Z">
        <seg>[304] These things wounded the heart of the Virgin, so prompt to compassion, so pliable and obedient: [who was overly anxious because of the imminent judgment of God,]&lt;/s2&gt; from which she instructed Peter to visit the sick man and, in her name, to bid him hope more confidently in the immense mercy of the most good and benevolent Deity: that she herself would pray for his happy passing, but at the same time offered herself from that moment, whatever merit she might have acquired through her pious exercises, to supply whatever the dying man needed to make up for any deficiency of merits, indeed, to claim the whole for herself, and, if any good work had ever come forth from Rosa in her entire life up to now, to have it transferred in its entirety from herself as a most free gift to the sick man, so that he himself, as if it were his own, might present it to the Judge, and in the meantime cease to feel contemptuously and humbly about the magnificent liberality of the Most High.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[305]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Fideliter universa ad infirmum retulit Petrus, &lt;s2&gt;[de salute æterna securum reddi jubet.]&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; confestim immodicus ille pavor gaudio lætioris spei locum cessit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060843Z">
        <seg>Peter faithfully reported everything to the sick man, [he commands him to be made secure of eternal salvation.] And immediately that excessive fear gave way to a more joyful hope.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[307]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Alia vice Gaspar Flores Virginis pater sat grande volumen candidioris telæ (donatum, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; telam sibi datam inter inopes distribuit,]&lt;/s2&gt; an emptum nescio) domum attulit, ut inde conjux Maria de Oliva sibi, gregique tot filiorum de omnibus, quæ ad munditiem spectant, copiosius provideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061029Z">
        <seg>[307]Another time Gaspar Flores, the Virgin's father, brought home a large bolt of whiter cloth (whether donated, [and he distributed the cloth given to him among the poor,] or purchased, I do not know), so that his wife, Maria de Oliva, could more generously provide for herself and her large family of sons in all things pertaining to cleanliness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[308]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Mater, ut vidit filiam nil minus, quam de conficiendis sibi linteis cogitare, &lt;s2&gt;[matrique id ægre ferenti]&lt;/s2&gt; suspicata quod erat, causam quæsivit, cur traditam sibi telam candidam, subtilem, bonam tardaret suis aptare usibus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061045Z">
        <seg>[308] The mother, seeing that her daughter was thinking of nothing less than making herself linens, and with the mother displeased at this, suspecting what was happening, asked why she was delaying to adapt the white, fine, good cloth she had been given to her own uses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[309]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Possidebat Rosæ mater in suo mundo muliebri duas pallas, &lt;s2&gt;[(hujus etiam pallam pauperi virgini dederat)]&lt;/s2&gt; seu oblonga &amp; a vertice in terram usque demissa pallia, femineum ac decens tegumentum, quotiescumque proceditur in publicum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061106Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[309] Rosa's mother possessed in her own feminine world two pallia, [she had also given one of these pallia to a poor virgin], that is, long, head-to-toe cloaks, a feminine and becoming covering whenever one goes out in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[30]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; afflictos consolabatur.]&lt;/s2&gt; Factum est, ac abundantius quidem, quam moribundæ Filiæ solicita pietas flagitarat: simul enim ut Virgo spiritum reddidit, matrem superne tanta solatiorum diluvia inundarunt, ut sub juramento fassa sit, diu cor suum capiendæ huic dulcedini angustum laborasse sub quadam tempestate gaudiorum, ac velut pectoris intersepta perrupturum, anhelis subsultasse tripudiis, ita quod in eodem Rosæ morientis conclavi quærendus sibi fuerit secretior angulus, in quo liberius sub tantæ lætitiæ suavissima divulsione palpitans respiraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035830Z">
        <seg>[30] [&amp; consolated the afflicted.]It happened, and more abundantly indeed, than the solicitous piety of the dying Daughter had demanded: for as soon as the Virgin yielded up her spirit, such a flood of consolations from on high inundated the mother, that she confessed under oath, her heart had long laboured to contain this sweetness, under a certain tempest of joys, and as if her chest were about to burst, she leapt about with gasping, triumphant leaps, so that in the very same chamber of the dying Rose she had to seek a more secluded corner, in which, under the most exquisite tearing-asunder of such great joy, she could breathe more freely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[310]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[remunerationem cum fœnore promittit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Evenit, quod Virgo prædixerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061128Z">
        <seg>[310] [He promises repayment with interest.]] What the Virgin had foretold came to pass;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[311]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Egenam ægramque nobilis prosapiæ virginem]&lt;/s2&gt; Extra mœnia in extremis Limanæ urbis pomœriis habitabat Joanna de Bobadilla &amp; Azevedo, virgo illustris parentelæ, sed orphana, ac virtute, quam censu ditior: nam amissis parentibus, in arcto res erant, miseramque sat premebat occulta frugalis œconomiæ angustia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061147Z">
        <seg>[311] [A poor and sickly virgin of noble lineage]Outside the walls, in the outermost suburbs of the city of Lima, lived Joanna de Bobadilla y Azevedo, a virgin of illustrious parentage but an orphan, and richer in virtue than in wealth: for having lost her parents, her circumstances were straitened, and she was sorely pressed by the hidden hardship of a frugal household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[312]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[in domo materna curari jubet,]&lt;/s2&gt; Joannam secreto adiit, metum anxietatemque ponere jussit, vacare in maternis ædibus locandum pretio conclave idoneum, &amp; usui peropportunum, eo concederet, ac de menstruo locationis sumptu pacisceretur cum matre, sibi deinde curam procurandæ solutionis totam relinqueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061212Z">
        <seg>[312] [He orders her to be cared for in her mother's house,]He secretly approached Joanna, told her to put aside her fear and anxiety, to find a suitable and very convenient room to rent in her mother's house for a reasonable price, and to agree with her mother on the monthly rent, leaving him to take care of the payment in full.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[313]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; quibuslibet infirmis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosæ, quoad matri convixit, nil jucundius accidere poterat, quam si permitteretur, cujuscumque sortis aut conditionis pauperes personas morbis afflictas, paternis inferre ædibus, ut illic suismet manibus assidue ministraret languentibus: nec solius tecti, cubilis, strati, sed &amp; medicamentorum, ciborumque solatio egentes affatim reficere conabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061231Z">
        <seg>[313][and to any and all infirm persons,] Nothing could have been more pleasing to Rosa, so long as she lived with her mother, than to be permitted to bring into her father's house any poor people afflicted by illness, of whatever sort or condition, so that she could there constantly minister to the sick with her own hands: and she tried to abundantly refresh those in need, not only with shelter, a bed, and bedding, but also with the comfort of medicine and food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[314]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Mox illa primo obvium mendicabulum invitabat ad lares proprios, &lt;s2&gt;[etiamsi sordido morbo laborarent,]&lt;/s2&gt; huic abstergebat, ungebat, alligabat purulenta ulcera, hujus mundabat vestes, reficiebat laceros pluderones&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, repurgabat subnatas plicatim sordes, lavabat pedes &amp; impetiginosum verticem, nullique ministerio deerat, quo illius fami, siti, pressuræ levamen adferret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061243Z">
        <seg>[314] Soon she would invite the first beggar she met into her own home, [even if they were suffering from a filthy disease,] she would clean him, anoint him, and bind up his purulent sores, She cleaned his clothes, mended his tattered garments, scraped away the caked-on filth, washed his feet and his impetigined head, and was lacking in no service that might bring relief to his hunger, thirst, and oppression.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[315]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Accidit, die quadam ab infirmæ mulieris cura Virginem tantisper ad maternas ædes divertere: &lt;s2&gt;[officiosissime inservit,]&lt;/s2&gt; sensit curiosa mater in veste filiæ insolitam mucidi putoris graveolentiam: inde conjecto in illius togam attentiori oculo, notavit, hinc inde respersim aliquid purulentarum sordium fortuito adhæsisse pannis ex aliena sanie, quam Virgo in detersione ulcerosæ mulieris inscia contraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061305Z">
        <seg>[315] It happened that, one day, while attending to an infirm woman, the Virgin was for a time diverted to her mother's house: [serving most dutifully,] the curious mother detected in her daughter's garment an unusual, musty odor. Casting a closer eye upon the tunic, she noticed that bits of purulent filth from a foreign abscess had accidentally adhered to the cloth, which the Virgin had unwittingly contracted while washing the ulcerous woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[316]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; semel de nausea sua]&lt;/s2&gt; Aliud in hoc genere vix imitabile præstitit Rosa, quod silendum non est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061327Z">
        <seg>[316] [and once about her nausea] Rosa performed another feat of this kind, scarcely imitable, which should not be kept silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[317]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Facile consensit homo, tali se putore exonerari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061355Z">
        <seg>[317] The man readily agreed, thinking that such a stench would rid him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[318]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Ægrotantem hospitis sui amicum invisens,]&lt;/s2&gt; Hinc sane credibiliora fiunt, quæ sequuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061429Z">
        <seg>[318] Visiting a friend of his host who was ill, what follows is indeed more credible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[319]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Itaque his tuta præsidiis, cum honestissima matrona &amp; pedissequis in viam se conjicit, &lt;s2&gt;[eum statim morbo liberat;]&lt;/s2&gt; prius tamen ad ædem sancti Dominici Missæ sacrificio interesse voluit, ac interim præmisit, qui nuntiaret ægro, se, uti petierat, a templo mox ei cum matrona adfuturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061454Z">
        <seg>[319] And so, with these safe precautions, she sets out on the road with the most honorable matron and her attendants, [she immediately cures him of his illness;]&lt;/s2&gt; but first she wished to attend the Mass at the church of Saint Dominic, and in the meantime she sent a messenger to tell the sick man that, as he had requested, she would come to him from the temple shortly with the matron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[320]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Plura hujuscemodi curationum miracula in alium tantisper locum tempusque differo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061516Z">
        <seg>[320] I defer the many miracles of this kind of treatment to another time and place for the time being.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[321]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut lepido exemplo demonstratur.]&lt;/s2&gt; Hic adstans juvencula Rosa miserta est bestiæ, &amp; innocenti simplicitate conversa pueriliter ad avem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061538Z">
        <seg>[321] [as is shown by a charming example.] The young woman Rosa, standing here, pitied the beast, and in her innocent simplicity turned childishly to the bird;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[323]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quærebat Sponsi pietas occasionem, qua charam Virginem privilegio singulari securam redderet æternæ felicitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061758Z">
        <seg>[323] The Spouse's piety sought an opportunity by which to make the beloved Virgin secure of eternal happiness through a unique privilege.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[324]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Doctor Joannes Castillus aliquando inter alia sciscitabatur ex Virgine, an prædestinationis suæ certam notitiam cælitus revelatam accepisset?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061820Z">
        <seg>Doctor John of Castile once asked the Virgin, among other things, whether she had received a certain knowledge of her predestination revealed from heaven?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[325]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ac in articulo mortis ostendit,]&lt;/s2&gt; Manifestius hæc patuere in ultimo Rosæ moribundæ decubitu: siquidem felicissimi sui transitus ad beatam requiem adeo certa erat, ut etiam præscierit, ab omni purgatorii mora immunem se fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061835Z">
        <seg>[325] [and at the point of death she showed,] These things were more manifestly evident in the dying Rosæ's final illness: for the most blessed passage of her soul to its beatific rest was so certain that she even foresaw she would be free from all purgatorial delay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[326]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[aliamque mysticam visionem]&lt;/s2&gt; Memorabilius est, quod Rosam, dum hortensem suam cellulam incolebat, salutis æternæ securam reddidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061851Z">
        <seg>[326] [and another mystical vision] It is more memorable that she made Rosa secure in eternal salvation while she was dwelling in her little garden cell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[327]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[certior reddita est de sua prædestinatione]&lt;/s2&gt; Porro præ magnitudine gaudii, quod se videbat rosam acceptabilem tuto vernare in pugillo Parvuli, nesciebat, nec anxie quærebat, quid de reliquis ipsa rosis ageret, nisi quod brevi consilio subitaneum inde sertum confecit, ac cælesti Infanti reverenter imposuit, qui Virgini suavissime arridens, data benedictione, disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061928Z">
        <seg>[327] [made aware of her predestination]Furthermore, from the greatness of her joy, seeing that she was a welcome rose safely flourishing in the Little One's palm, she neither knew nor anxiously cared what to do with the other roses, except that with a brief plan she quickly made a garland from them and reverently placed it upon the heavenly Infant, who, smiling most sweetly at the Virgin, vanished after giving his blessing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[328]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Pari imperturbabilis certitudinis fruebatur solatio felix Rosa in securitate gratiæ, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; confimato gratiæ statu,]&lt;/s2&gt; divinæque amicitiæ: nam præterquam quod post quindecennales illas horrendarum obtenebrationum eclipses (de quibus supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061947Z">
        <seg>[328] Rose, the happy solace, enjoyed a like unshaken certainty in the security of grace, [and with her state of grace confirmed,] and of divine friendship: for besides that after those fifteen years of the dreadful eclipses of obscuration (of which above chap.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[329]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Mirabatur cordata Rosa inusitatum rigidi censoris amphibolon&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;b&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;s4&gt;[licet confessarius eam terrere conaretur.]&lt;/s4&gt; permisitque illum ad satietatem loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062005Z">
        <seg>[329] The level-headed Rosa was amazed at the rigid censor's unusual amphiboly, [though the confessor tried to frighten her.]] and she allowed him to speak to his heart's content.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[32]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ex adverso credebat innocens Rosa, tempestivo (de quo supra) capillorum excidio, &lt;s2&gt;[cui mater illam destinabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; ut quondam S. Catharina de Senis, amputasse spem matris, ac nuptiarum tendiculas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035936Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, the innocent Rosa believed that by the timely (as mentioned above) cutting of her hair, [which her mother had destined for her,] she had, like St. Catherine of Siena of old, cut off her mother's hope and the snares of marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[330]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Eadem vis confidentiæ in Deum a prima ætate Rosam adversus qualibet mortalis vitæ difficultates, &lt;s2&gt;[Tanta erat ejus in Deo fiducia,]&lt;/s2&gt; pavores, incommoda prævalide obarmarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062033Z">
        <seg>[330] The same confidence in God from her earliest years had armed Rosa against any and all difficulties, fears, and hardships of mortal life. [Such was her trust in God.]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[331]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Rosa, ut vidit eminus, utrumque parentem ad se venire, &lt;s2&gt;[quam ex similitudine quadam confirmaverat]&lt;/s2&gt; processit obvia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062049Z">
        <seg>Rosa, seeing from a distance both her parents coming toward her, went out to meet them; [which she had confirmed by a certain resemblance]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[332]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut bis in manifesto vitæ periculo]&lt;/s2&gt; Nec defuit occasio, qua manifestaretur illa heroïca virginei pectoris soliditas inter discrimina ardua ac repentina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062114Z">
        <seg>[332] [as on two occasions in manifest peril of life] Nor was there lacking an opportunity to demonstrate that heroic fortitude of the virgin's breast amid arduous and sudden dangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[333]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[nequaquam trepidaret,]&lt;/s2&gt; Alia vice Limæ cum matre ac honestissimis matronis in quadriga revertebatur a festiva celebris ecclesiæ statione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062136Z">
        <seg>[333] [She would by no means be flustered.] On another occasion she was returning in a chariot from the festive station of a celebrated church in Lima, accompanied by her mother and the most honorable matrons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[334]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Cui tanta inerat divinæ protectionis fiducia in vitæ periculis, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; in domestica penuria panem,]&lt;/s2&gt; utique in necessariis vitæ sperandis subsidiis minor esse non potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062201Z">
        <seg>[334] He had such confidence in divine protection amid life's dangers, [and in having bread amid domestic poverty,] that it could not be less in the necessary means of life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[335]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Alio tempore in eadem domo (pernecessarium illis regionibus condimentum) mel defuit, &lt;s2&gt;[magnam mellis copiam,]&lt;/s2&gt; ejusque cupa fundo tenus exhausta arescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062219Z">
        <seg>[335] At another time in the same house, honey ran out (an essential condiment in those regions), [a large supply of honey,] and its barrel lay empty and drying out at the bottom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[336]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Gasparem Flores patrem Virginis premebat molesta ægritudo corporis, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; necessariam pecuniam mirabiliter obtineret,]&lt;/s2&gt; simul marcebat afflictus mœrore animus ob quinquaginta librarum debitum, cui præ inopia solvendo non erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062241Z">
        <seg>[336] A troublesome bodily illness was pressing upon Gaspar Flores, the father of the Virgin, [and he would miraculously obtain the necessary money,] at the same time his spirit was wasting away with sorrow over a debt of fifty pounds, for which he had no means to pay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[337]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ejuscemodi peregrinis ac inexspectatis subsidiis pluries egena domus recreata fuit, &lt;s2&gt;[ac inops Virgo speraret,]&lt;/s2&gt; ne sanctæ Filiæ constans ac robusta in Deum confidentia fraudaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062306Z">
        <seg>[337] The house was repeatedly refreshed by such foreign and unexpected aid, [and the poor Virgin would hope,] lest the holy Daughter's steadfast and robust confidence in God be defrauded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[338]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[a se novum monasterium fundari posse.]&lt;/s2&gt; Domi Rosa cum devotis virginibus mira ac seria fiducia sermocinabatur de fundatione prætacti monasterii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062322Z">
        <seg>[338] [that a new monastery could be founded.] At home, Rosa spoke with the devout virgins in a marvelous and earnest confidence about the foundation of the aforementioned monastery;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[33]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; In his matrona quædam, genere &amp; opibus clara, &lt;s2&gt;[acriter objurgatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; uni filio suo Rosam conjugem, sibi nurum, vehementer optabat, utique felici commercio plus bonorum adeptura, quam datura cum hærede filio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035954Z">
        <seg>[33] In these circumstances a certain matron, distinguished by her birth and wealth, was earnestly urging her one and only son to take Rosam as his wife, for she believed that by this happy alliance she would acquire more goods than she would give up with her son the heir.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[340]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nec ab extimo decore quidquam dissidet anterior exactæ observantiæ pulchritudo, &lt;s2&gt;[quod post obitum S. Rosæ construi cœpit,]&lt;/s2&gt; orationum, mortificationum, exercitiorum assiduitas, perfectionis studium, uniformis morum puritas &amp; sanctimonia, disciplinæ rigidus tenor, perque omnes angulos viva &amp; prorsus angelica virtutum omnium exemplaria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062427Z">
        <seg>[340] Nor does anything in the external adornment conflict with the inner beauty of exact observance, [which was begun to be built after the death of St. Rose,]&lt;/s2&gt; the assiduity of prayers, mortifications, and exercises, the study of perfection, a uniform purity and sanctity of morals, a strict observance of discipline, and throughout every corner, living and truly angelic examples of all virtues.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[341]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Denique illud est, quod integro fere ante fundationem decennio toties Rosæ, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; cujus ædificationem ipsa Sancta,]&lt;/s2&gt; nunc per symbola &amp; figuras, nunc manifeste in propria forma ac typo fuit divinitus præmonstratum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062436Z">
        <seg>[341] Finally, this is what was divinely foreshown to Rose almost a full decade before the foundation, [&amp; whose building the Holy Virgin herself,] now by symbols and figures, now manifestly in its own form and type.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[342]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[per crebras visiones]&lt;/s2&gt; Dudum expertus propheta monuit, intelligentia opus esse in visionibus, Daniel.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062504Z">
        <seg>[342] [through frequent visions] The prophet who had long since experienced them warned that insight is needed in visions, Daniel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[343]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; varias revelationes]&lt;/s2&gt; Atticuit Virgo, sed loqui cœpere prodigia: nam ecce, quas frater sursum projecerat, levissimo delapsu mox revolvebantur in terram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062529Z">
        <seg>[343] The Virgin caused various revelations, but prodigies began to speak: for behold, those which her brother had thrown up were soon rolled back to the ground by the slightest descent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[344]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[cælitus edocta]&lt;/s2&gt; Alia vice oranti Rosæ divinitus ostensum fuit miræ amœnitatis pratum, undique liliorum candore ac rosarum purpura (tametsi sparsim &amp; sine ordine) variegatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062545Z">
        <seg>[344] [Heaven-taught] On another occasion, while praying, Rose was divinely shown a meadow of marvelous beauty, variegated on all sides with the whiteness of lilies and the purple of roses (though scattered and without order).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[345]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062613Z">
        <seg>[345] P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[346]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliquando coram domesticis inter familiaria colloquia, &lt;s2&gt;[aliisque multis prædixit,]&lt;/s2&gt; dum B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062700Z">
        <seg>[346] At one time, in the presence of his household during familiar conversations, [and to many others he had foretold,] while B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[347]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; matri suæ incredulæ prænuniavit;]&lt;/s2&gt; Indignabatur præ ceteris Rosæ mater, nec æquo ferebat animo tam crebram, &amp; seriam domi forisque mentionem fieri novelli cœnobii, quod Filia tam asserte &amp; imperterrite notis atque ignotis præfigurabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062743Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[347] [and had foretold it to his unbelieving mother;] Above all others, Rosa's mother was indignant, and she could not bear with equanimity the frequent and serious mention, both at home and abroad, of the new convent that her daughter was so confidently and fearlessly presenting to friends and strangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[348]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[eam ibi monialibus adscribendam esse;]&lt;/s2&gt; At Rosa tot contradictionibus alacrior, voce humili, sed læta replicuit: “Eia, mater dulcissima, hic demum cessent verborum pugnæ, tu magno tuo emolumento experieris, quam durum sit contra stimulum calcitrare: in sanctæ Catharinæ Limensi monasterio, quod prædico, tu ipsa inter primas candidum sanctimonialium indues habitum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062824Z">
        <seg>[348] [that she should be assigned to the nuns there;] But Rosa, all the more eager for so many contradictions, replied in a humble but joyful voice: "Come, most sweet mother, let the battles of words cease here at last; you, for your great benefit, will have experienced how hard it is to kick against the pricks: in the monastery of Saint Catherine of Lima, which I foretell, you yourself, among the first, will don the pure habit of the nuns;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[349]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[sicut anno Christi 1629 contigit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Initium anni MDCXXIX non vidit Calendas Græcas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, vidit tamen Mariam de Oliva sanctimonialem in novello sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterio, ubi post mariti obitum sexagenaria religiosis S. Patris Dominici filiabus devotissime aggregata, cum sacra Ordinis veste nomen Sororis Mariæ de S. Maria assumpsit, exactoque probationis anno, solemnia vota emisit, ac plena dierum, sancto fine quievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062842Z">
        <seg>[349] [as happened in the year of Christ 1629.] The beginning of the year 1629 did not see the Greek Kalends, However, she did see the nun Mary of the Olive in the new monastery of St. Catherine of Siena, where, after her husband's death, the sixty-year-old, having most devoutly joined the religious daughters of St. Dominic, took the name of Sister Mary of St. Mary with the sacred habit of the Order. After completing her year of probation, she made her solemn vows and, having lived a full life, rested in holy peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[34]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[eique in novo S. Claræ cœnobio]&lt;/s2&gt; Videbantur in hoc unum conspirasse omnia, ne Rosa Tertiariarum assequeretur habitum, quem tot votis ambiebat, tot suspiriis, tanto lachrymarum sumptu licitabatur: nam ecce alia oblata est religiosi instituti via, quæ quidem Rosæ displicere neutiquam potuisset, nisi unice ad S. Catharinæ Senensis alta vestigia fuisset cælitus destinata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040013Z">
        <seg>[34] [and in the new convent of St. Clare] It seemed that everything conspired to prevent Rose from obtaining the habit of the Tertiaries, which she so earnestly desired, and for which she was entreating with so many vows, so many sighs, and at the cost of so many tears: for behold, another path to the religious life was offered, which indeed could in no way have displeased Rosa, had it not been divinely destined solely for the high footsteps of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[350]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Superest de ipsamet cœnobii fundatrice, ac prima ejusdem præfecta memorabile ac luculentum Rosæ vaticinium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062855Z">
        <seg>[350] There remains the memorable and lucid prophecy of Rose concerning the foundress of the very convent herself, and its first prefect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[351]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Rosa jam intus moneri se sentiens, affectu reciproco matronam vinxit, &lt;s2&gt;[occasione quadam S. Rosam invisisset,]&lt;/s2&gt; offerendo modicum id, quod posset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062914Z">
        <seg>[351] Rosa, now sensing she was being warned from within, bound the matron with a reciprocal affection, [on some occasion having visited St. Rose,] offering what little she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[352]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Necdum sat capiebat ipsamet, unde, aut cur ita ageret, &lt;s2&gt;[sensit, se excitari ad fundandum monasterium,]&lt;/s2&gt; donec præter morem oculis a terra in vultum Luciæ attentissime conjectis, cœpit exultare spiritu, suffundi colore inusitatæ lætitiæ, palpitare calidis præcordiis, ac demum hilaritate modestissima Luciam deosculans, “Euge, (inquit) mater, ingenti suo operi te Deus reservavit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062932Z">
        <seg>[352] She herself did not yet fully understand where or why she was acting as she did, [she sensed that she was being stirred to found a monastery,]&lt;/s2&gt; until, raising her eyes from the ground to Lucia's face more attentively than was her custom, she began to exult in spirit, to be suffused with a color from unusual joy, and her warm heart to palpitate. Finally, with the most modest cheerfulness, she kissed Lucia and said, "Well done, mother; God has reserved you for his great work."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[35]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[locus offertur;]&lt;/s2&gt; His, inquam, auspicibus formabatur recens monasterium, &amp; ultro invitabatur velut ad dignissimum tali flore plantarium Rosa, jam inter primas ejusdem fundatrices nominatim electa, censa, approbata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040028Z">
        <seg>[35] [place offered;] With these auspices, I say, the new monastery was being founded, and Rosa—already chosen by name, consecrated, and approved as one of its foremost foundresses—was invited of her own accord as if to the most worthy planting ground for such a flower.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[363]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Magister F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T063407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T063407Z">
        <seg>[363] Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[36]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[sed ex voluntate divina,]&lt;/s2&gt; Hoc ut magis innotesceret, binis e cælo prodigiis opus fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040053Z">
        <seg>[36] [but by divine will,] For this to become more widely known, two prodigies from heaven were required;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[37]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Ceterum vix ad orationem humi genua fixerat, &lt;s2&gt;[quæ declarata fuisse dicitur]&lt;/s2&gt; cum sensit pavimento sese immobiliter acclavatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040112Z">
        <seg>[37] However, she had scarcely knelt on the ground for the prayer, [which is said to have been declared], when she felt herself nailed immovably to the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[38]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Alterum prodigium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040146Z">
        <seg>[38] Another prodigy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[39]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nec fefellit eventus jam securam: nam paulo post, &lt;s2&gt;[statuit imitari S. Catharinam Senensem,]&lt;/s2&gt; obstaculis undique submotis, facta est voti compos, inque Rosariano Deiparæ sacello, ubi quondam immobilis hæserat, solemni ceremonia, de manibus P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040202Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[39] Nor did the outcome disappoint the now secure one: for a little later, [she resolved to imitate St. Catherine of Siena,] with all obstacles removed from every side, she fulfilled her vow, and in the Rosary chapel of the Mother of God, where she had once been motionless, by a solemn ceremony, from the hands of the Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[3]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quod mater ejus ob prodigium postea mutavit,]&lt;/s2&gt; Porro ut Rosæ nomen, divinitus soboli destinatum, post trium mensium ambages demum erumperet, prodigium exspectabatur, quod tale fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T200503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034646Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[3]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[which his mother later changed because of a prodigy,]&lt;/s2&gt; Furthermore, so that the name Rose, divinely destined for the child, might at last emerge after three months of twists and turns, a prodigy was awaited, which was as follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[40]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nimirum cum infante creverat hoc desiderium: siquidem a quinto suæ ætatis anno S. Catharinæ Senensi jam sese addixerat, &lt;s2&gt;[cujus exemplo,]&lt;/s2&gt; quod ex ore ipsiusmet Rosæ affirmant se percepisse testes secundus &amp; vigesimus secundus in citato sæpius Processu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040233Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[40] Indeed, this desire had grown with her from infancy: for from the age of five she had already consecrated herself to St. Catherine of Siena, [whose example,] as the second and twenty-second witnesses in the aforementioned Process repeatedly affirm, they learned from Rose's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[41]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[post superatas varias difficultates,]&lt;/s2&gt; Quæstor Gundisalvus, vir optimus, quique apud Virginem sibi concreditam valebat autoritate, imo &amp; domestico imperio, seu sponte, seu aliunde inductus, suggerebat reformatam sacri Carmeli regulam, quam Rosæ genio credebat aptius convenire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040249Z">
        <seg>[After overcoming various difficulties,] Quaestor Gundisalvus, a most excellent man, who had taken refuge with the Virgin,[41] [After overcoming various difficulties,] Questor Gundisalvus, a most excellent man, who, with the Virgin entrusted to his care, had authority—nay, even domestic sway—whether of his own accord or induced by others, proposed a reformed rule for the sacred Carmel, which he believed more aptly suited to the genius of the Rose;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[42]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; maturam deliberationem,]&lt;/s2&gt; Voto respondit eventus: nam operante Numine, supradictorum theologorum quaternio paribus utrimque votis pependit semper immobilis, stupuitque, neutri partium occurrere pondus, quo sese a tam constanti æquilibrio tandem explicarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040310Z">
        <seg>[42] [And mature deliberation,] The outcome answered the vow: for, with the Deity at work, the quartet of the aforementioned theologians hung in equal balance on both sides, ever motionless, and marveled that neither side could find the weight by which they might at last break away from such a steadfast equilibrium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[43]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Eliso igitur hoc ariete, superfuit alius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040332Z">
        <seg>[43] Therefore, having rammed this one, another remained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[44]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Evenit, uti speraverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040348Z">
        <seg>It happened, as he had hoped;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[46]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[morbos peccatis suis imputabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; Assueverat morborum suorum (quos in ægro exhaustoque corpusculo sustinuit crebros &amp; sævissimos) celare magnitudinem, ne quis subveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040524Z">
        <seg>[46] He attributed his illnesses to his own sins. He had grown accustomed to hiding the severity of his ailments (which he endured in his sick, emaciated little body, frequent and most severe) so that no one would come to his aid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[47]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Verum si eo procederent, ut refellendis hujuscemodi vituperiis encomia Rosæ laudesque opponerent, &lt;s2&gt;[a laudibus suis abhorrebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; expavescebat misera, ac velut fulminis ictu perculsa tremebat, pallore, silentio, gemitu mœstissime protestans se torqueri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040556Z">
        <seg>[47] But if they went so far as to oppose the praises and lauds of Rosa with refutations of such criticisms,[she abhorred her own praises,]&lt;/s2&gt; the poor wretch was terrified, and as if struck by lightning she trembled, protesting most mournfully with pallor, silence, and groans that she was being tormented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[48]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quoddam perarduum heroicæ virtutis opus ad S. Catharinæ Senensis imitationem Rosa tentaverat, &lt;s2&gt;[innocens reprehensa veniam deprecabatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; perfecerat: quod cum ipsius valetudini summe noxium &amp; exitiale crederet domina Isabella Mexia, horrore pio stimulata, P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040624Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[48] A certain exceedingly arduous work of heroic virtue, undertaken by Rosa in imitation of St. Catherine of Siena, [innocent, she reproved herself and begged for pardon,] she had accomplished: which, believing it to be exceedingly harmful and fatal to her health, Lady Isabella Mexia, moved by pious horror, Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[49]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Quandocumque ad confessariorum pedes in suimet accusationem procumbebat, &lt;s2&gt;[coram confessariis minimos defectus suos exaggerabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; ita mox obruebatur lachrymis, interpolabatur singultibus, dehiscebat profundis ex imo corde suspiriis, ut credidisses non nisi enormium flagitiorum ream esse, quæ sic plangebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040639Z">
        <seg>Whenever he fell at the feet of the confessors to accuse himself, [he exaggerated his slightest failings before them,]&lt;/s2&gt; she was so soon overwhelmed with tears, broken by sobs, and rent by deep sighs from the bottom of her heart, that you would have believed she was guilty of nothing less than enormous crimes, for which she wept so bitterly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[4]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ita ut primum appellaretur Rosa,]&lt;/s2&gt; Verumtamen lapsis aliquot annis, de Rosæ nomine aliam indixit litem ipsamet Virginis pavida conscientia, nimirum ut ita præberetur occasio locusque cælesti oraculo, quo Rosæ nomen ultimo erat confirmandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T201228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034717Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[4]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[so that she would first be called Rosa,]&lt;/s2&gt; However, after several years had passed, the Virgin herself, with a fearful conscience, brought another suit concerning the name Rosa, namely to provide an occasion and a place for a heavenly oracle by which the name Rosa was to be finally confirmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[50]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; se mancipiis inferiorem reputabat.]&lt;/s2&gt; Testabatur Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040658Z">
        <seg>[50] [He considered himself inferior even to his slaves.] The wife of the quaestor Gundisalvi, D., testified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[51]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Præter dotes corporis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Diu nescivit Rosa, quidquam vel minimum in se reliquum esse, quod laudi pateret, aut pulchrum æstimari posset, donec femina quædam obiter manus Virginis conspicata (ut est muliebre ingenium ad similia lynceum, &amp; insigniter oculatum) commendavit in iis candorem, decentiam, proportionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040723Z">
        <seg>[51][Besides her bodily charms,] For a long time Rosa was unaware that she had anything left in herself worthy of praise or that could be considered beautiful, until a certain woman, having casually caught sight of the Virgin's hands (for women's nature is keen and remarkably observant of such things), commended their whiteness, grace, and proportion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[52]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[austeritatem jejuniorum,]&lt;/s2&gt; Pridem studuerat humilis Virgo omni arte nativam speciosissimi vultus sui gratiam abolere, ne posset mortalibus oculis placere, idque non solum jugi inedia, dirisque macerationibus, sed &amp; aqua gelida creberrime toti corpori superfusa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040732Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[52] [the austerity of fasts,] The humble Virgin had long sought by every means to erase the native beauty of her most lovely face, so that it might not please mortal eyes, and this not only by constant fasting and dire austerities, but also by frequently pouring cold water over her entire body;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[53]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Inde lepidum obvenit quibusdam Argis&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; per otium curiosis, &lt;s4&gt;[ita ut post diuturnum jejunium laute epulata crederetur,]&lt;/s4&gt; qui in feria sexta Parasceves, forte in templi vestibulo confabulantes, observaverant Rosam cum matre domum ab ecclesia redeuntem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040745Z">
        <seg>[53] Hence a charming incident befell certain Argives who, out of idle curiosity, were chatting in the temple vestibule and saw Rosa returning home from church with her mother.&lt;/s4&gt; who, while chatting in the temple vestibule on the Friday of Holy Week, had seen Rosa returning home from church with her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[54]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Par ei solicitudo &amp; cura fuit abscondendi reliquos suorum charismatum thesauros, &lt;s2&gt;[aliasque virtutes]&lt;/s2&gt; ne humanis laudibus paterent in prædam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040809Z">
        <seg>[54] He had the same concern and care for concealing the remaining treasures of his charisms, [and other virtues], so that they would not fall prey to human praise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[55]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Diu recusavit, diu cunctatus est confessarius, haud ignarus, &lt;s2&gt;[solicite abscondebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; quam sibi id arduum futurum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040822Z">
        <seg>[55] He refused for a long time; the confessor hesitated for a long time, not unaware that he was hiding it [carefully], how arduous it would be for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[56]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; Deiparam adjutricem habuit hujus humilitatis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Favit mirabiliter huic virtuti Deipara, humilium idea;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040853Z">
        <seg>[56] [&amp; He had the Virgin Mary as an assistant in this humility,] The Virgin Mary wonderfully favored this virtue, the model of the humble;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[57]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quam cum blanda affabilitate conjungebat.]&lt;/s2&gt; Tantam ejus humilitatem indivulse comitabatur suavissima mansuetudo, placida affabilitas, mite supercilium: mirati sunt quotquot eam noverant, numquam ab illius ore excidisse verbum duriusculum, obstreperum, criticum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040915Z">
        <seg>[57] [which he combined with gentle affability.][57] Such great humility was inseparably accompanied by the sweetest meekness, placid affability, and a gentle brow: all who knew her were amazed that not a single harsh, boisterous, or critical word ever escaped her lips;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[59]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nondum infantiæ pannos &amp; annos pupula excesserat, &lt;s2&gt;[a teneris annis ita assueverat abstinentiæ,]&lt;/s2&gt; cum sibi (quod illi ætatulæ peregrinum &amp; grave est) omni fructuum esu interdixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041008Z">
        <seg>[59] The pupil had not yet passed the swaddling clothes and years of infancy, [having been accustomed to abstinence from a tender age,] when she forbade herself, which is unusual and burdensome for that age, to eat any fruit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[5]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Habent quidem hoc (Ambrosio teste) Sanctorum merita, &lt;s2&gt;[cui deinde cognomen a sancta Maria additum est.]&lt;/s2&gt; ut a Deo nomen accipiant, sed qui ultra nomen etiam cognomen e cælo accipiant, raros puto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T201245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034749Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[5]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; The merits of the Saints do indeed have this (as Ambrose testifies), &lt;s2&gt;[to whom the surname from Saint Mary was then added.]&lt;/s2&gt; but I think those who receive a surname from heaven in addition to their name are rare.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[60]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Interdum nobiles matronæ, devotionis ergo, &lt;s2&gt;[ut carnes aut jusculum digerere non posset,]&lt;/s2&gt; matrem cum Filia in suas ædes invitabant, epuloque sobrie instructo, rogitabant utramque secum una accumbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041026Z">
        <seg>Sometimes noble matrons, out of devotion, would invite the mother and daughter into their homes, and after a sober meal was set out, they would ask both of them to recline with them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[61]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Semel ex morbo sat ancipiti emerserat, utque citius instauraretur, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; post ea ex mandato degustata]&lt;/s2&gt; ex præscripto medici, pauxillum carnis paventi ejus stomacho obtruserant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041045Z">
        <seg>[61] Having once emerged from a rather serious illness, and so that he might recover more quickly, [and after that, by order, having tasted] by the doctor's prescription, they had forced a little meat down his stomach, which was still tender.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[62]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[identidem in morbum recideret,]&lt;/s2&gt; Laborabat frequenti ischiade&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, ac plerumque ea dolorum vehementia, quæ stomachi fundum everteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041058Z">
        <seg>[62] [He would repeatedly relapse into illness,] He suffered from frequent sciatica, and usually from such violent pains that they would upset the very depths of his stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[63]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[sicut frequens experientia docuit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Ibidem alia vice observatis in Virgine manifestæ debilitatis nimiis indiciis, voluit idem paterfamilias, quod ex ala coctæ aviculæ quid modicum sumeret, quo labentem stomachum nonnihil erigeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041110Z">
        <seg>[63] As frequent experience has shown. There, on another occasion, observing in the virgin clear signs of manifest weakness, the same paterfamilias wished to take a little from the roasted bird's wing to somewhat lift her failing stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[64]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Unde arbitrio ejus relinqui debuit]&lt;/s2&gt; Impulerunt cruda hæc experimenta commiserantem quæstorem, ut Virginis confessarios, medicos, parentes, sigillatim ac pene adjurato obsecraverit, ne deinceps imperato carnium esu, Rosam in vitæ discrimen adductam vellent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041123Z">
        <seg>[64][Wherefore it ought to have been left to his discretion] These cruel experiments moved the compassionate inquisitor to beseech the Virgin's confessors, physicians, and parents, one by one and almost under oath, not to wish to bring Rose to the brink of death by ordering her to eat meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[65]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Tardius matri persuasum fuit, ut Filiæ tam severam tenuissimi victus rationem formamque æquanimiter indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041147Z">
        <seg>It was later persuaded to the mother that she should equably indulge her daughter in such a strict regimen and form of the most meager diet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[66]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Audierat in vicina sylva pronasci herbam insigniter præ ceteris amaram: &lt;s2&gt;[quem herbis amarissimis]&lt;/s2&gt; hanc sibi clam adferri, ex ea deinceps insipidum pulmentarium sibi jussit apparari, &amp; ne tam exquisitæ provisionis annona deficeret, herbam eamdem suo hortulo implantavit, suis manibus solicite coluit, persuadens matri, valere in usum medicinæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041215Z">
        <seg>[66] He had heard that in a nearby forest a notably bitter herb grew: [which, among the most bitter herbs] was secretly brought to him; from then on he had a tasteless porridge made for himself from it, and so that this most exquisite provision would not run out, he planted the same herb in his own little garden, carefully tended it with his own hands, persuading his mother that it was useful for medicinal purposes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[67]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Fruticat in illo occiduarum Indiarum fertili tractu planta mirabilis, &lt;s2&gt;[occulte condiebat.]&lt;/s2&gt; GRANADILLA&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; vernaculis dicitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041243Z">
        <seg>[67] In that fertile tract of the western Indies grows a marvelous plant, [secretly prepared.] It is called GRANADILLA by the natives;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[68]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Jejunia ejus erant adeo frequentia]&lt;/s2&gt; Transeundum nunc ad jejunia, tametsi dubitari haud immerito possit, ab ipsa esurie, an a talibus cibis durius esse tormentum potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041302Z">
        <seg>[68] His fasts were so frequent. We must now turn to the fasts, although one might not unreasonably doubt whether the hunger itself or such food could have been a more severe torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[69]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[diuturnaque,]&lt;/s2&gt; Residuo anni tempore panis mensuram tam sibi curtam indixerat, ut quod unicæ diei sat esse credebatur, integro vix octiduo totum absumeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041321Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041321Z">
        <seg>[69] For the rest of the year he had prescribed such a scant measure of bread that what was believed to be enough for a single day would scarcely last him eight days in all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[6]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis infantia ac pueritia tam dulcis omnibus ac blanda fuisse legitur, &lt;s2&gt;[Puella adhuc tenera præbet indicium]&lt;/s2&gt; ut non Catharinam, sed Euphrosynam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;k&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; plerique cœperint appellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034813Z">
        <seg>[6] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone, [A still-tender girl provides a sign] so that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosyne.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[70]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; cibus tam parcus,]&lt;/s2&gt; Nescio, jejunium an plane inediam appellavero, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041330Z">
        <seg>[70] [And the food so meager,] I don't know whether I should call what follows a fast or outright starvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[71]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliquando communicanti solæ species sacramentales tantum roboris contulerunt, &lt;s2&gt;[ut merito inedia vocari posset.]&lt;/s2&gt; ut in fortitudine cibi illius toto octiduo nullum aliud nutrimentum admiserit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041346Z">
        <seg>[71] At one time, the mere sacramental species, which she received, imparted such strength that, rightly, it could be called a fast, for in the fortitude of that food she accepted no other nourishment for the entire eight days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[73]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[pro publica regni Peruani]&lt;/s2&gt; Siqua aut regno Peruano, aut urbi Limensi, patriæ suæ, flagella divinitus imminebant, suis conabatur flagellis divertere: nec animarum in purgatoriis flammis bullientium immemor, duplicabat ictus catenarum in nova cruoris profluvia, quæ rogi partem extinguerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041519Z">
        <seg>[73][for the public good of the Kingdom of Peru] If any divine scourges threatened either the Kingdom of Peru or the city of Lima, his native land, he strove to divert them by his own: and not oblivious of the souls seething in the flames of purgatory, he doubled the strokes of the chains in new torrents of blood, which would extinguish part of the pyre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[74]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[vel urbis Limensis necessitate]&lt;/s2&gt; Assueverant domestici (quamquam inscia Virgine) ex ædium parte secretissima, per singulas noctes audire hos disciplinarum ictus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041529Z">
        <seg>[74] [or by the necessity of the city of Lima] The household servants (though the Virgin was unaware) had grown accustomed, from the most secret part of the house, to hearing these blows of discipline every night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[75]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Magister F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041557Z">
        <seg>[75] Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[76]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Porro, quam avide in pœnitentiæ exercitia propenderit Rosæ genius, &lt;s2&gt;[quibus ab ipsa pueritia assueverat,]&lt;/s2&gt; jam inde ferme ab infantia seriis præludiis abunde ostenderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041618Z">
        <seg>[76] Moreover, how eagerly Rosa's genius inclined toward the exercises of penance, [to which she had been accustomed from her very childhood,] she had already abundantly shown by serious preludes, almost from infancy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[77]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliis vicibus, sub eodem silentio, rogabat sibi gravari scapulam informi trabe, &lt;s2&gt;[tenerum corpus affligebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; collectisque exiguis viribus, conabatur obnixa sufferre durissimam sarcinam, dum preculas suas bene longas absolveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041634Z">
        <seg>At other times, under the same silence, she would ask for an inform bar to be placed on her shoulder, afflicting her tender body, and, gathering her small remaining strength, she would try to bear the most difficult burden with all her might, while she unwound her very long braids.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[78]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; ferrea catena se flagellare prohibita,]&lt;/s2&gt; Inhibitum ei fuerat (uti supra attigimus) ne amplius ferream catenam in usum flagelli adhiberet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041650Z">
        <seg>[78] [and forbidden to flog himself with an iron chain,] He had been forbidden (as we noted above) to use the iron chain as a whip any longer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[79]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ea corpusculum tam arcte cinxit, ut carnem penetraret, ]&lt;/s2&gt; Crescebat interim sub indissolubili vinculo atrox ischiadis cruciatus, qui demum invitæ Rosæ, post longos gemitus, etiam miserandos ejulatus extorsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041721Z">
        <seg>[79] [It had so tightly girdled the little body that it pierced the flesh,] Meanwhile, under the unbreakable bond, the dreadful torment of sciatica grew, which at last, after long groans, wrung even pitiful sobs from a reluctant Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[7]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Trimula erat, quando in tenerrima indole promicare cœperunt supra ætatem robusta virtutum specimina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034826Z">
        <seg>[7] She was three years old when, in her most tender disposition, she began to display above her age robust examples of virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[80]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; ideo illam confessarius etiam auferri jusserit.]&lt;/s2&gt; Vix laceratæ cutis vulnera coïerant, quando Rosa ad suam catenam rediit, pristinoque ferri tormento se rursus incinxit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041749Z">
        <seg>[80] [And so the confessor even ordered her to be taken away.] The wounds of her torn flesh had barely begun to heal when Rosa returned to her chain and again fastened herself to the iron torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[81]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Neque pulpæ brachiorum ab ejusmodi vinculis immunes erant: &lt;s2&gt;[Deinde brachia vinculis constringebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; his enim Rosa, ad cruciatum, arcta quædam ligamina circumdederat, quæ perpetim torquebant complicatos musculos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041811Z">
        <seg>[81] Nor were the paps of the arms immune from such bonds: [Then he would bind the arms with cords,] for Rosa, for the purpose of torture, had fastened certain tight ligatures around them, which perpetually twisted the coiled muscles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[82]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Verum ubi pro ingenti munere ditatam se vidit brevi cilicio, &lt;s2&gt;[hispidum cilicium gestabat,]&lt;/s2&gt; quasi purpuram, byssum, trabeamve nacta, sibi suæque tremulæ carni gratulabatur de novo pœnitentiæ baltheo, rigoris paludamento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041829Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[82] But when she saw herself enriched with a huge gift—a short tunic of coarse cloth, as though she had received purple, linen, or a robe—she congratulated herself and her trembling flesh on the new belt of penance, the cloak of austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[83]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Aliam tamen ipsa adinvenit castigandi corpusculi rationem, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; singula corporis sui membra]&lt;/s2&gt; quam priori clam substitueret, minori forsan valetudinis damno, at non leviori molestia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041854Z">
        <seg>[83] She herself, however, devised another method of punishing her little body, [and each of her body's members], which she would secretly substitute for the previous one, perhaps with less harm to her health, but not with less inconvenience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[84]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[peculiari tormento cruciabat.]&lt;/s2&gt; His blandimentis Rosa demulcebat tenue illud corpusculum, cui alias a crebris infirmitatibus sat pœnarum erat, ut infra suo loco videbitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041928Z">
        <seg>[84] [tortured with a peculiar torment.] With these caresses Rosa soothed that frail little body, which otherwise had already suffered enough from its frequent infirmities, as will be seen below in its proper place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[86]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Annos complusculos duravit, &amp; induruit pia Virgo sub hoc oneroso capitis spineto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042102Z">
        <seg>[86] It lasted for several years, and the pious Virgin endured it under this burdensome crown of thorns;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[87]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Novo igitur ardore, ac patiendi desiderio, lamellam argenteam sibi flexit in circulum, &lt;s2&gt;[cujus forma describitur,]&lt;/s2&gt; huic ab intus ex eodem metallo tres infixit aculeorum ordines, ita ut quælibet series triginta &amp; tres contineret aculeos, videlicet pro numero annorum Christi, quos mortalis pro mortalibus explevit in terra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042114Z">
        <seg>[87] With new zeal, then, and a desire to suffer, he bent a silver plate into a circle for himself, [whose form is described,]&lt;/s2&gt; to this, from the inside, he affixed three rows of spikes of the same metal, so that each row contained thirty-three spikes, namely for the number of years of Christ, which a mortal fulfilled on earth for mortals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[88]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; qua Virgo studiose utebatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Dentatum hoc sertum quam dire momorderit, quam cruciabiliter omni ex parte pupugerit velatum sacræ puellæ caput, cui torquendo unica sat fuisset acicula, ex eo fas est conjicere, quod spinæ non simul omnes, sed aliæ post alias, binæ, ternæ, pluresve, successione molestissima, nudatam invadebant cutem, proque occasione ac varietate motuum, singulæ obliquabantur in singulis vulnerum angustiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042130Z">
        <seg>[88] [&amp; which the Virgin diligently used,]&lt;/s2&gt; How terribly this serrated garland bit, how painfully it pricked the veiled head of the sacred maiden from every side—a head for which a single needle would have been enough to twist—can be inferred from the fact that its spikes did not all strike at once, but some after others, in pairs, threes, or even more, in the most vexatious succession, invaded the bare skin, and, according to the opportunity and variety of the movements, each one was twisted in the individual straits of the wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[89]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[ut se quotidie]&lt;/s2&gt; Verum hæc Rosæ ludicra videbantur, &amp; artis digna succursu, quo sævirent inclementius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042139Z">
        <seg>[89] [as if to mock him daily] But these things seemed like child's play to Rosa, and worthy of the aid of art, by which they might rage more fiercely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[8]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[dum horridum chirurgi ferrum]&lt;/s2&gt; Quadriennium vivendo expleverat cum summam capitis sui cutem species quædam impetiginis cœpit obcrustare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034900Z">
        <seg>[8] He had completed four years of life when a sort of impetigo began to crust over the skin on the top of his head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[90]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[magis magisque torqueret;]&lt;/s2&gt; Nec tamen contenta hoc quotidiano cruciatu, modum reperit, quo nova indies vulnera aperiret: nam coronam mane circumdatura capiti, numquam eodem figebat loco, quo pridie steterat, sed mutato paulisper situ, in aliam aliamque partem transponebat redimiculum, ut alterna variatione immitius pungeret, atque in intacto dolorem renovaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042204Z">
        <seg>[90] [tortured more and more;]Yet not content with this daily torment, she found a way to open fresh wounds each day: for when she would place the crown on her head in the morning, she never fastened it in the same spot where it had stood the day before, but by shifting its position slightly, she would move the headband to a different part, so that by alternating its placement it would pierce more deeply and renew the pain in the uninjured spot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[91]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Diu familiares, domesticos, ipsamque matrem latuit hoc tam cruenti pectinis vepretum: &lt;s2&gt;[donec hoc tormentum, quod domesticos diu latuit,]&lt;/s2&gt; quis enim sub vitta ac velo cautissimæ Virginis, aut notasset, aut quæsivisset tam ferale supplicium?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042214Z">
        <seg>[91] For a long time this most bloody bramble of the comb eluded the household servants, the domestic staff, and even the mother herself: [until this torment, which had long eluded the household servants,] for who, under the band and veil of the most cautious Virgin, would have either noticed or sought out such a fatal punishment?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[92]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Hic Rosa magis se proditam quam percussam dolens, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; tandem casu detectum est,]&lt;/s2&gt; dupliciter erubuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042232Z">
        <seg>[92] Here Rosa, feeling more betrayed than struck, blushed twice; [and at last it was discovered by chance.]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[93]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Nec mora: delata fuit rei series ad eum, qui certius coronæ usum Virgini prohiberet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042257Z">
        <seg>[93] Nor was there any delay: the sequence of events was brought to the one who would more surely forbid the Virgin's use of the crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[94]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[Hæc aculeata corona]&lt;/s2&gt; At Rosa pluris faciens coronam, quam regnum, gaudebat hanc se recuperasse salvo spinarum numero, ac saltem non nisi modice ea castigatione exarmatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042328Z">
        <seg>[94] [This thorny crown] But Rose, valuing the crown more than the kingdom, rejoiced to have recovered it with the number of its thorns intact, and at least only slightly weakened by that chastisement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[95]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[tangentem spirituali gaudio replevit]&lt;/s2&gt; Quidam religiosissimus Dei servus, paulo post elatæ Rosæ exequias, domum Gundisalvi quæstoris adierat, eo dumtaxat animo, ut instrumenta, quibus Rosa virgineam carnem vivens domuerat, devote contrectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042350Z">
        <seg>[95]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[filled him with spiritual joy]&lt;/s2&gt; A certain most devout servant of God, shortly after his elevation,[95] [filled him with spiritual joy] A certain most devout servant of God, shortly after the funeral of the exalted Rose, had gone to the house of Gundisalvus the treasurer, with the sole intention of devoutly handling the instruments by which Rose had subdued her virgin flesh while alive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[96]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[post obitum sanctæ Virginis,]&lt;/s2&gt; Non pigeat, hic denuo reflectere oculos in spiniferum S. Catharinæ Senensis prototypon, quod Rosa nostra spinis coronata sic imitari visa est, ut plene idipsum non tam expresserit, quam in se transtulerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042403Z">
        <seg>[96] [After the death of the holy Virgin,] It will not be amiss to turn our eyes once more here to the thorny prototypon of St. Catherine of Siena, which our Rose, crowned with thorns, was seen to imitate in such a way that she did not so much fully express it as transfer it into herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[97]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; Huc demum &amp; illud videtur pertinere, quod, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; ipsa propter illam in cælo coronari visa est.]&lt;/s2&gt; dum mortualis pompa sic Rosam coronabat in terris, ostento non absimili Beatorum curia eamdem coronavit in cælis, ut utrimque deprehendatur, successisse in ipsammet S. Catharinæ Senensis coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042428Z">
        <seg>[97] Finally, this too seems to pertain, that [and she herself was seen to be crowned in heaven for that reason.]&lt;/s2&gt; while the funeral pomp thus crowned Rose on earth, the court of the Blessed, by a vision not unlike, crowned her in heaven, so that it is evident on both sides that she succeeded to the crown of St. Catherine of Siena herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[99]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[quibus ipsa durissime cubaret,]&lt;/s2&gt; Læta successu Rosa, pergebat securior non tantum nudare sibi tabulas, quibus indormisceret, sed &amp; rudius saxum circumspicere, quod cervici supponeret: verum post longas ambages, tandem interrupit Filiæ solertiam expergefacta mater, ac velut deprehenso immani facinore puellam increpuit, adjecto callidæ obstinationis convicio: Si sic ei constitutum erat matrem fallere, iret porro, sibique deinceps suopte arbitrio lectum, quem vellet, contabularet, dum tamen capiti saltem humanius cervical concederet, ipsisque tabulis unicum simplicis straguli induceret operimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042521Z">
        <seg>[99] [by whom she herself would sleep most harshly,] Gladdened by her success, Rosa went on more securely, not only laying bare for herself the planks on which she would sleep, but also roughly looking about for a stone to place under her neck: But after long detours, the mother, awakened at last, finally interrupted her daughter's cunning and, as if she had discovered some monstrous crime, rebuked the girl, adding a taunt about her clever stubbornness: If it was her intention to deceive her mother in this way, she might go on and from then on board up whatever bed she pleased of her own accord, so long as she at least provided her neck with a more humane pillow and covered the boards themselves with a single simple blanket.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;[9]&lt;/s1&gt;&lt;/b0&gt; &lt;s2&gt;[aliosque cruciatus invicto animo sustinet,]&lt;/s2&gt; Sed anxia parens, postridie revulso leniter cataplasmate, inhorruit, videns filiolæ verticem hinc bullis turgidum, inde fossulis profunde ulceratum, &amp; “O (inquit) misera, siccine totam hanc transegisti noctem sub hujusmodi cruciatus voraci ardore”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034910Z">
        <seg>[9] But the anxious mother, the next day gently removing the poultice, was horrified to see her little daughter's head, from one side swollen with blisters, from the other deeply ulcerated with pits, &amp; "O (she said) miserable one, did you spend the whole of this night in such a voracious ardor of this kind of torment?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;Lima&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; meridionalis Americæ, qua mari Pacifico&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt; lambitur, nobile emporium, regnique Peruani metropolis, hanc novam in orbe novo enixa est Rosam anno Virginei partus MDLXXXVI, mense Aprili, [Hæc Sancta anno 1586 nascitur Limæ in Peruvia,] qui, quod erupturis floribus terram aperiat&lt;a3&gt;c&lt;/a3&gt;, ab officio nomen traxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203308Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;Lima&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; of South America, which is lapped by the Pacific Ocean&lt;a2&gt;b&lt;/a2&gt;, a noble emporium and the metropolis of the Kingdom of Peru, gave birth to this new Rose in the New World in the year of the Virgin's birth, 1586, in the month of April, [This Saint is born in 1586 in Lima, Peru,] who, because she opens the earth with bursting flowers&lt;a3&gt;c&lt;/a3&gt;, drew her name from her office.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;Lima&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;&lt;/s1&gt; meridionalis Americæ, qua mari Pacifico&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; lambitur, nobile emporium, regnique Peruani metropolis, hanc novam in orbe novo enixa est Rosam anno Virginei partus MDLXXXVI, mense Aprili, &lt;s5&gt;[Hæc Sancta anno 1586 nascitur Limæ in Peruvia,]&lt;/s5&gt; qui, quod erupturis floribus terram aperiat&lt;s6&gt;&lt;a7&gt;c&lt;/a7&gt;&lt;/s6&gt;, ab officio nomen traxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T193820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211755Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;Lima&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;&lt;/s1&gt; of South America, which is lapped by the Pacific Ocean&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, a noble emporium and the metropolis of the Kingdom of Peru, gave birth to this new Rose in the New World in the year of the Virgin's birth, 1586, in the month of April, &lt;s5&gt;[This Saint is born in 1586 in Lima, Peru,]&lt;/s5&gt; who, because she opens the earth with bursting flowers&lt;s6&gt;&lt;a7&gt;c&lt;/a7&gt;&lt;/s6&gt;, drew her name from her office.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;Lima&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;&lt;/s1&gt; meridionalis Americæ, qua mari Pacifico&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; lambitur, nobile emporium, regnique Peruani metropolis, hanc novam in orbe novo enixa est Rosam anno Virginei partus MDLXXXVI, mense Aprili, [Hæc Sancta anno 1586 nascitur Limæ in Peruvia,] qui, quod erupturis floribus terram aperiat&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, ab officio nomen traxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202531Z">
        <seg>&lt;b0&gt;Lima&lt;/b0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;a&lt;/a2&gt;&lt;/s1&gt; of South America, which is lapped by the Pacific Ocean&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, a noble emporium and the metropolis of the Kingdom of Peru, gave birth to this new Rose in the New World in the year of the Virgin's birth, 1586, in the month of April, &lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;&lt;/a6&gt;[This Saint is born in 1586 in Lima, Peru,]&lt;/s5&gt; who, because she opens the earth with bursting flowersc, drew her name from her office.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b1&gt;Testabatur&lt;/b1&gt;&lt;a2&gt;c&lt;/a2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000902Z">
        <seg>He was testifying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b4&gt;Qui habitat&lt;/b4&gt;: Ubi hæc verba;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051549Z">
        <seg>Who lives: Where are these words?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;b4&gt;Testabatur&lt;/b4&gt;&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035629Z">
        <seg>He was testifying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Aculeata corona, qua caput suum cinxit&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042028Z">
        <seg>A thorny crown, with which he crowned his head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Amor solitudinis, fuga conversationum, &amp; perangustum cubiculum&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043212Z">
        <seg>Love of solitude, avoidance of company, and a very small room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Ardens ejus amor erga Jesum sponsum suum&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053009Z">
        <seg>Her ardent love for Jesus, her bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Ariditas spiritualis &amp; variæ mentis afflictiones, quibus sancta Rosa exercetur&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045431Z">
        <seg>Spiritual dryness and various mental afflictions, with which Saint Rose is exercised.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Assiduum orationis studium, &amp; intima cum Deo conjunctio&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044749Z">
        <seg>Constant study of prayer, and intimate union with God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Corporalia misericordiæ opera proximis impensa&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061010Z">
        <seg>Material works of mercy spent on one's neighbors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Devotio ipsius erga salutiferum crucis signum, &amp; imaginem S. Catharinæ Senensis&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054822Z">
        <seg>His devotion to the salutary sign of the cross and to the image of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Durus S. Rosæ lectulus, &amp; nocturnæ vigiliæ&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042456Z">
        <seg>The hard bed of St. Rose, and the night vigils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Ex editione tertia, quæ anno 1668 Lovanii post Romanam secundam impressa est&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211737Z">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;From the third edition, which was printed in Louvain in 1668 after the second Roman edition&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Eximius zelus pro salute periclitantium animarum&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060344Z">
        <seg>Eminent zeal for the salvation of endangered souls.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Familiaritas cum angelis custodibus, &amp; varia certamina adversus dæmones&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051536Z">
        <seg>Familiarity with guardian angels, and various contests against demons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Fervida pietas erga sanctissimam Eucharistiam, pro cujus fide se ad martyrium accingit&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055631Z">
        <seg>A fervent devotion to the Most Holy Eucharist, for the faith in which he prepares himself for martyrdom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Imitatio beatæ Catharinæ Senensis, &amp; ingressus in tertium Ordinem sancti Dominici&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035920Z">
        <seg>Imitation of Blessed Catherine of Siena, and entry into the Third Order of Saint Dominic</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Magna hujus Virginis in Deo fiducia, cujus fructum sæpius in suis suorumque necessitatibus experta est&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061714Z">
        <seg>Great was this virgin's trust in God, the fruit of which she often experienced in her own and her loved ones' needs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Magna sanctæ Puellæ erga parentes reverentia, mirum obtemperandi studium, &amp; laboriosa pro iis solicitudo&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T033138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035248Z">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;The holy maiden's great reverence for her parents, her wonderful eagerness to obey, and her diligent concern for them&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Manifestum sudantis imaginis miraculum precibus ipsius impetratum, ut homines ad amorem Christi excitarentur&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053644Z">
        <seg>The manifest miracle of the sweating image was obtained by his prayers, so that people might be stirred to the love of Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Mira familiaritas hujus Virginis cum Christo, Deipara, &amp; S. Catharina Senensi&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050854Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's wonderful familiarity with Christ, the Mother of God, and St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Mirabilis cum Christo desponsatio exemplo S. Catharinæ Senensis&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044015Z">
        <seg>A wonderful betrothal to Christ, following the example of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Persecutiones, ægritudines aliæque adversitates patienter toleratæ, &amp; in mira quadam revelatione prævisæ&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052417Z">
        <seg>Persecutions, illnesses, and other adversities patiently endured, and foreseen in a certain marvelous revelation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Prodigiosa Sanctæ abstinentia, &amp; diuturna jejunia&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040952Z">
        <seg>The prodigious abstinence of the Saint, and long fasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Prophetia de futuro S. Catharinæ Senensis cœnobio, quod Limæ postea fundatum fuit&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062408Z">
        <seg>A prophecy about the future convent of St. Catherine of Siena, which was later founded in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Sanctæ patria, parentes, nomen, &amp; pia pueritia&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211752Z">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Holy homeland, parents, name, and pious childhood&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Severum doctorum virorum examen circa spiritum &amp; visiones hujus Virginis&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050005Z">
        <seg>A severe examination by learned men concerning the spirit and visions of this Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Tener ejus affectus erga duas Deiparæ imagines&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054214Z">
        <seg>His tender affection for two images of the Virgin Mary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e0&gt;Vera animi submissio, benignitas, affabilitas, aliæque virtutes&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040453Z">
        <seg>True humility of spirit, kindness, affability, and other virtues.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e1&gt;Cum vero illa significatio hic locum non habeat, recurrendum est ad Hierolexicon utriusque Macri, qui ad vocem&lt;/e1&gt; Symmysta, &lt;e2&gt;Græce&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συμμύστης&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;affirmat, hoc nomen interdum accipi pro&lt;/e4&gt; secretario, &lt;e5&gt;vel eo, qui est alteri a secretis&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052311Z">
        <seg>But since that meaning does not apply here, we must turn to the Hierolexicon of both Macros, which under the word Symmysta, Greek συμμύστης, affirms that this name is sometimes taken for&lt;/e4&gt; secretary, or for one who is another's confidant&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e1&gt;Ex testimonio Thomæ Bozii, aliorumque hic scriptor superius asserit, abbatem Montis Serrati primum cum aliis Benedictinis in America fidemprædicasse, quod alia occasione inferius annotabimus.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054704Z">
        <seg>From the testimony of Thomas Bozii and others, this writer asserts above that the abbot of Monte Serrato was the first, together with other Benedictines, to preach the faith in America, which we will note below on another occasion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e1&gt;Ita Gorræus, qui in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041417Z">
        <seg>So Gorræus, who in the medical definitions on page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e1&gt;Ita Gorræus, qui in Definitionibus medicis pag. 198 fusius hunc morbum explicat.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000527Z">
        <seg>Thus Gorræus, who explains this disease more fully in the Medical Definitions, page 198.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e1&gt;Sed cum hic nomen&lt;/e1&gt; Pæana &lt;e2&gt;sit in accusativo singularis numeri, &amp; Hansenius satis eleganter ac Latine vitam&lt;/e2&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e3&gt;conscripserit, non immerito dubitamus, an typographus pro&lt;/e3&gt; Pæan &lt;e4&gt;non excuderit&lt;/e4&gt; Pæana.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040444Z">
        <seg>But since the name Pæana is in the accusative singular, and Hansenius has written the life of St. Rose quite elegantly and in Latin, we do not unreasonably doubt whether the printer, instead of Pæan, printed Pæana.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e2&gt;Deinde Aldrovandus ibidem pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044734Z">
        <seg>Then Aldrovandus, ibid., page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e2&gt;Deinde ibidem indicatur, quomodo&lt;/e2&gt; cataplasma &lt;e3&gt;ab emplastro differat&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055622Z">
        <seg>Then, in the same place, it is indicated how a cataplasm differs from an emplaster.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e2&gt;In auctiore Cangii Glossario mulieres illæ, quas Hispani&lt;/e2&gt; beatas &lt;e3&gt;vocant, Beguinis in Belgio comparantur&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040435Z">
        <seg>In the more extensive Kangxi Dictionary, those women whom the Spaniards call beatas are compared to the Beguines in Belgium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e2&gt;Tum post varios illius præparandi modos hæc subjungit:&lt;/e2&gt; Etiam pastam quamdam ex iis componunt, quam pectoris &amp; stomachi vitiis atque catarrhis mederi volunt, licet apud eos, quibus hæc opinio non est veluti agnata, difficulter inveniat fidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052355Z">
        <seg>Then, after various methods of preparing it, she adds the following: They also make a certain paste from these, which they claim cures disorders of the chest and stomach as well as catarrhs, although it finds little credence among those who do not share this view.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Aristoteles eamdem vocem pro erudito convicio accipit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050826Z">
        <seg>Aristotle takes the same word for learned abuse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Arnaldus d' Andilly tomo 1 Diversorum Operum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042018Z">
        <seg>Arnold of Andilly, Volume 1 of Miscellaneous Works, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Arnaldus d' Andilly tomo 1 Diversorum Operum pag. 157 &amp; sequentibus hanc Francisci Losæ testis oculati lucubrationem Gallice interpretatus est.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000934Z">
        <seg>Arnold d'Andilly translated this eyewitness work by Francis Lose from the original French into Latin in Volume 1 of his *Diverse Works*, pages 157 and following.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Differt a cataplasmate, quod emplastra crassiora &amp; sicciora sint, ut laudatus Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061649Z">
        <seg>It differs from a cataplasm, because emplasters are thicker and drier, as the lauded Gorræus states in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Nunc arbor illa in variis regionibus obvia est, &amp; ab Hispanis&lt;/e3&gt; membrillo, &lt;e4&gt;Gallis&lt;/e4&gt; coignier, &lt;e5&gt;ab Italis&lt;/e5&gt; cotogno, &lt;e6&gt;Germanis&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;b7&gt;quittenbaum&lt;/b7&gt;, &lt;e8&gt;ac Belgis denique&lt;/e8&gt; &lt;b9&gt;queenboom&lt;/b9&gt; &lt;e10&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052916Z">
        <seg>Now that tree is found in various regions, &amp; by the Spaniards&lt;/e3&gt; membrillo, the French&lt;/e4&gt; coignier, by the Italians&lt;/e5&gt; cotogno, Germans&lt;/e6&gt; quittenbaum&lt;/b7&gt;, and finally the Belgians&lt;/e8&gt; queenboom&lt;/b9&gt; &lt;e10&gt;is called.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Porro Menagius in eodem Opere ad vocem&lt;/e3&gt; Nicotiane &lt;e4&gt;hac occasione satyricum epigramma contra Catharinam de Medicis reginam Galliæ refert.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060949Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Menagius in the same work, under the heading "Nicotiane," reports a satirical epigram on this occasion against Catherine de' Medici, Queen of France.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e3&gt;Quomodo autem atheroma differat a meliceride, mox dicemus.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055557Z">
        <seg>How an atheroma differs from a meliceride, we will say shortly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e4&gt;Porro Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055609Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Gorræus in the medical definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e4&gt;Supra citatus Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T232150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035139Z">
        <seg>&lt;e4&gt;The above-cited John Latius, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e4&gt;Unde sanctus apostolus Paulus in Epistola ad Hebræos cap.&lt;/e4&gt; XI ℣.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040943Z">
        <seg>Hence the holy apostle Paul in the Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e5&gt;Cum porro chymistæ argentum vivum per metaphoram appellent&lt;/e5&gt; Mercurium, &lt;e6&gt;ex sensu suspicor, hic per&lt;/e6&gt; pulveres hermeticam pyxidem olentes &lt;e7&gt;indicari remedium mixtum argento vivo, cujus apud medicos &amp; chirurgos frequens &amp; jam passim notus est usus&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T001115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035229Z">
        <seg>&lt;e5&gt;Since chemists, by way of metaphor, call mercury&lt;/e5&gt; "Mercury," &lt;e6&gt;I suspect that here, by&lt;/e6&gt; the odorous powders of the hermetic box, a remedy mixed with mercury &lt;e7&gt;is being indicated, one whose use among physicians and surgeons is frequent and already well known.&lt;/e7&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e5&gt;Hac appellatione plerumque designatur pestis vel contagiosa lues, quæ etiam propterea&lt;/e5&gt; morbus epidemicus &lt;e6&gt;aliquando vocatur&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054738Z">
        <seg>By this term, a plague or contagious disease is generally designated, which is also sometimes called an epidemic disease for that reason.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e5&gt;Hic videtur accipi pro congregationibus judicum, qui huic supplicationi intererant.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054732Z">
        <seg>This appears to be taken to mean the gatherings of the judges who were present at this supplication.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e5&gt;Hinc quoque&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;ἱερατικὸς&lt;/s6&gt; &lt;e7&gt;adjectivum Latine&lt;/e7&gt; sacerdotalis &lt;e8&gt;aut&lt;/e8&gt; rebus sacris destinatus &lt;e9&gt;explicatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050817Z">
        <seg>Hence also the adjective ἱερατικὸς is explained in Latin as sacerdotalis, that is, pertaining to or destined for sacred things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e5&gt;Quod si forte biographus hanc vocem&lt;/e5&gt; Pæana &lt;e6&gt;adjective usurpaverit, ac subintellexerit substantivum&lt;/e6&gt; carmina, &lt;e7&gt;istud adjectivum apud auctores linguæ Latinæ nondum legimus&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040447Z">
        <seg>If, by chance, a biographer has used this word "Pæana" adjectivally and implied the substantive "carmina," we have not yet found that adjective in the authors of the Latin language.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e6&gt;De variis hujus morbi causis &amp; effectibus consule medicos obvios, si lubet&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061004Z">
        <seg>Consult physicians of your choice about the various causes and effects of this disease, if you wish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e7&gt;Hinc Bacchus apud poëtas Græcos&lt;/e7&gt; &lt;s8&gt;ἀνθοφόρος&lt;/s8&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054813Z">
        <seg>Hence Bacchus is called ἀνθοφόρος by the Greek poets.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e8&gt;Biographus itaque isto nomine composito indicare voluit, angelum cum&lt;/e8&gt; S. Rosa &lt;e9&gt;diu conversari vel colloqui consuevisse, ni fallor&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052320Z">
        <seg>The biographer, therefore, by that composite name wished to indicate that the angel had long been accustomed to converse or talk with St. Rose, if I am not mistaken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;e9&gt;id est&lt;/e9&gt;, floriger &lt;e10&gt;appellatur&lt;/e10&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054816Z">
        <seg>That is to say, he is called the flower-bearer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Capiti suo imposuit coronam]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Decebat&lt;/b1&gt; charam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042031Z">
        <seg>He placed a crown on his head. It was fitting for the beloved B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Describitur monasterium Limense,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Illustrioribus&lt;/b1&gt; Limensis emporii ornamentis demum accessit nobile, spatiosum, elegans beatæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium, numeroso sacrarum virginum collegio dicatum sub regulari S. Patris Dominici instituto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062412Z">
        <seg>[The monastery of Lima is described,] Finally, the noble, spacious, and elegant monastery of Blessed Catherine of Siena was added to the more illustrious ornaments of the port of Lima, dedicated to a numerous college of holy virgins under the regular institute of Saint Dominic.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Dum S. Rosa renuit inire matrimonium,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Variæ&lt;/b1&gt; ac sine numero sunt viæ, anfractus, itinera, quibus æterna Sapientia selectas animas in sublime perfectionis cacumen facit eluctari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035923Z">
        <seg>[While St. Rose refused to enter into marriage,] Various and countless are the ways, the winding paths, the journeys by which the eternal Wisdom leads chosen souls to struggle up to the lofty summit of perfection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Dum mater S. Rosam invitam]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;In&lt;/b1&gt; concursu duorum præceptorum, quo simul honorare patrem &amp; matrem, simul Deo magis quam hominibus obedire justum jussumque est, Rosæ frequenter ingenio &amp; solertia opus fuit, ut neutrum offenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035251Z">
        <seg>[While Mother was inviting St. Rose] In the dilemma between honoring one's father and mother and obeying God rather than men, it was often necessary for Rose, by her wit and prudence, to avoid offending either.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Erat Limæ inclyta statua Deiparæ,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Supradictis&lt;/b1&gt; pene gemella sunt, quæ hic narrabimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054218Z">
        <seg>[There was a famous statue of the Virgin Mary in Lima,] the following are almost identical to the above, which we will relate here;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Exemplo S. Catharinæ Senensis]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Fundamento&lt;/b1&gt; tam profundo, lato, ac solido dignum fuit ac tutum, prægrandia, excelsa, ponderosa superstitui fastigia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040955Z">
        <seg>[By the example of St. Catherine of Siena] A foundation so deep, broad, and solid was worthy and safe for supporting the immense, lofty, and ponderous superstructure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Huic Virgini libros legenti,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Animo&lt;/b1&gt;, cui amarescunt terrena, dulcescunt cælestia, seseque ultro insinuant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050857Z">
        <seg>To this virgin who reads books, the earthly grows bitter, the heavenly grows sweet, and they insinuate themselves of their own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Hæc Virgo imitata S. Catharinam Senensem]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Ex&lt;/b1&gt; Historia vitæ B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044018Z">
        <seg>[This virgin imitated St. Catherine of Siena] From the History of the Life of the Blessed</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Ingeniosa Virgovarios excogitavit modos,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Tanta&lt;/b1&gt; ei corporis macerandi cura fuit, ut nec pauculas nocturnæ quietis horas tormento vacuas esse permiserit: hinc strati sui voluit eam esse duritiem, quæ somnum abigeret magis, quam vocaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042459Z">
        <seg>[He devised ingenious contrivances for Virgil,] he was so intent on wearing his body out that he would not allow even a few hours of nightly rest to be free from torment: hence he had his bed made so hard that it would drive sleep away rather than invite it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Præter alios spiritualis vitæ magistros]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Arcanum&lt;/b1&gt; gratiæ lumen, quod ab infantia Rosam deduxit per vias rectas, simul reflexu admirabili certitudinem indiderat attractus vere divini, minimeque fallacis, ita quod de rectitudine semitæ, per quam ibat, ambigere non posset, quamvis hanc parum tritam, verisque &amp; obscuris vestigiis vix notabilem comperisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050008Z">
        <seg>[Besides other masters of the spiritual life] the Arcanum of grace, a light that had guided Rosa from infancy along the right paths, had at the same time, by a marvelous turn, instilled in her the certainty of a truly divine and most unerring attraction, so that she could not doubt the rectitude of the path she was on, although she found it little trodden, and scarcely noticeable for its true and obscure footprints.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Rosa in oratorio hospitii sui]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;In&lt;/b1&gt; domestico Gundisalvi quæstoris oratorio inter alias sacras imagines, tum venustate, tum reverentia celebris eminebat illa, quæ virilem Christi vultum scitissimis coloribus ac lineamentis exprimebat in tela.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053647Z">
        <seg>[A rose in the oratory of her lodging] In the oratory of Gundisalvus the treasurer's home, among other sacred images, one stood out, celebrated for both its beauty and its reverence, which depicted the manly face of Christ on canvas with the most skillful colors and features.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Rosa solicita de salute barbarorum,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Quæ&lt;/b1&gt; tam mascule ardebat circa mysterium, quo verum Christi Corpus velatur, non poterat mystica ejusdem membra negligere, sicubi videbat hæc ancipiti perdendæ salutis discrimini patere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060347Z">
        <seg>[Worried about the salvation of the barbarians,] she, who burned so zealously for the mystery by which the true Body of Christ is veiled, could not neglect its mystical members wherever she saw them exposed to the twofold danger of perishing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta Rosa vacabat]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Si&lt;/b1&gt;, qui adhæret Domino, unus spiritus est cum eo, 1 Cor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044753Z">
        <seg>[Saint Rose was vacant] If, he who adheres to the Lord, is one spirit with him, 1 Cor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta divinam iram placatura]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Mirum&lt;/b1&gt;, in arido totque jejuniis extenuato corpusculo superfuisse locum, qui flagella exciperet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041501Z">
        <seg>[The holy one, having placated the divine wrath] It is marvelous that in a body so dry and emaciated by so many fasts there was still room to receive the scourges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta esurit, ut egenos pascat,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Mirabitur&lt;/b1&gt;, quisquis legerit, Rosæ pauperrimæ funus publicis mendicorum Limensium ejulatibus, threnis, lachrymis fuisse celebratum, vociferante egenorum turba, veram pauperum nutricem ac matrem fato acerbissimo, &amp; præmaturo sibi ereptam fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061014Z">
        <seg>[She is hungry to feed the poor,]Whoever reads will be amazed that the funeral of the very poor Rosa was celebrated with the public wails, laments, and tears of the beggars of Lima, the crowd of needy people crying out that a true nurse and mother of the poor had been snatched from them by a most bitter and untimely fate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta firmiter Deo confidens,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Aprima&lt;/b1&gt; pueritia Rosam suam prævenerat Christus in benedictionibus dulcedinis, edocens confidere in adjutorio Altissimi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061717Z">
        <seg>[Saint, firmly trusting in God,] from her earliest childhood Christ had preceded Rose in blessings of sweetness, teaching her to trust in the help of the Most High.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta puellares ludos exosa]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Ut&lt;/b1&gt; olim S. Catharinam Senensem adhuc pusillam, solitudinis amor usque ad speluncæ quæsitionem abripuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043215Z">
        <seg>[Hating girlish games] As with St. Catherine of Siena, who, still a little girl, was carried away by a love of solitude to the point of seeking out a cave.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta sæpius communicabat Corpori Domini]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Si&lt;/b1&gt; talia acciderunt Rosæ, virginem Senensem vestienti ex aliena substantia, longe mirabiliora accepit ab illo mirabili Sacramento, in quo Christum vestiunt aliena accidentia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055634Z">
        <seg>[She often received the Body of the Lord]. If such things happened to Rose, a virgin from Siena clothed with another's substance, she received far more marvelous things from that wondrous Sacrament, in which Christ is clothed with other substances.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta tanto divini amoris igne flagrabat,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Quanta&lt;/b1&gt; in Rosæ pectore flagraret divini amoris Ætna&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt;, placuit Deo interdum visibilibus signis palam facere, dum ex vultu orantis jussit splendores &amp; flammulas scintillare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053012Z">
        <seg>[The Saint burned with so great a fire of divine love,] How great an Aetna of divine love burned in the heart of Rose, God pleased at times to make manifest by visible signs, while He commanded splendors and little flames to sparkle from the face of the one praying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Sancta virgo crucis signum,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Solitariæ&lt;/b1&gt; in horto cellulæ (de qua supra) prima &amp; fere tota supellex crux erat Virgine procerior altiorque, ut plenius tum Calvariæ supplicium meditanti ingereret, tum piis novæ Magdalenæ complexibus commodius stringi posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054825Z">
        <seg>[The holy virgin's sign of the cross,] the Solitaries in the garden of the cell (of which I have spoken above) had for their entire furnishings, and almost their only furniture, a cross taller and more stately than the Virgin herself, so that it might more fully impress upon her the Passion of Calvary, and at the same time be more conveniently embraced by the pious embraces of the new Magdalene.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Velut aurum in fornace,]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Quod&lt;/b1&gt; auro fornax, id justis electis tribulatio: præcipue, ubi obviandum periculo, ne magnitudo revelationum extollat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045434Z">
        <seg>Like gold in the furnace, that which the furnace is to gold, tribulation is to the elect just: especially when the danger must be faced lest the greatness of revelations should inflate them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Virgo ex humilitate]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;In&lt;/b1&gt; suo nihilo cominus ac penitissime agnoscendo, quam profunde jugiterque Rosa perstiterit occupata, omnis humillimæ suæ vitæ clamat actio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040456Z">
        <seg>In her acknowledgment of her own nothingness, how profoundly and unceasingly the Rose remained occupied, the very action of her humblest life proclaims.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s0&gt;[Virguncuta pasta est persecutiones]&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;b1&gt;Rosis&lt;/b1&gt; spinæ non tarde accedunt, sed connascuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052420Z">
        <seg>[A little bit of pasta is persecution] The thorns of roses do not come on quickly, but are born together.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[&amp; incredulis confessariis,]&lt;/s2&gt; dum initio, quod sibi persuaserant, nitebantur &amp; Virgini persuadere, videlicet, ipsam itinere non recto, aut saltem non tuto incedere, vertigine cerebri, aliisque noxiis intemperiis laborare, frivolas illusiones esse, quas ipsa supernas illustrationes credebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052525Z">
        <seg>[To the incredulous confessors,] while at first they strove to persuade the Virgin that she was not walking in a straight path, or at least not safely, that she was suffering from dizziness of the brain and other noxious disorders, and that the frivolous illusions she believed to be heavenly illuminations were nothing of the sort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[Christus illam sibi desponsaverit,]&lt;/s2&gt; “Rosa cordis mei!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044340Z">
        <seg>[Christ may have betrothed her to himself,] "Rose of my heart!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[Cum futura memorati cœnobii fundatrix]&lt;/s2&gt; Illustri matronæ Isabellæ de Mexia domi famula erat Mariana nomine, cui graviter ægrotanti Rosa (utpote illis in ædibus pene domestica) more suo ex charitate ministrabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062858Z">
        <seg>[The future foundress of the aforementioned convent] The illustrious matron Isabella of Mexico had a maid named Mariana at home, who, when gravely ill, was ministered to by Rosa (who, as was almost a member of the household, was accustomed to such acts of charity) in her usual manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[Joannes de Villalobos noster nonnihil mitigavit.]&lt;/s2&gt; nempe ad unum ex Patribus spiritualibus, quorum ductui Rosa obtemperabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042300Z">
        <seg>[Our John of Villalobos somewhat mitigated it.] namely, to one of the spiritual Fathers, to whose guidance Rosa submitted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[Sancta supplex coram hac imagine discordiam,]&lt;/s2&gt; Excrescebat infelix lolium, quodque initio nonnisi opinionum prælium fuerat, ipsa repugnandi dulcedine paulatim degenerabat in amarulentiam voluntatum, non sine scissura pacis &amp; offensa Numinis, cui extra fines pacis habitare non lubet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054416Z">
        <seg>[Holy suppliant before this image of discord,] Wicked tares grew, and what at first had been nothing more than a battle of opinions was gradually, by the very sweetness of opposing, degenerating into the bitterness of wills, not without a breach of peace and the offense of the Deity, who is not pleased to dwell outside the bounds of peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[ad studium orationis hortatur;]&lt;/s2&gt; at sub eo ritu ac norma, qua illam primitus, ex instructione Deiparæ, tradiderat mundo S. Pater Dominicus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, hoc est, cum innexa decadatim consideratione mysteriorum nostræ redemptionis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045128Z">
        <seg>[He encourages the study of prayer;] but in the same rite and rule by which St. Dominic had originally handed it down to the world at the instruction of the Mother of God, that is, with the inextricably linked decade-by-decade consideration of the mysteries of our redemption.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[cujus iram molli responso lenivit.]&lt;/s2&gt; “Quorsum (inquit) hæ fraudes?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043423Z">
        <seg>[whose anger he soothed with a gentle reply.] "What are these deceptions for?" (he said)</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[cælitus accepit duplex privilegium;]&lt;/s2&gt; Nacta hanc eremum felix Rosa, hoc sategit, ne quis illic temporis punctus sibi efflueret sine fructu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043652Z">
        <seg>She received a twofold privilege from heaven; having acquired this happy solitude, she strove that not a single moment of time there should pass by without fruit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[eamque minus incommode dormire coëgerit.]&lt;/s2&gt; vix tamen blandius aut commodius: nam tabulas suas asseresque repetiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042908Z">
        <seg>[and forced her to sleep less uncomfortably.] Yet hardly more gently or comfortably: for he repeated his tablets and planks;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[eximiam victoriam reportat.]&lt;/s2&gt; At Virgo sibimet indignata, quod horruisset, cum pelvi clam secessit in angulum foras, ibique semetipsam dure objurgans, “Itane (inquit) strenue ac serio studuisti dilectioni proximi, ut nauseæ tibi sit miserabilium infirmitas?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061358Z">
        <seg>[He reports an exceptional victory.] But the Virgin, indignant with herself because she had shuddered, secretly withdrew with the basin to a corner outside, and there, harshly rebuking herself, said, "Have you then so strenuously and earnestly devoted yourself to the love of your neighbor that the infirmity of the miserable makes you nauseous?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[familiariter ac mirabiliter]&lt;/s2&gt; Matrona honoris gratia secedenti comitem adjunxit septennem puellam, e mancipio domi suæ natam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051136Z">
        <seg>[Familiarly and wonderfully] The matron, as an honor, appointed a seven-year-old girl, born into her household, as companion to the retiring woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[futuræ sanctitatis ac patientiæ,]&lt;/s2&gt; Et primum quidem tolerantiæ fuit animique inter dolores impavidi, fortis, tranquilli, ut liqueret Rosam esse, quam spinæ nec terrent, nec terunt, aut dejiciunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034829Z">
        <seg>[of future holiness and patience,] And first of all, she was patient and her spirit among her pains was fearless, strong, and calm, so that it was clear she was a rose that neither the thorns frighten, wear down, nor bring down.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[humilitate &amp; pudicitia sic Deo placuit,]&lt;/s2&gt; puritas hic tractanda foret ante cælestes nuptias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044043Z">
        <seg>[By humility and chastity, God was thus pleased,] purity would have to be dealt with here before the heavenly nuptials.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[ibique piis exercitiis inhærens,]&lt;/s2&gt; Videbatur in se quadrare id, quod canebatur de justo Sene: &lt;b3&gt;Responsum accepit Simeon&lt;/b3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043624Z">
        <seg>[and there, devoted to pious exercises,] It seemed to him that what was being sung about the righteous old man was coming true in him: Simeon received an answer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[imo charitas ejus ad bruta animalia se extendebat,]&lt;/s2&gt; hic, ubi de sola Rosæ misericordia agitur, unum adjicere fas esto, sed lepidum: nam usque ad bruta animantia clemens Rosæ suavitas &amp; tenera extendebatur commiseratio: siquidem teste Salomone Prov.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061519Z">
        <seg>[Indeed, her charity extended even to brute animals,] here, where we speak only of Rose's mercy, let us be permitted to add one charming detail: for Rose's clemency and tender compassion extended even to brute creatures; for, as Solomon testifies in Proverbs…</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[nonaginta novem aculeis armatam,]&lt;/s2&gt; verum id nonnisi puerile fuit tirocinium robustioris tormenti, quod ex secunda corona, aculeis nonaginta &amp; novem armata, imminebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042105Z">
        <seg>[armed with ninety-nine spikes,] but that was nothing more than a childish trial run for a more powerful torment, which loomed from the second crown, armed with ninety-nine spikes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[per cælestem revelationem,]&lt;/s2&gt; quapropter semel eam permisit nonnihil concuti pavida formidine circa prædestinationis suæ arcanum, omni animæ tremendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061801Z">
        <seg>[by heavenly revelation,] therefore He once permitted her to be somewhat shaken by a fearful dread concerning the mystery of her predestination, a dread terrible to the whole soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[quibus recreata fuerat,]&lt;/s2&gt; petiitque ulterius, qua igitur forma apprehenderet Divinitatis præsentiam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050557Z">
        <seg>[by which she had been restored,] she went on to ask further, by what form, then, she might apprehend the presence of the Divine?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[sibi postea tolerandos esse,]&lt;/s2&gt; Respondit: multiplicis ac peregrini, adeo quod nulli prorsus colori in mundo isto visibili possit vel tenuissime aptari comparatio, nisi quod arcuum venustas omnium possibilium colorum varietatem ac gratiam in se collegisse videbatur cum infinita quadam excellentia, &amp; dignitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052742Z">
        <seg>[to be endured by them later,] He replied: manifold and strange, so that it could not be compared even faintly to any color in this visible world, except that the beauty of the rainbow seemed to have gathered in itself the variety and grace of all possible colors with a certain infinite excellence and dignity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>&lt;s2&gt;[spirituales vestes Christo nascituro præparabat.]&lt;/s2&gt; Compererat, Dictante historia, sanctam Catharinam Senensem aliquando latenti sub forma pauperis Christo dedisse vestem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053530Z">
        <seg>[She was preparing spiritual garments for Christ about to be born.] She had learned, as history relates, that the holy Catherine of Siena had once given a garment to Christ, who was hidden under the form of a poor man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ANNOTATA.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T044123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035046Z">
        <seg>Notes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Abnuebat præsens anni temperies, quæ in illis regionibus mense Maio id genus flosculorum ignorat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055123Z">
        <seg>The current season of the year refused, since in those regions the month of May knows no such flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Absit a me tam ignava desidia”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052544Z">
        <seg>Far from me be such lazy idleness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accedens quæstor Gundisalvus, hominemque comiter salutans, vidit hunc minime assurgere, mox vidit nec posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044619Z">
        <seg>As the quaestor Gundisalvus approached and greeted the man courteously, he saw that he barely rose and soon realized he could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accesserat in afflictionis cumulum vorax &amp; purulentus sub mamilla cancer&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, qui lividis ac tabificis incrementis vergebat in carcinoma&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, &amp;, tardante remedio, exitium minabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061150Z">
        <seg>Adding to the heap of affliction was a voracious and purulent cancer beneath the breast, which, with livid and gangrenous growths, was developing into a carcinoma and, with the remedy delayed, threatened destruction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accessit beneficio, quod exinde numquam in posterum arthritis manum illam invasit, quoad Rosa supervixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055448Z">
        <seg>It was a blessing that, from then on, arthritis never afflicted that hand again for as long as Rosa lived.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accessit denuo augustissima Excitatrix, &amp; (quod ante non consueverat) protensa nivea manu, pulsavit latus dormientis, agitavit familiarissimo attactu resupinum corpusculum, iterumque ait: “Surge Filiola, ne pigriteris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051357Z">
        <seg>The most august Awakener came again, and (which she had not been accustomed to do before) stretching out her snow-white hand, she tapped the sleeper's side, shook the supine little body with her most familiar touch, and said again: "Arise, my daughter, be not slothful;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accessit, vidensque nulla ex parte promicare lumen, subduxit pessulum, taciteque abiit, cunctationis exitum præstolatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052135Z">
        <seg>She approached, and seeing that no light shone from any part, she drew back the bolt and went away in silence, waiting to see the outcome of her hesitation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit quodam vespere in noctem proclivi &amp; magis umbroso, quod Rosa adhuc tenera diutius moraretur in horto, quo se ad meditandum receperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062043Z">
        <seg>It happened one evening, as night was falling and growing darker, that Rosa, still delicate, lingered longer in the garden where she had withdrawn to meditate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit tamen, quod abrepta dulcore, intrantes &amp; exeuntes domesticos non observarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053235Z">
        <seg>However, it happened that, carried away by sweetness, she did not notice the household servants coming and going.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit una circiter hora ante felicem Virginis ad meliora transitum, venire illuc religiosum quemdam sacerdotem, quem illa videre ante mortem desideraverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053337Z">
        <seg>About an hour before the blessed Virgin's happy passing to better things, a certain religious priest, whom she had desired to see before her death, came there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit, quod Gundisalvum quæstorem Michaël Garzes Canonicus familiariter domi suæ inviseret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040559Z">
        <seg>It happened that Michael Garzes, the Canon, would familiarly visit Gundisalvus the Quaestor at his home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit, quod Rosa per integram quamdam octavam, permissu confessarii, dietim altaris Sacramento pasceretur, &amp; tunc quidem integro illo octiduo ab omni abstinuit corporali alimonia, eo quod superna illa saturitas non tantum reliqui edulii omnem abegerat appetitum, sed plane cujuscumque alterius alimenti reddiderat pro illo tempore incapacem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060000Z">
        <seg>It happened that Rosa, by the permission of her confessor, was nourished daily by the Sacrament of the altar for an entire octave, and during that whole eight days she abstained from all bodily food, because that heavenly saturation had not only banished all appetite for any other nourishment, but had rendered her completely incapable of taking any for that time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit, quod prægrandis arca dum incaute propereque clauditur, dextrum Rosulæ pollicem fortuito corriperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034832Z">
        <seg>It happened that, as the very large chest was being closed carelessly and hastily, it accidentally caught Rosula's right thumb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accidit, unum ex suis confessariis graviter infirmari, ac tempore quidem valde importuno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053446Z">
        <seg>It happened that one of his confessors became gravely ill, and at a very inopportune time;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Accitus deinde chirurgus, plagæ sanandæ novæque cuti inducendæ quadraginta duos continuos insumpsit dies, tormento vix mitiori, quam quo prima nox Virginem lancinarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034916Z">
        <seg>The surgeon, when summoned, then spent forty-two consecutive days healing the wound and grafting on new skin, with a torment scarcely less severe than that with which the first night had lanced the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Acta Sanctorum: 26.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211715Z">
        <seg>Acts of the Saints: 26.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aculeus erat, qui Rosam non læderet, sed oblectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040806Z">
        <seg>It was a thorn that would not harm Rose, but delight her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad Rosam ut venit, (quæ ut sanctam Hostiam sumeret, illo momento vultum, ora pallii reducta, detegebat) vidit speciem humana formosiorem, claram, niveam, fulgentem, ac cælesti quadam pulchritudine obradiatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055817Z">
        <seg>As he approached the rose (which, to receive the Holy Host, at that moment revealed its face, its pallium's folds drawn back), he saw a form more beautiful than any human, bright, snow-white, radiant, and adorned with a certain heavenly beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad hanc ubi sacerdos cum sacratissima Hostia pervenit, faciem Virginis totam flammeam vidit, &amp; medullitus expavit, nondum gnarus, quid indicaret mira hæc &amp; improvisa coruscatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055755Z">
        <seg>When the priest arrived at this spot with the Most Holy Host, he saw the Virgin's face entirely aflame and was utterly terrified, not yet knowing what this marvelous and unexpected flash of light signified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad hanc vocem tripudiabat Rosæ animus, maternam permissionem, tametsi jurgio ac bile acidam, pro dulci blandimento arripuit, conditionem avida manu acceptavit, matrique pro tam liberali indulgentia gratias egit tenerrimas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042524Z">
        <seg>At this word, Rose's heart leapt for joy; she seized her mother's permission—though tinged with reproach and bitter bile—as a sweet caress, eagerly accepted the condition, and offered her mother the most tender thanks for such generous indulgence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad hujus exercitium scholamque, inter alios Ferdinandum germanum suum fratrem nitebatur allicere, promittens laborem exiguum, fructum eximium atque inæstimabilem fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045059Z">
        <seg>To attract others to this exercise and school, he relied on his brother Ferdinand, promising that the effort would be slight and the reward exceptional and invaluable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad hæc verba maluisset Rosa aut tacere, aut arte ac dissimulatione sermonem alio deflectere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045853Z">
        <seg>At these words, Rosa would have preferred either to remain silent or to deflect the conversation elsewhere with skill and dissimulation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad me descendat, vultum ostendat,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051632Z">
        <seg>Let him come down to me, let him show his face,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad nihilum redactus sum, &amp; nescivi &amp;c”,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050436Z">
        <seg>I was brought to nothing, and I knew not, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ad rem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045450Z">
        <seg>To the point.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Addebat rationes solidas ac ponderosas, quas cælum ac zelus divini obsequii dictarant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043506Z">
        <seg>He added solid and weighty reasons, dictated by heaven and the zeal for divine obedience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Addebat, mirum dictu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054928Z">
        <seg>He added, strange as it may seem!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Addebat, plura de hoc viro, ac grandiora se prolaturum in publicum, dummodo ultra illum supervivere contingat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050113Z">
        <seg>He added that he would reveal more, and greater things, about this man to the public, provided he should survive him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Addixit Virgo suarum precum fida subsidia, comiterque dimisit hominem, qui exinde juvenilibus desideriis valefaciens, gravem ac sobriam vivendi rationem instituit, adeo ut singulis octiduis minimum semel animam suam Pœnitentiæ sacramento expiare, ac divino Epulo reficere adsueverit, lætus incidisse in Rosam, quæ &amp; cælitus infelicis animæ suæ statum didicerat, &amp; salubres aculeos seriæ compunctionis noverat opportune suffigere minus exspectanti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060713Z">
        <seg>The Virgin added the faithful aid of her prayers and kindly dismissed the man, who from that time, bidding farewell to his youthful desires, established a grave and sober way of life, so that every eight days he would at least once cleanse his soul with the sacrament of Penance, and to refresh himself with the divine Banquet, rejoicing to have fallen upon a Rose, who had both learned from heaven the state of his unhappy soul and knew how to apply the salutary thorns of serious compunction at a timely moment to the less expectant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adeo a teneris discebat pati prius, quam vagire aut loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052441Z">
        <seg>He learned to endure from a very early age, before he could even cry or speak.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aderant Christianæ phalangi aliquot religiosi ex Prædicatorum Ordine, ac implorata in opem Regina sanctissimi Rosarii, dum utrimque concurrunt acies, visa est utrimque in aëre conspicua diva Virgo sub suæ Limensis imaginis notissimo agalmate&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, virgam distringens in hostes, ac, ni cederent, internecionem comminitans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054239Z">
        <seg>Present were several religious from the Order of Preachers, and having implored the help of the Queen of the Most Holy Rosary, while the armies rushed together from both sides, the divine Virgin was seen in the air on either side, under the well-known statue of her Liman image, brandishing a staff at the enemies, and, if they did not retreat, threatening them with utter destruction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aderat forte P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060546Z">
        <seg>Father was there by chance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adfuit demum statuta dies, qua Rosa obtentæ gratiæ fidejussorem intuitura, festinavit ad ecclesiam S. Dominici: &amp; ecce SS. Rosarii sacellum ingressa, vidit coronam suam, a collo divæ Virginis solutam, pendere palam de manu parvuli Jesu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043540Z">
        <seg>At last the appointed day arrived, when Rosa, about to see the surety of her granted grace, hurried to the Church of St. Dominic; and behold, upon entering the chapel of the Blessed Rosary, she saw her crown, loosed from the neck of the Virgin Queen, plainly hanging from the hand of the little Jesus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adfuit pictor, limpidissimi humoris claritatem miratus, digito guttas aliquot delibavit, fricuit, naribus admovit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053812Z">
        <seg>A painter was present, marveling at the clarity of the most limpid humor; he tasted a few drops with his finger, rubbed them, and brought them to his nostrils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adhuc infantem interna unctio Spiritus orare docuerat, fervorem indiderat, quem plerumque nec ipsa nocturna somnia distrahere ad alia objecta potuere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044759Z">
        <seg>Even as an infant, the inward anointing of the Spirit had taught her to pray and had instilled in her a fervor that, for the most part, not even her nightly dreams could divert to other objects.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adhuc juvencula examinanti conjessario erudite recensuit dignoscendorum spirituum regulas ab effectibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052240Z">
        <seg>Still a young woman, she learnedly reviewed for the examining magistrate the rules for distinguishing spirits from their effects.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Admiranti, unde ver tam repentinum adfloruisset, en conspicuus adfuit parvulus Jesus purissimæ Genitricis innixus ulnis, ac Rosam blande compellans, jussit extemplo sparsas rosas in repandam sinuosæ vestis laciniam colligere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061857Z">
        <seg>As I marvelled whence such a sudden spring had burst forth, behold, the little Jesus appeared, supported in the arms of the purest Mother, and gently urging the Rose, he at once commanded her to gather the scattered roses into the wide, sinuous fold of her garment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Admisit insperatæ nomenclaturæ gratiam puella non sine excelso intimi dulcoris sensu, adeo ut die quadam a sanctissima Communione domum revertens, ardore insolito obtestaretur matrem, unicum hoc gemini favoris nomen Rosæ a sancta Maria sibi quam creberrime repeteret, ac frequentius occantaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034752Z">
        <seg>The girl received the grace of her unexpected name, not without a lofty sense of inner sweetness, so much so that one day, returning home from the most holy Communion, she implored her mother with unusual ardor to repeat to her as often as possible the single name of her twin favorite, Rose, and to sing it more frequently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adquievit &amp; ipsa, considerans, ejusmodi coronam nec sub velo sat commode celari posse, punctis sine ordine prominentibus, nimiumque suo cruciatui decedere, si non omnia usque ad unum acumina introrsum vergerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042152Z">
        <seg>She too, upon reflection, realized that such a crown could not be hidden adequately even under a veil, with its points protruding at random and only adding to her torment unless all its spikes were bent inward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adquievit conscius veritatis confessarius, breviter excusans, quod sola probandi spiritus gratia censoriam hanc disceptationem excitarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062030Z">
        <seg>The self-aware confessor of the truth acquiesced, briefly excusing himself that he had stirred up this censorious debate solely for the sake of testing the spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adrepsit tamen necessaria dormitionis hora, exstinctum est lumen, &amp; pavida Virgo primum soporem vix libans, quas chirothecas induerat, pyrothecas&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; sentire cœpit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T033209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203454Z">
        <seg>However, the necessary hour for sleep arrived, the light was extinguished, and the fearful Virgin, barely tasting her first slumber, began to feel the pyrotechnic&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; gloves she had donned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adrepsit tamen necessaria dormitionis hora, exstinctum est lumen, &amp; pavida Virgo primum soporem vix libans, quas chirothecas induerat, pyrothecas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; sentire cœpit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035344Z">
        <seg>However, the necessary hour for sleep arrived, the light was extinguished, and the fearful Virgin, barely tasting her first slumber, began to feel the pyrotechnic gloves she had donned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Adstupuit novo huic frixorio inexperta Rosa, gaudebatque adesse licentiam, qua manus tormento tam noxio eximeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035353Z">
        <seg>Rosa, inexperienced with this new frying pan, was glad to have the opportunity to free her hands from such a harmful tool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Advocatur chirurgus, isque ut læso articulo tumidum pus educeret, acribus remediis totum pene unguem absumpsit, demum &amp; unguiculi residuum, lento ac violento forfice radicitus avulsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034838Z">
        <seg>A surgeon was summoned, and to draw out the pus that had accumulated in the injured joint, he used harsh remedies to remove almost the entire nail, and finally, with slow but forceful shears, he uprooted the remaining stub.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Advolavit turbata mater, sed dissimulabat tranquilla filiola dolorem, abscondit manum, donec post dies aliquot putresceret sub unguiculo emortuus sanguis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034835Z">
        <seg>The mother rushed in, alarmed, but the little daughter hid her pain, pretending to be calm and hiding her hand until, after a few days, the dead blood under her fingernail began to rot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Affectabat muliebri studio mater Filiæ manus vane poliri, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; vanos seculi mores imitari jubet,]&lt;/s2&gt; ad delicati candoris elegantiam ac nitorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035328Z">
        <seg>The mother, with a womanly zeal, urged her daughter to polish her hands in vain and to imitate the vain customs of the world, for the sake of delicate whiteness and elegance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Affectabat muliebri studio mater Filiæ manus vane poliri, [&amp; vanos seculi mores imitari jubet,] ad delicati candoris elegantiam ac nitorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203454Z">
        <seg>The mother, with a womanly zeal, urged her daughter to polish her hands in vain and to imitate the vain customs of the world, for the sake of delicate whiteness and elegance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Affectus erant, quibus nondum invenit nomina inops mortalium ruditas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044331Z">
        <seg>They were emotions for which the crude ignorance of mortals has not yet found names.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Age ut vis, nec si tibi mortem consciveris, te amplius interpellabo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042843Z">
        <seg>Do as you wish, and even if you bring death upon yourself, I will no longer interfere with you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Agnosce, Vincenti, periculum tuum, corrige animum vanæ oblectationis nimio venatu districtum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060657Z">
        <seg>Recognize your danger, Vincent, and correct a mind distracted by vain amusement and excessive hunting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aiebat intra se: “Venit per hortum mater, nec more suo trepidat, hoc solo jam impavida, quod maritum suum ad latus præsentem habeat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062055Z">
        <seg>She said to herself: "My mother comes through the garden, and does not, as usual, hurry; she is already fearless in this alone, because she has her husband present at her side."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aiebat tamen, ex virginea Dei carne in animam suam diffundi quamdam admirabilem mansuetudinem cælestis Agni, simulque vegetari se inusitato robore ex nobilitate hujus nutrimenti, instaurari ac refici vires per quamdam velut novam transsubstantiationem sui spiritus in hoc divinum alimentum, suave, succosum, potentissimum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055858Z">
        <seg>He said, however, that from the virgin flesh of God a certain admirable meekness of the heavenly Lamb was diffused into his soul, and that he was at the same time invigorated with unusual strength from the nobility of this nourishment, his powers being restored and renewed by a kind of new transubstantiation of his spirit into this divine food, sweet, succulent, and most potent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aiebat, illustrationes vere divinas esse, cum in anima relinquunt humilitatem, affectum reverentiæ, agnitionem propriæ vilitatis, ordinem ac unitatem cordis, gaudiumque in Deo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052243Z">
        <seg>He said that the illustrations are truly divine, for they leave in the soul humility, a sense of reverence, an awareness of one's own worthlessness, order and unity of the heart, and joy in God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aiebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060521Z">
        <seg>He was saying.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alia minora præterire hic liceat, cum putem ista sufficere, ut de inimitabili hujus Virginis frugalitate, victusque parsimonia nil vulgare conjiciatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041401Z">
        <seg>Let other minor matters be passed over here, since we believe these suffice to show that nothing vulgar can be inferred about the incomparable frugality and abstemiousness of this Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alii hanc appellationem a figura cancri animalis desumunt, inter quos Castellus in Lexico medico tradit sequentia:&lt;/e1&gt; Venæ in ea parte manifeste apparent, sanguine nigro &amp; crasso plenæ atque tumentes, ita tumori circumpositæ, ut cancri figuram referre videantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061618Z">
        <seg>Others derive this appellation from the shape of the animal's crab, among whom Castellus in the Medical Lexicon relates the following: Veins in that part are clearly visible, full and swollen with thick black blood, so arranged around the tumor that they seem to form the shape of a crab.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alii propterea definitiones appellant, quales in re medica sunt aphorismi Hippocratis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060927Z">
        <seg>Others therefore call them definitions, such as the aphorisms of Hippocrates in medicine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aliis quoque vicibus Rosæ promissum fuit a Domino, numquam se permissurum, quod vel ad momentum dulci amicitiæ suæ fœdere excideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061953Z">
        <seg>On other occasions, too, the Lord had promised Rosa that he would never allow her to fall away from the bond of their sweet friendship, even for a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aliis, de morbi atrocitate sciscitantibus, aiebat, suave ac melleum esse, quidquid a manu tam dulcis Sponsi veniebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052649Z">
        <seg>To others, when they inquired about the severity of the illness, she would say that whatever came from the hand of such a sweet Spouse was sweet and honeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alio loco &amp; occasione in ædibus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051949Z">
        <seg>Elsewhere and on another occasion, in the house of D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Aliorum suasu, an casu incertum, semel Rosam in hortensi cellula degentem invisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060728Z">
        <seg>At the suggestion of others, or perhaps by chance, he once visited Rosa, who was living in a garden shed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alius itidem Rosæ confessarius meminerat dicere solitam, quod in sanctissima Communione sibi videbatur ipsummet e cælo solem in pectus recipere: nam quidquid sol visibilis operatur in mundo, dum luce &amp; calore omnia recreat, dum terram floribus &amp; fructibus obornat, dum maria unionibus, montium viscera gemmis ac pretiosis metallis ditat, dum aviculas cæli lætificat, plantas &amp; animalia vegetat, omnesque vasti hemisphærii angulos diffusa amœnitate collustrat, succendit, deaurat, id totum in nudis animæ suæ spatiis efficere dominicæ Carnis realem præsentiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055923Z">
        <seg>Another of Rose's confessors also remembered her saying that in the most holy Communion it seemed to her that she received the very sun from heaven into her breast: for whatever the visible sun does in the world, while it recreates all things with light and heat, while it adorns the earth with flowers and fruits, while it unites the seas, the bowels of the mountains with gems and precious metals, while it delights the little birds of heaven, quickens plants and animals, and illuminates, enlivens, and gilds with its diffused loveliness every corner of the vast hemisphere, the real presence of the Lord's Flesh accomplishes all this in the naked spaces of her soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alius rem ad lemures &amp; spectra imperite rejiciebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045707Z">
        <seg>Another ignorantly dismissed the matter as ghosts and spectres.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Allata est, ac oratorio domestico illata, ut ibi pretioso ornatu, quem commodato a diversis Rosa acceperat, exueretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055337Z">
        <seg>She was brought in and led into the private chapel so that there she might remove the precious adornment she had borrowed from various Rosas.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Allatam prudens ac pius Pater non sine horrore &amp; commiseratione intuitus, rigidiorem esse censuit, quam quæ illi sexui, ætatisque teneritudini conveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042316Z">
        <seg>The prudent and pious father, having viewed the offering with horror and compassion, judged it to be more rigid than was fitting for that sex and the tenderness of that age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alludebat forsitan supernæ (quam recolebat) visioni, qua Christus, increpanti blandientique medius, Rosam docuerat, velle se ab ipsa tam rigidis abstinentiæ exercitiis constanter honorari, sibi illius vitam ac valetudinem curæ cordique fore, nec diffidendum, quin ille, qui pro ea suam in cruce vitam prodegerat, qui extrema fusi pretiosissimi sanguinis gutta ipsam tam caro emerat, qui in ejus animam tot gratiæ thesauros, tantamque cælestium donorum opulentiam congesserat, demum &amp; terreno suo corpusculo id præstiturus sit, jamque præstiterit, quo carnis edulio carere possit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041138Z">
        <seg>He was perhaps alluding to the heavenly vision (which he recalled), in which Christ, standing between the scolding and the flattering, had taught Rosa that he wished to be constantly honored by her with such strict exercises of abstinence, that his life and health would be a matter of care and concern for her heart. nor was it to be doubted but that He, who for her had poured out His life on the cross, who with the last drop of His most precious blood had so dearly purchased her, who had heaped into her soul so many treasures of grace, and such an abundance of heavenly gifts, would at last, for her frail little body, grant—and had already granted—the power to abstain from the nourishment of flesh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alphonsi Velasquez, Ordinis Prædicatorum sui tunc confessarii (cui id P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040208Z">
        <seg>Alphonsi Velasquez, then his confessor of the Order of Preachers (to whom it was entrusted by P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alphonso Vasquez Rosæ confessario, ex tribus unica dumtaxat Rosæ hortulo mansit residua, siquidem is alteram poposcit sibi, alteram proregina dono ejusdem obtinuit, summaque cura penes se nutrire studuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054949Z">
        <seg>To Alphonso Vasquez, Rose's confessor, only one of the three remaining in the rose garden was left; for he had asked for one, and had received the other as a gift from the Queen Regent, and he strove with the utmost care to nurture it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alphonsum Velasquez, confessarium Virginis, admonuit, ut acerba reprehensione factum, velut excessum temerarium improbaret, ac Rosam a similibus ultra vires tentandis absterreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040630Z">
        <seg>He admonished Alphonsus Velasquez, the confessor of the Virgin, to condemn the act with a harsh rebuke as a rash excess, and to deter Rosa from attempting similar things beyond her strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alternantes concrepando,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045301Z">
        <seg>Resounding in alternation,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Alterum, quod in illo horrendo, solisque inexpertis parum formidabili obtenebrationum tormento ea fuerit Rosæ fortitudo, animositas, constantia, ut fragilis virgo non solum ferendo esset, verum etiam, præmissa spontanea resignatione, quasi cum Deo certaret, tristiusne ille affligere, an illa affligi vellet, dummodo per omnia divino beneplacito victoria ac triumphus remaneret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050724Z">
        <seg>The other, that in that horrible torment of obscurations, which was terrifying only to the inexperienced, that was Rose's strength, her spirit, her constancy, so that the frail virgin was not only to bear it, but, having first offered a voluntary resignation, as if she were contending with God, whether He would more grievously afflict, or she more willingly suffer, provided that in all things victory and triumph might remain with the divine will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Amabat hanc precandi formulam, quod in ea coaluissent mentalis simul &amp; vocalis oratio, singulæque hujus partes &amp; affectus, petitio, vox laudis, gratiarum actio &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045131Z">
        <seg>He loved this form of prayer because it combined mental and vocal prayer, and each of its parts and affections—petition, praise, thanksgiving, and so on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>An autem festum Pentecostes etiam apud ipsos interdum&lt;/e4&gt; Pascha rosarum &lt;e5&gt;dicatur, ex indigenis aut copioso eorum Dictionario, non ita pridem edi cœpto, discere cupimus&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035158Z">
        <seg>We wish to learn from the natives, or from their copious dictionary not long since begun to be published, whether the festival of Pentecost is also sometimes called the&lt;/e4&gt; Feast of Roses &lt;e5&gt;among them&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Angebatur Rosa, quod se sola nec rumpere noxiam catenam, nec sine clave seram diffibulare poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041718Z">
        <seg>Rosa was tormented because she alone could neither break the harmful chain nor unfasten the lock without a key.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Angebatur innocens Rosa, considerans perire se posse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061804Z">
        <seg>Innocent Rosa was accused, considering that she might perish;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anima mea sicut passer solitarius in tecto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050433Z">
        <seg>My soul is like a lone sparrow on the roof.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Animadvertit Rosa, patrem incredulum de eventu ambigere, ac grandi fiducia sic eumdem affata est: “Quid hæsitas, mi pater?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062622Z">
        <seg>Rosa noticed that her father was uncertain about the outcome and, with great confidence, addressed him thus: "What are you hesitating about, my father?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Animadvertit fortuitas crucis figuras esse, quas in projectis stipulis, aura vel casus effinxerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054853Z">
        <seg>He noticed that the accidental cross-shaped figures had been formed in the projected splinters by the wind or by chance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno MDCXV mense Augusto, circa litora Peruana in oceano Pacifico hostilis comparuit Batavorum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; classis, oramque totam improvisa exterruit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060140Z">
        <seg>In August of the year 1615, a hostile Dutch fleet appeared off the coasts of Peru in the Pacific Ocean and, with a surprise attack, terrified the entire shore.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno MDCXVI, favente &amp; auxiliante Jesu Christo, suaque benedicta Matre, incipio parare vestimentum dulcissimo meo Jesu nascituro, tremulo, nudo, egeno in Bethlehem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053548Z">
        <seg>In the year 1616, with the favor and help of Jesus Christ and His blessed Mother, I begin to prepare a garment for my sweetest Jesus, about to be born, trembling, naked, and poor in Bethlehem.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno MDCXVI, mense Augusto, Rosa B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055327Z">
        <seg>In the year 1616, in the month of August, Rosa B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno igitur MDCXVII, die XV Aprilis (qui Sabbatum erat) ad vesperam, circa pulsum angelicæ Salutationis, Rosa cum uxore Gundisalvi &amp; filiabus in prædicto oratorio de more insistebant orationi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053657Z">
        <seg>Therefore, in the year 1617, on the 15th of April (which was a Saturday) in the evening, around the ringing of the Angelus, Rosa, with Gundisalvi's wife and daughters, were insistently praying in the aforementioned oratory, as was their custom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno salutis MDCXXII (hoc est, quinquennio post Rosæ beatum ad superos abitum) fundatrice illustri &amp; opulenta vidua D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062415Z">
        <seg>In the year of our Lord 1622 (that is, five years after the blessed Rose's passing to heaven), by the illustrious and wealthy widow, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno ætatis quintodecimo votum emisit, toto vitæ suæ decursu abstinendi a carnibus, quoad permitterent ii, quibus obedire teneretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041017Z">
        <seg>At the age of fifteen he made a vow to abstain from meat for the rest of his life, so far as those to whom he was bound to obey would permit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Anno, qui Rosæ ultimus fuit, per totam Quadragesimam sole occiduo advolabat prope adversus Rosæ cubiculum avis parvula, sed mire vocalis ac sonora;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045217Z">
        <seg>In the year that was Rose's last, throughout all Lent a small bird would fly in at the setting sun near Rose's bedroom, but wonderfully vocal and sonorous;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Annuit Rosa, statimque ad præsentem imaginem, amica, qua solebat, familiaritate conversa, dixit: “Heus tu, gloriosa mater, num acerbam hujus feminæ afflictionem non vides?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055238Z">
        <seg>Rosa nodded, and immediately, turning to the woman before her with her usual friendly familiarity, said, "Hey you, glorious mother, don't you see this woman's bitter affliction?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Annuit Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050352Z">
        <seg>The Virgin nodded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Annuit confessarius, utpote sat conscius, quanta coram Domino spirituum ponderatore esset Virgo, cum qua de mutuo fœnore paciscebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060431Z">
        <seg>The confessor nodded, well aware of how great a weight the Virgin—with whom he was negotiating a mutual loan—would be before the Lord, the weigher of spirits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Annuit ergo oblato fœderi castissima turtur, ac data utrimque maritali fide, gratiosus ille Sponsus, tamquam necessarium iter peracturus discessit, prius tamen novæ suæ Consorti aliquot ibidem marmora certo numero incidenda, formanda, polienda commisit, dum peregre rediret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044154Z">
        <seg>So the most chaste turtle dove assented to the proposed marriage, and with the marital vow exchanged on both sides, that gracious Spouse departed, as though about to undertake a necessary journey, but not before he entrusted his new Consort with the task of carving, shaping, and polishing a certain number of marbles there, until his return from abroad.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Annuit voto meo Inspector cordium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040831Z">
        <seg>The Inspector of Hearts granted my wish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Antonio Rodriguez prædicatore generali, Rosa cum aliis fidelibus adgeniculabat cancello altaris, Eucharistiam receptura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055752Z">
        <seg>While Antonio Rodriguez, the preacher general, was preaching, Rosa, along with other faithful, knelt at the altar rail to receive the Eucharist.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Antonius Rodriquez, titulo, gradu, officio prædicator generalis: ad hunc conversa Virgo, pari tum fervore tum fiducia (quam regebant modestia &amp; charitas) sic illum affata est: “Adverte, o Pater, quod divina clementia te esse concionatorem voluit, ut perditos ac obstipos peccatores ad frugem reducas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060549Z">
        <seg>Anthony Rodriguez, by title, rank, and office the preacher general: to him the Virgin turned, with equal fervor and confidence (guided by modesty and charity), and thus addressed him: "Mark well, O Father, that divine clemency has willed you to be a preacher, so that you may bring lost and obstinate sinners back to the right path."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Apage, sat nugarum, ad Calendas Græcas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; hæc impleta videbimus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062839Z">
        <seg>Away, enough of nonsense, we will see these fulfilled on Greek Calends.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Apud Grammaticos autem accipitur&lt;/e3&gt; crasis &lt;e4&gt;pro contractione duarum syllabarum in unam coalescentium, &amp; ea vox originem habet a verbo&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;κεράννυμι&lt;/s5&gt;, &lt;e6&gt;quod&lt;/e6&gt; misceo &lt;e7&gt;vel&lt;/e7&gt; tempero &lt;e8&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045918Z">
        <seg>Among grammarians, however, crasis is taken to be the contraction of two syllables coalescing into one, and the word has its origin in the verb kerañnuimi, which means to mix or to temper.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Arripuit Virgo postrema hæc verba, statimque nec palmo illius telæ retento sibi, totam donavit secreto duabus virginibus tam genere, quam virtute nobilibus, at pauperrimis, quasque noverat, clam domi ingentem rerum omnium penuriam pati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061042Z">
        <seg>The virgin seized upon these last words and, without even keeping the palm of that cloth for herself, immediately gave the whole thing in secret to two virgins, noble both by birth and virtue, yet exceedingly poor, whom she knew to be secretly enduring a tremendous scarcity of everything at home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Assedere primo examini, honestatis gratia, Rosæ mater &amp; D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050231Z">
        <seg>To sit for the first exam, for the sake of honor, Rosa's mother &amp; D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Catharina: “Dic potius hostes, non hospites, ecce enim quam sanguine meo plenus sit iste culex”, Replicuit illa: “Quid vero magni est, tantillum animalculum nostro pavisse sanguine, dum ejus Conditor nos suomet toties pascit cruore?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043903Z">
        <seg>But Catherine said, "Call them enemies rather than guests, for behold how this mosquito is full of my blood." She replied, "What great matter is it that we have fed this tiny creature with our blood, when its Creator, who sucks us so often, feeds on our very gore?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Filiæ suam unice eremum amanti molestissimum erat, tam videre, quam videri in publico;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043314Z">
        <seg>But for his only daughter, who loved the wilderness, it was most troublesome to see or be seen in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Rosa, quod certo sciret, hanc calamitatem non esse a corpore, tristabatur gravius, neminem se invenire, qui vel morbum intelligeret, vel in tantæ perplexitatis charybdi cynosuram aliquam ostenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045716Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, knowing for certain that this calamity was not bodily, was all the more distressed, finding no one who could either understand the illness or point out any anchor in so great a whirlpool of perplexity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Rosa, quod loco movere se neutiquam posset, erubescens, unaque dissimulans, tentabat subinde, si quo modo se tanti prodigii secretissimo retinaculo explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040118Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, since she could by no means move from her place, blushing and at the same time dissembling, would from time to time try to see if she could somehow explain herself by the most secret restraint of such a prodigy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Virgini Pæana&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt; hæc erant debellatæ tentationis, quam ita fregit, ut numquam redierit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203511Z">
        <seg>But for the Virgin Pæana, these were the vanquished temptations, which she so crushed that they never returned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Virgini Pæana&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; hæc erant debellatæ tentationis, quam ita fregit, ut numquam redierit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040406Z">
        <seg>But for the Virgin Pæana, these were the vanquished temptations, which she so crushed that they never returned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Virgini placuit secum disquirere, primo an secundo lusu plus lucri fecisset, cum longe ardentius faveret Sponso, quam sibimet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051034Z">
        <seg>But it pleased Virginia to inquire with herself, whether she had made more profit in the first or second game, since she was far more ardently partisan to the Groom than to herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Virgo id totum gloriæ crucis tribuebat, quod &amp; S. Catharinam Senensem jugiter fecisse meminerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055008Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin attributed the whole of it to the glory of the cross, since she remembered that St. Catherine of Siena had always done the same.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At Virgo piorum rhythmorum ex tempore ferax, odei proscenium claudebat hoc fere epilogo:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045329Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin, fertile in the moment with pious rhythms, closed the Odean stage with this nearly epilogue:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At conantibus intercessit Christus, officiumque, velut angelico sublimius arbitrio, sibimet assumens, manibus propriis stateram levavit in æquilibrio, &amp; de cumulis impositis afflictiones distribuit præsentibus animabus, inter quas &amp; mihi portionem seposuit gravissimæ adversitatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052758Z">
        <seg>But Christ interposed for those who were striving, and, assuming the office as though by a loftier, angelic decree, lifted the scales into balance with his own hands, and from the heaps laid upon them distributed afflictions to the souls present, among which he set aside for me the portion of the gravest adversity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At demum certa se Dei amicitiam non violasse, animadvertit, quo tenderet illa ambiguæ explorationis occulta industria: unde præfata veniam, modeste replicuit: “Juste moneor, cum metu &amp; tremore salutem meam operari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062009Z">
        <seg>But at last, certain that she had not violated God's friendship, she perceived where that covert industry of ambiguous exploration was tending; so, having first asked for leave, she modestly replied, "I am rightly admonished to work out my salvation with fear and trembling."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At displicuit, patere spatiosum ac nobile pratum cuilibet viatori;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062551Z">
        <seg>But it was displeasing that the spacious and noble meadow was open to any traveler.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At eidem placuit electam Rosam de S. Maria peculiariter per seraphicæ Catharinæ&lt;a1&gt;a&lt;/a1&gt; compendia ducere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203457Z">
        <seg>But it pleased her to lead the chosen Rose of St. Mary in a special way through the seraphic compendia of Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At eidem placuit electam Rosam de S. Maria peculiariter per seraphicæ Catharinæ&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; compendia ducere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035926Z">
        <seg>But it pleased her to lead the chosen Rose of St. Mary in a special way through the seraphic compendia of Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At exinde sensit, simul oratione Virginis, &amp; angelica tutela se repente destitui: nam Truxillum petens, in ejus convallibus gravissima quæque passus est, &amp; vix evasit incolumis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051846Z">
        <seg>But from then on he felt, along with the Virgin's intercession, that he had suddenly been deprived of angelic protection: for, on his way to Truxillo, he endured the gravest hardships in its valleys and barely escaped unharmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At hic videtur, nomen illud usurpari pro clivis aut lateribus montium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060905Z">
        <seg>But here it seems that that name is used for slopes or the sides of mountains.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At hoc commodius miraculorum historiæ reservetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035821Z">
        <seg>But this is more appropriately reserved for the history of miracles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At huic cum accedebat corporaliter is, qui ignem venit mittere in terram, non aliter atque inter Seraphim videbatur sibi Rosa, ceu tota empyrea ambulare in medio lapidum ignitorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055845Z">
        <seg>But when she drew near in person to the one who came to send fire on the earth, she appeared to herself no differently than among the Seraphim—like a rose walking amid burning stones in the very heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At hæc importunitatem rata, interdum cum bile ac nausea rejiciebat filiam ad muliebris officinæ domesticum armarium, vociferans, tot interpellationibus opus non esse, familiaris officii instrumenta ultro patere feminis laboraturis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035554Z">
        <seg>But deeming this importunity intolerable, she would sometimes, with bile and nausea, send her daughter back to the women's domestic workshop, shouting that there was no need for so many interruptions, since the tools of the household trade were freely available to women willing to work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At ipsa in summis periculis summe secura, accinebat cælesti Sponso: “Non timebo mala, quoniam tu mecum es”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062158Z">
        <seg>But she herself, supremely secure amid the greatest dangers, sang to her heavenly Spouse: "I will not fear any evil, for you are with me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At jam certior facta penuriæ, nihilque territa, brevi labiorum motu visa est rem Deo commendare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062213Z">
        <seg>But now, made certain of the scarcity and undaunted, she seemed with a brief movement of her lips to commend the matter to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At parco rubori tuo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060642Z">
        <seg>But I spare your blushes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At postea ex crebriori ejusdem prodigii successu didicit, Rosam esse, cui ad sacratissimæ Hostiæ contactum interior subito micabat amoris rogus, tam vividus ac potens, ut vel exteriorem faciem mutaret in facem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055758Z">
        <seg>But later, from the more frequent occurrence of the same prodigy, he learned that it was Rosa, in whom, upon contact with the most sacred Host, a flame of love would suddenly blaze within, so vivid and powerful that it would even change her outward appearance into a flame.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At pressius, &amp; seriatim currendum per singula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062501Z">
        <seg>More precisely, each item must be gone through in order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At præterea publicarum miserta calamitatum, satagebat his modis ad exemplar suæ magistræ placare Numinis iram, mitigare justitiam: unde nunc pro ærumnis totius S. Matris Ecclesiæ, nunc pro angustiis periclitantis reipublicæ, cruentam sese victimam cædebat inclementissime, ut clementiam impetraret, plagisque plagas averteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041516Z">
        <seg>Moreover, moved by pity for the public calamities, she strove by these means, following the example of her mistress, to appease the wrath of the Deity and to soften His justice. Hence, now for the sufferings of the whole Holy Mother Church, now for the straits of the imperiled republic, she most mercilessly slaughtered herself as a bloody victim, that she might win clemency and turn away the scourges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At quando præ nimio aut convulsionum laniatu, aut per symptomatum periculosam violentiam dissimulari non poterant, ingenue, quantis distabesceret cruciatibus, fatebatur, ut ex dolorum suorum immanitate æstimarent homines, quam foret Rosa coram Deo enormis ac peccatrix, quæ videlicet assidue tot scelerum justissimis vindiciis ab irato Numine meruisset flagellari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040527Z">
        <seg>But when, from too much or the tearing of convulsions, or when they could no longer be concealed by the dangerous violence of her symptoms, she would frankly confess how grievously she was tormented, so that from the immensity of her sufferings men might judge how enormously sinful and guilty Rosa was before God, who, it was said, had justly deserved to be continually scourged by the wrathful Deity for so many crimes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At quis tam crudelem equuleum apparasset innocentulæ jamjam morituræ?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042958Z">
        <seg>But who would have devised such a cruel little horse for the innocent little one who was already dying?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At rosis præcipue nec tutum, nec decorum est, spineo carere sepimento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042040Z">
        <seg>But for roses in particular, it is neither safe nor proper to be without a thorny hedge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At semper mecum permanens, sit benedictus Deus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045345Z">
        <seg>May God be blessed, who always remains with me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At sive incuria, &amp; errore properantis sacristæ, sive (quod potius crediderim) singulari Numinis consilio, accidit, quod hac vice Rosa sine ramo fuit, omnino quidem præter morem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044244Z">
        <seg>But whether through negligence, and the error of a hasty sacristan, or (what I am more inclined to believe) by a singular design of Providence, it happened that on this occasion the Rose was without a stem, altogether contrary to custom;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At subjecit Virgo, nunc nunc redire se a pedibus Magnæ Deiparæ, huic rem cordi fuisse, hujus benedictione, &amp; materno auspicio suam animam transisse in Rosam florido Jesulo Nazareno sacram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034801Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin added that she had just now returned from the feet of the Great Mother of God, that this had been dear to her heart, and that with her blessing and maternal auspices, her soul had passed into the Rose sacred to the flourishing Jesus of Nazareth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>At vicissim angelorum Regina Famulam suam eo honore dignata est, ut fere officia cubiculariæ ipsi exhibuerit, mane Virginem excitando a somno.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051307Z">
        <seg>But in turn the Queen of the Angels honored her handmaid with such distinction that she almost performed the duties of a chambermaid for her, waking the Virgin from sleep each morning.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attamen &amp; pauper hic thalamus ei videbatur sapere delicias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042917Z">
        <seg>Yet even this poor bed seemed to him to savor of luxury.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attamen Euphemia ægram suam domesticis anxie commendans, ad Rosam perrexit, communi pietatis officio suam operam adjunctura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055225Z">
        <seg>However, Euphemia, anxiously entrusting her sick sister to her household staff, went to Rosa to lend her assistance in the common duty of charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attamen majestatem Deiparæ paulo diutius obtutui suo solitam indulgeri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050551Z">
        <seg>However, the majesty of the Mother of God is accustomed to indulge her gaze for a little longer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attamen per diem hosce truncos exemptiles seorsim occultabat sub thoro, ne mater aut ex inæqualitate straguli hos deprehenderet, aut palam expositos traheret in legitimæ suspicionis argumentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042552Z">
        <seg>However, during the day he would hide these stolen logs separately under the mattress, so that his mother would neither discover them from the uneven spread of the bedding nor, if he left them out in the open, use them as grounds for legitimate suspicion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attamen quibusdam diebus velut per rimulam irrepebat afflictæ lentum tantillæ spei crepusculum, quo utcumque suspicaretur, hoc animi supplicium fine [non] cariturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045617Z">
        <seg>Yet on certain days a faint twilight of hope would creep in like a sliver, which, however she might look at it, she sensed would not be the end of this torment of the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attonita spectaculo, nec ausa propius accedere, misit qui maritum domo forte absentem citissime advocaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053750Z">
        <seg>Stunned by the spectacle, and not daring to approach any closer, she sent for her husband, who was fortunately away from home, to be summoned back as quickly as possible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Attulit illa coronam suam, ne quidem tantillo sibi indulto spatio, quo notas sanguinis inter cuspidum acumina satis abstergeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042313Z">
        <seg>She brought her crown, not even granting herself the briefest moment to wipe the drops of blood from between the points of the spikes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Auctor ad hanc Cypriæ deæ seu Veneris&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;e1&gt;fabulam alludit.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043959Z">
        <seg>The author alludes to this myth of the Cypriot goddess or Venus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Auctore R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211724Z">
        <seg>By R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Audebat &amp; Rosa sub tali advocato liberius præoccupare medicos, ne, quam sanam vellent, carnium edendarum objectu trucidarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041132Z">
        <seg>Audebat &amp; Rosa, under such an advocate, felt freer to urge the doctors not to slaughter them for the sake of eating their flesh, as healthy as they wished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Audebat cum eodem cantillare jucundum hoc celeusma: &lt;b4&gt;Nunc dimittis&lt;/b4&gt;, velut quæ portum suorum desideriorum jam pene attigisset, fruitura deinceps secretis amplexibus infantis Sponsuli, qui ducit in solitudinem, locuturus ad cor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043627Z">
        <seg>She would joyfully sing along with him this pleasant hymn: "Now You Dismiss," as if she had already nearly reached the harbor of her desires, to enjoy from then on the secret embraces of her infant Spouse, who leads into solitude to speak to the heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Audi candidissimæ simplicitatis responsum, &amp; obstupesce.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043726Z">
        <seg>Hear the response of the most candid simplicity, and be amazed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Audiit moribunda, deque veritate scrupulosius solicita, attenuatum spiritum collegit in vocem: “Non (inquit) erremus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053347Z">
        <seg>Hearing she was dying, and more scrupulously concerned about the truth, she gathered her weakened spirit into her voice: "Let us not err," she said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Audivit hæc foris mater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053743Z">
        <seg>The mother heard these things outside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>August - Ökumenisches Heiligenlexikon</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211718Z">
        <seg>August - Ecumenical Dictionary of Saints</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Auscultandis vero de ineffabili hoc mysterio solemnibus panegyricis nusquam satiari poterat, illosque semel auditos, pene verbatim post annorum interstitia recitabat mira felicitate memoriæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060102Z">
        <seg>He could never get his fill of listening to the solemn panegyrics on this ineffable mystery, and once he had heard them, he would recite them almost verbatim after an interval of years, with remarkable felicity of memory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Auxilium offerebat quæstor: admisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044653Z">
        <seg>The questor offered help: he accepted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Avicula me deserit, subit Succentor meus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045341Z">
        <seg>The little bird deserts me, suddenly my Succentor appears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Bartholomæus Lobo Guerrero&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, capiendis legitimis informationibus judicem constituit doctorem Joannem de la Rocca, parochum &amp; archidiaconum metropolitanum, qui coram notario Jacobo Blanca presbytero, auditis omnium (qui spectaculo interfuerant) unisonis testimoniis, rei gestæ seriem ex ordine totam excepit, ac tabellioni rite in protocollum referendam commisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053904Z">
        <seg>Bartholomew Lobo Guerrero&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, To take the legal depositions, he appointed Doctor John de la Rocca, parish priest and metropolitan archdeacon, who, before the notary James Blanca, priest, and having heard the unanimous testimonies of all who had been present at the spectacle, took down the entire sequence of events in order and committed it to the clerk to be duly entered in the protocol.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Beneficium tam repentinum totas ædes excitavit in securæ gratulationis lætitiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054025Z">
        <seg>Such a sudden blessing stirred the entire household to secure congratulations in joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Bernardus Marques, dum adhuc novitius sacrificantibus in sacello Rosarii subinde ministrabat, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alius externum ardorem sentiret.]&lt;/s2&gt; ac de more post sacerdotem ablutionis scyphum communicantibus circumferebat, quotiescumque coram Rosa cum scypho substitit, sensit a Virgine tamquam e clibano aut fornace vaporare ardores, ut interdum putarit sibi manum comburi, qua pateram seu porrigebat, seu resumebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055836Z">
        <seg>Bernard Marques, while still a novice, would from time to time minister to the priests celebrating Mass in the chapel of the Rosary, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; another would feel an external ardor.]and according to custom, after the priest had washed his hands, he would pass the chalice to those receiving Communion. Whenever he stood before the Rosary altar with the chalice, he felt as if ardors were steaming from the Virgin as from a kiln or furnace, so that at times he thought his hand was being burned by the paten, whether he was offering it or receiving it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Biographus alibi sæpius adhibet alias hujusmodi allusiones &amp; antitheses, quas eruditus lector facile observabit, quamvis de illis expreße non moneatur.&lt;/e8&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T045858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035846Z">
        <seg>The biographer elsewhere frequently employs other such allusions and antitheses, which the learned reader will easily observe, although he is not expressly warned about them.&lt;/e8&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Blasio Martinez tradidit, recta ad confessarium deferendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041758Z">
        <seg>He handed Blasio Martinez over to be taken straight to the confessor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Breviter ac modeste respondit Virgo “Satius foret, o mater, &amp; me cæcam esse, quam vana &amp; secularia affatim videre”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043432Z">
        <seg>The Virgin replied briefly and modestly, "It would be better, O mother, for me to be blind than to see vain and worldly things in abundance."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
