      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>CAPUT XXV.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062405Z">
        <seg>Chapter 25</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cadaveri adgemuit Rosa, &amp; abiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051220Z">
        <seg>Rosa kissed the corpse and left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Candelam igitur ultro extinxit ipsa, statimque latentem inimicum e sporta in arenam provocans “Heus tu (inquit) porce scabiose, adesdum, hic te expecto: prodi, si audes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052054Z">
        <seg>She therefore promptly extinguished the candle herself, and immediately challenging the hidden enemy from the basket into the arena, said, "Hey you, scabby pig, come on out, I'm waiting for you here: come out, if you dare."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Canonizata est a Benedicto PP.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035120Z">
        <seg>She was canonized by Pope Benedict.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Canonizata est a Benedicto PP. XIII.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T165635Z">
        <seg>She was canonized by Pope Benedict.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Capta fuit &amp; vastata ab Anglis anno 1595 &amp; iterum a Belgis unitis anno 1615.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035133Z">
        <seg>It was captured and plundered by the English in 1595 and again by the United Provinces in 1615.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Carebat inops vidua dote legitima, quæ quater mille libris argenti in singulas ibidem velandas erat diffinita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062846Z">
        <seg>The poor widow was deprived of her legitimate dowry, which had been fixed at four thousand pounds of silver for each of her weddings there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Castillo, &lt;s2&gt;[actiones S. Rosæ examinarunt,]&lt;/s2&gt; aut saltem non inferiorem obtinebat sanctimoniæ, publicæque opinionis gradum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050126Z">
        <seg>Castillo, [examined the actions of St. Rose,] or at least held her in no lower degree of sanctity and public esteem.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Castillus, integro trium horarum spatio, Virginem in domestico cellulæ horto, &lt;s2&gt;[quorum unus, Joannes Castillus,]&lt;/s2&gt; velut indagine cinctam, per varias quæstiones distinuit, animæque venis diligentissime pertentatis, omnia sana ac salva invenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050240Z">
        <seg>Castillo, over the course of a full three hours, questioned the Virgin in the domestic garden of her cell, &lt;s2&gt;[one of whom was John Castillo,]&lt;/s2&gt; as if it were surrounded by a siege, and after diligently probing the very veins of her soul, found everything sound and safe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Casus erat: germanum Rosæ fratrem ob puerile delictum pater quærebat ad virgidemiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042223Z">
        <seg>It happened that the father was seeking out Rose's brother for a boyish prank, to make him a eunuch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharina peteret, continuo impetraturam, idque patebit infra;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052550Z">
        <seg>Catherine would ask, and would immediately receive it, and this will be shown below.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensi, in certamine haud quidquam dispari, contigerunt, quando videlicet huic respondit Christus: “Dum pugnabas, dum vincebas, intus eram, in tuo corde spectator aderam &amp; fidus adjutor”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052228Z">
        <seg>To Catherine of Siena, in a contest by no means unequal, it happened when Christ answered her: "While you were fighting, while you were winning, I was within, in your heart I was a spectator and a faithful helper."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis Discipulam, in schola patientiæ ad magistræ suæ prototypon laureari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042034Z">
        <seg>A disciple of Catherine of Siena, in the school of patience, is crowned with the prototype of her teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis Rosa didicerat, virginem seraphicam, adhuc mortalem, Christo Sponso fuisse mirabiliter subarrhatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044021Z">
        <seg>Rosa of Siena had learned that the seraphic virgin, still mortal, had been wondrously betrothed to Christ as her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis devota secularium confraternitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055017Z">
        <seg>A secular confraternity devoted to Catherine of Siena;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis fuisse germanam discipulam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061426Z">
        <seg>To have been a direct disciple of Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis monasterium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062629Z">
        <seg>The Monastery of Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis tractabantur gloriosæ prærogativæ &amp; justa encomia, sensim ad futurum sub ejus tutelari nomine monasterium devolutus est sermo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062703Z">
        <seg>The glorious prerogatives and just encomiums of Catherine of Siena were being discussed, and gradually the conversation turned to the future monastery under her patronage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ Senensis vias deduci ad Sponsi unionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052531Z">
        <seg>May Catherine of Siena's ways lead to the union of the Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ iconem, in præcipua S. Patris Dominici solemnitate circumferendam, de more vestierat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055331Z">
        <seg>He had dressed the image of Catherine, to be carried in the principal solemnity of St. Dominic, according to custom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ impensius commendavit, rogans, ut ipsam tueretur ab imminenti periculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055306Z">
        <seg>He earnestly recommended Catherine, asking that she be protected from the imminent danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Catharinæ in faciem Rosæ transiisse lumina, quæ hanc illi per aliqua oris lineamenta propemodum assimilarint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051440Z">
        <seg>Catherine's eyes had crossed over into Rose's face, which by certain features of her mouth had almost made her resemble her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Causam rogata, aliam dabat, nimirum hac ratione se mederi somnolentiæ, quam aquæ præsertim frigidæ haustus provocaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041358Z">
        <seg>When asked the cause, he gave another, namely that he was treating his drowsiness in this way, which was brought on by drinking water, especially cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cella erat penuaria, ubi tunc instrumenta vindemiæ, canistra, lebetes, &amp; id genus utensilia asservabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052029Z">
        <seg>It was a storeroom, where at that time the tools of the vintage, baskets, cauldrons, and such utensils were stored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe dignus erat hac Rosa Carmelus, digna Carmelo Rosa, nisi hanc Ordini hortulano Rosarii altior zephyrus commendasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040329Z">
        <seg>Surely Carmel was worthy of this rose, and the rose worthy of Carmel, had not a loftier zephyr commended it to the gardener of the Order of the Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe hoc postea uno ore fassi sunt confessarii, pluries laborandum sibi fuisse, ut, in tanta innocentissimæ vitæ puritate, scrutando reperirent, quod ad pœnitentiæ forum certo spectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040648Z">
        <seg>Certainly, the confessors later admitted in unison that they had had to work repeatedly so that, in the purity of such an innocent life, by scrutinizing they might find something that clearly pertained to the tribunal of penance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe nil excogitari potuisset aut pro capitis (ut appellant medici) pia matre magis pium, aut duræ matri durius&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;, nisi hanc duritiem ipsa pietas suggessisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000952Z">
        <seg>Certainly nothing could have been devised either more pious than a loving mother toward her children (as physicians call them), or more severe than a harsh mother, unless this very severity had been suggested by piety itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe nil excogitari potuisset aut pro capitis (ut appellant medici) pia matre magis pium, aut duræ matri durius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, nisi hanc duritiem ipsa pietas suggessisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042118Z">
        <seg>Certainly nothing could have been devised either more pious than a loving mother for her children (as physicians call them) or more severe than a harsh mother, unless this very severity had been suggested by piety itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe non erravit, quicumque ille fuit, puerum qui pinxit amorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044405Z">
        <seg>Surely he did not err, whoever he was, the boy who painted love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Certe non nisi thalamum pollicebatur cellula, quam sibi designabat Rosa, longumque putabat, exspectare diei sequentis diluculum, quo opus inchoaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043633Z">
        <seg>The cell that Rosa had set aside for herself promised nothing more than a bed, and she thought it would be long before the next day's dawn, when she would begin her work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cessa, videamus manum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055427Z">
        <seg>Stop, let's see the hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cessit hujus compellationis dulcedini somnus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051406Z">
        <seg>Sleep yielded to the sweetness of this compulsion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum de miris deliciis, favoribus, officiis, quibus hæc seraphica magistra demulsit Rosam suam, crebior &amp; commodior infra redibit sermo locis propriis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051450Z">
        <seg>As for the wondrous delights, favors, and offices by which this seraphic teacher soothed her Rose, a more frequent and convenient discussion will return below in its proper places.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum de valore hujus monetæ, quam Galli&lt;/e3&gt; un ecu &lt;e4&gt;vocant, Cangius in auctiore Glossario ad vocem&lt;/e4&gt; pataco &lt;e5&gt;consuli potest&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055516Z">
        <seg>As for the value of this coin, which the French call an ecu, Cangius can be consulted in his larger glossary under the entry "pataco."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ceterum pro culpis alienis, pro compensandis peccatorum in Dei majestatem injuriis, pro eorundem conversione ad frugem meliorem, dici non potest, quot flagellorum sulcis, quot vibicum livoribus seipsam indies exararit: nesciebat ferias quotidianus feriendi labor &amp; dolor, quem sic dispensabat per diversas corporis partes, ut dum unius plagis detumescendi &amp; coalescendi dabantur vices, transiretur ad alteram, ab hac vero postridie reditus fieret ad priorem vixdum coalitam: porro hæc verberum alterna, sed cruda vicissitudo tanto dirius cruciabat, quanto acrior sensus inerat partibus pridie læsis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041525Z">
        <seg>However, for the sins of others, to atone for the insults to God's majesty, and for their conversion to a better path, it cannot be said how many furrows of scourges, how many bruises of rods she has plowed into herself daily: The daily labor and pain of being whipped did not know respite, for it was distributed so over the various parts of the body that, while one set of lashes was given time to swell and heal, it passed to another, and the next day it returned from that to the first, which had scarcely healed: Moreover, this alternating but cruel succession of blows tormented all the more terribly, the keener the sensation in the parts wounded the day before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cilicia, flagella &amp; catenæ, quibus sancta Virguncula tenerum corpus afflixit</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041457Z">
        <seg>Cilices, scourges &amp; chains, with which the holy Virgin afflicted her tender body</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Circuivit tamen cum reliquo fideli populo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044253Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless went around with the rest of the faithful people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Citatus Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043144Z">
        <seg>Citation: Latin, Book</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Collaudabo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045252Z">
        <seg>I will praise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Collaudemus Dominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045239Z">
        <seg>Let us praise the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Collegit illa, quas potuit, sinuque se pleno stitit divino Puerulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061900Z">
        <seg>She gathered what she could and, with her arms full, stopped the divine little boy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Colligito, lector, ex tam atroci remedio, quam fuerit juvenculæ importuna somni vexatio: in lecto, muricibus ac tribulis, foris autem clavis pugnandum fuit, &amp; Rosæ seu dormire vellet, seu vigilare, tormenta subeunda erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043106Z">
        <seg>You, reader, can gather from such a dreadful remedy how severe the young woman's unrelenting sleep disturbance must have been: in bed she was beset by mice and thorns, and outside she had to contend with keys; whether Rose wished to sleep or to stay awake, she had to endure torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Comparebat velut altera in civitate peccatrix, verecundabatur, tundebat præcordia, trepidabat quasi jamjam erebi rictu absorbenda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040642Z">
        <seg>She appeared like another sinner in the city, was ashamed, beat her breast, and trembled as if about to be swallowed by the gaping maw of hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compatiebatur Rosa communi tot capitum necessitati, &amp; secretissimo instinctu jussa sperare optime de Dei providentia, dixit matri: “Si mandas, descendam ego in nomine Domini, visura num quid residui mellis inveniam”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062229Z">
        <seg>Compatient with the common need for so many heads, and by a most secret instinct commanded to hope for the best in God's providence, she said to her mother, "If you command, I will go down in the name of the Lord to see if I can find any honey left."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compendium id fuit, quo Rosa celeri adjuta miraculo, nec deesset operi misericordiæ, nec in alienis ædibus præter Virginum morem diutius hæreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061510Z">
        <seg>It was a shortcut by which Rosa, swiftly aided by a miracle, would neither be lacking in the work of charity nor linger longer in a stranger's house than is the custom of virgins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Compertum est, sub hoc densissimo pungentis metalli vepreto, ipsam loquelam Virgini fuisse dolorificam: quid si tussi concutiebatur pectus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042133Z">
        <seg>It is known that, under this densest thicket of piercing metal, the Virgin's very speech was painful: what if her chest was shaken by a cough?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabantur pulte liquidiori supplere lactis inopiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052432Z">
        <seg>They tried to make up for the lack of milk with thinner porridge.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur ad amussim parere Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035712Z">
        <seg>The Virgin tried to obey to the letter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur inde venari tristissimum solatium, quod putabat sub his cruciatibus se sensim interituram: neque enim videbantur tales, qui sine totali destructione fragilis creaturæ subsistere diu possent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045555Z">
        <seg>She tried from this to draw the saddest consolation, thinking that under these torments she would slowly perish: for they did not seem to be of a kind that could long endure without the total destruction of the frail creature.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conabatur intellectus vel minimas Deitatis arripere stricturas&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, sed omne lumen diffugerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045509Z">
        <seg>He tried to grasp, even in the smallest degree, the strictures of the Deity, but all light had fled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conata quidem hæc fuit semel iterumque Filiam a tantæ austeritatis excessu divertere, &amp; lectum immanem frustatim dissipare, sed immisso divinitus conscientiæ stimulo, nec manum ausa est adhibere, nec imperium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042706Z">
        <seg>She indeed tried again and again to turn her daughter away from such an excess of austerity and to tear the enormous bed to pieces, but, divinely stirred by a promptings of conscience, she dared neither to lay a hand on it nor to command it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Concremer ego, deficiam ego, consumar ego igne tui divini amoris, o mi benignissime Jesu”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053117Z">
        <seg>I am consumed, I shall fail, I shall be consumed by the fire of your divine love, O my most benign Jesus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conditio autem hæc erat, quod Rosa interea sui confessarii febres in se reciperet, prout olim sanctæ Catharinæ Senensi contigit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053511Z">
        <seg>The condition, however, was that Rosa should in the meantime contract her confessor's fevers, just as once happened to Saint Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confessarii, quo penitus animæ statum nosse cœperant, facilius hanc ei frequentiam indulgebant, unanimiter attestantes in processu, tam limpidam se invenisse in ejus spiritu munditiem, tamque nitidam innocentiæ puritatem, tantam insuper Eucharistiæ famem, tam robustum devotionis calorem, ut exinde numquam ausi fuerint a participatione tanti mysterii Virginem distinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055705Z">
        <seg>The confessors, having thus come to know the state of the soul thoroughly, more readily indulged this frequency for her, unanimously testifying in the process, that they had found in her spirit such limpid purity, such radiant innocence, such a hunger for the Eucharist, and such a robust fervor of devotion, that from then on they had never dared to withhold from the Virgin participation in so great a mystery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confessarius nil differendum esse edixit, iret, properaret, gossipium apponeret, seseque divino favori submitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054004Z">
        <seg>The confessor decreed that nothing was to be postponed: go, hurry, prepare the gossiper, and submit yourself to divine favor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim Virgo, arrepta occasione, gravem ac nervosum de tranquillitate animi sermonem instituit, breves ac salubres acquirendæ mansuetudinis aphorismos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; præscripsit, æquanimitatis ac patientiæ apta consilia ingessit, sicque instructam a se dimisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060732Z">
        <seg>Immediately the Virgin, seizing the opportunity, launched into a serious and nerve-racking discourse on the tranquility of the mind, prescribed brief and salutary aphorisms for acquiring gentleness, offered fitting counsel on equanimity and patience, and thus dismissed her, having been instructed by them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim igitur ad Virginem Matrem obsecrando conversa rogavit anxie, periculo succurreret, ipsumque instrumentum in altero locorum, quos ipsa mente designabat, reconderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040909Z">
        <seg>Immediately, therefore, she turned in supplication to the Virgin Mother and anxiously begged her to come to the rescue in the peril and to conceal the very instrument in another of the places she had in mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confestim jacitur alea&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, favet sors Virgini, lucratur, vincit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051015Z">
        <seg>Immediately the die is cast, fortune favors the Virgin, she gains, she wins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confidit illa in homine mortali, didicitque ad illius fragilem assistentiam tantisper solitos metus ponere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062105Z">
        <seg>She trusted in a mortal man, and learned to set aside her usual fears for the time being for his fragile assistance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Confugit Rosa familiari fiducia ad cælestem Sponsum adjutorem in opportunitatibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051111Z">
        <seg>Rose, with familiar trust, fled to the heavenly Spouse for help in times of need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Congredere, cornuta bellua, eia congredere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052107Z">
        <seg>Come on, you horned beast, come on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conjectabat se a variegato papilione non solum ad concolorem Senensis Catharinæ habitum, verum etiam insigniri eodem emblemate cordis, quod olim cum sponsa Senensi Sponsus æternus permutarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044129Z">
        <seg>He conjectured that he would be distinguished not only by the multicolored butterfly but also by the same emblem of the heart that the Eternal Spouse had once exchanged with his Sienese bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conjectarunt subito, quod erat, permagnum aliquid actitari, velut sub iride Rosarii, cujus mysteria triplici hac tinctura distinguuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040357Z">
        <seg>They suddenly conjectured that something very great was being enacted, as if under the rainbow of the Rosary, whose mysteries are distinguished by this triple tint.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conjicio, te nunc in exornando gloriosæ matris Senensis simulachro occupari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055049Z">
        <seg>I suppose you are now busy decorating the statue of the glorious mother of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Consederat solitaria in horto domestico colligendis floribus intenta Virgo, jamque effrondibus rosis gremium impleverat, cum, arrectis in cælum oculis, &amp; arcano intus dictante spiritu, cœpit non sine devotis suspiriis jactare in altum singulas rosas, velut superno eas hortulano commendans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062514Z">
        <seg>A solitary virgin sat in the home garden, intent on gathering flowers, and had already filled her lap with freshly picked roses, when, raising her eyes to heaven and moved by a secret inner spirit, she began, with devout sighs, to toss each rose high into the air, as if commending them to a heavenly gardener.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Constat, ex devotæ Rosæ mystica officina pluries hoc vestimentorum genus prodiisse: nam subinde amicissimis personis eadem liberali dono cedebat offerenda, applicanda, circumponenda Jesulo nudo, amori suo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053613Z">
        <seg>It is clear that from the devout workshop of the Mystical Rose this kind of garment emerged on multiple occasions: for she would from time to time present the same as a generous gift to her dearest friends, to be applied and wrapped around the naked Jesus, her beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Consurgebat illis diebus Limæ novellum S. Claræ monasterium, totius sanctitatis, rigoris, atque observantiæ sacrarium, auspicante illustrissima D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040016Z">
        <seg>In those days the new monastery of St. Clare was being erected in Lima, a sanctuary of all holiness, rigor, and observance, under the auspices of the most illustrious D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Consurgebat illis diebus Limæ novellum S. Claræ monasterium, totius sanctitatis, rigoris, atque observantiæ sacrarium, auspicante illustrissima D. Maria de Quinnones, cui avunculus erat ille ipse famigeratissimus Dei servus, Limensium archiepiscopus tunc superstes, D. Turibius Alphonsus Mogroveio, de quo supra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000514Z">
        <seg>In those days the new monastery of St. Clare was being erected in Lima, a sanctuary of all holiness, rigor, and observance, under the auspices of the most illustrious Lady Mary of Quinnones, whose uncle was that very renowned servant of God, the then surviving Archbishop of Lima, Don Turibius Alphonsus Mogrovejo, of whom mention has been made above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit aliquando, citharæ omnes deesse chordas, nihilominus Rosa citharizabat, intenta magis interius sonanti decachordo psalterio cum cantico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053229Z">
        <seg>It happened once that all the harps were out of strings; nevertheless, Rosa played the harp, her attention more intent on the inner sound of the ten-string psaltery with its song.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit hoc anno salutis MDCVI, ipsa S. Laurentii martyris festiva die, cui haud absurde aptaveris illud Tibulli lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040214Z">
        <seg>This happened in the year 1606, on the very day of the feast of St. Lawrence the Martyr, to which you might not inappropriately apply that passage from Tibullus, Book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, Joannem ex cruciatibus stomachi periculose ægrotare, ita ut accito confessario, tamquam vitæ incertus animam per exomologesim duxerit expiandam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061438Z">
        <seg>It happened that John fell dangerously ill from stomach pains, so that, having summoned his confessor, he led his soul to be purified by confession, as if uncertain of his life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, ex medicinæ consilio ægrotanti phlebotomiam adhiberi, extractumque vena sanguinem in angulo seponi, dum medicus ad eum inspiciendum rediret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061339Z">
        <seg>It happened that, on the doctor's advice, the patient was subjected to a bloodletting, and the blood drawn from the vein was set aside in a corner while the doctor returned to examine it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Contigit, prout Virgo prædixerat: Franciscam ter culex adrosit, exinde illo die semperque in posterum libera ab his fuit, præsente Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043934Z">
        <seg>It happened, just as the Virgin had foretold: a mosquito stung Francisca three times; from that day forward, in the presence of Rosa, she was always free from them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Conveniebant ad eam e vicinis domibus parvulæ, at lusitandi gratia, quod illius ætatis primum studium: afferebant singulæ suas pupas, quibus vestiendis, comendis, ornandis, femellarum pueritia communissimo sexus genio addicitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043221Z">
        <seg>Little girls from the neighboring houses would gather at her home to play, for that is the first passion of that age; each would bring her own dolls, to whom dressing, feeding, and adorning are the common occupations of girlhood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Coram his D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050237Z">
        <seg>In the presence of these, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cordatissima est, planeque extrema hæc sui abnegatio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050727Z">
        <seg>This self-denial is most complete and, indeed, extreme.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Corporalem vero ex Eucharistico pastu saturitatem ipsamet Rosa tum confessariis, tum domesticis fassa est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055941Z">
        <seg>Rosa herself confessed to both her confessors and her household that she was sated in body by the Eucharistic meal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crebrum hoc ei in ore erat: “Domine, ecquis te non amaret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053034Z">
        <seg>He often had this in his mouth: "Lord, who would not love you?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credebat innocens Virgo, se suis peccatis has debere vindicias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041513Z">
        <seg>The innocent virgin believed that she deserved these punishments for her own sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credebat, se nec dimidium horæ quadrantem in aviculæ musica hæsisse attentam, &amp; forsan horæ integræ effluxerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045047Z">
        <seg>She believed she had not been attentive to the little bird's music for even half an hour, and perhaps a full hour had already passed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Credibile est, ipsammet, statim ut cum Quadragesima expirabat utendi facultas, ultro solvisse catastam, dispersisse testarum fragmina, projecisse paxillos &amp; ligna, ne quid innotesceret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042945Z">
        <seg>It is credible that she herself, as soon as the permission to use it expired with the Lenten fast, would have willingly paid the tax, scattered the fragments of shells, thrown away the pegs and the wood, so that nothing would be discovered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Creditur, S. Catharinam Senensem aliquando idem, quod Rosa, passam esse: de B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045831Z">
        <seg>It is believed that St. Catherine of Siena once suffered the same thing as Rosa, concerning the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crescebat cum ætate religio, cum statura corporis elevatio mentis in Deum: nam &amp; pueriles ipsius annos exercebat seria meditandi frequentia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044805Z">
        <seg>As he grew in years, so did his religion grow, and with his stature, the elevation of his mind to God: for even in his boyhood he practiced a serious and frequent meditation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Creverat puella, jamque suimet per ætatem compos, ut rescivit, se neutiquam in baptisterio sed in cunis traxisse hoc succedaneum nomen, suspicata est pro solo vanitatis lenocinio forsitan hoc sibi inditum fuisse nomen, quo vultus sui commendaretur forma ac venustas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034724Z">
        <seg>The girl had grown up, and now, of age, she learned that she had never been given this substitute name at baptism but in her cradle; she suspected that it had perhaps been bestowed on her for the mere lure of vanity, to commend the beauty and charm of her face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit pullus, sed ea inerat ventricoso pigritia, ut jugiter cubarit humi, &amp; vix umquam visus sit ultro in pedes attolli, numquam auditus cucurire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061531Z">
        <seg>The chick grew, but it was so sluggish and belly-heavy that it lay on the ground all the time, and was scarcely ever seen to rise to its feet of its own accord, nor was it ever heard to chirp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit subitis ac prodigiosis augmentis recens parthenon, ita quod hodie ultra ducentas numeret sanctimoniales, sponso Deo, sub inviolabilis clausuræ religiosa custodia, militantes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062421Z">
        <seg>The newly founded convent has grown by sudden and prodigious increases, so that today it numbers over two hundred nuns, who, with God as their spouse, serve under the inviolable religious enclosure in strict custody.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crevit ætas, sed pariter cum ætate afflictio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052445Z">
        <seg>Age has grown, but with it, affliction;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Crucem ex densiori charta, flosculis, plumisque versicolorem imposuit, &amp; quidquid sacrarum imaginum conquirere poterat, in hanc suam basilicam solicite comportabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043252Z">
        <seg>He placed a cross of thicker paper, with little flowers and multicolored feathers, and diligently gathered whatever sacred images he could find and brought them into his basilica.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cruciabatur ingenti scrupulo Rosa, quod exacte ad præscriptam horam evigilare non poterat, ut summe cupiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051329Z">
        <seg>Rosa was tormented by a great pang of conscience because she could not wake up at the exact prescribed hour, as she had so dearly wished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cui Rosa: “Quid ni curam tui peculiarem haberet seraphica nostra mater per totum hunc diem, posteaquam tu integram hanc noctem in ejus obsequio mecum laboriosam exegisti”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055318Z">
        <seg>Rosa said to him, "Why shouldn't our seraphic mother take special care of you this entire day, now that you have spent the whole of last night laboriously in her service alongside me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cui Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035558Z">
        <seg>To whom does Rosa belong?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cui modesta Virgo levi subrisu aiebat: “Continuo affabilis illa cæli Regina favoribus cumulat miserrimam hanc peccatricem”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054345Z">
        <seg>To whom the modest Virgin said with a slight smile, "Immediately that affable Queen of Heaven showers her favors upon this wretched sinner."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum tamen locus ille dicatur hic&lt;/e2&gt; metalli fodinis percelebris, &lt;e3&gt;verosimilius indicatur&lt;/e3&gt; Guanca &lt;e4&gt;cognomento&lt;/e4&gt; Velica, &lt;e5&gt;e cujus fodinis decies centena millia pondo hydrargyri vel argenti vivi quotannis eruuntur, &amp; terrestri itinere Limam deducuntur, ut proxime citatus Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T214718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035858Z">
        <seg>Although that place is said to be here&lt;/e2&gt;, it is more likely indicated by Guanca, nicknamed Velica, from whose mines a million pounds of mercury or quicksilver are extracted each year, and transported by land to Lima, as the recently cited Latius reports in book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum tu Creator sis, &amp; ego creatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045335Z">
        <seg>Since you are the Creator, and I am a creature.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cum vero in vicinum inde cubiculum vocaretur, sive a cadaveris tristi dimovenda spectaculo, sive ut cum aliis videret Aloysiam tunc forte rigentem in extasi, ire distulit, ne angulum suum tanti jubili conscium præmature desereret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035833Z">
        <seg>But when he was summoned into the adjoining room—whether to remove himself from the sad spectacle of the corpse or to go with others to see Aloysia, who at that moment was perhaps rigid in ecstasy—he delayed, so as not to desert his corner, aware of so great a jubilation, too soon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cumque nec sic proficeret, imperiosa præcepit, quotidie secum deinceps accumberet communi mensæ cum familia, visura demum, quid, quantumque manducaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041157Z">
        <seg>And when even that failed to help, she imperiously ordered that from then on she be seated at the family table with them every day, so that she could finally see what and how much she ate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cumque nonnemo præsentium dixisset, prægrandem &amp; omnino rarissimam esse hanc gratiam, sat prospere agi cum animabus hinc migrantibus, dum pro residua mortalis vitæ scoria detergenda ad purgatrices flammas mittuntur, se sibi id optare animitus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061838Z">
        <seg>And when one of those present had said that this grace was exceedingly great and altogether rare, and that souls departing from here fare well enough while they are sent to the purifying flames to have the remaining scum of mortal life washed away, he earnestly wished that for himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cunctabatur Virgo, metuens, præpropere sibi dolores eripi, quos pro Sponsi amore diutius tolerare optabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053954Z">
        <seg>The Virgin hesitated, fearing that her pains would be prematurely snatched from her, which she wished to endure longer for the love of her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Cur ille tardet, Rosa dum ardet,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051620Z">
        <seg>Why does he tarry while the rose is in bloom?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Currebat &amp; ipsa per hujus sponsæ vestigia in odorem Sponsi, sed inter adolescentulas, ut sibi videbatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044024Z">
        <seg>She too ran along the bride's trail, following the scent of the Bridegroom, but among the young women, as it seemed to her;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De Christi serto spineo canit Ecclesia: “Felix spina, cujus aculei vires frangunt regis tartarei”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042338Z">
        <seg>Of Christ's thorny crown the Church sings: "Blessed thorn, whose spikes break the power of the Tartarean king."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De apparitionibus hujusmodi consule Papebrochium nostrum tomo 6 Maii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051508Z">
        <seg>For apparitions of this kind, consult our Papebroch in the sixth volume of May, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De hac Virginis in divinam providentiam admiranda fiducia seorsim capitulo sequenti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061103Z">
        <seg>On the Virgin's admirable trust in divine providence, separately in the following chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De hujus remedii laudibus ac necessitate Gorræus &amp; Castellus ad vocem Græcam&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;φλεβοτομία&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;consuli possunt&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045952Z">
        <seg>For the praises and necessity of this remedy, Gorræus and Castellus can be consulted on the Greek word φλεβοτομία.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De igne hoc nostro elementali aiebat ridendam esse comparationem, quia is nonnisi pœnam sensus, quam vocant, infligere aptus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045806Z">
        <seg>He said that the comparison of this elemental fire of ours was laughable, because it is only capable of inflicting what they call the punishment of sensation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De primis duobus, pauca hic præfari necessum est, ut ex horum sanctitate &amp; peritia, vis, pondus, fides maturissimi examinis aptius innotescat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050033Z">
        <seg>Regarding the first two, it is necessary here to say a few things so that, from their holiness and expertise, the force, weight, and credibility of the most mature examination may be more aptly recognized.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De situ &amp; etymologia ipsius inter astrologosdisputatur.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054800Z">
        <seg>Its location and etymology are disputed among astrologers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>De variis ejus speciebus medici jam sæpe laudati ad hanc vocem consuli possunt.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053002Z">
        <seg>On its various species, physicians who have already been frequently praised on this subject may be consulted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Decumbebat agoni proxima, cum in ultimo vitæ suæ horario quadrante offertur ei pro confortatione cordis pretiosus liquor sorbendus e cyatho;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040713Z">
        <seg>She lay near death, when in the final quarter-hour of her life a precious liquid was offered to her from a chalice to comfort her heart, to be sipped.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Decumbebat in ædibus viduæ D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061330Z">
        <seg>He was lying down in the house of the widow D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deficiente fellis copia, succedebant in vicem folia quædam amarissimi fruticis, quorum suctu &amp; masticatione, Christi in morte sitibundi hyssopum, male jurulentum, saucia cogitatione recolebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041240Z">
        <seg>When the supply of gall failed, certain leaves of a most bitter shrub took its place; by sucking and chewing them, the thirsty for Christ in death recalled, with wounded thought, the ill-tasting hyssop.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Degebat in quodam Indorum pago cum parentibus duodennis Filiola, ubi die quadam cum matre ac fratribus domum rediens, in patenti area obvium vidit taurum ferocissimum, qui elapsus stabulo, vinculisque partim ruptis, partim raptatis, cursu præcipiti illuc ruebat, unde veniebat cum comitibus Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062117Z">
        <seg>She was living in a certain Indian village with her parents, the twelve-year-old daughter, when one day, as she was returning home with her mother and brothers, she saw in a clearing a most ferocious bull, which, having escaped from the stable and with its ties partly broken and partly torn away, was rushing at full speed toward the very spot where she was with her companions, Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dehinc chartam adhibuit, visurus, an forte ex coloribus oleo temperatis, unctuosa aliqua pinguedo humori inesset: commaduit charta, sed paulo post siccabatur: unde pure aqueum esse collegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053849Z">
        <seg>Then he applied a sheet of paper to see whether, perhaps, some oily, greasy substance was present in the moisture: the paper adhered, but shortly afterward it dried; from this he concluded that it was purely aqueous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinceps nemo (opinor) dubitabit, Rosam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061423Z">
        <seg>Henceforth, no one (I think) will doubt, Rosa B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde ad adstantes socias lepide conversa, subdebat:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045338Z">
        <seg>Then, turning gracefully to the women standing by, she added:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde fidentius ad specialia descendens, monuit hominem de arcanis, ipsi soli notis, quæque a tanta distantia non nisi divinitus, aut angelo internuntio, Virgini potuerant innotuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051908Z">
        <seg>Then, descending more confidently to particulars, he warned the man of the secrets known only to himself, which from such a distance could have been made known to the Virgin only by divine means or through an angelic messenger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde ibidem breviter virtutes ejus &amp; lucubrationes recenset, quibus hæc subjungit:&lt;/e2&gt; Vitam ejus testis oculatus in litteras misit Franciscus Losa parochus cathedralis Mexicanæ ecclesiæ, quem secuti sunt peculiaribus suis historiis Alphonsus Ramon Mercinariorum sodalis, &amp; Ludovicus Muños causarum procurator in Matritensi curia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042015Z">
        <seg>Then, in the same place, he briefly reviews his virtues and writings, to which he adds the following: His life was committed to writing by Francis Losa, parish priest of the cathedral church of Mexico City, whom followed, with their own particular histories, Alphonsus Ramon, a member of the Mercedarian Order, and Louis Muños, a lawyer in the Court of Madrid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde in vultum augustissimæ Dei Parentis intenta, ut vidit hunc sibi serenum &amp; solito dulcius adblandientem, resumpsit animum, jamque complacebat sibi in eo, quo fuerat contristata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044303Z">
        <seg>Then, gazing at the face of the most august Mother of God, when she saw it serene and smiling on her more sweetly than usual, she recovered her spirits, and was now pleased with that very thing which had before saddened her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deinde ponderibus denuo impositis, cumulabantur ex adverso gratiæ super gratias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052801Z">
        <seg>Then, with the weights once again imposed, graces were piled upon graces from the opposite side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Demum oranti in templo S. Dominici Rosæ innotuere omnia, sibique hanc desolatam &amp; perplexam reservari mox intelligens, modum, quo subveniret, prompte invenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061209Z">
        <seg>Finally, while praying in the church of Saint Dominic, Rose perceived everything, and, understanding that this desolate and perplexed case was soon to be entrusted to her, she promptly found a way to help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique ejusdem materiæ totidem ulnas, quæ pallio muliebri sufficerent, devota persona obtulerat Limano Fratrum Prædicatorum Conventui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061141Z">
        <seg>Finally, a devout person had offered to the Convent of the Friars Preachers of Lima the same number of yards of the same material, which would have been sufficient for a woman's cloak;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique fidem silentii obsecravit, &amp; abiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044712Z">
        <seg>Finally, he swore the oath of silence and left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique huc respexisse Davidem cum gemeret: “Dicitur mihi quotidie, ubi est Deus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050426Z">
        <seg>Finally, David looked back here as he groaned: "I am told every day, 'Where is God?'"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique in his Dei attributis pene tota movebatur sui cordis systole atque diastole, tantumque in iis profecerat, ut modeste aliquando fassa sit, etiam inter nendum, ad singulas acus puncturas, novo se Deum encomio compellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045000Z">
        <seg>Finally, her heart's very systole and diastole were driven almost entirely by these attributes of God, and she had advanced so far in them that she once modestly confessed that even while sewing, at each puncture of the needle, she compelled herself to utter a new encomium of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique ingemiscebat querula, tam raros &amp; paucos esse, qui Dominum tam suavem toto ac puro affectu redamarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054140Z">
        <seg>Finally, the querulous one sighed, that there were so few who would love the Lord with a whole and pure affection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique jam mutatus ab illo, qui paulo ante fuerat, sic Virginem affatus est: “Sentio in te loqui Christum, qui solus me tibi sic detegere potuit, ut intima pravi cordis mei penetrares;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060707Z">
        <seg>Finally, now changed from the one he had been a little while before, he thus addressed the Virgin: "I feel Christ speaking in you, who alone could have revealed me to you in this way, so that you might penetrate the inmost depths of my wicked heart."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique jam satis omnia terroribus impleverat taurus, quando venientem eminus rhedam conspicatus, recta in illam libravit cursum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062145Z">
        <seg>At last the bull had already filled everything with terror, when, spotting a chariot approaching from a distance, it charged straight at it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique jucundissimum ipsi erat, quod eodem die ac loco, &amp; palmam &amp; Rosam amiserat, utpote post hoc connubium non amplius sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044427Z">
        <seg>Finally, it was most painful to him that on the same day and in the same place he had lost both the palm and the rose, since after this marriage they were no longer his.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique materna licentia propius os naresque applicuit, odorem sensit, exertaque lingua mordacitatem sævissimi collyrii lambendo deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043416Z">
        <seg>Finally, with her mother's permission, she brought her mouth and nostrils closer, caught the scent, and with her tongue protruding, detected the sharp bite of the most savage collyrium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique metuebat simul ac sperabat conversæ peccatricis constantiæ ac stabilitati: verum in solatii cumulum adfuit superna revelatio, qua Rosam Christus ipse securam reddidit de seria reductæ monialis pœnitudine, adjungens, eam non modo perseveraturam, sed insignem quoque sanctimoniæ gradum adepturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053433Z">
        <seg>Finally, she feared as much as she hoped for the constancy and stability of the penitent's conversion; but to the abundance of her consolation came a heavenly revelation, by which Christ himself assured Rosa of the sincerity of her monastic repentance, adding that she would not only persevere but also attain an eminent degree of holiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique post multa ultro citroque de sui diffidentia, de ardore fidei, de securitate spei, de stimulis superni amoris ventilata &amp; sagacissime explorata, conclusit doctor, sinceram ac tutam esse viam, qua Virgo ambulabat, nullam hic subesse vaferrimi dæmonis imposturam, tales affectus, effectus, lumina, ab illusione aut principe tenebrarum nequaquam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050619Z">
        <seg>Finally, after much back-and-forth about self-doubt, the ardor of faith, and the security of hope, after many things had been tossed back and forth about self-doubt, the ardor of faith, the security of hope, and the stirrings of divine love, which were most shrewdly examined, the doctor concluded that the path the Virgin was treading was sincere and safe, and that no crafty imposture of the devil lay beneath it, and that such affections, effects, and illuminations could in no way be from illusion or the prince of darkness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique pro crepundiis puerulo offeram meas lachrymas, suspiria, actus amoris, cumque his animam &amp; cor meum, ut nihil mihi remaneat, quia convenit nihil me possidere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053610Z">
        <seg>Finally, in place of a cradle, I will offer my boy my tears, sighs, and acts of love, along with my soul and heart, so that nothing remains for me, for it is fitting that I possess nothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique sentiebat Rosa, se compellari primo illius diei Responsorio: &lt;b5&gt;Adorna thalamum tuum Sion&lt;/b5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043630Z">
        <seg>At last, Rosa felt compelled by the first Responsory of the day: "Adorn your bridal chamber, O Zion."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique solemni die Paschatis, hic annulus ex triduano Dominici sepulchri contubernio jam sacratior, gratiorque, ad Rosam rediit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044511Z">
        <seg>Finally, on the solemn day of Easter, this ring, now made more sacred and dear by its three-day companionship with the Lord's sepulchre, returned to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique subsannent, rideant, cogitent de me, ut lubuerit, ego quoad potero, non cessabo a cura, ne crux Domini mei, vel in abjecta, vilissimaque stipula usquam conculcetur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054924Z">
        <seg>"Finally, let them mock, let them laugh, let them think of me as they please; as long as I am able, I will not cease from my care, lest the cross of my Lord be trampled anywhere, even upon the most abject and vile stubble."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique umbraticus ille ductor sequentem Rosam ante genitricis cubiculum stitit, &amp; disparuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051821Z">
        <seg>Finally, that shadowy guide stopped the following Rosa before her mother's bedroom and vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique vel in simplicis aquæ potu adinvenit, quo, sive biberet, sive abstineret, gustum fraudaret: nam ut faucium ardori levamen ac refrigerium subtraheret, integras hebdomadas transigebat sine potu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041352Z">
        <seg>Finally, he even found a way to ruin the taste of plain water: whether he drank it or abstained, he would cheat the palate; for, in order to deprive his throat of relief and refreshment, he would go for entire weeks without a drink.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique vivacius hunc Rosæ cruciabilem horrorem effigiebat Christi in supremo judicio vultus asperatus, rugosus, ipsisque dæmonibus terribilis, dum cogitatur velut jamjam dicturus horrendum illud: &lt;b5&gt;Ite maledicti&lt;/b5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045841Z">
        <seg>Finally, it depicted with greater vividness this crucifying horror of the Rose—the face of Christ in the Last Judgment, hardened, wrinkled, and terrifying even to the demons themselves—as if about to utter that dreadful word: "Depart, you cursed."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denique, cum aliud non poterat, oblectabat se imagine ac meditatione suppliciorum, quæ sigillatim omnia pro Sponsi amore desiderabat in se experiri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060316Z">
        <seg>Finally, since she could do nothing else, she delighted herself with the image and meditation of torments, all of which she desired to experience in herself for the love of her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Denuo supplex obstitit Rosa, certo asseverans, quæstoris mancipium jamjam cum præparato medicamento adfuturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051710Z">
        <seg>Rosa again pleaded earnestly, firmly asserting that the questioner's slave would soon arrive with the prepared medicine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deplorabat inconsolabiliter vicinum Chili&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;b&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt; regnum, ubi dietim per myriades peribant animæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060354Z">
        <seg>He lamented the neighboring kingdom of Chili&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;b&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt; unconsolably, where souls perished by the myriads every day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Descendit, ipsamque cupam invenit usque ad supremum labrum recenti melle plenam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062232Z">
        <seg>He descended and found the honeycomb itself full of fresh honey up to the very top.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Describebat brevi linearum ductu murorum ambitum, qui sub prima futuri cœnobii exordia minus spatii complexurus erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062731Z">
        <seg>He was sketching out the outline of the walls with a few strokes, which, at the outset of the future monastery, was to encompass less space.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Desertum erat obscuritatis, languoris, hebetudinis, regio mortis, nox deliquii, antrum ultimæ calamitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045459Z">
        <seg>It was a desert of darkness, languor, and dullness; a region of death, a night of swoon, a cavern of the ultimate calamity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Detumescente animi procella, dolebat interdum ac damnabat ipsamet suæ ferocitatis effrenem impotentiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060722Z">
        <seg>When the storm of her spirit subsided, she would sometimes ache and condemn the unbridled impotence of her own ferocity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deum utriusque nostrum</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045249Z">
        <seg>God of us both</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deus sit amor noster.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053142Z">
        <seg>May God be our love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Deus, delicium meum, amare te velim, quantum te amat sanctissima Mater tua, Domina mea, Virgo purissima;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053111Z">
        <seg>God, my delight, I would love you as much as your most holy Mother, my Lady, the purest Virgin, loves you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dextera manus, quæ anulum implicuerat, sedis costæ laterali innitebatur, at fixe, velut acclavata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044604Z">
        <seg>The right hand, which had entwined the ring, rested on the side of the seat's rib, but it lay fixed, as if nailed in place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dici non potest, ex Virginis spiritu quos quantosque mox spiritus hauserit felix matrona: diriguit, sensitque Rosæ tum affatu, tum afflatu mutari se medullitus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062939Z">
        <seg>It cannot be said how many spirits the fortunate matron soon drew from the Virgin's spirit; she shuddered, and felt herself being changed to the very marrow by Rose's words and breath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dici non potest, quam sublimem, invictam, solidam fiduciam hæc paucula cordi Virginis impresserint: plus enim ipso proferendi modo, quam verbis sibi significari intellexit, nec deinceps eam permisit Sponsus in æternæ salutis anchora fixam, ullo incertitudinis fluctu turbari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061816Z">
        <seg>It cannot be said how sublime, invincible, and steadfast a confidence these few things had imprinted on the heart of the Virgin: for she understood that they signified to her more by the very manner of their utterance than by words, and thereafter the Spouse did not allow her, fixed as she was in the anchor of eternal salvation, to be troubled by any fluctuation of uncertainty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dicitur&lt;/e0&gt; cancer &lt;e1&gt;a similitudine cancri piscis, eo quod paulatim serpat, vel pertinaciter hæreat instar cancri, qui aliquid chelis suis apprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061615Z">
        <seg>Cancer is said to derive its name from the resemblance of the crab of the zodiac, because it creeps gradually or clings tenaciously like a crab that seizes something with its claws.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didacus Martinez Societatis Jesu, Patres magistri F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050216Z">
        <seg>Didacus Martinez of the Society of Jesus, Fathers teachers F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Didicit Rosa, vel ad zelotypiam usque amari se a Sponso, &amp; pro tantæ gratiæ crebriori experimento facile exposuisset in prædam, quidquid florum sibi residuum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051245Z">
        <seg>Rosa learned that she was loved by her Spouse even to jealousy, and for the more frequent experience of such grace she would have readily laid out as a prey whatever flowers remained to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Die quadam in paternis ædibus defecit numerosæ familiæ panis, neque vel tempus coquendi supererat, vel nummi ad manum erant, ut aliunde procuraretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062204Z">
        <seg>One day, the bread ran out for the numerous family in the father's house, and there was neither time to bake nor money on hand to procure it elsewhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Die quadam, inter alias adfuit prænobilis, nec virtute minus, quam parentela &amp; opibus clara, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062904Z">
        <seg>On a certain day, among others, was present a most noble lady, distinguished not less for virtue than for her lineage and wealth, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dilectum quis detinet?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050954Z">
        <seg>Who detains the draft?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dissimulavit, ut potuit, famulumque propere cum pelvi abeuntem rogavit, hanc sibi ferendam tradi, se non tantum vacuaturam, quod inerat, sed insuper vas ipsum nitide mundaturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061352Z">
        <seg>She concealed it as best she could and, calling to the servant who was hurrying away with the basin, asked that it be brought to her, saying she would not only empty its contents but also clean the vessel itself until it was spotless.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Diu placuit ferventi Rosæ crudelis hæc supinæ torsionis catasta, placuissetque diutius, si fuisset atrocior, hoc est, si acutius &amp; omni ex parte sæviisset: nam inter lignorum vacua spatia nil erat quod incubuam satis cruciaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042601Z">
        <seg>For a long time, this backbending rack of cruel Rose's pleased the fervent one, and would have pleased him longer, had it been more atrocious—that is, had it been sharper and more savage in every respect. For between the empty spaces of the wood, there was nothing that could sufficiently torment the incubus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixisses hanc indolem uni patientiæ natam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034925Z">
        <seg>You would say that this disposition was born of patience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixisses, non Virgini chirothecas, sed victimæ sartagines &amp; ignem apparari, adeo exterrita diriguit, ingemuit, hæsitavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035334Z">
        <seg>You would have said that not a virgin's gloves, but a victim's frying pan and fire were being prepared; so terrified, she stiffened, groaned, and hesitated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dixit, viduamque consolata, bene sperare jussit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055253Z">
        <seg>He said, and having comforted the widow, he bade her hope for the best.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dole etiam tu, ac resipisce.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060648Z">
        <seg>Grieve also, and repent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dolores sui gutturis mitigari poscit infirma, &amp; impetrat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051025Z">
        <seg>The sick woman asks for her throat pain to be eased, and she gets it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doluit Virgo, illic præter consuetudinem apparere hominem, ubi supra erat sine teste, sine comite.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052203Z">
        <seg>The virgin was distressed that a man appeared there out of the ordinary, where before there had been no witness, no companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Doluit primum de sacrilega injuria, qua divinus honor facinore tam fœdo ac publico fuerat violatus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053424Z">
        <seg>He was first pained by the sacrilegious insult, by which divine honor had been violated by such a foul and public outrage;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Domi, quotquot de familia erant, cavebant, ne quas coram Virgine proferrent ineptias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053312Z">
        <seg>At home, all the family members were careful not to utter any foolishness in the Virgin's presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dominici, &lt;s2&gt;[sicut experientia compertum est,]&lt;/s2&gt; ac D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043847Z">
        <seg>Dominic, &lt;s2&gt;[as experience has shown,]&lt;/s2&gt; and D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dulce prome canticum:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045233Z">
        <seg>Bring forth a sweet song:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dulcescebat exinde in corculo Virginis hic precandi modus, ut ab ea frequentanda nec dormiens cessaret, usque adeo nimirum Dei præsentia jam sensus Rosæ interiores undique occuparat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035024Z">
        <seg>From that time on, this manner of praying before the Virgin became so sweet to Rose that she did not cease visiting her, even in her sleep, for the presence of God had by then completely filled her inmost senses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum Rosa adhuc domi propriæ, suum, de quo infra, reclusorium inhabitabat, Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor ei hebdomadatim mittebat rudioris panis octo placentulas, ut diætim una ad minus necessario victui tota impenderetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041324Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was still at home, living in her own little cloister (as mentioned below), the wife of the treasurer Gundisalvus used to send her every week eight small loaves of coarser bread, so that one less daily meal would be necessary for her upkeep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum Rosa nendo occupabatur in telæ consutura, en iterum dilectum Jesulum nentis pulvillo infantiliter &amp; quietissime insidentem: illic nutibus taciturnis loquebatur ad cor Dilectæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050916Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was busy spinning at the loom, behold, she again saw the beloved Jesus sitting like a child on the spinning-wheel, spinning most quietly; there he spoke with silent nods to the beloved's heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum Rosa oblongam trabeam (scapulare dicimus) iconi amiciendæ consuit, mittit in conclave, ubi hæc stabat, Philippam de Montoya concivem suam, ut inde serici fili glomerem sibi apportaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055104Z">
        <seg>While Rosa was sewing the oblong plank (which we call a scapular) for the icon's vesting, she sent into the room where it stood her fellow townswoman Philippa de Montoya to fetch her a skein of silk thread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum defunctæ cadaver prostabat in feretro, disposuerat cælestis illa (cui nihil fortuitum) providentia, ut deesset florida corolla, quæ de more caput virgineum circumdaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042409Z">
        <seg>While the corpse of the deceased lay in the coffin, that heavenly providence (to which nothing is accidental) had arranged that the florid wreath, which by custom encircled the virgin's head, was missing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum ergo se vidit frustra exspectare, dum hæsitat, quid agendum, prospiciens per fenestellam, vidit cominus umbram candidam, motu decoro agilem, aspectuque non injucundam, quæ Virginem, ut se versus domum sequeretur, invitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051809Z">
        <seg>So, seeing that he was waiting in vain and hesitating over what to do, he looked through the little window and saw close by a white shadow, graceful in movement and not unpleasant to behold, which beckoned the Virgin to follow it home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum his cælestium decretorum arcanis avidissime inhiat auscultatque Rosa, videbatur interim suis manibus floridum hoc sertum paulatim connectere, Sponsoque imponere, qui vicissim ostendit, illud sibi gratissimum, &amp; intime charum esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062604Z">
        <seg>While Rosa hungrily absorbed and listened to the mysteries of these heavenly decrees, she meanwhile seemed to be gradually weaving this flourishing garland with her own hands and placing it upon the Bridegroom, who in turn showed that it was most pleasing and deeply dear to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum igitur supradicti congerrones inde ad proprias ædes cum matre redeuntem vident, roseo vultu instar auroræ subvividam, aspectuque vegetam, amœnam, &amp; minime decolorem, temere suspicati sunt, Virginem venire ab epulis ac lautitie;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040754Z">
        <seg>Therefore, when the above-mentioned comrades saw her returning home with her mother, her face rosy like the dawn, her appearance lively, charming, and by no means pale, they rashly suspected that the Virgin was coming from a feast and a sumptuous banquet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Dum revertitur, audit e pergula canoram aviculam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045022Z">
        <seg>As he returns, he hears a songbird on the pergola.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Duodecima sonuit, &amp; nondum adest?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050938Z">
        <seg>It's twelve, and he's not here yet?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Duplicabat angorem, quod alba vestis humano plausui videbatur ansam aditumque ultro concedere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040338Z">
        <seg>It doubled the anxiety, that the white garment seemed, to human applause, to offer a handle and an entrance of its own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Durissimum id erat frugali Rosæ, quam vel odor carnium offendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041030Z">
        <seg>That was the hardest thing for frugal Rosa, who was even offended by the smell of meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Durum sane effugium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043345Z">
        <seg>A hard escape indeed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ea occasione honestæ feminæ tum Isabellam, tum ægram invisuræ frequentes accedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062901Z">
        <seg>On that occasion, respectable women frequently came to visit both Isabella and the sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem infanti Rosæ fuit indoles quieta, suavis, lepidula, &amp; continuo serena, ut commune dumtaxat gaudium totius domus videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034817Z">
        <seg>The infant Rose had the same quiet, sweet, charming, and consistently serene disposition, so that she seemed to be the common joy of the entire household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eadem, ubi festiva processione effertur in publicum (quod annue fit in die octava SS. Rosarii mense Octobri) a dispositis per forum chiliadibus crebra scloporum displosione salutatur, fulgente in singularum cohortium explicitis vexillis nomine, tessera, figura imaginis Rosarianæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054304Z">
        <seg>Likewise, when she is carried in a festive procession into public (which is done annually on the eighth day of the Holy Rosary in October), she is greeted by the thousands of onlookers in the square with repeated blasts of gunfire, while the banners unfurled by each cohort gleam with the name, the emblem, and the image of the Rosarian.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eam hic, prout ipsamet Virgo paucis ante obitum diebus doctori Castillo ex fide pacta narravit, nudo simplicique adtexam filo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052720Z">
        <seg>I will present it here, in the same way the Virgin herself, a few days before her death, faithfully recounted it to Doctor Castillo, with a bare and simple thread.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce (inquit) ancillam, ecce mancipium tuum, o Rex majestatis æternæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044350Z">
        <seg>Behold (she said) a handmaid, behold your handmaid, O King of eternal majesty;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce me pallii tui furem domesticam, sed innoxiam, quia plus tibi proderit amissa palla, quam si intactam possideres.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061119Z">
        <seg>Behold me, a thief of your cloak—a domestic one, but harmless, for a lost cloak will serve you better than one kept intact.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce me”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051242Z">
        <seg>Behold me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecce moratur:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050957Z">
        <seg>Behold, he delays:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecclesiam S. Dominici de more precatura adierat, cum illic existentem subita consternavit recordatio unius ex variis instrumentis domando corpusculo adaptatis, quod ipsa domi fortuito reliquerat loco patulo, ubi facile ab aliquo domesticorum absentis cubiculum ingressuro videri poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040902Z">
        <seg>She had gone to the Church of St. Dominic to pray as was her custom when a sudden recollection of one of the various devices she had adapted to subdue the little body—which she had inadvertently left at home in a conspicuous spot—struck her, where it could easily be seen by any of the household entering the absent one's chamber.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eclipsis hæc docet æstimare divinæ familiaritatis præstantiam, &amp; dignationem pure gratuitam, sufflaminat timoris donum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050414Z">
        <seg>This eclipse teaches us to appreciate the excellence of divine familiarity and the purely gratuitous dignity, and it bestows the gift of fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Econtra ille experimentum quærens ejus, qui in Virgine loquebatur Christus, velut ambigenti similis extenuabat vires suas ceu tanto ministerio parum idoneas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060418Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, he, seeking an experiment of the one who spoke in the Virgin, Christ, like a doubter, belittled his own powers as if they were insufficient for such a ministry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Econtra non segnius laborabat Rosa, ut minimos quosque defectus in immensum exaggeraret, serioque ploratu ac gemitu posceret medicinam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040652Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Rosa was no less hard at work, magnifying every little flaw to an enormous degree and earnestly demanding a remedy with serious tears and groans.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ecquis vidit umquam, a rosis suppleri apum officia?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062238Z">
        <seg>Has anyone ever seen the duties of bees be supplanted by roses?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego in Domino confido non quod ita præservari merear, sed quia ille fidelis est in omnibus verbis suis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062027Z">
        <seg>I trust in the Lord, not because I deserve to be preserved in this way, but because he is faithful in all his words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego vero, mi bone Jesu, quando te digne amare incipiam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053037Z">
        <seg>But I, O good Jesus, when will I begin to love you worthily?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ego, ad quam regularis vitæ ac status nulla umquam cogitatio aditum invenit, ego necessariæ dotis inops, cantandi ac psallendi ignara, curis sæcularibus innutrita, spiritu vacua, clausuræ impatiens, onusta pluribus, ego in claustrum me abdam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062833Z">
        <seg>I, to whom no thought of a regular life or state has ever found access, I, lacking the necessary gift, ignorant of singing and psalming, nurtured in worldly cares, empty of spirit, impatient of enclosure, burdened with many things—should I enter a cloister?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Egregium sane stimulandæ orexi proritamentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041228Z">
        <seg>Truly an excellent appetizer to whet the appetite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eia fac Domine, fac te amari, ut decet, excute pharetram, vibra quaquaversum ignita purissima tui amoris jacula, erumpant ubique luculenta cordium incendia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053715Z">
        <seg>Come, do it, Lord, make yourself loved as is fitting; shake your quiver, hurl on every side the purest fiery arrows of your love, so that brilliant fires of the heart may break forth everywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eia itote, dicite infirmo, certissime ipsum statuta die prædicaturum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053505Z">
        <seg>Go, tell the sick man that he will most certainly preach on the appointed day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eidem coaptatæ lodices, at cilicinæ, setosæ, instarque subcerniculi aut cribri, ut simul &amp; ipsæ pungerent, &amp; testarum puncturas non impedirent, sed obiter absconderent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042629Z">
        <seg>Mattresses were fastened to the same frame, but they were made of Cilician wool, bristly and like the bottom of a sieve, so that they would prick at the same time and not hinder the punctures of the shells, but would instead conceal them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ejus pulverem ex vino sumptum nephriticum dolorem a flatibus aut frigida causa provenientem dicunt tollere &lt;e4&gt;&amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043156Z">
        <seg>They say that its powder, taken with wine, relieves kidney pain caused by gas or cold, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Elegans fructu suo onusta propter ejus amplitudinem: florem fert similem albæ rosæ, in cujus foliis aliquæ velut Passionis Christi notæ (inquit Monardes) delineatæ conspiciuntur, quas magna diligentia istic pictas existimes &lt;e1&gt;&amp;c.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041451Z">
        <seg>Elegant, laden with its fruit because of its size: it bears a flower like that of the white rose, on whose petals certain marks resembling the Passion of Christ (as Monardes says) are discernible, which you would think were painted there with great care, &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eleonoræ de Castro annosa contubernalis, Rosam in sua eremo visitabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043850Z">
        <seg>Eleanor of Castile's long-time companion visited Rose in her hermitage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eminebant in ipso, certabantque de primatu, excelsa vitæ perfectio &amp; scholasticæ theologiæ profunditas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050129Z">
        <seg>They shone in him, and contended for the primacy: the exalted perfection of life and the profundity of scholastic theology.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>En vultum Salvatoris totum sudor operuit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053740Z">
        <seg>Behold, the face of the Savior was completely covered with sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>En, inter flores pulchrescis &amp; inter olivas</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053241Z">
        <seg>Behold, among the flowers you grow more beautiful &amp; among the olives</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Eodem modo plura, quæ hoc capite de familiaritate&lt;/e1&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e2&gt;cum Christo narrantur, spiritualiter intelligi velim&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051527Z">
        <seg>In the same way, I would like the many things narrated in this chapter about St. Rose's familiarity with Christ to be understood spiritually.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Epithalamium id fuit, quod non Davidis plectro, sed ore proprio, Sponsi Mater accinuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044418Z">
        <seg>It was a nuptial song that the Bridegroom's Mother sang, not with David's harp, but with her own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erat Mariæ de Oliva in suo domestico gallinario miræ pulchritudinis pullus gallinaceus, ludebat in ejus tergore &amp; alis versicolor &amp; amœna striatarum plumarum varietas, collum quædam velut purpura decore incinxerat, postrema corporis pro pennarum arcuata venustate in iridem videbantur desinere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061525Z">
        <seg>There was in Mary of Oliva's own domestic hen-house a pullet of marvelous beauty; it played upon its back, and its wings displayed a delightful variety of striped feathers. A certain band encircled its neck with a purple-like beauty, and the hind part of its body, instead of feathers, seemed to end in an arc of charming iridescence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo cedat Æsculapius&lt;a0&gt;d&lt;/a0&gt;, quiescite, o medici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000525Z">
        <seg>Therefore, let Aesculapius give way, rest, O doctors;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo cedat Æsculapius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, quiescite, o medici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041141Z">
        <seg>Therefore, let Aesculapius give way, rest, O doctors;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo in confusa illa trepidaque hinc urgentium, inde cunctantium perplexitate, recurrendum denique fuit (non sine instinctu Numinis) ad coronatam S. Catharinæ Senensis imaginem, quam Rosa, dum viveret, ornare consueverat: hujus itaque sertum proprium, omnibusque publice notum, tantisper transfertur in Discipulam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042418Z">
        <seg>Therefore, in that confused and anxious perplexity of those pressing on from one side and those hesitating from the other, recourse was finally had (not without a divine prompting) to the crowned image of St. Catherine of Siena, which Rosa had been accustomed to adorn during her lifetime: and so her own proper crown, publicly known to all, is transferred for the time being to the Disciple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo luctam &amp; afflictionem suam Deiparæ, ceu veræ Stellæ matutinæ questa est, ut subveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051332Z">
        <seg>Therefore, she appealed to the Virgin Mary, as the true Morning Star, for help in her struggle and affliction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo ne perrexeris meos interimere culices, &amp; ego vicissim tibi spondeo, quod æque tecum uti mecum pacem colent”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043906Z">
        <seg>Therefore, do not go on killing my mosquitoes, and I in turn promise you that they will treat you as they treat me and keep the peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo notum Religiosum adiit, coronam porrexit, collo imaginis quantocyus immittendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043525Z">
        <seg>Therefore he approached the known religious man, offered him the crown, and hastily placed it around the image's neck.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo puta exosos Deo capillos, quos tu amare videris” Hæc ludibundus puer gestu velut concionatorio profatus, repetebat ludum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034956Z">
        <seg>"So suppose the hair you seem to love is hateful to God." The boy, jesting, having spoken this with a sort of oratorical gesture, repeated the game.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ergo, proxima Dominica die ad pium hoc sui furtum electa, in viam se conjicit, unico germano suo lætissimi itineris assumpto comite, ac pone juxta S. Dominici vicinam ecclesiam transiens, in eam divertit cum fratre, ut illic in divæ Virginis sacello (cui a Rosario nomen &amp; cultus est) maternam augustissimæ Deiparentis benedictionem, velut opportunum suæ profectionis viaticum præsumeret, exinde reliquum viæ alacrius decursura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040109Z">
        <seg>Therefore, having chosen the next Sunday for this pious theft of herself, she sets out on the road, taking her only brother as her most joyful companion, and makes for St. Passing by the neighboring church of St. Dominic, she turned into it with her brother, so that there in the chapel of the Virgin (which is named and venerated as the Rosary) she might presume to receive the maternal blessing of the most august Mother of God as a fitting viaticum for her journey, and from there would more eagerly complete the rest of the way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Erupit tandem puerulus Jesus in hoc canticum dramatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044337Z">
        <seg>The little boy Jesus finally burst into this song of the drama.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Est herba passim nota, cujus varias species apud Dioscoridem explicatas curiosus lector inveniet.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055510Z">
        <seg>It is a herb commonly known, whose various species the curious reader will find explained by Dioscorides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce post paululum, sociabus nil minus cogitantibus, adfuit illustris feminæ Hieronymæ de Gama ancilla Æthiopissa, cumque pecunia litterulas attulit, quarum hic tenor: “Salve Rosa soror.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055045Z">
        <seg>And behold, after a little while, while the companions were thinking nothing less, the illustrious woman Jerome de Gama's Ethiopian slave girl appeared, and she brought letters with money, the contents of which were: "Greetings, sister Rosa."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, a Virginis alloquio vix quinque dies effluxerant, quando virum invasit omnimoda tabaci nausea, mox &amp; horror, ac vehemens abominatio, ita ut deinceps non modo facillime sese abstinuerit sorbitione tubi cretacei&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, sed neque amplius sufferre potuerit odorem hujus infaustæ fornaculæ, quam deliciis &amp; cupediis humanæ fatuitatis accensere is solus potuit, qui Job 41 sic depingitur: “De naribus ejus procedit fumus, sicut ollæ succensæ:” &amp; denique hic etiam per Europam invenit, qui secum fumos helluentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060803Z">
        <seg>And behold, scarcely five days had passed since the Virgin's address, when the man was seized by a complete nausea of tobacco, soon followed by horror and a vehement abhorrence, so that from then on he not only abstained most easily from the smoke of the chalky pipe, but could no longer bear the smell of this ill-omened little furnace, which could be kindled by the delights and cravings of human folly only by him who is thus described in Job 41: "From his nostrils comes forth smoke, like the smoke of a cauldron that boils over:" and finally, even here in Europe, he found those who would smoke with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et ecce, vidit juxta Virginem puerulum Jesum amictu polymito cærulei purpureique coloris decentissime tunicatum, quem ut conspexit fulgida claritate circumdatum, ac radiis undique coruscantem, non ausa conversationem interrumpere, substitit eminus, contenta permissione dulcissimi spectaculi, cujus arcana pro ætate nondum capiebat, nec, antequam Rosa migrasset e vivis, rem detexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051143Z">
        <seg>And behold, she saw beside the Virgin a little boy, Jesus, most decently dressed in a multicolored tunic of blue and purple, whom, as soon as she saw him, she perceived to be surrounded by a shining brightness, and shining with rays from every side, she, not daring to interrupt the conversation, stood back from a distance, content with the permission of the most sweet sight, whose mysteries her age could not yet grasp, nor did she discover the matter before Rosa had passed from this life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et en, quidquid sentire ex attactu coronæ cœperat, longe validius sensit ab anulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044529Z">
        <seg>And behold, whatever he had begun to feel from the touch of the crown, he felt far more strongly from the ring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et forsitan ob id Virgini datum fuit divinitus, quod plerumque species sacramentales in ejus stomacho ad septem vel octo horas perdurarent, antequam ventriculi calore dissolverentur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055926Z">
        <seg>And perhaps for this reason it was divinely given to the Virgin that the sacramental species would usually last in her stomach for seven or eight hours before being dissolved by the heat of her stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et nonne sub tanta gratia dignum erat, Reginæ nostræ laudibus diutius immorari”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054546Z">
        <seg>And was it not fitting, under such grace, to linger longer in praising our Queen?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et paulo infra: “Jam hoc vestimentum confectum est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054630Z">
        <seg>And a little below: "This garment is now finished;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et quamvis sermo nonnisi de Deo esset, protestabatur utilius sibi ac jucundius semper fore, cum Deo, quam de Deo loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043500Z">
        <seg>And although the conversation was only about God, he maintained that it would always be more useful and more pleasant for him to speak with God than about God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et quid non egit, ut is ab omnibus diligeretur?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053616Z">
        <seg>And what did he not do to be loved by all?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et quotiescumque hoc suave ostentum suis referebat, una testabatur, florituras in illo monasterio præclaras Dei famulas, quæ virtutum odore ac sanctitatis fama gloriose excellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062610Z">
        <seg>And whenever she presented this sweet spectacle to her own people, she bore witness to the splendid blossoms in that monastery—the devoted handmaidens of God who gloriously excelled in the fragrance of virtue and the renown of holiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera is erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044142Z">
        <seg>And indeed, it was he.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera proprium Rosæ centrum sacra Hostia erat, ubicumque eam poterat visu assequi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060022Z">
        <seg>And truly, the sacred Host was the very center of Rose's being, wherever she could see it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et revera, ut durum sit, animam separari a corpore, durius revelli a semetipsa, durissimum fuit revelli a Dilecto post experimentum unionis, ac suavissimorum amplexuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045642Z">
        <seg>And truly, as hard as it was for the soul to be separated from the body, harder still to be torn away from itself, but hardest of all to be torn away from the Beloved after the experience of union and the sweetest embraces.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et tamen in Rosæ thesauris eminebant virginea puritas &amp; abyssalis humilitas, quæ propriam his nuptiis dispositionem parant, dotemque constituunt: quo fit, quod raris ejusmodi hymenæis peculiari jure præsideat augustissima angelorum Imperatrix, utpote supremæ tum puritatis, tum humilitatis privilegio Virgo singularis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044030Z">
        <seg>And yet in the treasures of the Rose there shone virgin purity and abyssal humility, which prepare the proper disposition for these nuptials and constitute the dowry: whereby it comes to pass that the augustest Empress of the angels presides by a special right over such rare weddings, since the singular Virgin is privileged with the supreme purity and humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et te quidem æterno suo decreto selegit Deus, qui primus ibidem sacrosanctam litabis Hostiam in solemnitate: quia primus illic fabricandæ ecclesiæ lapis ritu celeberrimo collocabitur in fundamentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062650Z">
        <seg>And indeed God has chosen you by His eternal decree, you who will first offer the most sacred Host there in solemnity: for the first stone of the church to be built there will be laid in the foundations with the most solemn rite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et vere sic erat: nam Rosa sibi, ac genuino vultus colori demum reddita, erupit in jubilum victotiæ: “Eia, (inquit) Sorores, laudandus est nobis Deus, qui nos Tertiarias, communi solidæ charitatis vinculo, sibi dignatur adstringere, ac perpetim sociare”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040400Z">
        <seg>And indeed it was so: for Rosa, her own color and the genuine hue of her face at last restored, broke into a cry of victory: "Come, sisters," she said, "we must praise God, who deigns to bind us Tertiary Sisters to Himself by the common, steadfast bond of charity, and to unite us forever."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et verius &amp; venustius hoc vulnere pedis rubuit Rosa nostra Peruana, quam quod de Rosis Cypriis fabulata est antiquitas, nimirum has non ante rubuisse, quam ex Cypriæ deæ sauciato calcaneo fortuitus eas cruor aspersisset&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043330Z">
        <seg>And our Peruvian Rose blushed more truly and more charmingly with this wound to its foot than the legend tells of the Cyprian Roses, namely that they did not blush until the chance blood from the wounded heel of the goddess of Cyprus had sprinkled them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et videbat tamen, quia sic mulcebant hi favores, ut ab exteriore opere attentionem, nec totam avocarent, nec partem exdividerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050932Z">
        <seg>And yet he saw that these favors soothed him in such a way that they neither wholly diverted his attention from the external work nor even parted it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Et, quod omnem tum rationem, tum admirationem supergreditur, occupabat divina hæc præsentia interiores Rosæ potentias, sine notabili abstractione a sensibus, ita quod, dum Deo loquebatur intus, absque impedimento simul alia quæque necessaria cum domesticis tractaret foris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044836Z">
        <seg>And, what surpasses all reason and admiration, this divine presence occupied Rose's interior faculties without any notable abstraction from the senses, so that, while she was speaking to God inwardly, she would simultaneously, and without impediment, attend to other necessary matters with her household outside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Etiam hoc nomen metaphorice pro vili vel fœdo homine sumitur, quo sensu humilis Virgo nostra superius num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061627Z">
        <seg>This name is also taken metaphorically for a vile or foul person, in which sense our humble Virgin is referred to above, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evenit, uti Virgo prædixerat: paucis ante concionem horis prædicator convaluit, officioque præclare functus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053517Z">
        <seg>It happened, as the Virgin had foretold: a few hours before the sermon the preacher recovered and performed his duty splendidly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evicit tandem illa, accedente confessarii nutu, ut Virgo quæstoris uxori se comitem fore promitteret ituræ ad ægrum invisendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061445Z">
        <seg>At last, with the confessor's nod, she prevailed upon the Virgin to promise the questor's wife that she would accompany her on her visit to the sick man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Evigilans Mariana prosiliit, ægram sublevatura accessit, obsequium quodcumque in rem foret obtulit, simulque de morbo ac morbi causa perplexe interrogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041724Z">
        <seg>Waking, Mariana sprang up, approached to lift up the sick woman, offered whatever assistance was needed, and at the same time asked perplexedly about the illness and its cause.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex adjunctis arbitror, hic significari tuberculum in naribus exortum, quod forsan metaphorice&lt;/e2&gt; phaseolus &lt;e3&gt;a chirurgis vocatur, quia similitudinem pisi vel fabæ refert.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T025753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035235Z">
        <seg>From the accompanying notes, I take it that this refers to a growth in the nose, which is perhaps metaphorically called a&lt;/e2&gt; "bean" &lt;e3&gt;by surgeons, because it resembles a pea or a bean.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex adverso contrarias his impressiones, tumorem, confusionem divisi cordis, complacentiam sui, &amp; inquietudinem erga Deum, nonnisi picem improbi authoris redolere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052246Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, these impressions—pride, confusion of a divided heart, self-complacency, and restlessness toward God—smell of nothing but the pitch of an impious author.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex hac significatione periodus illa satis intelligitur.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054723Z">
        <seg>From this meaning, that period is sufficiently understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex his duo referam, quæ victrici ejus constantiæ validius incubuerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040245Z">
        <seg>Of these, I will relate two that most heavily weighed upon her steadfastness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ex hoc loco colligo, istud embamma frigidum sumi, &amp; alibi reperi, eo vulgus &amp; rusticos in Hispania vesci.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041439Z">
        <seg>From this passage I gather that this embamma is eaten cold, and I found elsewhere that the common people and country folk in Spain eat it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exanimis subito pavore mater cum filiis, circumspiciebat fugam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062120Z">
        <seg>Struck suddenly with terror, the mother with her children looked around for a way to escape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Excitata Virgo respondit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051350Z">
        <seg>The excited virgin answered;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exclamavit denuo matrona: “Quid agis Rosa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055411Z">
        <seg>The matron exclaimed again, "What are you doing, Rosa?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Excusabat puella, se ipsam non satis habere compertum, quid in anima sua pateretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045738Z">
        <seg>The girl excused herself, saying she did not fully understand what was going on in her own soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exemit, viditque luculentis e chirothecis promicare flammas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035356Z">
        <seg>He drew it out, and saw flames shooting from the bright sheaths.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exhorruit Virgo ad improvisam tantilli præconii vanitatem, arreptaque viva calce cruciabiliter manum utramque deformavit, usque dum ardore mordacissimo pellis undique finderetur in rugas, bullulisque sparsim tumesceret, eo quidem dolore ac damno, ut per dies triginta &amp; amplius vestire seipsam Rosa nequierit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040726Z">
        <seg>The Virgin recoiled at the unexpected vanity of the little herald, and, seizing a live coal, horribly disfigured both her hands, until the most biting heat split the skin on every side into wrinkles, and it swelled in scattered blisters, with such pain and damage that for thirty days and more Rosa could not manage to dress herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exin, quasi tessera data, invitabat aviculam metro, quod hunc in usum composuerat, fere hoc sensu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045227Z">
        <seg>Then, as if a signal had been given, he invited the little bird with a meter he had composed for that purpose, in almost these words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde crevit sacræ imagini popularis veneratio, cumque fama tanta miraculi, regnum ac universam regionem pervasit mirifica salutiferæ devotionis propago.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054246Z">
        <seg>From then on, popular veneration of the sacred image grew, and with the fame of such a miracle, a marvelous propagation of salutary devotion spread throughout the kingdom and the entire region.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde mox conquisitis, &amp; in sui cubiculi capaci angulo compaginatis tabulis rude stragulum injecit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042527Z">
        <seg>He soon threw a rough mat of planks, which he had just gathered, into the corner of his spacious room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Exinde sæpius per diem sonoris cantibus implevit viciniam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061553Z">
        <seg>From then on, he often filled the neighborhood with sonorous songs throughout the day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Existimo rarum hoc divortium sublimioribus Rosæ operibus posse accenseri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041950Z">
        <seg>I think this divorce can be counted among Rose's more sublime works.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expalluerunt, quotquot cum Rosa erant feminæ, prosiliere foras, quæ citius poterant, spemque salutis in fuga collocabant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062148Z">
        <seg>The women who were with Rosa, pale with fear, rushed outside as fast as they could, placing their hope of salvation in flight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expalluit confusa Virgo, timens hoc indicio deprehensum iri, quantum exercitiis pœnitentiæ soleret incumbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040906Z">
        <seg>The Virgin paled with confusion, fearing that this sign would reveal how devoted she was to penitential exercises.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expavit insperato gaudio, remque trepida ad Rosam in proximo cubiculo nentem retulit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055110Z">
        <seg>She burst out with unexpected joy and, trembling, ran to Rosa, who was spinning in the next room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Expleto opere, disparuit, &amp; ecce cordis effigiem plene ac scitissime formatum in veste Rosæ, quam a papilione ibi depictam, relictamque, conspicue observarunt puellæ, quotquot in eodem conclavi laborabant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044120Z">
        <seg>When the work was finished, she vanished, and behold, the image of a heart, fully and most skillfully formed, was seen on Rose's garment, which, left there by the butterfly depicted on it, was clearly observed by the girls who were working in the same room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Extimuit mater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035433Z">
        <seg>The mother was terrified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211730Z">
        <seg>Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Facile ex internis criteriis, quæ a sancta Catharina Senensi didicerat, agnovit Rosa latentem sub umbra tutelarem angelum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051812Z">
        <seg>Rosa easily recognized, by the internal criteria she had learned from Saint Catherine of Siena, the guardian angel hidden in the shade.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est: cum archiepiscopo prorex, &amp; uterque, tum ecclesiasticus, tum secularis regni senatus, inito consilio, collatisque suffragiis, Limanam D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054255Z">
        <seg>It was done: with the archbishop as pro-regent, and both the ecclesiastical and secular senates of the kingdom, having held a council and cast their votes, appointed D. to Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043909Z">
        <seg>It is done.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factum, ut ille imperaverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054007Z">
        <seg>It was done, as he had commanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Factus est deinde voti compos, dum minime cogitabat: nam superna providentia hunc postea Rosæ non modo confessarium dedit, sed &amp; præceptorem, cui Virgo obedientiam usque ad mortem velut regulari suo superiori exhibuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055830Z">
        <seg>Thus his vow was fulfilled when he least expected it: for by divine providence he was later given to Rose not only as a confessor but also as a teacher, to whom the Virgin showed obedience unto death, just as she did to her regular superior.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fama erat, Rosam impetrare omnia, quæcumque apud sacram imaginem ab augustissima Rosarii Regina serio flagitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054407Z">
        <seg>It was said that Rosa could obtain whatever she earnestly requested from the sacred image of the most august Queen of the Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Familiare quoque sibi effecit quoddam orandi genus altissimum, luminosum, ignitum, quo nominatim gloriosa Dei attributa mente pervolvens, singulis proprium latriæ cultum exhibebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044926Z">
        <seg>He also established for himself a certain highest, luminous, and fiery mode of prayer, by which, mentally reviewing the glorious attributes of God one by one, he offered to each its proper worship of latria.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fasciam præbento quinque coronæ Domini, 5 dies inediæ, totidem stationes in reverentiam suæ circumcisionis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053601Z">
        <seg>I offer the Lord's cincture: five crowns, five days of fasting, and as many stations in reverence of His circumcision.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fassa est aliquando seraphica Catharina beato Raymundo&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; confessori suo, cum nullo hostium æque ut cum somno, operosius sibi fuisse confligendum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043016Z">
        <seg>Seraphic Catherine once confessed to her blessed confessor Raymond, that she had to struggle more diligently against no enemy than against sleep.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fassus est postea, multoties inter arcana illius fori alloquia se obmutuisse ad profunditatem &amp; eruditionem solidorum discursuum, quibus Rosa prompte, facillime, &amp; ex ordine satisfaciebat quæstionibus præarduis &amp; abstrusis, ac subito se agnovisse spiritum altiorem, qui in Virgine loquebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050705Z">
        <seg>He later confessed that, many times during the secret colloquies of that forum, he had been silenced by the depth and erudition of the solid discourses, by which Rosa promptly, most easily, and in order satisfied the most arduous and abstruse questions, and suddenly recognized a higher spirit speaking in the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fatebatur ingenua Rosa, ad omnem illius picturæ intuitum novo se perfundi calore &amp; gaudio, subsultare præcordia, inflammari spiritum, eo quod divinus ille Puerulus videbatur sibi nunc blando arrisu salutem dicere, nunc suavi radiorum effluxu animam transfigere, aliquando etiam curta brachiola foras protendere, velut in amplexum Rosæ jamjam ultro procursurus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054125Z">
        <seg>The ingenuous Rosa confessed that at every glance of that painting she was filled with a new warmth and joy, her heart leaping for delight, her spirit was inflamed, because that divine Little Boy seemed now to greet her with a gentle smile, now to pierce her soul with a sweet effulgence of rays, and at times even to stretch out his short little arms, as if about to rush into Rosa's embrace of his own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fatebatur, sibi permolestum, ac grave esse, inter feminarum colloquia cernere pomposas vestes, pallia, fasciasque pretiosas, interesse otiosis fabulis, distineri reciprocis tot inanium urbanitatum ritibus: præcipue tamen dolebat, se neque in domestico sui recessus asylo tutam a visitationibus, liberamque esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043444Z">
        <seg>She admitted that it was exceedingly troublesome and burdensome to her to see, amid women's conversations, pompous garments, cloaks, and precious headbands, to be drawn into idle tales, to be distracted by so many reciprocal, vain urbanities and rituals. Above all, she lamented that she was neither safe from visits nor free in her own private domestic retreat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fecit confestim Rosa, ut sibi permissum erat, summæque curæ summum adjunxit secretum, adeo ut neque post suum obitum (nam tunc primum res innotuit) secundus iste pœnarum lectulus usquam potuerit inveniri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042942Z">
        <seg>Rosa immediately did as she had been permitted and added the utmost secrecy to the utmost care, so that not even after her death (for it was only then that the matter became known) could this second bed of punishment be found anywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Felices spinæ, quibus de tali manu tale successit in cælis diadema!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042437Z">
        <seg>Blessed are the spines, for such a hand has bestowed upon them a diadem in the heavens!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Festus erat dies beatissimæ Virginis in templo purificatæ, quando internuntia hæc trias, Mariæ de Oliva (gratulando magis, quam flagitando) exposuit Rosæ suæ constans desiderium fabricandi sibi cellulam angustam ac solitariam, in qua sine confessarii permissu nullius admitteret conspectum aut alloquium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043612Z">
        <seg>It was the feast of the Blessed Virgin's purification in the temple when the three nuns, among them Mary of Oliva (more in congratulation than in reproach), laid before Rose her steadfast desire to build for herself a narrow, solitary cell in which, without her confessor's permission, she would admit no one's presence or conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Filia, ne quos innocentes oneraret furti imputatio, lepide affata matrem, “Quid (inquit) jacturam tuam incertumque authorem dubitabunda incusas?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061116Z">
        <seg>The daughter, so as not to burden any innocent person with an accusation of theft, sweetly said to her mother, "Why do you hesitantly accuse your loss and its uncertain author?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Filius tandem fidejubebat pro Matre;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043559Z">
        <seg>The son finally stood as surety for his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fimbrias ac limbos tam straguli, quam fasciolarum ambiunto 33 sacræ Communiones, 33 interventus Missarum, 33 horæ mentalis orationis, 33 Orationes dominicæ, angelicæque Salutationes, totidem vicibus recitatio Symboli Apostolorum, cum &lt;b3&gt;Gloria Patri&lt;/b3&gt;, &amp; antiphona &lt;b4&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b4&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053604Z">
        <seg>33 recitations of the Fimbriæ et limbos tam straguli, quam fasciolarum, 33 Holy Communions, 33 Masses offered, 33 hours of mental prayer, 33 Our Fathers and Hail Marys, the Apostles' Creed recited the same number of times, with the Glory be to the Father and the antiphon Salve Regina;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Finitis demum omnibus, ac imagine decenter exornata, Rosa fatigatis sociabus compatiens, “Ite (inquit) respirate, lentaque deambulatione pariter vos reficite: nam lassæ estis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055228Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055228Z">
        <seg>When at last everything was finished and the image had been properly adorned, Rosa, commiserating with her weary companions, said, "Go," she said, "breathe, and refresh yourselves with a leisurely stroll, for you are tired."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Flos es, ac florem amas?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051239Z">
        <seg>Are you a flower, and do you love a flower?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fluebat sanguis in humerum, nec Rosa exhorruit tincturam suam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034857Z">
        <seg>Blood flowed onto the shoulder, and Rosa did not recoil from her own stain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forma ingentis molossi erat, villosa, tetra, picea: lucem, qua videretur, dabant solæ rubentes flammæ, quas naribus, oculis, auribus affatim protrudebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051924Z">
        <seg>The form was that of a huge molosser, shaggy, dark, and pitch-black; the only light by which it could be seen came from the red flames it was spewing from its nostrils, eyes, and ears in abundance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Formatur fere in pilulas, quo facilius deglutiri possit, &amp; in ventriculo dilui, indeque transire in venas.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045946Z">
        <seg>It is formed into almost pill-like shapes so that it can be more easily swallowed, dissolved in the stomach, and then pass into the veins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forte Rosa in Seraphicæ magistræ suæ bicolori habitu attentius defixa, secum de capessendo Dominicani Ordinis amictu deliberabat anceps;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040153Z">
        <seg>Chance had Rosa, more attentively fixed on her Seraphic mother in her two-colored habit, undecidedly deliberating with herself whether to take the habit of the Dominican Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forte de proximo conclavi hanc invita percipiebat Rosa confabulationem, sed laudum suarum nauseam non ferens, exsiliit præ dolore, inque cubiculum interius, ubi filiola quæstoris Michaëla erat, sese proripuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040605Z">
        <seg>Rosa happened to overhear this conversation from the next room, but unable to bear the nausea of her own praises, she leapt up in pain and rushed into the inner chamber where the questor's little daughter, Michaela, was.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Forte hanc petebat umbram, quæ ambiebat a Domino custodiri ut pupilla oculi, &amp; sub umbra alarum ejus protegi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051824Z">
        <seg>Perhaps he sought this shadow, which surrounded the Lord, to be kept as the pupil of the eye, and to be protected under the shadow of His wings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055749Z">
        <seg>Brother</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Francisca sat certa, se incolumitatem suam Rosæ orationibus debere, Virgini eventum retulit, ac pro benevola cura gratias egit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055315Z">
        <seg>Francisca was quite certain that she owed her safety to Rose's prayers, recounted the event to the Virgin, and gave thanks for her benevolent care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscus Solanus, in vita &amp; post mortem, constanti fama sanctitatis ac prodigiorum gloria clarus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, pro concione obscurius forte nonnulla protulerat, quæ Limensium vulgus prave intellecta detorserat in sensum prorsus alienum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041541Z">
        <seg>Francis Solanus, both during his life and after his death, renowned for his constant reputation of holiness and the glory of his miracles, had perhaps delivered certain things more obscurely in his sermon, which the common people of Lima, having misunderstood them, twisted into a meaning entirely foreign to them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Franciscæ Urtado de Bustamante: “Utinam idonea ratio modusve occurreret adornandi fugam ad barbarorum provincias, ut illic ab idololatris pro Jesu Christo immanissime trucidemur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060313Z">
        <seg>Francisca Urtado de Bustamante: "If only a suitable plan or method could be found for decorating the escape to the provinces of the barbarians, so that there we might be most horribly slaughtered by the idolaters for the sake of Jesus Christ."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Frater Petrus de Loaysa Ordinis Prædicatorum, qui cupiens evidentius experiri, quam firmiter in tantæ promissionis fide sibimet constaret Virgo, in quadam ejus confessione rem puerilem ac mininam cœpit attollere tamquam dubiam, ac solicitiori dignam examine, visurus, quid improvisus nec forte inutilis hic terror operaretur in Virgine, quam tanta (ut noverat) divinæ gratiæ securitas possidebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062002Z">
        <seg>Brother Peter of Loaysa of the Order of Preachers, desiring to experience more clearly how firmly the Virgin stood by the faith of so great a promise, in one of her confessions began to raise a childish and insignificant matter as if it were doubtful, and worthy of a more careful examination, he was to see what this sudden and not perhaps useless terror would work in the Virgin, whom such security of divine grace, as he knew, possessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuerat is truncus ex arbore, quam illic pacay&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; vocant indigenæ, excavatus in medio, ut caput Virginis collumque exciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042821Z">
        <seg>It was a trunk from a tree, which the natives call pacay, hollowed out in the middle to receive the head and neck of the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuere &amp; alii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050027Z">
        <seg>There were others as well.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fuit, qui gratis offerret ædes suas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061203Z">
        <seg>There was one who offered his house for free.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Fundatrix, eademque cœnobii tunc præfecta, jusserat omnes perquiri angulos, excuti scrinia, lustrari singula, sicubi amissum pignus inveniretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035641Z">
        <seg>The foundress, who was also the abbess at the time, had ordered every corner to be searched, the chests to be ransacked, and everything to be gone through, in case the lost pledge could be found.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Galli, quod Joannes Nicotius, regius aliquando in Lusitania orator, ejus semen primus ad reginam, regis Galliæ matrem detulerit, illiusque facultates docuerit &lt;b2&gt;Nicotianam &amp; herbam reginæ&lt;/b2&gt; nuncuparunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060946Z">
        <seg>The French, because John Nicot, once a royal envoy to Portugal, was the first to bring its seed to the queen, mother of the King of France, and to teach its properties, called it the Nicotian herb or the queen's herb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gaudebat, inter flores &amp; odorifera plantaria pariter &amp; crucem oculis suis continuo obversari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054940Z">
        <seg>He rejoiced, continually turning his eyes among the flowers and fragrant plants alike and the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gavisa domina, de modo ac mora curationis inquisivit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054019Z">
        <seg>The lady, pleased, inquired about the manner and duration of the treatment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gazam prodigis soli Numinis thesauro inferendam?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053402Z">
        <seg>Are you going to lavish the treasure of the one and only God on Gaza?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Germanus nil ultra simplicem jocum &amp; animi oblectamentum suspicans, replicuit: “Spargam &amp; ego rosas in aëra, &amp; forte, quam tu, jaciam altius”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062526Z">
        <seg>Germanus, suspecting nothing more than a simple jest and pastime, replied, "I too will scatter roses into the air, and perhaps I will throw them higher than you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Grande id fuit victrici solatium, grandis &amp; eruditione plenissima lectio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052221Z">
        <seg>It was a great consolation to the victor, a great and most instructive lesson.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Grandia sunt ac prolixa, quæ circa mansuetissimam Rosæ humilitatem notavit, edixitque Gundisalvi domus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040707Z">
        <seg>He noted the great and lengthy things concerning the most gentle humility of Rose, and he ordered them to be read in Gundisalvi's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gratiæ sympathia hæc erat, quæ Rosam docebat amare secreta, quæ non noverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044151Z">
        <seg>This was the sympathetic grace that taught Rosa to love the secrets she did not know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gundisalvi de la Massa, e paterno domicilio invitari ad suarum ædium gratuitum contubernium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035613Z">
        <seg>Gundisalvi de la Massa, to be invited from his father's home to a free lodging in his own house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Gundisalvus, qui forte cum medico aderat, tristi manus spectaculo æger, oratorio abscessit, velut nimiæ compassioni impar.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055352Z">
        <seg>Gundisalvus, who by chance was present with the physician, distressed by the sad spectacle, withdrew from the oratory, as though he were unfit for such excessive compassion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Habebat in paupere thesauro coronam precariam ex corallis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043519Z">
        <seg>She had in her poor treasure a precious crown of coral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac ambitione pia Virgo obibat mulierum valetudinaria, quotiescumque honestæ comitivæ aderat occasio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061259Z">
        <seg>With this pious ambition, the Virgin would visit the women's infirmaries whenever there was an opportunity to be in the company of honorable women.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac fiducia cellulam reliqui, ad te huc veni, consedi expectatura hic servum quæstoris, &amp; cum parente beneficium divisura”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051753Z">
        <seg>With that confidence I left the cell, came here to you, sat down here to wait for the questor's servant, and to share the benefit with my parent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac fornace probatur aurum, nitescit charitas, loricatur mascula virtute robustus amor, assuescens diligere non tam delicias Dei, quam Deum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050417Z">
        <seg>In this furnace gold is tested, charity shines, and love, armored in robust masculine virtue, grows accustomed to loving not so much the delights of God as God Himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac permissione obtenta, æque solicita erat Rosa, ut eum numerum non transiliret, quam ut impleret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041603Z">
        <seg>Having obtained this permission, Rosa was just as anxious not to exceed that number as she was to fill it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac specie circuibat orantem Rosam latratu horrisono, rictu immani, ferociterque dentato, lingua fœdissime exerta, odore sulphureo, simulque pendulas aures, caudam, villos arrigebat, quasi impetum facturus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051927Z">
        <seg>In this manner it prowled around the praying Rose, barking with a horrid bark, a monstrous, fiercely-toothed mouth, its foul tongue lolling, with a sulfurous stench, while simultaneously pricking its hanging ears, tail, and bristles, as if about to pounce.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hac ætate velabor, profitebor religionem severam, arctam, difficilem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062836Z">
        <seg>In this age, shall I embrace and profess a strict, narrow, and difficult religion?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc Christiani conquirendarum Indiarum auspicem ex Hispaniis secum advectam, inter prima Limanæ urbis molimina, primo ibidem veræ religionis templo (cui a sanctissimo Rosario coævum nomen est) solemni ritu collocarunt, adstructo Fratrum Prædicatorum insigni cœnobio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054227Z">
        <seg>This, brought from Spain by the Christians as a banner for the conquest of the Indies, was, among the first undertakings of the city of Lima, solemnly placed in the first temple of the true religion there (which bears the name of the Most Holy Rosary, contemporary with it), with the founding of a distinguished monastery of the Friars Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc interim ego donavi Montoyæ pauperrimæ, quæ, quod hujuscemodi veste careat, ecclesiam decenter adire, Missisque &amp; concionibus adsistere non potest.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061122Z">
        <seg>In the meantime, I have given this to the very poor Montoya, who, because she lacks such clothing, cannot attend church decently or attend Mass and sermons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc itaque nunc in remotiorem horti angulum, nunc in solarium domus sevocatam, flexis humi geniculis precabatur, ut (quod suismet ipsa manibus tenella nondum poterat) humerulos suos vi laterum ibi sparsorum oneraret: sub ea mole, gravique obtritu, durabat parvula in oratione prolixa &amp; laboriosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041628Z">
        <seg>And so she, now plucked to a more remote corner of the garden, now to the house's solarium, would kneel on the ground and pray that (which she herself with her tender hands could not yet do) she might load her little shoulders with the force of the stones scattered there: under that weight and crushing pressure, the little one endured in a long and laborious prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc nectaream sanguinis &amp; aquæ mamillam non ori, sed cordi Virginis benigne applicuit: hic illa debilitati suæ pharmacum, hic vitalem succum invenit &amp; traxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051118Z">
        <seg>This nectarous breast of blood and water she did not apply to her mouth, but kindly to the Virgin's heart: here she found and drew the medicine for her weakness, here she drew the vital sap.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc posteriorem, decimo circiter ante obitum anno, hoc est, paulo postquam Dominicani Ordinis togam sumpserat, sibi imposuit, nec nisi cum vita deposuit: nimirum, ut cœperat attentius contemplari spineam sui Redemptoris coronam, in caput S. Catharinæ Senensis mirabiliter translatam, minime sibi quiescendum duxit, nisi eamdem pariter in suum inde verticem, æmulatione qua posset religiosiori, accerseret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042108Z">
        <seg>She imposed this latter upon herself about ten years before her death, that is, shortly after she had taken the habit of the Dominican Order, and she did not lay it aside until her life ended: For, having begun to contemplate more attentively the crown of thorns of his Redeemer, miraculously transferred to the head of St. Catherine of Siena, she judged it by no means fitting to rest until she, too, had brought that same crown to her own head with as pious an emulation as she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc sæpius in secreta domus parte oravit, exoravit, adegit Rosa, ut se dejectam humi, nunc pronam, nunc supinam calcaret pedibus, ac velut contemptibile peripsema studiose plantis detereret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040509Z">
        <seg>Rosa often prayed, implored, and compelled her in the secret part of the house to lie down on the ground, now prone, now supine, and to trample her with her feet, and as if she were a contemptible rag, to diligently wear her away with her soles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc vero in negotio, de quo agimus, Rosæ non defuisse ad assequendum in symbolicis prævisionibus genuinum ostenti sensum, patebit ex sequentibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062511Z">
        <seg>However, in this matter under discussion, it will become clear from the following that Rosa did not lack the ability to grasp the genuine meaning of the symbolic visions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hanc vigilandi facilitatem juvere longæ inediæ, cilicii, lectique carduëtum, frigidæ omnisque potus diutina abstinentia, profunda meditatio &amp; applicatæ mentis ad superna tenax defixio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043029Z">
        <seg>This facility for vigilance is aided by prolonged fasting, by wearing sackcloth and the hair-shirt, by abstaining for a long time from all food and drink, by deep meditation, and by the steadfast fixing of the mind on heavenly things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has cum Jesulo Sponso delicias Virgini frequentes obtigisse, imo fortasse quotidianas argumento est, quod si ille tardaret interdum, nec solito more adesset, Rosa querulis, at submissis vocibus, quasi secum perplexe sermocinans, ita lamentabatur:“ Hora est, &amp; nondum approperat?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050935Z">
        <seg>That she had often enjoyed these delights with Jesus, her Spouse, indeed perhaps daily, is evidenced by the fact that if he were ever delayed and did not come as usual, Rosa would lament in querulous yet subdued tones, as if perplexedly speaking to herself, saying: "It is time, and he has not yet come?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has innocenti æmulatione ostentabant silenti Rosæ, hortantes, ut &amp; suam promeret, aut formaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043224Z">
        <seg>They displayed these with innocent rivalry to the silent Rosa, urging her to produce or fashion her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Has rugas, hoc tonitru, hunc pavorem videbatur sibi experiri mœstissima Rosa, dum laborabat sub hora sui deliquii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045844Z">
        <seg>The most sorrowful Rosa seemed to experience these wrinkles, this thunder, this terror, as she labored under the hour of her fainting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Henrico Susone&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; id, velut compertum, tradit historia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045834Z">
        <seg>History relates it as a matter of fact.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Herinaceum credidisses, non rosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041848Z">
        <seg>You would have thought it was a hedgehog, not a rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu me, quam procul disto a perfecta, intima, robustissima tui dilectione!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053040Z">
        <seg>Alas, how far I am from a perfect, intimate, and most robust love for you!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu mihi!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050950Z">
        <seg>Woe is me!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu tantilla soporis indulgentia, quantam subinde jacturam infert!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051412Z">
        <seg>Alas, how little indulgence in sleep, and what a loss it brings!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Heu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053148Z">
        <seg>Alas!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Rosa exterrita, quasi colubrum pressisset pede, resilivit, expalluit, obmutuit, probe conscia, si respondendum sit, quanta &amp; rerum copia, &amp; verborum inopia sibi foret laborandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050442Z">
        <seg>Rosa, startled as if she had trodden on a snake, recoiled, turned pale, and fell silent, well aware that, if she were to reply, she would have to contend with both the abundance of the subject matter and her own poverty of words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Rosa fratrem intuita, exspectabat, quid ille decerneret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044449Z">
        <seg>Here Rosa, looking at her brother, waited to see what he would decide.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Rosa, mendacem larvam sonoris vocibus detestata, lemuri dorsum obvertit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052209Z">
        <seg>Here Rosa, detesting the lying mask with sonorous voices, turned her back on the lemur.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Sponsus subridens, “Ne credideris (inquit) dilecta mea, solam te de feminis esse, quam duris hisce laboribus occuparim”: statimque proximi conclavis ostio latissime diducto, ostendit Rosæ lapidariam officinam peramplam ac speciosam, quam solæ implebant virgines arduo labori gnaviter intentæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044207Z">
        <seg>The Groom, smiling, said, "Do not think, my beloved, that you are the only woman I have occupied with these hard labors": and immediately, with the door of the adjoining room thrown wide open, he revealed to Rose a vast and splendid stone-cutting workshop, filled by the virgins alone, who were diligently intent on the arduous work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Virgo inops subitanei consilii tantillam impetravit dilationem, dum adiret confessarium, cui anxie protestata est, nisi præceptum revocaretur, se non posse non morem gerere matri hoc gestamen imperanti: malle se huic quantumvis exosi ornatus redimiculo suum illigare verticem, quam resistendo solvere jus, quo matris obligabatur imperio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035427Z">
        <seg>This penniless virgin obtained only a brief postponement of the sudden decree while she went to her confessor, to whom she earnestly protested that unless the order were revoked, she could not but obey her mother's command to wear this adornment; she would rather bind her head with this most odious ornament than, by resisting, forfeit the duty by which she was bound to her mother's authority.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Virgo primum ingenue fassa ac protestata est, se quidem illud mortalibus oculis minime conspecturam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062706Z">
        <seg>Here the Virgin first ingenuously confessed and protested that she would by no means behold it with mortal eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic Virgo ultra solitum cœpit vehementius succuti amoris stimulis, ut nec voci temperare jam posset, quin erecta in pedes, fervidissime ac sonore affaretur sacram imaginem, quasi sola in oratorio fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053703Z">
        <seg>This virgin, more than usual, began to be stirred more vehemently by the pangs of love, so that she could no longer restrain her voice; instead, rising to her feet, she addressed the sacred image with the greatest fervor and in a loud voice, as if she were alone in the oratory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic ab arboribus ad flores transfertur.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054807Z">
        <seg>Here it is transferred from trees to flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic autem pro&lt;/e8&gt; convenientia affectuum &lt;e9&gt;sumitur, sicut&lt;/e9&gt; &lt;s10&gt;ἀντιπάθεια&lt;/s10&gt; &lt;e11&gt;pro&lt;/e11&gt; repugnantia affectuum, &lt;e12&gt;de quo Castellus consuli potest&lt;/e12&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054748Z">
        <seg>Here, however, it is taken for the congruence of affections, just as antipathy is taken for the repugnance of affections, on which Castell can be consulted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic demum in se reflexa mater, postquam se Ludovicæ comiter excusarat, pensitare cœpit attentius, quid sibi velit, quod Rosæ oculos tunc potissimum obsideret importuna ista defluxio, quandocumque honestarum matronarum visitatio, aut domi excipienda, aut foris exequenda instabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043407Z">
        <seg>The mother, now at last reflecting on herself, after she had politely excused herself to Louisa, began to ponder more attentively what she herself wanted, why that troublesome discharge was besieging Rose's eyes just then, whenever a visit from respectable matrons was to be received at home or paid abroad.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic ei tenor est: “Domine mi Jesu Christe, Deus &amp; homo, vere conditor &amp; Redemptor noster, quod umquam te offenderim, equidem mente discrucior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053058Z">
        <seg>This is his tenor: "My Lord Jesus Christ, God and man, truly our founder and Redeemer, for whatever I have ever offended you, I am truly tormented in mind;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic igitur non mutata, sed duplicata fuit obediendi occasio: siquidem humilis Rosa paterno tecto, at non subjectione exemptam se reputans, qua promptitudine domi propriæ, eadem alienæ parebat, nec solum mandatis Gundisalvi aut uxoris, sed ad nutum quoque filiarum, domesticæque familiæ, usque ad infima domus mancipia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035619Z">
        <seg>Thus the occasion to obey was not changed but doubled: for humble Rosa, though she considered herself free from the paternal roof, did not deem herself exempt from submission; with the same promptness with which she obeyed in her own home, she obeyed in a foreign one, not only the commands of Gundisalvus or his wife, but also at the beck of their daughters and of the household staff, down to the lowest servants of the house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic igitur puerilis sui lusus &amp; crepundiorum exitus fuit, ut mirabilis, sic felicissimus: nam rarus est hic gratiæ tractus, qui Rosam ab innocentibus jocis unico subitoque passu deduxit in illam maturitatem judicii, quo simul nosset æstimare Dei celsitudinem, æternitatis pretium, &amp; virginei floris arcanam prærogativam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035034Z">
        <seg>Thus, this childish play of hers and the outcome of her toys were, as was marvelous, so exceedingly happy: for it is a rare stroke of grace that led Rosa, from innocent amusements, with a single, sudden step, into that maturity of judgment by which she might at once know how to appreciate God's majesty, the price of eternity, and the secret prerogative of the virgin's flower.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic illi a prima pueritia sensus fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053322Z">
        <seg>This sense had been with him since early childhood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic mirifico naturæ lusu, pleraque Dominicæ mortis nostræque redemptionis instrumenta, velut in exigui proscenii compendium, sese conspicue explicant: nam inter foliola, cruentis maculis venuste dispuncta, assurgit columnula flagellati Dei statumen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041250Z">
        <seg>Here, in this marvelous play of nature, most of the instruments of the Lord's death and our redemption unfold themselves conspicuously, as if in a miniature stage-set: for among the little leaves, charmingly dotted with bloody spots, the little column of the God of the scourge rises up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic misera Virgo secretum suum prodere debuit, ut ab ancilla consilium modumque exquireret, quo solvatur catena, priusquam experrecta mater in auxilium utrique accurrat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041727Z">
        <seg>This poor virgin had to reveal her secret in order to ask the maid for advice and a way to free herself, before her mother, upon waking, would rush to help them both.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic morbus, quod vitæ minus putetur insidiari, pro quantitate miseriæ commiserationem, pro magnitudine doloris remedium nusquam invenit aut levamen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052627Z">
        <seg>This disease, because it is considered less life-threatening, finds no remedy or relief anywhere for the amount of misery, nor for the magnitude of the pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic nimirum spiritus erat seraphicæ suæ magistræ, quæ &amp; ipsa optaverat (si fieri posset) suopte corpusculo obturari gehennæ fauces, ne tam populosæ animarum catervæ illuc deinceps immigrarent, suæque frustrarent lytrum redemptionis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060406Z">
        <seg>This was indeed the spirit of her seraphic mistress, who herself had wished (if it could be done) to be sealed up in her own little body to block the jaws of hell, so that such populous throngs of souls would no longer migrate there and frustrate her ransom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic resalutandi pariter &amp; excusandi formulas tentabat proloqui, sed frustra: relabebatur enim in eadem Dei &amp; Rosæ præconia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044622Z">
        <seg>He tried in vain to utter the customary greetings and excuses; for he kept slipping back into the same praises of God and Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic rursus inter adjectiva&lt;/e6&gt; anareticum &lt;e7&gt;&amp;&lt;/e7&gt; anachoreticum &lt;e8&gt;est analogia, qualem biographus sæpius affectat, ut jam supra monuimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041426Z">
        <seg>Here again, among the adjectives anareticum and anachoreticum, there is an analogy such as the biographer often affects, as we have already noted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hic, ut erat in quæstoris ædibus frequens ac pene domesticus, ibidem ex negotiorum occasione Rosam persæpe viderat, sæpius de illius sanctimonia vitæque singularitate mira audierat, ita de Virgine sublimiter, ut dignum erat, sentiebat, præsertim quod sciret, Gundisalvum Rosæ precibus meritisque pluries gravissimis infirmitatibus fuisse liberatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061435Z">
        <seg>He, as he was a frequent and almost domestic visitor to the questor's house, had there often seen Rosa on business occasions, and had heard more than once of her sanctity and the wonderful singularity of her life; so he thought of the Virgin in a sublime manner, as was fitting, especially since he knew, Gundisalvus had been freed from very serious illnesses by Rosa's prayers and merits on several occasions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hieronymi sacellum traxit, illic exporrecto in hilaritatem vultu, pavidas hortari cœpit ad subeundum secum martyrium, occasionem hanc nimis pretiosam esse ac fortunatam, videlicet non quomodocumque, sed sub ipso sanctæ Hostiæ conspectu nostiam cadere, coram &amp; pro divina illa Victima victimam cædi, pro exposito Christi Corpore animam exponere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060214Z">
        <seg>He dragged Jerome into the chapel, and there, with a face beaming with joy, he began to urge the fearful ones to undergo martyrdom with him, saying that this was an opportunity too precious and fortunate, namely, not just any kind of death, but to fall under the very gaze of the Holy Host, to be slain before and for that divine Victim, to lay down one's life for the exposed Body of Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hilarior tamen fuit Rosa, quod talem nacta esset, cui tam occulte, &amp; pie viscera suæ misericordiæ impertiret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061218Z">
        <seg>Rosa, however, was all the more joyful, because she had found someone to whom she could so secretly and piously extend the bowels of her mercy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Ferdinandus (id fratri nomen erat) tot morarum impatiens, ac velut superfluæ pietati Sororis admurmurans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054859Z">
        <seg>Hence Ferdinand (for that was his brother's name), impatient of so many delays, and as it were murmuring at his sister's superfluous piety;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Ludovica de Melgareyo femina sanctissima tanti Rosam faciebat, ut, sicubi cum ea concurrisset, nonnisi flexis humi genibus exciperet repugnantem honorari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050712Z">
        <seg>Hence, the most holy Ludovica of Melgareyo held Rosam in such high esteem that, whenever she met her, she would greet her with nothing less than a humble bow on the ground, honoring her even when she resisted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Rosa velut armillam brachio circumligarat, parvum ex minutissimis orbiculis rosariolum: cujus granula occulte trahebat ad singulas salutationes angelicas, etiam ubi alloquiis &amp; conversationi se reperiebat implicitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045134Z">
        <seg>Thus Rosa had fastened a bracelet of the finest little beads around her arm, whose beads she secretly drew at each Angelic Salutation, even when she found herself absorbed in conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc Rosæ colloquia, salutationes, responsa, jugiter amoris divini mentione condiebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053130Z">
        <seg>Henceforth, Rose's conversations, greetings, and responses were continually seasoned with mention of divine love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc apud Limenses suos Rosa, vulgariter altera Catharina Senensis appellabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051443Z">
        <seg>Hence, among her fellow Limans, Rosa was commonly called the other Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc apud antiquos celebrantur apes Hybleæ, sicut patet ex hoc carmine Virgiliano:&lt;/e0&gt; Hyblæis apibus florem depasta salicti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044721Z">
        <seg>Hence the Hylæan bees are celebrated among the ancients, as is clear from this Virgilian verse: "The Hylæan bees have stripped the willow of its blossom."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc biographi allusio ad nomen&lt;/e2&gt; S. Rosæ, &lt;e3&gt;&amp; hoc epitheton&lt;/e3&gt; rosata &lt;e4&gt;saltem in idiomate Italico satis apta est&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035204Z">
        <seg>Hence the biographers' allusion to the name&lt;/e2&gt; of St. Rose, &lt;e3&gt;and this epithet&lt;/e3&gt; "rosata" &lt;e4&gt;is at least suitably apt in the Italian idiom.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc carmen illud Ovidianum:&lt;/e5&gt; Centum luminibus cinctum caput Argus habebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040937Z">
        <seg>Hence that Ovidian poem: Argus had a head surrounded by a hundred eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc colligimus, cur Baudrandus in Geographia sua insulam&lt;/e4&gt; Tabago &lt;e5&gt;etiam&lt;/e5&gt; Nicotianam &lt;e6&gt;vocaverit&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060955Z">
        <seg>From this we conclude why Baudrand in his Geography called the island of Tabago also Nicotiana.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc conversa ad titionem, quem manu gerebat, undequaque extinctum vidit, mirata se tanto tempore illic substitisse, dum totus paulatim extingui titio potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045044Z">
        <seg>Then, turning to the torch she had been carrying, she saw it completely extinguished, and was amazed that she had stood there for so long while it could gradually be put out by the tinder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc etiam fortasse nomen acceperunt nonnullæ Sanctæ, quæ in Ecclesia Catholica coluntur.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T213902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035223Z">
        <seg>Hence, perhaps, some of the Saints who are venerated in the Catholic Church also received their names.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc factum est, quod pleraque illustria ejus opera, visiones, &amp; arcanæ Spiritus deliciæ sic delituerint sub taciturnæ humilitatis abstruso inumbraculo, ut matris, sodalium, confessariorum assidua sagacitas, vix ad paucorum notitiam potuerit umquam penetrare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040813Z">
        <seg>Hence it came about that most of her illustrious works, visions, and the secret delights of the Spirit were so concealed under the obscure veil of silent humility that the constant vigilance of her mother, companions, and confessors could scarcely ever penetrate to the knowledge of a few.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc fuit, quod vocatus ad excipiendam Virginis confessionem, dicente subsacrista, in templo ipsum expectari a Rosula seu Rosella, velut irreverenter locutum increpuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050647Z">
        <seg>Hence, when he was called to hear the Virgin's confession, he rebuked the subdeacon for saying that Rosula, or Rosella, was waiting for him in the church, as if he had spoken irreverently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc in Filiæ vultum curosius intenta, vidit, non fingi, quod apparebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043410Z">
        <seg>Hence, looking more closely at her daughter's face, she saw that what appeared to be the case was not a sham.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc in ipsius anima mirabiles illi effectus, tamque isti Sacramento connaturales ac proprii, quos nemo novit, nisi qui accipit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055848Z">
        <seg>Hence in his very soul those marvelous effects, so innate and proper to this Sacrament, which no one knows except the one who receives it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc itum ad sinistram, quæ ut pectori avelleretur, pari anhelitu laborandum fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044703Z">
        <seg>From there, turning to the left, it was necessary to struggle with equal breath so that it might be torn from the chest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc mater anxia, sibique &amp; familiæ probrum metuens, arguebat Filiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062802Z">
        <seg>Hence the anxious mother, fearing disgrace for herself and her family, rebuked her daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc opus fuit confessariorum imperio, qui Virgini præcepere vespertinum lactucæ ac soporiferorum seminum usum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051313Z">
        <seg>Hence, it was necessary to use the authority of the confessors, who prescribed to the Virgin the evening use of lettuce and soporific seeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc patet, Majores nostros non potuisse Acta hujus sancti præsulis illustrare, cum judicium Sedis Apostolicæ illis exspectandum fuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035213Z">
        <seg>Hence it is clear that our ancestors could not have illustrated the Acts of this holy prelate, since they had to await the judgment of the Apostolic See.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc per Bilacongæ vasta juga descenditur in vallem Xaquixaguanam (quam Garcilassus vocat Sacsahuanam) inter altissimos montes angusto spatio conclusam, in qua veteres Peruviæ reges hortos suos &amp; viridaria habuerunt &lt;e1&gt;&amp;c.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054717Z">
        <seg>From here, one descends the vast ridges of the Bilacongas into the Xaquixagua Valley (which Garcilaso calls Sacsahuanam), enclosed by the narrow space between the highest mountains, in which the ancient kings of Peru had their gardens and orchards &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc pluries in ipsa observarunt quæstoris filiolæ, quod, cum inter suendum Rosa filum &amp; acum trahebat sursum, una cum brachio videbatur &amp; animus elevari in exstasim, brevique morula in sublimi suspensa manus, redibat ad opus, nec aberrabat acus a puncto, quem reliquerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044849Z">
        <seg>Hence they often observed the quæstor's little daughter herself doing this: when, while sewing, she drew the thread and needle upward, her arm and spirit seemed to be lifted into ecstasy; and, after a brief moment, her hand suspended in midair, she returned to her work, and the needle never strayed from the point it had left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc statim exarsit in ipsa conatus orandi, qui &amp; formulam dictavit jaculatoriam, hanc videlicet: “Jesus sit benedictus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035018Z">
        <seg>From this, an immediate attempt to pray arose within him, which dictated the following short prayer: "Jesus be blessed."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc subitus pavor totam pervasit urbem, quasi, ex publico P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041544Z">
        <seg>Hence a sudden panic seized the entire city, as if from the public square of P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc subitus pavor totam pervasit urbem, quasi, ex publico P. Solani vaticinio, terræ hiatu proxime absorbendam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000530Z">
        <seg>Hence a sudden panic seized the entire city, as if, by the public prophecy of P. Solanus, it were about to be swallowed up by an opening in the earth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc successive tot sive scholarum, sive cœnobiorum, ac provinciarum in eum confluxerant præfecturæ: nam regiæ Universitatis Limanæ matutinus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050141Z">
        <seg>Thus, one after another, the prefectures of so many schools, monasteries, and provinces had converged on him: for he was the morning prefect of the Royal University of Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc sæpe in ea notarunt religiosæ feminæ, quod sicubi in populosissimo templo angulum occuparat, e regione majoris aræ, attentius precatura, sedebat per horas immota, defixis in aram oculis, nec prætereuntes videbat, nec ad repentinos circa se strepitus deflectebat obtutum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044855Z">
        <seg>Hence, devout women often noted that whenever she had taken a corner in the most crowded temple, opposite the high altar, to pray more attentively, she would sit for hours motionless, her eyes fixed on the altar; she neither saw those who passed by nor turned her gaze to the sudden noises around her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc sæpissime per noctem audita est dormiens, numerato, totidemque verbis reddere precatiunculas, quibus per diem prolixius invigilarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044802Z">
        <seg>Hence, she was very often heard at night, while asleep, counting and repeating in equal number the little prayers with which she had kept vigil more at length during the day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc tanto devotionis studio suebat lintea, quæ CORPORALIA dicimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060108Z">
        <seg>Hence, with such devotion, he would prepare the linens we call CORPORALS.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc tantopere afficiebatur initio Psalm.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061723Z">
        <seg>Hence he was so deeply moved at the beginning of the Psalms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc vice quadam ab illustri femina interrogata, cur non rogaret seraphicam magistram suam, ut his ærumnis potenti illius suffragio eriperetur, heroïce respondit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052534Z">
        <seg>Hence, on one occasion, when asked by a distinguished woman why she did not ask her seraphic mistress to be delivered from these torments by her powerful intercession, she heroically replied;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc vicinam mortem non aliter ac certam paradisi janitricem læta aspexit: nam ut primum Rosæ denuntiarunt medici, naturam succubuisse morbo, nec aliud restare præter decretorium diem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061844Z">
        <seg>Hence she looked upon the neighboring death with joy, as upon the certain gatekeeper of paradise: for as soon as the doctors announced to Rosa that nature had succumbed to the disease, and that nothing remained but the decisive day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc, si quidpiam falsum etiam bona fide narrabatur aliis, pro accurato veritatis zelo, placidissime corrigebat referentem, interloquendo comiter, non sic, sed tali modo rem accidisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053331Z">
        <seg>Hence, if anything false were told to others, even in good faith, out of a zeal for accurate truth, he would most calmly correct the person reporting it, interjecting politely that it had not happened that way, but in this manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc, sæpe rogata, ut hoc illudve peteret, si videretur reipublicæ, aut cujusquam privatæ salutis esse, curam in se haud gravate recipiebat: deinde a sacra imagine rediens bonos successus tanta securitate pollicebatur, quasi concessæ gratiæ diploma recepisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054410Z">
        <seg>Hence, often asked to seek this or that if it seemed to be for the good of the republic or for anyone's private welfare, she would not take on the task without some reluctance; then, returning from the sacred image, she would promise good results with such assurance as if she had received a diploma of granted grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e1&gt; dies caniculares &lt;e2&gt;nomen accipiunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054757Z">
        <seg>Hence the days get their name.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e1&gt; veredarius &lt;e2&gt;ab aliquibus pro rhedario accipitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052327Z">
        <seg>Hence a courier is taken by some for a charioteer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e2&gt; gargarismus &lt;e3&gt;a voce Græca&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;γαργαρεὼν&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;qua medici indicant eam gutturis partem, qua faucibus adjacet, &amp; quam nonnulli&lt;/e5&gt; plectrum &lt;e6&gt;sive&lt;/e6&gt; vocis refractorium &lt;e7&gt;appellant, quia ad vocis modulationem facit, ut Castellus in Lexico medico pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045406Z">
        <seg>Hence the gargle from the Greek word γαργαρεὼν, &lt;e5&gt;by which physicians indicate that part of the throat adjacent to the pharynx, and which some call the plectrum or vocal refractor, because it contributes to the modulation of the voice, as Castellus in his Medical Lexicon, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e4&gt; bronchocele &lt;e5&gt;dicitur magnus &amp; rotundus gutturis tumor, qui in aspera arteria exoritur&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043128Z">
        <seg>Hence a bronchocele is called a large, round swelling of the throat that arises in the trachea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hinc&lt;/e9&gt; &lt;s10&gt;τρίγωνος&lt;/s10&gt; triangularis &lt;e11&gt;&amp; similia originem habent&lt;/e11&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043134Z">
        <seg>Hence the triangle and similar figures have their origin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His Rosæ dormientis vultus immodice dispungebatur, adeo quod mater in genis ac fronte Filiæ persæpe notarit extemporanea hæc stigmata, nec tamen originem divinare poterat, jam secura de lana, quæ cervical implesset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042753Z">
        <seg>The face of the sleeping Rose was excessively smudged, so much so that her mother often noticed these impromptu stains on her daughter's cheeks and forehead, yet could not divine their origin, being already certain of the wool that had filled the pillow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His ad plenitudinem solatii adjecit, velle se (modo Deus permiserit) ab obitu conveniri a defuncti anima, ut si forte ulterioribus suffragiis eguerit, statim adjuvetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060840Z">
        <seg>He added to this full consolation that he wished, God willing, to be visited by the deceased's soul upon his death, so that if it should happen to need further suffrages, it might be helped at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His auditis, (pergebat Rosa) invasit me validissimus impetus, quem verbis effari nequeo, prosiliendi in medium fori, ut publice fortissimisque clamoribus vociferarer ad omnem hominum ætatem, sexum, statum: Audite populi, audite gentes universæ, ex ore Christi vobis denuntio, non adquiri gratiam sine prævia afflictione, ærumnis supra ærumnas concumulatis opus est, ut ad intimum divinæ naturæ participium, ad gloriam filiorum Dei, ad perfectam animæ pulchritudinem pertingatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052829Z">
        <seg>Upon hearing this, (Rosa continued) I was seized by a most powerful impulse, which I cannot express in words, to leap into the middle of the forum, and there to cry out with the loudest and most powerful shouts to all men of every age, sex, and station: Hear, O people, hear, O all you nations, I declare to you from the mouth of Christ, that grace is not acquired without prior affliction; that it is necessary to heap up tribulation upon tribulation, in order to attain to the innermost participation of the divine nature, to the glory of the sons of God, to the perfect beauty of the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His auditis, adeo exarsit mater, irritari se putans, ut filiam a conspectu suo abegerit, altum vociferans, “Egone monialis ero?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062830Z">
        <seg>Upon hearing this, the mother, thinking herself insulted, was so enraged that she drove her daughter from her sight, crying out loudly, "Shall I become a nun?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His cum fremitu in se prolatis (obstrue nares lector) pelvim ori apposuit, totumque illum saniosum cruorem (dicam, an cruentam saniem) ausu heroico sensim epotavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061410Z">
        <seg>Having brought these forth with a roar (reader, stop up your nostrils), he placed the basin to his mouth and with heroic daring slowly drank down all that pus-filled gore (shall I call it pus or bloody pus?).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His deliciis gustum mulcebat, quibus, ne simile condimentum abesset, persæpe cineres admiscebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041212Z">
        <seg>He would season these delicacies with ashes, so that no similar condiment would be lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His dictis, feminas (Catharina, &amp; Francisca Montoya vocabantur) dimisit domum ridentes, quod illo anni tempore, ex languido, sterilique frutice cariophylla expectaret, imo promitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055145Z">
        <seg>Having said this, he dismissed the women (Catherine and Francisca Montoya) to their home, laughing, because at that time of year they expected, indeed promised, cloves from a languid, barren shrub.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His igitur ambas manus applicabat in somnum pugnatura, ab horum tenaci complexu pendulum librabat corpusculum, dum Deiparæ Virginis Officium absolveret, his cuneolis findebat impetum somni, erudiebatur ad excubias, crucifigebat hostem, nec nisi victrix obrigidos lacertorum tendines retrahebat a charis victoriæ manubriis: sic palmam obtinebat, sed palmis callosis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043051Z">
        <seg>So she applied both hands to the struggle against sleep, her little body swinging in a pendulum from their tenacious clasp, while she finished the Office of the Virgin Mother, broke the onslaught of slumber with these little jabs, roused herself for her watch, crucified the enemy, and only as a victor did she draw back the rigid tendons of her arms from the dear handles of victory: Thus she won the palm, but with calloused palms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His igitur mensibus, etiam herbarum panisque diminutione accuratius se macerabat, donec ineunte Quadragesima, omnem sibi omnino subtraheret panem, solis malorum aureorum seminibus parcissime victitans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041315Z">
        <seg>During those months, therefore, he more strictly mortified himself by also reducing his intake of herbs and bread, until, at the beginning of Lent, he deprived himself of all bread entirely, subsisting only on the seeds of golden apples, most sparingly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His pœnis eruditur anima ad sui notitiam, dum in lucis ac tenebrarum vicissitudine discit experimento sui nihili, quid a seipsa, quid a Deo habeat, quidque inter illa &amp; ista intersit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050408Z">
        <seg>By these punishments the soul is taught to know itself, as in the alternation of light and darkness it learns by experience of its own nothingness what it has from itself, what from God, and what the difference is between the two.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His ultro citroque velut ex pacto mutuo constitutis, prudens Filia culinariam ancillam Marianam, suarum partium fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041203Z">
        <seg>With these things thus established by mutual agreement, the prudent daughter made the kitchen maid Mariana one of her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>His vero exercitationibus tantum insumebat studium &amp; curam, ac si per totam vitam suam pro illa dumtaxat unica vice communicandum sibi fuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055734Z">
        <seg>He devoted such study and care to these exercises as if he were to communicate with her for that one and only time in his entire life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hispani TABACO nominarunt ab insula quadam ejus nominis, in qua frequentissima reperitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060942Z">
        <seg>The Spaniards named it TABACO after an island of that name, on which it is found most abundantly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc Rosæ obtigit, cui tota conversatio in cælis erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050901Z">
        <seg>This happened to Rose, whose entire conversation was in the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc agone defuncta Rosa, quietem repetiit, ut eamdem ancilla repeteret, ac mane facto, velut nil grave passa, ad consuetos labores properavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041746Z">
        <seg>When this task was done, Rosa rested, so that the same maid could take her turn, and at dawn, as if she had suffered nothing serious, she hurried back to her usual labors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc de se credebat ipsa, hoc itidem credi ab aliis peranxie cupiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040531Z">
        <seg>She believed this of herself, and she was also desperately eager for others to believe it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc in tenera puella canæ prudentiæ moderamen fuit, quo sic corpusculo detractum voluit, ut simul alieno in se juri atque imperio nil detraheret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041020Z">
        <seg>This was the prudent guidance of a wise dog for the tender girl, by which he wished to withdraw from her little body so that at the same time he would take nothing away from another's right or authority over her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc ipsum Rosæ insciæ pluries coram diversis accidisse, compertum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053027Z">
        <seg>It has been discovered that this very thing happened to an unsuspecting Rose on multiple occasions in front of different people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc itaque castissimo schemate obarmavit se Rosa, ut illius præsidio a se longius repelleret omnem secularem muliebris ornatus vanitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035446Z">
        <seg>Thus, Rosa armed herself with this most chaste scheme, so that by its protection she might repel from herself all worldly vanity of female adornment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc loco honestam animi recreationem seu relaxationem significari, ex adjunctis abunde colligitur.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050845Z">
        <seg>From the accompanying circumstances, it is abundantly clear that this term signifies an honorable recreation or relaxation of the mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc nimirum suæ quondam novellæ Sponsæ inter paraphernalia&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; &amp; antidora&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;e&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; pollicitus erat cælestis Lapicida (de quo supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062309Z">
        <seg>This, indeed, the heavenly Lapicida had promised among the paraphernalia and antidora for his former bride (of whom above, chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc nomen etiam ad brevem notitiam aliarum scientiarum transferri potest.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060930Z">
        <seg>This name can also be applied, for a brief introduction, to other sciences.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc perfuncti officio, evolabant ad pastum: similiter, ubi ad solis occubitum repetebant hospitalem domunculam, rursum instabat Rosa, ut, priusquam se quieti darent, secum tantisper serotinas laudes communi depangerent Creatori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043835Z">
        <seg>Having completed this duty, they flew off to feed; similarly, when at sunset they returned to the hospitable little house, Rosa would again insist that, before they gave themselves to rest, they share with her a few evening praises to the Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc potu indigenæ hospites suos etiam magnates excipiunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052349Z">
        <seg>With this drink, the natives also entertain their guests of high rank.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc pro similibus citationibus, quæ postmodum occurrent, semel monuisse sufficiat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035907Z">
        <seg>This should suffice as a single warning for similar citations that will arise later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc summarie constat, Virginem per S. Catharinæ Senensis vestigia gradatim abstinendi jejunandique usum ferme ad ipsum usque inediæ culmen mirabiliter produxisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041002Z">
        <seg>It is briefly evident that the Virgin, by following in the footsteps of St. Catherine of Siena, gradually led her to the practice of abstinence and fasting, almost to the very height of starvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc supplemento credebat Rosa utcumque satisfactum iri generoso ardori suo, quo ultra femineam mensuram ac modum avida sitiebat salutem proximorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060511Z">
        <seg>Rosa believed that this supplement would in any way satisfy her generous ardor, by which, beyond the measure and bounds of a woman, she eagerly thirsted for the well-being of her neighbors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoc vero omnium molestissimum erat, tam procul exulare a Dilecti notitia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045524Z">
        <seg>This, however, was the most troublesome of all: to be exiled so far from the knowledge of the Beloved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hoccine te docuit seraphica tua magistra?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061401Z">
        <seg>Did your seraphic teacher teach you this?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hora duodecima est, nec tamen ille venit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051000Z">
        <seg>It is twelve o'clock, and yet he has not come.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Horum alterum domi forte in sedili a matre depositum conspicata Rosa, libere corripuit, &amp; inopi cuidam virgini donum fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061110Z">
        <seg>Rosa, having by chance at home spotted one of them left on a seat by her mother, freely took it and gave it as a gift to a certain poor virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hos itaque timores quotidie ingerebant cum jurgio, his minis exagitabant undique derelictam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052516Z">
        <seg>Thus they would daily instill these fears with their scolding, tormenting her with these threats, leaving her abandoned on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc Rosa ut intravit, arrectis horrore subito pilis, agnovit, ibidem sese a SCABIOSO ad pugnam expectari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052032Z">
        <seg>As soon as Rosa entered here, her hair suddenly standing on end with horror, she recognized that she was being awaited there by the Scabby One for a fight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc igitur artes artusque omnes intendebat Rosa, ut piorum operum singulari ac pretiosa consutura dignas Amori infantulo vestes præpararet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053539Z">
        <seg>Thus, Rosa devoted all her skill and effort to preparing for little Love garments worthy of a singular and precious stitching of pious deeds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc jussa Rosa cum parentibus secesserat, nec multo post, noxiis illis frigoribus impar, cœpit in manibus ac pedibus dira nervorum attractione laborare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035501Z">
        <seg>Rosa, having been ordered here, withdrew with her parents, and not long after, unprepared for those noxious frosts, she began to suffer in her hands and feet from a dreadful strain of the nerves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huc postquam se recluserat, cuidam probatæ sanctitatis matronæ in raptu visa est sub specie ac figura lucidissimæ stellæ, cujus radios &amp; quaquaversum scintillantem claritudinem opaca parietum angustia cohibere nequaquam poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043705Z">
        <seg>After she had withdrawn here, she appeared in a rapture to a matron of proven sanctity in the form and likeness of a most brilliant star, whose rays and all-around sparkling brightness the narrow walls could in no way contain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic Rosa peculiari devotione erat addictissima, hanc effigiem (quotiescumque diducto velo prostabat in altari) fixis obtutibus, avidissimis oculis hauriebat, ac fere sorbebat intentissima contemplatione;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053650Z">
        <seg>Rosa was devoted to this particular image with the utmost devotion; she would gaze at this effigy (whenever the veil was drawn back on the altar) with fixed eyes, drinking it in with the most avid eyes, and almost absorbing it in her most intense contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic Virgo succincte, quantum oportuit eam scire, respondit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054435Z">
        <seg>The virgin replied to him succinctly, as much as she needed to know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic dextre, ac sub fide silentii extorsit, ut sibi in singula prandia solam pararet offam ex panis frustis &amp; herbarum pugillo, at sine sale &amp; condimento, simplici aqua decoctam, pauculis dumtaxat uvis passis nonnumquam inspersis, ut matri, in singulos bolos attentæ, videretur genialiter obsonare ac dulciter, ubi rarus bolum augebat acinus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041206Z">
        <seg>He skillfully extorted from her, under an oath of silence, that she prepare for herself at each meal a single morsel of bread crusts and a handful of herbs, but without salt or seasoning, boiled in plain water, with only a few raisins sometimes sprinkled on it, so that the mother, attentive to every morsel, seem to be cheerfully and sweetly providing for her, where a rare grape enhanced each mouthful.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic per certa intervalla nonnullos indiderat claviculos introrsum præacutos, eoque diademate clam sibi caput non sine vulnere obcingebat, sub vittis victima coronata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042059Z">
        <seg>He had set certain small, inwardly sharpened pins at intervals in it, and with that diadem he secretly bound his head, not without wounding it, the victim crowned beneath the bands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic per edictum maternum proscripto successerat nodosorum stipitum fascis, in extremitate funibus collectus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042824Z">
        <seg>In place of the bundle of knotted sticks, which had been banished by a mother's decree, a bundle bound at the ends with ropes had taken its place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic periculose infirmabatur ancilla domestica, Francisca nomine, quæ simul nutricis fungebatur officio, &amp; tunc quidem viduæ filiolum Josephum lactabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055219Z">
        <seg>A household servant named Francisca, who also served as a nurse, was dangerously ill; at that time she was nursing the infant son of a widow, Joseph.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic usui singulare genus suaveolentium chirothecarum emerat, ut iis more femineo Rosa saltem gestatione nocturna cogeret suas manus in adscititiam venustatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035331Z">
        <seg>For this purpose he had purchased a special kind of fragrant gloves, so that with them, in a feminine manner, Rosa might at least compel her hands, during the night's wear, into the borrowed charm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Huic ut innotuit quod gestum erat, extemplo mandavit Rosæ, quidquid illud esset, quo caput suum hactenus spinose discruciaverat, sibi adferret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042310Z">
        <seg>As soon as he learned what had been done, he immediately ordered Rosa to bring him whatever it was with which she had so far been spikily tormenting his head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus aliquot ansulæ seu circuli post Rosæ obitum apud D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041804Z">
        <seg>Several of these little rings or circles were found after Rose's death at D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus aliquot ansulæ seu circuli post Rosæ obitum apud D. Mariam de Usategui aliquamdiu asservati, mirabilem, at omnibus peregrinam spirarunt suaveolentiam, testes nimirum pœnosi sudoris, quem de Rosæ corpusculo imbiberant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000921Z">
        <seg>Some of these little vials or circles, after Rose's death, were kept for a while at Our Lady of Usategui and gave off a marvelous yet unfamiliar fragrance to everyone, a testament to the painful sweat they had absorbed from Rose's little body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus amœni fulgoris centrum insederat arcus multicolor, splendidus, ac multiplici luminum pictura gratiose variegatus, supraque istum alius extendebatur arcus, paris cum inferiori pulchritudinis, ac majestatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052727Z">
        <seg>The center of this delightful radiance was occupied by a multicolored, splendid arch, gracefully variegated with a manifold painting of lights, and above that another arch extended, of equal beauty and majesty with the lower one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus antibari&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; molestissimo pondere tenetur spiritus in suo æquilibrio, ne extollatur in donis Altissimi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050411Z">
        <seg>The spirit is held in its own balance by the most troublesome weight of this antibari&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus insigne specimen habes infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035622Z">
        <seg>You have a prime example of this below the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus mensis vigesimus dies beatæ Agneti de Monte Politiano&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt; virgini Dominicanæ a cælesti rore, ac diversis repente enatis floribus notissimæ sacer est, idemque Rosæ huic natalis fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203310Z">
        <seg>The twentieth day of this month is sacred to Blessed Agnes of Montepulciano&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;, the Dominican virgin, renowned for the heavenly dew and the various flowers that suddenly sprang up, and it was also the birthday of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hujus mensis vigesimus dies beatæ Agneti de Monte Politiano&lt;s8&gt;&lt;a9&gt;d&lt;/a9&gt;&lt;/s8&gt; virgini Dominicanæ a cælesti rore, ac diversis repente enatis floribus notissimæ sacer est, idemque Rosæ huic natalis fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T193837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211758Z">
        <seg>The twentieth day of this month is sacred to Blessed Agnes of Montepulciano&lt;s8&gt;&lt;a9&gt;d&lt;/a9&gt;&lt;/s8&gt;, the Dominican virgin, renowned for the heavenly dew and the various flowers that suddenly sprang up, and it was also the birthday of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Humiliter replicuit modesta Filia: “Utique si ad mortalem opem, si ad hominum manus respicerem, faterer ultro, impossibilia esse, quæ tracto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062331Z">
        <seg>The modest Daughter humbly replied: "Indeed, if I were to rely on mortal aid, if I were to look to human hands, I would freely admit that what I undertake is impossible."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc cibum paravit in dulcedine sua pauperi Deus, attamen recolendum, cui dixerit: “Cibus sum grandium, cresce, &amp; manducabis me”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055637Z">
        <seg>God prepared this food in His sweetness for the poor, yet it is to be remembered, to whom He said: "I am the food of the great, grow, and you will eat me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc forte colebat studiosius, quod odore ac nomine regius, Regi seculorum immortali &amp; invisibili aptius conveniret in tributum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051211Z">
        <seg>He was perhaps cultivating this one more diligently because, by its fragrance and name, it was more fittingly suited as an offering to the immortal and invisible King of ages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc itaque ambire Virgo, hortari, stimulare verbis ardentissimis, obsecrare serio, nil metueret, maturaret iter, subveniret pereuntibus, Deo acceptius obsequium præstari non posse, apostolici pectoris &amp; dignitatis id esse munus, cui divina adsistentia deesfe nequeat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060412Z">
        <seg>Therefore, to urge this Virgin to go forth, to exhort, to spur on with the most ardent words, to beseech earnestly that she fear nothing, hasten her journey, come to the aid of the perishing—that no more acceptable obedience could be offered to God, that it is the duty of the apostolic heart and dignity, to which divine assistance can in no way be lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc miser corripuit, laceravit, ac procul in spurcitiarum sentinam abjecit, rem magnam se præstitisse ratus, quod tantæ Amazoni quidquam saltem armorum furto ignobili sublegisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052007Z">
        <seg>The wretch seized him, tore him apart, and cast him far away into a sewer of filth, believing he had done a great thing by having at least stolen some weapons for such an Amazon in a base theft.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc tamen Rosa &amp; hospitio frequenti æquanimiter excepit, &amp; cum invicta tolerantiæ virtute, pacifice uno communi lectulo conjacere fecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052634Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rosa received him with frequent hospitality and equanimity, and with the invincible virtue of tolerance, peacefully made him lie down with her on the same bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hunc versum jugiter ferebat in ore, hunc inter labores manuum submissa vocula suaviter cantillabat, hunc sedens, stans, ambulans devotissime iterabat, nec satiabatur frequenti illius repetitione, præsertim cum aliunde rescivit postea, eumdem versum beatæ Catharinæ Senensi perpetim in usu, ac deliciis fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061730Z">
        <seg>She constantly had this verse on her lips; while working with her hands she sweetly hummed it under her breath; whether sitting, standing, or walking she devoutly repeated it, and she never grew tired of its frequent repetition, especially when she later learned that the same verse had been in the blessed Catherine of Siena's constant use and delight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc &amp; similia quamcumque aliam exanimassent, at minime Rosam, cui intus patientia pro chalybe erat, eoque amplius, quod ex hujuscemodi præduris afflictionum cautibus deprehendebat manifestius, se per ipsissimas B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052528Z">
        <seg>These and similar things would have stunned any other, but not Rosa, who had steel for patience within her, and moreover, because she perceived more clearly from such harsh trials of affliction that she was being led by the very hand of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc Paulo, hæc Rosæ fornax divinitus obtigit, sed diverso igne.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045438Z">
        <seg>This oven was divinely granted to Paul, this one to Rosa, but by a different fire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc Rosam (de qua memoranda audierat) illic infirmæ Marianæ servitio pie districtam conspicata, eidem cœpit devotius affici, &amp; captata affandi occasione, post mutuos ultro citroque urbanæ comitatis ritus, impense Virginem rogavit, sui meminisse ut velit in quotidianis ad Deum precibus, sanctisque exercitiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062911Z">
        <seg>She saw Rosa (of whom she had heard) there, piously occupied in the service of the infirm Mariana, and began to feel a more devoted affection for her. Seizing the opportunity to speak, and after the mutual courtesies of urban courtesy had been exchanged, she earnestly begged the Virgin to remember her in her daily prayers to God and in her holy exercises.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc de doctore Castillo tunc superstite, post alios juridice testatus est Petrus de Ortega Sotomayor, ex Limensi cathedratico primario, canonico magistrali, archidiacono metropolitano, &amp; sancti Officii consultore episcopus Truxilli&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, dein Arequipæ,&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;c&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, denique Cusci&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;d&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050110Z">
        <seg>These things about Doctor Castillo, then still living, were then legally attested by Peter of Ortega Sotomayor, from Lima, a primarius professor, master canon, metropolitan archdeacon, and consultant to the Holy Office, bishop of Trujillo&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, later of Arequipa,&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;c&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, and finally of Cuzco&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;d&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc est mera conjectura nostra, cui certiorem hujus vocis significationem libenter præferemus.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055541Z">
        <seg>This is merely our conjecture, to which we would gladly prefer a more certain meaning of this word.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc illustris visio, tempestive Rosam præparavit ad ultimos illos extremæ suæ paralysis&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dolores, qui præmaturam ei mortem intulerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052857Z">
        <seg>This illustrious vision timely prepared Rosa for the final pains of her extreme paralysis, which brought about her untimely death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc inter sacra cum Rosa colloquia, viso tot muscarum famelico assultu, nonnihil extimuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043922Z">
        <seg>These sacred conversations with Rosa, seeing the hungry assault of so many flies, were somewhat frightened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc lusum aut jocum arbitrata, subrisit, interrogans, qua manu hos arripere velit, sat sciens in dextra sic tumere digitos, ut nullis forficum ansulis immitti queant, sicque velut per ludum, angustiores, quos reperire potuit, forfices porrexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055405Z">
        <seg>Thinking it a game or a joke, she smiled and asked which hand he wanted to use to grab them, well aware that her fingers on her right hand were so swollen that they couldn't fit into any scissor handles, and so, as if in play, she offered the smallest pair of scissors she could find.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc narrans inardescebat, nec cohibere se poterat, quin fortius vocem attolleret in parænesim: “Eia (inquiebat) o mater, æstimato hunc cælestem parvulum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054128Z">
        <seg>As she spoke these words she grew animated and could not restrain herself from raising her voice more forcefully in her exhortation: "Come now, O mother, esteem this heavenly little one;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc obiter de Rosea imagine, nunc ad Rosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054311Z">
        <seg>This, by the way, about the Rosea image; now to the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc ordine aptissimo in quindecim decades secrevit, unicuique decadi versum subjecit: &lt;b5&gt;Gloria Patri &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044948Z">
        <seg>He arranged these in the most perfect order into fifteen decades, and placed the verse under each decade: Glory be to the Father, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc recole, hæc pœnali strato tuo in trutina oppone, &amp; videbitur tibi sub charitatis dictamine lectulus noster floridus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042657Z">
        <seg>Remember these things, weigh them against your penitential cloak on the scales, and you will see our bed, under the dictate of charity, appear in bloom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc ut Rosæ innotuerunt, vidisses flammigerum ejus zelum tot succendi affectibus, quot rerum species in diversa sensum abripiebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053421Z">
        <seg>When these things became known to Rosa, you would have seen her zeal blazing with as many affections as the various objects of sense carried her off in different directions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæc, ubi Rosam meditationi implicatam reliquerat, clam sese proripuit ad matrem suam, in vicino conclavi laborantem: inde post horæ spatium erepsit, visura num Rosa ab oratione surrexisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051140Z">
        <seg>She, having left Rosa absorbed in meditation, slipped quietly to her mother, who was working in the adjoining room; after an hour she crept back to see if Rosa had risen from her prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hærebat in illo plexili senticeto, quod Christi verticem cruente insederat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042049Z">
        <seg>It clung to that thorny crown which had crimsonly sat upon Christ's head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hærebat parens mirata novitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034758Z">
        <seg>The parent stood amazed at the novelty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Hæsit in spectaculo vere mellifluo tota domus attonita, eoque magis, quod novo prodigio mel cælitus datum integris octo mensibus toti familiæ sat fuit in usum quotidianum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062235Z">
        <seg>The entire household stood amazed at the truly wondrous spectacle, and all the more so because, by a new miracle, the honey that had been given from heaven lasted the whole family for eight full months for their daily use.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>IV) superne advolans insedit Rosæ, notatum est, quod aliquamdiu per sinistrum Virginis latus reptando oberrabat, donec recta e regione cordis constitisset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044114Z">
        <seg>IV) Flying down from above, it settled on Rose. It was noted that for a while it wandered, crawling along the left side of the Virgin, until it came to a stop directly opposite the heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>IX) adeo quod Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor, quæ Virginis arcana præ aliis noverat, rem in familiare adagium converterit, dicere solita: “Numquid iterum empyreus Lapicida tuo monitu excussit loculos”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062312Z">
        <seg>IX) so much so that Gundisalvi the quaestor's wife, who knew the Virgin's secrets better than anyone else, turned the matter into a familiar proverb, saying: "Has the empyrean, at your bidding, once again rattled your Lapicida's little casket?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ibi altaris ornandi curam, ibi totius nitoris œconomiam sibi devote assumpserat, ibi quotidianum sibi cum Deipara ex sedulo ministerio commercium erat, ac mutua conversatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051304Z">
        <seg>There she had devoutly taken upon herself the care of adorning the altar, the management of all its splendor; there she maintained a daily communion with the Mother of God through diligent service and mutual conversation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Icta responso genitrix, permisit Filiæ, quantum vellet latere domi, tantum ne amplius sibi oculos periculoso hoc defrictu obpiperaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043435Z">
        <seg>Stunned by the reply, the mother allowed her daughter to stay at home as long as she wished, only on the condition that she would no longer tear her eyes with this dangerous rubbing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Id itaque faciendum erit in supplemento Operis nostri ad diem 27 Aprilis, quo nunc in Martyrologio Romano annuntiatur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035216Z">
        <seg>This must therefore be done in the supplement to our work on April 27, the day on which he is now commemorated in the Roman Martyrology.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Id porro erat, quod Christus beatam Catharinam Senensem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052249Z">
        <seg>That, moreover, was what Christ did to the blessed Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idcirco lamella utendum fuit, eaque argentea, quo &amp; solidius adjaceret vulnerandæ cuti, &amp; infixas spinulas tenacius retineret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042155Z">
        <seg>Therefore a blade had to be used, and a silver one at that, so that it would lie more solidly against the wounded skin and hold the embedded little spines more firmly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Rosæ jam sexennis patuit robur sub ejusdem chirurgi forcipe, cum sibi ex intimo nare eximendus fuit phaseolus&lt;a0&gt;m&lt;/a0&gt;, tertio demum tractu erutus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203037Z">
        <seg>The same was true for Rosa, now six years old, whose strength was tested under the same surgeon's forceps when a bean had to be removed from deep within her nose&lt;a0&gt;m&lt;/a0&gt;, finally extracted on the third attempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Rosæ jam sexennis patuit robur sub ejusdem chirurgi forcipe, cum sibi ex intimo nare eximendus fuit phaseolus&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;m&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;, tertio demum tractu erutus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202845Z">
        <seg>The same was true for Rosa, now six years old, whose strength was tested under the same surgeon's forceps when a bean had to be removed from deep within her nose, finally extracted on the third attempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Rosæ jam sexennis patuit robur sub ejusdem chirurgi forcipe, cum sibi ex intimo nare eximendus fuit phaseolus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;m&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, tertio demum tractu erutus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034922Z">
        <seg>The same was true for Rosa, now six years old, whose strength was tested under the same surgeon's forceps when a bean had to be removed from deep within her nose, finally extracted on the third attempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem S. Catharinæ Senensi cum Lapa matre certamen, idem stratagema fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042517Z">
        <seg>The same contest between St. Catherine of Siena and her mother Lapa, and the same stratagem.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem Sabbato repetebat, compassura Virgini Matri, cujus animam in morte Filii doloris gladius pervaserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042210Z">
        <seg>She repeated it again on Saturday, in commemoration of the Virgin Mother, whose soul had been pierced by the sword of sorrow at the death of her Son.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem est, quod mare Meridionale &amp; Austrinum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035105Z">
        <seg>The same is true of the Southern and Australasian Sea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem hic de Sancta nostra refertur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051521Z">
        <seg>The same is reported here about our Saint.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem hoc deinceps admiratus est in methodo confitendi, qua Rosa utebatur, ea sermonis tum perspicuitate, tum proprietate ac circumspectione, ut sibi videretur Lorenzana non feminam audire, sed annosum utriusque theologiæ professorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050640Z">
        <seg>He was likewise impressed thereafter by the method of confession used by Rosa, by the clarity, propriety, and circumspection of her speech, so that it seemed to him he was not hearing a woman, but an elderly professor of both theology.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem huic Virgini accidit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052423Z">
        <seg>The same thing happened to this virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem in ædibus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051146Z">
        <seg>The same in the houses of D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem matri, quæstori Gundisalvo, hujus consorti, aliisque accidisse constat, ne dubitari posset de innocentis Rosæ in domesticos sibi culices admirando imperio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043915Z">
        <seg>It is likewise established that the same thing happened to the mother, to the quaestor Gundisalvus, to his wife, and to others, so that there could be no doubt about the innocent Rose's admirable authority over the household flies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem me stimulus urgebat impetuosissime ad prædicandam divinæ gratiæ venustatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052832Z">
        <seg>The same stimulus urged me most impetuously to preach the beauty of divine grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem pavoris genius Rosam filiam ex materna crasi&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; velut hæreditarius insederat, nec tamen ideo Puella orationi dedita solitudinem ac loca obscuriora devitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062040Z">
        <seg>The same spirit of terror had, as if by inheritance, taken up residence in Rosa, the daughter born of her mother's blood, yet the girl, devoted to prayer, did not shun solitude or darker places.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem probavere demum confessarii, urserunt viri graves, &amp; spirituum magisterio spectabiles, quorum concordi judicio ut suum Rosa humiliter submitteret, conscia &amp; annuente avia, successum tentavit per germanum fratrem, divino interim (ut supra) arbitrio se remittens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040103Z">
        <seg>The confessors themselves at last approved it; men of standing and distinguished in the spiritual life urged it on, and, with the grandmother aware of it and consenting, Rosa humbly submitted to their unanimous judgment and sought success through her brother, meanwhile entrusting herself to the divine will (as above).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem quæstoris Gundisalvi, idem confessariorum erat judicium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062759Z">
        <seg>The same was the judgment of the quæstor Gundisalvus and of the confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idem viderunt quotquot promiscue aderant in sacello;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043544Z">
        <seg>All who were present in the chapel saw the same thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Idque crebrius liquenti gaudio replicans, simul decenti osculo attonitam, minimeque repugnantem dissuaviabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062935Z">
        <seg>And, repeatedly echoing it with overflowing joy, he at the same time, with a fitting kiss, stunned her and, without the slightest resistance, won her over.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur addenda videbantur tum duriora tum stabiliora in equulei supplementum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042545Z">
        <seg>Therefore, additions seemed to be both tougher and more stable for the stirrup supplement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur caveto, ne tuæ facundiæ dives talentum in plausibilibus acutorum conceptuum flosculis inutiliter consumas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060552Z">
        <seg>Therefore, be careful not to waste the rich talent of your eloquence on flowery, clever notions that are merely pleasing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur matrem adiit, prætexens aliquot sibi ex tenui carbaso pretiosis collaribus opus esse, hæc se velle a Rosa confici, quam constabat eo opificio victum parentibus lucrari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060614Z">
        <seg>So she went to her mother, pretending that she needed some precious collars made from a thin piece of cloth, saying she wanted them made by Rosa, who was known to earn her parents' living by that craft.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur quoties domi coquendis panibus accendebatur clibanus, prensabat occasionem Rosa, nudatisque plantis calcabat immota summum fornacis orificium, ubi vis ardoris vehementior erumpebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041919Z">
        <seg>So whenever the oven was fired up at home to bake bread, Rosa would seize the opportunity, and barefoot she would step onto the still-warm top of the oven opening, where the fiercest heat burst forth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur respexit denuo ad pristina dolorum supplementa, jamque pœnoso tæniarum attractui etiam hoc addebat, ut interdum illiso pugno coronam tunderet, quo altius in caput penetraret: neque enim diligens lima sic detriverat aculeos, ut suo non fungerentur cruentandi officio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042331Z">
        <seg>Thus he once again looked back to his former remedies for pain, and to the painful pulling of the bands he now added this: that he would sometimes strike the crown with his knuckles so that it would penetrate more deeply into his head. For the diligent file had not worn down the barbs so much that they could not perform their bloody work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur usque ad hanc subtilissimam divisionem carnis &amp; spiritus, elegerat conformari Sponso crucifixo, quatenus ille nec gloriam animæ in suum adhuc mortale corpus, nec gaudia partis superioris in inferiorem derivari permisit, ne passionem impediret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041941Z">
        <seg>Thus, down to this most subtle division of flesh and spirit, he had chosen to conform himself to the crucified Spouse, in that he permitted neither the glory of the soul to flow into his still mortal body, nor the joys of the higher part to descend into the lower, lest he impede the Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur ut erat immobilis, ad S. Rosarii clementissimam Reginam conversa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040131Z">
        <seg>Therefore, being motionless, she turned to the most merciful Queen of the Holy Rosary;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Igitur vade, ancilla, jussa exequere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051707Z">
        <seg>"Therefore, go, servant girl, and carry out the command."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa advertens, ratione, non imperio secum agi, suas vicissim rationes humiliter opposuit, demum in hoc utrimque conventum est, quod poscenti sua redderetur corona, sed mitigata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042322Z">
        <seg>She, perceiving that reason, not force, was being used with her, humbly set forth her own reasons in return; finally, it was agreed on both sides that she would be restored to her crown, but with its powers diminished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa mysterium celans, “Hoc, (inquit) quod vides me agere”;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062520Z">
        <seg>Hiding the mystery, she said, "This, (she said) that you see me doing";</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illa sciens filiam Rosam, pro ægritudinum frequentia ac munditiei studio, pluribus ex eodem lino indigere, seorsim huic notabilem illius telæ partem (XXXVI ulnæ erant) attribuit, ut ipsamet sibi pro arbitratu ad usus proprios sudaria, pepla, fascias, gremialia, vela, mantilla, tegmina, vittas, cervicalia, mappas, omnemque necessariam candidi nitoris supellectilem formaret, ac sueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061032Z">
        <seg>Knowing that her daughter Rosa, because of her frequent illnesses and her love of cleanliness, needed more than one of the same linen, she set aside for her a notable portion of that cloth (it was thirty-six ells), so that she herself could make handkerchiefs for her own use as she saw fit, handkerchiefs, peplums, bandages, aprons, sails, mantillas, coverings, ribbons, collars, napkins, and all other necessary articles of pure whiteness, and to sew them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illam quot osculis Rosa dissuaviarit, quot lachrymis humectarit, quot ignitis suspiriis adusserit, quam tenacibus ulnis ad livorem usque sibi interdiu noctuque adstrinxerit, quam crebra &amp; supplici adoratione coluerit, non omnino domesticos latuit, qui interdum per rimulas insciam observabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054828Z">
        <seg>How many kisses Rosa had sweetened it with, how many tears she had moistened it with, how many fiery sighs she had scorched it with, how tightly she had pressed it to herself day and night until it was bruised, how often and with what suppliant devotion she had worshiped it—this was not entirely hidden from the household servants, who at times watched her unwittingly through the keyhole.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille Victricis animositate confusus, rabiem, qua puellæ nocere non poterat, in ejusdem libellos spirituales turpissime effudit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052001Z">
        <seg>Confused by Victrix's spirit, he poured out his rage, which could not harm the girl, in the most disgraceful manner upon her spiritual writings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille de fructu nil dubitans, laborem difficultatemque majorem credidit, quam Soror aut prædicabat, aut credi vellet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045103Z">
        <seg>He, not doubting the fruit, believed the labor and difficulty to be greater than the Sister either preached or would have it believed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ille, arrepto circino, justam anuli formam delineavit in charta, debitæque proportionis addidit palam figuram circuli, cui Virgo parvulum Jesum optarat insculpi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044442Z">
        <seg>He, seizing a compass, drew a perfect ring shape on the paper, and to the proper proportion added the figure of a circle, on which the Virgin had wished the little Jesus to be engraved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illic plexili ramorum, palmitum, virgultorum circumductu, modicum inclusit spatium, ac superne frondosis arcubus affabre concameravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043246Z">
        <seg>There, by weaving a lattice of branches, palm fronds, and twigs, he enclosed a small space and skillfully arched it above with leafy curves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illic volebat suum hoc amoris pignus tantisper cum Sponso mortuo tumulari, ut cum eodem resurgente pariter rediret ad lucem: sic protestabatur amabili Sponso, se nec illo defuncto solutam esse a lege viri, cum quo &amp; mortis thalamum cupiebat sortiri communem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044507Z">
        <seg>There she wished to bury her pledge of love with her dead Spouse for a time, so that when he rose again she might return to the light along with him: thus she declared to her beloved Spouse that she was not freed from the law of the husband even by his death, since she longed to share with him a common marriage bed even in death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Illum stimulus carnis subjecit colaphis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045441Z">
        <seg>He subjected that fleshly stimulus to slaps.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Immarcescibilem fateri oportet Rosam, quam ejuscemodi spineta non suffocant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050733Z">
        <seg>One must declare the Rose imperishable, for thorns of that sort do not suffocate it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Implexas alibi dum trahit ille moras.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051006Z">
        <seg>He drags out delays elsewhere while she weaves intricate patterns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Imprimis otiosas in ecclesia fabulas (nobilitatis rusticum malum) adeo detestabatur, ut, quamquam timida, verborumque parcissima, continere se non posset, quin loquaces, ob loci reverentiam, debitæ urbanitatis admoneret, ea tamen moderatione, humilitate, prudentia, ut obsecrare potius, quam corripere videretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053309Z">
        <seg>First of all, she detested idle tales in church (the rustic scourge of nobility) so much that, though timid and very sparing of words, she could not help admonishing the talkative, out of respect for the place and for proper urbanity, yet with such moderation, humility, and prudence that she seemed more to beseech than to rebuke.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In Canta ex dira nervorum attractione triennium lecto decubuit, cruciatu immenso, gemitu nullo, nisi quod compassione acerbius, quam passione torquebatur ex illorum incommodo, quorum ministeriis carere non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052606Z">
        <seg>In Canta, she lay in bed for three years, afflicted by a dreadful nerve-traction, enduring immense torment without a single groan—except that she was wrung out more by compassion for their plight than by her own suffering, since she could not do without their care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In Rosariano beatissimæ Virginis sacello Rosa plane domestica evaserat, illic totos hærebat dies;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051257Z">
        <seg>In the chapel of the Most Blessed Virgin, the Rose had become thoroughly domestic, and there she lingered all day long.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In cellulæ pariete oblongum fixerat clavum paulo altiorem, quique Rosæ verticem palmi fere interstitio superaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043057Z">
        <seg>He had fixed a slightly taller nail oblong on the cell wall, which rose almost to the palm's tip.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In domestico horto areolas discreverat sativis violis cura sua manuque excolendis, quas simul, ut maturuissent, fasciculatim collectas ancillæ dabat venum exponendas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035759Z">
        <seg>In the home garden, she had divided the beds for the violets she tended with her own hands, and when they were ripe she would gather them in bunches and give them to the maids to be sold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his Rosæ eximie charus erat Ludovici Granatensis codex, quo ad meditationum digerendos articulos diligentius utebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052004Z">
        <seg>Among his roses, the codex of Louis of Granada was especially dear to him, which he used more diligently for arranging the articles of his meditations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his affectu tenerrimo recolebat singula Dei in se beneficia, mirabatur ejus erga se dignationem, adorabat munificam prodigamque tot donorum, gratiarum, charismatum liberalitatem: &amp; sane habebat quotidie nova, quæ ex pugillaribus attentissimæ gratitudinis recitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044923Z">
        <seg>In that most tender affection she recalled each of God's benefits toward her, marveled at His condescension toward her, and adored the munificent, prodigious generosity of so many gifts, graces, and charisms; and indeed she had new ones every day, which she would recite from the little book of her most attentive gratitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his biviis, plusquam humanæ artis fuit, incolumi utrimque obedientia neque a Sponsi ductu vel latum unguem deflexisse, nec tamen abivisse umquam a maternis præceptis in Sponsi sequela.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035257Z">
        <seg>At these crossroads, it was more than human skill to remain obedient on both sides, never to have deviated so much as a fingernail from the Groom's guidance, yet never to have strayed from her mother's precepts in following the Groom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his eminebant Ludovici Granatensis erudita Opera&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; de oratione ac meditatione, quibus assidue evolvendis, revolvendisque, Rosa hebdomadam diviserat, variisque, colorum signaculis, quid sibi quoque die legendum, distinxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045112Z">
        <seg>Among these stood out the learned Works of Louis of Granada on prayer and meditation, which Rosa had divided the week for, and to which she had assigned, by various colored markers, what she was to read each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In his nexibus clam dolorosis meditabunda, vinctum in horto Getsemani Sponsum comitabatur ad ædes Caïphæ, Annæ, &amp; Pilati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041817Z">
        <seg>Meditating on these secretly painful bonds, she accompanied the bound Spouse from the Garden of Gethsemane to the houses of Caiaphas, Annas, and Pilate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In hujus textura sumptuosa Dominicarum orationum, &amp; angelicarum Salutationum numerum Virgo non jam centuriis (ut supra) sed chiliadibus diffiniebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054645Z">
        <seg>In this sumptuous texture of Dominican prayers and angelic salutations, the Virgin defined the number not by hundreds (as above) but by thousands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ictu oculi se inveniebat illic, ubi nulla suavissimi Numinis memoria, nullus divinæ præsentiæ gustus, nullum cujuscumque solatii aderat vestigium, umbra, mentio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045456Z">
        <seg>In the blink of an eye he found himself there, where there was no memory of the most sweetest Deity, no taste of divine presence, no trace, shadow, or mention of any consolation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ipso adolescentiæ ac pueritiæ confinio, Rosæ corculum acerbissime compunxerat devota ac tenerrima compassio ex avidissimo obtutu piæ imaginis, quæ muto præconio clamitabat, &lt;b1&gt;Ecce Homo&lt;/b1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000940Z">
        <seg>At the very threshold of her adolescence and childhood, a devout and most tender compassion had pierced Rose's little heart most acutely, from the avid gaze of a pious image that proclaimed with a silent cry, "Behold the Man."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ipso adolescentiæ ac pueritiæ confinio, Rosæ corculum acerbissime compunxerat devota ac tenerrima compassio ex avidissimo obtutu piæ imaginis, quæ muto præconio clamitabat, &lt;b2&gt;Ecce Homo&lt;/b2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042046Z">
        <seg>At the very threshold of her adolescence and childhood, a devout and most tender compassion had pierced Rose's little heart most acutely, arising from the avid gaze of a pious image that, with a silent proclamation, cried out, "Behold the Man."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In omni conversatione unicum Rosæ oblectamentum erat ac solatium, de amore divino aut audire, aut loqui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053155Z">
        <seg>In every conversation, Rose's only delight and comfort was to hear or speak of divine love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In omni familiari consessu, conversatione, societate, crux siqua prostabat, Rosæ sol, ipsa cruci heliotropium&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054835Z">
        <seg>In every family gathering, conversation, and society, if a cross was laid out, Rosæ sol, herself, was the heliotrope to the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In primis haud absurda spe tumida mater, jam a teneris filiolam nobili atque honestissimo connubio destinarat: nec abludebant proposito egregia formæ venustas, acre judicium, suavitas erectæ indolis, moresque ad omnem civilitatis gratiam apprime tum facti, tum eruditi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035932Z">
        <seg>At first, the mother, inflated with not unreasonable hope, had already destined her tender little daughter for a noble and most honorable marriage; nor did the outstanding beauty of her form, her keen judgment, the sweetness of her upright disposition, and her manners—both then and thereafter most polished in every grace of civility—deter her from her purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In pœnarum cumulum recordabatur, velut in nebula, aliquando se novisse Deum, &amp; dilexisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045518Z">
        <seg>He remembered, as if in a fog, that he had once known and loved God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>In ultimo vitæ jamjam fugientis articulo, Rosa dolentem genitricem juxta se conspicata, neque ignara, quam immaniter hanc exanimaturus esset proximus ex Filiæ morte dolor, rogavit Sponsum, (isque ultimus precantis anhelitus fuit) matrem immodico mœrori imparem, ac facile succubituram, robusto firmaret solatio, adque fatalem hunc ictum miseram obduraret suavi potenti manu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035827Z">
        <seg>In the very last article of her life, now about to flee, Rosa, seeing her suffering mother beside her, and not unaware how terribly the impending blow of her daughter's death would overwhelm her, begged her Spouse—and this was the dying woman's last breath—to spare her mother, who was not equal to such excessive sorrow, and easily succumbing, would strengthen her with robust consolation, and, with a sweet and mighty hand, harden the poor mother for this fatal blow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inanem hunc rumorem ipsa publici timoris caligo pepererat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060201Z">
        <seg>This empty rumor had been born of the very fog of public fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Incertum tamen, acus an ornatus exosi circuli dirius pupugerit Rosam, spinis quam sertis amiciorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035322Z">
        <seg>Uncertain, however, whether the needle or the circle's hated adornments pierced Rosa more deeply, she who was friendlier to thorns than to garlands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Increpuit ille Virginem, utpote nondum ex circumstantiis tunc assequens, quid laudis illa potius meruisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040633Z">
        <seg>He reproached the Virgin, since he had not yet, from the circumstances at the time, understood what praise she might more fittingly have deserved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde Virgini cum suo angelo tam domestica fiducia, ac necessitudo, ut illo soleret uti veredario&lt;s13&gt;&lt;a14&gt;e&lt;/a14&gt;&lt;/s13&gt;, quotiescumque ad horam consuetam Sponsus non comparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051604Z">
        <seg>Hence the Virgin had with her angel such a domestic confidence and familiarity that she would use him as a messenger whenever the Bridegroom failed to appear at the usual hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde est, quod sæpius coram intimis ac familiaribus suis timida frequentabat hujusmodi voces;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040534Z">
        <seg>Hence it was that she often used such timid expressions in the presence of her intimates and family.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde pergebat Rosa: “Persensi ex vicina Christi humanitate suaviter in animæ meæ fundum defluere inexplicabiles flammas &amp; gloriæ stricturas, adeo quod existimarim, a mundo corruptibili me penitus jam solutam, &amp; ad perpetuæ fruitionis beata gaudia translatam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052745Z">
        <seg>Then Rosa continued: "I perceived from Christ's neighboring humanity sweetly flowing into the depths of my soul inexplicable flames and strains of glory, so that I believed myself already wholly released from this corruptible world and translated to the blessed joys of perpetual fruition."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde quos quantosque insultus, quæ domestica prælia, probra, convicia a parente, fratribus, agnatis sustinuerit æquanimis, at inermis Filia, nemo ad vivum propius aut comprehenderit aut æstimarit, quam quisquis ad sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis historiam respexerit, ubi Lapa mater tenacem propositi virginem inclementer objurgat, premit, exagitat, nisi quod in Rosam ultra verba, etiam verbera, calces, alapæ sævierint.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040007Z">
        <seg>Hence, no one has more closely understood or appreciated the insults, the domestic quarrels, the slanders, and the abuse that the Daughter, meek but unarmed, endured from her parent, brothers, and kinsmen, than whoever has looked into the history of Saint Catherine of Siena, where Lapa, the mother, cruelly reproves, presses, and agitates the tenacious virgin of her purpose, were it not that against Rosa, beyond words, even beatings, kicks, and slaps have raged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inde satis colligitur, quid&lt;/e7&gt; gargarismis &lt;e8&gt;istius aviculæ canentis indicetur.&lt;/e8&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045413Z">
        <seg>From this, one can gather enough about what is indicated by the gurgles of this singing little bird.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Indignata parens substomachabatur filiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061308Z">
        <seg>An indignant parent was scolding her daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Indusiolum sunto 50 Litaniæ, 900 Rosaria, 5 dies inediæ in reverentiam SS. Incarnationis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053551Z">
        <seg>Let there be 50 Litanys, 900 Rosaries, and 5 days of fasting in reverence of the Holy Incarnation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infimæ dumtaxat pedum supererant plantæ, quas neque cilicium nec disciplinæ verbera attingebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041913Z">
        <seg>Only the soles of their feet remained untouched, by neither cilice nor the scourge of discipline.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Infremuit parens, &amp; “Aut nugaris (inquit) aut somnias: quis enim intempesta hac hora calentem chocolatam ad te mittet?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051657Z">
        <seg>The parent frowned and said, "Either you're joking or you're dreaming: for who at this ungodly hour would send you hot chocolate?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ingemiscebat deinde ruditati suæ, quod præ inopia sterilis (ut putabat) ingenioli, ac loquendi inscitia, non poterat afflictionem suam idoneis vocabulis explicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045720Z">
        <seg>He then groaned over his own rudeness, because, in the face of what he believed to be a barren lack of wit and an ignorance of how to speak, he could not express his distress in fitting words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Initio Rosa diversos adibat confessarios, si quid forte remedii, levaminis, aut saltem consilii dare nossent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045655Z">
        <seg>At first, Rosa went to various confessors, if perhaps they might know of some remedy, relief, or at least advice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Initio quidem illud tametsi asperum, minus tamen, quam Virgo optaverat, latum, sinuosumque videbatur, utpote quod vix duorum palmorum mensuram excedebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041832Z">
        <seg>At first, although rough, it seemed less broad and winding than the Virgin had wished, since it scarcely exceeded a span of two palms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Initio, cum S: Catherinæ de Senis gestare cœpisset habitum, non contenta vulgaribus funiculis, duas sibi catenas ferreas aptarat in flagrum, hisque ad imitationem S. Patris sui Dominici per singulas noctes tam dure ac dire sibi dorsum &amp; scapulas cruentabat, ut rivi sanguinis in parietibus, in pavimento, in subligaculis fuerint notati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041510Z">
        <seg>At first, when she had begun to wear the habit of St. Catherine of Siena, not content with ordinary cords, she had fastened two iron chains to her back as a whip, and with these, in imitation of her holy Father Dominic, she scourged her back and shoulders so harshly and cruelly each night that streams of blood were left on the walls, on the floor, and on her undergarments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Innotuit Virginis confessario gliscens malum, isque Rosæ injunxit, SS. Rosarii sacellum adiret, ibique coram Deiparæ imagine periclitantis illius congregationis necessitatem Matri misericordiæ fusis precibus commendaret, nec curam dimitteret quoadusque gratiam &amp; succursum impetrasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054419Z">
        <seg>The confessor of the Virgin perceived the evil creeping in, and he ordered Rose to go to the chapel of the Holy Rosary and there, before the image of the Mother of God, commend the need of that congregation in peril to the Mother of Mercy with fervent prayers, and not to desist until she had obtained grace and help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Innotuit postea (revelante ancilla Mariana) Rosam omni fere diluculo, quo sacra Synaxi reficienda non erat, austero hoc fellis promulsidario in Dominicæ Passionis memoriam os totum imbuere solitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041231Z">
        <seg>It later became known (as revealed by the handmaid Mary) that Rosa, at almost every dawn when she was not required to renew the sacred Synaxis, used to bathe her entire mouth with this austere gall-based ointment in memory of the Passion of the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Instabat hora funeris ad sepulturam efferendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042412Z">
        <seg>The hour of the funeral was at hand; the body was to be carried out for burial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Insuper ad hujus lectuli caput occulte ampullam felle plenam appenderat, nec ante ad somnum sese componebat, quam isthoc sorbillo amaricasset fauces, in memoriam Sponsi felle ac myrrha potati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042632Z">
        <seg>Moreover, she had secretly hung a vial filled with gall at the head of this little bed, and she would not settle down to sleep until she had moistened her throat with a drop of it, in memory of the Bridegroom who had drunk gall and myrrh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Insuper concupisco amare te, o Deus cordis ac vitæ meæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053108Z">
        <seg>Moreover, I long to love you, O God of my heart and my life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Insuper vivis floribus neutiquam contenta, pretiosiores e filis sericis fingebat versicolori artificio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060115Z">
        <seg>Moreover, not at all content with living flowers, she fashioned more precious ones from silk threads with a multicolored artistry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intellexit Rosa mysterium, nempe ex decreto cælestis Sponsi non esse, quod ipsa populosissimi illius monasterii optato receptaculo furtim sese abderet, aliud de genere sui instituti provisum esse in secreto æternitatis consilio: denique summa rei erat, quod solius Catharinæ Senensis vestigia Rosam exspectabant spinis sepiendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040128Z">
        <seg>Rosa understood the mystery: that by the decree of the heavenly Spouse it was not that she should secretly hide herself in the most populous monastery's eagerly sought refuge, but that something else of her order's institution had been provided for in the secret counsel of eternity. In short, the essence of the matter was that only the footsteps of Catherine of Siena awaited Rosa to be enclosed among thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intellexit Virgo sapiens, quorsum delicati Sponsuli verba tam seria ac pretiosa tenderent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061912Z">
        <seg>The wise Virgin understood where the delicate Spouse's words were tending, so serious and precious as they were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter alios præcipui, quos bona sors huic subtilissimæ explorationi destinavit, fuere doctor Joannes de Castillo, &amp; P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050017Z">
        <seg>Among other prominent figures whom fortune destined for this most subtle exploration were Doctor John de Castillo and Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter has similesque voces, quæstoris uxor clam ex oratorio in vicinum atrium se proripuit cum filiabus, ne forte loquenti Virgini instreperent, aut liberiores hos impetus respectu importunæ præsentiæ impedirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053731Z">
        <seg>Amid these and similar voices, the quaestor's wife slipped quietly from the oratory into the adjoining atrium with her daughters, so that they might not interrupt the Virgin while she was speaking, or, by their more unrestrained impulses, hinder these impulses in deference to her importunate presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter has vero mendicas ac miserabiles nulla tam pannosa aut sordida, nulla tam deformis aut ulcerosa, nulla aspectu horribilis aut intolerabilis fœtore, quam Rosa vel dedignari posset, vel præ nausea aversari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061252Z">
        <seg>Among these beggars and wretches there was none so ragged or filthy, none so deformed or ulcerous, none so hideous in appearance or so intolerably foul-smelling that Rosa could not but loathe or turn away in disgust.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter hos duos mellifluos vultus, æstu reciproco, oberrabat Rosa, festinabat ab uno ad alterum, deliciabatur in alterno spectaculo, &amp; quo sæpius ad alterutram redibat, faciem inveniebat dulciorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044318Z">
        <seg>Between these two honeyed faces, in mutual ardor, fluttered Rosa, hurrying from one to the other, delighting in the alternating spectacle, and the more often she returned to either, the sweeter she found the face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Inter hæc Rosa, quæ cum honestis matronis in ecclesia S. Dominici exitum horum tumultuum expectabat, soli metuebat prostanti in ara divinissimo Sacramento, cui utique sacrilegæ manus perfidæ gentis parcituræ non erant, fortasse nec pedes, quin illud ausu horrendo protererent, humique projectum rabie blasphema proculcarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060155Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, Rosa, who with the honorable matrons in the Church of St. Dominic was awaiting the outcome of these disturbances, feared alone for the Most Blessed Sacrament on the altar, which the sacrilegious hands of that perfidious people would surely not spare—perhaps not even its feet—but would trample with a horrendous audacity, and, cast upon the ground, would be kicked about with blasphemous fury.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interdum nimio algore excitata, roris marini ramusculis ad ellychnium accensis, obiter hoc fumo tantillum fugitivi caloris, quo se foveret, nitebatur concipere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042927Z">
        <seg>Sometimes, roused by the bitter cold, she would kindle sprigs of sea spray at her brazier, and in doing so would try to catch a little fugitive warmth from the smoke to warm herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interdum prope erat, ut inconditos clamores ederet imploratura auxilium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045602Z">
        <seg>Sometimes she was close to uttering incoherent cries to implore for help.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea Rosam torquebat febris non sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053520Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, a fever not her own was tormenting Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea bis, ter, ab ecclesiæ porta reversus frater cunctantem increpuit, moræ periculum exaggeravit, surgere conanti brachium subjecit: connituntur ambo, sed incassum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040121Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, having turned back twice or three times from the church gate, the brother rebuked the hesitating man, exaggerated the danger of delay, and seized his arm as he tried to rise. They both struggled, but to no avail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea memor viscerum charitatis, quibus erga ægrotos norat Virginem abundare, summis precibus egit per quæstoris conjugem, ut Rosam adduceret in tanto sibi periculo bene precaturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061441Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, mindful of the charitable deeds with which he knew the Virgin abounded toward the sick, he earnestly implored the husband of the questor to bring Rosa, who in such a peril would pray well for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea terror &amp; angor strenue obibant partes suas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045534Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, terror and anguish were vigorously playing their parts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interea, cum aliis sororibus tertiariis expectabat ibidem &amp; Rosa palmam suam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044238Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she was waiting there with the other tertiary sisters and Rose for her palm;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim cautæ filiolæ ingeniosa non defuere stratagemata, quibus matri diu occuleret hunc severum abstinentiæ tenorem, ne ab ipsa valentioribus eduliis vesci cogeretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041023Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, the cautious little daughter did not lack ingenious stratagems by which she could hide from her mother this strict regimen of abstinence for a long time, so that she would not be forced by her to eat more substantial food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim cœpit eminus ille nutibus procum, mox &amp; procacem agere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052206Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, from a distance he began to woo her with gestures, soon acting boldly and impudently.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim esuriei, sitis, lassitudinis immemor, tota occupabatur cum dilecto, ea quidem veneratione ac reverentia, ut toto XXIV horarum decursu, nec sedere coram illo, nec muro contiguo tantisper acclinari præsumeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060047Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, oblivious to hunger, thirst, and fatigue, she was entirely occupied with her beloved, with such veneration and reverence that throughout the entire twenty-four-hour period she would not presume to sit before him or even lean for a moment against the adjoining wall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim ipsa velut moræ jam impatiens, nunc experiri incessum, nunc ad aram sacramque Hostiam, mox ad ecclesiæ fores prospectare, si qua venirent hostes, quibus velocior ad altare convolaret, certa ibidem occumbere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060248Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she herself, as if already impatient of delay, would now try to walk, now approach the altar and the sacred Host, then soon look toward the church doors to see if any enemies were coming, to whom she would rush more swiftly to the altar, certain to fall there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim pergebat lingua, quæ sola mobilis erat, prosequebatur nunc Dei, nunc Rosæ encomia, dum simul persona tota cum nimia interni sui gaudii vehementia luctabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044613Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile the tongue, the only part that could move, went on, now praising God, now praising Rose, while the whole person struggled with the excessive vehemence of her inner joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interim precibus, gemitibus, suspiriis pulsabat ad cæli fores;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035815Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, he knocked at the gates of heaven with prayers, groans, and sighs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata aliquando a viro religioso (teste vigesimo primo) quantum commodi suis parentibus ex tenui isto flosculorum mercimonio accederet, respondit, illud quidem permodicum esse, ni Sponsus cælestis aliunde miris, at arcanis, modis suppleret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035805Z">
        <seg>When once asked by a religious man (the twenty-first witness) how much benefit her parents received from this meager trade in flowers, she replied that it was indeed very small, unless the heavenly Spouse supplied it from elsewhere by wondrous, yet mysterious, means.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata de modo &amp; qualitate visionis, imaginaria sit an intellectualis, diuturna an momentanea, facie ad faciem, an oblique per transparitiones?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050541Z">
        <seg>When asked about the manner and quality of the vision—whether imaginary or intellectual, lasting or momentary, face to face or indirectly through transparencies?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata de successivis in hoc studio profectibus, an pari semper applicatione animi, facilitate, collectione, ac imperturbatæ mentis serenitate oraverit, respondit, usque ad duodecimum circiter ætatis annum, se varia inter orandum temperie, difficultatum subinde vicissitudines percepisse, at breves, &amp; modicas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050250Z">
        <seg>When asked about her successive progress in this study, whether she had always prayed with equal application of mind, facility, concentration, and an untroubled serenity of mind, she replied that until about the age of twelve, she had perceived various fluctuations in her prayerful temper, though brief and moderate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, cur ex tot millibus unice hunc versiculum amasset præ ceteris, respondebat: Seraphicæ matris suæ totum illo solatium contineri, singulas hujus syllabas turgere quodam arcano succo familiaris in Deum confidentiæ, nec suo palato aliud his tam propriis verbis sapidius posse occurrere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061739Z">
        <seg>When asked why, out of so many thousands, she loved this single verse above all others, she replied that the entire consolation of her seraphic mother was contained in it, that each of its syllables swelled with a certain secret sap of familiar confidence in God, and that nothing could taste sweeter to her palate than these words so uniquely her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, quid ageret, quid sibi vellet hac novitate?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060220Z">
        <seg>When asked what she was doing and what she wanted with this novelty?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Interrogata, quid solatii captaret ex creaturis, si contingeret animum contemplandi labore fessum nonnihil remittere, aut per eutrapeliam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; tantisper relaxare?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050343Z">
        <seg>When asked what solace she would draw from creatures, should it happen that her mind, weary from the labor of contemplation, needed to rest a little, or be relaxed for a while through good humor?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intravit denique Rosa jacentis cubiculum, salutavit ægrum, sed ea compassionis ac misericordiæ dulci affluentia, ut nec circumstantibus sanis ambiguum fuerit, loqui in modestissima Virgine Deum totius consolationis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061500Z">
        <seg>At last Rosa entered the sick woman's chamber, greeted the patient, but with such a sweet abundance of compassion and mercy that even to the healthy bystanders it was plain that in that most modest Virgin spoke the God of all consolation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intravit uterque, ac brevi oratione præmissa, ad altare propior accessit, sudantem adhuc vultum diu, attenteque contemplatus, demum petito gossipio leviter humorem abstersit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053840Z">
        <seg>Both of them entered, and after a brief speech, approached the altar; having long and attentively contemplated the still-sweating face, he finally wiped away the moisture with the requested rag.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intumuerat manus ad æqualitatem doliolorum, quibus mella selectissima ex Hispaniis Limam deferuntur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055343Z">
        <seg>The hand had swollen to the size of the casks, by which the choicest honey is brought from Spain to Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Intus humile altariolum muro applicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043249Z">
        <seg>Inside, he affixed a humble little altar to the wall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsa diligenter collectis undequaque monilibus, velis, torquibus, floccis, lemniscis, omnique pretiosa supellectile (cujus nulla umquam pars amissa fuit, quin tota rediret ad dominos) operi strenue incumbebat, assumptis fidissimis adjutricibus in laboris ac meriti participium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055033Z">
        <seg>She herself, having diligently collected from every quarter jewels, veils, torques, flocks, love-knots, and all other precious furnishings (of which no part was ever lost without the whole being returned to its owners), eagerly applied herself to the task, taking on the most faithful helpers as partners in the labor and its reward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsa rem sic interpretata est, non ut omnino sibi catena parceret, sed tantum non cæderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041653Z">
        <seg>She interpreted the matter thus: not that the chain would spare her altogether, but only that it would not kill her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsa, hoc urbanissime recusato, potius deposcebat familiarem sibi adferri dapem, panem &amp; aquam: his allatis, velut pollucibiliter deliciatura, panis medullam intingebat frigidæ, eaque sumpta, post modicum surgebat e lecto, quasi refecta, satisque opipare recreata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041104Z">
        <seg>She herself, most urbanely declining this, would rather request that familiar fare be brought to her—bread and water. When these were brought, as if to indulge herself deliciously, she would dip the heart of the bread into the cold water, and having taken it, after a little she would rise from her bed, as though refreshed and amply restored.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipse confessarius, qui Rosæ arcanum hoc pœnitentiæ genus permiserat (nam citra illius nutum Virgo morigera nihil aggrediebatur vel minimum) obiter dumtaxat aculeati gestaminis conscius, neque coronam viderat, neque vel tenui suspicione attigerat peregrinas inventiones, quibus hanc Rosa in se consueverat atrocius irritare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042217Z">
        <seg>The confessor himself, who had permitted Rose this secret form of penance (for without his nod the obedient Virgin would undertake nothing, not even the slightest thing), merely aware in passing of the thorny garment, had neither seen the crown nor even in the faintest suspicion touched the foreign contrivances by which this Rose had trained herself to provoke it more atrociously within herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipsemet doctor Joannes de Castillo (quem Rosa ob notam viri sanctimoniam tanti faciebat) conabatur Virginem ab hac persuasione divertere, quod opinaretur difficultates plane insuperabiles fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062756Z">
        <seg>Doctor John de Castillo himself (whom Rosa held in such high esteem for his renowned piety) was trying to dissuade the Virgin from this conviction, believing the difficulties to be utterly insurmountable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipso dein festivissimo Pentecostes die (qui Hispanis&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; æque ut Italis&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt; &lt;b3&gt;Rosarum pascha&lt;/b3&gt; dicitur) baptizata est Rosa, primoque rore cælestis gratiæ permaduit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203314Z">
        <seg>Then on the most festive day of Pentecost itself (which to the Spaniards&lt;a1&gt;g&lt;/a1&gt; is called just as to the Italians&lt;a2&gt;h&lt;/a2&gt; the &lt;b3&gt;Feast of the Roses&lt;/b3&gt;), Rose was baptized, and was made entirely wet by the first dew of heavenly grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ipso dein festivissimo Pentecostes die (qui Hispanis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;g&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; æque ut Italis&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;h&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; &lt;b9&gt;Rosarum pascha&lt;/b9&gt; dicitur) baptizata est Rosa, primoque rore cælestis gratiæ permaduit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T200406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034637Z">
        <seg>Then on the most festive day of Pentecost itself (which to the Spaniards&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;g&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; is called just as to the Italians&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;h&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; the &lt;b9&gt;Feast of the Roses&lt;/b9&gt;), Rose was baptized, and was made entirely wet by the first dew of heavenly grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is erat P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061959Z">
        <seg>He was P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is erat R.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042303Z">
        <seg>He was R.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is ex omnibus unicam sibi in donum poposcit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061903Z">
        <seg>He asked for only one gift from all of them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is miratus Virginis eximiam pulchritudinem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060607Z">
        <seg>He marveled at the Virgin's exceptional beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Is nil moratus, veluti si arcanis Rosæ nuptiis arbiter interfuisset, hæc eademmet verba circumscripsit annulo: “Rosa cordis mei, tu mihi sponsa esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044452Z">
        <seg>He, without delay, as if he had been the arbiter at Rose's secret wedding, inscribed these very words in a ring: "Rose of my heart, be my bride."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellæ Mexiæ contigisse, quæ vidit, retulit filia Isabellæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051149Z">
        <seg>The daughter of Isabella reported what she saw, that it had happened to Isabella of Mexico.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellæ Mexiæ ignavus hostis Rosam a fronte, &amp; cominus aggredi formidans, a tergo eminus rude saxum in eam libravit, connisu tam impetuoso ac valido, ut euntem solo tenus prostraverit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051952Z">
        <seg>A cowardly enemy of Isabella of Mexico, fearing to attack Rosa in the front and at close quarters, from behind and at a distance hurled a rough stone at her with such violent and powerful force that it struck her to the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Isabellæ de Mexia quædam ex domesticis famulabus, putri humore, febriumque æstu prætumida;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061333Z">
        <seg>A certain one of Isabella of Mexico's maids, swollen with a putrid humor and feverish heat;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Istud est, quod Virgo tam secure, ac fidenter, tam crebro &amp; palam, tam libenter ac libere dulci suæ patriæ vaticinando promittebat, ea quidem asseverationis firmitate atque constantia, quasi illud jam constructum ante oculos habuisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062440Z">
        <seg>This is what the Virgin so securely and confidently, so often and openly, so gladly and freely promised to her sweet homeland by prophesying, with such firmness and constancy of assertion, as if she already had it built before her eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita accidit, &amp; quidem hoc ordine: Dominica Palmarum, annuo ritu peracta benedictione, distribuebant ramos sacristæ per chorum &amp; ecclesiam, dum adornatur solemnis processio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044235Z">
        <seg>Thus it happened, and in this order: on Palm Sunday, after the annual blessing had been performed, the sacristans distributed the branches throughout the choir and the church while the solemn procession was being prepared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita accidit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061934Z">
        <seg>So it happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita brevissimum somnum Rosa libabat magis, quam capiebat, præsertim hyberno tempore, quo plerumque ex nocturnis frigoribus sic tremebat, rigebat, stupebat, ut surgens pedibus consistere vix posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042923Z">
        <seg>Thus Rosa took her briefest sleep more in measure than in depth, especially in winter, when she was so chilled, stiffened, and stunned by the night's frosts that upon rising she could scarcely stand on her feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita cunctorum suffragio uni Rosæ delatum hoc religiosæ pietatis officium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055030Z">
        <seg>Thus, by the unanimous vote of all, this office of religious piety was entrusted to one Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita cupiebat cum S. martyre Ignatio mundus Christi panis inveniri, pro illa charitate, qua Christus Panis angelicus fit panis hominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060319Z">
        <seg>He so desired that the world might find the Bread of Christ with St. Ignatius the martyr, for that charity by which Christ, the Angelic Bread, becomes the bread of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita dum mater egressura vocabat Filiam in comitatum, sufficiebat ostendisse fluidos, noxioque pruritu tumentes oculos, tamquam perferendæ apertæ luci tunc minime pares.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043339Z">
        <seg>Thus, as the mother was about to leave and called her daughter to accompany her, it was enough to show eyes fluid and swollen with a noxious itch, as if they were then in no way fit for the open light to bear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita evenit: nam paulo post mirabiliter coalueruut divisi animi, evanuit pomum discordiæ, dissiliit sathanæ cuneus, qui fissuras diduxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054456Z">
        <seg>Thus it happened: for shortly thereafter the divided minds were wonderfully reconciled, the apple of discord vanished, and Satan's wedge, which had opened the fissures, splintered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita fuit: nam matre adhuc disceptante cum filia, pulsatur ad fores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051713Z">
        <seg>It was so: for while the mother was still arguing with her daughter, there was a knock at the door.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita hic evenit: nam Gundisalvus imprimis Angelinum Medorium Romanum, qui imaginem pinxerat, in ædes suas advocavit, ut sudoris originem, si forte a natura colorum esset, investigaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053808Z">
        <seg>This is how it happened: for Gundisalvus first summoned Angelinus Medorius the Roman, who had painted the image, into his house, so that he might investigate the origin of the sweat, if perhaps it was due to the nature of the colors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita nimirum nec indulgebatur quidquam afflicto corpori, &amp; demebatur somno, quod orationi accederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042505Z">
        <seg>Thus, of course, nothing was permitted to the afflicted body, and sleep was taken away so that it might be devoted to prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ita plane accidit: nam paulo post a maternis ædibus translata in dominæ Mariæ Usategui contubernium, pietate matronæ linea supellectile adeo non eguit, ut etiam abundarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061100Z">
        <seg>And so it happened: for shortly thereafter, having been moved from her mother's house into the household of Lady Maria Usategui, she, by virtue of the matron's piety, lacked no furnishings, indeed had more than enough.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itane oscitasti somnolenta, dum has ineptias tam deformes tua imperita segnities abortiret?” Cui illa respondit mitissime, pariter quidem &amp; sibi, quamquam indoctæ, visum fuisse talem pingendi modum oppido peregrinum ac devium, nec tamen ausam aliter, quam mater præscripserat, acum filumque ducere, aut proprio arbitratu conari in adversa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035545Z">
        <seg>"Did you doze off, sleepyhead, while your unskilled laziness was producing such hideous nonsense?" To this she replied very meekly, and indeed to herself, that although untrained, she had found this way of painting utterly foreign and misguided to the town, yet she had not dared to guide the needle and thread any other way than her mother had prescribed, nor to attempt anything on her own in the opposite manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Limanus archiepiscopus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053901Z">
        <seg>And so the Archbishop of Lima, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Rosa, mutato consilio, in infimam domus partem descendit, profundioris silentii quæsitura latibulum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052026Z">
        <seg>So Rosa, having changed her mind, descended to the lowest part of the house, seeking a hiding place of deeper silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Rosæ in cibum placuerunt hujus benedicti floris non grana, quia dulcia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041256Z">
        <seg>And so the roses were pleasing to eat, not the grains of this blessed flower, because they were sweet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Rosæ lectulum ærumnis trium lustrorum gravem cupidæ matri dedunt in direptionem ac prædam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042852Z">
        <seg>And so they hand over Rose's little bed, heavy with the hardships of three lustrums, to the greedy mother for plunder and spoil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Virginem per tenebras aggressus est tenebrio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051921Z">
        <seg>And so the dark one attacked the Virgin in the darkness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque Virgo, ut matri satisfaceret, post diurni laboris pensum, aliquam noctis partem conficiendis altarium ornamentis seponebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060124Z">
        <seg>Thus the Virgin, in order to please her mother, would set aside part of the night after her daily labor to make altar ornaments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque a P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044938Z">
        <seg>And so from P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque coram sacra imagine (quæ adstabat mensulæ imposita) procubuit, breviterque oravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055359Z">
        <seg>And so he knelt before the sacred image (which stood upon a small table) and prayed briefly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque hanc lumbis suis triplicato circumductu fortissime adstrinxit, extremis commissuræ ansulis seram immisit, claviculam abdidit, ubi recuperare nec ipsa posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041656Z">
        <seg>And so she fastened it most securely around her waist with a triple wrap, inserted the key into the little loop at the seam's end, and hid the key where even she herself could not retrieve it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque hoc scrupulo saucia, &amp; animo inquieta, sacellum Dei Genitricis in æde FF.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034727Z">
        <seg>Thus, wounded by this scruples and troubled in mind, she entered the chapel of the Mother of God in the house of the Fathers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque hoc scrupulo saucia, &amp; animo inquieta, sacellum Dei Genitricis in æde FF. Prædicatorum SS. Rosario dicatum adiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203315Z">
        <seg>[4] [so that she would first be called Rosa,] However, after several years had passed, the Virgin herself, with a fearful conscience, brought another suit concerning the name Rosa, namely to provide an occasion and a place for a heavenly oracle by which the name Rosa was to be finally confirmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque invitam aliquoties adegit imperio, certa, edicto sine replica obedituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043323Z">
        <seg>And so he compelled her, unwilling as she was, several times by his authority, certain that she would obey the order without reply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ludum abrupit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034950Z">
        <seg>And so he interrupted the game.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque omni alio catoscopo&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; desperato, Virginis confessarium authoritate, qua valebat, induxit, ut ipse per anfractuosæ quæstionis obliquas ambages, si quæ posset, vel ab invita eliceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203526Z">
        <seg>And so, having despaired of every other catoptroscope, he, by the authority of the Virgin's confessor, which he wielded, compelled her to herself, through the winding, oblique detours of the question, extract whatever she could, even from an unwilling source.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque omni alio catoscopo&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; desperato, Virginis confessarium authoritate, qua valebat, induxit, ut ipse per anfractuosæ quæstionis obliquas ambages, si quæ posset, vel ab invita eliceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040819Z">
        <seg>And so, having despaired of every other catoptroscope, he, by the authority of the Virgin's confessor, which he wielded, compelled her to herself, through the winding, oblique detours of the question, extract whatever she could, even from an unwilling source.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque primam sibi tunc coronam (de secunda agetur paulo inferius) ex ductili stanno complicuit, quæ seu funium, seu virgularum mutuo implexu contortarum, spiram effigiabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042056Z">
        <seg>And so he then fashioned his first crown (the second will be discussed a little later) from malleable tin, which, whether of cords or of rods twisted together, formed a spiral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque præfecta Lucia de SS. Trinitate ad pendulam illic Rosæ jam defunctæ imaginem conversa, sub ingenti cogitationum tumultu erupit in hæc verba: “Audito, Rosa benedicta, tibi ego præcipio sub illa obedientia, quæ mihi in hoc cœnobio debetur ab omnibus, faxis inveniatur argentum, quod perdidimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035650Z">
        <seg>And so, Prefect Lucia of the Holy Trinity, turning to the portrait of the late Rosa hanging there, broke out amid a great tumult of thoughts into these words: "Hear me, blessed Rosa, I command you, by the obedience owed to me in this convent by all, to have the silver we lost found."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque simplex ac timida negavit tandem, quidpiam sibi innotuisse de prædestinationis subtilissima notione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061829Z">
        <seg>And so the simple and timid woman finally denied that she knew anything about the most subtle notion of predestination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque socias obiter secum in D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060211Z">
        <seg>And so, incidentally, he takes his associates with him to D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque tam rarum pudicitiæ donum, tam humilis ac pura virginitas, a primo ætatulæ suæ quinquennio jam Christo sacrata per votum, flos simul &amp; dos erat futuro Sponsi Nazareni connubio: Rosa quantum amabat lilium inter spinas, tam abjecte sentiebat de seipsa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044101Z">
        <seg>Thus such a rare gift of chastity, such humble and pure virginity, consecrated to Christ by a vow from the first five years of her life, was both a flower and a dowry for the future marriage of the Nazarene Spouse: as the rose loved the lily among thorns, so she felt herself to be despicable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque tenerrimis blanditiis genitricem obsedit, donec permissionem eliceret, qua decolorem rudemque saccum ex obvia vilique materia assumeret pro amictu, quod vestimenti genus nec immodestum est, nec inusitatum feminis, quæ a pompis, &amp; nuptiis alienæ, sub simplicissimo cultu solitariam nituntur sectari devotionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035443Z">
        <seg>And so he besieged his mother with the most tender flatteries until he had elicited permission to take a plain, unadorned sack of coarse, undyed cloth for a cloak—a type of garment that is neither immodest nor unusual for women who, estranged from pomp and nuptials, strive under the simplest attire to pursue a solitary devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque tunc, velut inscia mysterii, calente vino caput filiæ leniter abluit, quantum læsis partibus sat fore ad medelam aut credebat, aut credere simulabat, aliud interim adversus ipsam coronam r emedium excogitatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042254Z">
        <seg>And so then, as if unaware of the mystery, she gently washed her daughter's head with hot wine, believing—or pretending to believe—that it would be enough to heal the wounded parts, while she devised another remedy against the crown itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque ut alias usa ingenio, selectis ex Ordine Prædicatorum quatuor præstantissimis theologis, rem permisit examini, pollicita id amplecti, quod ipsorum potiora suffragia diffinirent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040304Z">
        <seg>And so, as she had done on other occasions, she entrusted the matter to the examination of four of the most eminent theologians selected from the Order of Preachers, promising to embrace whatever their majority vote might decide.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque vernant hic omnia: siquidem &amp; progenitores Virgini fuere Gaspar de Floribus, Maria ab Oliva;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211802Z">
        <seg>Therefore, everything here is in springtime: for indeed the Virgin's ancestors were Gaspar de Floribus, Maria ab Oliva;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque, &amp; ipsa novellam sponsam affata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044411Z">
        <seg>And so, she herself addressed the new bride;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Itaque, ubi coronæ seu bracteæ extrema coibant, decussatim tænias innexuit, quarum attractu non solum cohibebat cuspidatum hunc circulum, ne aut loco exerraret, aut sponte laxaretur, verum etiam quoties lubebat dolorem intendere, tæniarum capita strictius adducendo, ipsi tormento suo vim faciebat, præcipue vero sextis feriis placebat Virgini hoc pœnarum epimetron, in memoriam Sponsi, sentibus coronati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042142Z">
        <seg>Therefore, where the ends of the crowns or plates met, he fastened bands crosswise, by the tension of which he not only held this pointed circle in place, so that it would neither slip from its position, or loosen of its own accord, but also, whenever she wished, she would intensify the pain by drawing the ends of the bands tighter, thus applying force with her own torture; and on the sixth day, in particular, the Virgin delighted in this epimetron of punishments, in memory of the Spouse, crowned with thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Item alio anno, totam ejusdem quinquagenæ intercapedinem decurrit prorsus sine potu, ne guttulam [quidem] aquæ sorbens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041336Z">
        <seg>Also, in another year, he ran the entire length of that same fifty-mile stretch completely without drinking, not even sipping a drop of water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Item, suis demeritis illum apud inferos deberi locum, qui omnium sit infimus &amp; summe infamis: vilissimum se terræ pondus, abominabile generis humani carcinoma, purulentam sentinam ac fæcem esse, indignam, cui luceant cæli, cui aspiret aër, cui tellus dorsum substernat: suo contagio inquinari elementa, suis piaculis gravari solum, amarescere oceanum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040540Z">
        <seg>Likewise, by his own demerits, he deserves in the underworld the place that is the lowest of all and most infamous: the vilest burden of the earth, an abominable cancer of the human race, a purulent sewer and dregs, unworthy of the light of heaven, of the breath of air, or of the earth's sustaining back: that by his contagion the elements are polluted, the soil burdened by his crimes, and the ocean made bitter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Iteratur bis, ter, frustrata perquisitio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055132Z">
        <seg>The search is repeated twice, three times, in vain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Iterum obedit Rosa, sed ultra litteram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042812Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeys again, but beyond the letter;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Iverunt, &amp;, ut Virgo dixerat, invenerunt tria caryophylla de illomet, quem pridie Rosa indicarat, cauliculo, trepidæ carpserunt, apportarunt, suæque diffidentiæ veniam precatæ, cum Rosa in gratiarum actionem Deo se prostraverunt, simul admirabundæ ac lætæ, quod illa die sacra imago cum ornatu tam peregrino in publicum erat proditura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055210Z">
        <seg>They went, and, as the Virgin had said, they found three cloves of that very thing which Rosa had indicated the day before, plucked them from the trembling stalk, brought them, and, begging pardon for their own distrust, They prostrated themselves with Rose in thanksgiving to God, at once amazed and joyful that on that sacred day the image, with such a foreign adornment, was to be brought out in public.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ivit illa, viditque imaginis vultum insolito splendore fulgidius radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055107Z">
        <seg>She went, and saw the face of the image shining more brightly with unusual splendor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam desierat matrem ad ejuscemodi officia foras comitari, sed ne ipsa domi visitaretur, effugere cupiebat, nec poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043447Z">
        <seg>She had already stopped accompanying her mother to such duties outside, but she wanted to escape being visited at home herself, and she could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam in SS. Incarnationis monasterio, (quod sub regula S. Patris Augustini militat) a sanctimonialibus Rosam exoptantibus, admissionem locumque internuntia fratris industria impetrarat, nilque deerat, quam ut relicta paterna domo, clam sine pompa ac strepitu ad cœnobium recta pergeret, valvis patulis admittenda, candidisque ibidem liliis interserenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040106Z">
        <seg>Now in the Holy Scriptures At the Monastery of the Incarnation (which follows the Rule of Saint Augustine), the nuns, eager for a rose, had by the brothers' diligence secured her admission and a place; and nothing remained but for her to leave her father's house and secretly, without pomp or noise, make her way to the convent, to be admitted through its open gates and planted among the white lilies there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jam pene convaluerat, &amp; denuo invitam ad carnis ferculum compulerunt: denuo item rediere prædicta symptomata, nec remedium nisi ab usitato panis &amp; aquæ pastu superfuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041055Z">
        <seg>She had almost recovered, and once again they forced her, unwillingly, to eat a meat dish. The aforementioned symptoms returned again, and there was no remedy except the usual diet of bread and water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jesule;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053244Z">
        <seg>Jesus, little one</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jesuli somnum, &amp; Mariæ vigilias ignem suis medullis injicere, ut nec parvuli mansuetissimum soporem audeat interrumpere, nec tamen vigilem Matrem possit sine canoro succensi cordis alloquio præterire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054515Z">
        <seg>To kindle in Jesus sleep, and in Mary watchings a fire in their very marrow, so that neither the little one dares to interrupt the most gentle slumber, nor yet can the watchful Mother pass by without a song-filled, ardent heart's discourse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jesus mecum sit, Amen”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035021Z">
        <seg>Jesus be with me, Amen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanna, tam inops consilii, quam periculo &amp; calamitati impar, non inveniebat rationem, qua discrimini occurreret citra pejus discrimen: nam ut eo, ubi habitabat ultra amnem, quotidie accerseret ex urbe medicum, obstabat viæ longinquitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061153Z">
        <seg>Joanna, as lacking in counsel as she was unprepared for danger and calamity, could find no way to meet the crisis without bringing on an even worse one: for to summon a doctor from the city each day to the place where she lived across the river, the long distance was an obstacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanne Miguel Ord.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051428Z">
        <seg>Joanne Miguel, Order</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joanne de Lorenzana&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; grandius supplementum obtinuit, donec summa numeri ad centum quinquaginta pertingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044945Z">
        <seg>Joanne de Lorenzana received a larger supplement, until the total number reached one hundred and fifty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050801Z">
        <seg>John the Latter, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes Perez: at primorum duorum examen tot ac tanta complectitur, ut ex reliquis nil addi possit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050225Z">
        <seg>John Perez: for the examination of the first two encompasses so much and so many things that nothing can be added from the rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Laet lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035052Z">
        <seg>John of Laet, book</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana Ordinis prædicatorum (testante eodem illustrissimo præsule) eumdem cum D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050123Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana of the Order of Preachers (as attested by the same most illustrious prelate) the same with D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana confessarius Virginis, &lt;s2&gt;[ferreo flagello aliisque modis,]&lt;/s2&gt; cum hunc disciplinarum rigorem imbecillitati corporis paulo æquius censuisset attemperandum, Rosa maximis ab eo precibus extorsit, quo saltem liceret sibi intra certum dierum spatium verbera quinquies mille sibi infligere, qui pie creditur esse numerus ictuum, quos in sua crudeli flagellatione Christus excepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041600Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, &lt;s2&gt;[with an iron scourge and other methods,]&lt;/s2&gt; When John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, &lt;s2&gt;[with an iron scourge and other means,]&lt;/s2&gt; judged that this severity of discipline should be tempered somewhat more justly to her bodily frailty, Rose extorted from him by the most earnest prayers that she might at least be permitted, within a certain space of days, to inflict upon herself five thousand lashes, which is piously believed to be the number of blows that Christ received in his own cruel scourging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana simillimo ostento feliciter perculsus occasionem inde obtinuit penitius Rosam cognoscendi, quam illactenus numquam de facie noverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055811Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, happily struck by a very similar vision, thereby had the opportunity to get to know Rosa more deeply, whom until then he had never known face to face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana, Gundisalvus de la Massa quæstor regius, &amp; quæstoris uxor Maria de Usatigui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043608Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, Gundisalvus de la Massa, royal treasurer, and the treasurer's wife, Maria de Usatigui.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Lorenzana, cui in Rosam totum, usque ad extrema, competebat imperium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050023Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, to whom the entire Rose, down to its very extremities, was subject.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Joannes de Villalobos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, Rector Limensis collegii Societatis Jesu, Vir sanctimonia ac religione celeberrimus, &amp; animarum per tutiora compendia perfectionis deducendarum auriga peritissimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042307Z">
        <seg>John of Villalobos, Rector of the Society of Jesus's college in Lima, a man celebrated for his holiness and piety, and a most skilled guide in leading souls to perfection by safer paths.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jocus erat, ac puerilis ridendi materies;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034944Z">
        <seg>It was a joke, and a childish cause for laughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jubentibus confessariis obtemperans Virgo, conabatur ex his nonnullos indicare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055851Z">
        <seg>Obeying the orders of the confessors, the Virgin tried to point out some of them from among these.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Jurejurando testata est genitrix (in Processu Remissoriali) sibi fuisse hunc partum, ut felicissimum, ita facillimum, minimeque obnoxium illis angoribus, quibuscum solebat in parturitione reliquarum prolium dirissime conflictari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211817Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211817Z">
        <seg>The mother testified under oath (in the Remissorial Process) that this birth had been for her, as the most joyful, so the easiest, and least subject to those agonies with which she was wont to struggle most terribly in the childbirth of her other children.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Juvenis coram Virgine sedere jussus, comiter ex urbano ritu sciscitari cœpit, qui valeret, venisse se pro demandando opere conficiendorum aliquot collarium: petiit in singula quot ulnæ melioris telæ forent necessariæ, quibus impendiis, quo loco aptius emenda materies, intra quod tempus absolvi possit opus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060620Z">
        <seg>The young man, having been ordered to sit before the Virgin, politely began, in the urban fashion, to inquire what was required, saying he had come on a commission to have several collars made: he asked how many ells of the finest cloth would be needed for each, at what cost, where the material could most conveniently be purchased, and within what time the work could be completed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laborabat hic Rosa, ut verba reperiret idonea, sed tandem per solos terminos remotionis, eminentiæ, &amp; causalitatis, utcumque se explicuit, insinuans lumen esse absque figura, mensura, fine: unde patuit ex ea parte, visionem pure intellectualem esse, incomprehensum, &amp; omnia comprehendens, subtile, stabile, mundissimum, summe multiplex summeque unum, summe distans &amp; summe propinquum, intimum &amp; ambiens, nobile, excelsum, &amp; nulli prorsus creaturæ vel tenuiter comparabile, quodque magis in vitalium quarumdam effluxionum mirandis &amp; immediatis effectibus, quam in suamet substantia deprehendi possit ab animo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050600Z">
        <seg>Rosa here labored to find suitable words, but at last, by the terms of remoteness, eminence, and causality alone, however she expressed herself, she insinuated that the light is without figure, measure, or end: from which it became clear on that score that the vision is purely intellectual, incomprehensible, and all-comprehending, subtle, stable, most pure, supremely manifold and supremely one, most distant and most near, intimate and surrounding, noble, exalted, and in no way at all comparable to any creature, even faintly, which can be apprehended by the mind more in the marvelous and immediate effects of certain vital effluxes than in its own substance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laborabat sub gaudiorum mole, nec se capiebat Rosa, experta verissime dici: “Sit licet ignis amor, non leve pondus habet”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044421Z">
        <seg>Rosa was laboring under a burden of joy, and could not contain herself, for she truly knew it to be said: "Though love is a fire, it bears no light weight."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lachrymas, quia unice ad Dei ærarium pertinere didicerat, non patiebatur alio a quoquam inutiliter profundi, cupiens has soli divinæ majestati in tributum ab omnibus reservari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053356Z">
        <seg>Tears, because he had learned that they belonged exclusively to God's treasury, he would not allow anyone to shed them in vain, desiring that they be reserved by all as a tribute to the divine majesty alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Langueo, deficiunt oculi, præcordia frigent,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051003Z">
        <seg>I languish, my eyes fail, my vitals grow cold,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latine hæc satis efferri non possunt, quæ in Hispano Rosæ idiomate, propriis numeris venustius illigabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045348Z">
        <seg>In Latin, these cannot be rendered adequately, since in the Spanish dialect of Rosæ they were more charmingly bound by their own numbers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latinis rei tam sero visæ proprium adhuc deesse nomen crediderim, nisi quod supplente periphrasi &lt;b1&gt;florem Passionis&lt;/b1&gt; vocitet fidelis Europa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203332Z">
        <seg>I believe that the Latins still lack a proper name for a thing so late discovered, except that faithful Europe, by way of a circumlocution, calls it the Flower of Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latinis rei tam sero visæ proprium adhuc deesse nomen crediderim, nisi quod supplente periphrasi &lt;b5&gt;florem Passionis&lt;/b5&gt; vocitet fidelis Europa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041247Z">
        <seg>I believe that the Latins still lack a proper name for a thing so late discovered, except that faithful Europe, by way of a circumlocution, calls it the Flower of Passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latuisset perpetuo nobile hoc stratagema, ni postea manu aliena sat operose extrahenda fuisset acus, quæ sub florido blandimento profunde sævierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035319Z">
        <seg>This noble stratagem would have remained hidden forever, had it not later been so laboriously extracted by a foreign hand—the needle that had deeply gnawed beneath a flowery blandishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Latuit ejus matrem generosum facinus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061417Z">
        <seg>His mother's noble deed was hidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudabat assidue priscorum eremicolarum felicissima tempora, suspirabat ad omnem Nitriæ ac Thebaïdis&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; mentionem, &amp;, ut verbulo absolvam, migrarant in Rosam ignita S. Catharinæ Senensis desideria, quibus olim antra deserti haud perfunctorie depoposcerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043953Z">
        <seg>He constantly praised the most blessed times of the ancient hermits, sighed at every mention of Nitria and Thebaid, and, to sum up in a word, his desires had migrated to the burning Rose of St. Catherine of Siena, for whom he had once earnestly longed for the caves of the desert.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudavit simplicem obedientis Rosæ candorem doctor, præcepitque, ne trepidaret, nihil hactenus erratum esse, tantum ad reliqua procederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050532Z">
        <seg>The doctor praised the simple purity of the obedient Rose and instructed her not to be alarmed, saying that nothing had been done wrong so far and that she should proceed with the rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laudetur SS. Trinitas, cui in excelso gloriæ fastigio, inter præcipuos paradisi cives assistit Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044548Z">
        <seg>Praised be the Holy Trinity, before whom Rose stands among the chief citizens of paradise on the highest summit of glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laus tibi Christe”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054639Z">
        <seg>Praise to you, Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Laxa fibras philomela</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045230Z">
        <seg>Loose the threads, Philomela</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Legebat quandoque interdiu libellos spirituales, &amp; seligebat studiose capitula præsenti affectui temporique magis accommodata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050904Z">
        <seg>He would sometimes read spiritual pamphlets during the day, and would diligently select chapters more suited to his present mood and the occasion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen Ordinis Prædicatorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211734Z">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen of the Order of Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211746Z">
        <seg>Leonardo Hansen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lepores erant, quibus Virgo liberius adblandiebatur dignationi sui Dilecti, qui Rosam pauperem, utpote matre Oliva &amp; patre de Floribus natam, minime fastidierat, ex quo ab ea se tam tenere amari permiserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053251Z">
        <seg>They were charms by which the Virgin more freely courted the favor of her Beloved, who, having been born of a poor mother, Olive, and a father, de Floribus, had in no way disdained the Rose, since he had allowed himself to be so tenderly loved by her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lexikon der Heiligen, Seligen und Verehrten der katholischen, orthodoxen und protestantischen Kirchen</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211702Z">
        <seg>Dictionary of the Saints, Blessed, and Venerated of the Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant Churches</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Libeat interim mirari lusum æternæ Sapientiæ, quæ ad fructum uberiorem electas animas sic mortificat &amp; vivificat, deducit ad inferos, &amp; reducit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045906Z">
        <seg>Let us meanwhile marvel at the play of eternal Wisdom, which, to bring forth a more abundant fruit, thus mortifies and vivifies the chosen souls, leads them to the underworld, and brings them back.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lima, alio nomine dicta Civitas Regum, debuit &amp; suam habere stellam, ad arcta Salvatoris cunabula tacite invitantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043708Z">
        <seg>Lima, otherwise called the City of Kings, ought to have had its own star, silently inviting to the Savior's narrow cradle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limam redux, amare questus est de Virgine, tandem &amp; coram Virgine, quod in medio viæ ipsum destituisset orationum suarum præsidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051849Z">
        <seg>Upon his return to Lima, he bitterly complained about the Virgin, and at last even before her, that she had abandoned him in the middle of the road without the protection of his prayers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limanus ager ut psittacos, ita papiliones gignit, &lt;s2&gt;[per duplex miraculum]&lt;/s2&gt; colorum varietate ac elegantia plane mirabiles, ut fere de utriusque gloria hi cum illis certare videantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040149Z">
        <seg>The Limian plain produces butterflies as well as parrots, &lt;s2&gt;[by a double miracle]&lt;/s2&gt; truly marvelous in the variety and elegance of their colors, so that they almost seem to rival one another in glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limanus ager ut psittacos, ita papiliones gignit, [per duplex miraculum] colorum varietate ac elegantia plane mirabiles, ut fere de utriusque gloria hi cum illis certare videantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203504Z">
        <seg>The Limian plain produces butterflies as well as parrots, [by a double miracle] truly marvelous in the variety and elegance of their colors, so that they almost seem to rival one another in glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limense emporium fama impleverat, quamdam mulierem Deo solemniter dicatam, fracto religionis vinculo, e Cantabria profugisse in Indias, ac sub virili habitu in civitate Guamanga&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; diu errabundam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053415Z">
        <seg>A rumor had spread throughout the market of Lima that a certain woman, solemnly consecrated to God, having broken the bonds of her religion, had fled from Cantabria to the Indies, and, under the guise of a man, had long wandered about the city of Guamanga.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Limæ ab annis centum &amp; amplius floret sub invocatione B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055014Z">
        <seg>Lima has flourished for more than a hundred years under the invocation of the Blessed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Litarat &amp; ipse in Rosarii sacello, &amp; post Sacrificium, promiscuo populo ibidem communicaturo Sacramentum distribuebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055814Z">
        <seg>He himself would pray in the chapel of the Rosary, and after the Sacrifice, would distribute the Sacrament to the common people there to receive Communion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Locum tibi designavi, illic erit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062647Z">
        <seg>I have marked the place for you; he will be there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longe alias, quam de collaribus, cogitationes versat cor tuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060635Z">
        <seg>Your heart turns its thoughts far away from collars.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longe mirabilius fuit, quod notarunt domestici, Rosam plus virium semper hausisse a jejuniis &amp; longa inedia, quam a cibo corporali: verum (ut infra narrabimus) etiam hoc ipsi cum magistra sua S. Catharina Senensi commune fuit, quod os famelicum vulnerato Christi lateri applicuerit: siquidem extra hunc favorem &amp; favum tam Discipula quam magistra non norant aliud, quo saturari possent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041404Z">
        <seg>Far more marvelous was what her household noticed, That Rosa always drew more strength from fasting and prolonged hunger than from bodily food: yet (as we shall relate below) she shared with her teacher, St. Catherine of Siena, the fact that she applied her hungry mouth to the wounded side of Christ: for apart from this favor and honey, neither disciple nor teacher knew of anything else by which they could be satisfied.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Longe suavius erat Rosæ, filiolam vocari a tali Matre, quam Rosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051403Z">
        <seg>It was far sweeter for Rosa to be called "little daughter" by such a mother than "Rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lubet hic simplicius ex ipsis Rosæ pugillaribus recitare formulam, qua sibimet Virgo hoc opus præfiniebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053542Z">
        <seg>It seems fitting here to quote more simply from Rose's own pamphlets the formula by which the Virgin defined this work for herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lucia Guerra de la Daga, loco situque commodissimo stetit in Lima celebre hoc cœnobium, &amp; introducto candidi velaminis novitio gynæceo, cœpere Divina celebrari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062418Z">
        <seg>Lucia Guerra de la Daga stood in a most convenient place and location in Lima, and with the introduction of a novice of the white veil to the convent, divine services began to be celebrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lucia Guerra de la Daga.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062907Z">
        <seg>Lucy, daughter of the Sword</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Lucia hoc solatio dives, ac domum reversa, quiescere non potuit, dum Rosam, plane jam suam, denuo conveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062920Z">
        <seg>Rich with this comfort, Lucia, upon returning home, could not rest until she met Rosa, who was now truly her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovica ejusque filia Isabella Alexia substitit ante fores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043358Z">
        <seg>Ludovica and her daughter Isabella Alexia paused before the doors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao Virginis confessarius, &lt;s2&gt;[asseveranter suo confessario]&lt;/s2&gt; tametsi reliquis ejus dictis plurimum deferret, huic tamen ejusdem vaticinio scrupulosius addubitabat, eo quod ad humana respiciens, ne quidem e longinquo videre poterat vel minimam verisimilitudinis stricturam, quæ tanti moliminis ullum aliquando successum promitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062616Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, confessor of the Virgin, &lt;s2&gt;[firmly to his confessor]&lt;/s2&gt; although he gave the greatest deference to his other sayings, he nevertheless hesitated more scrupulously over this same prophecy of his, because, looking to human things, he could not even from a distance see the slightest semblance of probability that could promise any success for so great an undertaking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao post Missam in eodem sacello, dum Virgini sacram Synaxim porrigit, notavit sæpius vultum ipsius cælesti splendore perfusum, ita quod præ fulgoris vehementia sibi tremuerint oculi, velut impares illi sidereæ claritati, quam gratiæ venustas conspicue accenderat, &amp; Panis angelorum in angelicam (uti aiebat) pulchritudinem mirifice transformarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055805Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, after Mass in the same chapel, while offering the sacred Communion to the Virgin, often noticed her face bathed in a heavenly splendor, so that her eyes trembled before the intensity of the glow, as if unworthy of that starry clarity which the beauty of grace had conspicuously kindled. and had wonderfully transformed the Bread of Angels into an angelic (as he said) beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao, &amp; F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050222Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao, &amp; F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ludovicus de Bilbao, notavit eadem in Rosa, dum ejus confessiones excipiebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050659Z">
        <seg>Louis of Bilbao noted the same in Rosa, while he was taking her confession.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magister Fr.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055802Z">
        <seg>Master Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magistri F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040205Z">
        <seg>Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magistrum F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040627Z">
        <seg>Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Magna profecto Rosæ dignitas, matris ac magistræ Senensis fuisse, non modo filiam ac discipulam, sed &amp; collactaneam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051127Z">
        <seg>It was truly a great honor for Rosa to have been a mother and teacher of the Sena, not only her daughter and pupil, but also her foster-sister.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluisset auro gemmisque rigentem cycladem subinde offerre sacræ imagini florum loco, sed obstabat inopia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054556Z">
        <seg>He would have preferred to offer the sacred image a gilded and jeweled cycad from time to time in place of flowers, but poverty stood in the way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluisset, pro conformiori similitudine, gestare coronam ex spinis agrestibus, eaque de re serio tractaverat cum confessario;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042146Z">
        <seg>He would have preferred, for a closer resemblance, to wear a crown of wild thorns, and had seriously discussed the matter with his confessor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluit deinde ab ipso Virginis ore modum repentinæ curationis ediscere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055436Z">
        <seg>He then preferred to learn the method of the sudden cure from the Virgin's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maluit itaque per confessarios tentare successum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042710Z">
        <seg>He therefore preferred to try to succeed through the confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mane facto, crudeles chirothecas matri placidissime restituens, totam rei seriem candore simplici exposuit, manus fibratim ambustas ostendit, ac, ne deinceps ad hujusmodi revocaretur supplicia, obnixe rogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035402Z">
        <seg>When morning came, he most placably returned the cruel gloves to his mother, laid out the whole affair in simple candor, showed his hands, which were burned in places, and earnestly begged that he might not be subjected to such punishments again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Manicatum hoc erat, atque a collo &amp; humeris infra genua propendebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041839Z">
        <seg>This was a tunic, and it hung from the neck and shoulders down below the knees.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mansuetudo, comitas, modestia Virginis trahebat notas feminas, ut specie honoris dum accedebant ad matrem, postularent videre &amp; Filiam, quam suavis odor virtutis, honestas indolis, admiratio sanctitatis, quam plurimis etiam illustribus commendarat invitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043451Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's meekness, courtesy, and modesty drew distinguished women, so that, under the guise of honor as they approached the mother, they would ask to see the Daughter as well—whom the sweet fragrance of virtue, the nobility of her character, and the admiration of her sanctity had, even against her will, recommended to many distinguished persons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Marcebat stupore lætissimo quæstor, ac tacite in uxorem respiciens, pendebat attonitus ab ore narrantis, ut Virgo se in preces, ipso abeunte, prostraverat, ut brevissimo intervallo sana in pedes se erexerat, strenue laborem, forficibus postulatis, fuerat auspicata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055433Z">
        <seg>The quaestor was overcome with the happiest astonishment, and, silently glancing at his wife, hung spellbound on the narrator's lips, as the Virgin had prostrated herself in prayer when he departed, and in the briefest interval had risen to her feet in perfect health and had vigorously undertaken the task, scissors in hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria Usategui propriis manibus, &lt;s2&gt;[statim Sancta sanatur,]&lt;/s2&gt; Rosæ domum reduci brachium nudavit, gossipium circumduxit, alligaturam reposuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054013Z">
        <seg>Maria Usategui, with her own hands, immediately healed the Saint, and, having stripped her arm to the house of Rosæ, she wound a bandage around it and put the splint back in place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria Usatigui, quod ejus in ædibus dum triennlum vixit Rosa, adeo sese omnium reputarit infimam, ut non soli patrifamilias &amp; conjugi, sed tenellis quoque filiabus, imo servis, ancillis, mancipiis domesticis subjecta ad nutum, singulorum æque placita, velut imperia, exceperit, favorem arbitrata, si quid sibi ceu precario in domum receptæ heriliter mandabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040701Z">
        <seg>Maria Usatigui, because Rosa had lived in her house for three years, considered herself so inferior to everyone that she submitted to the wills of not only the head of the household and her husband, but also to her tender daughters, indeed, subject to the will of servants, maids, and household slaves, she received everyone's commands as if they were orders, believing she had favor whenever anything was commanded of her as a mistress, as if she had been taken into the house on sufferance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Mesta, pictoris Medori Angelini conjux, impatientiæ ac præcipitis iracundiæ pugnaci morbo jam pene sibimet ipsi facta erat intolerabilis: quavis re minima turbabatur ac turbabat, manu prompta, minax clamoribus, &amp; sonora rixandi intemperie totarum ædium implacabilis tempestas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060719Z">
        <seg>Maria de Mesta, wife of the painter Medor Angelini, had by her impatient and headlong temper become almost unbearable to everyone: she was disturbed and disturbing over the slightest thing, ready with her hand, menacing with her shouts, and with her loud quarrelsomeness an implacable storm throughout the entire house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva Rosæ mater, singulari complexionis indole, tenebras &amp; spectra metuebat, nec facile aut noctu prodibat ad secretiora domus, aut ad vesperam sine comite sese proprio fidebat horto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062037Z">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva, mother of Rosæ, of a singular temperament, feared darkness and spectres, and would not easily venture at night into the more secluded parts of the house, nor did she trust herself alone in the garden after dusk.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva, seu ignara, seu immemor penuriæ, bis ter in caveam misit, qui de vase omnibus noto mel adferrent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062222Z">
        <seg>Maria de Oliva, whether ignorant or forgetful of the scarcity, sent three times into the cage those who were to bring honey from the jar known to everyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Quinnones, cui avunculus erat ille ipse famigeratissimus Dei servus, Limensium archiepiscopus tunc superstes, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040019Z">
        <seg>Maria de Quinnones, whose uncle was that very infamous servant of God, the then surviving Archbishop of Lima, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Sala ultro per famulum misit ferici subtegminis aptam materiam, quanta conficiendæ novæ pallæ sat erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061138Z">
        <seg>Maria de Sala sent, through a servant, a suitable piece of coarse under-cloth, enough to make a new pall.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui porrectus fuerat, modicæ chartæ involutum stringebat intra pugnum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044526Z">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui lay stretched out, clutching in his fist a small roll of paper.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui, consorte regii quæstoris D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035610Z">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui, wife of the royal treasurer, D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Maria de Usategui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050234Z">
        <seg>Mary of Usategui</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariam de Usategui aliquamdiu asservati, mirabilem, at omnibus peregrinam spirarunt suaveolentiam, testes nimirum pœnosi sudoris, quem de Rosæ corpusculo imbiberant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041807Z">
        <seg>They preserved Mary of Usategui for some time; marvelous though she was, they exhaled a sweet fragrance to all, a testament, indeed, to the painful sweat they had absorbed from the little body of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariam de Usategui, ne toto illo temporis interstitio evocaretur, quidquid demum occurreret, si vel ipsa Rosæ mater veniret Filiæ locutura: causam rogata, candide respondit, se toto illo temporis spatio immobilem de pavimento surgere in pedes non posse, ne quidem ad oratorii januam aperiendam, si quis forte pulsaret ad limen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044917Z">
        <seg>As for Maria de Usategui, she said that during that entire interval she could not be summoned—whatever might happen, even if Rosa's own mother came to speak to her daughter. When asked why, she frankly replied that she could not rise from the floor and stand on her feet during that whole time, not even to open the door of the oratory if someone happened to knock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariana quædam serviebat in Rosæ paternis ædibus, Inda genere, agrestis conditione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040506Z">
        <seg>A certain Mariana was serving in Rose's father's house; she was of Indian descent and of humble origin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariana, ancilla domestica, horum arcanorum sola aliquamdiu conscia, sola Virgini a secretis erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041625Z">
        <seg>Mariana, the household maid, was the only one aware of these secrets for some time; she alone was the Virgin's confidante.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariæ Magdalenæ sacro pervigilio, visa est formidabilis illa hæreticorum classis, acie disposita, in ipsum Limensis emporii portum (Callao indigenis dicitur) recta procurrere, velut in urbis excidium militares suas copias effusura in terram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060149Z">
        <seg>During the sacred vigil of Mary Magdalene, that formidable host of heretics, drawn up in battle array, was seen rushing straight for the very harbor of the port of Lima (called Callao by the natives), as if about to land its military forces on the ground for the sacking of the city.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mariæ de Usategui suggereret, me illo, quem vidisti, succursu indigere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051744Z">
        <seg>It would suggest to Mary of Usategui that I need the help you saw.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Martinez, &amp; tertia noctis hora imminebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053836Z">
        <seg>Martinez, and the third hour of the night was approaching.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater (quæ horti clavem jugiter tenebat secum) ut notavit in vultu Filiæ signa instantis deliquii, sine mora promptis a marsupio duobus denariis, jussit ancillæ, adiret subito vicinioris propolæ apothecam, emeret saccharum, carbonem, &amp; libum Indicum, quod CHOCOLATE&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; vocant indigenæ, jam &amp; Europæis tam usu quam nomine sat notum: quippe in aqua calida resolutum cum triente sacchari, stomacho simul &amp; cordi potenter succurrit&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;h&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051642Z">
        <seg>The mother (who always kept the key to the garden with her), seeing the signs of an impending fainting fit on her daughter's face, without delay took two denarii from her purse and ordered the maid to go at once to the nearest apothecary's and buy sugar, charcoal, and an Indian cake, which the natives call CHOCOLATE, already well known to Europeans both in use and by name: for when dissolved in hot water with a third part sugar, it powerfully helps both the stomach and the heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater advertens Filiæ decumbenti pristinos rediisse dolores, &amp; quidem cum auctario, suspicata periculum, extimuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051040Z">
        <seg>The mother, noticing that her daughter who was lying down had her former pains return, and indeed with an increase, suspected danger and was terrified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater filiam quæsitura venit, sed comite marito, quia (ut dictum est) ad nocturnos terrores timidissima erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062046Z">
        <seg>The mother came to look for her daughter, but with her husband as companion, because (as has been said) she was extremely fearful of nighttime terrors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater obvio, sed imperito suasa consilio, modicos pulveres inspersit effectu plane improspero: nam ii, utpote olentes Hermeticam&lt;a0&gt;l&lt;/a0&gt; pyxidem, affectas partes caustica sua acrimonia rodebant ita immaniter, ut certis intervallis toto corpusculo tremuisse puellam tacite notarint domestici.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000831Z">
        <seg>The mother, on the advice of an inexperienced person, dusted her lightly with a powder, but the result was a complete failure: for it, smelling of the Hermetic box, with its caustic acrimony gnawed so horribly at the affected parts that the household servants quietly noted the girl trembling all over at regular intervals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater obvio, sed imperito suasa consilio, modicos pulveres inspersit effectu plane improspero: nam ii, utpote olentes Hermeticam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;l&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; pyxidem, affectas partes caustica sua acrimonia rodebant ita immaniter, ut certis intervallis toto corpusculo tremuisse puellam tacite notarint domestici.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034903Z">
        <seg>The mother, on the advice of an inexperienced person, dusted on some mild powders with completely unfortunate results: for they, smelling of the Hermetic box, gnawed at the affected parts with their caustic acrimony so terribly that the household servants quietly noted the girl trembling throughout her whole little body at regular intervals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater vim morbi præsentissimo (ut putabat) remedio depulsura, quorumdam animalium illic frequentium hispidas pelliculas arcte circumposuit patienti Filiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035505Z">
        <seg>The mother, thinking to drive off the force of the disease with the most immediate remedy she could find, tightly wrapped the patient daughter with the rough skins of certain animals that were common there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater, quæ in illam usque horam ad ejusmodi voces Apenninis cautibus durior fuerat, statim ut cera liquefacta cessit, nec vel momentum temporis aut deliberationi, aut dilationi in re tam ardua deposcens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043615Z">
        <seg>The mother, who until that moment had been more impervious to such appeals than the stony slopes of the Apennines, as soon as she melted like wax gave way, demanding neither a moment's time for deliberation or delay in so arduous a matter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mater, ubi frustra totis ædibus suum requisivit pallium, suspicionibus indulsit, pene usque ad temerarium judicium, quod vicinorum aliquis clam irrepsisset domui, pallamque neglectim a sede pendulam sublegisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061113Z">
        <seg>The mother, having searched in vain throughout the entire house for her cloak, indulged in suspicions, almost to the point of rash judgment, that one of the neighbors had crept into the house secretly and had picked up the cloak, which had been carelessly left hanging from the chair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Matrona facile animadvertit inter imaginem &amp; Rosam singulare quidpiam geri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054531Z">
        <seg>The matron easily noticed that something peculiar was happening between the image and Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Matutino crepusculo Rosa de more ad cellulam suam anachoreticam reditura, simul ut horti januam pandebat, eminus conspectas arbores, virgulta, herbulas omnes invitabat, ut secum benedicerent Creatori: “Benedicite universa germinantia in terra Domino.” Et ecce, moveri subito in euphoniam &amp; concentum ramusculos, collidi frondibus frondes, plaudere omni ex parte streperos foliorum susurros, motitari humiliorum plantularum capita;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045146Z">
        <seg>At early dawn, Rosa, as was her custom, was returning to her hermit's cell. As she opened the garden gate, she invited the trees, the shrubs, and all the herbs she saw in the distance to bless the Creator with her: "Bless the Lord, all you things that germinate on the earth." And behold, the twigs suddenly stirred into harmony and concert, leaves collided with leaves, the rustling of leaves resounded from every side, the humble little plants stirred their heads;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Me sine vita vivere, me præstolando mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051617Z">
        <seg>To live without you is to die of longing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Medici Lugdunenses apud Menagium in Dictionario etymologico ad vocem&lt;/e0&gt; tabac &lt;e1&gt;de hac herba sic scribunt:&lt;/e1&gt; Quemadmodum hortis omnibus magno est ornamento, ita facultatibus insignibus celeberrima est herba, quam PETUM ab Indis vocari refert Thevetus, Nicolaus Monardus PICIELT;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060936Z">
        <seg>The physicians of Lyon, in the Dictionary of Etymology under the word&lt;/e0&gt; "tabac," &lt;e1&gt;write thus of this herb:&lt;/e1&gt; "As it is a great ornament to all gardens, so it is celebrated for its remarkable properties; the herb which Thevetus reports to be called PETUM by the Indians, and Nicholas Monardus PICIELT."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Medici inflammationes faucium cum tumore&lt;/e0&gt; tonsillas &lt;e1&gt;appellant, quas Græci nominant&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;παρίσθμια&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;per similitudinem, de qua Lexicon medicum Castelli pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043113Z">
        <seg>Physicians call inflammations of the throat with swelling of the tonsils "quae Graeci nominant parysthmia," by reason of a similarity, concerning which see Castell's Medical Lexicon, p.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meditabatur illic sensu acerrimo dira gehennæ incendia, quæ sibi deberi plus quam serio autumabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041922Z">
        <seg>He was there brooding with the keenest sense of the dreadful fires of hell, which he believed were due to him more than seriously.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meliceride dissecta, inibi contentus humor non aliter, quam mel e favis diffluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055606Z">
        <seg>When the honeycomb is cut open, the honey contained within flows out just like honey from the combs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Melos demus vocibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045304Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045304Z">
        <seg>Let us give melody to voices.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meminerat ancilla, ejusmodi seras valida contusione nonnumquam aperiri: dum igitur abit quæsitura saxum, anxia Rosa, matrem aut vocari aut sponte mox affore timens, ad orationem confugit, quæ instar clavis, &amp; cælum, &amp; seram penetravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041733Z">
        <seg>The maid remembered that such strong locks could sometimes be opened by a powerful blow; so, while she went to fetch a stone, anxious Rosa, fearing that her mother would either be summoned or would soon appear of her own accord, fled to prayer, which, like a key, pierced both heaven and the lock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memini (inquit) de nonnullis præcipuis ac sanctissimis Dei amicis (quorum nomina publicis Ecclesiæ tabulis adscripta sunt) eadem legisse, quæ de te commemoras;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050420Z">
        <seg>I remember (he says) having read the same things about some of the chief and most holy friends of God (whose names are inscribed on the public tables of the Church) that you mention about you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Memor deinde, eamdem Gundisalvi uxorem assedisse examini, de quo supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054348Z">
        <seg>Remember, then, that the same Gundisalvi's wife was subjected to the ordeal, about which see above, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mendacium est, quidquid ad Deum non ducit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060651Z">
        <seg>A lie is whatever does not lead to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meos mihi hospites trucidas”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043900Z">
        <seg>"Have my guests been slaughtered for me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Metrico sui idiomatis rhythmo&lt;s15&gt;&lt;a16&gt;f&lt;/a16&gt;&lt;/s15&gt; tunc familiarius affabatur angelum, hoc fere sensu:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051607Z">
        <seg>In the metrical rhythm of his own idiom, he then addressed the angel more familiarly, in this sense:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Meum ego Salvatorem:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045245Z">
        <seg>My Savior, I:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mi Pater, neque si vastos cælorum orbes quis huic telluri complicuerit, in eam me sententiam flectet, qua credere possum, Dei permissu me noxam mortalem commisisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062021Z">
        <seg>My Father, not even if someone were to fold the vast orbs of the heavens onto this earth could he bend me to the belief that, with God's permission, I have committed a mortal sin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mi vere Deus, &amp; Sponse animæ meæ, tota lætitia cordis mei: ego, ego amare te desidero, benignissime Jesu, amore illo perfectissimo, amore efficacissimo, sincerissimo, ineffabili, intensissimo, incomparabili, incomprehensibili, irrefragabili, invictissimo, quo simul universi cives empyrei te diligunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053105Z">
        <seg>My true God and Spouse of my soul, the entire joy of my heart: I, I desire to love you, most benign Jesus, with that perfect, most efficacious, most sincere, ineffable, most intense, incomparable, incomprehensible, irrefutable, unconquerable love with which all the citizens of heaven love you at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mira hæc Sanctorum est ambitio, qua malunt Christi spineam, quam auream coronam in hujus vitæ exilio circumferre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042037Z">
        <seg>This is the remarkable ambition of the saints, by which they prefer to carry Christ's thorny crown rather than a golden one in the exile of this life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mira res!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041736Z">
        <seg>What a wonder!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabantur, Virginem modestissimam in publico revoluto circa humeros pallio, veste curta, projectis crepidis, liberrimis brachiis, alto præcinctu, solisque rosarii globulis precariis ferociter obarmatam, spe martyrii ultro lacessere fatalem horam, ac morti pugnacissime minitari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060245Z">
        <seg>They marveled that the most modest virgin in public, with her pallium thrown over her shoulders, her dress cut short, her sandals flung aside, her arms bare, her waist high-cinched, and armed ferociously only with the precious beads of the rosary, would of her own accord provoke the fatal hour of martyrdom and most fiercely threaten death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabantur, sub Ægyptia hac plaga, Rosam integros dies immotam consistere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043810Z">
        <seg>They were amazed that, under this Egyptian plague, Rosa stood motionless for entire days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirabatur Virgo tam celeriter absumi potuisse annonam, quam pridie posuerant, utique in biduum suffecturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062210Z">
        <seg>The virgin was amazed that the provisions they had laid out the day before could have been consumed so quickly, provisions that would surely have lasted for two days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirata est mater præsentaneum &amp; velox Angeli obsequium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051756Z">
        <seg>The mother was amazed at the prompt and swift obedience of Angel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirata simplicitatem parens, dum pelliculas dimovet, videt harum mordaci ardore pustulatim Virginis carnem vesicatam, accensam, &amp; turgidis undique tuberculis ulceratam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035517Z">
        <seg>The mother, amazed at the simplicity, while removing the films, sees the Virgin's flesh, eaten away, inflamed, and ulcerated on all sides by pustules burning with a biting heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirati sunt, quicumque Rosæ hortulum norant, ejus areolis numquam deesse flores, quos altari dedicaret: sive enim hoc germen alibi sustulisset consueta variantis anni temperies, sive ardentior Sirius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;k&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; ubique torrida siccitate hoc terræ solatium corruperat, semper in modico Rosæ viridario intacti vernabant pulluli&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;l&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, quos in proximum Sabbathum cælesti Antophoræ&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;m&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; consecraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054553Z">
        <seg>Those who knew Rose's garden were amazed that its flowerbeds were never without flowers for the altar: for whether the changing seasons of the year had carried this plant elsewhere, or the more scorching Sirius had with its burning heat ruined this comfort of the earth everywhere, always in Rose's modest garden, untouched, the shoots of spring would sprout, which she would consecrate for the next Sabbath to the heavenly Antiphora.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirum dictu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040137Z">
        <seg>How strange!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirum, quam alte Virginis animum hæc verba penetrarint, quantum ei roboris constantiæque affuderint, ne ab horrido illo patientiæ exercitio resiliret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042700Z">
        <seg>It is remarkable how deeply these words penetrated the Virgin's soul, and how much strength and constancy they gave her, so that she would not falter from that dreadful exercise of patience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mirum, quot quantasque uno hoc telo victorias contra stygios dolos reportarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052237Z">
        <seg>It is amazing how many and what great victories he has won with this single weapon against the Stygian deceptions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Misit igitur, qui Rosæ nuntiarent, improvisa ægritudine se obrui, nec vires sibi futuras ad prædicandum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053452Z">
        <seg>He therefore sent messengers to tell Rose that he was overcome by a sudden illness and would not have the strength to preach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Missas quidem auscultabat omnes, quotquot in æde FF.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060012Z">
        <seg>He indeed attended all the Masses, however many there were in the Franciscan friary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Molestum hominibus animal si quod aliud: nam unica sua proboscide, &amp; tubicinem agit &amp; militem, cum non minus feriat tubo, quam tuba ac bombo sopitos infestet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043748Z">
        <seg>A troublesome animal to people, if any other: for with its single proboscis, it both plays the pipe and serves as a soldier, since it strikes with its tube no less than it torments the drowsy with its pipe and drum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Monuere, qui de Rosæ virtutibus post obitum solemniter testificati sunt, duplex jejuniorum ei fuisse genus, unum commune, quo semel in die pertenuem panis &amp; aquæ libabat refectiunculam, at non nisi sub noctem: alterum vero appellabat suum, quo per totum diem noctemque penitus gustabat nihil.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041305Z">
        <seg>Those who solemnly testified to Rose's virtues after her death reported that she observed two kinds of fasts: one common, in which she once a day took a very small refection of bread and water, but only at night; the other, which she called her own, in which she tasted nothing at all, neither by day nor by night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox Virgo in oratorium se proripuit, ac inde post duas circiter horas egressa brachium undequaque sanum &amp; flexile ostentavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054016Z">
        <seg>Soon the virgin rushed into the oratory, and when she emerged about two hours later, she displayed her arm completely sound and supple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox altis ac prætumidis passibus totum conclave perambulans, ad Rosæ nutum pluries toto distento pectusculo alacriter cantavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061547Z">
        <seg>Soon, pacing the entire chamber with lofty and proud strides, he sang eagerly several times at Rose's nod, his whole chest distended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox cogitabundus ait intra se: “Quæcumque est hæc Virgo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055823Z">
        <seg>Soon, pondering, he said to himself: "Whatever this Virgin may be;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox consurgens, hilariori voce poposcit forfices ab uxore Gundisalvi, quæ illic cum aliis ad opus substiterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055402Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055402Z">
        <seg>Rising soon after, he asked in a more cheerful voice for scissors from Gundisalvi's wife, who was standing there with others at work.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox conticescente Rosa, ex abrupto carmen suum resumebat avicula, duplicabat intercisionum minutias, tremulis arteriolis volubiles confundebat accentus, deprimebat, elevabat, torquebat facillimas lubricæ voculæ argutias, &amp; repente, velut ad pausæ signum, interquiescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045317Z">
        <seg>As soon as Rosa fell silent, the little bird would abruptly resume its song, double the minutiae of its interruptions, confuse the accents with trembling, whirling arterioles, depress, elevate, and twist the most delicate witticisms of its slippery little voice, and suddenly, as if on a pause sign, it would fall silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox hilares susurri certatim implebant angulos, organique pneumatici modulos gestiebat æmulari volatilis harmonia, donec, jubente Rosa, simul omnes conticescerent velut sub una lege nocturni silentii.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043838Z">
        <seg>Soon cheerful whispers filled the corners in eager competition, and the organ's pneumatic pipes boasted a flying harmony, until, at Rosa's command, they all fell silent at once, as if under a single law of nocturnal silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox ingenti fiducia panariam arcam aperuit, plenamque reperit panibus, ultra domus illius morem candidis, azymis, ac saporosis, testante eorumdem figura illic inusitata ac peregrina, hos nequaquam a domestico panificio prodiisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062216Z">
        <seg>He soon opened the bread chest with great confidence and found it full of loaves—unusually white, unleavened, and flavorful for that household—as evidenced by their shape, which was entirely unfamiliar and exotic there, proving they could in no way have come from the household baker.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox intra penum occlusa animadvertit, porriginosum hostem suum in prægrandi sporta, quam labri fundus occultabat, licentius tumultuari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052048Z">
        <seg>Soon, shut inside the pantry, she noticed her slimy enemy stirring more licentiously in a huge basket hidden beneath the lip of the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox nivei candoris Attalicam&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; emit, novamque iconi vestem aptavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055101Z">
        <seg>He soon painted Attalus white as snow and dressed the icon in a new garment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox puparum lusu coæqualibus relicto, abdebat se in secretum angulum, quasi arcanis cogitationibus quietius vacatura: illic interdum deprehensa a fratre, ac interrogata, quidni mallet, puerulis festive nugantibus colludere, quam in pulverulento, ac pulicibus referto angulo desidere?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043234Z">
        <seg>Soon, having left her playmates to their dolls, she withdrew to a secret corner, as if to devote herself more quietly to her secret thoughts. There, sometimes discovered by her brother and asked what she would rather do, she would reply that she preferred to play with the boys in their festive nonsense than to sit in that dusty, flea-infested corner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox rediit genis detritis vividus color, reddita faciei corpulentior plenitudo, fronti nitor, luminibus vigor, ut propemodum jurari potuisset, Rosam, quid sit jejunium, penitus ignorare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040741Z">
        <seg>Soon a vivid color returned to her cheeks, the fuller roundness of her face was restored, a glow to her brow, vigor to her eyes, so that one could almost swear that Rosa was utterly ignorant of what fasting is.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox tota urbe trepidatum est, jussique cum civibus ecclesiastici arma corripere: siquidem certum erat, Calvinianum hunc hostem non solis incolarum fortunis &amp; jugulis, sed &amp; sacrorum profanationi, templorum spoliis, religionis exterminio sævissime inhiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060152Z">
        <seg>Soon the whole city was in an uproar, and the clergy, together with the citizens, were ordered to seize arms; for it was certain that this Calvinist foe was most savagely intent not only on the fortunes and throats of the inhabitants, but also on the desecration of sacred things, the plundering of churches, and the extermination of religion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mox ubi in mea cellula cœpi deficere, misi angelum, qui D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051741Z">
        <seg>Soon, when I began to fail in my cell, I sent an angel, who D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Multa de similibus amantis Rosæ vaporariis, de anteroticis&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt; ad sponsum aspiriis, &amp; eclogis dicenda superessent, forte non indigna relatu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053254Z">
        <seg>Many things would remain to be said about the similar vaporings of the lover Rose, about the pre-nuptial aspirations to the bridegroom, and about the eclogues; perhaps not unworthy of relation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mutua harum Virginum conversatio tam amica &amp; crebra fuit, ut sicut Moysi, post longam cum Deo in monte confabulationem, remansere in vultu luminum vestigia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051434Z">
        <seg>The mutual conversation of these Virgins was so friendly and frequent, that, just as with Moses, after a long conversation with God on the mountain, traces of light remained on his face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mysterii intelligentia hic fratri defuit, non item Rosæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062536Z">
        <seg>The brother here lacked an understanding of the mystery, but Rose did not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Mysticam theologiam non nudis speculationibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050051Z">
        <seg>Mystical theology not by bare speculation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nam quotiescumque Rosa primo diluculo reserabat cellulæ portam, &lt;s2&gt;[eam non pungerent,]&lt;/s2&gt; &amp; laxabat fenestellæ valvulas, culicibus (quotquot intus ad parietes densi pernoctarant) præcipiebat: “Eia amici, ad laudes omnipotentis Dei”;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043829Z">
        <seg>For whenever Rosa opened the cell door at first light and loosened the little window shutters, she would call out to the mosquitoes (all those that had spent the night clustered against the walls): "Come, friends, to the praises of Almighty God."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nam reversa domum, &amp; vixdum evoluta palliolo, recta properabat ad secretum cubiculi, ibi in seram usque noctem se recludens, meditabatur suscepti beneficii magnitudinem, neque in ullius prodibat conspectum aut conversationem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055944Z">
        <seg>For when she returned home, and had scarcely taken off her cloak, she hurried straight to her private chamber, where, shutting herself in until late at night, she pondered the greatness of the favor she had received, and she appeared to no one nor conversed with anyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Narravit Virgini confessarius dolorem proreginæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054956Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's confessor told the queen regent of her pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ne matris præceptum floccifieret, hos floccos condensasti in solidæ petræ duritiem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042840Z">
        <seg>So that you would not flout your mother's command, you condensed these flakes into the hardness of solid rock.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ne tamen oblatam remedii gratiam videretur aspernari, continuo ipsamet confessarium adiit in æde S. Dominici: candore, quo solebat, rem exposuit, nutum expectavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054001Z">
        <seg>However, so as not to appear to spurn the offered remedy, she immediately went herself to the confessor in the church of St. Dominic: with the candor she was accustomed to, she laid the matter before him and awaited his nod.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ne tamen sic pendula paucis capillis totam corpusculi molem committeret, utcumque summis pedum digitis insistens, tangebat terram, sed ægre satis, ac per alternas successiones nutante lubrico fulcimento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043103Z">
        <seg>Yet so that she would not entrust the entire weight of her little body to a few strands of hair, she stood on the tips of her toes, touching the ground—but barely—and, with each successive sway, her slippery support wobbled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec (quod fere illi regioni præter morem est) ullum Virgini discrimen erat originis aut nationis, quin ex æquo &amp; indifferenter egentibus Hispanis, &amp; Indis, Æthiopibus, candidis, mixtisque (quas Mulatas&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; vocant) prompta charitate succurreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061246Z">
        <seg>Nor (which is almost unusual for that region) was there any distinction in Virginia of origin or nation, but she would equally and impartially come to the aid of needy Spaniards, Indians, Ethiopians, whites, and mixed-race people (whom they call mulattos) with ready charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec Rosa his contenta, ipsos quoque divini verbi præcones frequentissime compellabat, suos auditores ad orationis exercitamenta inflammarent, usum piarum meditationum populariter inveherent, disciplinæ hujus utilitatem, suavitatem, præstantiam, omni argumentorum vi, totoque facundiæ suæ connisu, privatim ac publice commendarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045121Z">
        <seg>Nor was Rosa content with this; she also most frequently urged the preachers of the divine word to inflame their hearers with exercises in prayer, to popularize the practice of pious meditations, and to recommend, both privately and publicly, the usefulness, sweetness, and excellence of this discipline with all the force of their arguments and the full exertion of their eloquence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec Virgo rosa mystica virgunculam Rosam distulit consolari: siquidem in alto, sed inexplicabili intimæ illustrationis radio, pavidam affata, docuit, mire hoc suum nomen probari divino, quem gestabat, Puerulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034736Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin, the mystical Rose, delay to console the little maiden Rose; for from on high, yet by an inexplicable ray of inner illumination, she spoke to the fearful one and taught her that, remarkably, this very name of hers was being proven divine by the little Boy she carried.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec adeo evanuit hic sudor, quin sub barba, usque ad oram marginis, vestigia reliquerit, multis post annis &amp; lustris palam conspicua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053855Z">
        <seg>Nor has this sweat so vanished but that it has left traces under the beard, right to the very edge, plainly visible many years and lustres later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec adeo has alligaturas, quamquam strictissimas, pellis obduxerat, quin videre eas potuerit Mariana, quando medebatur laceris Rosæ scapulis: nam sæpe postulabatur hoc genus officii, eo quod jugiter in Rosæ dorso patebant scissuræ, quibus, ex intervallo, jussa Mariana inspergebat ignotos pulveres, ne saniem conciperent, chartaque bibula obducebat, repetitis postridie tunsionibus renovandas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041820Z">
        <seg>Nor had the skin, though it covered these ligatures so tightly, closed up so completely that Mariana could not see them when she was treating Rose's torn shoulders: for this kind of service was often required, because the cracks on Rose's back were constantly open, into which, at intervals, Mariana would sprinkle unknown powders by order, so that they would not fester, and cover them with blotting paper, to be renewed by repeated rubbings the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec alio fuste ad abigendum hunc Cerberum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;k&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; opus fuit: nam continuo elumbis, victus, fractus resiliit, fugit, evanuit, &amp; Rosa prorsus illæsam, indemnemque se admirata, corde tranquillo ad orationem rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051942Z">
        <seg>Nor was there any need for another club to drive off this Cerberus: for at once, his loins struck, defeated and broken, he recoiled, fled, and vanished; and Rosa, admiring herself completely unhurt and unharmed, returned to her prayer with a tranquil heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec defuerunt, qui in morientis pallido vultu notarint ipsissimam Christi in cruce exspirantis effigiem, quod alio loco, &amp; tempore, in S. Catharina Senensi, itidem lecto decubua, fuerat observatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043010Z">
        <seg>Nor were there lacking those who recognized in the dying man's pale face the very image of Christ expiring on the cross, which had been similarly observed in St. Catherine of Siena when she was also bedridden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec dubitavit Virgo pacisci cum Deo sub tali onere: malebat enim alienis febribus torreri per omnes medullas, quam quod ex concionis defectu gloria Dei in mutila festivitate detrimentum pateretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053514Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin hesitate to make a pact with God under such a burden: for she preferred to be scorched to the very marrow by others' fevers, rather than have the glory of God suffer detriment in a mutilated celebration because of a lack of participants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec fefellit eventus: nam post Virginis obitum aurifaber, cui injunctum erat aliquot bracteolæ spinas evellere, (forsitan ut devotis personis eas flagitantibus in domum usumve cederent) adhibitis forcipibus, adeo singulas invenit firmas, ut extrahi nulla potuerit, quamquam ad ferri tractum huc illuc inflecterentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042158Z">
        <seg>And the outcome did not disappoint: for after the Virgin's death the goldsmith, who had been instructed to remove several of the little plates from the spikes (perhaps so that they might be given to devout persons who requested them for home or devotional use), using forceps found each one so firmly set that none could be pulled out, although they bent here and there under the pressure of the iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec fefellit eventus: nam sub ejusmodi luteo, nullaque tinctura infecto corporis sui tegumento Rosa tutissime latuit, usque ad vigesimum ætatis annum, quo tandem ad Dominicanæ togæ candorem sublevata, facile perrexit reliquas eludere pomposi luxus insidias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035449Z">
        <seg>And the outcome did not disappoint: for under such a clay-like coating, unstained by any dye, Rosa lay hidden in the covering of her body until she reached the age of twenty, when at last, lifted up to the whiteness of the Dominican habit, she easily went on to evade the remaining snares of pompous luxury.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec longe abfuit præsentanea occasio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060137Z">
        <seg>A present opportunity was not far off.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minor ipsi cura fuit citissime abeundi, ut plausum effugeret, quem merito timebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061513Z">
        <seg>He was no less anxious to depart as quickly as possible, to avoid the applause he justly feared.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minus adstupuit obstetrix, ut vidit hanc infantem secundarum involucris prodire gravem, &amp; quasi penulatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034630Z">
        <seg>Nor was the midwife any less astonished when she saw this infant emerging from the umbilical wrappings heavy and as if encased in a little coat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minus aperte, quotquot sudoris spectaculo coram fruiti erant, fatebantur, se sub illius intuitu novos, acutos, insolitos sensisse divini amoris stimulos, idque erat, quod Virgo tam ardenter &amp; anxie flagitarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053933Z">
        <seg>Nor did those who had enjoyed the spectacle of sweat before him any less openly confess that, under his gaze, they had felt new, sharp, unusual stirrings of divine love—and this was what the Virgin had so ardently and anxiously longed for.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec minus ejusdem ardoris indices ac favillæ erant, ignita illa suspiria, quibus, veluti flabellis, cor illud succensum &amp; æstuans, simul difflabat calores suos, simul eosdem validius succendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053030Z">
        <seg>Nor were the sparks of that same ardor any less numerous: those fiery sighs by which, like fans, that kindled and burning heart both blew away its heat and at the same time fanned it more fiercely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mirum si medicinæ cælestes a centro ad superficiem soleant operari, ac prius animam, quam corpus attingere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055458Z">
        <seg>It is no wonder that heavenly medicines are accustomed to work from the center to the surface, and to touch the soul before the body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mitiores in eam fuere domestici, postquam nimirum innotuit ejus vitæ singularitas, cælestium visorum frequentia &amp; commercium, ac præcipue illa humanis viribus evidenter superior abstinentia, qua facile arguebatur, tam modico ac fere nullo alimento, juvenculam naturaliter in vita sustentari non posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052510Z">
        <seg>Nor were her household any gentler toward her, once it became known how extraordinary her life was—the frequency and intercourse of heavenly visions, and especially that abstinence, clearly beyond human strength, by which it was easily shown that a young woman could not naturally be sustained on so meager, almost nonexistent, a diet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mitiori successu obediebat medicis, si quando aut languentem, aut lentius convalescentem tentabant esu carnium ad vires pristinas revocare: tantum enim aberat isthoc genere alimenti juvari infirmam, ut etiam a bolo, velut a toxico, recidiva magis debilitaretur, morboque redintegrato, non ægra, sed ægritudo vires acciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041042Z">
        <seg>Nor did he obey the physicians with any better success when, at times, they tried to restore a languishing or slowly recovering patient to his former strength by having him eat meat: for it was so far from helping a weak person with this kind of nourishment that even a single dose, like a poison, would further weaken the patient, and with the disease renewed, it was not the patient but the illness that gained strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mora, postridie adornavit thalamum, comportavit in hortum tabulas, intraque pauculos dies perfectum stetit solitariæ devotionis breve, arctum, humile sacrarium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043637Z">
        <seg>Without delay, the next day he adorned the bridal chamber, carried boards into the garden, and within a few days a perfect, brief, narrow, humble shrine of solitary devotion stood complete.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec mora;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052110Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052110Z">
        <seg>Without delay</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec pavori cedens, mandavit transeunti illac ancillulæ, candelam sibi adferri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052035Z">
        <seg>Not yielding to fear, she commanded the little maid passing by to bring her a candle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec perfunctorie hæc dicebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053220Z">
        <seg>Nor did he say these things perfunctorily.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec reliquum anni tempus vacuum est a perpetuo concursu, præsertim quoties aut terræmotus, aut epidemiæ&lt;s8&gt;&lt;a9&gt;f&lt;/a9&gt;&lt;/s8&gt; contagium, aliave, seu publica seu privata necessitas admonet cives præsentanei sui asyli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054307Z">
        <seg>Nor is the rest of the year's time free from a constant influx, especially whenever earthquakes, epidemics, or other contagion, or some other public or private necessity, compels citizens to seek refuge in their present asylum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec sine fructu abiit solicita intercessio: nam dum gyrantem processionem hinc inde pro magnifico applausu, vario fragore excipiunt sulphurata missilium ignium crepitacula, unum istorum recta Franciscæ oculum petiit, inque supercilium impegit, nullo tamen Franciscæ damno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055309Z">
        <seg>Nor did the anxious intercession go without result: for while the circling procession was being greeted here and there with magnificent applause, the sulphurous crackling of fiery missiles met it with varied din; one of these struck Francis squarely in the eye and struck her eyebrow, yet without any harm to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec sinebat Virginem tantæ commiserationis affectus quiescere: unde rationem excogitavit, qua (nisi morte præventa fuisset) sperabat in apostolicæ missionis negotio supplere per alium, quod per semetipsam præstare non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060502Z">
        <seg>Nor did she allow the Virgin to rest in such a compassionate affection; hence she devised a plan by which, had she not been prevented by death, she hoped to supply by another in the work of the apostolic mission what she could not accomplish by herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec solis suis occiduis Indis condolebat, sed quidquid nationum vastissimi Chinarum tractus, aut Orientis imperia continent, Rosæ quotidiana plangendi materies erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060400Z">
        <seg>Nor did she lament only the western suns of the Indians, but whatever nations the vastest tract of China or the empires of the East contain were daily matter for Rose's lament.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec spei eventus defuit: nam e Lima usque Potosium&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, per varios casus, per mille rerum discrimina, iter habuit tutissimum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051843Z">
        <seg>Nor did the outcome disappoint: for from Lima to Potosí, through various incidents and a thousand perils, he had the safest journey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen (quantumvis certa) videri volebat, quod erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050011Z">
        <seg>Yet he did not want to appear to be what he was, however certain it was.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen diu latuit matrem solertia, fartum corripuit, lignea inde retrimenta projici, lanam substitui, lanam ingeri, diserte &amp; cum clamore præcepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042741Z">
        <seg>But the mother's shrewdness did not remain hidden for long; she snatched the stuffing, threw away the wooden bits, had wool substituted, had wool stuffed in, and gave the orders clearly and loudly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen hic jugis, &amp; acerrimus dolor ab ea, vel minimum potuit exprimere gemitum aut molestiæ indicium, nisi quod a matre interrogata de dolore, cum totum negare non posset, modicum ac toleratu facilem esse respondit: sicque in iis tormentis cum sua matre pernoctavit silens, immota, quietissima.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034906Z">
        <seg>Yet this constant, most intense pain from her could not draw even the slightest groan or sign of distress, except that when her mother asked about the pain, since she could not deny it entirely, she replied that it was slight and easily borne. And so she spent the night with her mother in those torments, silent, motionless, and very still.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen in tot culicum legionibus vel unicus fuit, qui Rosam in sua cellula umquam attingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043754Z">
        <seg>Yet not even among so many legions of mosquitoes was there a single one that ever touched Rosa in her cell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen omnino id latere permisit Altissimus, dum pluries in vultu Rosæ visibiliter scintillare fecit interioris flammæ indices, evibrationes atque stricturas, tunc, cum anima sumptione divinæ Hostiæ succendebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055743Z">
        <seg>Yet the Most High did not allow it to remain entirely hidden, for He repeatedly made visible in Rose's face the signs of the inner flame—its vibrations and constrictions—whenever her soul was kindled by the reception of the divine Host.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen plorabat Rosula, sed, proprii pollicis inutili suctu, famem tacita fraudabat magis, quam solabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052438Z">
        <seg>Yet Rosula did not weep, but by the idle sucking of her thumb she more deceived her hunger in silence than she soothed it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec tamen sermone abesse voluit, quia febrilem æstum facile superabat exæstuans divinæ gloriæ zelus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053523Z">
        <seg>Yet he did not wish to be absent from the conversation, for he easily overcame the feverish heat, inflamed by zeal for the glory of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nec verebatur ultro improvida mater, publice coram suis &amp; alienis confundere Filiam, proclamando uti hypocritam, fallacem, simulatricem, omnisque sinceræ virtutis inanem &amp; vacuam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052519Z">
        <seg>Nor did the thoughtless mother hesitate to publicly shame her daughter before friends and strangers, proclaiming her a hypocrite, a deceiver, a pretender, and utterly devoid of any genuine virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negabat illa, se pupam vel habere, vel etiam contrectare velle;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043227Z">
        <seg>She denied that she wanted to have or even touch a doll.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negasses Rosam esse, quæ adeo carebat spinis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040927Z">
        <seg>You would have denied that the rose was, which was so devoid of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit Rosa laborem, vim, connixum adesse a parte sua, ultro ad tractum quasi magneticum potentias sequi, &amp; suo centro obverti cum suavitate, cujus vel unica stilla infiniti Oceani amaritudinem corrigeret, ac dulcoraret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050259Z">
        <seg>Rosa denied that labor, force, or effort was on her part, but that she willingly followed the powers as if by a magnetic attraction, and turned toward her center with a sweetness whose single drop would correct and sweeten the bitterness of an infinite ocean.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051651Z">
        <seg>Rosa denied it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Negavit inops Virgo talium librorum sibi adesse copiam aut usum, in his solam experientiam &amp; praxim sibi fuisse pro codice;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050315Z">
        <seg>The poor Virgin denied having a supply or use of such books, saying that for her, experience and practice alone had served as her code.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neglexit ille, quod scala sibi deesset, qua ad sublimem in ara imaginem pertingeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043528Z">
        <seg>He neglected that he lacked a ladder by which he could reach the lofty image on the altar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe corpusculum ex inedia vacuum sustentabat pinguedo spiritus, cui cælestium meditatio sagina erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041343Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the spirit's fatness sustained the body, which was emptied by fasting, and the meditation of heavenly things was its nourishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe de obedientia Rosæ jam prædefunctæ remanserat tam celebris apud omnes conceptus, tam alta præsumptio, ut crederetur, vel post mortem obeditura superiori, cui numquam in vita subfuerat, videbaturque sat esse ad imperium, quod sua illic hospitabatur imago.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035700Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the reputation of Rose, now deceased, for obedience had become so celebrated among all, the presumption so lofty, that it was believed she would even in death obey a superior to whom she had never been subject in life, and it seemed enough to command the very image that had once dwelt there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe hoc unum adhuc defuerat tantæ suæ calamitatis fastigio, quod insuper cogebatur, velut male sana, ad nauseam catapotia&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, pharmaca, phlebotomias&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; admittere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045757Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this one thing still remained to crown the height of her great calamity, that she was furthermore compelled, like a sick woman, to endure, to the point of nausea, purges, medicines, and bloodlettings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe hyblæam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; Rosam non tangit scarabæus&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044058Z">
        <seg>Surely the scarab does not touch the Hylæan Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nempe sic Rosam exaudierat, qui novit totum hominem sanum facere in Sabbatho.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060815Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this was how he had heard Rosam, who knows how to make a whole person well on the Sabbath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hic stetit Virginis beneficium: simul cum vitio inveteratum asthma pectoris, hæmorrhagiam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, flatuumque tormina depulit, quibuscum miser ille per annos plus minus quatuordecim fuerat colluctatus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060809Z">
        <seg>Nor did the Virgin's benefit stop there: at the same time, it drove out a chronic asthma of the chest, hemorrhage, and colic, with which that poor man had been struggling for more than fourteen years.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque his contenta, præter frequentes in hebdomada exomologeses, insuper quotidianam sibi coram S. Patre suo Dominico indixerat confessionem, qua, velut in Capitulo correctionis, eidem culpas suas minutim exponebat, veniamque &amp; remedium ab ejus suffragio humiliter postulabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040655Z">
        <seg>Not content with these, besides the frequent weekly confessions, she had also imposed upon herself a daily confession before her holy Father Dominic, in which, as if in a chapter of correction, she minutely laid open her faults to him and humbly asked for pardon and a remedy from his counsel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque his facile accessus poterat denegari, quantumcumque se Rosa absconderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043454Z">
        <seg>Nor could access to these be easily denied, however much Rosa hid herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hoc satis: ipsemet parvus Jesulus, digiti statura vix procerior, sæpe apparebat amanti Rosæ in eadem pagellæ planitie: stabat pusillus ac nudus Amor, mox inambulabat passu levissimo, ac interim obtutu serenulo, tenero, suavi adblandiebatur Virgini, ingerebat se, utpote &lt;b2&gt;Verbum&lt;/b2&gt; attenta Rosæ lectione dignissimum, in quo omnes thesauri scientiæ &amp; sapientiæ Dei.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050910Z">
        <seg>Nor was this enough: the little Jesus himself, scarcely taller than a finger, often appeared to the beloved Rose on the same flat page: Standing there was little naked Love, who would soon be pacing in the lightest of steps, and meanwhile with a serene, tender, sweet gaze he would caress the Virgin, pressing himself upon her, for he was the Word, most worthy of Rose's attentive reading, in whom are all the treasures of the knowledge and wisdom of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hujus incomparabilis viri admiranda sanctitas, pastoralis zelus, numerosa miracula ignorantur Romæ in Sacro Rituum Senatu, ubi de illius apotheosi pridem ac strenue agitur&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203459Z">
        <seg>Nor are the admirable sanctity, pastoral zeal, and numerous miracles of this incomparable man unknown in Rome at the Sacred Congregation of Rites, where his apotheosis has long been vigorously debated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque hujus incomparabilis viri admiranda sanctitas, pastoralis zelus, numerosa miracula ignorantur Romæ in Sacro Rituum Senatu, ubi de illius apotheosi pridem ac strenue agitur&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040025Z">
        <seg>Nor are the admirable sanctity, pastoral zeal, and numerous miracles of this incomparable man unknown in Rome at the Sacred Congregation of Rites, where his apotheosis has long been vigorously debated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque id satis: ipsa simul arbusta flectebant ramos in terram, velut osculatura solum, in Conditoris sui reverentiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045152Z">
        <seg>Nor was that enough: the shrubs themselves bent their branches to the ground, as if to kiss the soil in reverence to their Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque jam de argento, sed de fama cruciabatur solicitudo: nam periculum erat, nequa innocens temere in furti suspicionem veniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035647Z">
        <seg>He was now troubled not by money but by his reputation: for there was a danger that some innocent person might rashly be suspected of theft.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Neque spem matrisfamilias fefellit, cui nimirum paulo post a pulcherrimo hoc gallo pulcherrimi fuere pulli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061559Z">
        <seg>Nor did it disappoint the housewife, for shortly thereafter this most handsome rooster produced the most beautiful chicks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus &amp; tunc sævissime urebat Sponsi morosa absentia, affligebat durissimi exilii cruda relegatio, contristabat amarissime viduitatis longum divortium, &amp; quod reliquam superabat molestiam, tunc quoque conabatur &amp; nosse, &amp; amare, sed neutrum poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045624Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, even then he was most fiercely consumed by the bridegroom's morose absence, afflicted by the harsh exile of a cruel banishment, most bitterly saddened by the long divorce of widowhood, and what surpassed all the rest of his trouble was that even then he strove to know and to love, but could do neither.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Rosa, quod futurum erat, spe bona percipiens, aiebat: “Potens est Dominus concedere, quod optamus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055138Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rosa, perceiving with good hope what was to come, said, "The Lord is able to grant what we desire."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Rosa, ut agnovit sic placitum esse ante Deum, virili planeque heroïco spiritu amplexa tormentum, “Non mea (inquit) voluntas fiat, sed tua.” Sicque constanter ad secuturæ diei similem equuleum sese præparabat, numquam secura, in quam horam is pavidam expectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045648Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rosa, recognizing that it was God's will, embraced the torment with a truly manly and heroic spirit, saying, "Not my will be done, but Yours." And so she steadfastly prepared herself for a similar rack the next day, never certain at what hour it would await her in fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Rosæ tantus inerat castigandi corporis ardor ac studium, ut ei per confessarios modus fuerit imponendus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041507Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rose was so zealous and eager to punish her body that a limit had to be set for her by her confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus Virgo &amp; librum suum recuperavit, &amp; pudefecit reduncum depeculatorem, quem non alio, quam improbæ felis, aut SCABIOSI nomine dignabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052010Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Virgo recovered her book and shamed the backstabbing thief, whom she dignified with no other name than that of a wicked cat or a SCABIOTIC.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus remotiora hæc erant, quam ut tanti thalami spem certam invergerent, &amp; tamen (ut postea claruit) eminus ista felici sponsalitio præludebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044132Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, these things were too remote to give any certain hope of such a marriage, and yet (as later became clear) they were, from afar, a prelude to that happy betrothal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nihilominus ubi semel iterumve, sub gravi onere conscientiæ, tum a confessariis, tum a doctore Joanne de Castillo (ut infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040841Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, whenever, under the heavy burden of conscience, he did so—whether at the hands of confessors or of Doctor John de Castillo (as below in the chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum conceperat animo, adoptare sibi aliunde pupillum inopem, &amp; undique desolatum, quem a primis unguiculis ipsa materno studio ad virtutem &amp; litteras educaret ex piis familiarium sibi matronarum eleëmosynis: huic puero mox a teneris proposuerat firmiter ac sensim implantare avida euangelicæ missionis desideria, cum parvulo gradatim adultura, ac intetim providere, ut in pietate sacrisque disciplinis accuratissime formaretur, donec grandior factus ac sacerdotio rite initiatus jam censeri posset idoneus operarius ad laborandum in conversione gentilium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060505Z">
        <seg>She had indeed conceived in her mind to adopt from elsewhere an impoverished and utterly destitute ward, whom she would raise from his very infancy with a mother's care to virtue and learning, from the pious alms of the matrons of her family: For this boy, she had soon, from his tender years, firmly and gradually proposed to implant eager desires for the evangelical mission, as he would grow little by little, and to provide in advance that he be formed most carefully in piety and sacred disciplines, until, having become older and duly initiated into the priesthood, he could be considered a suitable worker to labor in the conversion of the Gentiles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum obedivit matri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035312Z">
        <seg>He certainly obeyed his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum propria hæc&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;*&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt; est superno amori hydrops, ut solum id sitiat, quo magis abundat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053052Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this is the very thirst of the higher love, that it thirsts only for that by which it abounds the more.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum sic nascuntur Rosæ, congenitis integumentis undique velut amictu paludatæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034633Z">
        <seg>Indeed, this is how roses are born, their innate coverings cloaking them on all sides like a mantle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum simillimo ritu olim S. Catharina Senensis excepta fuerat in cælis, cum reliquit terras, ut sic in magistram ac Discipulam a spinea usque ad auream coronam nulla superesset difformitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042434Z">
        <seg>Indeed, in a very similar manner, St. Catherine of Siena was once received into heaven when she left the earth, so that as both teacher and disciple there was no flaw from the thorn to the golden crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum tanta ei inerat cura veritatis, quia Deus est veritas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053353Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he had such great concern for the truth, because God is truth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum tanto viatico illam hinc abeuntem ditarat profusa misericordis Rosæ liberalitas, cui nil tam pretiosum aut charum esse potuit, quod animarum saluti non impenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060859Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the generous charity of Rose had enriched her with such a provision for her departure from here, for to her nothing could be more precious or dear than what she would not expend for the salvation of souls.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nimirum, sic exolvit promissa fidejussor Deus, qui non minus parentis cor, quam coralium Filiæ gestabat in manu sua.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043618Z">
        <seg>Surely, thus the God who stood as surety fulfilled His promises, holding in His hand not only a parent's heart but also His Daughter's heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nobis sufficit ex his epithetis distinctius morbum illum nosse, cujus causæ &amp; remedia apud ipsum legi possunt.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045937Z">
        <seg>It is sufficient for us, from these epithets, to know that disease more distinctly, the causes and remedies of which can be read in the original.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nocte concubia Rosam in strato acutissimus coxendicum dolor invaserat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041708Z">
        <seg>During the night, Rosam was seized by the sharpest pain in her hip while lying in bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nocte quadam Rosæ dormiendum fuit in uno conclavi cum socia virgine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053015Z">
        <seg>One night, Rose had to sleep in a room with a virgin companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nolint errare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052823Z">
        <seg>They do not want to be wrong.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nomen habet a parte læsa: os enim, quod femoris caput excipit &lt;s0&gt;ἰσχίον&lt;/s0&gt;, id est, coxa appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041414Z">
        <seg>It takes its name from the injured part: for the mouth, which receives the head of the femur, is called the ischion, that is, the hip.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non defuit, qui nugas, aut sane dæmonum præstigias, &amp; vana terriculamenta suspicaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045710Z">
        <seg>There was no shortage of those who suspected nonsense, or indeed the prestidigitations of demons, and vain terrors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non negavit Rosa, id verum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051853Z">
        <seg>Rosa did not deny that it was true.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non probabatur matri sumptuosa Filiæ devotio, ægreque ferebat tantum temporis subduci aliis Rosæ laboribus, qui parentes, &amp; inopem familiam sustentabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060121Z">
        <seg>The mother did not approve of her daughter's devotion to luxury, and she resented so much time being taken away from Rose's other labors, which supported her parents and her needy family.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non puduit fateri ista coram matre, quæ quam vera diceret, nimis conscia erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050247Z">
        <seg>He was not ashamed to confess those things in front of his mother, who was all too aware of how true they were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non quidem ad hoc tibi religiosus iste candicat amictus, ut eum alienis spurcitiis devenustes, neque te Rosam appellavimus, ut sic oleres: quando te murinis ac suaveolentibus chirothecis uti jubebam, repugnasti parvula: at nunc adulta, si musco fragrare non vis, muco saltem ne fœteas alieno”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061314Z">
        <seg>"I'm not at all asking you to don that pious, snow-white robe so you can soil it with other people's filth, nor did I call you Rosa so you'd smell like a cabbage. When I told you to use mousemeat and fragrant gloves, you little one, you resisted; but now that you're grown, if you don't want to smell of musk, at least don't stink of someone else's sweat."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non recusavit Castillus edicere, quæ sentiebat: “Quando (inquit) in illa obtenebratione videbaris tibi exitum finemque sperare posse, scito animarum in carcere purgatorii lugentium, dilationes pœnosissimas te delibasse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050401Z">
        <seg>Castille did not hesitate to declare what he felt: "When," he said, "in that darkness you thought you could hope for an outcome and an end, know that you have tasted the most grievous delays of the souls wailing in the prison of purgatory."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Non satiantur sic amantes orare jugiter: “Flammescat igne charitas, accendat ardor proximos”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053936Z">
        <seg>Those who love this way never grow weary of praying continually: "May charity blaze with fire, may ardor kindle those around."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum Rosa (fixis interim in cælum oculis) verba finierat, cum ferox ac torosum animal, terrifico mugitu instar turbinis prætervectum, procul in alias personas impetum fecit, velut si Rosam ejusve imbellem comitatum ne vidisset quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062126Z">
        <seg>Rosa had not yet finished speaking (her eyes fixed on the sky) when a fierce, muscular beast, rushing past with a terrifying roar like a whirlwind, charged off at a distance at other people, as if it had not even seen Rosa or her unarmed companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum Rosa loqui desierat, cum Gundisalvi uxori in memoriam recurrerunt ignita alloquia, quibus Virgo imaginem affata fuerat ante sudorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053926Z">
        <seg>Rosa had not yet ceased speaking, when the fiery words with which the Virgin had addressed the image before the sweat came back to Gundisalvi's wife.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum Rosa quadrimula erat, cum mordax suppuratio in ejus auricula succrevit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034848Z">
        <seg>Rosa was not yet four months old when a painful abscess developed in her ear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nondum quartumdecimum annum egressa fuerat, cum notari cœpit inter alia pœnitentiæ opera, quod noctu perambulabat hortum nudipes, imposita sibi oblonga cruce, quam dum lividis gestabat humeris, semetipsam crebris genuflexionibus terræ allidebat, amarissimam Calvariæ viam æquis tum passibus, tum suspiriis dimetiens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041643Z">
        <seg>She had not yet turned fourteen when she began to be noted, among other penitential works, for walking barefoot at night through the garden, a long cross laid upon her, which, as she carried it on her livid shoulders, she brought herself to the ground with frequent genuflections, measuring the most bitter Way of Calvary with equal steps and sighs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nonnulla attigimus supra;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054320Z">
        <seg>We have touched on some things above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nonnumquam de confessariorum, ipsiusque Gundisalvi permissu, maternam adibat domum, ut pristinæ cellulæ suavi eremo per aliquot dies frueretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043947Z">
        <seg>Sometimes, with the permission of her confessors and of Gundisalvus himself, she would go to her mother's house to enjoy the sweet solitude of her former cell for a few days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nonum ætatis mensem attigerat, cum lactanti matri aruerunt ubera, lac defecit, &amp; ne aliunde nutrix conduceretur, obstabat res angusta domi: en primos infanti tribulos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052429Z">
        <seg>He had reached the ninth month of his life when his nursing mother's breasts failed, her milk ran out, and, since there was no room at home to hire a wet nurse, the infant's first hardships began.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nosti, quid fellis ibidem propter tui amorem exhauserim: scis, quæ illic non testeæ, sed ferreæ cuspides mihi manus pedesque terebrarint, donec ipsam mihi extunderent dilectam animam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042654Z">
        <seg>You know what torments I endured there for the sake of your love; you know what iron spikes, not wooden, pierced my hands and feet until they had torn my beloved soul from me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notarunt hunc Rosæ spiritum domestici, &amp; quotiescumque roganda erat, ut fomenta suo corpusculo admitteret, aut requiem captaret, aliave se ratione sineret indulgentius haberi, subjiciebant ita mandasse confessarium, statimque Rosa sine replica plane infantiliter obediebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035718Z">
        <seg>They noted this spirit of domestic obedience in Rosa, and whenever she was asked to apply fomentations to her little body, or to take rest, or to allow herself in any other way to be treated more indulgently, they would say that her confessor had so commanded, and at once Rosa, without a word, obeyed in a thoroughly childlike manner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notarunt precantis vultum circumstantes ejusdem tertii habitus Sorores, videruntque Rosam a sacræ imaginis obtutu supplici diu pendulam, initio quidem exalbescere pallore niveo, post intervallum decora suffusione rubescere, denique tota facie lucescere, ac scintillatim radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040354Z">
        <seg>The Sisters of the Third Order, who were standing by, noticed the supplicant's face and saw Rosa hanging for a long time before the sacred image with a devout gaze; at first she turned pale with a snowy pallor, then after a while she flushed with a lovely blush, and finally her whole face shone and sparkled with rays.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notavi adstantes animas eodem gratiæ thesauro tam distentas, &amp; ad summum usque refertas, ut ore &amp; oculis exundaret arctata plenitudo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052813Z">
        <seg>I noticed the souls of those present so distended by the same treasure of grace, and so filled to the brim, that their mouths and eyes overflowed with its restrained fullness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Notent, quicumque religiosis communitatibus divisionis machinas invergunt, quam abominabilem Deo, quam gratam cacodæmoni, quam perniciosam proximis operam præstent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054500Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054500Z">
        <seg>Let those who sow discord among religious communities know how abominable their work is to God, how pleasing to the devil, and how harmful to their neighbors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Noti ignotique pone transibant coram geniculata Virgine, ac fere incurrebant in oculos, nec tamen ipsa quemquam tunc norat, nec vel leviter movebat palpebras, intensissima iis, quæ gerebantur in altari, quorum intuitum forsitan alieni objecti intercursus nec obiter eclipsabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060028Z">
        <seg>Known and unknown alike passed by her, kneeling before the Virgin, and almost ran into her gaze; yet she herself recognized no one at that moment, nor did she so much as bat her eyelids, so intent was she on what was taking place at the altar, whose sight perhaps no intervening object even briefly eclipsed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Noverat æstimare animas ex infinito pretio, quo redemptæ sunt: hinc, quoties ad interiora meridionalis Americæ montana flectebat oculos, cruciabatur medullitus, illachrymans perditioni tot barbarorum, qui, post illa nivosa altissimarum costarum&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt; juga, formicatim innumeras, sed inaccessas convalles populabant, avita adhuc idolomania obcæcati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060350Z">
        <seg>He knew to value souls by the infinite price for which they were redeemed: Hence, whenever he turned his eyes to the interior mountainous regions of South America, he was deeply tormented, weeping over the perdition of so many barbarians who, beyond those snowy summits of the highest ridges, swarmed like ants through countless but inaccessible valleys, still blinded by ancestral idolatry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Novercali corporis odio, nec vigil insidere usquam pulvino voluit, sed vel stans peragebat labores suos quousque poterat, vel cum necessario sedendum erat, duro palo utebatur pro sellula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042930Z">
        <seg>Hating the very body he loathed, he would not sit on a cushion anywhere, but either carried out his labors standing as long as he could, or, when he had to sit, used a hard staff instead of a stool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nulla ægritudo tam putrida ac fœda, nullum ulcus tam tetrum aut saniosum, nulla scabies tam crustosa aut fluida, cui Rosa medelam, famulatum, curam offerre, cui virgineas admovere manus, dimovere spurcitiem, applicare, mundare, mutare emplastra&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt; defugeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061256Z">
        <seg>No sickness so putrid and foul, no sore so dark or festering, no scab so crusted or oozing, to which Rosa would offer a remedy, her service, her care; to which she would press her virgin hands, remove the filth, apply, cleanse, and change the poultice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nullus apparebat negotii tam ardui procurator, nemo fundator, nemo fautor aut suasor, nullibi auspex vel patronus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062746Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062746Z">
        <seg>There appeared to be no manager for such a difficult undertaking, no founder, no supporter or persuader, nowhere an augur or patron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Numquam sane officiosissima Rosæ pietas aut solicitius, aut lautius refecit matrem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035836Z">
        <seg>Never, indeed, did Rose's most dutiful piety care for her mother more solicitously or more sumptuously.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc (sed brevius) ad alterum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050116Z">
        <seg>Now (but more briefly) to the other.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nunc est episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Limensi, a cujus metropoli 120 leucis Hispanicis in ortum distat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050758Z">
        <seg>It is now a diocese under the Archbishop of Lima, from whose metropolis it lies 120 Spanish leagues to the east.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nuntiatum fuit Rosæ, vacua esse penuaria, neque vel crustam reliquam esse ab ultima pistura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062207Z">
        <seg>It was reported to Rosa that the larders were empty, and that not even a crust remained from the last baking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Nusquam is sibi occurrebat pauperior, quam ubi in Bethlehem nascebatur parvulus, nudus, cum inopi Matre extorris diversorio, algens in stabulo, panniculis obvolutus in vili præsepio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053536Z">
        <seg>Nowhere was he poorer than when he was born in Bethlehem as a little child, naked, with his destitute mother driven out of the inn, freezing in a stable, wrapped in swaddling cloths in a humble manger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O gratum surgendi tintinnabulum!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051344Z">
        <seg>Oh, welcome alarm clock!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O quis det, ut cuncti agnoscant, quanti quantoque amore dignus sis, ut capiant, non pœnarum servili formidine, non mera spe mercenaria præmiorum, sed propter te amandum te esse?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053712Z">
        <seg>Oh, who would grant that all might recognize how worthy you are of love, and embrace you not for fear of servile punishments, nor for the mere hope of mercenary rewards, but for the sake of loving you—because you are you?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>O quis donet, ut eum perfecte diligamus!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053145Z">
        <seg>Oh, who will grant that we may love him perfectly!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obedivit Rosa, sed non ultra litteram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042744Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042744Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeyed, but not beyond the letter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obedivi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035526Z">
        <seg>I obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obiter hic attigerim, quod in Rosa, dum vixit, plurimi observarunt, mirifice oblectatam fuisse aspectu sereni cæli, adeo ut puella omnis astrologiæ penitus ignara, magnam interdum noctis partem immota soli fiderum spectaculo dulciter inhæreret: nimirum nova Regum stella amabat sphæram suam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043711Z">
        <seg>By the way, I should mention here that, as many who knew Rosa during her life observed, she was wonderfully delighted by the sight of a clear sky, so much so that the girl, utterly ignorant of all astrology, would sometimes spend a great part of the night motionless, sweetly absorbed in the spectacle of the fixed stars: for the new Star of the Magi loved her sphere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Oblata ei cum anulo fuit corona spinis horrida, vixque his manus applicuerat, cum repente æstuare cœpit inusitato quodam divini amoris incendio, colliquescere in intimo fundo spiritus, dulcescere gustu admirabili supernarum deliciarum, tamquam ex illismet spinis paradisi uvas, ex tribulis ficus collegisset in animæ palatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042353Z">
        <seg>A crown of horrid thorns was offered to him along with the ring, and he had scarcely touched it with his hands when suddenly he began to blaze with an unusual fire of divine love, to melt in the inmost depths of his spirit, to grow sweet with the wondrous taste of heavenly delights, as if he had gathered grapes from the very thorns of paradise and figs from thistles for the palate of his soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obmutuit, stupuitque repentino gaudio Rosa, ut vidit, non deliberatione, sed impetu, raptim incidisse fratrem in symbolum, quod unica ac propria totius negotii erat periocha;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044455Z">
        <seg>Rose was dumbfounded and stunned by a sudden joy when she saw that her brother had, not by deliberation but by impulse, rashly plunged into the symbol, which was the very crux of the entire affair.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obscurum ænigma perfacile capiebant, quotquot prævii conflictus consciæ fuerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040403Z">
        <seg>They grasped the obscure enigma with great ease, as many as had been aware of the previous conflicts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obsecundavit ille, collegitque pluscula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044932Z">
        <seg>He complied, and gathered a few things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observarunt domestici, numquam in fasciis parvulam voce querula, aut stridulis vagitibus ædium turbasse silentium, numquam plorasse, nisi quod semel, cum mater nobilem matronam visitans foras extulisset Rosam, compesci a planctu non potuit, quoadusque ex aliena in propriam domum referretur, quasi jam tunc nosset publicum fugere, &amp; aversari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034820Z">
        <seg>The household observed that the little one never cried in her swaddling clothes, or with shrill cries had ever disturbed the silence of the house, had never cried, except that once, when her mother had taken Rosa outside while visiting a noble matron, she could not be restrained from crying until she was brought back from the other house to her own, as if even then she knew to shun and avoid public company.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observat hæc tacite Maria de Usategui, prudentique solertia demum a Virgine per ambages elicuit, quid rerum illic lateret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054122Z">
        <seg>Maria of Usategui observed these things in silence and, with prudent skill, finally elicited from the Virgin, by indirection, what was hidden there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observatum fuit, Virginem cum pane unico, illoque sat exiguo, &amp; aquæ vasculo, integram dierum quinquagenam transegisse absque omni alio cibo aut potu, nimirum a Paschate Resurrectionis usque ad Pentecosten.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041333Z">
        <seg>It was observed that the Virgin, with a single loaf of bread, and that a very small one, and a small vessel of water, spent the entire fifty days without any other food or drink, namely from the Feast of the Resurrection until Pentecost.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Observatum id crebro, inque diversissimis materiis, quarum vix ulla effugiebat hanc spiritualem &amp; sanctam æquivocandi solertiam, ne quid Rosæ ori excideret, quod non esset oratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045013Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045013Z">
        <seg>This was often observed, and in the most diverse matters, so that scarcely any of them escaped this spiritual and holy skill of equivocation, so that nothing might escape the Rose's mouth that was not a prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstabant demum alia, providente Numine, quod florido S. Patris Dominici viridario hanc Rosam incomparabilem per tot ambages reservabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040050Z">
        <seg>At last, other obstacles arose, by the providence of God, who was reserving this incomparable Rose for the flourishing garden of Saint Dominic through so many twists and turns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstabat simul ætate morbisque fatiscens avia, cui decumbenti unica a fomentis erat, assiduoque ministerio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040047Z">
        <seg>Also standing in the way was the grandmother, who, worn out by age and illness, had as her only support a bed and constant care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstabat, quod Rosæ pro pudore virgineo durum &amp; insolitum erat, viri, quamquam familiarissimi &amp; infirmi, subire conspectum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061448Z">
        <seg>What prevented Rose, out of virgin modesty, from submitting to the sight of the man, though he was the most familiar and infirm, was that it was harsh and unusual.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstitit tamen, causata domus inopiam, quam Rosa manuum labore sustentabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040044Z">
        <seg>However, she objected, citing the household's poverty, which Rosa supported by her labor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuere omnes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034653Z">
        <seg>Everyone was stunned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuit parens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051722Z">
        <seg>The parent was stunned.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuit præ aliis Joannes Perez de Zumeta (id erat chirurgo nomen) haud capiens, unde tantillæ Virgunculæ tanta in iis tormentis obduruisset constantia: nam toto illo acerbissimæ curationis tempore nec gemitum ediderat, nec umquam expalluerat ad styli, sanguinis, novorumque vulnerum aspectum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034845Z">
        <seg>John Perez de Zumeta, the surgeon, was more astonished than the others, unable to understand how so slight a maiden could have endured such steadfastness in those torments; for throughout that entire period of the most bitter treatment she had neither uttered a groan nor ever paled at the sight of the stylet, the blood, or the fresh wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obstupuit socia, præsertim quod videret, id Rosæ peregrinum, aut novum non esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045202Z">
        <seg>The companion was astonished, especially because she saw that what Rosæ was seeing was neither strange nor new.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Obtemperavit Virgo, quia parentes jubebant, imo ut Gundisalvo &amp; uxori in omnibus morigeram se exhiberet, imperabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035616Z">
        <seg>The virgin complied, because her parents were ordering her—indeed, commanding her to be obedient in all things to Gundisalvo and his wife.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Occasionem dederat popularis quædam in Limana civitate turbatio, levibus (ut assolet) orta principiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041535Z">
        <seg>A popular disturbance had broken out in the city of Lima, arising from trivial (as is usual) beginnings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Occultissimum hoc erat tormenti genus, quodque vel diu vel forte semper latuisset universos: nam Rosa, vel ipsam operum suorum secretariam Marianam, hujus insciam voluit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041659Z">
        <seg>This was the most secret kind of torture, which, whether for a long time or perhaps always, had remained hidden from everyone: for Rosa, or even her own secretary Mariana, wished her to be unaware of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Opinor hic voce&lt;/e9&gt; hieratica &lt;e10&gt;artem sacram contemplationis indicari&lt;/e10&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050820Z">
        <seg>I think that here the term "hieratic" indicates the sacred art of contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Optabat numquam fieri noctem, ne sibi lectulus adeundus esset cum tali manuum obvolucro.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035340Z">
        <seg>She wished that night would never come, so that she wouldn't have to go to bed with such a bandage on her hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Optabat visceratim discerpi, ac retis instar obpandi latissimæ viæ, qua tot miserabiles animæ ruebant ad infernum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060403Z">
        <seg>He longed to be torn to pieces and spread out like a net across the broadest road, down which so many wretched souls were rushing to hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Orabat Virgo, &amp; in mentis excessu vidit circa se undique solum conspersum rosis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061854Z">
        <seg>The Virgin was praying, and in a trance of mind she saw all around her the ground strewn with roses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Os homini intus omnino fuligineum, camini speciem referebat, olidæ nares mucebant cremore favillaceo, atta nebula vexatum cerebrum stillabat in pectus, &amp; obstructis pulmonibus raucum asthma vocalem obsidebat arteriam, nec tamen cessabat miser a noxio suctu accensæ fistulæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060747Z">
        <seg>The man's mouth inside was utterly sooty, like a chimney; his foul nostrils were caked with a sooty cream; a fog-vexed brain dripped onto his chest; and with his lungs blocked, a hoarse asthma besieged his windpipe, yet the wretch did not cease from the noxious draught of the kindled pipe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Oviedus in Hispaniola insula PETEBECENUC.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060939Z">
        <seg>Oviedo on the island of Hispaniola, PETEBECENUC.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211727Z">
        <seg>Father</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. F. Leonardo Hansen Ordinis Prædicatorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000825Z">
        <seg>Father Francis Leonard Hansen of the Order of Preachers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>P. Joannes de Villalobos&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, Rector Limensis collegii Societatis Jesu, Vir sanctimonia ac religione celeberrimus, &amp; animarum per tutiora compendia perfectionis deducendarum auriga peritissimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001017Z">
        <seg>Father John de Villalobos, Rector of the Society of Jesus's college in Lima, a man celebrated for his holiness and piety, and a most skilled guide for leading souls to perfection by safer means.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Palpitabant cæteris anhela præcordia etiam post periculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062130Z">
        <seg>The other's panting hearts still fluttered even after the danger;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pande guttur cantillando:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045258Z">
        <seg>Open your throat by singing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pande rostrum,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045255Z">
        <seg>Open your beak,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pangat hymnum vox anhela,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045236Z">
        <seg>Let a breathless voice sing a hymn,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Panniculi constanto 9 stationibus ad SS. Eucharistiam, novem trientibus psalterii rosariani, &amp; 9 diebus jejunis, pro numero mensium, quos in utero purissimæ Matris exegit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053554Z">
        <seg>The devotion consists of 9 stations for the Holy Eucharist, nine decades of the rosary, and 9 days of fasting, corresponding to the number of months the Most Pure Mother carried Him in her womb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parce manui ad ista jam inutili;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055414Z">
        <seg>Spare the hand that is now useless for these things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parebat mœsta Filia, sed maximo inassueti stomachi supplicio: nam quidquid carnium reluctanti ingesserat, paulo post, captato secretiori angulo, totum superne reddendum erat, non sine miserabili cruciatu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041036Z">
        <seg>The daughter appeared sorrowful, but suffering the greatest punishment of an unsettled stomach: for whatever meat she had forced down with reluctance, she would shortly afterward, having found a more secluded corner, have to bring it all back up, not without miserable torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari commiseratione vernaculas, peregrinas, serviles, ingenuas, notas, ignotas, agrestes, &amp; indigenas prosequebatur, solo evidentioris indigentiæ respectu habito.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061249Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061249Z">
        <seg>He treated vernacular, foreign, servile, free, familiar, unfamiliar, rustic, and indigenous people with equal compassion, regarding only the more obvious need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari obedientia suo confessario adquievit, quando nimiam ferreæ catenæ atrocitatem in aliud, ex filis contortum, at nodosum asperumque flagellum præcepit commutari, ut sui Ordinis communiori usui humilius sese conformaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041612Z">
        <seg>With equal obedience he acquiesced to his confessor, when he ordered the excessive cruelty of the iron chain to be exchanged for another, twisted from strands into a knotted and rough scourge, so that he might conform himself more humbly to the common practice of his Order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari solicitudine pios libros, quotquot de oratione peritius tractare noverat, &amp; legebat ipsa, &amp; legendos aliis facunda efficacitate consulebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045109Z">
        <seg>With equal diligence she read the devout books she knew how to handle most skillfully on the subject of prayer, and she also advised others to read them with eloquent effectiveness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pari sumptu connitebatur agonizantibus impetrare subsidia pro illo tanti momenti supremo momento, a quo pendet æternitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041522Z">
        <seg>At great expense, he strove to secure for the agonizing the means for that supreme moment of such momentous importance, from which eternity depends.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit Rosa, sed eo ingenio, quo nihilominus obedientiæ ac pœnitentiæ merita in illomet lecto materno dexterrime conjungeret: vix enim obrepserat matri sopor, cum Virgo culcitræ partem, cui incubuerat, sensim removebat, donec substratæ tabulæ corpusculum exciperent, capiti vero lignum, aut prope absconditus later cervicali subducto succederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042514Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeyed, but with that ingenuity by which she nonetheless most skillfully combined the merits of obedience and penance on that very motherly bed: For scarcely had slumber overtaken her mother when the Virgin slowly withdrew the part of the mattress on which she had been lying, until the planks beneath received her little body, and the wood of the headboard, or one hidden nearby, took the place of the removed side rail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035533Z">
        <seg>Rosa obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit Virgo, ad obsequia charitatis mire proclivis, sacellum adiit, preces fudit, sed mœsta præter consuetudinem, animoque saucia domum rediit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054422Z">
        <seg>The Virgin, wonderfully inclined to acts of charity, went to the chapel, offered prayers, but returned home sadder than usual and wounded in spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit humilis Rosa, cui metus &amp; pudor totum verecundo rubore vultum accenderat, veniamque præfata, si quod paradoxum rudi, &amp; incautæ excideret, sic orsa est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050504Z">
        <seg>The humble Rosa, whose face fear and shame had all aflame with bashful blush, and having first asked pardon for any rude or careless paradox that might slip from her, thus began:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paruit illa, catenam denuo carni adnatam a lumbis revulsit, pannoque diligenter una cum sera obvolutam sacristæ B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041755Z">
        <seg>She complied, tore the chain, which had once again grown into the flesh, from her loins, and carefully wrapped it, together with the key, in a cloth for the sacrist of B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Parum ei videbatur, quod domi, Filiam se oblita, ancillæ domesticæ vilissima quæque ministeria occupabat, nisi &amp; infra ancillam sese demitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040503Z">
        <seg>It seemed to her that it was not enough that at home, having forgotten her daughter, she occupied herself with the vilest household tasks, unless she also humbled herself below a slave.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pater F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060821Z">
        <seg>Father F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Patuit Joanna, &amp; insperatum beneficium verecunda hilaritate acceptavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061215Z">
        <seg>Joanna opened up and accepted the unexpected favor with modest cheerfulness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Patuit hoc vice quadam, cum Rosa, fortuito in terram lapsu caput leviter ad vicinum pluteum impegit, ubi confestim ob coronam vertici validius impactam, copiosus sanguis defluxit in humerum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042335Z">
        <seg>It happened once when Rosa, by chance falling to the ground, lightly bumped her head on a nearby shelf, where, because the crown had been struck more forcefully, a copious flow of blood ran down onto her shoulder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucas hic e plurimis compendio substringemus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040459Z">
        <seg>Here we will briefly summarize a few of the many.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucis accipe hujus rei notabile argumentum, quod in Rosæ funere observatum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042406Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042406Z">
        <seg>Here is a notable argument for this matter, observed at Rose's funeral.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paucis ante diebus (nempe feria secunda Paschatis) Rosa, lapsu fortuito terræ allisa, brachium graviter offenderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053942Z">
        <seg>A few days before (namely, the second day of Easter), Rosa, having accidentally fallen to the ground, had severely injured her arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Paulo aliter id experta est soror Francisca de Montoya, itidem S. Patris Dominici alumna Tertiaria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043919Z">
        <seg>Sister Francisca de Montoya experienced it somewhat differently; she too was a tertiary alumna of Saint Dominic the Father.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Peccatrix sum, equidem fateor, &amp; ut fatear, hic provolvor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062015Z">
        <seg>I am a sinner, I confess, and to confess, I cast myself down here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Penetrarunt medullas Rosæ hæc verba, &amp;, velut ictu improviso, exanimis in se recidit, brevique lucta confusorum affectuum, simul &amp; dehiscebat tremula in abyssum sui nihili, &amp; natabat sublimis tumore lætitiæ, nec tamen succumbens tantæ dignationis miraculo animus inveniebat, quid responderet, ni succurrissent verba humilitatis purissimæ: “Ecce ancilla Domini.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044346Z">
        <seg>These words penetrated to the marrow of Rose, &amp;, as if struck by a sudden blow, she fell lifeless within herself, and in a brief struggle of confused emotions, she was at once torn apart by a trembling plunge into the abyss of her own nothingness, and she swam, exalted by a swelling of joy; yet her spirit, not yielding to the miracle of such exaltation, could find no answer, save for the words of the purest humility: "Behold the handmaid of the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perculsi superno spectaculo idololatræ, pacem armorum experimento prætulerunt, &amp; obstinatione una cum ferro excussa, certatim jugo fidei cum gaudio succollarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054242Z">
        <seg>Struck by the divine spectacle of the idolatry, they preferred the peace of arms by experience, and having cast off obstinacy along with iron, they joyfully submitted to the yoke of faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perfecit ille, quod mandaveram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051747Z">
        <seg>He did what I had commanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pergebat alacris Virgo, nec aliud replicuit, nisi quod idem ille, qui sibi manus dederat, quas vestiendæ sacræ imagini impenderet, tunc dextram sanarat, qua eidem devestiendæ famularetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055420Z">
        <seg>The Virgin went on eagerly, and said nothing else, except that the same man who had helped her dress the sacred image then healed the right hand with which he would serve in its undressing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pergebat avicula modulari, variabat lepidæ vocis argutias, duplicabat gargarismos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045028Z">
        <seg>The little bird continued to sing, varying the witty turns of its charming voice, doubling its gurgling trills.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perpaucis Dei amicis id curæ fuisse legitur, de unico Gregorio Lopes Mexicano&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt; id Rosa legerat, statimque sibi imitandum sumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000924Z">
        <seg>It is recorded that very few of God's friends cared about it; Rosa had read of the one Mexican, Gregorio Lopes, and immediately resolved to imitate him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perpaucis Dei amicis id curæ fuisse legitur, de unico Gregorio Lopes Mexicano&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; id Rosa legerat, statimque sibi imitandum sumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041938Z">
        <seg>It is recorded that very few of God's friends cared about it; Rosa had read it in the single work of the Mexican Gregory Lopes, and immediately resolved to imitate it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Perrexit ille securus promissi, quod non ignoraret, quid Rosa valeret apud Deum, &amp; angelos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051840Z">
        <seg>He went on, secure in the promise, for he was not unaware of how much Rosa was worth to God and the angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pertæsa mater, quod crederet frustra ab ignavo progeniem exspectari, in mensa coram marito &amp; filiis constituit, illo vespere pullum cetera inutilem jugulare, ac postridie comedendum apponere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061534Z">
        <seg>Fed up, the mother, believing that a cowardly offspring would be of no use, decided at the table before her husband and sons to slaughter the useless chick that evening and serve it for dinner the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pervenere ambo ad horti januam, quæ ad umbræ tactum mox sponte patuit sine clavi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051818Z">
        <seg>They both reached the garden gate, which, at the touch of the shade, soon opened of its own accord without a key.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petiit doctor, quandoquidem in eum gradum nonnisi per tribulos &amp; spineta ascenditur, num saltem aliunde persecutiones &amp; ærumnas passa fuerit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050349Z">
        <seg>The doctor asked, since that rank can only be reached through tribulations and thorns, whether he had at least suffered persecutions and hardships elsewhere?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Petrus de Loaysa Dominicanus, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alium morti proximum,]&lt;/s2&gt; probe conscius zeli &amp; charitatis, qua flagrabat misericors Rosa in opem periclitantium proximorum, indicarat Virgini, decumbere in cœnobio, cumque mortis angoribus trepide luctari N.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060825Z">
        <seg>Peter de Loaysa, a Dominican, [and another near death,] well aware of the zeal and charity with which the merciful Rose burned for the aid of her fellow travelers in peril, had informed the Virgin that she was lying in the convent, and that N. was anxiously struggling with the pangs of death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Peñalosa cum socio Francisco Lopez coadjutore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053830Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053830Z">
        <seg>Peñalosa with his associate Francisco Lopez, assistant;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Philosophiæ ac medicinæ studiis ad summum apicem eruditus, insuper in omnia metaphysices adyta summo ingenii acumine penetrarat: celebris ob id in scholis, in exedris, in doctissimorum virorum congressibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050042Z">
        <seg>He had been trained to the highest degree in the study of philosophy and medicine, and had moreover penetrated with the keenest intellect into all the inner recesses of metaphysics; celebrated for this in schools, in lecture halls, and in the gatherings of the most learned men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placebat Virgini bicolor tot vernantium plantularum venustas &amp; gratia, maxime ubi deintus responsum accepit, omnes &amp; singulas uni Dilecto candido ac rubicundo servari in coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062548Z">
        <seg>The Virgin delighted in the twofold beauty and charm of so many budding plants, especially when she received the reply that all of them, one by one, were being reserved for the crown of the one Beloved, fair and ruddy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placet ei, quod ambæ in elaborando ipsius scapulari jucunde occupamur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055117Z">
        <seg>He is pleased that we are both happily occupied with making his scapular.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placuit hic Rosæ ardor Salvatori, tantamque propagandi sui amoris sollicitudinem ingenti miraculo obsignavit Deus, quod capite sequenti seorsim narrandum sepono.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053619Z">
        <seg>Salvator was pleased by Rose's ardor here, and God sealed that great zeal for spreading her love with an immense miracle, which I set aside to relate separately in the next chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placuit multis novum amœnitatis artificium, quod, irrigante gratia, sub hortulanæ devotione feliciter adoleverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054943Z">
        <seg>Many were pleased with the new art of beauty, which, watered by grace, had happily grown under the gardener's devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Placuit utrimque tam lauta conditio, præsertim quod Rosæ parentes ut facultatibus tenues, ita graves erant multitudine prolium: nam undecim numerabantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035957Z">
        <seg>Both sides were pleased with the generous terms, especially since Rosa's parents, though modest in means, were heavy-handed in the number of their children—for they numbered eleven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Planta similis est hederæ, eodem modo repens &amp; scandens quocumque loco ponatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041448Z">
        <seg>The plant is like ivy, creeping and climbing in the same way wherever it is placed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plura addidit ex mysticæ theologiæ aphorismis, de simplificatione cordis, de cynosura purificatæ intentionis, de nuditate affectuum, de indifferentia resignata, de arcanis introductionibus, de abysso luminis, de sapore omnis discursus intuitu cælestium, de fonte vitæ, de aliis, quæ Rosæ talium capaci &amp; placuere supra modum, &amp; profuere deinceps, ut suis confessariis, ex hoc vocabulario, se nosset clarius, atque significantius explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050330Z">
        <seg>He added more from the aphorisms of mystical theology: on the simplification of the heart, on the guiding star of purified intention, on the nakedness of the affections, on resigned indifference, on the secret introductions, on the abyss of light, on the savor of all discourse through the intuition of heavenly things, on the fountain of life, and on other matters, which the Rose, capable of such things, found exceedingly pleasing. and thereafter, to her confessors, she would know herself more clearly and explain herself more meaningfully from this vocabulary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Plura parabat dicere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044356Z">
        <seg>He was preparing to say more.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pluribus horis duravit obscurissima lucta, ingenti probro adversarii, qui victoriam desperans, vel unicum saltem gemitum, aut metus indicium extorquere tentabat, nec poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052128Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052128Z">
        <seg>The darkest struggle lasted for many hours, to the great shame of the adversary, who, despairing of victory, tried to wring out at least a single groan or a sign of fear, but could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pluries deinde Virginem, non tam examinaturus, quam collaturus, fidenter convenit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050622Z">
        <seg>He then repeatedly approached the Virgin, not so much to examine her as to converse with her, with confidence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pluries in eadem S. imagine (ut infra narrabitur) demulserat hujuscemodi suavis annictus orantem Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044322Z">
        <seg>On several occasions in the same holy image (as will be related below), this sweet gesture had moved the praying Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ponderosa hæc asperitas ut suapte grossitie fere implicabilis, ita duritie implicabilis, cuivis robustiori pœnitentiæ alumno sufficere potuisset ad summam austeritatem, nec tamen Rosæ sat fuit: nam infixis undique brevissimis acubus, hirsutam interulam diligentius in se armavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041845Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041845Z">
        <seg>This ponderous harshness, almost unwieldy by its own coarseness and unyielding in its rigidity, could have sufficed for the most robust penitent to attain the utmost austerity; yet it was not enough for Rosa, for she armed herself more diligently with a shaggy undergarment, fixing on every side the shortest spikes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pone, suppone, ut volueris, obstitura vetantium imperia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062632Z">
        <seg>Suppose, for whatever reason you wish, that the commands of those who forbid are obstructed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Poro affirmabat hanc orandi formulam terrori esse dæmonibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044954Z">
        <seg>Poro affirmed that this formula of prayer was a terror to demons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro adjectivum generis masculini, quod ab auctore vel typotheta huic malo perperam addebatur, in femininum commutavi.&lt;/e10&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052919Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, I changed the masculine adjective, which had been mistakenly added to this word by the author or the typographer, to the feminine form.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro celeberrimo tot illustrium heroïnarum contubernio digne apteque respondet augusta nobilissimæ fabricæ amplitudo, fontes, horti, viteta, porticus, dormitoria, peristylia, officinæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062430Z">
        <seg>Moreover, the august and noblest fabric responds fittingly and appropriately to the company of so many celebrated and illustrious heroines with its spaciousness, its fountains, gardens, vineyards, porticoes, dormitories, peristyles, and workshops.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro de immensitate gaudii ex arcta unione cum Sponso amicissimo, de fruitionis altitudine, de spirituali sapore veræ dulcedinis, ubi gustatur in suo proprio fonte, de fructuum præstantia, copia, sagina, deque similibus, pigebat tentare quemcumque sterilis elocutionis modum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055904Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, regarding the immensity of joy from the close union with the most beloved Spouse, the height of fruition, the spiritual savor of true sweetness where it is tasted at its own fountain, the excellence, abundance, and richness of the fruits, and the like, it was deemed unworthy to attempt any barren mode of expression.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro dum brachia &amp; lumbos vincula nervique arctabant, manipulatim urticæ ac minutuli sentes morsu creberrimo vexabant pectus, axillas, latera, ne qua pars vacaret a cruciatu, dum forte cilicium adhuc deerat præ inopia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041823Z">
        <seg>Moreover, as ropes and thongs bound their arms and loins, they tormented the chest, armpits, and sides with handfuls of nettles and tiny thorns in a most frequent bite, so that no part was spared from the torture, while, by chance, the cilicium was still lacking on account of the scarcity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro in ipso ad tremenda illa mysteria accessu quis fuerit Virgini attentissimæ devotionis ardor, quæ vultus gestuumque religiosa compositio, quod bullientis spiritus incendium, aliter describi non potest, quam si ipsammet quis Catharinam Senensem, Eucharistico bolo excipiendo procubuam, huc transcribendo delineet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055737Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, at the very approach to those awesome mysteries, the ardor of the Virgin's most devoted fervor, the reverent composure of her face and gestures, the blazing fire of her spirit—none of this can be described otherwise than by transcribing here the very image of Catherine of Siena herself, prostrate as she received the Eucharistic Host.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro interiorem arcum impleverat humanitas Domini mei Jesu Christi radiosa, clara, prælucida, tanto gloriæ amictu circumdata, quantum in ea numquam alias fueram conspicata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052733Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the inner arc was filled by the radiant, clear, and pre-lucid humanity of my Lord Jesus Christ, surrounded by so great a mantle of glory as I had never before beheld in it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro ne capilli, qui post rescissionem breviores succreverant, profundiori obstarent aculeorum impressioni, ad vivam usque cutem novacula crinem omnem submoverat, aliquid tamen inculti cincinni relictum in fronte, quo corona interdiu obtegeretur, ut matrem lateret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042121Z">
        <seg>Moreover, so that the hair, which had grown back shorter after the cutting, would not hinder a deeper impression of the spikes, she had shaved every strand right down to the living skin with a razor, leaving only a few unkempt curls on her forehead to cover her crown by day and hide it from her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro probabilius existimamus, hæc &amp; similia potius imaginationi sanctæ Virginis repræsentata esse, quam re ipsa contigisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051505Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, we consider it more probable that these and similar things were rather represented to the imagination of the holy Virgin than actually happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro qualibet sexta feria hoc addebat in cumulum, ut usque ad auricularum cartilagines imumque occiput coronam demitteret, quo tempora (ubi caro mollior, sensusque delicatior) prædensa stimulorum obsidio &amp; molestius incingeret, &amp; profundius sauciaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042207Z">
        <seg>Moreover, every Friday he would add to the heap so that he would lower the crown down to the cartilages of the ears and the lower part of the occiput, by which the temples (where the flesh is softer and the sensation more delicate) were besieged by the pointed stimuli and more painfully girdled, and wounded more deeply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro quantus fuerit Lorenzana in ea, quam jure scientiam Sanctorum dixeris, mystica theosophia, quantus in sapore &amp; experimento rerum cælestium, quam exercitatus in altissimæ contemplationis disciplina, quam oculatus ac lynceus in prudenti spirituum discretione, hoc unum (si reliqua deessent) judico sufficere, quod divina providentia singulariter ipsi spiritum Rosæ commiserit gubernandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050209Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, how great Lorenzana was in it, however you may rightly call the knowledge of the Saints a mystical theosophy, how great he was in the savor and experience of heavenly things, how practiced in the discipline of the highest contemplation, how keen-eyed and lynx-like in the prudent discernment of spirits—this one thing alone, if all the rest were lacking, I judge sufficient: that divine providence singularly entrusted to him the spirit of the Rose to govern.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro quatuor postremis vitæ suæ annis in majori (quam vocamus) hebdomada, quamdiu sacrum Christi corpus adorabatur clausum de more in monumento, nec noctu inde recedebat affixa tumulo Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060040Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, during the last four years of her life, in the greater week (as we call it), while the sacred body of Christ was being adored, enclosed as was the custom in the monument, and the Rose was affixed to the tomb, nor did she depart from it at night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro reliquam ipsius erga parentes observantiam, amorem, pietatem nulla satis capit historia: quam solicita jugiter sategerit, ut genitorum suorum inopiæ provideret ex labore suarum manuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035731Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, history cannot adequately capture the rest of his respect, love, and piety toward his parents: how diligently he always worked to provide for his parents' needs through the labor of his own hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro singulas distantium lignorum intercapedines præacutis tegularum fragmentis, coctilium patinarum frustulis, testarumque fractarum triquetris sic implevit, ut singulorum pars mucronata obverteretur corpori, ac ea quidem confertissima densitate, qua nutare aut cedere nulla posset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042610Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, he filled each of the gaps between the distant timbers with sharp fragments of tiles, shards of clay pots, and broken triangular pottery, so that the pointed end of each piece was turned inward toward the body, and with such a dense packing that none could waver or give way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro specimen obedientiæ hic assignatum apud nos infra num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035911Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the specimen of obedience assigned here is found below in our number.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro visionem neque cassam aut inanem fuisse, nec pure imaginariam, probavit effectus: nam Rosa mox convaluit, redierunt corpusculo vires, agilitas, robur, quo postridie templum adiit, Panem angelorum manducavit, satura, vegeta, sana domum repetiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051121Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051121Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, the effect proved that the vision had been neither empty nor vain, nor purely imaginary: for Rosa soon recovered, her strength, agility, and vigor returned, and the next day she went to the temple, ate the Bread of Angels, and returned home full, refreshed, and healthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro, quam plene cum illo magistræ suæ virtutes induerit (quamquam id sequentibus capitibus reponamus) ad coronidem hujus capitis, unicum id argumentum esto, quod semel tacite spectanti, mirantique confessario visa fuerit Rosa, specie, vultu, lineamentis sic transformata, ut viva similitudine ipsissimam S. Catharinæ Senensis effigiem referret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040413Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, how fully she had put on the virtues of her Master (though we shall reserve that for the following chapters), let this be the crowning point of this chapter: that once, when the confessor was silently observing and marveling, Rosa appeared to him, in form, countenance, and features, so transformed that in her living likeness she bore the very image of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Porro, quæ statuæ ornamenta conquireret, tractaret, circumponeret, una hac Rosa (dum vixit) nulla reperiebatur aptior, dignior, peritior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055024Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, for the task of procuring, handling, and arranging the ornaments for the statues, no one was found more suitable, more worthy, or more skilled than this one Rose (during her lifetime).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post Virginis obitum ancillula in Limensi S. Catharinæ monasterio argenteum cochlear per incuriam perdiderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035638Z">
        <seg>After the Virgin's death, a little handmaid at the Lima monastery of St. Catherine had carelessly lost a silver spoon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post assumptam tertii Ordinis candidam vestem, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; alteram]&lt;/s2&gt; nocte quadam in somnis ostensa fuit Rosæ pulcherrima ac decentissima Viri species: pene suggerebat animus cælitem quemdam esse, imo ipsummet Speciosum forma præ filiis hominum, nisi quod habitus latomum fingebat, seu marmorum cædendorum nobilem artificem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044139Z">
        <seg>After assuming the white habit of the Third Order, &lt;s2&gt;[and another]&lt;/s2&gt; one night in a dream the most beautiful and most graceful form of a man was shown to Rose: the mind almost suggested that he was some heavenly being, indeed the very one Fairer in form than the sons of men, except that his attire suggested a noble craftsman of marble, or of hewing stone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post cassas preces, &amp; obstinatas repulsas, Virgo de more confugit ad cælitum auxilia, Deum in primis Deique Matrem assidue invocans, ut maternum sibi consensum potenti afflatu elicerent: nec dubitavit exaudiri;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043512Z">
        <seg>After fruitless prayers and stubborn refusals, the Virgin, as was her custom, fled to the help of the heavenly powers, constantly invoking God first and the Mother of God, so that they might by their powerful intercession win for her the maternal consent: and she did not doubt that she would be heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post hæc, ille Speciosus forma præ filiis hominum bilancem, &amp; nescio quæ pondera disposuit in propatulo: mox populosæ angelorum phalanges festivo ornatu, ac nitore illustres accessere, decora cum reverentia sese inclinantes Domino majestatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052748Z">
        <seg>After these things, he, fair in form above the sons of men, set up a balance and, I know not what, weights on the open platform; soon the populous phalanxes of angels, adorned with festive attire and shining with splendor, drew near, bowing with graceful reverence to the Lord of majesty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Post menses quatuor aut quinque Joanna integre convaluit, suumque foris habitaculum sana repetiit, hoc unum dolens, quod hospitæ palam referre grates minime liceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061224Z">
        <seg>After four or five months, Joanna had fully recovered and returned to her lodgings abroad in good health, grieving only that she could not publicly express her gratitude to her hostess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Posterior ei continuus erat, &amp; quantum nostræ mortalitatis via permittit, nulli interruptioni obnoxius, nisi dum ad horam patiebatur visiones, de quibus capite sequenti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044827Z">
        <seg>His posterior was continuous, and, as far as the course of our mortality allows, subject to no interruption, except when he endured visions at the appointed hour, of which in the next chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postrema hæc verba ænigma erant, quod tunc quidem sacristanus non satis assequebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043534Z">
        <seg>These last words were an enigma, which the sacristan did not quite understand at the time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie Gundisalvi uxor non destitit, donec, enarrante Rosa, universam rei seriem prolicuisset, ubi inter alia &amp; hoc notatum fuit, quod hujuscemodi conflictuum duri agones constantissimæ Virgini neque peregrini erant, aut minus frequentes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052147Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052147Z">
        <seg>The next day, Gundisalvi's wife did not stop until, with Rosa narrating, she had elicited the entire sequence of events, where, among other things, it was noted that such conflicts were neither unfamiliar nor infrequent for the most steadfast Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Postridie reversa ad pedes sacræ imaginis diutius procubuit, ferventius oravit, lachrymas addidit, prolixæque moræ æquanimi patientia favorem expectavit a solo Deiparæ vultu pendula, nec surrectura de loco, donec exauditam se intelligeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054425Z">
        <seg>The next day she returned and prostrated herself longer at the feet of the sacred image, prayed more fervently, added tears, and with patient equanimity awaited favor, her hopes hanging solely on the face of the Mother of God, and she would not rise from her place until she understood herself to have been heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pridie sacræ Communionis asperius emacerabat corpusculum flagellis &amp; rigido jejunio, ut famem animæ comitaretur esuries corporis: venturo Sponso adornabat secretum mentis thalamum affectibus reverentiæ, humilitatis, ac desiderii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055724Z">
        <seg>The day before Holy Communion, she more harshly scourged her little body with whips and a strict fast, so that the hunger of the soul would be accompanied by the thirst of the body: she adorned the secret chamber of her mind for the coming Bridegroom with affections of reverence, humility, and desire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primam futurarum monialium præfectam (priorissam dicimus) ex vultu agnovit, spiritu suo imbuit, osculo pacis quodammodo initiavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062458Z">
        <seg>He recognized the first future abbess (whom we call the prioress) by her face, imbued her with his spirit, and in a manner initiated her with the kiss of peace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primo interrogata, a quanto tempore cælestes stimulos, &amp; tranquillum orationis spiritum concepisset, respondit simplicissime, temporis quidem se non meminisse, eo quod a prima pueritia se repererit fundendis precatiunculis apprime deditam, ac tota mente ad serios meditandi conatus erectam, ut nihil occurrere sibi dulcius potuerit, quam Deo loqui, Deum cogitare, inhiare supernis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050244Z">
        <seg>When first asked how long she had conceived heavenly aspirations and a tranquil spirit of prayer, she answered most simply that she did not remember the time, for she had found herself from her earliest childhood most devoted to pouring out little prayers and with her whole mind raised to serious acts of meditation, so that nothing could have seemed sweeter to her than to speak with God. To think of God, to long for heavenly things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primo tunicam interulam conficiunto 600 Salutationes angelicæ, totidemque vicibus recitanda antiphona &lt;b3&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b3&gt;, cum 15 diebus jejuniorum, in reverentiam purissimi gaudii ex nuntio angelico, quod in suis castissimis visceribus humana carne induendum erat æternum Patris Verbum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054608Z">
        <seg>First, let a tunic be made of interweaving 600 Hail Marys, and let the antiphon "Hail Queen" be recited as many times, together with 15 days of fasting, in reverence for the purest joy of the angelic announcement that the eternal Word of the Father was to be clothed in human flesh in her most chaste womb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primum æterna sua beatitudo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061745Z">
        <seg>First, his eternal beatitude;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Primum, tametsi prope quotidianum esset, præsertim ultimis vitæ annis, quos in Gundisalvi quæstoris ædibus consummavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041308Z">
        <seg>First, although it was almost a daily occurrence, especially in the last years of his life, which he spent in the house of the treasurer Gundisalvus;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Priori, (ut supra attigimus) cujusvis diei naturalis horas non minus duodecim seposuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044824Z">
        <seg>He had set aside no fewer than twelve hours of any natural day (as we noted above).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pro his vere miseris quotidie cruenta sibi infligebat verbera, gemebat, suspirabat coram offenso Numine, nullique parcebat lachrymarum sumptui, ut talibus sinceram scelerum pœnitentiam vitæque seriam emendationem a Deo exoraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060518Z">
        <seg>For these truly wretched ones, he inflicted bloody scourges on himself daily, groaned and sighed before the offended Deity, sparing no tears, so that by such deeds he might implore from God a sincere repentance for his sins and a serious amendment of his life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Probarunt factum quicumque mysterium agnoverant, certi non ab humano studio, sed necessitatis fortunato potius quam fortuito consilio hoc singulare virtutis testimonium Virgini obtigisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042424Z">
        <seg>All who had recognized the mystery attested to the fact that this singular testimony of virtue had been granted to the Virgin not by human design, but rather by the necessity of fortune—more by chance than by deliberate plan.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Processit Rosa, dixitque voce humili ac intercisa, plerumque post illas horrendas tenebras conspicue sibi ostendi humanitatem Christi, nunc virili, nunc infantili aut puerili facie blandam, formosam, affabilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050535Z">
        <seg>Rosa stepped forward and said in a low, broken voice that, for the most part, after those horrible darknesses, the humanity of Christ was clearly revealed to her, now with a manly face, now with an infantile or boyish one, gentle, beautiful, and affable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prodigiosum erat videre, tenerrimam Virginem ardentibus juncturis, tumentibus nodis, inflatis articulis, lancinantibus ex consensu musculis &amp; ligaminibus, inter extremos dolorum laniatus decumbere quietam, serenam, placidam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052637Z">
        <seg>It was a prodigious sight to see the most tender Virgin, with burning joints, swollen knots, and distended limbs, her muscles and ligaments tearing apart in unison, lying amid the extremes of pain, yet calm, serene, and placid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Profecto &amp; hic quæri posset: Quis dedit gallo intelligentiam, qua obediret sanctæ Puellæ jubenti jugulum dare cantui, ne daret cultro?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061606Z">
        <seg>Indeed, one might ask here: Who gave the rooster the intelligence to obey the holy maiden's command to give his throat to song, rather than to a knife?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Profuit sane permultis hoc exemplum, Rosæque ardens hortatio, ut frequentandis Dei laudibus familiarius adsuescerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045137Z">
        <seg>This example certainly benefited many, and Rose's ardent exhortation that they become more familiar with frequenting the praises of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde Rosa ferreum hunc scapularum vomerem sanctiusne posuerit, an sumpserit, in medio relinquo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041615Z">
        <seg>Therefore, I leave it undecided whether Rosa placed this iron plow of scapulae more sacredly or took it up.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde cum de felici Virginis transitu agetur infra, opportunus de his redibit sermo: neque enim unico capitulo tota Rosæ patientia potuit explicari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052900Z">
        <seg>Therefore, when the happy passage of the Virgin is treated of below, the discussion of these matters will be resumed; for the whole patience of the Rose could not be explained in a single chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde in se denuo retracta, cœpit &amp; ipsa laudare Deum, eo fervore, qui sensim Rosam abripuit in extasim, nec ab ea usque ad vesperam sese explicuit, cum mane, tribus circiter ante meridiem horis, aviculæ melodiam cœpisset auscultare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045050Z">
        <seg>Thus, drawing herself inward once more, she too began to praise God with such fervor that it gradually carried Rosa into ecstasy, and she did not come out of it until evening, having begun to listen to the little bird's melody in the morning, about three hours before noon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde nil mirum quod ([ut] postea innotuit) Rosam cetera intrepidam sæpius horror pavorque medullitus pervaserit ad unius sui tam immitis lectuli cogitatum: tremebat, æstuabat, sudabat misera, priusquam vel spondam attingeret, memor, quales ipsam expectarent ibidem plumæ, quot minaces cristæ arrigerentur illic in suam lanienam, quo sibi cruciatu distabescendum, quoties in alterum latus revolvendum esset lassum corpusculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042638Z">
        <seg>Therefore, it is no wonder that (as later became known) horror and dread often pervaded the very marrow of the otherwise intrepid Rosa at the mere thought of her own so cruel little bed: the poor woman trembled, was on fire, and sweated before she even touched the pillow, remembering what feathers awaited her there, how many menacing quills would be pricked up there for her slaughter, what torments she would have to endure, how many times she would have to turn her weary little body to the other side;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde rationibus adortus est Virginem, ut omnino severissimum crudelis instrumenti usum dissuaderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042319Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he appealed to the Virgin on the grounds that she should entirely discourage the use of the most severe and cruel instrument.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde se sui pudere, quod tantis cæli beneficiis nondum, uti par erat, responderit: atque ex hoc capite, se creaturarum omnium, quotquot per orbem vivunt, merito ingratissimam esse reputandam, ex quo nimirum viderat, eo se modo tractari a Domino, quo ipse nonnisi amicissimos suos tractare solet, nec tamen se pro dignitate redamare tam amabilem, &amp; amantem Deum: &amp; forsan hic ei sensus erat, quando cum doloribus una augeri amorem flagitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052714Z">
        <seg>Therefore she was ashamed of herself, that she had not yet responded to so many heavenly benefits as she ought; and from this, she rightly considered herself the most ungrateful of all creatures living throughout the world, since she had seen that the Lord treated her in the same way that he is accustomed to treat only his dearest friends, and yet she did not love so loving and amiable a God in a manner worthy of him; and perhaps this was her feeling when she prayed that, together with her pains, her love might be increased.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Proinde videns, frustra se repugnare, nec fidem suis dictis haberi, tacuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045751Z">
        <seg>Therefore, seeing that he was fighting in vain and was not believed, he fell silent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prolixum foret singula membratim prosequi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050631Z">
        <seg>It would be too lengthy to go through each item in detail.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Promisit Rosa, ut erat ad subveniendum proximis mire liberalis &amp; prompta, hominemque proficiscentem Deo, &amp; custodi angelo impense commendavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051837Z">
        <seg>Rosa promised, as she was wonderfully liberal and ready to help her neighbors, and she earnestly commended the man setting out to God and his guardian angel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prompte respondit Rosa, nil eam cum corporeis figuris habere commune, at longe minus cum colore, aut quavis creata pulchritudine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052844Z">
        <seg>Rosa promptly replied that she had nothing in common with corporeal forms, but far less with color, or any created beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prosperrimum id propagandæ illic fidei nomen &amp; exordium fuit: nam simul in hoc templo, quod unicum tunc extabat, sub tutelari præsidio divæ Virginis, ad titulum S. Rosarii, constituta fuit parochia: hic primus instructis catechumenis patuit fons lustralis, quo infideles ad veri Numinis cultum adducti, cœpere per sacri baptismi januam intromitti ad caulam ovium Christi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054230Z">
        <seg>That was the most auspicious name and beginning for spreading the faith there: for at that time, in this one and only temple, under the protective patronage of the Blessed Virgin, dedicated to the title of St. of the Rosary, a parish was established: here the font was opened for the first time to the instructed catechumens, by which the infidels, brought to the worship of the true God, began to be admitted through the door of holy baptism into the fold of Christ's sheep;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prostabat in altari oratorii imago Jesu parvuli, venustis coloribus adumbrata: in hanc Virgo sæpissime jaciebat ardentes oculos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054109Z">
        <seg>On the altar of the oratory stood an image of the infant Jesus, painted in charming colors; to this the Virgin would often turn her ardent gaze.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prosultu, cursuque flexuoso dextrorsum, lævorsum properabat minax animal, cauda rigide arrecta, spumante ore, boatu horrisono, naribus ira fumantibus, arenas calcibus jactabat in aëra, &amp; sicubi in truncum, pannum, aut studiose objectum pileolum offenderat, atroci &amp; crebra impactorum cornium vi infodiebat solo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062142Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062142Z">
        <seg>With a forward rush and a winding course to right and left, the menacing beast hurried on, its tail rigidly erect, mouth frothing, with a horrid bellow, nostrils smoking with rage, flinging sand into the air with its hooves, and whenever it struck a log, a rag, or a cap eagerly thrown in its path, it drove them into the ground with the cruel and repeated force of its horns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Protestabatur innocens Virgo, incassum hujusmodi sumptus, labores, tempus disperdi, malum suum nequaquam in corpore, sed intus in spiritu sævire ac dominari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045745Z">
        <seg>The innocent Virgin protested that such expenses, labors, and time were wasted in vain, that her evil did not rage and dominate in her body at all, but within, in her spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Protestabatur medicus, dissuadebant, rogabant amici, demum prælati regulares severissimis inhibitionibus addidere mulctas, præcepta, dirarum ac censurarum fulmina;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060750Z">
        <seg>The doctor protested, they dissuaded him, friends begged him; finally, the regular prelates added to the severest prohibitions fines, precepts, and the thunderbolts of anathemas and censures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Provincialis commiserat) induta est veste bicolori, quam in Senensi magistra dilexerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040211Z">
        <seg>The provincial had taken pity on her; she was dressed in a two-colored garment that she had loved in her Sena teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcipuam vero &amp; cognominem hujus insulæ urbem laudatus Baudrandus assignat his verbis:&lt;/e3&gt; Urbs Americæ Septemtrionalis episcopalis sub archiepiscopo sancti Dominici, munita cum arce duplici &amp; portu capaci in parte Septemtrionali insulæ cognominis in parte boreali.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T232141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035130Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the celebrated Baudrand assigns as the principal and eponymous city of this island the following words:&lt;/e3&gt; "A North American episcopal city under the Archbishop of Saint Dominic, fortified with a double fortress and a capacious harbor on the northern part of the eponymous island in the northern region."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcipue in majori Dominicæ Passionis hebdomada, a cruce fidelium adorationi palam exposita divelli non poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054841Z">
        <seg>Especially during the greater week of the Passion of the Lord, it could not be removed from the public adoration of the faithful;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præcipue tamen sextis feriis, velut ex acetosa spongia cum Christo in cruce pransura, ex eodem felle &amp; panis crustula sibi componebat electuarium, mordaci aceto &amp; copiosis lachrymis stillatim superfusis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041234Z">
        <seg>Especially on Fridays, however, she would prepare for herself an electuary from the same gall and crusts of bread, as if to dine with Christ on the cross on a sponge soaked in vinegar, sprinkling it drop by drop with biting vinegar and copious tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatores sacello invisunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054301Z">
        <seg>Preachers visit the chapel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatorum SS. Rosario dicatum adiit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034730Z">
        <seg>He attended the convent of the Preachers of the Holy Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatorum celebrabantur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060016Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060016Z">
        <seg>The preachers were celebrated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prædicatorum religioso laïco) transmitti demum impetrarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051431Z">
        <seg>He had finally obtained the transmission of the religious preacher to the layman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præparata mater advocat Rosam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043401Z">
        <seg>The prepared mother calls Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Præsertim mane, dum instabat surgendi hora, incipiebant soporifera modicum operari, somnumque lentissimum vi attrahebant in cerebrum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051325Z">
        <seg>Especially in the morning, as the time to rise drew near, the soporifics would begin to take effect and would force the slowest sleep upon the brain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Prævaluit tamen apud constantissimam puellam idea Seraphicæ magistræ suæ (ita virginem Senensem appellare consueverat) quam a teneris elegerat statu, habitu, moribus imitandam, prout diserte in Processu testis sexagesimus octavus asseverat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040010Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, the idea of her Seraphic mistress (as she had grown accustomed to calling the Virgin of Siena) prevailed in the mind of the most steadfast maiden—one she had chosen from her earliest years to imitate in bearing, dress, and manners, as the sixty-eighth witness clearly affirmed in the Process.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Psal.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051546Z">
        <seg>Psalm</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Pudebat Rosam adversario succumbere tam ignavo, pugnabat etiam cadendo, illidebat cervicem parieti, tundebat sibi latera, si quo modo extunderet irrepentem lethargum: vincebat tandem, at non sine agone;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043044Z">
        <seg>Rosam was ashamed to succumb to such a feeble adversary; she fought even as she fell, striking her neck against the wall, beating her sides to drive out the creeping lethargy. She was victorious in the end, but not without a struggle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Puduit generosam Virginem præsentis ibi luminis uti solatio, aut qualicumque præsidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052051Z">
        <seg>It was shameful for the noble Virgin to use the light present there as a comfort, or as any kind of protection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Putabatur hæc prima omnium consensura, sive ob tanti archiepiscopi reverentiam, sive quod onusta prolibus, Filiam sine dote posset decentissime accommodare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040041Z">
        <seg>This was thought to be the first thing everyone would agree to, either out of reverence for such a great archbishop, or because, being burdened with children, she could most fittingly provide a dowryless daughter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qua de re infra loco proprio cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061941Z">
        <seg>On this matter, see the proper place below, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qua in re, ut humanum effugeret plausum, nec de singularitate notari posset, singulis his diebus studiosissime variabat horam, nunc maturius nunc serius ad sacram Communionem accedendo: cælestis enim thesaurus (monente Gregorio) absconditur, ut servetur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055712Z">
        <seg>In this matter, to avoid human applause and not be noticed for his singularity, he most diligently varied the hour on each of these days, approaching the sacred Communion now earlier, now later: for the heavenly treasure (as Gregory advises) is hidden, so that it may be preserved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qua vero animi demissione tunc ad circumstantes conversa, petiverit condonari sibi, quidquid forsitan in eam domum aut familiam, seu pravo exemplo, seu tetricis ac inofficiosis moribus deliquisset, longiorem exigeret periodum, nisi ad reliqua foret properandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040720Z">
        <seg>With what humility she then turned to those present, asking that whatever she might have wronged in that household or family—whether by a bad example or by gloomy and ungracious conduct—be forgiven her, provided it did not require a longer period than was necessary to attend to the remaining matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quadam vice inter suspiria, exciderunt Virgini (at perfunctorie, &amp; submisse) hæc verba: “Nosti utique, mater mea dulcissima, quod si XV aut XVI pataconum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; mihi facultas esset, vestirem te, pro genii mei dictamine, novo candidiori amictu”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055042Z">
        <seg>At one point, between sighs, these words escaped the Virgin (but perfunctorily and in a low voice): "You know full well, my sweetest mother, that if I had the means of fifteen or sixteen patacons, I would, according to the dictates of my genius, clothe you in a new, whiter garment."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quadriennis erat, cum tenellos humeros (quibus flagellandis tunc adhuc deerant rudi puellæ funiculi) incepit vexare nunc crudorum laterum ponderibus, nunc gravis trunci onere, velut ex tunc conata bajulare crucem post Christum, sub infamis ligni molestia laborantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041622Z">
        <seg>He was four years old when he began to torment his tender shoulders—shoulders that then still lacked the rough girls' whips—now with the weight of raw sides, now with the burden of a heavy trunk, as if from that moment he had set out to carry the cross after Christ, laboring under the infamous wood's vexation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quam bene mellifluus Bernardus in suo exclamat jubilo: “Qui te gustant esuriunt”!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055656Z">
        <seg>How sweetly Bernard exclaims in his joyful exultation: "Those who taste you hunger!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quam doluit Rosa, pro illa vice maternum sibi vultum ereptum fuisse, dum cunctabatur!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051415Z">
        <seg>How Rosa regretted that, on that occasion, the maternal face had been snatched from her while she hesitated!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quam putas luctabatur hic cum obedientia vani ornatus pudicus horror!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035306Z">
        <seg>How do you suppose he struggled here with the obedience of vain, modest adornment—horror!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quamquam permodici hujus capillamenti in sincipite residui alius quoque usus fuerit, ad nova dolorum incrementa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042124Z">
        <seg>Although the very small remainder of this hair on the crown had been used for something else, it was also used for new intensifications of pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quando in isto Rosarii sacello Rosa coram Virgineo hoc simulacro Dominicani Ordinis habitum suscepit, adfuit publicæ functioni plorans mater, viditque filiam, blando favore Deiparæ elevari in cælum, unde perfusa gaudio facile compescuit lachrymas, cœpitque tacito applausu felici puellæ gratulari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054326Z">
        <seg>When in this chapel of the Rosary Rosa, before the Virgin, received the habit of this Order of the Dominicans, her mother was present at the public ceremony weeping, and saw her daughter, by the gentle favor of the Mother of God, being lifted up to heaven; where, filled with joy, she easily restrained her tears and began to congratulate the happy maiden with silent applause.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quantulum est, quod huic donavit animalculo naturæ Auctor, &amp; en totis præcordiis, totis viribus, ei rependit laudis tributum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045038Z">
        <seg>How little the Author of nature has given to this little creature, and yet with its whole heart and all its strength, it repays a tribute of praise to Him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quantum hæ scruporum sub stragulo extantium dissitæ cotes jacentem Rosam dispunxerint, facilis conjectura est, sane in ipsis solidis asseribus conspicue apparuerunt impastinata lapillorum vestigia, ipsæ tabulæ scrobiculatim hinc inde extritæ protestabantur satis, quam molliter his incubuerit macilenta Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042539Z">
        <seg>It is easy to guess how many of the pebbles from the scree beneath the blanket pierced the lying Rose; indeed, on the solid planks themselves the traces of the stones were clearly visible, and the boards themselves, worn away here and there in little hollows, testified amply how gently the gaunt Rose had lain upon them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quare suspicor, hic&lt;/e0&gt; dentilum &lt;e1&gt;pro denticulo scriptum vel usurpatum esse, quia flores e scapis acuminati prodeunt instar parvorum dentium seu denticulorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055538Z">
        <seg>Therefore I suspect that here a denticle has been written or used in place of a small tooth, because the flowers emerge from the stems in a pointed fashion like little teeth or tiny teeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quarto fibulas apponunto 600 Salutationes (ut supra) in reverentiam intimi gaudii, dum benedictum Filiolum suum offerebat in templo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054621Z">
        <seg>Fourth, let us present 600 greetings (as above) in reverence of the innermost joy, while she offered her blessed little Son in the temple.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quatriduum effluxerat, quando applicitæ curæ recordata mater, silentem Filiam interrogavit, qui valeret, quidve egisset de pellium involucris?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035511Z">
        <seg>Four days had passed when, remembering the care she had given, the mother asked her silent daughter how she was and what she had done with the bundles of skins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quatuor continuis horis &amp; ultra duravit pium ostentum, cunctis per gyrum admirantibus, quod nullo colorum aut venustatis detrimento, vultus ille sacerrimus, post sudores pulchrior, vividiorque solito appareret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053852Z">
        <seg>The pious spectacle lasted for four consecutive hours and beyond, with everyone in the crowd marveling that, without any loss of color or beauty, that most sacred face appeared more beautiful and more vivid than usual after the sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui Rosam viderant adeo cælestium avidam, adeo spiritualibus exercitiis, solitudini, macerationi corporis addictam, &amp; seculi pertæsam, suadebant identidem alicujus monasterii ingressum, quo liberius vacaret Deo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040100Z">
        <seg>Those who had seen Rosa so eager for heavenly things, so devoted to spiritual exercises, solitude, and the mortification of the body, and so weary of the world, repeatedly urged her to enter a monastery, where she could more freely devote herself to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui conscientiæ Virginis præerant, tribus circiter ante illius obitum annis consideraverant, ipsam viribus nimium attenuari, corpusculum tot flagris, jejuniis, morbis exhaustum, arduo tam asperi lecti supplicio non amplius par fore, &amp;, si fomenta illi persuaderi nequeant, saltem tormenta debili aut subtrahenda esse, aut moderanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042849Z">
        <seg>Those who were in charge of the Virgin's conscience had, about three years before her death, observed that she was being unduly weakened by her own exertions: her little body, exhausted by so many scourges, fasts, and illnesses, was no longer fit for the ordeal of such a harsh bed, and that, if she could not be persuaded to accept the remedies, the torments should at least be eased or removed, or their severity reduced.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Qui virgam Aaronis florere jussit, providit &amp; nobis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055206Z">
        <seg>He who commanded the rod of Aaron to blossom, also provided for us.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quia amore langueo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051635Z">
        <seg>Because I am sick with love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid ageret sub his tormentis Virgo tam divulsa in se, quam avulsa a Dilecto?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045549Z">
        <seg>What would the Virgin do under these torments, so torn within herself as to be wrenched away from her Beloved?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid cogitabunt, quid dicent obvii, hærere te in plateis, ut transversas ubique stipulas, statuo&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;*&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; labore divarices?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054906Z">
        <seg>What will they think, what will they say, the passersby, that you stand in the streets, spreading your thighs everywhere, straining your groin with your labors?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid cunctamini?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055203Z">
        <seg>What are you waiting for?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid egisti?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035542Z">
        <seg>What did you do?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid enim tenuium parentum inops filia partiri aliis indigentibus potuit, vix sibi, alendisque genitoribus sufficiens?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061017Z">
        <seg>For what could the poor daughter of poor parents share with other needy people, when she herself had barely enough to support herself and her parents?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid hic remedii inter saxum &amp; sacrum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051108Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051108Z">
        <seg>What remedy is here between rock and sacred?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid hæsitatis?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055200Z">
        <seg>What are you hesitating about?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quid multa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051022Z">
        <seg>What's the matter?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidam ex Virginis confessariis dono tam inæstimabili tacitus adstupuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061956Z">
        <seg>One of the Virgin's confessors stood speechless, dumbfounded by such an inestimable gift.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid hortabatur proximos, quidquid inculcabat, unum orationis studium erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045056Z">
        <seg>Whatever he urged on his neighbors, whatever he impressed upon them, his sole aim in speaking was one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quidquid sit de certa horum nominum significatione, potuisset scriptor Actorum supersedere plerisque his synonymis, quæ non satis apte angelis tribui videntur.&lt;/e3&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052333Z">
        <seg>Whatever the precise meaning of these names may be, the author of the Acts could have omitted most of these synonyms, which do not seem to be sufficiently appropriate to be attributed to angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quiescebat in cunis trimestris parvula, quando cum ancillula &amp; filiabus accita mater conspexit filiolæ vultum undique opertum repanda, sed paulo post disparente rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034649Z">
        <seg>The little three-month-old lay resting in her cradle when, summoned with the handmaid and the daughters, her mother came and saw her daughter's face covered all over with a rash, though the rose soon vanished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin &amp; usque ad animam transiit energia saluberrimæ correctionis: nam mens illa ad sobrietatem mirabiliter revocata, cœpit tractare attentius æternæ salutis negotium, emundare conscientiam, reformare ignavos mores, veternum excutere, gratulantibus cunctis, qui umquam antea in tabe illius vitii putrescentem defleverant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060812Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060812Z">
        <seg>Indeed, the energy of the most salutary correction penetrated to the very soul: for that mind, wonderfully restored to sobriety, began to attend more diligently to the business of eternal salvation, to purify its conscience, to reform its cowardly habits, to shake off its old sluggishness, to the joy of all who had ever before lamented its decay in the corruption of that vice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin potius omnis undequaque tum ratio, tum regiminis potestas videbatur inexorabiliter obstitura, præsertim cum pro conditione temporis abunde sat monasteriorum esset in civitate novella, cujus tunc ætas primam annorum centuriam nondum expleverat, plurimumque adhuc distabat ab ea, qua hodie tumet, amplitudinis vastitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062619Z">
        <seg>Rather, every consideration and the authority of the government everywhere seemed inexorably opposed, especially since, given the times, there were already more than enough monasteries in the new city, which then had not yet completed its first century and was still a long way from the vast extent it now boasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin tu potius apud hanc seraphicam matrem, qua polles &amp; gratia &amp; fiducia, vitam ac valetudinem ancillæ meæ exora”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055234Z">
        <seg>Rather, you, who are endowed with both grace and confidence by this seraphic mother, pray for the life and health of my handmaid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin tu potius memineris pretiosum hunc liquorem deberi soli Deo in ablutionem nostrorum peccatorum”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053405Z">
        <seg>Rather, you should remember that this precious liquid is owed to God alone for the washing away of our sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quin usque adeo menti Virginis insederat sanctæ crucis religio, ut, ubicumque a fortuita trabium commissura, in fenestrarum clathris, in decussatis sepium bacillis, in ostiorum cardinibus, in vectibus, in ipsis demum festucis ac paleis, aut alio quovis rejectaneo figura crucis occurrisset, hanc sine attenta ac tacita veneratione nusquam præteriret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054847Z">
        <seg>For the devotion to the holy cross had so taken possession of the Virgin's mind, that wherever she encountered the figure of a cross, whether from the chance meeting of beams, in the bars of windows, in the crossed rods of hedges, on door hinges, on beams, or even in the very straws and poles, or wherever she encountered the figure of a cross on any other scrap or refuse, she would nowhere pass it by without a careful and silent veneration.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinimo de illorum consensu, bis quot hebdomadis puella reficiebatur divino hoc pabulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055647Z">
        <seg>Rather, with their consent, the girl was nourished twice a week with this divine food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinimo erga locupletissimum hunc Sponsum ea erat Rosæ fiducia, ut ausu liberali Virgo pauperrima frequenter &amp; palam in se receperit providere de toto sumptu ad excitandum a fundamentis novum sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium, dummodo in diebus suis appelleret, quod solum tunc deerat, regiæ permissionis diploma.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062315Z">
        <seg>Indeed, Rose's confidence in this exceedingly wealthy sponsor was such that, with a generous spirit, the very poor virgin frequently and openly accepted from him the provision of the entire cost for erecting from the foundations a new monastery of Saint Catherine of Siena, provided that in her lifetime she obtained the only thing then lacking—a royal permission diploma.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quinto collari impenduntor 600 Salutationes, &lt;b6&gt;Salve&lt;/b6&gt;, jejunia (ut supra) in reverentiam felicissimi gaudii, dum amissum triduo Filium invenit in templo inter doctores disputantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054624Z">
        <seg>On the fifth step they offer 600 greetings, "Hail," and fasts (as above) in reverence of the most blessed joy, when she found her Son, who had been missing for three days, in the temple among the doctors disputing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis credet videri denique potuisse dignum hujuscemodi pratum, in quo Rosa quiesceret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042620Z">
        <seg>Who would believe that it was finally possible to see a meadow worthy of this kind, in which Rosa could rest?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis enim perciperet laxari in hunc clamorem poros, nisi cum sudant?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053923Z">
        <seg>For who would perceive their pores opening to this clamor unless they were sweating?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis porro netrici inter hæc solatia fuerit sensus lætitiæ, æstiment, quibus vacat: plus miror, tunc Rosæ oculos superfuisse, quibus filum, acum, telam videret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050929Z">
        <seg>Let those who have the leisure judge what sense of joy the midwife might have had amid these consolations: I am more amazed that Rosa's eyes were still intact, able to see the thread, the needle, the web.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis pudor!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053046Z">
        <seg>What a shame!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quis tot tamque seria exspectasset e ludo ac luto?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035012Z">
        <seg>Who would have expected so many serious things from play and mud?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quo animo id acceperit Filia pœnitentiæ, quam vitæ studiosior, non facile dixerim;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042855Z">
        <seg>I could hardly say what spirit the Daughter of Penance, more eager for life, received it in.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod Rosa bibendi vices in triduum aut quatriduum differre sit solita, &lt;s2&gt;[Ceterum minimos matris nutus observans,]&lt;/s2&gt; ex eo patuit, quod legem sibimet indixerat, dum parentibus cohabitabat, numquam bibere, nisi quoties veniam rogata mater expresse annuisset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035725Z">
        <seg>That Rosa was accustomed to postpone her drinking for three or four days, [nevertheless observing her mother's slightest wishes,] was evident from the fact that she had imposed a rule upon herself, while living with her parents, to never drink unless her mother, when asked for permission, had expressly consented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod Rosa bibendi vices in triduum aut quatriduum differre sit solita, [Ceterum minimos matris nutus observans,] ex eo patuit, quod legem sibimet indixerat, dum parentibus cohabitabat, numquam bibere, nisi quoties veniam rogata mater expresse annuisset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000915Z">
        <seg>That Rosa was accustomed to postpone her drinking for three or four days at a time [nevertheless observing her mother's slightest wishes], was evident from the fact that she had imposed a law upon herself, while living with her parents, to never drink unless her mother, when asked for permission, had expressly consented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod autem idonea careret dote, replicabat quæstor, se curam hanc totam sibi deposcere, acturum se omnia, ut a religiosissimo monasterio haud gravate admitteretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040255Z">
        <seg>But when he lacked the necessary endowment, the treasurer would reply that he was entrusting this entire matter to him and would do everything to secure his admission to the most devout monastery without difficulty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod hoc miræ immunitatis privilegium secessus ille solitarius e cælo possederit, bene est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043937Z">
        <seg>That this marvelous privilege of immunity was possessed by that solitary retreat from heaven, is well;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si Mariana conabatur parcere, aut, non nisi obiter, imperata exsequi, acrius instabat supplex Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040515Z">
        <seg>But if Mariana tried to be sparing, or, only in passing, to carry out the orders, Rosa, as a supplicant, pressed her more sharply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si aliquando æger ventriculus buccellam exosam nulla vi potuisset eluctari, paroxysmus&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; erat in foribus, ac nauseantem ardentissima febris invadebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>If ever the sick stomach could not by any force rid itself of the loathsome morsel, a paroxysm was at the door, and the most burning fever would seize the one who was nauseous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si aliquando æger ventriculus buccellam exosam nulla vi potuisset eluctari, paroxysmus&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; erat in foribus, ac nauseantem ardentissima febris invadebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041039Z">
        <seg>If ever the sick stomach could not by any force rid itself of the loathsome morsel, a paroxysm was at the door, and the most burning fever would seize the one who was nauseous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si hac ratione crucis figuræ prospectum cupis, utique ingentem tibi sumpsisti operam, imo ridiculam, cui a prætereuntium sannis ac sarcasmis totum sit præmium”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054909Z">
        <seg>If, by this method, you desire a view of the figure of the cross, you have certainly undertaken a tremendous—nay, a ridiculous—labor, the sole reward of which is the jeers and sarcasm of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si in aliena manu tanta potuit nuptialis Rosæ anulus, ipsæmet nuptiæ quid non potuerint in Sponsæ anima?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044715Z">
        <seg>If in another's hand the bridal ring of Rose could do so much, what could not the marriage itself do in the soul of the Bride?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod si ob gratiam nuperrimi miraculi pictura illa hinc auferenda est, totum simul oratorium cum reliquis ejusdem sanctis iconibus auferant necesse est, quia pleræque miris in nos beneficiis certant, scatentque arcanis prodigiis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054103Z">
        <seg>"If, because of the favor of the most recent miracle, that painting must be removed from here, then the entire oratory, together with the other holy icons, must also be taken away, for they all compete in bestowing wondrous benefits upon us and abound with secret prodigies."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod ubi advertit Rosa, “Ne (inquit) metuas, soror;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043925Z">
        <seg>When Rosa turned and saw it, she said, "Do not be afraid, sister;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod ubi certius rescivit, ante omnia sategit explorare divinæ voluntatis beneplacitum, suæque vocationis mysterium supremo Arbitro cernua commendavit, non recusans pro asylo suæ virginitatis, &amp; coævo sibi strictioris vitæ desiderio arctissimam oblati cœnobii clausuram, arduumque vivendi genus amplecti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040032Z">
        <seg>When she learned this for certain, she first and foremost sought to discern the divine will's good pleasure, and she commended the mystery of her vocation to the supreme Arbiter, not refusing, for the sake of the asylum of her virginity and her desire for a stricter life, to embrace the most strict enclosure of the offered convent and the arduous way of living.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quod vero conjugum sit relinquere patrem &amp; matrem, ut sint duo in carne una, monuit Rosam suam Neonymphus, alendorum parentum curam posthac ipsa deponeret, aliunde se illis provisurum, ne quo egeant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044157Z">
        <seg>And Neonymphe, admonishing his wife Rosa that it is proper for a married couple to leave father and mother and become two in one flesh, told her to henceforth lay aside the care of supporting her parents, saying that he would otherwise provide for them so that they would lack for nothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodque admirere magis, a prima pueritia hoc insigni munere dotata fuit, quo sibi &amp; aliis mirifice profuit, ut nulla umquam Virgini occurrerit tam fallax adversarii tentatio, nulla tam fraudulenta seductoris vafricies, quam non clare, statimque primo in limine deprehenderit, arguerit, exarmarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052234Z">
        <seg>What is even more admirable is that from early childhood she was endowed with this remarkable gift, by which she benefited herself and others so wonderfully that she never encountered a Virgin who faced a more deceitful temptation from an adversary, nor a more fraudulent cunning from a seducer, than she did not at once and clearly perceive, expose, and disarm at the very threshold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodque mirabilius est, ab eo tempore, quoad Rosa supervixit, ejus hortulo nulla anni parte recentia caryophylla defuerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055213Z">
        <seg>What is even more remarkable is that, for as long as Rosa lived, her garden never lacked fresh carnations at any time of the year.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quodque sat mirabile videbatur, inter hos impolitos saxorum cumulos &amp; pulverulentas operas, vestis erat singulis virginibus, non plebeia ex more opificii, sed pretiosa, fulgida, solemnis, quæ nuptiale convivium, aut publicam theatri pompam saperet magis, quam sudoriferam officinam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044216Z">
        <seg>It seemed all the more marvelous that, amid these rough heaps of stones and dusty works, each virgin wore a garment not of plebeian, artisan-made cloth, but a precious, gleaming, and solemn one, more fitting for a wedding feast or a public theatrical pageant than for a sweat-drenched workshop.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quondam post lachrymarum prolixa diluvia capitis exinanitione languebat non sine discrimine, nec tamen solitis moderabatur vigiliis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035706Z">
        <seg>Once, after prolonged floods of tears, he languished from a headache not without danger, nor was he soothed by his usual vigils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoquo se verteret, prævidebat aut virium defectu, aut sumptione cujuscumque fomenti impeditum iri, ne postridie sacra Communione frui liceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051105Z">
        <seg>Whichever way he turned, he foresaw that either a lack of strength or the taking of any nourishment would prevent him from partaking of Holy Communion the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quot, quantaque mysteria, ex unica hac visione didicerit Rosa, particulatim apparebit in sequentibus: hic ad solius cum Lapicida depacti sponsalitii eventum festinat historia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044229Z">
        <seg>How many and what mysteries Rosa learned from this single vision will appear in detail in what follows; here the story hastens to the outcome of the betrothal arranged solely with the Mason.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties Rosa sibi aut aliis grande aliquod ac peculiare e cælo subsidium postulare constituisset, supplex ad aram sanctissimi Rosarii procumbebat in preces, illic vultum charæ imaginis speculabatur attentissima, &amp; non sine ingenti fiducia post teneros affatus, ab illius faciei pendebat oraculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054335Z">
        <seg>Whenever Rosa resolved to ask for some great and special help from heaven for herself or for others, she would fall prostrate at the altar of the Most Holy Rosary in prayer, there she would gaze most attentively at the face of the beloved image, and not without great confidence, after tender words, would hang upon its face for an oracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties a matre præsensit, se invitandam ad visitationis obsequia, clam Indico pipere, quod mordacissimum est, cilia palpebrasque sibi oblinebat, quo quidem affrictu mox ardebant oculi, rubebant, stillabant, velut si acris e capite defluxio eos suffudisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043336Z">
        <seg>Whenever she foresaw that her mother would invite her to a visitation, she would secretly smear her eyelashes and eyelids with Indian pepper, which is the most pungent, so that by this very rubbing her eyes would soon burn, redden, and stream, as if a bitter discharge from her head had suffused them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties aut nimiam in exangui vultu maciem, &lt;s2&gt;[sumendus vilis &amp; modicus cibus,]&lt;/s2&gt; aut vires infra mediocritatem dejectas notabat, unam hanc rigidam castigatissimæ parsimoniæ diætam, instar atrocis delicti, vocabat in crimen, vociferabatur impatiens, ac pene illismet, quibus olim in S. Catharinam Senensem Lapa mater, tonabat verbis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041151Z">
        <seg>Whether he noted an excessive emaciation in a pale face, &lt;s2&gt;[one should take cheap &amp; moderate food,]&lt;/s2&gt; or noted the strength depressed below the mediocre level, he would call this one rigid diet of the most severe frugality, as if it were a heinous crime, to account, crying out impatiently, and almost at those very people who once thundered with words at Mother Lapa of St. Catherine of Siena;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties aut nimiam in exangui vultu maciem, [sumendus vilis &amp; modicus cibus,] aut vires infra mediocritatem dejectas notabat, unam hanc rigidam castigatissimæ parsimoniæ diætam, instar atrocis delicti, vocabat in crimen, vociferabatur impatiens, ac pene illismet, quibus olim in S. Catharinam Senensem Lapa mater, tonabat verbis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203530Z">
        <seg>Whether he noted an excessive emaciation in a pale face, [one should take cheap &amp; moderate food,] or noted the strength depressed below the mediocre level, he would call this one rigid diet of the most severe frugality, as if it were a heinous crime, to account, crying out impatiently, and almost at those very people who once thundered with words at Mother Lapa of St. Catherine of Siena;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties improsperum quidpiam aut domi aut foris accideret, non dubitabat suis id imputare delictis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040543Z">
        <seg>Whenever something unfortunate happened, whether at home or abroad, he did not hesitate to blame it on his own crimes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quoties sibi per diem ad nendi, suendi, texendive labores redeundum erat, numquam e scrinio, semper e manu parentis acum, fila, colum, ceteraque opificio necessaria demisse postulabat, credens id deberi filiali matris reverentiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035551Z">
        <seg>Whenever she had to return during the day to the tasks of spinning, sewing, or weaving, she never took anything from the chest but always, with her hand outstretched, asked her parent for the needle, the threads, the shuttle, and all the other necessities of the craft, believing that such things were owed to a daughter's reverence for her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quotiescumque in colloquio, seu proprio seu alieno ore nominabatur sanctissimum altaris Sacramentum, Rosa, capitis totiusque corporis profunda inclinatione, reverentiam testabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060053Z">
        <seg>Whenever, in conversation, whether spoken by her own lips or by someone else's, the most holy Sacrament of the altar was mentioned, Rosa would show her reverence with a deep bow of her head and entire body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quotiescumque ob singularitatem vitæ a fratribus aut matre proscindebatur injuriis, credebat se iis, imo &amp; decuplo gravioribus tam dignam esse, ut etiam ultro, quæ sibi imputabantur ad culpam, soleret in majus attollere, suumque reatum amplificare, ne præter, aut ultra meritum videretur affligi ac despici, quæ tantopere ambiebat ubique succumbere &amp; conculcari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040521Z">
        <seg>Whenever she was assailed with insults by her brothers or mother on account of the singularity of her life, she believed herself to be so worthy of them, nay, and of ten times more grievous ones, that she would even of her own accord magnify the faults imputed to her, and increase her own guilt, lest besides, or be seen as suffering and being despised beyond her deserts, which she so eagerly sought to do everywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quotiescumque sacramentaliter confessura procubuerat ad confessarii pedes, munita crucis signaculo, erumpebat in hæc verba: “Dominus tecum, mi Pater.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053139Z">
        <seg>Whenever she had fallen down to the confessor's feet to make her sacramental confession, fortified by the sign of the cross, she would burst out with these words: "The Lord be with you, my Father."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quousque patieris hominum peccatis te irritari, atque irreverenter haberi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053709Z">
        <seg>How long will you put up with being angered by people's sins and treated with disrespect?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæ sic vulnerabatur ex laudum molestia, qua putas animi jucunditate exceperit contemptum, probra, convicia?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040617Z">
        <seg>How was she wounded by the very praise she believed would be met with delight by her spirit—contempt, slanders, insults?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quædam spiritu ac religione apprime celebris persona flagrabat annoso desiderio, penitius divinæ gratiæ magnalia explorandi, quæ in Rosæ prato celari jam ex toto non poterant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040816Z">
        <seg>A certain person, exceedingly celebrated for her spirit and piety, burned with a long-standing desire to explore more deeply the wondrous works of divine grace, which could no longer be hidden in the Rose's meadow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsitum de modo, &amp; compertum, id superne datum fuisse Virgini, quod spiritu (non secus acsi corporaliter præsens adfuisset) intererat quotidie Missis singulis, quotquot in vicino sancti Spiritus xenodochio legebantur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043733Z">
        <seg>It was inquired into how this was, and it was discovered that it had been granted to the Virgin from above, that by spirit (as if she had been physically present) she was present daily at each Mass, however many were said in the neighboring hospice of the Holy Spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsitum diu in Rosæ hortulo, sicubi forte in caryophyllorum areola, vel noduli apparerent, propediem erupturi in florem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055126Z">
        <seg>Seeking for a long time in Rose's garden, whether by chance in the bed of carnations, or buds would appear, about to burst into a flower;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsitura utique, egone aliam mihi exoptem semitam, præter illam, quam ipsamet gloriosa calcavit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052541Z">
        <seg>I will surely seek another path for myself, apart from that one which the glorious one herself trod?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæsivit doctor, num forte de mystica theologia libros lectitaret, qui aut methodum artemque hujus introversionis docuissent, aut saltem illius naturam, signa, proprietates, effectus dissertando exponerent?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050312Z">
        <seg>The doctor asked whether he had perhaps been reading books on mystical theology that either taught the method and art of this introversion or at least expounded its nature, signs, properties, and effects through dissertations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor Gundisalvus domi suæ bis terve hoc medicamine, non anaretico&lt;a1&gt;c&lt;/a1&gt;, sed plane anachoretico, vidit Rosam convalescere, protestans neutiquam se capere, unde huic jejunio vis nutriendi inesset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>Questor Gundisalvus, at his home, saw Rosa recover from this medicine—not an anaretic one, but a thoroughly ascetic one—and he protested that he could in no way understand how this fast could have the power to nourish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor Gundisalvus domi suæ bis terve hoc medicamine, non anaretico&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, sed plane anachoretico, vidit Rosam convalescere, protestans neutiquam se capere, unde huic jejunio vis nutriendi inesset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041107Z">
        <seg>Questor Gundisalvus, at his home, saw Rosa recover from this medicine—not an anaretic one, but a thoroughly ascetic one—and he protested that he could in no way understand how this fast could have the power to nourish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstor Gundisalvus una cum uxore &amp; liberis aliquando domi suæ clam auscultabat Rosam loco solitario divinos amores piis clamoribus fervidius celebrantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053213Z">
        <seg>Questor Gundisalvus, together with his wife and children, would sometimes secretly listen at home to Rosa, in a secluded spot, more fervently celebrating divine loves with pious cries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstori Gundisalvo, tum communione officii, tum pari virtutis &amp; amicitiæ studio necessarius intimeque familiaris erat Joannes de Tineo Almianza, regiæ (quam vocant) cameræ chartophylax&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, &amp; Quitensis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;g&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; tribunalis supremus circitor&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;h&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, vir pietate ac religione spectabilis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061432Z">
        <seg>To the Quaestor Gundisalvus, bound by both the communion of office and a like zeal for virtue and friendship, was intimately familiar John of Tineo Almienza, chartophylax of the royal (as they call it) chamber, and supreme judge of the Quito tribunal, a man distinguished for his piety and religion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris conjux, ut semel infirmam vidit fatiscere, totoque latere imbecillem ac stupidam vix trahendo spiritui sufficere, graviter hortata est, labenti corpusculo tunc saltem ab iis laboribus tantillum vacationis indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035743Z">
        <seg>The Questor's wife, once she saw her frail form beginning to collapse and her entire side, weak and numb, scarcely able to draw breath, earnestly urged her to grant her failing little body at least a brief respite from those labors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris uxor utraque vice frontem Virginis domum redeuntis tacite observarat, ac diversitatem mirata, quid novæ rei gereretur, interrogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054432Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054432Z">
        <seg>The questor's wife had silently observed the Virgin's brow on both occasions as she returned home, and, marveling at the difference, asked what new matter was afoot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Quæstoris uxor, quasi de Virginis ore verbum rapiens, confestim gossipium attulit, fascias solvi, floccos imponi jubet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053951Z">
        <seg>The Questor's wife, as if snatching a word from the Virgin's mouth, immediately spread the gossip, ordering the bandages to be loosened and the plaitings to be put on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Radix est in verbo Græco&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;προξενέω&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod significat&lt;/e5&gt; conciliatorem contractuum ago.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052305Z">
        <seg>The root is in the Greek word προξενέω, which means to act as a conciliator of contracts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Reclusæ Virginis arctissimæ solitudini magnum id, prodigiosumque fuisse solatium, nemo ibit inficias.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043739Z">
        <seg>No one will deny that it was a great and marvelous consolation for the cloistered Virgin's most strict solitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Recta domum festinavit Euphemia, illic Franciscam suam, depulso periculo, convalescentem invenit, adeo ut postridie, jubente medico, rursus infanti nutritias, ut ante, præbuerit mamillas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055259Z">
        <seg>Euphemia hurried straight home, where she found her Francisca, the danger having passed, recovering so well that the next day, at the doctor's orders, she once again provided the infant with nourishment, as before, by nursing her at the breast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Recusabat interdum austera parens, quod existimaret, Filiam raro sanam ac jugiter valetudinariam, priusquam in aliorum curam se intromitteret, debere charitatis ordinem a semetipsa auspicari, suæque magis, quam alienæ valetudini prospicere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061237Z">
        <seg>Her austere mother would sometimes object, believing that a daughter who was seldom well and constantly ailing, before she undertook the care of others, ought to begin the work of charity with herself and look after her own health more than that of others.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Redibat ad luctam conatuum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045543Z">
        <seg>He returned to the struggle of efforts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Redibat inde protinus ad eam, de qua supra, virilem Servatoris nostri effigiem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054134Z">
        <seg>A manly effigy of our Savior, of whom we have spoken above, returned thence directly to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Religiosus quidam per ardua, &amp; prolixa itinera prælatum ecclesiasticum associaturus, necessitatem suam Virgini aperuit, precum suffragia pro avertendis periculis flagitavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051834Z">
        <seg>A certain religious man, about to accompany a church prelate on arduous and lengthy journeys, revealed his need to the Virgin and begged for prayers to avert dangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Reliquum, quod sibi in illa sacra Communione acciderat, Virgo humilis verecunde subticuit, matrique sat fuit, Rosæ nomen, quod infanti prima imposuerat, cælo placuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034810Z">
        <seg>The humble Virgin modestly kept silent about the rest that had happened to her in that sacred Communion, and it was enough for her mother to know that the name Rose, which she had first given the infant, had pleased heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rem adfero inauditam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045143Z">
        <seg>I bring an unheard-of thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rem tamen indigne tulit avia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034705Z">
        <seg>However, the grandmother took the matter ill.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Renuntiarunt singuli, nonnisi vacuam sese ollam reperisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062226Z">
        <seg>They each reported that they had found the pot empty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit Catharina: “Tu vero, charissima, non meministi, nos heri tecum inutili quæsitione floris totum aliquoties hortum permeasse, nec usquam vel nodulum in stylis, aut dentilum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;f&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; reperisse in scapis, qui huic integro mensi pubem ullius flosculi polliceatur”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055154Z">
        <seg>Catherine replied, "But you, dearest, do not remember that yesterday we went through the entire garden with you several times in that useless search for a flower, nor did we find anywhere so much as a bud on the stems or a tiny tooth on the stalks that would promise the bloom of a single little flower for this whole month."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit mater: Putasne in tota hac domo vel micam chocolatæ reperiri?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051648Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051648Z">
        <seg>The mother replied, "Do you think there's a single chocolate crumb to be found in this whole house?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit mater: “At ego tuis impendi usibus præceperam, solicita, ne quid desit, quando infirmaberis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061051Z">
        <seg>The mother replied, "But I had instructed you to spend on your own needs, making sure that nothing would be lacking when you fall ill."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Replicuit morigera Filia: “ Et nonne diserte vetueras mater, ne illas absque tuo nutu deponerem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035523Z">
        <seg>The obedient daughter replied, "And did you not, mother, wisely forbid me to put them down without your permission?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Reposuit operosa Virgo, se parentum suorum necessitatibus absque scrupulo deesse non posse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035747Z">
        <seg>The industrious virgin replied that she could not, without scruple, fail to attend to her parents' needs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Repræsentabatur coloribus in tela Genitrix Virgo, dormientem parvulum Jesum complexa sinu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054509Z">
        <seg>The Virgin Mother was depicted in colors on canvas, embracing the sleeping little Jesus in her arms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res cum risu excepta ab omnibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062712Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062712Z">
        <seg>The matter was met with laughter by all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita contigit: Lusitabat cum ea natu major germanus frater, isque (casu an studio, incertum) luto imbuit rutilam pulcherrimamque innocentis puellæ cæsariem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034941Z">
        <seg>This is how it happened: her older brother was playing with her, and he (whether by accident or on purpose, it is uncertain) smeared the innocent girl's beautiful, golden hair with mud.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita evenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034721Z">
        <seg>This is how it happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita innotuit: mox a felici Rosæ transitu, persona quædam eximiæ sanctitatis ac nominis in visione imaginaria conspexit inter angelorum myriades gloriosam sanctarum Virginum catervam, recta gradientem ad thronum SS. Trinitatis: in medio agminis fulgebat Rosa palmam manu præferens, niveoque candicans amictu, sed nondum ut reliquæ coronata: siquidem ad divini illius throni crepidinem stabat virgo Deipara, coruscam in dextera coronam tenens, qua Rosam suis ipsa manibus inauguratura, solemnem hanc Virginum processionem exspectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042431Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042431Z">
        <seg>The matter became known in this way: soon after the blessed Rose's passing, a certain person of exceptional holiness and renown saw in an imaginary vision among the myriads of angels a glorious host of holy Virgins, walking straight toward the throne of the Saints. of the Trinity: in the midst of the procession shone Rose, holding a palm in her hand, resplendent in a snow-white robe, but not yet crowned like the others: for at the foot of that divine throne stood the virgin Mother of God, holding a radiant crown in her right hand, with which she was to crown Rose with her own hands, awaiting this solemn procession of virgins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Res ita se habuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040859Z">
        <seg>This is how things were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondebat planta cultricis industriæ, &amp; quo prodibat lætior, magis lætificabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051214Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051214Z">
        <seg>The plant responded to the industrious gardener's care, and the more it grew, the more it delighted him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit Euphemia: “Deambulare me jubes, cum non ignores, quæ me domi meæ exspectet afflictio?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055231Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055231Z">
        <seg>Euphemia replied, "You order me to walk, when you know full well what affliction awaits me at home?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit Virgo, nescire se, quo illa nomine apud talium rerum peritos appelletur, modum tamen locutionis hunc fuisse, quod ab ore Christi promanabat clarus &amp; purissimus sapientiæ conceptus, qui animæ fundum penetrans, significabat intus, quæcumque vellet intelligi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052854Z">
        <seg>The Virgin replied that she did not know by what name she would be called among those skilled in such matters, but that this was the manner of speech: that from the mouth of Christ proceeded a clear and most pure conception of wisdom, which, penetrating to the depths of the soul, signified within whatever it wished to be understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit filia: “Jam aptata est, ut aptari melius non possit, quinimo nunc primum illa decore canduit, ex quo in eleësynam conversa est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061048Z">
        <seg>The daughter replied, "It is already fitted as well as it can be; indeed, it has only now first shone with beauty since it was turned into a charitable gift."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit subridens filia: “Ne mireris, dulcissima mater: nam sæpe hujusmodi officiis me dignatur angelus meus tutelaris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051738Z">
        <seg>The daughter replied with a smile, "Do not be surprised, dearest mother: for my guardian angel often honors me with such duties."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit ægra, se quidem nihilo melius ac ante valere, pelles autem ita, ut mater adstrinxerat, immotas adhuc sibi adhærere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035514Z">
        <seg>She replied in her illness that she was no better than before, and that the bandages, just as her mother had bound them, were still stuck to her without moving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, nulla re creata recreari se posse, in hoc solo &amp; unico omne suum oblectamentum consistere, quod tanta cum certitudine sentiebat in anima sua præsentem Deum, quem si vel ad momentum e conspectu perderet, id sibi acerbius omni jactura, dirius omni gehenna videri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050346Z">
        <seg>He replied that he could be refreshed by nothing created; that his sole and only delight consisted in feeling the God who is so certain in his soul; and that if he were to lose Him from his sight, even for a moment, it would seem to him more bitter than any loss, more dreadful than any hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, propria illa differentium visionum nomina sibi quidem adhuc ignota esse, at gloriosam Christi humanitatem exhiberi sibi in transitu quodam vicino &amp; claro, sicut cum fulgidum aliquod sidus moroso tractu procurrit in cælo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050544Z">
        <seg>He replied that the proper names of those different visions were indeed still unknown to him, but that the glorious humanity of Christ was displayed to him in a certain near and clear passage, just as when some bright star, with a sluggish course, runs across the sky.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Respondit, vix se meminisse talium certaminum, ab infantia se magno Dei favore sensisse faciles, &amp; summe connaturales propensiones in virtutem, absque ullo tumultu passionum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050337Z">
        <seg>He replied that he could barely remember such struggles, that from infancy he had felt himself to be the object of God's great favor, and that his inclinations toward virtue were supremely natural, without any turmoil of the passions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Retinuit tamen, quamquam varie tentata, ut alium assumeret: neque enim cessabat humana prudentia, divini ignara consilii, diversa suadere, offerre, ingerere, quasi meliora ac digniora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040242Z">
        <seg>Yet it held back, though sorely tempted to take another: for human prudence, ignorant of the divine counsel, did not cease to urge, offer, and thrust forward various things as if they were better and more worthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Risere, quotquot aderant, revocata subito mortis sententia, plaudebat cantu repetito pullus cum plaudentibus, spatiabatur velut alte præcinctus, colloque arrecto stridulus identidem renovabat domesticorum acclamantium cachinnos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061550Z">
        <seg>All who were present laughed; with the death sentence suddenly revoked, the chick, its song repeated, clapped along with the others, strutted as if proudly belted high, and with its neck held erect, the shrill little one again and again renewed the household's shrieks of delight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Robur ex S. Communione deprehendit in filia mater: nam quotiescumque Rosa sacram Synaxim receptura templum adibat cum parente, ex pridianis jejuniis, flagris, ac vigiliis tam fessa, attenuata ac debilis erat, ut sæpe per viam [in] angiportis hincinde fuerit utrique subsistendum, quoad lassa Virgo recuperaret anhelitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055935Z">
        <seg>The mother perceived the strength drawn from Holy Communion in her daughter: for whenever Rosa went to the church to receive the sacred Communion with her parent, she was so weary, emaciated, and weak from the previous days' fasts, scourges, and vigils that they were often forced to pause in the lanes along the way so that the exhausted Virgin could catch her breath.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Robustus hic fuit aries ad pulsanda Rosæ præcordia, maxime quod ipsa quoque mater, cui res angusta domi, contenta jam erat, pluriumque servorum Dei conspirabant arbitria.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040258Z">
        <seg>He was a mighty ram for smiting Rose's heart, especially since the mother herself, who was already content in her straitened circumstances at home, was also pleased, and the designs of many servants of God were conspiring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Roga;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051626Z">
        <seg>Ask</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rogabat interdum Latinam horum verborum emphasim doctius ac profundius sibi enucleari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061733Z">
        <seg>He would sometimes ask that the emphasis of these words be explained to him more learnedly and profoundly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa angelum suum non modo custodem, sed &amp; congerronem&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;a&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; habuit amicissimum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051558Z">
        <seg>Rosa had her angel not only as a guardian, but also as a most friendly companion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa candidissimi Agni sponsa!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044557Z">
        <seg>Rose, bride of the most pure Lamb!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa deprehensam se videns, simpliciter respondit, illo se uti ad offulam suam inspergendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041225Z">
        <seg>Seeing that she had been discovered, Rosa simply replied that she was using it to sprinkle her doll.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa dilecta!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044551Z">
        <seg>Beloved Rose!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa in præsentem imaginem defixis oculis avide, quæ dicebantur, auscultabat: sed cum matrona sensim ad alia diverteret, insolita verborum emphasi interpellavit Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054524Z">
        <seg>Rosa listened eagerly to what was being said, her eyes fixed on the present image; but when the matron slowly turned to other matters, Rosa interrupted her with an unusual emphasis of words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa miranti similis, “Quid agis (inquit) charissima soror?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043857Z">
        <seg>Like a rose in wonder, she said, "What are you doing, dearest sister?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nec læsa, nec territa, agilior surrexit, quam ceciderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051955Z">
        <seg>Rose, neither hurt nor frightened, rose more agile than she had fallen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nomine florebat &amp; opere: nam ut altaribus nulla anni parte deessent flores, diligentissime procurabat, discretis hunc in usum areolis, quarum uni basilicum (plures ozimum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dicunt) implantarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051208Z">
        <seg>She excelled both in name and in deed: for she made sure that flowers would never be lacking on the altars at any time of the year, carefully providing them by means of small flower beds, one of which she had planted with basil (which many call ozimum).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nondum ultra pueriles annos creverat, jamque hunc manducabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055641Z">
        <seg>Rosa had not yet grown beyond her childhood years, and was already devouring him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa nostra idipsum experiri meruit &amp; in suo: nam quotiescumque hostis tartareus appropinquabat Virgini, suggesturus fœda ac sordida, aliis ad eum procul abigendum catapultis opus non fuit, quam quod vigil Rosa ter digito pulsaret suam coronam, in SS. Triadis reverentiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042341Z">
        <seg>Our Rose herself deserved to experience it: for whenever the Tartar foe drew near to the Virgin, about to utter foul and filthy words, no other catapult was needed to drive him away than the Rose's vigil, who thrice touched her crown with her finger in reverence to the Holy Trinity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa patiendi assuetudine ac desiderio fortis, hæc quasi de alieno brachio dicta tranquille excipiebat: nihilominus die quadam familiariter cum Gundisalvi uxore de prodigio sudantis imaginis conferens, repente se sensit exciri ad fiduciam recuperandæ sanitatis, modo gossipium, quod prodigioso imaginis sudore maduerat, contusis brachii sui nervis ac musculis applicaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053948Z">
        <seg>Rosa, strong in habit of suffering and in desire, calmly received these words, as if spoken of another's arm. Nevertheless, one day, while familiarly discussing with Gundisalvi's wife the miracle of the sweating image, she suddenly felt herself drawn to the hope of regaining her health, provided that the gossamer which had been drenched by the image's miraculous sweat were applied to the crushed nerves and muscles of her arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa periculi admonita, quo tum sancti Viri celebre nomen, tum marcentis Limæ nutabat tranquillitas, mœsta indoluit, noctemque illam horrisonis flagris in sua carne depurpuravit, ut proprii sanguinis hostimento sopiret communem populi sui turbationem, quæ cordatis periculosior quocumque terræ motu videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041553Z">
        <seg>Rose, warned of the danger that threatened both the renowned name of the holy Man and the tranquility of a withering Lima, mourned sorrowfully and imprinted that night's horrid scourges upon her own flesh, so that by the sacrifice of her own blood she might soothe the common turmoil of her people, which to the thoughtful seemed more perilous than any earthquake.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa pro compassionis affectu diligentius pelvim, qua coagulus sanguis asportabatur, intuita, sensit ab imo fundo sibi evert i stomachum, alterari in faucibus salivam, totisque se visceribus in vomitum propendere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061349Z">
        <seg>Rosa, moved by compassion, gazed more intently at the basin in which the blood clot was being carried away, and felt her stomach turn from deep within, her saliva alter in her throat, and her entire being incline toward vomiting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa præelecta!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044554Z">
        <seg>Rose is chosen!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tametsi vocationis suæ tenax &amp; certa, noluit tamen aut cerebrosa videri, aut palam tot peritissimorum benevola judicia immodesto supercilio aspernari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040301Z">
        <seg>Rosa, though tenacious and certain of her calling, nevertheless refused to appear conceited or to openly scorn the favorable judgments of so many experts with an immodest air.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tot jam edocta malis, attamen urgenti conabatur obtemperare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041113Z">
        <seg>Rose, already so well-versed in evil, nevertheless tried to comply with the urgent request.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tristi nuntio exterrita, post brevem deliberationem respondit: “De Dei honore &amp; obsequio hic agitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053501Z">
        <seg>Rose, startled by the sad news, after a brief deliberation replied: "It is a matter of honor and obedience to God."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa tunc in oratione defixa venientibus innuit, hortum adirent, atque in nomine sanctissimæ Trinitatis tria inde caryophylla confestim sibi adferrent, sacræ iconi coaptanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055151Z">
        <seg>Rosa then, with her eyes fixed in prayer, beckoned to those who were coming to approach the garden and, in the name of the Most Holy Trinity, to bring her three carnations from there at once to be attached to the sacred icon.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa virgo, ex tertio Ordine S. Dominici, Limæ in Peruvia, Americæ provincia (S.)</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211740Z">
        <seg>Rose, virgin, of the Third Order of St. Dominic, of Lima in Peru, Province of the Americas (S.)</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, conspecto hoc suo vespertino choraule, sese &amp; ipsa parabat ad laudes Deo modulandas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045224Z">
        <seg>Rose, upon seeing this evening choir, herself prepared to sing praises to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, cui nec per somnium ulla umquam placuerat nuptiarum cogitatio, sentiebat intus, uno isto conjugio nihil felicius in tota sibi vita posse obtingere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044148Z">
        <seg>Rosa, to whom the very thought of marriage had never once been pleasing, even in her dreams, felt deep down that nothing more fortunate could come into her entire life than that one marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, disploso aliorum metu, cœpit verecundari ipsa de brevitate truncatarum vestium, &amp; ut decenter rediret domum, necesse fuit in prædicto sacello noctem expectare cum dolore erepti martyrii, quamquam gratularetur patriæ liberatæ, ac præcipue inviolatæ templorum religioni.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060301Z">
        <seg>Rosa, her fear of others dispelled, began to feel self-conscious about the brevity of her truncated garments, and in order to return home decently, she had to wait out the night in the aforementioned chapel with the pain of her martyrdom being snatched away, although she rejoiced in her liberated homeland and especially in the inviolable sanctity of the temples.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, dum exesæ cartilaginis pars vitiata præscinditur, ea vultus animique firmitate vulnus excepit, qua nec intueri adstantes poterant sulcantem novaculam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034854Z">
        <seg>Rosa, while the damaged part of her cartilage was being cut away, bore the wound with such strength of face and spirit that even those standing by could not look at the razor cleaving it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, quod putaret in hoc genere se pauca, &amp; admodum vulgaria præstare, obiter de suis jejuniis, ciliciis, disciplinis, &amp; reliquis austeritatibus nonnulla edixit, absente matre, quodque in his non suo, sed confessariorum arbitratu modum ac mensuram constituisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050615Z">
        <seg>Rosa, thinking that in this regard she was doing little and very ordinary things, on the side issued some edicts about her fasts, hairshirts, disciplines, and other austerities in her mother's absence, and that in these she had established the measure and limit not by her own judgment, but by that of her confessors.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, ut prodigium, quod viderat, non suo, sed matronæ merito tribueret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054537Z">
        <seg>Rose, attributing the prodigy she had seen not to her own merit, but to that of the matron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, vel admirata munus tam copiosum &amp; affluens, vel ut humilius parenti gratias ageret, “Mihine (inquit) omnem hanc lini portionem donas mater?” Respondit illa: “Tibi, filia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061035Z">
        <seg>Rosa, either admiring the generous gift or, more humbly, to thank her mother, said, "Are you giving me this entire portion of linen, mother?" She replied, "To you, my daughter."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosa, velut si Luciæ cor penitius inspexisset, eamdem familiarius, quam pridie complexa, nil de marito, nil de filiola filiisve tunc respondit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062926Z">
        <seg>Rosa, as if she had looked more deeply into Lucia's heart, answered her more familiarly than the day before, saying nothing about her husband, nor about her little daughter or sons.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam Catharinæ discipulam idem certamen exercuit, eadem victoria cohonestavit: nam &amp; ipsa somnum suum intra duarum horarum angustias coërcuit, &amp; aliquando etiam minus spatii indulsit importuno exactori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043023Z">
        <seg>He put the same test on his pupil Rosa Catharina and honored her with the same victory: for she too confined her sleep within the narrow limits of two hours, and at times even granted the importunate exactor less time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam ceu vanam prophetidem cum dedecore propediem vulgi fabulam fore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062811Z">
        <seg>That Rosam, like a vain prophetess, will soon be a disgrace and a public spectacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosam ingenti solatio perfudit hæc visio, quod oblique per eam intelligeret, suo præcipue labori ac merito hoc donari a Deo, quod Lima tali aliquando sacrarum virginum asceterio, velut monili pretioso, erat condecoranda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062607Z">
        <seg>This vision filled Rosa with immense consolation, for she understood by it, especially in regard to her own labor and merit, that God was granting that Lima, at some future time, would be adorned with such an astery of holy virgins as a precious jewel.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ permagno stetere hæc prælia: nam, sive genibus humi fixis inchoabat preces, videbatur inesse plumbum gravatis palpebris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043035Z">
        <seg>These contests were a great trial for the roses: for whenever she began her prayers with her knees fixed to the ground, it seemed as if lead had been placed in her heavy eyelids.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rosæ videbantur in hac imagine omnia spirare, ac vivere, sæpiusque fatebatur, hanc apud se peculiariter magno in pretio esse, quod inde suavissimis interdum solatiis fuerat delibuta;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054512Z">
        <seg>In this image, the roses seemed to breathe and live, and she often confessed that she held this one in particularly high esteem, because from it she had at times been treated to the sweetest consolations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudimenta hæc erant severioris militiæ, quam ætas maturior, ut promittebat animo, sic corpori minabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041640Z">
        <seg>These were the rudiments of a more severe discipline, which threatened the body as much as the more mature age threatened the mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudis Juvencula hæsit ad PRÆDESTINATIONIS scholasticum vocabulum, &lt;s2&gt;[sicut doctori Castillo indicavit,]&lt;/s2&gt; cujus propriam vim, ac etymon haud satis assequebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061823Z">
        <seg>The uneducated young woman was stuck on the scholastic term PREDESTINATION, [as she indicated to Doctor Castillo,] whose true meaning and etymon she did not fully grasp.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rudis ista rhetorica est in auribus seculi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053120Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053120Z">
        <seg>That rhetoric is crude in the ears of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rupem dixissos adnatam solo, aut ingentem plumbi massam, nulla trochlearum vi attollendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040124Z">
        <seg>A rock you would call firmly fixed to the ground, or a huge mass of lead, to be lifted by no power of pulleys.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Rursus igitur chirurgi manu, ac post lene emplastrum, denique &amp; ferro opus fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034851Z">
        <seg>Thus, once again, by the surgeon's hand, and after a gentle plaster, it was finally necessary to use iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>S. Catharinam Senensem, cui modo ipsa famulata erat, famulæ suæ discrimini minime defuturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055256Z">
        <seg>Saint Catherine of Siena, to whom she herself had just been a servant, would by no means fail her servant in her hour of need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Saccum villis crassissimis gravem sibimet in formam indusii aptaverat, &amp; ne posset deprehendi pilosa ruditas, extremis manicis carbasina integumenta obduxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041857Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041857Z">
        <seg>He had fashioned a sack of the thickest wool into a tunic, and so that its hairy coarseness could not be detected, he had covered the cuffs of the sleeves with carbasin cloth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacrista ex pondere suspicatus aureos inesse torques, aut pretiosa monilia, digressus a Virgine explicuit involucrum, ac dissimilium auro thesaurum reperit, catenam recenti imbutam sanguine, quam frustillatim adhærescens caro &amp; pellicula variis locis conspicue satis interpunxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041801Z">
        <seg>The sacristan, suspicious from the weight that it contained gold torcs or precious jewels, stepped away from the Virgin, unfolded the wrapping, and discovered a treasure of miscellaneous gold—a chain freshly stained with blood, the flesh and membrane clinging to it in places, quite clearly interspersed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sacrista prodigii admonitus venit, vidit, adstupuit, certus, nec se nec suorum ullum attigisse coronam a die, qua collo divæ Virginis circumducta fuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043550Z">
        <seg>The sacristan, warned of the miracle, came, saw, and was amazed, certain that neither he nor any of his own had touched the crown since the day it had been placed around the neck of the holy Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Saltem optabat ex illo, cui incumbebat, humaniori stratu, humi deponi, ac nudo in pavimento mori.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043001Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043001Z">
        <seg>At least he wished to be laid on the more humane layer of the couch he was leaning against, and to die naked on the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sanctus Thomas 2.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050829Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050829Z">
        <seg>Saint Thomas 2.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane Rosam verecundæ taciturnitatis amantissimam solus animarum zelus disertam fecerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060738Z">
        <seg>Indeed, it was only the zeal of souls that had made the shy and taciturn Rosa eloquent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane apud omnes (quotquot tunc Limæ vivebant) multorum opinione sanctimoniæ &amp; perfectionis fama celebres, ea sensim invaluerat constantissima opinio, Rosam spiritu Dei agere &amp; agi, dono sapientiæ abundare, scientia cælitus infusa gubernari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050708Z">
        <seg>Indeed, among all who then lived in Lima—celebrated by many for their sanctity and perfection—a most steadfast opinion had gradually prevailed: that Rosa was acted upon and carried along by the Spirit of God, abounded in the gift of wisdom, and was guided by knowledge poured down from heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane per ipsos decem &amp; sex annos in hujus agonis acerbissimo tormento invicta perseveravit, &amp; (quod mireris amplius) conscia demum atque invita, sed tamen connivente matre.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042703Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042703Z">
        <seg>Indeed, for sixteen years she remained unconquered in the most bitter torment of this ordeal, and (what will surprise you even more) only at the end did she become aware of it and unwilling, though her mother looked the other way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane quam corpori suo Rosa amica non fuerit, supremum hoc paradigma esto, quod Virgo cautissima nec delicias spiritus usque ad corporis solatium exundare sinebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041935Z">
        <seg>Indeed, if Rosa was not a friend to her own body, let this be the ultimate example: that the most cautious virgin did not allow the delights of the spirit to overflow into the solace of the body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane videbatur eum unctio docuisse de omnibus, ut neque errate posset in Rosæ nomine, cui exinde pariter &amp; avia adquievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034714Z">
        <seg>Indeed, it seemed that the anointing had taught him everything, so that he could not err in the name of Rose, to whom from then on his grandmother also consented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sane vim narrationis transcendunt, quæ de Rosæ jejuniis jejune satis innotuerunt: nam (ut diximus) latent plurima.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040958Z">
        <seg>Indeed, they surpass the force of a narrative that has become rather dryly familiar through the bare accounts of Rose's fasts; for (as we have said) many things lie hidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sat esto, quod Rosæ ea orationis vis fuerit, qua volucres cæli, qua terræ germina sibi attraxit in alternam adorandi societatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045351Z">
        <seg>Let it suffice that for Rose the power of that prayer was such that she drew to herself the birds of the air and the earth's seedlings into a reciprocal fellowship of worship.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Satis est, imo plusquam satis: nam tibi &amp; lanam in lanienam scivisti convertere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042834Z">
        <seg>It is enough, indeed more than enough: for you have known how to turn wool into a slaughterhouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scatebant his undique parietes, personabat ostiolum, implebatur fenestra perpetuo commeantium refluxu;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043757Z">
        <seg>The walls were dripping with them from every side, the doorway echoed, the window was filled by the constant backwash of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sciebant ambæ, quidquid Rosa a B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052547Z">
        <seg>Both of them knew whatever Rosa had from B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scio, hæc retrimenta sine scrupulo aut culpa promiscue calcari ab aliis, horum incuriam nec damnare ausim, nec arguere præsumo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054918Z">
        <seg>I know that these shortcomings are trampled by others without scruple or guilt; I neither dare condemn their carelessness nor presume to reproach them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Scripsit (jubente Didaco Alvarezio) Castillus iste nobilem tractatum de iis, quæ sibi inter contemplationum radios ac lumina occurrerunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050104Z">
        <seg>At the behest of Diego Alvarez, this Castille wrote a noble treatise on those things that occurred to him amid the rays and lights of contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Secundo pannum hujus amictus contexunto 600 Salutationes angelicæ, totidem vicibus &lt;b4&gt;Salve Regina&lt;/b4&gt;, 15 Rosaria, &amp; 15 dies jejunii, in reverentiam lætissimi gaudii, dum visitabat cognatam suam Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054611Z">
        <seg>Secondly, let 600 Angelic Salutations, as many Hail Marys, 15 Rosaries, and 15 days of fasting be woven into the cloth of this vestment, in reverence of the most joyful gladness, when she visited her kinswoman Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed Dominica Palmarum, quam Galli vulgo&lt;/e1&gt; Paque-fleurie &lt;e2&gt;vocant, ab Hispanis&lt;/e2&gt; Pascua florida &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; Domingo de Ramos &lt;e4&gt;nominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T233133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035155Z">
        <seg>But Palm Sunday, which the French commonly call&lt;/e1&gt; Paque-fleurie, &lt;e2&gt;is called by the Spaniards&lt;/e2&gt; Pascua florida &lt;e3&gt;or&lt;/e3&gt; Domingo de Ramos&lt;e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed Virgo memor propositi, ac suæ pulchritudinis obtundendæ, quam acuendæ, solicitior, hæc omnia defixis humi oculis excipiebat invicto silentio, &amp; quotidianam hanc cramben usu ac dissimulatione surda concoquere assuescebat, quoadusque ultima materni præcepti violentia deerat, cui refragari aut lentius parere æquale nefas putabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035418Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin, mindful of her purpose, and more anxious to blunt her own beauty than to sharpen it, she received all this with fixed eyes on the ground in unconquerable silence, and grew accustomed to digest this daily drudgery with habitual use and deaf dissimulation, until the final violence of her mother's command was at hand, against which to rebel or to obey more slowly she deemed equally forbidden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ad alia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054503Z">
        <seg>But to other matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ad graviora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061609Z">
        <seg>But to graver matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed animosa Virgo, ut comperit anxium Gundisalvum hac formidine cruciari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054053Z">
        <seg>But the spirited Virgin, upon learning that the anxious Gundisalvus was being tormented by this fear;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed confestim Sponsus gloriosus Virgini se stitit conspicuum, dixitque: “Audi Rosa, ni tecum ego fuissem, tunc victoriam reportasset”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052218Z">
        <seg>But at once the glorious Spouse appeared to the Virgin, and said, "Hear, Rose, if I had not been with you, then victory would have been won."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed cordata Filia, ut metum eximeret, blande modesteque detexit secretum, nempe merum hunc Sponsi ludum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051043Z">
        <seg>But the prudent daughter, to dispel the fear, gently and modestly revealed the secret, namely that this was merely a game of the suitor's.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed diversissimum erat, quod reipsa gerebatur: nam herbæ, quas sibi Virgo in pastum singulari cura selegerat, amarissimæ erant, &amp; agresti saporis malignitate torquebant palatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041209Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041209Z">
        <seg>But what was actually happening was quite different: for the herbs the Virgin had selected with special care for her own nourishment were exceedingly bitter, and their rustic, malignant flavor tormented the palate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed divinæ buccellæ refecta Pabulo, cum a templo redibat domum, præibat matrem, &amp; hortabatur sequi, velut si in fortitudine Cibi illius usque ad montem Horeb&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; fuisset ambulandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055938Z">
        <seg>But having been refilled with the divine morsel, Paul, as he was returning home from the temple, outpaced his mother and urged her to follow, as if, by the strength of that Food, they were to walk all the way to Mount Horeb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed en arcanum cælestis providentiæ obstaculum a matre fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040038Z">
        <seg>But behold, the mother was an obstacle to the secret of divine providence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed evoluta septimana, ac superveniente totidem recentium panum demenso, inveniebatur, Rosam ex octo panibus permodicis, unum dumtaxat cum dimidio per totam illam hebdomadam comedisse, restitutis nimirum sex reliquis cum dimidio, qui in penu superfuerant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041327Z">
        <seg>But at the end of the week, with an equal number of fresh loaves having arrived, it was found that Rosa had eaten only one and a half of the eight very small loaves over the entire week, and had returned the remaining six and a half that had been left in the pantry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed exemit solicitudinem Petro secura Rosa, dixitque cum defuncti anima feliciter ac bene agi, jam illam regioni lucis adscriptam frui perenni refrigerio, ut ei nequaquam opus fuerit redire ad alterutrum, pro stipe suffragiorum emendicanda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060856Z">
        <seg>But the secure Rose dispelled Peter's anxiety and said that the deceased's soul was doing well and happily, already enrolled in the region of light and enjoying perpetual refreshment, so that it would by no means need to return to either place to beg for the alms of suffrages.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed hoc loco maxime mihi placent sequentia auctoris verba:&lt;/e0&gt; Hanc nectaream sanguinis &amp; aquæ mammillam non ori, sed cordi Virginis benigne applicuit &lt;e1&gt;Christus, de quo sermo est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051524Z">
        <seg>But here I especially like the author's following words: "This nectarous nipple of blood and water Christ, of whom we speak, kindly applied not to the mouth but to the heart of the Virgin."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed ignoramus, an truncus hujus plantæ tam magnus sit, ut ad hunc usum adhiberi possit.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043159Z">
        <seg>But we do not know whether the trunk of this plant is large enough to be used for this purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed in vacuo animæ exinanitæ, respondebat nemo, nec echo quidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045540Z">
        <seg>But in the void of the emptied soul, no one answered, not even an echo.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed inde ad quemlibet non est extendenda consequentia, cum Latius capite mox citato sic moneat:&lt;/e0&gt; Immodicus usus potionis e cacao confectæ plurimarum infirmitatum &amp; morborum causa est, quia generat obstructiones, corrumpit colorem, &amp; inducit cacochymiam &amp; similes pravos affectus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052401Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052401Z">
        <seg>But from that one cannot draw a conclusion for everyone, since Latius, in the chapter soon to be cited, warns as follows: "Excessive use of a potion made from cacao is the cause of many infirmities and diseases, because it produces obstructions, corrupts the complexion, and induces cacochymia and similar perverse affections."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed magis dolebat Rosa, quod illo die, qui sibi anniversarius erat assumpti Dominicani habitus, prohiberetur seraphicæ matris suæ festivum habitum solito religionis ministerio contrectare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055355Z">
        <seg>But Rosa was even more distressed that on the day which was the anniversary of the Dominican's assumption she was forbidden to handle the festive habit of her seraphic mother in the usual religious service.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed mirum est, hæc Rosæ nequaquam fuisse mira, utpote cui a beata Catharina Senensi ceu matre, pene quotidiana erant ex mutuis commerciis solatia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055501Z">
        <seg>But it is strange that these things were by no means remarkable to Rose, for she had almost daily consolations from the blessed Catherine of Siena, as from a mother, through their mutual communications.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed neque tunc Rosæ defuit par dolori tam moroso constantia, quantam ab illa imo ab ulla ætate sperari posse chirurgus diffidebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034919Z">
        <seg>But even then, Rose did not lack the steadfastness to endure such a stubborn pain, a quality the surgeon doubted could be expected from her or indeed from anyone at her age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed parens, matronis, quam Filiæ complacendi studiosior, jubebat hanc omnino carnibus vesci, ne inurbana singularitate adstrictioris parsimoniæ, videretur mensæ honorem profanare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041033Z">
        <seg>But the mother, a matron more eager to please her daughter, ordered her to eat nothing but meat, so that she would not appear to be profaning the honor of the table with an uncouth singularity of parsimony.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed paulo post aliud successit ex religiosæ personæ liberali dono, quod Rosam undequaque contentam reddidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041835Z">
        <seg>But shortly afterward, another thing happened thanks to the generous gift of a religious person, which made Rosa content in every way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed pergamus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040620Z">
        <seg>But let's go on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed plura sentiebat in se Rosa, quam verba ista exprimerent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045816Z">
        <seg>But Rosa felt more within herself than those words could express.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed quinam hi effectus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050603Z">
        <seg>But what are these effects?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed rudimenta hæc erant ac tempestiva præludia animi corpusculum proprium ceu alienum tractaturi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034928Z">
        <seg>But these were the rudiments and timely preludes of a mind about to treat its own little body as if it were another's.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed simul timorem exemit superna irradiatio, Rosam erudiens, tempus fore, quo selectum illud germen floridæ propaginis colligatur in unum S. Catharinæ Senensis hortum conclusum, septoque proprio segregetur a profani seculi commercio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062557Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062557Z">
        <seg>But at the same time, a heavenly radiance dispelled the fear, instructing Rosa that the time would come when that chosen germ of the flourishing stock would be gathered into one enclosed garden of St. Catherine of Siena, and separated by its own fence from the commerce of the profane world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed tandem Numinis providentia rem diutius occultari non sivit, effecitque, ut tantæ virtutis exemplum, in plurium opportunam notitiam promanaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042220Z">
        <seg>But at last, by the providence of the Deity, the matter could no longer be hidden, and it brought about that such an example of virtue would come to the attention of many.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed tandem deprehendi, nomen&lt;/e0&gt; dinomilus &lt;e1&gt;compositum esse ex adverbio Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;δὴν&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt; diu, &lt;e4&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;ὁμιλέω&lt;/s5&gt;, &lt;e6&gt;hoc est&lt;/e6&gt;, colloquor &lt;e7&gt;aut&lt;/e7&gt; conversor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052317Z">
        <seg>But I finally realized that the name&lt;/e0&gt; dinomilus is composed of the Greek adverb&lt;/e1&gt; δὴν&lt;/s2&gt; that is&lt;/e3&gt; long, &amp; the verb&lt;/e4&gt; ὁμιλέω&lt;/s5&gt;, that is&lt;/e6&gt;, I converse or&lt;/e7&gt; conversational.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sed, deposito anulo, cessarunt gaudia, cessarunt vincula, surrexit liber e sella, qui paulo ante tenebatur in captivitate lætitiæ nuptialis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044706Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044706Z">
        <seg>But once the ring was removed, the festivities ceased, the bonds were broken, and the freed man rose from his seat, who had just a little while before been held captive in the joyful bonds of marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sedebat labori manuum cum puellis intenta Rosa, cui mater innuit, audiret Vincentium, deque pretio ex more, &amp; æquo coram se convenirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060617Z">
        <seg>Rosa sat intent on the girls' handiwork, when her mother motioned for her to listen to Vincent and to meet him face to face, as was the custom, to discuss the price.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel cujusdam occulta seu arcta necessitas ad virginem relata fuit, sed cum aliud, unde subveniret, ad manum non esset, octo dierum alimoniam (contenta interim solo pane &amp; aqua) sibi detraxit pro egentis subsidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061026Z">
        <seg>Once, when a certain person's hidden or urgent need was reported to the virgin, but since no other help was at hand, she withdrew eight days' worth of food (living in the meantime on only bread and water) to provide relief for the needy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel cum a matre invitaretur ad secum obeunda foris urbanarum salutationum officia, Virgo juxta domesticum furnum transiens, saxum a clibani orificio studiose sibi attraxit in pedem, eoque vulnere &amp; contusione obtinuit, ut non ficte claudicans residere domi sineretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043326Z">
        <seg>Once, when her mother was urging her to accompany her on the customary rounds of urban greetings, the virgin, passing by the domestic furnace, eagerly drew a stone from the furnace's opening onto her foot, and from that wound and contusion she was allowed to remain at home, limping not feignedly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel curiosa parens in horti sepimento vasculum reperit felle vervecino plenum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041219Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041219Z">
        <seg>Once a curious mother found a pot full of goat's bile in the garden fence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel ergo sic abdicato exclusoque mundo muliebri, haud visum Rosæ difficile, in ceteris quamquam arduis parenti obedire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035452Z">
        <seg>Once, then, having thus abdicated and been excluded from the feminine world, it did not seem difficult to Rosa to obey her parents in all other matters, however arduous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semel extra consuetum tempus quid modicum sumere coacta, cum illud stomacho retinere non posset, reddidit, ac velut intemperantiæ rea, seipsam decem dierum rigida inedia damnavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041349Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041349Z">
        <seg>Once, forced to take a small amount outside her usual mealtime, she could not keep it down, so she returned it and, as if guilty of intemperance, condemned herself to a strict ten-day fast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Semianimis in suo illo extremi doloris extremo lectulo dolebat, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; in ea usque ad mortem perseverare cupiens,]&lt;/s2&gt; altero illo se carere lectulo extreme dolorifico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042955Z">
        <seg>Semianimus lay suffering on his deathbed of extreme pain, wishing to persevere in it until death, and deprived of that other, most painful bed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sensit infirmus illomet puncto temporis, quo nuntium acceperat, dolores suos mitigari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061457Z">
        <seg>The sick man felt his pains eased at the very moment he had received the message.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sentiebat &amp; Rosa ex mirifico cælestium sapore ac pergustu suum dulcescere spiritum, inebriari mentem, saginari animum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041944Z">
        <seg>She felt that from the marvelous, heavenly flavor and savor, her spirit was sweetened, her mind intoxicated, her soul enriched.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sentiebat ægra incerti mali certa incrementa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051056Z">
        <seg>She felt the certain growths of an uncertain evil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Serpebat incertus per urbem rumufculus, sacram imaginem de privato oratorio auferendam iri, velut publico ac celebriori loco dignissimam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054038Z">
        <seg>A rumor was spreading uncertainly through the city that the sacred image was to be removed from the private oratory, as though it were most worthy of a public and more celebrated place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Servus erat quæstoris, intromitti petiit, adferre se a quæstoris consorte, quod Rosæ traderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051716Z">
        <seg>He was the quaestor's servant; he asked to be admitted, saying he had come from the quaestor's associate to deliver something to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sexennis, feria quarta, sexta, sabbatho (ni mater aut medicus ad aliud cogerent) omni prorsus abstinebat obsonio, nil præter panem &amp; aquam degustans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041014Z">
        <seg>On Wednesdays, Fridays, and Saturdays (unless his mother or a doctor compelled him otherwise), he abstained entirely from all food, tasting nothing but bread and water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sexto ramusculum Virgineis suis manibus gestandum componunto, 33 orationes Dominicæ, totidem Salutationes angelicæ, cum versu, &lt;b7&gt;Gloria Patri, Salve Regina&lt;/b7&gt;, totidemque rosariis laudum divinarum, &amp; rursus totidem rosariis de laudibus Virginis, in reverentiam 33 annorum, quos Dominus meus Jesus vixit in terris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054627Z">
        <seg>On the sixth, let them prepare a small sprig to be carried in their Virgin's hands, 33 Our Fathers, as many Hail Marys, with the verse, &lt;b7&gt;Glory be to the Father, Hail Mary&lt;/b7&gt;, and as many rosaries of divine praises, and again as many rosaries of the Virgin's praises, in reverence of the 33 years that my Lord Jesus lived on earth."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si cum familiaribus, matronis, virginibus sermocinandum, prima Rosæ vox erat: “Amemus Deum nostrum, amemus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053133Z">
        <seg>When speaking with relatives, matrons, and virgins, Rose's first words were: "Let us love our God, let us love."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si de quacumque persona absente proferendum encomium, rem totam Rosa hoc absolvebat compendio: “Deum fortiter ac serio amat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053136Z">
        <seg>If an encomium was to be offered for any absent person, Rosa would sum up the whole matter with this concise summary: "She loves God with great strength and seriousness."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Si rogabatur, qui valeret: nonnisi bene sibi esse respondebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052643Z">
        <seg>If he was asked how he was, he would answer that he was fine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic Gundisalvi uxor, quandoque pomeridiana hora quarta invenit Rosam eodem geniculatam angulo, quem in puncto meridiei, ad meditationis exercitium occuparat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044907Z">
        <seg>Thus Gundisalvi's wife, at around the fourth hour of the afternoon, found Rosa in the same kneeling corner she had occupied at noon for the exercise of meditation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic Rosa, quod dura sibi quæreret, durissima sufferre debuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042806Z">
        <seg>Thus Rosa, who sought hard things for herself, had to endure the hardest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic Rosa, tota foris, &amp; tota intus, dum exterius satagebat circa frequens ministerium, simul in secretissimo cordis thalamo deliciabatur cum Sponso.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044846Z">
        <seg>Thus Rose, wholly outward and wholly inward, while outwardly she busied herself with frequent ministry, was at the same time delighted with the Spouse in the secretest chamber of her heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic affecta, domum rediit, per viam sic Deum alloquens: “Domine, si ex tuo æterno placito, tuique nominis gloria est, quod ego a seculari isthoc ad religiosum aliquando statum transeam, perge operari in me, quod mirabiliter cœpisti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062948Z">
        <seg>Thus affected, she returned home, speaking to God along the way in this way: "Lord, if it is according to your eternal will, and for the glory of your name, that I should at some time pass from this secular to a religious state, go on working in me what you have so wonderfully begun;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic compertum in examine, de quo infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044811Z">
        <seg>Thus it was established in the examination, about which see below, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic demum a planta pedis usque verticem capitis nil ei residuum fuit, quo voluntarios cruciatus exciperet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041925Z">
        <seg>Thus, from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head, there was nothing left in him to endure voluntary torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic ergo incurabilitatis suæ culpam imputabat totam sibi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045723Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045723Z">
        <seg>Thus, he blamed himself entirely for the fault of his incurability.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic excrescentia cancrosa in naribus appellatur&lt;/e3&gt; polypus &lt;e4&gt;a similitudine animalis, quod hoc nomen habet, ut Sennertus tomo 2 Operum medicorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035238Z">
        <seg>Thus a cancerous growth in the nose is called a polyp because of its resemblance to the animal of the same name, as Sennertus states in volume II of his Medical Works, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic excrescentia cancrosa in naribus appellatur&lt;/e3&gt; polypus &lt;e4&gt;a similitudine animalis, quod hoc nomen habet, ut Sennertus tomo 2 Operum medicorum pag. 347 ex Galeno tradit&lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T030031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T165636Z">
        <seg>Thus a cancerous growth in the nose is called a&lt;/e3&gt; polyp &lt;e4&gt;because of its resemblance to the animal of the same name, as Sennertus states in volume II of his Medical Works, page 347 &lt;/e4&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic hærentem &amp; ultra condictum morosius cunctantem interpellavit frater, eundi tempus esse, longiori orationi commodius in monasterio spatium fore.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040115Z">
        <seg>Thus he interrupted the brother who was lingering and, beyond the appointed time, dawdling morosely, saying that it was time to go and that there would be more convenient space in the monastery for a longer prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic in ecclesiis, ubi aut quadraginta horarum supplicationi, aut alias SS. Eucharistiæ Sacramentum prostabat adorandum, Rosa (quem summo mane insederat) loco velut impetrita, durabat in vesperam, sine refectione, sine motu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044910Z">
        <seg>Thus in the churches, where either the Forty Hours' devotion or otherwise the Sacrament of the Holy Eucharist was exposed for adoration, Rosa (who had been seated there from early morning) would remain in her place as if petrified until evening, without refreshment or movement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic in quæstoris domestico oratorio, cum sese occludebat Rosa a mane feriæ quintæ usque ad Sabbatum (nonnumquam &amp; usque in Dominicam) primo rogabat D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044914Z">
        <seg>Thus, in the domestic oratory of the treasurer, when Rosa shut herself in from the morning of the fifth day of the fast until Saturday (sometimes even until Sunday), she first prayed to the Lord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic nempe Rosa in sentibus configurabat se lilio inter spinas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041826Z">
        <seg>Thus indeed, Rosa, in the thickets, fashioned herself into a lily among thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic nondum didicisti omni hac putredine te viliorem esse ac putridiorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061404Z">
        <seg>Thus you have not yet learned that with all this rot you are both more worthless and more rotten.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic per totam, quæ festo SS. Corporis Christi appendet, solemnem octavam, integros illos dies transigebat coram patulo extantis Sacramenti tabernaculo, mirantibus præcipue confessariis, unde tam extenuato corpusculo vires suppeterent ad genuflexiones tam prolixas, continuas, arduas, cum dierum inedia sustinendas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060034Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060034Z">
        <seg>Thus, throughout the entire solemn octave of the Feast of Corpus Christi, she spent those full days before the open tabernacle of the exposed Sacrament, to the amazement of the confessors, who wondered whence such a frail little body could draw the strength to endure such prolonged, continuous, and arduous genuflections, all the while fasting.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic placide experrecta videbat cominus, &amp; (uti fassa est) facie ad faciem illud gloriæ sidus gratiosum, illam frontem decora majestate plenissimam, illum gloriæ &amp; amœnitatis paradisum, agnoscebat vocem, venerabatur præsentiam, tacite secum disquirens: “Unde hoc mihi, ut Mater Domini mei veniat ad me”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051341Z">
        <seg>Thus, gently awakened, she saw it up close and, as she confessed, face to face—that gracious star of glory, that brow most full of majestic beauty, that paradise of glory and delight—she recognized the voice, she revered the presence, silently questioning herself: "Where did this come from for me, that the Mother of my Lord should come to me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic quæ trepidabat in nomine, firmabatur cognomine, secura &amp; læta, quod utrumque cælitus datum possideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034745Z">
        <seg>Thus she, who trembled at her given name, was steadied by her surname, secure and joyful that she possessed both as gifts from heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic tandem utrimque ex pacto conventum est, quod Rosa huic confessario medietatem cederet omnium fructuum, quos interea ex toto bonorum operum exercitio se concumulaturam sperarat: ac vicissim ille dimidium lucri transcriberet Virgini ex conversione animarum, quas sua prædicatione in viam salutis erat attracturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060434Z">
        <seg>Thus, by mutual agreement, it was finally settled that Rosa would cede to this confessor half of all the fruits she had hoped to accumulate in the meantime through the full exercise of good works; and, in return, he would transfer to the Virgin half of the gain from the conversion of souls he was to lead onto the path of salvation through his preaching.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic videbatur sibi rediisse ad ligneum illud pulvinar, quod pridem sibi mater subtraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042818Z">
        <seg>It seemed to him that he had returned to that wooden pillow which his mother had long ago taken away from him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sic, alternante feminarum bile, patiens Virguncula quoquo se verteret, incidebat in pœnam, &amp; ob hoc ipsum, quod non peccarat, luebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052457Z">
        <seg>Thus, as the women's wrath alternated, the suffering Virgin, wherever she turned, fell into punishment and, for that very reason, because she had done no wrong, suffered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Siccine jejunant Beatæ?” Displicuit matri ineptissimæ censuræ temeraria dicacitas: at mire placuit humili Rosæ, sic sua latere jejunia, etiam usque ad tam ridiculam saginæ ac ingluviei calumniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040803Z">
        <seg>"Do the Blessed fast like this?" The mother was displeased by the rash, inept criticism; but humble Rosa was strangely pleased that her fasts should thus remain hidden, even to the point of such a ridiculous charge of gluttony and gorging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicque providus ac discretus Pater, arrepta lima hinc inde prominentiores coronæ aculeos hebetavit, obtudit, defricuit, si non satis, attamen nec parum hoc temperamento se putans assecutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042325Z">
        <seg>Thus the prudent and discreet Father, seizing a file, smoothed down the more prominent thorns of the crown, dulling, blunting, and scraping them; if not enough, he nonetheless considered himself to have achieved no small measure with this treatment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicubi audisset eminus campanis signum dari ad ejus adorationem, vel sacris ejusdem solemniis tintinnabula præsonare, subitanea intimæ lætitiæ signa cohibere non poterat, quin cor &amp; caro sua exultarent in Deum vivum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060056Z">
        <seg>Whenever he heard the signal of bells from afar given for his adoration, or the little bells ringing at his solemn rites, he could not restrain the sudden signs of inner joy, so that his heart and flesh rejoiced in the living God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicubi in templis, sacellis, ædibus frequens vivificæ crucis simulachrum incurrisset in oculos, singula mœsto obtutu &amp; calente gemitu salutabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054831Z">
        <seg>Whenever in temples, chapels, or houses he happened to come upon the frequent image of the life-giving cross, he greeted it with a sorrowful gaze and a warm sigh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicubi in vicinia aut nota domo rescivisset, quodcumque vile mancipium ex ægritudine decumbere, ac negligentius haberi, sibi confestim illius deposcebat curam, multisque precibus exorabat matrem, annueret, languentem in suas ædes deportari ad curam solicitiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061234Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061234Z">
        <seg>Whenever she learned in the neighborhood or from a familiar household that any humble servant was down with illness and being cared for more negligently, she would at once demand to look after him, and with many entreaties she would persuade her mother to allow the ailing one to be brought to her own home for more attentive care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sicut erat &amp;c&lt;/b5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044951Z">
        <seg>As it was &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simile, at multo operosius vestimentum divæ Virginis consuere cœpit prima die anni MDCXVI.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054642Z">
        <seg>Likewise, but much more laboriously, she began to sew the garment of the Virgin Queen on the first day of the year 1616.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similia de nomine hujus herbæ in Trivultiano linguæ Gallicæ Dictionario legi possunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060952Z">
        <seg>Similar things about the name of this herb can be read in Trivulzio's Dictionary of the French Language.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similiter hæc sunt, &amp; plane germana iis, quæ B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052224Z">
        <seg>Similarly, these are, and are plainly genuine, like those of B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Similiter iisdem fere diebus D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061135Z">
        <seg>Similarly, at about the same days of D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Simillima hæc sunt iis, quæ de S. Catharina prodit vitæ suæ historia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060003Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060003Z">
        <seg>These are very similar to those which appear in the life story of St. Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sinistram cordi apposuerat, contra immodicam palpitationem exundantis lætitiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044607Z">
        <seg>He had placed his left hand over his heart, against the excessive fluttering of overflowing joy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Siquidem tumor ipsi cancri corpus, venæ tumidæ crassæque ejusdem pedes oculis repræsentant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061621Z">
        <seg>For the swelling itself of the cancer, its swollen and thick veins represent the body and its feet to the eye.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Siquidem, ne transiliret, novam a confessario permissionem toties exorabat, quoties aut aliqua proximorum necessitas, aut causa publica videbatur peculiarem ab ea succursum exigere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041606Z">
        <seg>Indeed, so that he would not overstep, he would ask his confessor for a new permission as often as either some need of his relatives or a public cause seemed to demand special assistance from him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sive enim invocata sub Rosæ nomine accurrisset domesticis, aut matri, frendens avia in parvulam virgis sæviebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052451Z">
        <seg>For whether, when called by the name of Rose, she had rushed to the household, or to her mother, the grumbling grandmother would be raging in the little one with her switches.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Rosa conscia miraculi, signique tacita interpres, exultabat arcano jubilo, sat intelligens, quo ista spectarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043553Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone, aware of the miracle and the silent interpreter of the sign, exulted in a secret joy, fully understanding what it all meant.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Rosa impavido gradu fixa perstitit, matremque, ne fuga periculum jam nimis propinquum irritaret, hortata est, una promittens, immanem belluam innoxie transituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062123Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone stood firm with unshaken step, and urged her mother not to provoke the already too-near danger by fleeing, promising together that the immense beast would pass by harmlessly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola Rosa, tantum abfuit, quod sibi metueret, fugamve aut latibulum circumspiceret, ut potius triumphanti similis ingentis lætitiæ signa cohibere nequiverit, rata nimirum adesse felicissimam, totque suspiriis optatam horam, qua posset pro honore prostantis ibidem sanctissimi Sacramenti vitam animamque profundere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060208Z">
        <seg>Rosa alone was so far from being afraid for herself or looking for a way to flee or hide that, on the contrary, she, like one triumphant, could not restrain the signs of immense joy, convinced, of course, that the most blessed and long-sought hour had come, when she could pour out her life and soul for the honor of the Most Holy Sacrament exposed there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola iterum Rosa, oculis in cælum jactis, &amp; illico reductis, exemit trepidis metum, stitit fugam velut minime necessariam affirmans, taurum usque ad rhedam neutiquam procursurum, sed via, qua veniebat, retrocessurum, Dixerat: &amp; respondit eventus, stupentibus universis non magis tauri velocem absque nocumento reditum, quam Virginis animum quovis metu superiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062154Z">
        <seg>Rosa, alone again, with eyes cast up to heaven and immediately brought back down, dispelled the trembling fear, halting the flight as if it were by no means necessary, affirming that the bull would by no means rush toward the chariot, but would turn back the way it had come, She had spoken: and the outcome responded, to the astonishment of all, that the bull's swift return without harm was no more wondrous than the Virgin's spirit, superior to any fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sola temporis &amp; anni, quo construendum erat, circumstantia, ob altiores divinæ sapientiæ fines, Virginem diu latuit, seroque didicit, se illud corporeis oculis non visuram;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062443Z">
        <seg>The Virgin was hidden from the time and year in which it was to be built for a long time, and learned too late that she would not see it with her physical eyes;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Solani vaticinio, terræ hiatu proxime absorbendam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041547Z">
        <seg>By the prophecy of Solanus, to be soon swallowed by an earth-sinkhole;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Solus deerat consensus Virginis, quam voti sui memorem omnis mentio nuptiarum exanimabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040000Z">
        <seg>Only the Virgin's consent was lacking, whom any mention of marriage, mindful of her vow, terrified.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Spatiabantur ambo pariter consertis manibus, gressu gestuque tenere amantium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051155Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051155Z">
        <seg>They strolled together, hand in hand, with the gentle gait and bearing of lovers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sperabat, transitoriam fore debilitatem, sed durabat ultra consuetam periodum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051053Z">
        <seg>He hoped it would be a temporary weakness, but it persisted beyond the usual period.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Statim evanuit metus, abscessit pavor, &amp; Rosa ab oratione domum regressa, instrumentum suum reperit, non ubi reliquerat, sed ubi illud a Matre gratiæ recondi flagitarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040912Z">
        <seg>Fear vanished at once, terror fled, and Rosa, returning home from the sermon, found her instrument not where she had left it, but where the Mother of Grace had insisted it be kept.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Statua est ex ignoto ligno, ad humanæ staturæ proceritatem, affabre sculpta in formosam effigiem magnæ Deiparæ, parvulum Jesum gestantis in ulna, manuque altera mundo Rosarium suum porrigentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054224Z">
        <seg>The statue is crafted from an unknown wood to the height of a human figure, skillfully carved into a beautiful effigy of the Blessed Virgin Mary, holding the infant Jesus in her arms and with her other hand offering her Rosary to the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stetimus utrimque pactis, nec solum communi hoc tecto fruimur sine hostilitate;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043819Z">
        <seg>We have stood by our agreements on both sides, and we enjoy this common shelter without hostility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stomachi, pericardii&lt;s11&gt;&lt;a12&gt;g&lt;/a12&gt;&lt;/s11&gt;, intestinorum dolores, sine lege, sine numero, frequentes convulsiones invehebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052615Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052615Z">
        <seg>Stomach, pericardial, and intestinal pains, without order or number, brought on frequent convulsions;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stragulis, 5 dies esuriales, 5 stationes, totidemque integra rosaria impenduntor pro honore sui Natalis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053558Z">
        <seg>They spend five days of fasting, five stations, and as many entire rosaries in honor of His Nativity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Stupebat ista, nondum assequens, unde idipsum, quod reliquæ juvenculæ ultra modum exambire solent, uni Rosæ tam exosum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043320Z">
        <seg>She was astonished, not yet understanding why that very thing, which the other young women are accustomed to praise excessively, was so distasteful to Rosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suavius, ac forte mirabilius est, quod de hujus coronæ virtute post obitum Virginis compertum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042347Z">
        <seg>More delightful, and perhaps more marvelous, is what was discovered about the virtue of this crown after the Virgin's death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub hoc pondere æstuabat, rigebat, lassabatur extenuata viribus Rosa, incessu non nisi difficillimo gradum promovebat, &amp; sive consederat, sive genua humi fixerat, ægre se attollebat in pedes;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041900Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041900Z">
        <seg>Under this weight, she was burning, stiffening, and weakening from her exhausted strength; she could advance her foot with the greatest difficulty, and whether she had sat down or fixed her knees on the ground, she could scarcely raise herself to her feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sub hoc tormento integros quandoque cum noctibus dies sine cibo transigebat: verum sedato nonnihil cruciatu, mox instabat domesticorum pietas, ut a carnibus jusculum admitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041101Z">
        <seg>Under this torture he would sometimes spend entire days, even with the nights, without food; but once the torment was somewhat eased, his family's piety soon compelled him to take a little broth with his meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subest autem dominio Hispanorum, &amp; dicitur etiam Fanum sancti Joannis de Portu divite.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035136Z">
        <seg>It is, however, under the dominion of the Spaniards, and is also called the Church of Saint John of the Rich Port.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subinde tamen, ne misericordem immisericorditer contristaret, ei indulsit pro domestica sua tenuitate operari, quæ posset, non quanta vellet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061240Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061240Z">
        <seg>However, from time to time, so as not to grieve the merciful one mercilessly, he granted her permission to work according to her domestic frailty, as much as she could, not as much as she wished.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subintrabat continuo æmula vox Rosæ, instaurabat jubilum, magnificabat ineffabiles perfectiones altissimi Numinis, attollebatur urgente spiritu, languescebat facundis suspiriis, demum prærupto suo silentio, antiphonascum suum revocabat ad cantum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045320Z">
        <seg>Rose's rival voice would continually enter, renewing the jubilation and magnifying the ineffable perfections of the Most High. It was lifted up by the urgent spirit, languished with eloquent sighs, and finally, with its abrupt silence, would bring its antiphon back to song.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subjecit Rosa, neutiquam id suo reclusorio imputandum, sed quia mater (quacum sola jusserant confessarii primis annis ecclesiam adire) domesticis impediebatur curis, ne omni die ad Missam exiret, sibi tamen divina bonitate prospectum esse, ut in cellula residens, non unicam, sed complusculas quotidie Missas videret, audiretque.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043729Z">
        <seg>Rosa added that this was by no means to be attributed to her own convent, but because her mother (with whom, in her early years, the confessor had ordered her to attend church alone) was hindered by domestic cares from going to Mass every day, divine goodness had nonetheless provided for her so that, while remaining in her cell, she might see and hear not one but several Masses each day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subjecit Rosa: Non vane conjectasti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051902Z">
        <seg>Rosa said: You did not guess in vain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sublime augurium &amp; observatu dignum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054329Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054329Z">
        <seg>A sublime omen &amp; worthy of observation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suboluit tempestive attentæ matri, quo Filia tenderet: nam pene ab infantia utrumque, &amp; durius cubandi, &amp; austeritatem dissimulandi studium notarat in puella;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042508Z">
        <seg>The attentive mother promptly sensed where her daughter was headed; for she had noticed in the girl, almost from infancy, both a penchant for sleeping on hard beds and a habit of hiding her austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subrisit Virgo &amp; “Nonne (ait) potestatem mihi diserte fecisti utendi, ut vellem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061054Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061054Z">
        <seg>The virgin smiled and said, "Haven't you clearly given me the power to use it as I please?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subrisit Virgo, blandeque subintulit: “Christi bonus odor sumus, dum ministramus infirmis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061317Z">
        <seg>The Virgin smiled, and sweetly added: "We are the good odor of Christ, while we minister to the sick;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subrisit Virgo, matrique &amp; aliis respondit: “Quando huc intravi, cum culicibus amicitiam, pactumque inii, ne umquam me turbarent aut affligerent, vicissim in nullo me ipsis nocituram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043816Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043816Z">
        <seg>The maiden smiled and replied to her mother and the others: "When I entered here, I made friends with the mosquitoes and struck a bargain with them that they would never disturb or harm me, and in return I would never harm them."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subsecuta deinde opulentissimi Sponsi nuptialia antidora: nam (ut in examine, de quo infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044430Z">
        <seg>Then followed the most opulent bridegroom's nuptial gifts: for (as in the examination, of which below in the chapter…</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Substitit Rosa, non dedignata volucrem auscultare tantisper, utpote Dei laudes (ut sibi imaginabatur) modulo suo cantillantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045025Z">
        <seg>Rosa paused, not deigning to listen to the bird for a while, since it was, as she imagined, singing praises of God in its own way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subvenit ergo cordatæ Virginis pietati simul ac nodo prudens confessarius, ac matrem e vestigio conveniens graviter dehortatus est, cessaret tandem pretiosæ vanitatis inutili molestia tentare perplexam Filiam, quam ab infantia divinus Spiritus præparaverat secretissimis instinctibus ad ratam altioris sanctimoniæ sublimitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035430Z">
        <seg>A prudent confessor, perceiving the Virgin's pious devotion and the knot of her perplexity, at once came forward and earnestly dissuaded her mother, urging her to cease at last from needlessly testing the perplexed Daughter with the vain and costly burden of useless cares—the Daughter whom from infancy the divine Spirit had prepared by most secret instincts for the fitting sublimity of a higher sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Subvenit illa, imo &amp; venit: siquidem exinde, ubi imminuisset surgendi hora, en in puncto aderat conspicua Dei Mater, &amp; voce melliflua compellabat sopitam Rosam: “Surge ad orationem Filia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051335Z">
        <seg>She came to her aid, nay, she came: for from that moment, when the time to rise had grown short, behold, in an instant the Mother of God appeared in sight, and with a honeyed voice she called to the slumbering Rose: "Arise to prayer, Daughter;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Succendebatur vividior in facie color, &amp; exultabat spiritus ejus in Deo salutari suo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060059Z">
        <seg>His face grew more vivid, and his spirit exulted in God, his Savior.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Successum videbunt hi oculi tui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062343Z">
        <seg>These eyes of yours will see success.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Succurre huic necessitati, nec distuleris solatium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055241Z">
        <seg>Come to this need, and do not delay comfort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Summa contentæ vociferationis hæc erat: Invitabat per ordinem singulos creaturarum ordines ad amorem dulcissimi Creatoris, elementa, cælos, angelos, plantas, animalia compellabat, identidem replicando: “Amemus Deum, Deum amemus, amor Deus, Deus amor”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053216Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053216Z">
        <seg>The gist of the outcry was this: it invited, in turn, each order of creatures to the love of the sweetest Creator, urging the elements, the heavens, the angels, the plants, the animals, repeatedly repeating: "Let us love God, let us love God, love is God, God is love."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suo ordine dispiciamus singula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041005Z">
        <seg>Let us consider each item in turn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supererat publicæ authoritatis legale examen &amp; disquisitio, ut evidentius innotesceret miraculi certitudo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053858Z">
        <seg>A legal examination and inquiry by public authority remained, so that the certainty of the miracle might be more clearly recognized.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supererat, ut de lemmate, quod anuli convexo inscriberetur per gyrum, convenirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044446Z">
        <seg>All that remained was for them to agree on the motto to be inscribed on the ring's convex surface around its band.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Superest, quod reliquam ejus pueritiam cursim delibemus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034931Z">
        <seg>All that remains is to briefly touch on the rest of his childhood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Supervenit germanus Virginis frater, ac sororem innocenti ludo se oblectare, nec serium quidquam subesse autumans, accessit familiariter, quidque ageret, interrogavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062517Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062517Z">
        <seg>The Virgin's brother arrived, and, thinking his sister was amusing herself with an innocent game and that there was nothing serious about it, he approached familiarly and asked what she was doing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Surgo Domina, nunc surgo: conseditque erecta in lectulo, sed prægravante somni pondere, oculisque caligantibus, recidit misera in cervical.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051353Z">
        <seg>I rise, my lady, I rise now: and she sat up in her bed, but weighed down by the burden of sleep and her eyes growing dim, the poor woman fell back onto her pillow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspexit in cælum Rosa, &amp; “O (inquit) suavissime Jesu, quam fidelis amicus es”!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055058Z">
        <seg>Rosa looked up at the sky and said, "O sweetest Jesus, what a faithful friend you are!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspicata est postmodum, rosis ceteris, quas per terram sparsas viderat, significari pientissimas virgines per Limam sparsas, quas tandem aliquando colligi oportebat in novellum sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium, ut illic religiosæ professionis nexu votivo colligatæ, Sponsum virginum casto famulatu coronent, vicissim ab illo gloria, &amp; honore coronandæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061931Z">
        <seg>She later suspected, that the other roses, which she had seen scattered across the ground, signified the most pious virgins scattered throughout Lima, who at long last ought to be gathered into the new monastery of Saint Catherine of Siena, so that there, bound by the vow of religious profession, they might crown the Spouse of virgins with chaste service, and in turn be crowned by him with glory and honor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Suspicor, hic ob similitudinem officii virum illum vocari&lt;/e1&gt; circitorem, &lt;e2&gt;quia observare debebat, an ministri tribunalis Quitensis munere suo fungerentur&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061708Z">
        <seg>I suspect that this man is called a circuit judge because of the similarity of his office, since he was supposed to observe whether the ministers of the Quito tribunal were performing their duties.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe obstupuit attenta mater, parvulam suam nulla talium cupediarum aut suavitate, aut varia pulchritudine capi: nam fructus sibi datos mox aliis donabat, numquam vel primis labellis admovebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041011Z">
        <seg>The attentive mother was often astonished that her little daughter was not captivated by any such delicacies, sweetness, or varied beauty; for she would at once give away the fruits offered to her, never even bringing them to her lips.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpe quæstoris uxor notavit, Rosam a sacello reducem aliquid novi solatii ipso premendi conatu prodere: plerumque enim bulliebat lætitiæ fervor, ut familiarem &amp; pie curiosam observatricem latere indicia, aut fallere ex toto non possent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054339Z">
        <seg>The questioner's wife often noted that Rosa, returning from the chapel, would betray some new comfort in her very effort to suppress it: for she was usually bubbling with such fervent joy that she could neither hide the signs from her familiar and piously curious observer, nor deceive her altogether.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Sæpius conversa ad Sponsum: “Domine (inquit) auge, auge dolorem, dummodo augeas &amp; amorem”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052659Z">
        <seg>Often turning to the Spouse: "Lord," she said, "increase, increase the pain, so long as you increase love as well."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tabulis arcte commissis ligna omnino septem instravit justis intervallis, quæ, ne laxarentur, bubulis loris apte distrinxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042607Z">
        <seg>He laid seven planks entirely on the tightly joined boards at even intervals, which he properly secured with ox-hide thongs so they wouldn't loosen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacenti aviculæ succedebat Rosa, ac voce, qua præstabat, tinnula suavissimaque prosequebatur divinas laudes, ingenio ad miraculum fœcundo, ac prompto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045313Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045313Z">
        <seg>Following the silent little bird was Rosa, and with the voice in which she excelled, she continued the divine praises with a most sweet and tinkling sound, her talent miraculously fruitful and prompt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuit Rosa, nec urgenti aliud umquam replicuit, quam stetisse intra mediocritatem suos dolores.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034913Z">
        <seg>Rosa fell silent, and when pressed she never replied to anything except that her sufferings had remained within moderation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuit Rosa, pulvinar suscepit, infarsit, sed non lana, uti credebat mater: vim segmentorum collegit, quæ de fabri lignarii dolabra excidunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042734Z">
        <seg>Rosa fell silent, took up a pillow, stuffed it, but not with wool, as her mother believed: she gathered the force of the shavings that are cut from the carpenter's adze.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tacuit homo, demum annuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044647Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044647Z">
        <seg>The man was silent, then finally nodded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tam caro constitit Rosæ optati secessus tarda licentia, quam illo pretio perpaucæ sibi emptam (pleræque ademptam) vellent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043438Z">
        <seg>Rose's long-awaited leave of absence proved as costly as very few would have been willing to buy (and most would have been unwilling to grant) at that price.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tam cito oblita es, nuperrime Ferdinandi Perez mancipium, simillimo abusu allii &amp; piperis, omnino visum usumque oculorum perdidisse”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043429Z">
        <seg>Have you so soon forgotten that only recently the slave of Ferdinand Perez, by a very similar abuse of garlic and pepper, lost the sight and use of his eyes altogether?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tam profunde animo Juvenculæ insedit robusta hac cogitatio, tam funditus evacuavit congenitam formidandi proclivitatem, ut ex eo momento seipsam mirata sit Rosa, quod noctu, interdiu, domi, foris, ad nullius periculi, monstri, belluæ pavebat occursum, unico præsentis Sponsi præsidio animosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062111Z">
        <seg>This robust thought had taken such deep root in the maiden's mind, and so completely dispelled her innate propensity for fear, that from that moment on, Rosa marveled at herself, for she feared no encounter, by night or day, at home or abroad, with any danger, monster, or beast, emboldened by the sole protection of her present Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tamen ex sensu conjicio, per eam hic viles vestes indicari.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061637Z">
        <seg>However, from the context I infer that by it cheap clothes are meant here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem Rosa nictu fortuito in se reflexa, vidit eodem, quo reliquæ ibi virgines, splendidissimo amictu se pariter circumdatam inclaruisse, &amp; quæ illactenus suo dumtaxat habitu Dominicano simpliciter canduerat, repente se deprehendit in cyclade gemmis &amp; auro distincta, ut ad easdem illic operas se quoque nosset auctoratam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044225Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044225Z">
        <seg>At last, Rosa, by a chance glance cast upon herself, saw that she too, like the other virgins there, had been made glorious by the same most splendid robe. And she, who until then had shone only in her simple Dominican habit, suddenly found herself arrayed in a girdle adorned with gems and gold, so that she recognized herself as having been commissioned to join in those same labors there.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem adfuit medicus, obiterque examinato sanguine, ac notatis in eo periculosi morbi criteriis, fœtentem massam de more projici imperavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061345Z">
        <seg>At last the physician arrived, and after examining the blood and noting in it the criteria of a dangerous disease, he ordered the foul-smelling mass to be thrown away as usual.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem in puncto horæ sextæ pomeridianæ, quasi perfuncta officio, avolabat, postridie ad idem reditura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045326Z">
        <seg>Finally, at exactly six o'clock in the afternoon, as if her duty were done, she would fly away, to return to the same place the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem interrogavit doctor, quo genere locutionis, intellectuali an vocali Christus verba superius recitata protulerit?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052851Z">
        <seg>Finally, the doctor asked by what kind of utterance—intellectual or vocal—Christ had spoken the words quoted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem media nox dispescuit longum certamen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052138Z">
        <seg>At last, the middle of the night broke the long contest;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem opportunitate seu facta seu arrepta, tectis interrogationibus eminus circumivit Virginem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040825Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040825Z">
        <seg>Finally, seizing the opportunity, he skirted around the Virgin with veiled questions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem repentina hilaritate perfusa, in pedes se erexit, &amp; gratiis Virgini matri devotissime persolutis læta domum properavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054428Z">
        <seg>Finally, overcome with sudden joy, she rose to her feet and, having most devoutly given thanks to the Virgin Mother, she hurried home happily.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem severius attracto supercilio;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050451Z">
        <seg>Finally, with a more severe frown;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem supremo obedientiæ præcepto adegit filiam, ediceret, unde sciverit, tali hora e domo quæstoris sibi adferri chocolatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051735Z">
        <seg>Finally, by the supreme command of obedience, he ordered his daughter to find out, from wherever she could, at what hour chocolate was to be brought to him from the treasurer's house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem ut in postremum hoc mere laneum cervical incidit, ulterioris inops consilii, sic Filiam affata est: “Sane paruisti, Rosa mea, paruisti: siquidem hic modo nil nisi lanam reperio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042831Z">
        <seg>Finally, when this last purely woolen shawl came to hand, with no further counsel at hand, she said to her daughter, "You have indeed prepared, my Rose, you have indeed prepared; for here I find nothing but wool."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tandem, ipso B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060146Z">
        <seg>Finally, by B. himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanta Virgini zeloti cura fuit, ne quid vel honori vel tributo cælestis Sponsi decederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053409Z">
        <seg>The Virgin was so zealous that nothing might detract from the honor or tribute of the heavenly Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanti favoris occasio hæc fuit: Rosam a multo tempore continuis integrisque noctibus adeo destituerat necessarius somnus, ut valetudini suæ non parum fuerit timendum, si medelam respueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051310Z">
        <seg>This was the opportunity for such a favor: for a long time, necessary sleep had so deprived Rosa of uninterrupted, full nights that her health would have been seriously endangered had she refused treatment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanti nimirum faciebat etiam leviores parendi occasiones.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035604Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035604Z">
        <seg>He certainly valued even the slightest opportunities to obey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tanto constitit victrici Rosæ candidum seraphicæ Catharinæ habitum &amp; obtinere, &amp; retinere constantissime.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040410Z">
        <seg>It cost so much for the victor Rosa to obtain and most steadfastly retain the white seraphic habit of Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum effecit, quod unus ex confessariis indulsit Virgini, ut per totam illius &amp; proximi anni (qui Rosæ postremus fuit) Quadragesimam, ex pristini rigoris forma denuo sibi lectulum instrueret e lignis, testis, tegulis constipatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042939Z">
        <seg>All he achieved was that one of the confessors granted the Virgin permission to spend the entire Lent of that year and the next (which was Rose's last) in a bed he had rebuilt from the old form of austerity, packed with wood, stones, and tiles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum in vilissimas bestiolas imperium statui innocentiæ reservatum fuerat, quem quidem Rosa tam prope attigit, ut in solitudine cellæ versaretur tamquam in paradiso.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043841Z">
        <seg>Only over the vilest little creatures was the power of innocence reserved, which Rosa approached so closely that she wandered in the solitude of her cell as if in paradise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tantum vel in bruti animalis salutem valuit unica vox miserentis Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061602Z">
        <seg>The single, merciful voice of Rosa was enough to save even a brute animal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tardavit is ultra biduum, atque interea sanguis ab ipsa scaturigine jam notatus de putredine, horridius obputruit, seroso virore, flavis nigrisque maculis ac ramosa sanie undequaque pinguis &amp; &amp; decolor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061342Z">
        <seg>It delayed for more than two days, and in the meantime the blood from the very source, already noted for its putrefaction, had rotted even more horribly, becoming a foul, serous pus streaked with yellow and black, and everywhere thick and discolored with branching matter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Te vivo ac vidente, stabit in urbe hac illud, quod sæpe dixi, B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062625Z">
        <seg>While you are alive and present, that which I have often said will stand in this city, B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tempestiva hæc fuere in tenella indole præludia, ut quid maturior esset molitura, ex his præambulis conjiceretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043308Z">
        <seg>These were timely preludes to her tender disposition, so that from these forerunners one could infer what she would undertake later.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentabat Rosa gradatim abjectis mollioribus, ad exemplum S. Catharinæ Senensis, eo pertingere, ut, inscia matre, demum informe saxum capiti dormitura supponeret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042722Z">
        <seg>Rosa was trying, by gradually discarding softer things, following the example of St. Catherine of Siena, to get to the point where, without her mother's knowledge, she would finally place an unformed stone under her head to sleep on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentabat deinde, saltem per vestigia illum quærere in creaturis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045527Z">
        <seg>He then tried, at least by the traces, to seek him in the creatures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tentarunt ambæ, quid possent, alternabat vices lucta &amp; consultatio: sed neutri cedebat tenacis seræ firma contumacia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041730Z">
        <seg>Both tried their utmost; alternately they wrestled and conferred, but neither could overcome the stubborn, late-clinging obstinacy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Terruissent hæc quamcumque aliam, aut saltem eripuissent loquendi fiduciam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062722Z">
        <seg>These would have terrified any other, or at least robbed them of the confidence to speak.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tertio oras ac fimbrias huic vestitui circumdato 600 Salutationibus angelicis, toties &lt;b5&gt;Salve&lt;/b5&gt; &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054614Z">
        <seg>Thirdly, the borders and fringes of this garment are surrounded by 600 Angelic Greetings, each one saying "Hail," etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Teste Castello, dicitur etiam&lt;/e5&gt; lingula, ligula, &lt;e6&gt;id est&lt;/e6&gt;, laryngis lingua: &lt;e7&gt;est enim corpus quoddam simile linguæ alicujus fistulæ, ubi arteria &amp; larynx inter se connectuntur, &amp; velut operculum omni tempore, quo animal respirat, apertum stans, quo transglutit, laryngi accumbens &amp;c&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043122Z">
        <seg>According to Castalio, it is also called the lingula, ligula, that is, the tongue of the larynx: for it is a certain body resembling the tongue of some kind of pipe, where the artery and the larynx are connected with each other, and like a lid, always standing open whenever the animal breathes, through which it swallows, lying against the larynx, etc.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tibi, o Mater, alterum superest pallium novum adhuc fere intactum, quod priusquam vel modicum veterascat, divina bonitas non uno, sed pluribus te palliis ditabit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061125Z">
        <seg>"To you, O Mother, another new cloak remains, still almost intact; and before it has even slightly worn, divine goodness will bless you not with one, but with several cloaks."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tibi, tibi famulentur universi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053719Z">
        <seg>Let all things serve you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Timuerunt germani fratres, ne forte propediem soror, magno familiæ suæ dedecore, ad sanctæ Inquisitionis&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; tribunal raperetur, velut suspecta de imposturis ac fraudibus, aut sacrilegi mendacii fictæque sanctimoniæ palam vel rea, vel convincenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052513Z">
        <seg>The brothers feared that their sister might soon be dragged before the tribunal of the Holy Inquisition, to the great disgrace of their family, as a suspect in impostures and frauds, or openly guilty of sacrilegious falsehood and feigned piety, or to be convicted of them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tormenta hæc erant, quæ sacram illi vestem reddere exosam poterant, ac primo verecunde occultandam, denique &amp; seponendam suggerebant, usque adeo, ut suum familiare asylum (sacellum dico SS. Rosarii, in quo Rosa habitum assumpserat) cursim adierit, quasi denuo illic firmitate immobili radicanda, solitoque magnæ Deiparæ præsidio tentationem molestissimam depulsura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040345Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040345Z">
        <seg>These were torments that could make her sacred habit hateful to her, and at first suggested it should be shyly hidden, then finally set aside, to the point that her familiar refuge (I mean the chapel of the Holy Rosary, in which Rosa had taken her habit) she ran to it, as if to be there once again rooted in an immovable firmness and, by the usual protection of the great Mother of God, to drive off the most troublesome temptation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Torquebat dæmonem illa hortensis cellula, tot cæli deliciis plena;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051914Z">
        <seg>That garden cell tormented the demon, so full of heavenly delights.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Torquebat hæc fama quæstorem, consortem, filias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054041Z">
        <seg>This rumor tormented the quaestor, his partner, and his daughters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tota serena, comis, benigna, de se nesciebat altum sapere, sed tantum subjici omni humanæ creaturæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040924Z">
        <seg>Entirely serene, courteous, and kind, she was unaware of her own high-mindedness, but only of her submission to every human being.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Totum evenit, prout Virgo prædixerat: nam anno MDCXXII accedens ad eam functionem Ludovicus, inter solemnia, sub Missa recordatus est vaticinii, peractoque Sacrificio, palam adstantibus enarravit, quam asseveranter, clare, diserte ante complures annos Rosa superstes, quod illa die locoque gesserat, prænuntiarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062657Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062657Z">
        <seg>Everything happened just as the Virgin had foretold: for in the year 1622, as Louis was about to assume that office, during the solemn Mass he remembered the prophecy, and after the Sacrifice was completed, he publicly explained to those present how confidently, clearly, and eloquently the surviving Rose had, many years before, predicted what she had done that day and in that place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Trahebat cælestis Sponsus electam sibi Virginem per arcanas sanctimoniæ ac perfectionis semitas: ex adverso trahebat rudis &amp; cruda mater Filiam morigeram ad pompas nugasque seculi, ad vanos corporis ornatus, ad mundi commercia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035254Z">
        <seg>The heavenly Bridegroom was drawing the Virgin he had chosen for himself along the secret paths of holiness and perfection; on the other hand, the rough and crude mother was drawing her obedient daughter to the pomps and vanities of the world, to vain adornments of the body, to the affairs of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transegerat Virgo totam illam Quadragesimam solo pane &amp; aqua, sed præsertim illis hebdomadæ sanctæ postremis diebus vix micatim aliquid cibi admiserat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040748Z">
        <seg>The Virgin had spent the entire Lenten season on nothing but bread and water, but especially in those last days of Holy Week she had barely allowed herself a morsel of food.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transeundum erat per amplissimum urbis forum, ubi tunc metu dilapsa clamosi populi multitudo ferocienti tauro spatium fecerat, confusaque vociferatione, &amp; lapidum jactu furiosam bestiam pro voluptate spectaculi sævius irritabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062139Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062139Z">
        <seg>One had to pass through the city's largest forum, where, then, a fear-stricken, clamorous crowd had made way for a raging bull, and with confused shouting and the hurling of stones, they more fiercely provoked the furious beast for the sake of the spectacle's enjoyment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transeundum per singula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061755Z">
        <seg>Go through each one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Transivit, certus, ejuscemodi coruscum decorem a mortalitatis nostræ regione non esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055820Z">
        <seg>He passed on, certain that such a brilliant splendor did not belong to the realm of our mortality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tria potissima erant, de quorum assecutione Virginem dubitare non sinebat cælitus concessa fiduciæ certitudo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061742Z">
        <seg>There were three things of paramount importance, and the Virgin did not allow herself to doubt their attainment, for a heavenly certainty of her trust had been granted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tristabatur perplexa mater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062244Z">
        <seg>The perplexed mother was grieving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tu in horto occlusa, utique non habuisti, quem illuc mitteres, neque ex hac domo ullus abiit, qui tuam necessitatem quæstori indicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051704Z">
        <seg>You, shut up in the garden, of course had no one you could send there, nor did anyone from this house go who could inform the tax collector of your need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tuguriolum erat quinque pedes longum, quatuor pedes latum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043640Z">
        <seg>The little hut was five feet long, four feet wide;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tum mater velut substomachans, “Eia (inquit) non flores, sed monstra florum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035539Z">
        <seg>Then the mother, as if scolding, said, "Come on, not flowers, but monsters of flowers."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tum vero attonita, “Ut quid (inquit) tot dies noctesque exegisti sub his cruciatibus, nec abjecisti pelles, quæ sic te excarnificabant”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035520Z">
        <seg>Then, truly astonished, she said, "Why did you endure so many days and nights under these torments, and not cast off the skins that were so dismembering you?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc memineris, hæc modo fuisse tibi prædicta a vili muliercula”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062653Z">
        <seg>Then you will remember that these things were just now foretold to you by a lowly little woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc noctes transigebat insomnes, assidebat lectulo, stratum componebat, agebat omnia, præteribat nihil, quod solatium adferre posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035811Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035811Z">
        <seg>Then he would spend sleepless nights, sit on his bed, make the bed, do everything—omitting nothing that could bring comfort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tunc vero statuerat Virgo, hoc unicum ab alumno exigere diuturnæ alimoniæ maternæque solicitudinis præmium, iret, properaret ad gentes Christianæ legis ignaras, trophæum salutis in medio barbararum nationum erigeret, &amp; quotquot posset animas a restibus diaboli strenue explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060508Z">
        <seg>Then indeed the Virgin had resolved to demand this one thing from her protégé as the reward for her long-standing support and maternal care: that he go, that he hurry to the peoples ignorant of the Christian law, that he raise the trophy of salvation in the midst of barbarian nations, and that he mightily free as many souls as he could from the snares of the devil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Turibius Alphonsus Mogroveio, de quo supra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040022Z">
        <seg>Turibius Alphonsus Mogrovejo, of whom mention has been made above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tuum lauda Creatorem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045242Z">
        <seg>Praise your Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Tympanum erat, cujus levi tactu perculsus dæmon fuga turpissima declinabat certamen.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042344Z">
        <seg>It was a drum, whose light touch struck the demon, who, in the most shameful flight, shunned the contest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi anachoretica Virginis cella constiterat, soli humiditas &amp; arbuscularum frequentia infinitam culicum multitudinem aut gignebat, aut trahebat in amicum suo generi umbraculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043745Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043745Z">
        <seg>Where the Virgin's hermitage had been established, the damp soil and the abundance of saplings either bred an endless multitude of gnats or drew them into the shade of her dwelling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi leges Rosam in ipso mortis articulo morigeram Gundisalvo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035632Z">
        <seg>Where you will see Rosa, at the very point of death, obedient to Gundisalvo.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi vero Seraphicam ejusdem vitæ historiam domi per capitula legi audivit, attentissima, tamquam soli legeretur sibi, medullitus se sensit inflammari ad sequelam, non quietura, donec &amp; foris in habitu, &amp; intus in fimbriis aureis, tantæ heroïnæ ac magistræ discipula conformaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040236Z">
        <seg>But when she heard the Seraphic history of that same life read at home by chapters, she listened with the utmost attention, as if it were being read to her alone, and felt herself kindled to the marrow to follow in its footsteps, not to rest until she conformed herself, both outwardly in her dress and inwardly in her golden fringes, to the example of so great a heroine and teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubi vero quæstoris eam domus contubernalem excepit, nil novum fuit, si complures continuos dies absque ullo omnino alimento transmittebat: plerumque etiam feria quinta solebat ibidem se domestico oratorio includere, nec inde se movere usque ad Sabbatum: ita quod infra hoc triduum plane insomnis, non modo carebat omni cibo potuque corporali, verum etiam (quod quæstorem Gundisalvum maxime rapiebat in admirationem) toto illo tempore remanebat uni affixa angulo, ut neque ad momentum pro quacumque necessitate ab illo discederet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041340Z">
        <seg>When, however, the quæstor's house received her as a housemate, it was nothing new if she passed several consecutive days without any food at all: she also usually used to shut herself in her private oratory on Thursdays, and would not leave until Saturday: So that during this three-day period, completely sleepless, she not only lacked all bodily food and drink, but also (which most amazed the quæstor Gundisalvus) remained for the entire time fixed to one corner, so that she did not leave it for a moment for any need.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ubique conclamatum ad arma, &amp; interim clerus uterque (ut fit publicis periculis) divina imploraturus præsidia, Limæ per omnes ecclesias sanctissimum Corporis dominici Sacramentum exposuerat palam, ab imbelli multitudine adorandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060143Z">
        <seg>Now everywhere the call to arms rang out, and in the meantime both clergy, as is done in public dangers, to implore divine aid, had publicly exposed the most holy Sacrament of the Body of the Lord in all the churches of Lima, to be adored by the unarmed multitude.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Una hic commemorandi forent, laude non multum impari, P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050212Z">
        <seg>One here might be mentioned, not much less worthy of praise, P.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde ad faciliora effatu prudenter digrediens, inquisivit de exercitiis mortificationis ac pœnitentiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050612Z">
        <seg>Therefore, prudently turning to easier matters, he inquired about exercises in mortification and penance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde arrepta coram incredulis tabella &amp; cera, futuri monasterii extemporaneum formavit archetypon, ea tum peritia, tum securitate, ut spectantes rapuerit in stuporem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062728Z">
        <seg>So, seizing a tablet and wax before the unbelievers, he improvised an extemporaneous archetype of the future monastery, with such skill and confidence that he left the onlookers in astonishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde dicit, sanctam Virgunculam&lt;/e6&gt; chirothecas, &lt;e7&gt;quas jussu matris induerat&lt;/e7&gt;, pyrothecas &lt;e8&gt;sensisse, id est, illas ex thecis seu tegumentis manuum thecas ignis factas esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T044109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035843Z">
        <seg>Hence he says that the holy Virgin's&lt;/e6&gt; gloves, &lt;e7&gt;which she had put on at her mother's command&lt;/e7&gt;, had become pyrotechnic &lt;e8&gt;gloves, that is, that they had been made into fire-boxes from the cases or coverings of the hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde facile obtinuit, ut tum in Paschate, tum in solemnitate Corporis Christi quotidie per totas octavas sibi licuerit vesci hoc delicato Pane angelorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055709Z">
        <seg>Thus he easily obtained permission to partake daily, throughout the entire octave, of this delicate Bread of Angels, both at Easter and on the Solemnity of Corpus Christi.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde in hac, aliisque suis infirmitatibus solebat dicere: “O quam salubris, quam jucunda &amp; felix sors foret, crebriori ægritudine divexari, dummodo proximorum abesset incommodum!” Sæpe Virginis guttur &amp; fauces, ex inferiorum musculorum inflammatione, acutissima invadebat angina&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, cui interdum periculosior succrescebat cynanche&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;: sæpius totis pulmonibus &amp; bronchis decertandum fuit cum asthmate&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052609Z">
        <seg>Hence, in this and her other infirmities, she was accustomed to say: "Oh, what a healthy, pleasant, and happy fate it would be to be beset by more frequent illness, so long as it were free from the inconvenience of those around her!" Often the Virgin's throat and windpipe, from inflammation of the lower muscles, was invaded by the most acute angina&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, to which at times a more dangerous cynanche&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;d&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;would sometimes grow: more often the whole lungs and bronchi had to contend with asthma&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;e&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde iterum anima ejus in malis tabescebat, iterum diffugiebat omne evadendi solatium, iterum exanimem abyssi operiebant, &amp; Rosa tota jam spinea, imo spina, rigebat in ima voragine tribulationum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045614Z">
        <seg>Whereupon her soul withered again in evil, again fled from every comfort of escape, again the abysses covered her lifeless, and Rosa, now all thorny, nay, a thorn herself, lay rigid in the deepest vortex of tribulations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde miserata parens, ne affectis luminibus liberior foris noceret aër, indulgebat filiæ optatissimam solitudinis ac latibuli facultatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043342Z">
        <seg>Therefore, out of pity, the mother, so that the air outside might not harm her daughter's affected eyes, granted her the most eagerly desired freedom of solitude and concealment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde per&lt;/e1&gt; Attalicam &lt;e2&gt;hic vestis pretiosa vel magnifica intelligitur&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055529Z">
        <seg>Hence, by Attalic here, a precious or magnificent garment is understood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde pluries de cantantis ore auditi sunt versus isti, numeris Hispanicis illigati, quos utcumque latine reddiderim:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053238Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053238Z">
        <seg>Hence, these verses, bound to Spanish numbers, have often been heard from the singer's mouth, which I have rendered into Latin however I could:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde quadam occasione videns plorantem matrem, quasi divinæ quæsturæ correpta zelo, “Hem (inquit) o mater, quid agis?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053359Z">
        <seg>So, on a certain occasion, seeing a weeping mother, as if seized by zeal for a divine quest, she said, "Hey there, mother, what are you doing?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde quæ tunc verecunde invita aperuit, perpauca ac pene vulgaria crediderim, si conferantur iis, quæ Rosa sub divino secreti beneficio ac humilitatis sigillo retinuit obsignata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040850Z">
        <seg>Therefore, what she then modestly and reluctantly revealed, I would consider very few and almost commonplace, if compared to those things that Rosa kept sealed under the divine benefit of secrecy and the seal of humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde satis clarum est, hic eam vocem pro scintillis usurpari.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045912Z">
        <seg>Hence it is quite clear that this word is used here for sparks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde sic diurnum nocturnumque tempus distribuit, ut ex utroque duodecim horas universim orationi seponeret, alias decem impenderet labori manuum, quo parentes sustentabat, reliquas corporis necessitatibus &amp; brevi quieti deputaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043026Z">
        <seg>Thus he divided his time between day and night so that he would set aside twelve hours in all for prayer from both, devote another ten to manual labor, by which he supported his parents, and allocate the rest to the body's necessities and brief rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde synodus Mexicana apud eminentissimum Cardinalem de Aguirre tomo 4 Conciliorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040428Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040428Z">
        <seg>Hence the Mexican synod, at the eminent Cardinal de Aguirre, in the fourth volume of the Councils, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unde synodus Mexicana apud eminentissimum Cardinalem de Aguirre tomo 4 Conciliorum pag. 352 § 16&lt;/e1&gt;, Præcepit, ut nulla prædictarum mulierum, quas BEATAS vocant, habitum alicujus religionis approbatæ deferat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000521Z">
        <seg>Hence the Mexican synod, under the Most Eminent Cardinal de Aguirre, in volume 4 of the Councils, page 352, section 16, decreed that none of the aforementioned women, whom they call BEATAS, should wear the habit of any approved religious order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unica demum Mariana ancilla secreti conscia fuit, quam validis obtestationibus adegit Rosa, ne proderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042555Z">
        <seg>Finally, only Mariana's handmaid was privy to the secret, whom Rosa had compelled by strong entreaties not to reveal it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum hujus paradigma dabo, unde conjiciatur de reliquis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035455Z">
        <seg>I will give a single example of this paradigm, from which the rest can be inferred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum istud singularissimæ pietatis erga matrem, &amp; extremæ commiserationis specimen sileri hic minime debet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035824Z">
        <seg>That single act of the most singular piety toward his mother, and the most extreme example of compassion, should by no means be omitted here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unicum paradigma dabo, &amp; finiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055324Z">
        <seg>I will give a single paradigm, and I will finish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unum hoc superioribus addidisse sufficiat, quod, ubi Rosam in via illuminativa probare cœpit, adstupuit responsis puellæ simplicis atque illiteratæ, cum de arcano SS. Trinitatis mysterio, de hypostatica Verbi unione, de sacramento Altaris, de gloria Beatorum, de libro vitæ ac prædestinationis, de natura gratiæ, aliisque fidei ac theosophiæ secretis interrogata, promebat axiomata tam profunda ac solida, conceptus tam acutos ac sublimes, sententias tam perspicuas, breves, fœcundas ac ponderosas, verba tam propria, apposita, clara, succincta, ut alius examinator non dubitarit fateri ingenue, numquam se in tam luminosum &amp; perspicax ingenium incidisse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050634Z">
        <seg>Let it suffice to add this one thing to the foregoing: that when he began to test Rosa on the illuminative way, he was astonished by the replies of the simple and uneducated girl, when speaking of the mystery of the Holy Sacrament. When she was questioned about the mystery of the Trinity, the hypostatic union of the Word, the sacrament of the Altar, the glory of the Blessed, the book of life and predestination, the nature of grace, and other secrets of faith and theology, she produced axioms so profound and solid, concepts so acute and sublime, sentences so clear, brief, fecund and weighty, words so proper, apt, clear, and concise, that another examiner did not hesitate to frankly confess that he had never encountered such a luminous and perspicacious mind;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unus ex confessariis Virginis obiter habitaculi angustiam perstrinxit, cui Rosa lepide replicuit, tantum inesse spatii, quantum sibi Sponsoque cælesti abunde sufficeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043646Z">
        <seg>One of the Virgin's confessors casually remarked on the narrowness of her dwelling, to which Rose wittily replied that there was just enough space for herself and her heavenly Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Unus respondebat, aut deliria esse, aut insomnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045704Z">
        <seg>One answered that it was either delirium or insomnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Urgebat interim doctor, instabat bis, ter;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050445Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile the doctor urged him on, pressing him twice, three times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usa sum, nec mihi deinceps, ubi decubuero ex morbo, quidquam defuturum puta: Dominus abunde providebit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061057Z">
        <seg>I have used it, and from now on, when I fall sick, do not think that I will lack anything: the Lord will provide abundantly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque ad gradum perpetis cum Deo unionis Virgo ascenderat, simulque cœpit in dies singulos atrocissima pati mentalium obtenebrationum intervalla, quæ non obiter, sed solidis plerumque horis eo deprimebant miseram, ut sæpe nescierit in gehenna esset, an in purgatorii diro carcere, vel quibus in ergastulis retrusa caligaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045453Z">
        <seg>She had ascended to the degree of perpetual union with God, and at the same time she began to suffer each day the most atrocious intervals of mental obscuration, which, not merely occasionally but for solid hours, so oppressed the poor soul that she often did not know whether she was in hell, in the dreadful prison of purgatory, or in what dungeons she was shut up and blinded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Usque adeo non aberravit religiosa matrona, quæ Rosam, stellis amicam, in stellæ figura agnovit, cui tunc pro firmamento exiguum cellæ latibulum erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043720Z">
        <seg>The devout matron did not stray so far as to fail to recognize Rose, a friend of the stars, in the shape of a star, whose only shelter then was a tiny cell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut autem impleret, observabat attentissime, quot diebus, subinde ex infirmitate decumbens, ab usu quotidianæ disciplinæ fuisset impedita, subductoque diligenti calculo, ubi convaluisset, ita in proximam dierum periodum dividebat omissorum ictuum portiones, ut compensatione facta, permissum numerum quinque millium exacte adæquaret, nec segnius numerum, præsenti dierum periodo tunc respondentem, una absolveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041609Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041609Z">
        <seg>However, to make up for it, she paid the closest attention to how many days, from time to time being laid up by illness, she had been prevented from her daily discipline, and after carefully calculating, once she had recovered, she would divide the portions of the missed strokes into the next period of days in such a way that, with the compensation made, the permitted number of five thousand would be exactly met, and no less diligently would she complete the number corresponding to the current period of days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut ergo ita esse reipsa comprobarunt, desiere cibi mentionem importune ingerere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055954Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055954Z">
        <seg>So, since they had confirmed that this was indeed the case, they stopped bringing up food so importunately.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut intelligas tam suas Rosæ patienti non cessisse spinas, quam his non cessit illa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042201Z">
        <seg>So that you may understand that the rose's thorns yielded no more to its own patient than they did to her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut tamen te, o Rex, me cuncta stringunt jura;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045332Z">
        <seg>Yet, O King, all rights bind you to me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Ut vero ad horti januam, volando magis, quam currendo pervenerat, in propinqua porticu substitit, correptaque ferrea catena, nudas sibi scapulas flagris acerbissimis cruentavit: inter fluentes tum sanguinis, tum lachrymarum rivos, Sponso suo querula, quod ipsam ad tempus destituerat, dum in ejuscemodi angustias incideret: numquam enim sibi, præsente ipso, tam periculosam larvatæ impuritatis imaginem fuisse occursuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052215Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052215Z">
        <seg>When she had reached the garden gate more by flying than by running, she paused in a nearby portico, and seizing an iron chain, she lacerated her bare shoulders with the most bitter whips: amid the flowing streams of both blood and tears, she complained to her Spouse that He had forsaken her for a time while she was plunged into such straits: for she had never imagined that, in his very presence, she would encounter such a dangerous image of impure disguise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Uterque præ angustia clamavit in cælum, utrique responsum cælitus: “Sufficit tibi gratia mea: nam virtus in infirmitate perficitur”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045447Z">
        <seg>Both, in their anguish, cried out to heaven, and both received a heavenly answer: "My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utique plenam hæc fidem faciunt illi religiosissimæ cujusdam mulieris extaticæ visioni, cui in raptu protestatus est Christus, se Rosam gestare in intimo sui divini cordis penetrali;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051248Z">
        <seg>They certainly give full credence to the ecstatic vision of a most devout woman, to whom Christ, in a rapture, declared that He bore the Rose in the innermost sanctuary of His divine heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utitur eo nomine Latino Plautus in significatione tam vili, ut angelis non conveniat.&lt;/e4&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052259Z">
        <seg>Plautus uses that Latin name in such a base sense that it is unworthy of angels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utriusque angorem ut rescivit Filia, clave, qua solebat, cordialis in Deum confidentiæ tentavit opulentum æterni provisoris gazophylacium: vixque in templo preces fuderat, cum inde domum redeunti adstitit vir plane ignotus, at vultu modeste decorus, ac voce comis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062251Z">
        <seg>When the daughter learned of both their distress, she tried with the key she was accustomed to use to open the opulent treasury of the eternal Provider's confidence in God. And scarcely had she poured out prayers in the temple when a man, entirely unknown to her, appeared as she was returning home, his countenance modestly handsome and his voice courteous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Utut sit, Rosæ non tam diluculum fuit quam meridies, dum sic illustrata præferbuit &amp; viam elegit quam debuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035040Z">
        <seg>Be that as it may, for Rose it was not so much dawn as midday, while thus enlightened she blossomed and chose the path she ought to have taken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>V utcumque tractavimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044040Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044040Z">
        <seg>We have treated V in any case;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>VIII) tempora clam obsederat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040614Z">
        <seg>VIII) had secretly besieged the times.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>VITA</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211721Z">
        <seg>Life</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vale”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055055Z">
        <seg>Goodbye.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Varias hujus morbi species vide pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052944Z">
        <seg>See various species of this disease, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vas erat argenteum calenti chocolata plenum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051719Z">
        <seg>The vessel was silver and filled with hot chocolate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Velociter inquirito:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051623Z">
        <seg>Quickly investigate:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venerunt ambo, ignari quid rerum ageretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053756Z">
        <seg>They both came, unaware of what was going on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Veniet dies, qua universi intelligent, quam sit eximie alta, quam magna, &amp; spectabilis hæc Rosa”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050653Z">
        <seg>A day will come when all will understand how exceedingly high, how great, and how magnificent this Rose is.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venit P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053827Z">
        <seg>The father came.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Venit cum prætextu commendandi maritum &amp; filiolam: nam Virginis apud Deum suffragio tunc forsitan magis indigebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062923Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062923Z">
        <seg>She came under the pretext of recommending her husband and little daughter; for they perhaps then stood in greater need of the Virgin's intercession with God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verbis supra ætatem sensatis respondit: “Sinito hic me delitescere solam cum Deo: nam illic inter pupas, an pariter adsit Deus, quis novit”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043237Z">
        <seg>She replied to the words, wise beyond her years: "Let me here withdraw alone with God; for among dolls, who knows whether God is present there as well?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verecunde renuit modesta Virgo: instabant molestæ feminæ, quibus complacitura mater jussit edicto parere filiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035303Z">
        <seg>The modest virgin demurred shyly; the troublesome women pressed her, and the mother, wishing to please them, ordered her daughter to obey the decree.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Veredos etiam adhibebant veteres principum tabellarii, qui mutatis per stationes equis, iter velocissime conficiebant, atque inde&lt;/e2&gt; veredarii &lt;e3&gt;dicti sunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052330Z">
        <seg>The ancients also used relays of horses for their princes' couriers, who, changing horses at the stations, completed the journey very swiftly, and from this they were called riders.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; hoc Virgini tandem novæ vexationis auctarium fuit: nam ut aliquoties in vultu Filiæ notarat parens, eam statis horis interdiu expallescere, angi, palpitare, ac mortali sudore propemodum congelari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045732Z">
        <seg>But this, too, was a fresh torment for the Virgin: for, as the parent had often noticed on his daughter's face, at certain hours of the day she would grow pale, be seized with anguish, tremble, and be almost frozen with a deathly sweat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; hoc patri spirituali (forsitan suggerente &amp; exaggerante matre) visum Filiæ vires excedere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060127Z">
        <seg>But this, too, seemed to the spiritual father (perhaps suggested and exaggerated by the mother) to exceed the daughter's strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum &amp; hunc mater abegerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042827Z">
        <seg>But this one, too, had been driven away by his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum (quæ Sponsi mei summa est benignitas) intra me sentio tam infallibiles suæ erga me non interruptæ hactenus dignationis syngraphas, ut facilius mihi persuaserit quivis, me saxum esse aut paleam, quam quod dulce Numen a me aversum sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062018Z">
        <seg>But I feel within me such infallible tokens of His uninterrupted favor toward me that it would be easier for anyone to persuade me that I am a rock or chaff than that the sweet Deity has turned away from me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum Joannes vultu Rosæ observato, notavit in eodem majestatem angelicam, tesseram pacis, &amp; vivam spem incolumitatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061503Z">
        <seg>But John, observing Rose's face, perceived in it an angelic majesty, a token of peace, and a living hope of safety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ad alia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051130Z">
        <seg>But to other matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ad rem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042043Z">
        <seg>But to the point.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum angelico ad hoc opus foret tum calamo tum penicillo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055740Z">
        <seg>Indeed, for this work, an angelic pen and brush would be required.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum biduo vel triduo ante, horrenda arthritis&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;g&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dexteram Virginis manum per omnes articulos importunissimo cruciatu invaserat, ut non modo operi vel tenuiter attingendo prorsus inutilem reddiderit, verum etiam præ immanitate monstruosæ inflationis, medicum terruerit, gravioris mali atheromata&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;h&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt; suspicantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055340Z">
        <seg>But two or three days before, a horrible arthritis had invaded the Virgin's right hand through all its joints with the most excruciating torment, so that it had rendered her not only completely useless for any work or even the slightest touch, but had also terrified the doctor with the monstrous enormity of the swelling, suspecting a more serious atherosclerotic condition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de his opportunius in descriptione examinis capite sequente.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045902Z">
        <seg>But more about these at a more opportune time in the description of the examination in the following chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de hoc in sequenti capitulo, ubi ad Rosæ vigilias transibit succincta narratio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042127Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042127Z">
        <seg>But of this in the next chapter, where a concise account will pass to Rose's vigils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum de hoc prodigio commodior redibit tractatio infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042356Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042356Z">
        <seg>A more convenient treatment of this prodigy will be given below in chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum divinus Collusor (quasi jacturam non ferens) rediit, ludum instauravit, jactuque feliciori victor evasit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051028Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051028Z">
        <seg>But the divine Collutor (as if not bearing the loss) returned, restored the game, and with a more fortunate throw emerged victorious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum dubitanti &amp; prope exanimi subvenit Christus, visibili blandaque specie se Virgini objiciens, ipsamque placidissime his verbis compellans: “Memento Filia, longe duriorem, angustiorem, horribiliorem fuisse lectulum Calvariæ, in quo mortis somnum pro te cepi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042651Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042651Z">
        <seg>But Christ came to the aid of the doubting and nearly fainting Virgin, appearing to her in a visible and gentle form, and most peacefully addressed her with these words: "Remember, Daughter, that the bed of Calvary was far harsher, more constricting, more dreadful, on which I took the sleep of death for you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ex quo in hujus mentionem incidimus, seorsim capite sequenti narranda sunt, quæ circa fundandum illud monasterium Virgini cælitus &amp; promissa &amp; ostensa fuerunt: hic breviter, quod præsenti capiti sufficit, superestque in coronidem, subnecto &amp; finio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062319Z">
        <seg>But since we have come to mention this, the things that were divinely promised and shown to the Virgin concerning the founding of that monastery must be told separately in the next chapter. Here, briefly, what suffices for the present chapter remains, and I append and conclude it in the coronis.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hac solerti fuga trepidam matrem nec fefellit nec effugit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042239Z">
        <seg>But with this clever escape, the anxious mother was neither deceived nor eluded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hanc mater, ut animadvertit, sustulit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042728Z">
        <seg>But this mother, as she noticed, lifted it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum his facile Rosa, totius arcani probe conscia, occurrit, voce tranquilla &amp; placida jubens, ne timerent: nihil terrificum, nil sinistrum, horridumve portendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053911Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, readily meeting them, fully aware of the entire secret, commands them with a calm and placid voice not to be afraid: nothing terrifying, nothing sinister, or horrible is foretold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum humilis Rosa velut culpam in opere sanctissimo agnosceret, veniam deprecata, spopondit emendationem, læta ibi se objurgari, ubi maxime plausum, vanamque gloriam metuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040636Z">
        <seg>But the humble Rose, as if acknowledging a fault in the most sacred work, begged for forgiveness, promised to amend, and was glad to be rebuked there where she had most feared applause and vain glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum hæc minime admirans, respondit paucis: “Numquid vidisti, soror, quam sereno indicio dignatur approbare seraphica nostra mater, quod agimus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055113Z">
        <seg>But paying no heed to this, she replied briefly, "Have you seen anything, sister, that our seraphic mother deigns to approve by a clear sign—what we are doing?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum illic populosissima glirium orchestra, imo hippodromus erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052017Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052017Z">
        <seg>But there was a most populous orchestra of gnomes, nay, a hippodrome.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum incredula mater, dum pium hic Filiæ commentum suspicatur, curiosius utramque inspexit manum, quas ut revera pustulatas, ustulatas, torridas comperit, ut contrectavit, demum adtremuit, desiitque in posterum Filiam obedientissimam ad eadem incendia durius invitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035405Z">
        <seg>But the unbelieving mother, while suspecting her daughter's pious pretext, more curiously inspected both hands and, finding them truly blistered, scorched, and parched, as soon as she touched them she finally trembled and thereafter ceased to urge her most obedient daughter more harshly toward the same fires.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum institit Rosa efficaciori imperio: “Itote, sorores, trinum, quem dixi, florem maturrimum, decussatum, repandum ocyus adferte.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055157Z">
        <seg>But Rosa insisted with more effective authority: "Go, sisters, and quickly bring me the triple flower I mentioned—the ripest, cross-pollinated one."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ipsa præcipue indigitabat hortum animæ, cui a Deo precabatur florida augmenta virtutum: sicque ingeniose uni parœmiæ duplicem sensum includens, altero satisfaciebat alloquenti, altero Deum alloquebatur ipsa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045010Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045010Z">
        <seg>But she herself especially pointed to the garden of her soul, for which she prayed to God for the flourishing growth of virtues; and thus, ingeniously enclosing a double meaning in a single proverb, she satisfied the speaker with one sense and addressed God with the other.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum is conflictus erat, non afflictio, aut fuga roboris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045828Z">
        <seg>But it was a conflict, not an affliction, nor a flight of strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ne sic quidem effugere sat potuit, quin complures tum celebri virtutis opinione, tum pudici vultus majestate in sui admirationem, mox &amp; honesti thalami ambitum invita accenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035951Z">
        <seg>Yet even so she could not escape, but would kindle admiration for herself in the minds of many, both by her then celebrated reputation for virtue and by the majesty of her modest countenance, and would soon, against her will, set aflame the circle of her honorable nuptials.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum negabat mater, se permissuram Filiæ, quod tali sepulchro vivani se abderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043509Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043509Z">
        <seg>The mother denied that she would allow her daughter to hide herself alive in such a tomb.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum post Rosæ obitum, soluta promissæ taciturnitatis vinculo, beneficium liberrime omnibus, prædicavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061227Z">
        <seg>But after Rose's death, freed from the bond of her promised silence, she freely proclaimed the benefit to all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum post fratris decessum non levis cura momordit Petrum, quid esset, quod juxta condictum defuncti anima non comparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060853Z">
        <seg>But after his brother's death, Peter was deeply troubled by the question of why, according to the deceased's own stipulation, his soul did not appear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum pro illa vice mater, vel quod crederet, se filiam ab horto jam reduxisse, vel quod aliis distracta minus applicuisset animum, oblita fuerat hortum ad horam consuetam exituræ Virgini aperire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051806Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051806Z">
        <seg>But on that occasion the mother, whether because she believed she had already brought her daughter back from the garden, or because she was distracted by other matters and had paid less attention, had forgotten to open the garden at the usual time for the Virgin to go out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum præstantius fuit alterum, quod intus Rosæ animam dulcorabat eodem illo momento, quo foris convalescebat manus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055452Z">
        <seg>But the other was more wonderful, that within, Rose's soul was being sweetened at the very moment that the hand was being healed without.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum quia isthoc solatio non nisi interdiu licebat frui, paulo grandior facta, impetravit a matre aliud intra ædes cubiculum, in quo seorsim a cæteris prolibus solitaria pernoctaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043305Z">
        <seg>But since she was only allowed to enjoy this comfort by day, when she had grown a little older she persuaded her mother to provide another room inside the house, in which she could sleep alone at night, apart from the other children.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum quoties nox appetebat, remotis pulvillis succedebat truncus undique impolitus, ac lapilli in hunc usum interdiu sub lecto absconditi, disponebantur sub stragulo, ut ea inæqualitate offenderetur corpus, cui prædura tabularum planities in deliciis fore timebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042536Z">
        <seg>But whenever night fell, the pillows were removed and in their place a roughly hewn log was set, with small stones hidden under the bed for this purpose during the day; these were arranged beneath the blanket so that the body would be jolted by the uneven surface, for it was feared that the hard flatness of the planks would prove too luxurious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum si Rosæ perarduum fuit, vigesimo demum ætatis anno, per tot obstacula eluctari ad sacrum habitum, vix minori lucta ipsi constitit, eumdem usque ad mortem retinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040239Z">
        <seg>But if it was exceedingly arduous for Rose, at the age of twenty, to struggle through so many obstacles to the sacred habit, it was scarcely any less a struggle for her to keep it until her death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum singula quæque suæ devotionis exercitia minutim prosequi non vacat, præsertim cum ex innumeris innotuerint pauciora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054649Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054649Z">
        <seg>However, it is not possible to pursue each and every one of his devotional exercises in detail, especially since only a few have become known among the countless.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum sub eodem radio progressa ulterius Rosa limpide agnovit, Dei auxilium sibi fore &amp; necessarium, &amp; jugiter implorandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035015Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035015Z">
        <seg>But under the same radio, as she progressed further, Rosa clearly recognized that God's help would be for her, necessary, and to be implored continually.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum tunc mysterii ob ætatem ignarus, taciteque mirari contentus præteribat, sed post annos XV ab obitu Virginis, jam sacerdos judicioque maturior, prodigium, uti acciderat, sub fide juramenti enarravit, adjiciens, ex ejusmodi sacris ardoribus sibi juvenculo rude cor nihilominus concaluisse arcana veneratione præsentis Sacramenti, quod suscipi tanta religione &amp; æstu viderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055839Z">
        <seg>But then, ignorant of the mystery because of his age, and contentedly passed by in silent wonder, but fifteen years after the Virgin's death, now a priest and more mature in judgment, he recounted the prodigy, as it had occurred, under oath, adding that from such sacred ardors his young and uncouth heart had nevertheless been stirred by the secret reverence for the present Sacrament, which he had seen received with such great religion and fervor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ut comperit, his paulatim indiciis omnem austeritatem suorum jejuniorum a curiosis deprehendi, æstimari, extolli, plus sibi a vanæ gloriæ laudumque tinea, quam ab eximiæ formæ præstantia timendum rata, confugit ad solitum orationis præsidium, anxiisque precibus impetravit a Deo talem sibi aptari vultum, qui saltem abstinentiæ suæ rigores, ac tot jejuniorum extima signa mortalium oculis non venditaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040738Z">
        <seg>But when he discovered that, by these gradual clues, the austerity of his fasts was being detected, judged, and extolled by the curious—deeming he had more to fear from the moth of vain glory and praise than from the splendor of an exceptional form—he resorted to his customary refuge of prayer, and with anxious prayers he implored God to grant him a face that would at least not betray to mortal eyes the rigor of his abstinence and the many outward signs of his fasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum ut neque hoc se impetraturam a domesticis intellexit, adstantem germanum fratrem ultimis affata verbis rogavit, cervicalia ut submoveret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043004Z">
        <seg>But when she realized that she would not be able to get this from her household servants, she addressed her brother, who was standing by, with her last words and asked him to remove her necklace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ne Mariæ obstaret egestas, providerat omniscia cura Numinis inspirans fundatrici, ut in ipso fundationis programmate reservarentur loca nonnulla gratuitæ receptionis digniorum, sed inopum personarum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062849Z">
        <seg>Indeed, so that poverty would not stand in the way of Mary, the founder, inspired by the care of Providence, had provided in the very program of the foundation that certain places for the free reception of worthy but needy persons be reserved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, operante magistro P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054946Z">
        <seg>But, with Master P. at work</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, quo plus detergebatur, plus novi sudoris erumpebat: rursus ergo Peñalosa gossipium, qua siccum erat, applicuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053843Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053843Z">
        <seg>But the more it was wiped away, the more fresh sweat broke out; so Peñalosa again applied the gossypium, which was dry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ubi prudens examinator per idoneas periphrases&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; dilucidius, quid sciscitaretur, explicuit, Rosa diffiteri amplius nec debuit, nec potuit, superna illustratione admodum tempestive se didicisse, quod ab æterno electa erat ad cælestem gloriam, idque miro Dei beneficio se tanta certitudine tenere, ut limpidiorem hujus revelationem nequeat desiderare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061832Z">
        <seg>But when the prudent examiner, by means of suitable circumlocutions, more clearly explained what he was asking, Rosa could neither deny nor refuse any longer, having by a heavenly illumination learned with great promptness that she had been chosen from all eternity for heavenly glory, and that she held this with such certainty by a marvelous gift of God that she could not desire a clearer revelation of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verum, ut breve est a floribus gaudium, Rosa mane quodam invenit basilicum suum non modo siccum ac marcidum, sed radicitus evulsum ac mortuum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051217Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051217Z">
        <seg>But, as the joy of flowers is short-lived, one morning Rosa found her basil not only dry and withered, but uprooted and dead.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen &amp; hanc supplevit ingeniosæ devotionis industria: nam, ut quotannis Reginam sanctissimi Rosarii bis spirituali veste amiciret, materiam, artem, formam excogitavit longe pretiosiorem ad modum, quem supra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054559Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, she also supplied this with the industry of her ingenious devotion: for, in order to clothe the Queen of the Most Holy Rosary twice each year in a spiritual vestment, she devised a material, workmanship, and form far more precious, in a manner described above in the chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen siluit, haud ignara, Filiam, si spinetum suum prodere aut abjicere cogeretur, facile alia &amp; quidem longe truculentiora mox adinventuram, quibus verticem pergeret divexare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042251Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, she remained silent, well aware that if her daughter were forced to betray or cast aside her own thorn, she would soon invent other—and indeed far more cruel—means to continue tormenting her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen subdebat, plurimum se præsidii ac spei in Rosæ orationibus, jejuniis, ceterorumque bonorum operum fido suffragio collocasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060425Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, he added that he had placed the greatest reliance and hope in the faithful intercession of the prayers, fasts, and other good works of Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Verumtamen, ut in his alieno potius, quam suo fideret ingenio, pium doctumque e Societate Jesu theologum adiit, divinorum epithetorum sinceram e sacris Paginis nomenclaturam sibi in epitomen colligi flagitavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044929Z">
        <seg>However, since in these matters he trusted rather to another's than to his own ingenuity, he consulted a pious and learned theologian of the Society of Jesus, demanding that a sincere compendium of the divine epithets from the sacred Scriptures be compiled for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicissim a parente Filia supplex impetravit, saltem ea sibi permitti ac parari fercula, quæ non modo ingerere, sed &amp; digerere posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041200Z">
        <seg>In turn, the daughter implored her parent to grant her at least those dishes that she could not only eat but also digest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicit ergo constantia Virginis, quæ exinde fidentior, Gundisalvum aliosque suasores modesto ac gravi responso interpellavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040314Z">
        <seg>Thus the virgin's steadfastness prevailed, and she, now more confident, interrupted Gundisalvus and the other suitors with a modest and serious reply.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vicit nihilominus Rosæ ingenium: nam serto clam ab ima parte suffixit oblongam acum, sicque corollam latenter aculeatam suo capiti non tam imposuit, quam impressit, valideque impegit, ut sibi non nisi tormentum esset, quod ornamentum aliis videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035309Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, Rose's ingenuity prevailed: for she secretly fastened a long, pointed spike to the underside, and thus she did not so much place the thorn-studded wreath upon her head as press and drive it in, so that it was nothing but torture to her, though to others it seemed an ornament.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Victoriæ præmium fuit Rosæ patientia, adauctis repente doloribus, qui totam ei noctem fecere insomnem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051031Z">
        <seg>The reward for Victoria was Rose's patience, as her pains suddenly increased, keeping her awake all night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur Gaspari narrari insomnia, donec Filia explicato linteolo, quinquaginta libras, uti acceperat, prompte adnumeravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062303Z">
        <seg>Gaspari's nightmares seemed to be coming true until the daughter, having unfolded her handkerchief, promptly counted out the fifty pounds she had received.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur analecta esse nuptialis convivii, quo Rosa cum Sponso fruebatur in cælis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044542Z">
        <seg>They seemed to be excerpts from the wedding feast with which Rosa was enjoying herself in heaven with her Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbantur fartæ Mulcibero: nec videbantur solum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035347Z">
        <seg>They seemed full of Mulciber; nor did they seem alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur anulo restituto simul exuisse manicas &amp; pedicas, quamquam fateretur, longe melius sibi fuisse in illa deliciarum compede, quam in miserantis amici præpropera solutione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044709Z">
        <seg>He seemed to have shed his sleeves and stockings at once when the ring was restored, though he admitted it had been far better for him in that delightful shackle than in his compassionate friend's hasty release.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur ex pondere &amp; permodico involucri tumore parum metalli inesse sudariolo: at ubi Rosa id domi explicuit, ipsam argentearum librarum quinquagenam, quam pater debebat, reperit, ægrique lectulum cum nummis adiens, identidem repetebat, plurimum divinæ bonitati fidendum esse, hanc modo succurrisse medio peropportuno, quantum sufficiat ad amoliendam duri creditoris molestiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062300Z">
        <seg>It seemed from the weight and the very slight bulge of the wrapper that there was little metal in the handkerchief: But when Rosa unwrapped it at home, she found the very fifty silver pounds her father owed, and going to the sick man's bed with the money, she repeated again and again that one must trust greatly in divine goodness, which had just come to the rescue by the most opportune means, enough to remove the hard creditor's trouble.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur fortitudo lapidum fortitudo ejus, &amp; caro ejus ænea, id quod invictissimus Job sibi deesse querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060037Z">
        <seg>The strength of his stones seemed to be his strength, and his flesh was like bronze, which the most invincible Job complained was lacking in him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur huic angello Rosula universas suas inclusisse delicias, adeo totos in eo hærebat dies solitaria: illuc velut ad orandi, meditandique secessum a prandio, a lectulo, a cœna properabat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043255Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043255Z">
        <seg>It seemed that this little angel, Rosula, had packed all her delights into him, so completely did the solitary girl dwell in him all day long: she would hurry there from lunch, from her bed, from supper, as if to a retreat for prayer and meditation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videbatur, sub his innocentissimis symphoniæ conatibus, Rosa cum puello suo Amore repuerascere, &amp; oblita omnium dolorum, indulgebat simplici teneritudini intimorum affectuum: bulliebat corde, &amp; existimans, nullos e vicino sibi imminere arbitros, tantilla evaporatione ferventem spiritum solabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053232Z">
        <seg>It seemed that, beneath these most innocent attempts at harmony, Rosa, with her boy Love, was being reborn, and, forgetting all her sorrows, she indulged in the simple tenderness of her innermost affections: her heart bubbled with joy, and, believing no judges to be looming near her, she soothed her fervent spirit with that slight evaporation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur aliquid hujus artigisse Augustinus, quando invenit se longe a Deo in regione dissimilitudinis: aliquid item in se percepisse Rex citharœdus ps.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045809Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045809Z">
        <seg>It seems that Augustine sensed something of this when he found himself far from God in a region of dissimilarity; and likewise that the king-harpist perceived something in himself (Ps.).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur autem hæc antithesis vel oppositio in eo constistere, quod anachoretæ cibo non&lt;/e8&gt; selecto, &lt;e9&gt;sed primum obvio utantur&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041429Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041429Z">
        <seg>However, this antithesis or opposition appears to consist in the fact that the anchorites use not selected food, but rather whatever is readily available.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Videtur itaque hoc loco vox&lt;/e8&gt; crasis &lt;e9&gt;pro mixtionedesolationum accipi&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045921Z">
        <seg>It therefore appears that the word "crasis" is used here to mean a mixture of desolations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidisses hic denuo Lapam matrem filiæ Catharinæ probra, convicia, criminationes acervantem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042759Z">
        <seg>You would have seen here again Lapa, the mother of Catherine's daughter, heaping up insults, abuse, and accusations;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidisses immotam sub his cruciatibus Rosam, acsi non suus esset pollex, cui toties ferrum insæviebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034842Z">
        <seg>You would have seen Rose unmoved under these torments, as if the thumb it was so often lashing with iron were not her own.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidit librum, &amp; obstupuit Myrensis archiepiscopus, talium rerum scientissimus, illumque transcribi fecit, Romano Pontifici coram offerendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050107Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050107Z">
        <seg>The Archbishop of Myrenae, who was most learned in such matters, saw the book and was astonished, and had it transcribed to be presented to the Roman Pontiff in person.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vidit, illamque sinistræ nihil absimilem reperit, sanam, valentem, agilem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055430Z">
        <seg>He saw her and found her to be nothing like the left one: healthy, strong, and agile.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Viget in hoc casto pietatis sacrario diurnus &amp; nocturnus Dei cultus, devota festorum pompa, canonicus Officiorum ordo, jugis virginei chori psalmodia, sacræ supellectilis ac ecclesiæ accuratus nitor, apparatuum magnificentia copiosa.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062424Z">
        <seg>In this chaste sanctuary of piety, the daily and nightly worship of God flourishes, the devout pomp of feasts, the canonical order of the Offices, the unceasing psalmody of the virgin choir, the meticulous splendor of the sacred furnishings and church, and the abundant magnificence of the apparatus.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir apostolicus, ac Seraphici Ordinis jubar eximium, venerabilis Pater F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041538Z">
        <seg>Apostolic man, and an outstanding luminary of the Seraphic Order, the venerable Father F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir apostolicus, ac Seraphici Ordinis jubar eximium, venerabilis Pater F. Franciscus Solanus, in vita &amp; post mortem, constanti fama sanctitatis ac prodigiorum gloria clarus&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt;, pro concione obscurius forte nonnulla protulerat, quæ Limensium vulgus prave intellecta detorserat in sensum prorsus alienum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000529Z">
        <seg>The apostolic man, and an outstanding luminary of the Seraphic Order, the venerable Father F. Francis Solanus, in life and after death, renowned for his constant reputation of holiness and the glory of his miracles, had perhaps delivered some things more obscurely in his sermons, which the common people of Lima, having misunderstood them, twisted into a meaning entirely foreign to them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vir ille sanctitatis fama conspicuus (de quo supra in fine capitis 8) dum Rosæ defunctæ coronam spineam devotionis ergo contrectarat&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, diutius hæsit in anulo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044520Z">
        <seg>That man, conspicuous for his reputation of holiness (of whom above, at the end of chapter 8), while he had handled the crown of thorns of the deceased Rose for the sake of devotion, lingered longer on the ring;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginem per humeros corripuit, strinxit, contorsit, quasi in minutissimas partes membratim laceraturus obluctantem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052116Z">
        <seg>He seized the virgin by the shoulders, squeezed her, and twisted her, as if he were going to tear her limb from limb into the smallest pieces while she struggled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virgini tamen hæc rudissima gausapina non tam cilicium, quam abolla videbatur, eaque per complures annos est usa, donec ob frequentem sanguinis vomitum, alieno coacta fuit imperio, noxiam hanc exuere cataphractam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041851Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041851Z">
        <seg>To the virgin, however, this most rudimentary hairshirt seemed less a hairshirt than a scourge, and she wore it for several years, until, because of frequent vomiting of blood, she was forced by another's command to cast off this harmful armor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginis modestia, sobrietas, devotio, seculi fuga, silentium, vigiliæ, impatienti matri mera delicta erant, quibus castigandis non clamores, scommata, convitia, non demum pugni sufficiebant, &amp; calces: nodosus ex malo cydonia&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; baculus persæpe ad manum fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052503Z">
        <seg>The virgin's modesty, sobriety, devotion, flight from the world, silence, and vigils were mere offenses to her impatient mother, for chastising which shouts, scoffs, insults, and finally fists and kicks were not enough; a gnarled cudgel of bad quince was often at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virginis sanctissimo Rosario præstantis imaginem elegerunt, &amp; quotannis feria secunda post Dominicam, &lt;b3&gt;Quasi modo geniti&lt;/b3&gt;&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;d&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt;, instituta publica supplicatione (cui tum ipsi, tum singulorum Ordinum Regulares, &amp; universa tribunalium dicasteria&lt;s6&gt;&lt;a7&gt;e&lt;/a7&gt;&lt;/s6&gt; interveniunt) eamdem in suo apud FF.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054258Z">
        <seg>They chose an image of the most holy Rosary of the Virgin, and every year on the second feria after Dominica, &lt;b3&gt;As newly born&lt;/b3&gt;&lt;s4&gt;&lt;a5&gt;d&lt;/a5&gt;&lt;/s4&gt;, by a public decree of supplication (to which both they themselves, the Regulars of each Order, and all the dicasteries of the tribunals&lt;s6&gt;&lt;a7&gt;e&lt;/a7&gt;&lt;/s6&gt; attend), they instituted the same in their own church at the Franciscan Fathers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Virgo sat perite sentiebat, non tam a noxa vel intemperie humorum, quam a benevola Sponsi manu hæc sibi agonalia festa provenire.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052621Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052621Z">
        <seg>The virgin rightly perceived that these agonizing festivals did not arise so much from harm or the temperaments of the humors, but from the Spouse's benevolent hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Visa est ad hujus doloris aliquam similitudinem aut proportionem assurgere ille, quem a mordacissimis scrupulis patitur mens delicata, cum cælum sibi vectibus clausum, Deum hostiliter aversum, infernum rictu immani patulum suspicatur anima, sibimet ad modicum derelicta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045837Z">
        <seg>It seems that some likeness or proportion of this pain arises in the delicate mind that suffers from the most biting scruples, when the soul, left to itself for a little while, suspects heaven barred against it by its beams, God turned away in hostility, hell gaping open with a monstrous yawn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vita ipsius merum erat pietatis speculum, ita quod putaretur virtutes singulas in gradu heroico possidere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050048Z">
        <seg>His life itself was a pure mirror of piety, so that he was thought to possess each virtue in a heroic degree.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix Puella verbum protulerat, cum sub oculis omnium pullus repente assurrexit in pedes, alisque fortiter excussis, canore &amp; hilariter cucurivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061544Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061544Z">
        <seg>Scarcely had the girl spoken a word when, before everyone's eyes, the chick suddenly sprang to its feet, flapped its wings vigorously, and chirped cheerfully.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix bolum glutiverat, cum præsaga periculi, comiter mensam reliquit, in vicinum conclavi oratorium se proripuit, valvas adduxit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041116Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041116Z">
        <seg>She had scarcely swallowed the morsel when, with a premonition of danger, she politely left the table, rushed into the adjoining oratory, and drew the bolts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vix pluribus horis, hac sese angustia Virgo explicuit, ac per aliquot dies nec passum progredi, nec plantis consistere potuit, donec restituta sibi consuetæ abstinentiæ potestate, sensim &amp; ipsa restitueretur sibi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041051Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041051Z">
        <seg>In barely a few hours, this anguish had worn the Virgin out, and for several days she could neither walk a step nor stand on her feet, until, having regained the power of her accustomed abstinence, she too was gradually restored to herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vociferabatur cor afflictum, Deus, Deus meus, ut quid me dereliquisti?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045537Z">
        <seg>The afflicted heart cried out, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vola ephebe cælice;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051611Z">
        <seg>Fly, heavenly youth;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Vox Græca&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;περικάρδιον&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;oritur ex præpositione&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;περὶ&lt;/s4&gt; circum &lt;e5&gt;&amp; substantivo&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;καρδία&lt;/s6&gt;, &lt;e7&gt;quod&lt;/e7&gt; cor &lt;e8&gt;significat&lt;/e8&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052953Z">
        <seg>The Greek wordpericardion&lt;/e1&gt; comes from the preposition peri&lt;/s4&gt; "around" and the noun kardia&lt;/s6&gt;, which means "heart."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>X referetur) de consensu parentis adinvenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040143Z">
        <seg>X is referred to) concerning the parent's consent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XI inter dotalia pacta solicitudinem alendorum parentum a Rosa in se transtulerat) ipsa de consensu matris manum suam aperuit inopi, &amp; palmas suas extendit ad pauperem, cum his dividendo eleëmosynas, quas insperato receperat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061023Z">
        <seg>Having taken upon herself the care of her parents' support as one of her dowry stipulations, she, with her mother's consent, opened her hand to the needy and stretched out her palms to the poor, sharing with them the alms she had unexpectedly received.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XI, cum de pronubo Rosæ anulo agendum erit: hic sufficiat innuisse, quam serio ac festive cælum ipsum huic coronæ applauserit, quando in honorem tam rigidæ compunctionis, unctionem distillavit tam suavem ac pretiosam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042400Z">
        <seg>XI, when it comes to the betrothal ring of the Rose: it suffices here to have hinted at how seriously and festively heaven itself applauded this crown, when in honor of such rigid compunction it distilled an anointing so sweet and precious.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XII) spiritus examini subjecta fuit, grandia ac miranda detexit, quia debuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040844Z">
        <seg>XII) The spirit was subjected to an examination, discovered great and wonderful things, because it had to;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIII) quotidie in suo ad divinam unionem reditu nova illustratione comperiebat, sese in Sponsi favore confirmatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061950Z">
        <seg>XIII) Every day, in her return to divine union, she discovered a new illustration, finding herself confirmed in the favor of the Bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIII.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035123Z">
        <seg>Thirteen</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIV, atque ex eo plura &amp; potiora rescivisse de arcanis suis cum hac imagine commerciis, visionibus, alloquiis, paulo fidentius deinceps huic matronæ fassa est magnam eorum partem, quæ examinatori suo pridem aperuerat eo candore, quo debebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054351Z">
        <seg>XIV, and having learned from this more and greater things about her secrets through this image, commerce, visions, and conversations, she thereafter confessed to this matron with a little more confidence a large part of them, which she had long since revealed to her examiner with the candor that was due.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIV, fateri compulsa fuit) de intimis gratiæ thesauris rara quædam seraphici amoris incendia descenderunt in Rosam, secumque traxere longo ordine divitias inæstimabiles variorum charismatum, quibus animam Sponsæ ditabat, illustrabat, ornabat, profusa tam nobilis Sponsi magnificentia: pignora erant, &amp; monilia, datæque fidei pretiosi arrhabones, humanis verbis inexplicabiles: unde &amp; Virgo de his a suo examinatore pressius interrogata, nec negare ausa, simplicissime per solam vocabulorum inopiam se excusavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044433Z">
        <seg>XIV, compelled to confess) rare fires of seraphic love descended upon Rose from the innermost treasures of grace, and with them drew in long train the inestimable riches of various charisms, by which the soul of the Bride was enriched, illuminated, and adorned, through the lavish magnificence of so noble a Spouse: they were pledges and jewels, and precious arrabones of the given faith, inexpressible in human words: hence, when the Virgin was pressed by her examiner on these matters, and dared not deny, she excused herself most simply by the mere poverty of words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XIV.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044814Z">
        <seg>Fourteen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>XXVI.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061944Z">
        <seg>Twenty-six.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[100] [&amp; matrem hæc observantem] Cæterum lapilli sparsim ac mobiliter dispositi facile cedebant moli, quoties in aliud latus vertendum erat corpusculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001045Z">
        <seg>[100] [and the mother observing these things] Moreover, the little stones, scattered and arranged so as to be movable, gave way easily to the force whenever the little body had to be turned to the other side.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[101] [pie falleret;] Subiit ergo nova ac postrema lectuli dirissime asperandi cogitatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001058Z">
        <seg>[101] [pie would deceive;] Then a new and final thought occurred to him for making the little bed most horribly harsh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[10] [&amp; tantum quinquennis,] Rosæ quinquenni &amp; adhuc inter pueritiæ infantiæque confinia constitutæ, notitia ac timor Dei sese insinuavit modo prorsus admirabili;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203451Z">
        <seg>[10] [Only five years old,] to Rose, who was five and still standing on the border between childhood and infancy, the knowledge and fear of God insinuated itself in a truly marvelous way;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[11] Hinc subito in se reducta cœpit odisse suosmet capillos, [accepta singulari divinitatis cognitione,] firmare animum adversus omnes offensas Dei, detestari occasiones, quæcumque inferno militarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203317Z">
        <seg>[11] [Only five years old,] to Rose, who was five and still standing on the border between childhood and infancy, the knowledge and fear of God insinuated itself in a truly marvelous way;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[12] Demum sub hac tam præmatura ac sublimi Dei notitia quinquennis Rosa sensit, [perpetuam virginitatem] apertius trahi se ac præparari ad S. Catharinæ Senensis excelsa tirocinia: siquidem hujus exemplo confestim &amp; ipsa virginitatis votum emisit inter hæc primula tenellæ ætatis crepuscula, quinto nimirum ætatis anno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202758Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202313Z">
        <seg>[12] Finally, under this so early and sublime knowledge of God, five-year-old Rosa felt, [perpetual virginity] was more clearly drawn and prepared for the exalted disciplines of St. Catherine of Siena: for by her example she too immediately made a vow of virginity in the very dawn of her tender years, namely in the fifth year of her age;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[13] Unum hic prætereundum non est, quod, qui deinde Virginis conscientiam moderati sunt, [Deo vovit.] prudenter conjecere, superna hæc gratiæ allectamenta ipsi obtigisse sub illo primo usus rationis diluculo, quo juxta veriorem theologiam quilibet in Deum finem ultimum sese tenetur convertere pro suæ capacitatis modulo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203453Z">
        <seg>[13] One thing here must not be overlooked, that those who have since moderated the Virgin's conscience, [Vowed to God.] prudently conjectured that these heavenly allurements of grace had been granted to her at that first dawn of the use of reason, when, according to the more accurate theology, every person is bound to turn himself to God as his ultimate end, in proportion to his capacity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[15] Accidit, quod coram honestis matronis Rosa, [corpus ornare] præsente genitrice, importunius rogaretur suo capiti circumdare textum e floribus sertum, quod illic forte odoris gratia in medio positum cunctis arridebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203318Z">
        <seg>[15] It happened that in the presence of honorable matrons, Rosa, with her mother present, was more importunately asked to place the wreath of flowers on her head, which by chance, for the sake of its fragrance, had been set in the middle and pleased everyone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[16] Aliud.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000837Z">
        <seg>[16] Another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[17] [filia subtiliter excogitavit modum,] Verumtamen induciæ fuerunt istæ, non sincera pax: nam mater ad alias artes brevi conversa, nunc tentare Filiam, ut puellari usu se comeret, ut collo monilia, brachiis armillas, vultu cerussam pro more regionis admitteret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000841Z">
        <seg>[17] [The daughter had subtly devised a plan.] Nevertheless, these were only a truce, not a sincere peace: for the mother, soon resorting to other arts, now tried to persuade her daughter to adopt a girlish style, to wear necklaces around her neck, bracelets on her arms, and ceruse on her face according to the custom of the region.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[18] [quo matri obediret,] Visum hoc matri opportunum compendium, quidquid vellet, extorquendi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000844Z">
        <seg>[18] [so that he would obey his mother,] She saw this as a convenient way to extort from him whatever she wanted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[19] Excogitavit tandem ingeniosa Virgo rationem idoneam, [ac Deum non offenderet.] qua deinceps &amp; matris substaret imperio, &amp; nihilominus reliquam de corporis sui cultu vexam redimeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000847Z">
        <seg>[19] The ingenious Virgin at last devised a suitable plan [so as not to offend God], by which she would henceforth both submit to her mother's authority and nevertheless free the rest of her person from vexation in its adornment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[20] Canta&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt; in regno Peruano locus est metalli fodinis percelebris, [In aliis autem rebus] at putris soli exhalationibus, &amp; algore tetro admodum insalubris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000850Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000850Z">
        <seg>Cañab in the Peruvian kingdom is a place renowned for its metal mines, but in other respects it is extremely unhealthy because of the exhalations from its putrid soil and its bitter cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[21] Aliquando mater exploratura tam accuratæ obedientiæ pronitatem, [mira simplicitate &amp; reverentia] mandavit Filiæ, quod sericos flores, quos tunc forte pingebat acu, præpostero filorum ductu formaret contra omnem artis ideam, rectumque ordinem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000853Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000853Z">
        <seg>[21] At one point, the mother, to test her daughter's readiness for such exacting obedience, [with remarkable simplicity and reverence] commanded her to shape the silk flowers she was then painting with a needle by running the threads in reverse, against all the rules of the art and the proper order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[22] [obtemperabat matri] Triennio circiter ante felicem Rosæ obitum contigit, eam ab illustri matrona D. Maria de Usategui, consorte regii quæstoris D. Gundisalvi de la Massa, e paterno domicilio invitari ad suarum ædium gratuitum contubernium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000856Z">
        <seg>[22] [She obeyed her mother] About three years before the blessed death of Rose, it happened that she was invited by the illustrious matron D. Maria de Usategui, wife of the royal treasurer D. Gundisalvi de la Massa, from her father's home to a free lodging in her own house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[23] [aliisque superioribus] Sed longe mirabilior obedientia ultra mortem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000905Z">
        <seg>[23] [and other preceding ones] But obedience beyond death is far more marvelous.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[24] [ac confessariis suis.] Ex his quilibet facillime æstimarit, quam accurate suis confessariis obtemperarit Rosa ad huc mortalis: nam ex quo vestem S. Patris Dominici assumpserat, ab ore suorum patrum spiritualium pendebat tota, &amp; quidquid hi vel obiter innuissent, id Virgini non mandatum, sed cæleste oraculum videbatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000909Z">
        <seg>[24] [and to her confessors.] From this anyone could easily see how precisely Rosa had obeyed her confessors up to this point: for ever since she had taken the habit of St. Dominic, she hung entirely on the words of her spiritual fathers, and whatever they even hinted at seemed not a command to the Virgin but a heavenly oracle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[25] Sed redeamus ad matrem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000912Z">
        <seg>But let us return to the mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[26] Quæstor Gundisalvus (de quo supra, &amp; frequentius infra) tacitus mirabatur exhaustam viribus Rosam, [indefesso manuum labore,] ac diurnis operibus extreme lassam, nihilominus in mediam usque noctem labori manuum insistere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203455Z">
        <seg>[26] Quaestor Gundisalvus (of whom mention is made above, and more frequently below) marveled in silence that Rosa, though drained of strength, [with tireless labor of her hands,] and utterly weary from her daily tasks, nevertheless persisted in manual work until the middle of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[27] Mirabilius est, quod Ludovica Urtado de Bustamante, [&amp; solerti industria] vidua Bartholomæi Alphonsi de Lombrera vexilliferi, asseverat in Processu, Rosam tot sanctis exercitiis interdiu noctuque districtam, tot affatim morbis impeditam, tot supernis raptibus crebro absorptam, unico tamen die tot laborum pensa solitam absolvere, quot alia quævis etiam celerrima, peritissima, vix integro quatriduo absolvisset: &amp; ne putemus ob tantam festinæ Rosæ velocitatem, (ut fit) hiulca, manca, imperfecta prodiisse opera;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000918Z">
        <seg>[27] More marvelous is the fact that Ludovica Urtado de Bustamante, [by her diligent industry] widow of Bartholomew Alphonsus de Lombrera, standard-bearer, affirms in the Process, that Rosa was so occupied with holy exercises day and night, so impeded by a multitude of illnesses, so often absorbed in heavenly raptures, yet accustomed to complete in a single day so many tasks that any other, even the swiftest and most skilled, would have scarcely finished in four days: and lest we suppose that, because of Rose's great haste, her works were (as is often the case) produced hollow, incomplete, and imperfect;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[28] Neque hic stetit anxius amor ac solicitudo filiæ, [egestatem parentum suorum sublevare conabatur,] conantis omni ingenio parentum egestatem sublevare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203456Z">
        <seg>[28] Nor did the anxious love and concern for his daughter stop here; he was trying with all his ingenuity to relieve his parents' poverty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[29] [eos ægrotantes obsequio juvabat,] Si quando patrem aut matrem dejecisset corporis ægritudo, nil Filiæ antiquius, nil sanctius, nil solemnius erat, quam, sepositis omnibus, decumbenti servire, medicinas conquirere, suis manibus parare, inferre, bolatimque porrigere esculenta, refrigeria adaptare, applicare lenitiva.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203456Z">
        <seg>[29] [she helped the sick with care,]If at any time her father or mother was laid low by illness, nothing was more important, more sacred, or more solemn for the daughter than, setting all else aside, to serve the invalid, to seek out medicines, to prepare them with her own hands, to administer them, to bring food in small portions, to provide cooling remedies, and to apply soothing lotions.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; in Baptismo accipit nomen Elizabethæ] Nascenti Rosæ paterna domus obtigit in vico S. Dominici, ac pone quidem juxta Ædem S. Spiritus&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, ut vel ipse locus videatur futuræ tum sanctimoniæ tum instituti signasse præsagium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203313Z">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; at Baptism she receives the name Elizabeth] When Rose was born, the paternal house on St. Dominic Street fell to her lot, and indeed right next to the Church of the Holy Spirit&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt;, so that even the place itself seems to have foretold both her future sanctity and her institute.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; in Baptismo accipit nomen Elizabethæ] Nascenti Rosæ paterna domus obtigit in vico S. Dominici, ac pone quidem juxta Ædem S. Spiritus&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;f&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;, ut vel ipse locus videatur futuræ tum sanctimoniæ tum instituti signasse præsagium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202332Z">
        <seg>[2] [&amp; at Baptism she receives the name Elizabeth] When Rose was born, the paternal house on St. Dominic Street fell to her lot, and indeed right next to the Church of the Holy Spiritf, so that even the place itself seems to have foretold both her future sanctity and her institute.&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[30] [&amp; afflictos consolabatur.] Factum est, ac abundantius quidem, quam moribundæ Filiæ solicita pietas flagitarat: simul enim ut Virgo spiritum reddidit, matrem superne tanta solatiorum diluvia inundarunt, ut sub juramento fassa sit, diu cor suum capiendæ huic dulcedini angustum laborasse sub quadam tempestate gaudiorum, ac velut pectoris intersepta perrupturum, anhelis subsultasse tripudiis, ita quod in eodem Rosæ morientis conclavi quærendus sibi fuerit secretior angulus, in quo liberius sub tantæ lætitiæ suavissima divulsione palpitans respiraret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203456Z">
        <seg>[30] [&amp; consolated the afflicted.]It happened, and more abundantly indeed, than the solicitous piety of the dying Daughter had demanded: for as soon as the Virgin yielded up her spirit, such a flood of consolations from on high inundated the mother, that she confessed under oath, her heart had long laboured to contain this sweetness, under a certain tempest of joys, and as if her chest were about to burst, she leapt about with gasping, triumphant leaps, so that in the very same chamber of the dying Rose she had to seek a more secluded corner, in which, under the most exquisite tearing-asunder of such great joy, she could breathe more freely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[32] Ex adverso credebat innocens Rosa, tempestivo (de quo supra) capillorum excidio, [cui mater illam destinabat,] ut quondam S. Catharina de Senis, amputasse spem matris, ac nuptiarum tendiculas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203458Z">
        <seg>On the contrary, the innocent Rosa believed that by the timely (as mentioned above) cutting of her hair, [which her mother had destined for her,] she had, like St. Catherine of Siena of old, cut off her mother's hope and the snares of marriage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[33] In his matrona quædam, genere &amp; opibus clara, [acriter objurgatur,] uni filio suo Rosam conjugem, sibi nurum, vehementer optabat, utique felici commercio plus bonorum adeptura, quam datura cum hærede filio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203458Z">
        <seg>[33] In these circumstances a certain matron, distinguished by her birth and wealth, was earnestly urging her one and only son to take Rosam as his wife, for she believed that by this happy alliance she would acquire more goods than she would give up with her son the heir.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[34] [eique in novo S. Claræ cœnobio] Videbantur in hoc unum conspirasse omnia, ne Rosa Tertiariarum assequeretur habitum, quem tot votis ambiebat, tot suspiriis, tanto lachrymarum sumptu licitabatur: nam ecce alia oblata est religiosi instituti via, quæ quidem Rosæ displicere neutiquam potuisset, nisi unice ad S. Catharinæ Senensis alta vestigia fuisset cælitus destinata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203459Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203459Z">
        <seg>[34] [and in the new convent of St. Clare] It seemed that everything conspired to prevent Rose from obtaining the habit of the Tertiaries, which she so earnestly desired, and for which she was entreating with so many vows, so many sighs, and at the cost of so many tears: for behold, another path to the religious life was offered, which indeed could in no way have displeased Rosa, had it not been divinely destined solely for the high footsteps of St. Catherine of Siena.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[35] [locus offertur;] His, inquam, auspicibus formabatur recens monasterium, &amp; ultro invitabatur velut ad dignissimum tali flore plantarium Rosa, jam inter primas ejusdem fundatrices nominatim electa, censa, approbata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203501Z">
        <seg>[35] [place offered;] With these auspices, I say, the new monastery was being founded, and Rosa—already chosen by name, consecrated, and approved as one of its foremost foundresses—was invited of her own accord as if to the most worthy planting ground for such a flower.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[36] [sed ex voluntate divina,] Hoc ut magis innotesceret, binis e cælo prodigiis opus fuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203503Z">
        <seg>[36] [but by divine will,] For this to become more widely known, two prodigies from heaven were required;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[37] Ceterum vix ad orationem humi genua fixerat, [quæ declarata fuisse dicitur] cum sensit pavimento sese immobiliter acclavatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203503Z">
        <seg>[37] However, she had scarcely knelt on the ground for the prayer, [which is said to have been declared], when she felt herself nailed immovably to the floor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[38] Alterum prodigium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203503Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203503Z">
        <seg>[38] Another prodigy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[39] Nec fefellit eventus jam securam: nam paulo post, [statuit imitari S. Catharinam Senensem,] obstaculis undique submotis, facta est voti compos, inque Rosariano Deiparæ sacello, ubi quondam immobilis hæserat, solemni ceremonia, de manibus P. Magistri F. Alphonsi Velasquez, Ordinis Prædicatorum sui tunc confessarii (cui id P. Provincialis commiserat) induta est veste bicolori, quam in Senensi magistra dilexerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000518Z">
        <seg>[39] Nor did the outcome disappoint her now that she was secure: for a little later, [she resolved to imitate St. Catherine of Siena,] with all obstacles removed, she fulfilled her vow and, in the Rosary chapel of the Blessed Virgin, where she had once been held motionless, by solemn ceremony, from the hands of Father Master F. From the hands of Father Master F. Alphonsus Velasquez, her then confessor of the Order of Preachers (to whom the Provincial Father had entrusted the task), she was clothed in the two-colored habit which she had loved in her Sienese mistress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[3] [quod mater ejus ob prodigium postea mutavit,] Porro ut Rosæ nomen, divinitus soboli destinatum, post trium mensium ambages demum erumperet, prodigium exspectabatur, quod tale fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211840Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203314Z">
        <seg>[3] [which his mother later changed because of a prodigy,] Furthermore, so that the name Rose, divinely destined for the child, might at last emerge after three months of twists and turns, a prodigy was awaited, which was as follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[40] Nimirum cum infante creverat hoc desiderium: siquidem a quinto suæ ætatis anno S. Catharinæ Senensi jam sese addixerat, [cujus exemplo,] quod ex ore ipsiusmet Rosæ affirmant se percepisse testes secundus &amp; vigesimus secundus in citato sæpius Processu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203505Z">
        <seg>[40] Indeed, this desire had grown with her from infancy: for from the age of five she had already consecrated herself to St. Catherine of Siena, [whose example,] as the second and twenty-second witnesses in the aforementioned Process repeatedly affirm, they learned from Rose's own mouth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[41] [post superatas varias difficultates,] Quæstor Gundisalvus, vir optimus, quique apud Virginem sibi concreditam valebat autoritate, imo &amp; domestico imperio, seu sponte, seu aliunde inductus, suggerebat reformatam sacri Carmeli regulam, quam Rosæ genio credebat aptius convenire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203506Z">
        <seg>[After overcoming various difficulties,] Quaestor Gundisalvus, a most excellent man, who had taken refuge with the Virgin,[41] [After overcoming various difficulties,] Questor Gundisalvus, a most excellent man, who, with the Virgin entrusted to his care, had authority—nay, even domestic sway—whether of his own accord or induced by others, proposed a reformed rule for the sacred Carmel, which he believed more aptly suited to the genius of the Rose;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[42] [&amp; maturam deliberationem,] Voto respondit eventus: nam operante Numine, supradictorum theologorum quaternio paribus utrimque votis pependit semper immobilis, stupuitque, neutri partium occurrere pondus, quo sese a tam constanti æquilibrio tandem explicarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203507Z">
        <seg>[42] [And mature deliberation,] The outcome answered the vow: for, with the Deity at work, the quartet of the aforementioned theologians hung in equal balance on both sides, ever motionless, and marveled that neither side could find the weight by which they might at last break away from such a steadfast equilibrium.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[43] Eliso igitur hoc ariete, superfuit alius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203507Z">
        <seg>[43] Therefore, having rammed this one, another remained.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[44] Evenit, uti speraverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203511Z">
        <seg>It happened, as he had hoped;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[46] [morbos peccatis suis imputabat,] Assueverat morborum suorum (quos in ægro exhaustoque corpusculo sustinuit crebros &amp; sævissimos) celare magnitudinem, ne quis subveniret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203513Z">
        <seg>[46] He attributed his illnesses to his own sins. He had grown accustomed to hiding the severity of his ailments (which he endured in his sick, emaciated little body, frequent and most severe) so that no one would come to his aid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[47] Verum si eo procederent, ut refellendis hujuscemodi vituperiis encomia Rosæ laudesque opponerent, [a laudibus suis abhorrebat,] expavescebat misera, ac velut fulminis ictu perculsa tremebat, pallore, silentio, gemitu mœstissime protestans se torqueri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203514Z">
        <seg>[47] But if they went so far as to oppose the praises and lauds of Rosa with refutations of such criticisms,[she abhorred her own praises,] the poor wretch was terrified, and as if struck by lightning she trembled, protesting most mournfully with pallor, silence, and groans that she was being tormented.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[48] Quoddam perarduum heroicæ virtutis opus ad S. Catharinæ Senensis imitationem Rosa tentaverat, [innocens reprehensa veniam deprecabatur,] perfecerat: quod cum ipsius valetudini summe noxium &amp; exitiale crederet domina Isabella Mexia, horrore pio stimulata, P. Magistrum F. Alphonsum Velasquez, confessarium Virginis, admonuit, ut acerba reprehensione factum, velut excessum temerarium improbaret, ac Rosam a similibus ultra vires tentandis absterreret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000523Z">
        <seg>[48] Rosa had attempted a certain exceedingly arduous work of heroic virtue, in imitation of St. Catherine of Siena, [an innocent woman reproved, she begged for pardon,] and had accomplished it: which, believing it to be exceedingly harmful and fatal to her health, Lady Isabella Mexia, moved by pious horror, She admonished Father Master Friar Alphonsus Velasquez, the Virgin's confessor, to disapprove of the act with a harsh reprimand, as a rash excess, and to deter Rosa from attempting similar things beyond her strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[49] Quandocumque ad confessariorum pedes in suimet accusationem procumbebat, [coram confessariis minimos defectus suos exaggerabat,] ita mox obruebatur lachrymis, interpolabatur singultibus, dehiscebat profundis ex imo corde suspiriis, ut credidisses non nisi enormium flagitiorum ream esse, quæ sic plangebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203520Z">
        <seg>Whenever he fell at the feet of the confessors to accuse himself, [he exaggerated his slightest failings before them,] she was so soon overwhelmed with tears, broken by sobs, and rent by deep sighs from the bottom of her heart, that you would have believed she was guilty of nothing less than enormous crimes, for which she wept so bitterly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[4] [ita ut primum appellaretur Rosa,] Verumtamen lapsis aliquot annis, de Rosæ nomine aliam indixit litem ipsamet Virginis pavida conscientia, nimirum ut ita præberetur occasio locusque cælesti oraculo, quo Rosæ nomen ultimo erat confirmandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203449Z">
        <seg>[4] [so that she would first be called Rosa,] However, after several years had passed, the Virgin herself, with a fearful conscience, brought another suit concerning the name Rosa, namely to provide an occasion and a place for a heavenly oracle by which the name Rosa was to be finally confirmed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[50] [&amp; se mancipiis inferiorem reputabat.] Testabatur Gundisalvi quæstoris uxor D. Maria Usatigui, quod ejus in ædibus dum triennlum vixit Rosa, adeo sese omnium reputarit infimam, ut non soli patrifamilias &amp; conjugi, sed tenellis quoque filiabus, imo servis, ancillis, mancipiis domesticis subjecta ad nutum, singulorum æque placita, velut imperia, exceperit, favorem arbitrata, si quid sibi ceu precario in domum receptæ heriliter mandabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203521Z">
        <seg>Maria Usatigui, because Rosa had lived in her house for three years, considered herself so inferior to everyone that she submitted to the wills of not only the head of the household and her husband, but also to her tender daughters, indeed, subject to the will of servants, maids, and household slaves, she received everyone's commands as if they were orders, believing she had favor whenever anything was commanded of her as a mistress, as if she had been taken into the house on sufferance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[51] [Præter dotes corporis,] Diu nescivit Rosa, quidquam vel minimum in se reliquum esse, quod laudi pateret, aut pulchrum æstimari posset, donec femina quædam obiter manus Virginis conspicata (ut est muliebre ingenium ad similia lynceum, &amp; insigniter oculatum) commendavit in iis candorem, decentiam, proportionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203524Z">
        <seg>[51][Besides her bodily charms,] For a long time Rosa was unaware that she had anything left in herself worthy of praise or that could be considered beautiful, until a certain woman, having casually caught sight of the Virgin's hands (for women's nature is keen and remarkably observant of such things), commended their whiteness, grace, and proportion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[52] [austeritatem jejuniorum,] Pridem studuerat humilis Virgo omni arte nativam speciosissimi vultus sui gratiam abolere, ne posset mortalibus oculis placere, idque non solum jugi inedia, dirisque macerationibus, sed &amp; aqua gelida creberrime toti corpori superfusa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203524Z">
        <seg>[52] [the austerity of fasts,] The humble Virgin had long sought by every means to erase the native beauty of her most lovely face, so that it might not please mortal eyes, and this not only by constant fasting and dire austerities, but also by frequently pouring cold water over her entire body;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[53] Inde lepidum obvenit quibusdam Argis&lt;a0&gt;a&lt;/a0&gt; per otium curiosis, [ita ut post diuturnum jejunium laute epulata crederetur,] qui in feria sexta Parasceves, forte in templi vestibulo confabulantes, observaverant Rosam cum matre domum ab ecclesia redeuntem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203525Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203525Z">
        <seg>[53] Hence a charming incident befell certain Argives who, out of idle curiosity, were chatting in the temple vestibule and saw Rosa returning home from church with her mother.&lt;/s4&gt; who, while chatting in the temple vestibule on the Friday of Holy Week, had seen Rosa returning home from church with her mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[54] Par ei solicitudo &amp; cura fuit abscondendi reliquos suorum charismatum thesauros, [aliasque virtutes] ne humanis laudibus paterent in prædam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203526Z">
        <seg>[54] He had the same concern and care for concealing the remaining treasures of his charisms, [and other virtues], so that they would not fall prey to human praise.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[55] Diu recusavit, diu cunctatus est confessarius, haud ignarus, [solicite abscondebat,] quam sibi id arduum futurum esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203527Z">
        <seg>[55] He refused for a long time; the confessor hesitated for a long time, not unaware that he was hiding it [carefully], how arduous it would be for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[56] [&amp; Deiparam adjutricem habuit hujus humilitatis,] Favit mirabiliter huic virtuti Deipara, humilium idea;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203527Z">
        <seg>[56] [&amp; He had the Virgin Mary as an assistant in this humility,] The Virgin Mary wonderfully favored this virtue, the model of the humble;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[57] [quam cum blanda affabilitate conjungebat.] Tantam ejus humilitatem indivulse comitabatur suavissima mansuetudo, placida affabilitas, mite supercilium: mirati sunt quotquot eam noverant, numquam ab illius ore excidisse verbum duriusculum, obstreperum, criticum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203527Z">
        <seg>[57] [which he combined with gentle affability.][57] Such great humility was inseparably accompanied by the sweetest meekness, placid affability, and a gentle brow: all who knew her were amazed that not a single harsh, boisterous, or critical word ever escaped her lips;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[59] Nondum infantiæ pannos &amp; annos pupula excesserat, [a teneris annis ita assueverat abstinentiæ,] cum sibi (quod illi ætatulæ peregrinum &amp; grave est) omni fructuum esu interdixit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>[59] The pupil had not yet passed the swaddling clothes and years of infancy, [having been accustomed to abstinence from a tender age,] when she forbade herself, which is unusual and burdensome for that age, to eat any fruit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[5] Habent quidem hoc (Ambrosio teste) Sanctorum merita, [cui deinde cognomen a sancta Maria additum est.] ut a Deo nomen accipiant, sed qui ultra nomen etiam cognomen e cælo accipiant, raros puto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203450Z">
        <seg>[5] The merits of the Saints do indeed have this (as Ambrose testifies), [to whom the surname from Saint Mary was then added.] but I think those who receive a surname from heaven in addition to their name are rare.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[60] Interdum nobiles matronæ, devotionis ergo, [ut carnes aut jusculum digerere non posset,] matrem cum Filia in suas ædes invitabant, epuloque sobrie instructo, rogitabant utramque secum una accumbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>Sometimes noble matrons, out of devotion, would invite the mother and daughter into their homes, and after a sober meal was set out, they would ask both of them to recline with them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[61] Semel ex morbo sat ancipiti emerserat, utque citius instauraretur, [&amp; post ea ex mandato degustata] ex præscripto medici, pauxillum carnis paventi ejus stomacho obtruserant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>[61] Having once emerged from a rather serious illness, and so that he might recover more quickly, [and after that, by order, having tasted] by the doctor's prescription, they had forced a little meat down his stomach, which was still tender.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[62] [identidem in morbum recideret,] Laborabat frequenti ischiade&lt;a0&gt;b&lt;/a0&gt;, ac plerumque ea dolorum vehementia, quæ stomachi fundum everteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>[62] [He would repeatedly relapse into illness,] He suffered from frequent sciatica, and usually from such violent pains that they would upset the very depths of his stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[63] [sicut frequens experientia docuit.] Ibidem alia vice observatis in Virgine manifestæ debilitatis nimiis indiciis, voluit idem paterfamilias, quod ex ala coctæ aviculæ quid modicum sumeret, quo labentem stomachum nonnihil erigeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203529Z">
        <seg>[63] As frequent experience has shown. There, on another occasion, observing in the virgin clear signs of manifest weakness, the same paterfamilias wished to take a little from the roasted bird's wing to somewhat lift her failing stomach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[64] [Unde arbitrio ejus relinqui debuit] Impulerunt cruda hæc experimenta commiserantem quæstorem, ut Virginis confessarios, medicos, parentes, sigillatim ac pene adjurato obsecraverit, ne deinceps imperato carnium esu, Rosam in vitæ discrimen adductam vellent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203530Z">
        <seg>[64][Wherefore it ought to have been left to his discretion] These cruel experiments moved the compassionate inquisitor to beseech the Virgin's confessors, physicians, and parents, one by one and almost under oath, not to wish to bring Rose to the brink of death by ordering her to eat meat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[65] Tardius matri persuasum fuit, ut Filiæ tam severam tenuissimi victus rationem formamque æquanimiter indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203530Z">
        <seg>It was later persuaded to the mother that she should equably indulge her daughter in such a strict regimen and form of the most meager diet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[66] Audierat in vicina sylva pronasci herbam insigniter præ ceteris amaram: [quem herbis amarissimis] hanc sibi clam adferri, ex ea deinceps insipidum pulmentarium sibi jussit apparari, &amp; ne tam exquisitæ provisionis annona deficeret, herbam eamdem suo hortulo implantavit, suis manibus solicite coluit, persuadens matri, valere in usum medicinæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203328Z">
        <seg>[66] He had heard that in a nearby forest a notably bitter herb grew: [which, among the most bitter herbs] was secretly brought to him; from then on he had a tasteless porridge made for himself from it, and so that this most exquisite provision would not run out, he planted the same herb in his own little garden, carefully tended it with his own hands, persuading his mother that it was useful for medicinal purposes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[67] Fruticat in illo occiduarum Indiarum fertili tractu planta mirabilis, [occulte condiebat.] GRANADILLA&lt;a0&gt;f&lt;/a0&gt; vernaculis dicitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203331Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203331Z">
        <seg>[67] In that fertile tract of the western Indies grows a marvelous plant, [secretly prepared.] It is called GRANADILLA by the natives;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[68] [Jejunia ejus erant adeo frequentia] Transeundum nunc ad jejunia, tametsi dubitari haud immerito possit, ab ipsa esurie, an a talibus cibis durius esse tormentum potuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203332Z">
        <seg>[68] His fasts were so frequent. We must now turn to the fasts, although one might not unreasonably doubt whether the hunger itself or such food could have been a more severe torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[69] [diuturnaque,] Residuo anni tempore panis mensuram tam sibi curtam indixerat, ut quod unicæ diei sat esse credebatur, integro vix octiduo totum absumeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203333Z">
        <seg>[69] For the rest of the year he had prescribed such a scant measure of bread that what was believed to be enough for a single day would scarcely last him eight days in all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[6] Sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis infantia ac pueritia tam dulcis omnibus ac blanda fuisse legitur, [Puella adhuc tenera præbet indicium] ut non Catharinam, sed Euphrosynam&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt; plerique cœperint appellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T214253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203315Z">
        <seg>[6] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone [The still tender girl provides a clue.] that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosyne&lt;a0&gt;k&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[6] Sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis infantia ac pueritia tam dulcis omnibus ac blanda fuisse legitur, [Puella adhuc tenera præbet indicium] ut non Catharinam, sed Euphrosynam&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt; plerique cœperint appellare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T202149Z">
        <seg>[6] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone, [A still-tender girl provides a sign] so that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosyne&lt;s0&gt;&lt;a1&gt;k&lt;/a1&gt;&lt;/s0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[70] [&amp; cibus tam parcus,] Nescio, jejunium an plane inediam appellavero, quod sequitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203333Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203333Z">
        <seg>[70] [And the food so meager,] I don't know whether I should call what follows a fast or outright starvation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[71] Aliquando communicanti solæ species sacramentales tantum roboris contulerunt, [ut merito inedia vocari posset.] ut in fortitudine cibi illius toto octiduo nullum aliud nutrimentum admiserit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203334Z">
        <seg>[71] At one time, the mere sacramental species, which she received, imparted such strength that, rightly, it could be called a fast, for in the fortitude of that food she accepted no other nourishment for the entire eight days.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[73] [pro publica regni Peruani] Siqua aut regno Peruano, aut urbi Limensi, patriæ suæ, flagella divinitus imminebant, suis conabatur flagellis divertere: nec animarum in purgatoriis flammis bullientium immemor, duplicabat ictus catenarum in nova cruoris profluvia, quæ rogi partem extinguerent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203336Z">
        <seg>[73][for the public good of the Kingdom of Peru] If any divine scourges threatened either the Kingdom of Peru or the city of Lima, his native land, he strove to divert them by his own: and not oblivious of the souls seething in the flames of purgatory, he doubled the strokes of the chains in new torrents of blood, which would extinguish part of the pyre.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[74] [vel urbis Limensis necessitate] Assueverant domestici (quamquam inscia Virgine) ex ædium parte secretissima, per singulas noctes audire hos disciplinarum ictus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203336Z">
        <seg>[74] [or by the necessity of the city of Lima] The household servants (though the Virgin was unaware) had grown accustomed, from the most secret part of the house, to hearing these blows of discipline every night;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[75] Magister F. Joannes de Lorenzana confessarius Virginis, [ferreo flagello aliisque modis,] cum hunc disciplinarum rigorem imbecillitati corporis paulo æquius censuisset attemperandum, Rosa maximis ab eo precibus extorsit, quo saltem liceret sibi intra certum dierum spatium verbera quinquies mille sibi infligere, qui pie creditur esse numerus ictuum, quos in sua crudeli flagellatione Christus excepit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203338Z">
        <seg>John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, [with an iron scourge and other methods,] When John of Lorenzana, the Virgin's confessor, [with an iron scourge and other means,] judged that this severity of discipline should be tempered somewhat more justly to her bodily frailty, Rose extorted from him by the most earnest prayers that she might at least be permitted, within a certain space of days, to inflict upon herself five thousand lashes, which is piously believed to be the number of blows that Christ received in his own cruel scourging.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[76] Porro, quam avide in pœnitentiæ exercitia propenderit Rosæ genius, [quibus ab ipsa pueritia assueverat,] jam inde ferme ab infantia seriis præludiis abunde ostenderat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203339Z">
        <seg>[76] Moreover, how eagerly Rosa's genius inclined toward the exercises of penance, [to which she had been accustomed from her very childhood,] she had already abundantly shown by serious preludes, almost from infancy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[77] Aliis vicibus, sub eodem silentio, rogabat sibi gravari scapulam informi trabe, [tenerum corpus affligebat,] collectisque exiguis viribus, conabatur obnixa sufferre durissimam sarcinam, dum preculas suas bene longas absolveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203339Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203339Z">
        <seg>At other times, under the same silence, she would ask for an inform bar to be placed on her shoulder, afflicting her tender body, and, gathering her small remaining strength, she would try to bear the most difficult burden with all her might, while she unwound her very long braids.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[78] [&amp; ferrea catena se flagellare prohibita,] Inhibitum ei fuerat (uti supra attigimus) ne amplius ferream catenam in usum flagelli adhiberet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203340Z">
        <seg>[78] [and forbidden to flog himself with an iron chain,] He had been forbidden (as we noted above) to use the iron chain as a whip any longer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[79] [ea corpusculum tam arcte cinxit, ut carnem penetraret, ] Crescebat interim sub indissolubili vinculo atrox ischiadis cruciatus, qui demum invitæ Rosæ, post longos gemitus, etiam miserandos ejulatus extorsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203340Z">
        <seg>[79] [It had so tightly girdled the little body that it pierced the flesh,] Meanwhile, under the unbreakable bond, the dreadful torment of sciatica grew, which at last, after long groans, wrung even pitiful sobs from a reluctant Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[7] Trimula erat, quando in tenerrima indole promicare cœperunt supra ætatem robusta virtutum specimina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T211913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203315Z">
        <seg>[7] The infancy and childhood of Saint Catherine of Siena are said to have been so sweet and gentle to everyone, [A still-tender girl provides a sign] so that most people began to call her not Catherine, but Euphrosynek.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[80] [&amp; ideo illam confessarius etiam auferri jusserit.] Vix laceratæ cutis vulnera coïerant, quando Rosa ad suam catenam rediit, pristinoque ferri tormento se rursus incinxit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203341Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203341Z">
        <seg>[80] [And so the confessor even ordered her to be taken away.] The wounds of her torn flesh had barely begun to heal when Rosa returned to her chain and again fastened herself to the iron torment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[81] Neque pulpæ brachiorum ab ejusmodi vinculis immunes erant: [Deinde brachia vinculis constringebat,] his enim Rosa, ad cruciatum, arcta quædam ligamina circumdederat, quæ perpetim torquebant complicatos musculos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203346Z">
        <seg>[81] Nor were the paps of the arms immune from such bonds: [Then he would bind the arms with cords,] for Rosa, for the purpose of torture, had fastened certain tight ligatures around them, which perpetually twisted the coiled muscles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[82] Verum ubi pro ingenti munere ditatam se vidit brevi cilicio, [hispidum cilicium gestabat,] quasi purpuram, byssum, trabeamve nacta, sibi suæque tremulæ carni gratulabatur de novo pœnitentiæ baltheo, rigoris paludamento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203350Z">
        <seg>[82] But when she saw herself enriched with a huge gift—a short tunic of coarse cloth, as though she had received purple, linen, or a robe—she congratulated herself and her trembling flesh on the new belt of penance, the cloak of austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[83] Aliam tamen ipsa adinvenit castigandi corpusculi rationem, [&amp; singula corporis sui membra] quam priori clam substitueret, minori forsan valetudinis damno, at non leviori molestia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203532Z">
        <seg>[83] She herself, however, devised another method of punishing her little body, [and each of her body's members], which she would secretly substitute for the previous one, perhaps with less harm to her health, but not with less inconvenience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[84] [peculiari tormento cruciabat.] His blandimentis Rosa demulcebat tenue illud corpusculum, cui alias a crebris infirmitatibus sat pœnarum erat, ut infra suo loco videbitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203543Z">
        <seg>[84] [tortured with a peculiar torment.] With these caresses Rosa soothed that frail little body, which otherwise had already suffered enough from its frequent infirmities, as will be seen below in its proper place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[86] Annos complusculos duravit, &amp; induruit pia Virgo sub hoc oneroso capitis spineto;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000943Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000943Z">
        <seg>It lasted for several years, and the pious Virgin endured under this burdensome crown of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[87] Novo igitur ardore, ac patiendi desiderio, lamellam argenteam sibi flexit in circulum, [cujus forma describitur,] huic ab intus ex eodem metallo tres infixit aculeorum ordines, ita ut quælibet series triginta &amp; tres contineret aculeos, videlicet pro numero annorum Christi, quos mortalis pro mortalibus explevit in terra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000949Z">
        <seg>[87] With a new zeal, then, and a desire to suffer, he bent a silver lamella into a circle [whose shape is described], and into this he fixed from the inside, of the same metal, three rows of spikes, so that each row contained thirty-three spikes, namely, for the number of years of Christ, which a mortal fulfilled on earth for mortals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[88] [&amp; qua Virgo studiose utebatur,] Dentatum hoc sertum quam dire momorderit, quam cruciabiliter omni ex parte pupugerit velatum sacræ puellæ caput, cui torquendo unica sat fuisset acicula, ex eo fas est conjicere, quod spinæ non simul omnes, sed aliæ post alias, binæ, ternæ, pluresve, successione molestissima, nudatam invadebant cutem, proque occasione ac varietate motuum, singulæ obliquabantur in singulis vulnerum angustiis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000955Z">
        <seg>[88] [&amp; which the Virgin diligently used,] How terribly this crowned headgear gnawed, how painfully it pricked the veiled head of the sacred maiden from every side—for twisting it, a single needle would have been enough—can be inferred from the fact that the spikes did not all come at once, but one after another, in pairs, threes, or even more, in the most troublesome succession, invaded the exposed skin, and, according to the occasion and variety of the movements, each one was twisted in the individual narrowness of the wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[89] [ut se quotidie] Verum hæc Rosæ ludicra videbantur, &amp; artis digna succursu, quo sævirent inclementius.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000958Z">
        <seg>But these things seemed like child's play to Rosa, and worthy of the aid of art, by which they might rage more fiercely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[8] [dum horridum chirurgi ferrum] Quadriennium vivendo expleverat cum summam capitis sui cutem species quædam impetiginis cœpit obcrustare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203316Z">
        <seg>[of future holiness and patience,] And first of all, she was patient and her spirit among her pains was fearless, strong, and calm, so that it was clear she was a rose that neither the thorns frighten, wear down, nor bring down.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[90] [magis magisque torqueret;] Nec tamen contenta hoc quotidiano cruciatu, modum reperit, quo nova indies vulnera aperiret: nam coronam mane circumdatura capiti, numquam eodem figebat loco, quo pridie steterat, sed mutato paulisper situ, in aliam aliamque partem transponebat redimiculum, ut alterna variatione immitius pungeret, atque in intacto dolorem renovaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001002Z">
        <seg>[90] [torturing more and more;] Yet not content with this daily torment, she found a way to open fresh wounds each day: for when she put the crown on her head in the morning, she never fastened it in the same spot where it had stood the day before, but by shifting its position slightly she moved the headband to one side and then the other, so that by alternating its placement it would pierce more deeply and renew the pain in the uninjured spot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[91] Diu familiares, domesticos, ipsamque matrem latuit hoc tam cruenti pectinis vepretum: [donec hoc tormentum, quod domesticos diu latuit,] quis enim sub vitta ac velo cautissimæ Virginis, aut notasset, aut quæsivisset tam ferale supplicium?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001005Z">
        <seg>[91] This most bloody briar-patch long eluded her familiars, her household, and even her own mother: [until this torment, which had long eluded her household,] for who, under the band and veil of the most cautious Virgin, would have either noticed or sought out such a fateful punishment?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[92] Hic Rosa magis se proditam quam percussam dolens, [&amp; tandem casu detectum est,] dupliciter erubuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001008Z">
        <seg>[92] Here Rosa, feeling more betrayed than struck, blushed doubly; [and at last it was discovered by chance,]</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[93] Nec mora: delata fuit rei series ad eum, qui certius coronæ usum Virgini prohiberet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001011Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001011Z">
        <seg>Without delay, the series of events was brought to the one who would more surely forbid the Virgin's use of the crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[94] [Hæc aculeata corona] At Rosa pluris faciens coronam, quam regnum, gaudebat hanc se recuperasse salvo spinarum numero, ac saltem non nisi modice ea castigatione exarmatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001020Z">
        <seg>[94] [This thorny crown] But Rose, valuing the crown more than the kingdom, rejoiced to have recovered it with the full number of its thorns intact, and at least only slightly weakened by that chastisement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[95] [tangentem spirituali gaudio replevit] Quidam religiosissimus Dei servus, paulo post elatæ Rosæ exequias, domum Gundisalvi quæstoris adierat, eo dumtaxat animo, ut instrumenta, quibus Rosa virgineam carnem vivens domuerat, devote contrectaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001023Z">
        <seg>[95] [He filled the one who touched it with spiritual joy] A certain most devout servant of God, shortly after the funeral of the exalted Rose, had gone to the house of Gundisalvus the treasurer, with the sole intention of devoutly handling the instruments by which Rose, while still living, had mortified her virgin flesh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[96] [post obitum sanctæ Virginis,] Non pigeat, hic denuo reflectere oculos in spiniferum S. Catharinæ Senensis prototypon, quod Rosa nostra spinis coronata sic imitari visa est, ut plene idipsum non tam expresserit, quam in se transtulerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001026Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001026Z">
        <seg>[96] [After the death of the holy Virgin,] Let us not be reluctant to turn our eyes once more here to the thorny prototypal figure of St. Catherine of Siena, for our Rose, crowned with thorns, was seen to imitate it in such a way that she did not so much fully express it as she did transfer it into herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[97] Huc demum &amp; illud videtur pertinere, quod, [&amp; ipsa propter illam in cælo coronari visa est.] dum mortualis pompa sic Rosam coronabat in terris, ostento non absimili Beatorum curia eamdem coronavit in cælis, ut utrimque deprehendatur, successisse in ipsammet S. Catharinæ Senensis coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001030Z">
        <seg>[97] This, too, seems to pertain to what has been said: that while the funeral procession thus crowned Rose on earth, the court of the Blessed, by a vision not unlike a miracle, crowned her in heaven; so that it is evident from both that she succeeded to the very crown of St. Catherine of Siena herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[99] [quibus ipsa durissime cubaret,] Læta successu Rosa, pergebat securior non tantum nudare sibi tabulas, quibus indormisceret, sed &amp; rudius saxum circumspicere, quod cervici supponeret: verum post longas ambages, tandem interrupit Filiæ solertiam expergefacta mater, ac velut deprehenso immani facinore puellam increpuit, adjecto callidæ obstinationis convicio: Si sic ei constitutum erat matrem fallere, iret porro, sibique deinceps suopte arbitrio lectum, quem vellet, contabularet, dum tamen capiti saltem humanius cervical concederet, ipsisque tabulis unicum simplicis straguli induceret operimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001042Z">
        <seg>[99] [on which she herself would sleep most harshly,] Pleased with her success, Rosa went on more securely, not only laying bare for herself the planks on which she would sleep, but also roughly hewing a stone to place under her neck: But after long detours, the mother, awakened at last, finally interrupted her daughter's cunning and, as if she had discovered some monstrous crime, rebuked the girl, adding a taunt about her clever stubbornness: If it was her intention to deceive her mother in this way, she might go on and from then on board up whatever bed she pleased of her own accord, so long as she at least provided her neck with a more humane pillow and covered the boards themselves with a single simple blanket.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[9] [aliosque cruciatus invicto animo sustinet,] Sed anxia parens, postridie revulso leniter cataplasmate, inhorruit, videns filiolæ verticem hinc bullis turgidum, inde fossulis profunde ulceratum, &amp; “O (inquit) misera, siccine totam hanc transegisti noctem sub hujusmodi cruciatus voraci ardore”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203303Z">
        <seg>[9] But the anxious mother, the next day gently removing the poultice, was horrified to see her little daughter's head, from one side swollen with blisters, from the other deeply ulcerated with pits, &amp; "O (she said) miserable one, did you spend the whole of this night in such a voracious ardor of this kind of torment?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Capiti suo imposuit coronam] &lt;b0&gt;Decebat&lt;/b0&gt; charam B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000937Z">
        <seg>[He placed a crown on her head] It was fitting for the beloved B.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Dum S. Rosa renuit inire matrimonium,] &lt;b0&gt;Variæ&lt;/b0&gt; ac sine numero sunt viæ, anfractus, itinera, quibus æterna Sapientia selectas animas in sublime perfectionis cacumen facit eluctari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203457Z">
        <seg>[While St. Rose refused to enter into marriage,] Various and countless are the ways, the winding paths, the journeys by which the eternal Wisdom leads chosen souls to struggle up to the lofty summit of perfection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Dum mater S. Rosam invitam] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; concursu duorum præceptorum, quo simul honorare patrem &amp; matrem, simul Deo magis quam hominibus obedire justum jussumque est, Rosæ frequenter ingenio &amp; solertia opus fuit, ut neutrum offenderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T033146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203453Z">
        <seg>[While Mother was inviting St. Rose] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; the dilemma between honoring one's father and mother and obeying God rather than men, it was often necessary for Rose, by her wit and prudence, to avoid offending either.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Exemplo S. Catharinæ Senensis] &lt;b0&gt;Fundamento&lt;/b0&gt; tam profundo, lato, ac solido dignum fuit ac tutum, prægrandia, excelsa, ponderosa superstitui fastigia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203528Z">
        <seg>[By the example of St. Catherine of Siena] A foundation so deep, broad, and solid was worthy and safe for supporting the immense, lofty, and ponderous superstructure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Ingeniosa Virgovarios excogitavit modos,] &lt;b0&gt;Tanta&lt;/b0&gt; ei corporis macerandi cura fuit, ut nec pauculas nocturnæ quietis horas tormento vacuas esse permiserit: hinc strati sui voluit eam esse duritiem, quæ somnum abigeret magis, quam vocaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001039Z">
        <seg>[He devised ingenious contrivances for the virgarians,] He was so intent on emaciating his body that he did not even allow the few hours of nightly rest to be free from torment: hence he had his bed made so hard that it would drive away sleep rather than invite it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Joannes de Villalobos noster nonnihil mitigavit.] nempe ad unum ex Patribus spiritualibus, quorum ductui Rosa obtemperabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001014Z">
        <seg>[Our John of Villalobos somewhat mitigated it.] namely, to one of the spiritual Fathers, whose guidance Rosa obeyed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Sancta divinam iram placatura] &lt;b0&gt;Mirum&lt;/b0&gt;, in arido totque jejuniis extenuato corpusculo superfuisse locum, qui flagella exciperet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203335Z">
        <seg>[The holy one, having placated the divine wrath] It is marvelous that in a body so dry and emaciated by so many fasts there was still room to receive the scourges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[Virgo ex humilitate] &lt;b0&gt;In&lt;/b0&gt; suo nihilo cominus ac penitissime agnoscendo, quam profunde jugiterque Rosa perstiterit occupata, omnis humillimæ suæ vitæ clamat actio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203513Z">
        <seg>In her acknowledgment of her own nothingness, how profoundly and unceasingly the Rose remained occupied, the very action of her humblest life proclaims.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[futuræ sanctitatis ac patientiæ,] Et primum quidem tolerantiæ fuit animique inter dolores impavidi, fortis, tranquilli, ut liqueret Rosam esse, quam spinæ nec terrent, nec terunt, aut dejiciunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203316Z">
        <seg>[7] She was three years old when, in her most tender disposition, she began to display above her age robust examples of virtue.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>[nonaginta novem aculeis armatam,] verum id nonnisi puerile fuit tirocinium robustioris tormenti, quod ex secunda corona, aculeis nonaginta &amp; novem armata, imminebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000946Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000946Z">
        <seg>[armed with ninety-nine spikes,] but that was nothing more than a childish trial of a more powerful torment, which loomed from the second crown, armed with ninety-nine spikes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;περίφρασις&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;idem est, quod apud Latinos&lt;/e2&gt; circumlocutio, &lt;e3&gt;quæ huic examinatori adhibenda fuit, cum sancta Virgo terminos scholasticos non intelligeret&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062350Z">
        <seg>a Greek&lt;/e0&gt; periphrasis&lt;/s1&gt;, the same as the Latin circumlocutio&lt;/e2&gt;, which had to be used with this examiner, since the holy Virgin did not understand scholastic terms&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hi curiosi&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;observatores per tropum vocantur&lt;/e1&gt; Argi, &lt;e2&gt;alludendo ad Argum, filiæ Inachi custodem, quem poëtæ plurimis oculis præditum fuisse fabulantur, &amp; qui propterea a Græcis&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;πανόπτης&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, omnia videns, &lt;e5&gt;cognominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040934Z">
        <seg>These curious observers of St. Rose are tropically called Argus, alluding to Argus, the keeper of Inachus's daughter, whom poets fabled to have been endowed with many eyes, and who was therefore called by the Greeks panoptes, that is&lt;/e4&gt;, all-seeing, is nicknamed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hi similesque lusus amatorii forte non placebunt iis, quorum judietum seu cenfuram dehujusmodi ludis in Commentario prævio num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051453Z">
        <seg>These and similar amateur games may not please those whose judgment or censure of such games is expressed in the preliminary commentary, no.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hic alluditur ad duas voces Græcas&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;χεὶρ&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;&amp;&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;πῦρ&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;quarum prima&lt;/e4&gt; manum, &lt;e5&gt;altera&lt;/e5&gt; ignem &lt;e6&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T043948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035839Z">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Here an allusion is made to two Greek words&lt;/e0&gt;, &lt;s1&gt;χεὶρ&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;and&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;πῦρ&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;the first of which means&lt;/e4&gt; hand, &lt;e5&gt;the second&lt;/e5&gt; fire&lt;e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis &amp; Apostolicus vir post Hansenianam hujus Vitæ editionem anno 1675 in numerum Beatorum relatus est, quem deinde Benedictus XIII anno Christi 1726 solenniter catalogo Sanctorum adscripsit, ut in Actis ejus apud nos ad diem 24 Julii illustratis, sive tomo&lt;/e0&gt; V &lt;e1&gt;istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041953Z">
        <seg>This venerable and Apostolic man was included among the Blessed after the Hansenian edition of this Life in 1675, who was then solemnly enrolled in the catalog of the Saints by Benedict XIII in the year 1726, as is shown in his Acts with us for the day of July 24, or in volume V of this month from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis &amp; Apostolicus vir post Hansenianam hujus Vitæ editionem anno 1675 in numerum Beatorum relatus est, quem deinde Benedictus XIII anno Christi 1726 solenniter catalogo Sanctorum adscripsit, ut in Actis ejus apud nos ad diem 24 Julii illustratis, sive tomo&lt;/e0&gt; V &lt;e1&gt;istius mensis a pag. 847 licet videre&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000927Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000927Z">
        <seg>This venerable and Apostolic man was included among the Blessed after the Hansenian edition of this Life in 1675, who was then solemnly enrolled in the catalog of the Saints by Benedict XIII in the year 1726, as may be seen in his Acts illustrated by us for the day of July 24, or in the fifth volume of this month from page 847.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hoc loco biographus alludit ad medicam vel chirurgicam piæ duræque matris appellationem, de qua Castellus in Lexico medico apud nos pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042440Z">
        <seg>Here the biographer alludes to the appellation of the pious and stern mother as a physician or surgeon, which Castellus discusses in his Medical Lexicon, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Hoc loco biographus alludit ad medicam vel chirurgicam piæ duræque matris appellationem, de qua Castellus in Lexico medico apud nos pag. 322 sic disserit:&lt;/e0&gt; Membrana dura, inviolata, crassa, cuticularis (idem enim significant) est, quæ primum calvariæ ossibus exemptis, aut saltem apertis, cerebrum in orbem contegens apparet (quæ vulgo DURA MATER appellatur) cui altera tenuis mollisque subnectitur, quæ PIA MATER nominatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001033Z">
        <seg>Here the biographer alludes to the medical or surgical appellation of a pious and strong-willed mother, about which Castellus in his Medical Lexicon, at our page. 322 thus discusses:&lt;/e0&gt; It is the dura, an inviolate, thick, cuticular membrane (for they signify the same thing), which first appears, the bones of the skull having been removed or at least opened, covering the brain in a sphere (which is commonly called the DURA MATER), to which another, thin and soft, is attached, which is called the PIA MATER.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Honorius Philoponus Benedictinus eamdem Christianæ fidei originem Ordini suo adscribit, ut patet ex epilogo Operis sui de Navigatione ad Indiam occidentalem, quod anno 1620 edidit, &amp; in quo pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054652Z">
        <seg>Honorius Philoponus Benedictine ascribes the same origin of the Christian faith to his Order, as is evident from the epilogue of his work On Navigation to the West Indies, which he published in 1620, and in which page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;In Bibliotheca Societatis nostræ pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050736Z">
        <seg>In the Library of our Society, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Laudes &amp; munia hujus præclari viri apud Echardum in Bibliotheca Prædicatorum tomo 2 pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045354Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045354Z">
        <seg>Praises and offices of this distinguished man are in Echard, in the Library of the Preachers, volume 2, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Papebrochius noster ad diem 30 Aprilis Acta S. Catharinæ Senensis edidit, in quibus varia cum gestis hodiernæ Sanctæ concordant, ut tomo 3 istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040416Z">
        <seg>Our Papebrochius, on April 30, published the Acts of St. Catherine of Siena, in which various things correspond with the deeds of the present-day Saint, as in the third volume of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Papebrochius noster ad diem 30 Aprilis Acta S. Catharinæ Senensis edidit, in quibus varia cum gestis hodiernæ Sanctæ concordant, ut tomo 3 istius mensis a pag. 851 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203512Z">
        <seg>Our Papebrochius, on April 30, published the Acts of St. Catherine of Siena, in which various things correspond with the deeds of the present-day Saint, as in the third volume of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Rosa est flos sacer Veneri, quæ dum Adonidem ab apro læsum nudis pedibus opem latura accurrit, spinæ aculeo vulnerata est in pede, &amp; suo cruore tinxit rosam, ex quo sanguine rosæ, quæ prius albæ fuerant, deinde rubrum colorem induerunt, ut fabulosa poëtarum historia tradit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043956Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043956Z">
        <seg>The rose is a sacred flower to Venus, who, while rushing to aid Adonis wounded by a boar, ran on bare feet and was pricked in the foot by a thorn, staining the rose with her own blood, from which the roses, which had previously been white, then took on a red color, as the fabled history of the poets relates.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Sancta nostra hic metaphorice&lt;/e0&gt; heliotropium &lt;e1&gt;vocatur a flore, quem Græci&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἡλιοτρόπιον&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;appellant, eo quod ad solem se convertat, ex&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἥλιος&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;id est&lt;/e5&gt;, sol, &lt;e6&gt;&amp;&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;τρέπω&lt;/s7&gt; verto &lt;e8&gt;vel&lt;/e8&gt; converto, &lt;e9&gt;sicut passim notum est&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055504Z">
        <seg>our Holy One is here metaphorically&lt;/e0&gt; called a heliotrope, from the flower which the Greeks&lt;/e1&gt; ἡλιοτρόπιον&lt;/s2&gt; call, because it turns toward the sun, from&lt;/e3&gt; ἥλιος&lt;/s4&gt;, that is, the sun, &amp; τρέπω turn or convert, as is well known.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a &lt;e0&gt;Scriptor Vitæ hoc loco alludit ad historiam Eliæ, cui persecutionem Jezabelis fugienti cibus ab angelo allatus est, &amp; qui in fortitudine cibi illius usque ad Horeb montem Arabiæ pervenit, ut lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060322Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060322Z">
        <seg>The author of the Life here alludes to the story of Elijah, to whom, fleeing Jezebel's persecution, food was brought by an angel, and who, by the strength of that food, reached Mount Horeb in Arabia, as in the book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Callao &lt;e0&gt;est famosus Americæ portus, qui duabus leucis ab urbe Limana distat, &amp; quem Latius mox citatus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054153Z">
        <seg>Callao is a famous port of America, which lies two leagues from the city of Lima, and which Latius, soon to be cited, mentions in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Cancer &lt;e0&gt;est morbus ab atra bile effervescente proficiscens, tum alias corporis partes, tum frequenter mammas mulierum infestans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061612Z">
        <seg>Cancer is a disease arising from black bile that bubbles up, then invades other parts of the body and frequently attacks women's breasts.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Congerro &lt;e0&gt;vocatur is, cum quo familiariter versamur &amp; facete confabulamur, qui a Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;συνόμιλος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνομιλήτης&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052256Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052256Z">
        <seg>He is called a companion with whom we associate familiarly and converse wittily, who is called by the Greeks synomilos or synomilites.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Costæ &lt;e0&gt;sunt in humano corpore ossa illa, quæ spinæ cohærentiam, intestinaque suo complexu coërcent, ut notum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060902Z">
        <seg>The ribs are those bones in the human body which, as is well known, connect to the spine and enclose the intestines.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Hybla &lt;e0&gt;est mons Siciliæ thymo abundans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044718Z">
        <seg>Hybla is a mountain of Sicily abundant in thyme.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Lima &lt;e0&gt;est celeberrima novi orbis civitas, quæ ab Hispanis vernacule&lt;/e0&gt; ciudad de los reyes, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt; civitas regum, &lt;e2&gt;vocatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035049Z">
        <seg>Lima &lt;e0&gt;is the most famous city in the New World, which the Spaniards call in their native tongue&lt;/e0&gt; "Ciudad de los Reyes," &lt;e1&gt;that is&lt;/e1&gt;, "City of the Kings."&lt;e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Lima autem 120 in meridiem, &amp; 75 a Cusco, de qua posteriori civitate nunc aliquid notandum est&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050752Z">
        <seg>From Lima, however, it is 120 miles to the south and 75 from Cusco, about which latter city something must now be noted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Malus Cydonia, &lt;e0&gt;quam alii&lt;/e0&gt; cotoneam &lt;e1&gt;cognominant, est arbor passim nota, de qua Plinius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052903Z">
        <seg>The Cydonian apple, which others call the cotoneaster, is a tree commonly known, about which Pliny writes in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Paroxysmus &lt;e0&gt;aliter vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; accessio, &lt;e1&gt;vel&lt;/e1&gt; invasio, &lt;e2&gt;teste Castello in Lexico medico, estque&lt;/e2&gt; deterius tempus totius circuitus, quod est a primo insultu morbi usque ad tempus consistendi, sive per tertium, sive per quartum, sive quotidie fiat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041408Z">
        <seg>A paroxysm, also called an accession or an invasion, according to Castelo in the Medical Lexicon, is the worst part of the entire cycle, which is from the first onset of the disease until the time of its cessation, whether it occurs on the third, the fourth, or every day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Stricturæ &lt;e0&gt;proprie dicuntur scintillæ, quæ de ferro candenti micant, quando ignita ferri massa malleo tunditur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045909Z">
        <seg>Strictly speaking, sparks are called those that flash from glowing iron when a red-hot mass of iron is struck with a hammer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Tonsillæ &lt;e0&gt;sunt extremæ faucium partes circa radicem linguæ, gulam utrimque attingentes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043109Z">
        <seg>The tonsils are the extreme parts of the throat around the root of the tongue, touching the gullet on both sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a cælesti Sponso hæc mihi securissima fiducia est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052103Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052103Z">
        <seg>From the heavenly Spouse I have this most certain confidence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a prima Dei notitia repletam se fuisse timore &amp; horrore peccati, &amp; si quis motus indeliberatus adversus rationem nitebatur assurgere, repente ex apprehensione divinæ præsentiæ citra conflictum evanuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050340Z">
        <seg>that from the very first awareness of God she had been filled with fear and horror of sin, and if any involuntary movement against reason sought to arise, it would suddenly vanish, beyond any conflict, at the apprehension of the divine presence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>a Ætna &lt;e0&gt;mons ignivomus Siciliæ, cujus nomen hic metaphorice ac hyperbolice flagranti&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;cordi tribuitur&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053622Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053622Z">
        <seg>from Mount Etna, the fiery mountain of Sicily, whose name is here metaphorically and hyperbolically attributed to the burning heart of St. Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>abhorrere omnes, tamquam rem humanitus impossibilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062750Z">
        <seg>to abhor all, as if it were a humanly impossible thing;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac certa eram, illum officio non defuturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051750Z">
        <seg>And I was certain he would not fail in his duty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac medicinæ se subjecit, quam sciebat nil profuturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045754Z">
        <seg>and submitted himself to a medicine which he knew would be of no benefit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ac sæpe coram intimis gravissime questa est, jugi se dolore affici, quod sexus ac seculi conditio sibi non permitteret, in asperrimos montes procul secedere, ibique cavam rupem inquirere, ubi remota ab omni humano commercio, vitam degeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043950Z">
        <seg>and she has often complained most grievously in the presence of her intimates that she is afflicted by a constant pain, because her sex and her station in life would not permit her to retreat far away into the most rugged mountains, and there seek out a hollow rock, where, removed from all human contact, she might live.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>accendebat piarum meditationum lumina præcipue ex Ludovici Granatensis libello, qui de Oratione inscribitur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055727Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055727Z">
        <seg>He would kindle the lights of pious meditations, especially from the little book of Louis of Granada, which is titled On Prayer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>acceptavit æger munificam Rosæ largitatem, haud ignarus, quantum opulentiæ in dono tot meritorum lateret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060846Z">
        <seg>The patient accepted Rose's generous largess, not unaware of how much wealth lay hidden in a gift of so many merits.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>accessere item copioso numero felices animæ, similique adorationis ritu Salvatorem reveritæ, seorsim constiterunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052752Z">
        <seg>A great number of happy souls also approached, and, with a similar rite of adoration, reverently honored the Savior, standing apart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad SS. Trinitatis honorem soli tres hodie culices te pungent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043928Z">
        <seg>In honor of the Holy Trinity, only three mosquitoes will bite you today.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad hujus aspectum cor Virginis pulsu validiori, &amp; concitato videbatur in horologium transmutari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054838Z">
        <seg>At the sight of him, the Virgin's heart seemed to be transformed into a clock, beating more strongly and agitated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad istud (quod puerile, nimisque femineum aliis videbatur) crucis honorandæ studium se stimulis tam excelsis ac seriis excitari, ut repugnandi modus aut ratio numquam sibi ad manum sit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054931Z">
        <seg>that his zeal for honoring the cross (which to others seemed childish and overly feminine) was stirred by such lofty and serious incentives that he never had a way or reason to resist;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad quem, velut ad commune urbis oraculum, ardua quæque negotia certatim adferebantur ex consiliis antistitum, ex regio audientiæ (ut vocant) senatu, ex consistoriis, judiciis, dicasteriis, ex interno extimoque foro;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050203Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050203Z">
        <seg>to whom, as to the city's common oracle, the most arduous affairs were eagerly brought from the councils of the high priests, from the royal senate (as they call it), from the consistories, courts, and dicasteries, from the internal and external forum;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ad quid mihi cor istud, si nondum id pro tuo amore totum diffluit in cineres”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053049Z">
        <seg>What good is that heart to me, if it has not yet completely burned to ashes for your love?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adde his in obnixum universam hanc nostram Americam, imo totum potius terrarum orbem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062635Z">
        <seg>Add to these, in a manner of speaking, all of our America, or rather the whole world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>addebat ejusdem sumptus &amp; operis ramusculos prasinos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, porraceos, herbidos, omnique viroris amœnitate variegatos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060118Z">
        <seg>He added to it sprigs of the same cost and workmanship, greenish, leek-like, herbaceous, and variegated with every pleasing hue of foliage.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>addebat, torqueri ægrum urentibus scrupulis, affligi non certitudine vicinæ mortis, sed incertitudine vitæ melioris, eo quod meritis se vacuum agnosceret, contremiscere pavidum, ac sudare ad continuam severi Judicis, quem aditurus erat, memoriam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060831Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060831Z">
        <seg>He added that the sick man was tormented by burning scruples, afflicted not by the certainty of imminent death, but by the uncertainty of a better life, because he recognized himself to be devoid of merit; he trembled in fear and sweated from the constant memory of the stern Judge he was about to approach.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo hæc sacra imago trahebat, adeo firmabat virgunculam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054317Z">
        <seg>So powerfully did this sacred image draw her, so strongly did it fortify the maiden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo post varias, at numquam sat æquas comparationes, effari aliud non poterat quam: “Dolores inferni circumdederunt me, præoccupaverunt me laquei mortis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045847Z">
        <seg>After various, but never sufficiently fair comparisons, he could say nothing other than: "The pains of hell have surrounded me; the snares of death have overtaken me."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo quod famosus ille nostri seculi mystagogus Didacus Alvarez de Paz&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; Societatis Jesu, in Peruano tunc regno Provincialis, dum suas de oratione, meditatione, &amp; contemplatione præclarissimas lucubrationes apparabat, hunc virum in plurimis sibi duxerit consulendum, ipsumque tomo 3 de contemplatione lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050058Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050058Z">
        <seg>so much so that the famous mystagogue of our century, Diego Alvarez de Paz of the Society of Jesus, then Provincial of the Peruvian province, while preparing his most excellent treatises on prayer, meditation, and contemplation, considered this man the foremost to consult in many matters, and even cites him in the third volume on contemplation, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo quod toto illo quadriennio, quo cum parentibus in Canta morabatur, nec pedem ultra domicilii limen extulerit, nec vicinum deliciarum hortum videre dignata fuerit, nisi quod semel dumtaxat ibidem, ad invisendas Indicarum operarum molas, jussa fuit matronarum filias comitari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035945Z">
        <seg>so that during the entire four years she lived with her parents in Kent, she neither set foot beyond the threshold of her home nor deigned to see her neighbor's pleasure garden, except that on a single occasion she was ordered to accompany the matrons' daughters there to visit the mills of the Indian works.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adeo ut citra extremam vim &amp; cruciatum, nec panis bolum glutire, nec aquæ guttulam sorbillare se posse adverteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055951Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055951Z">
        <seg>He realized that, short of the utmost violence and torture, he could neither swallow a loaf of bread nor sip a drop of water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adjunge per hypothesim totius orci vires, artes, potentiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062638Z">
        <seg>Add, by hypothesis, the forces, arts, and power of the entire underworld;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>admonitus confessarius mandavit, a media saltem nocte horas omnino quatuor somno tribueret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035709Z">
        <seg>The confessor, having been admonished, commanded him to devote at least four hours to sleep, beginning at midnight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>adverterent, magnam hanc esse pharmacopœam, omni adversus peccata antidoto instructissimam, orationis usu purgari spiritum, religari animarum vulnera, hac aperiri cellam Sponsi unguentariam, omnisque ceromatis apothecam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045118Z">
        <seg>if they would turn their attention, this is a great pharmacopoeia, most fully equipped with every antidote against sins, by which the spirit is purified through the use of prayer, the wounds of the soul are bound up, the Spouse's perfumed chamber is opened, and the entire apothecary of ointments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>advocatoque in consilium unico germano fratre, paucis desiderium suum explicuit, mysterium celavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044439Z">
        <seg>And with his brother, the only one of his kind, as his advocate, he briefly explained his desire and concealed the mystery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>affabatur, venerabatur, dissuaviabat sacram imaginem, acsi præsentem e cælis haberet magistram suam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055039Z">
        <seg>She spoke to, venerated, and sweetly caressed the sacred image, as if she had her teacher present from the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>affirmat præ aliis testis sexagesimus octavus, illa singula tam consummatæ fuisse perfectionis, elegantiæ, curæ, ut plerumque metas humanæ artis &amp; industriæ viderentur excedere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035753Z">
        <seg>The sixty-eighth witness, above the others, affirms that those particulars were so consummate in perfection, elegance, and care that they generally seemed to exceed the limits of human art and industry.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>age in hoc vile corpusculum, quæ tibi Deus permiserit, certe in animam nil poteris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052100Z">
        <seg>Act in this vile body, whatever God has permitted you; you can certainly do nothing to the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>agnoscite periculum, cui vos implicuistis, misereamini vestrarummet animarum, oves perditæ, quas bonus Pastor per spinas &amp; tribulos, per sudores sanguineos, per vulnera &amp; crucem requirit, ut salvet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060533Z">
        <seg>Recognize the danger you have involved yourselves in, have mercy on your own souls, O lost sheep, whom the good Shepherd seeks to save through thorns and thistles, through bloody sweat, through wounds and the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aiebat terrores illos, horridasque imagines talem sibi inferre dolorem, qui millies &amp; millies sufficeret cor, vitam, animam eripere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045819Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045819Z">
        <seg>He said that those terrors and horrid images inflicted upon him a pain sufficient a thousand times over to snatch away his heart, his life, his soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aliis, de inferorum pœna cogitandum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052652Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052652Z">
        <seg>that others should think about the punishment of the underworld.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alter, quando inter labores manuum, externasque occupationes, mentem immobiliter figebat in Deum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044821Z">
        <seg>the other, when, amid manual labors and external occupations, he fixed his mind immovably on God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>alterum perpetua &amp; numquam interpolanda Dei amicitia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061748Z">
        <seg>the other, the perpetual and never-to-be-interpolated friendship of God;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>an non ego Flos campi melior tibi sum, omni basilico, totoque floreto paradisi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051232Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051232Z">
        <seg>Am I not better for you, Flower of the Field, than all basil and the entire flower of paradise?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>angebar, sudabam, anhelabam, videbaturque anima corporeo carcere teneri jam non posse, quin, eo relicto, sola agilior erumperet, clamatura per mundum: O si nossent mortales, quid sit gratia, quam formosa, nobilis, pretiosa, quantas in se complectatur divitias, quas opes, jubilos, delicias, utique ambirent omnes affligi &amp; pati, ultro postularent cuncti molestias, persecutiones, ægritudines, tormenta pro solo gratiæ opulentissimo lucro assequendo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052835Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052835Z">
        <seg>I was in anguish, I was sweating, I was gasping for breath, and it seemed the soul could no longer be held in its bodily prison, but, leaving it behind, would burst forth alone and more agile, crying out through the world: Oh, if mortals knew what grace is, how beautiful, noble, and precious it is, and how many riches, what treasures, what joys, and what delights it embraces, surely everyone would desire to be afflicted and to suffer, and all would willingly ask for hardships, persecutions, illnesses, and torments for the sake of obtaining the single, most opulent gain of grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>animam interimit, quidquid carni petulanti lenocinatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060654Z">
        <seg>It destroys the soul, whatever flatters the licentious flesh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>apage hos inanium argutiarum calamistros, facessant theatricæ ostentationis cothurni &amp; phaleræ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060555Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060555Z">
        <seg>Away with these calamus-wielding pedants of empty cleverness; let the theatrical display of cothurnus and phalera make way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ardores Spiritus usque ad animæ liquefactionem efficaces, blandos, subtiles.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044539Z">
        <seg>The ardors of the Spirit, effective up to the melting of the soul, are gentle, subtle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>art.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T071539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T071539Z">
        <seg>Article 72</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>aspirat mox gratiarum placidus Auster, &amp; fluunt aromata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050516Z">
        <seg>The gentle South Wind soon breathes its grace, and spices flow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>asserebat, hac se medullitus instaurari ac refici, nonnullosque in anima &amp; corpore effectus experiri simillimos iis, quos in sumptione SS. Eucharistiæ percipiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054137Z">
        <seg>He asserted that he was thereby thoroughly renewed and restored, and experienced certain effects in soul and body very similar to those he perceived in the reception of the Holy Eucharist.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>assultu, dente, ungue sæviebat, qua poterat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051933Z">
        <seg>He raged with a rush, with his teeth and nails, as best he could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Isabellæ (Deo mirabiliter disponente) innotuit, quæ &amp; pannum, quo Virgo labia deterserat, fortuito demum inventum, studiosius in facti memoriam asservavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061420Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061420Z">
        <seg>But to Isabella (as God miraculously arranged), it became known, and she, having at last by chance found the cloth with which the Virgin had wiped her lips, more devoutly preserved it as a memorial of the event.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa confestim servum expediens, heræ suæ renuntiare jussit, opportune beneficium venisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051725Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, immediately freeing the slave, ordered him to tell his mistress that the favor had come at the right time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa subito pudore in se reducta, objurgavit seipsam: “Quid (inquiens) rudis ista bestiola meum suumque Creatorem laudat, pastus sui oblita;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045031Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, suddenly abashed, rebuked herself: "What," she said, "is this rude little creature praising my Creator and its own, forgetting its own pasture?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa, nec mutato leviter colore, monuit, sperandum firmius in adjutorio Altissimi, &amp; maxime tunc, quando periculi vicinitas omne effugium interclusit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062133Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062133Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, her color not having changed in the slightest, warned that one should place a firmer hope in the help of the Most High, especially then, when the proximity of danger had cut off all escape.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosa, quæ interim opus suum lapidarium ex toto nondum absolverat, pudore suffusa, multis excusare se conabatur, quod necessitatibus parentum impedita, quod rudis adhuc illius artis, quæ tam inusitata esset femineis manibus, lino dumtaxat ac lanæ assuetis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044204Z">
        <seg>But Rosa, who had not yet completely finished her stonecutting work, blushing with shame, tried in many ways to excuse herself, saying she had been hindered by her parents' needs and was still unskilled in an art so unusual for a woman's hands, accustomed only to linen and wool.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Rosæ præ ipsa pupilla oculi charior erat sua anachoresis, longoque tempore ipsi valuit hæc solertia, donec tandem suspecta frequentia rem prodidit genitrici, quod ita accidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043348Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043348Z">
        <seg>But to Rosa, her own seclusion was dearer than the pupil of her eye, and this cunning served her well for a long time, until at last the frequent comings and goings betrayed the matter to her mother, which is how it happened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at Sabbatum erat, ac mane proximo ad sacram Synaxim accessura, cavebat jejunio, si forte medietas noctis jam præterierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051102Z">
        <seg>But it was the Sabbath, and she was to approach the sacred Synaxis the next morning; she was fasting, in case the midnight hour had already passed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at alacris mater, velut signo ad assultum dato, sævissimam illam catastam non aliter ac hostilem arcem invasit, truncos, ligna, tigna dejecit, luxavit asseres, &amp; non sine præcipiti manuum offendiculo testacea fragmenta sublegit, sparsit, tandemque in vicinum urbi fluvium, ne ad usum redirent, abjecit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042859Z">
        <seg>But the eager mother, as if a signal had been given for an assault, invaded that most savage pile as if it were a hostile fortress, threw down logs, beams, and timbers, loosened the planks, and not without a great risk of stumbling, picked up the broken pottery fragments, scattered them, and finally threw them into a nearby river, so that they could not be used again.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at biliosa complexio, &amp; assuetudo callum vitio induxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060725Z">
        <seg>but a choleric temperament, and habit had bred a callousness to wrongdoing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ceteris altius icta spectaculo mater subito complexu tenerisque osculis dissuaviavit parvulam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034656Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034656Z">
        <seg>But when the others were struck higher, the mother, suddenly distracted by the spectacle, soothed the little one with a embrace and tender kisses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at exercitio, conversatione, spiritu, plane religiosus, Limæ illis diebus inter selectos Dei servos eximius habebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050039Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050039Z">
        <seg>At prayer, in conversation, and in spirit, he was a thoroughly devout man, and in those days at Lima he was regarded as an outstanding member of God's chosen servants.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at honesta &amp; timida virgo se ignotam ignotis credere noluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061206Z">
        <seg>But the honorable and timid virgin would not trust strangers with her identity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at hæ ignorabant arcanum symbolum cordis, virgineo cordi inaratum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044123Z">
        <seg>but these were ignorant of the secret symbol of the heart, inscribed on the virgin's heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at hæc prodit turgentibus rursum oculis, palpebrisque subtristi rubore inflammatis, ut facile constaret, visum ea defluxione accensum, &amp; nimio dolore nictitantem, auræ solisque pericula neutiquam laturum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043404Z">
        <seg>But this is revealed by his eyes swelling again and his eyelids inflamed with a saddened flush, so that it was clear his vision, inflamed by that discharge and blinking from excessive pain, could in no way endure the dangers of the breeze or the sun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at illi nec satis assequebantur, quid Virgo dicere conaretur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045658Z">
        <seg>but they did not fully understand what the Virgin was trying to say.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at inventum nihil.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055129Z">
        <seg>but nothing was found.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ipsa, quo illæ tendebant, celerrime prægressa, occupavit indaginem, hisque mitissimis verbis abrupit molestiam: “Scito (inquit) mi Pater, me usque a teneris ardenter orasse Deum, ne sinat ulli mortalium innotescere, si quid altissima sua misericordia cœperit forsan intra animulæ meæ penetralia dignanter operari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040828Z">
        <seg>and she herself, having most swiftly anticipated their purpose, seized the inquiry, and with these most gentle words put an end to the disturbance: "Know, my Father," she said, "that from my earliest years I have ardently prayed to God not to allow any mortal to learn of it, should He perhaps begin to work within the innermost recesses of my little soul by His highest mercy."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at is dissuasit, quod vereretur facile sub putri aculeo, gravem in Rosæ capite purulentiam posse succrescere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042149Z">
        <seg>But he dissuaded her, for he feared that a serious, pus-filled infection could easily develop on Rose's head under a rotten thorn.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at magis, ut vidit filiam non mirari, utpote his usu ac frequentia dudum assuetam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051800Z">
        <seg>but rather, seeing that his daughter was not surprised, since she had long been accustomed to such things by frequent use.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at mater fastidio impar, Filiam, quasi manifeste insomniis rerum impossibilium delirantem, objurgavit coram omnibus: “Desine (inquit) ineptire fatua, memento non esse plebeiæ sortis, aut patrimonii vulgaris negotium, cœnobia fundare;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062325Z">
        <seg>But the mother, with unfeigned disgust, rebuked her daughter before everyone, as if she were clearly delirious with impossible dreams: "Stop your foolish nonsense," she said, "remember you are not of common birth, nor is it a matter of ordinary patrimony to found monasteries."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at mysterium ignorabant, quod supponerent, coronam per ipsummet sacristam e collo Deiparæ in digitos Filioli fuisse translatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043547Z">
        <seg>But they were unaware of the mystery they were implying: that the crown had been transferred by the sacristan himself from the neck of the Mother of God to the fingers of the little Son.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at mœsta &amp; verecunda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044257Z">
        <seg>but sad and shy</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at ne labente memoria quidquam præteriret, hoc ordine singula notarat in pagella: “Jesus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053545Z">
        <seg>So that nothing might be overlooked through a failing memory, he had noted each item in the following order on a small slip of paper: "Jesus."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at nihil elicuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050448Z">
        <seg>but nothing came of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at non ita placuit Virgunculæ, quam non contemptus, sed sordes offendebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034947Z">
        <seg>But this did not please the maiden, who was offended not by contempt, but by the filth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at nondum se scire, num forsitan illi divinitus sit reservata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040326Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040326Z">
        <seg>but he does not yet know whether perhaps it is divinely reserved for him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at numquam eo, quo tunc dulcedinis excessu, ea familiaritatis aura ac gratia, illo reconditi favoris singularissimo fulgetro;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044325Z">
        <seg>but never with that excess of sweetness, that aura and grace of familiarity, that most singular flash of hidden favor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at omnium importunior, quia subtilior: nam per ipsos humilitatis cuniculos inferne adactus, quatiebat Rosam unius commotione scrupuli, qua patuit via cogitationi pavidissimæ, &lt;s2&gt;[tertium Ordinem S. Dominici]&lt;/s2&gt; nimirum se sordidam peccatricem illo niveo Senensis Catharinæ velamento nimis indignam esse, plus eo vestis candore promitti foris, quam lateret intus, uni sibi deesse omnia, quæ extimo nitori respondeant: proinde habitum, qui Seraphicæ magistræ fuerat protestativum virtutis ac religionis symbolum, in se non nisi larvam esse, publicumque usurpatæ sanctimoniæ mendacium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040335Z">
        <seg>but the most importunate of all, because the most subtle: for through the very narrow passages of humility, hellishly driven, it shook Rose with a single tremor of scruples, which opened the way to the most fearful thought, &lt;s2&gt;[the Third Order of St. Dominic]&lt;/s2&gt; namely, that she was a filthy sinner, far too unworthy of that snowy veil of Catherine of Siena; that more was promised by the garment's whiteness outwardly than was hidden within; that she lacked everything that would correspond to its external splendor: Therefore, the habit, which for the Seraphic mistress had been a protestative symbol of virtue and religion, was in herself nothing but a mask and a public lie of assumed sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at omnium importunior, quia subtilior: nam per ipsos humilitatis cuniculos inferne adactus, quatiebat Rosam unius commotione scrupuli, qua patuit via cogitationi pavidissimæ, [tertium Ordinem S. Dominici] nimirum se sordidam peccatricem illo niveo Senensis Catharinæ velamento nimis indignam esse, plus eo vestis candore promitti foris, quam lateret intus, uni sibi deesse omnia, quæ extimo nitori respondeant: proinde habitum, qui Seraphicæ magistræ fuerat protestativum virtutis ac religionis symbolum, in se non nisi larvam esse, publicumque usurpatæ sanctimoniæ mendacium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203508Z">
        <seg>but the most importunate of all, because the most subtle: for through the very narrow passages of humility, hellishly driven, it shook Rose with a single tremor of scruples, which opened the way to the most fearful thought, [the Third Order of St. Dominic] namely, that she was a filthy sinner, far too unworthy of that snowy veil of Catherine of Siena; that more was promised by the garment's whiteness outwardly than was hidden within; that she lacked everything that would correspond to its external splendor: Therefore, the habit, which for the Seraphic mistress had been a protestative symbol of virtue and religion, was in herself nothing but a mask and a public lie of assumed sanctity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at prælibanda sunt ex multis paucula de singulari prærogativa celeberrimæ hujus imaginis, quæ inde ab introductæ Christianæ fidei primis initiis prima toti Peruano regno per publica beneficia innotuit, &amp; privatim Rosæ nostræ tam eximie chara fuit atque propitia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054221Z">
        <seg>But a few things must be selected from the many concerning the singular prerogative of this most celebrated image, which, from the very beginnings of the Christian faith, first became known throughout the entire kingdom of Peru by its public benefits, and was privately so exceedingly dear and gracious to our Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at puellæ animum altius hæc verba, quam pro dicentis impetu, suæve ætatis modulo penetrarunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034959Z">
        <seg>But the words penetrated the girl's mind more deeply than was fitting for the speaker's impetuosity or her own age.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at quando nulla tibi affulgebat spes evadendi, &amp; quidam æternitatis fumus pervadebat horroris caliginem, ipsissimus inferni typus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050405Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050405Z">
        <seg>But when no hope of escape shone for you, and a certain smoke of eternity permeated the darkness of horror, it was the very type of hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at reprimebat cassos impetus, memor, neminem posse calamitati tam inexplicabili succurrere, sibique deesse verba idonea, quibus eam proximis vel tenuiter adumbraret, sed neque in orbe toto reperiri doctorem adeo disertum, profundum, &amp; eruditum, qui hujus pœnæ gravitatem aut speculatione assequi, aut animo complecti posset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045605Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045605Z">
        <seg>but he restrained his futile impulses, mindful that no one could come to the aid of such an inexplicable calamity, and that he himself lacked the fitting words to even faintly sketch it for those close to him; nor could a doctor be found anywhere in the whole world so eloquent, profound, and learned that he could either grasp the gravity of this punishment through speculation or encompass it with his mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at schola divini amoris hic suam agnoscit, reperitque phrasim.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053123Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053123Z">
        <seg>But in the school of divine love, one recognizes oneself here and finds one's own expression.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at scito, illa tibi accidisse incommoda, quia non ille eras, qui esse solebas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051905Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051905Z">
        <seg>But know that those misfortunes happened to you because you were no longer the person you used to be.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at scito, mater, me altius collocasse fiduciam meam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062334Z">
        <seg>But know, mother, that I have placed my trust higher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at semel ultra solitum cohorruerunt, dum Rosa, nullo jam læsarum aut illæsarum partium discrimine, vehementius ac diutius in se totam desæviebat plagarum strepitu late sonoro.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041532Z">
        <seg>But once, they shuddered beyond the usual, while Rosa, with no distinction now between her wounded and uninjured parts, raged more fiercely and for longer against her entire self with the loud clatter of her blows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sentiebat, se nunc eumdem nec nosse, nec diligere, utpote quem velut peregrinum, absentem, alienum, extraneum, vixque de fama notum, eminus, raptim, tenuiter adnictabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045521Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045521Z">
        <seg>But he felt that he no longer knew or loved the same man, whom he now barely glanced at from a distance, hastily and faintly, as if he were a stranger, absent, alien, a foreigner, and barely known by reputation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at simul currebat in odorem Sponsi spinis coronati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035316Z">
        <seg>but at the same time she ran toward the scent of the Spouse crowned with thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at simul discessit suavissima Excitatrix: nam ut Rosa diduxit graves palpebras, ut respexit, agnovit Deiparam, sed a tergo: jam enim suspensis vestigiis abire cœperat, velut perfuncta amico officio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051409Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051409Z">
        <seg>But at once the most delightful Exciter departed: for as soon as Rosa lifted her heavy eyelids and looked back, she recognized the Mother of God, though only from behind, for she had already begun to go away with suspended footsteps, as if having fulfilled a friendly duty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at simul ei tripudiabat animus in victoria divinæ gratiæ: compatiebatur lapsæ peccatrici, simulque effuse lætabatur, in ea miram Domini bonitatem ac misericordiam publice ab omnibus celebrari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053427Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053427Z">
        <seg>but at the same time his soul rejoiced in the victory of divine grace: he pitied the fallen sinner, and at the same time he overflowed with joy that in her the Lord's wondrous goodness and mercy were publicly celebrated by all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at stupebant magis, postquam observarunt, in ejus vultu ac manibus nec minimum hujus cruentæ molestiæ signum apparere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043813Z">
        <seg>But they were even more astonished when they noticed that not the slightest sign of this bloody ordeal appeared on his face or in his hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sub fragili carne vigebat infractus animus, &amp; corculum in Deo fixum ridebat, despuebat, laxabat furentem cyclopis insaniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052125Z">
        <seg>But beneath fragile flesh, an unbroken spirit thrived, and the little heart fixed in God laughed, scorned, and loosed the raging madness of the cyclops.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sub hoc affectuum duello animam suam liquefieri ex arcano Dilecti alloquio, quasi dicat: “Ego dormio, &amp; cor meum vigilat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054518Z">
        <seg>but under this duel of affections, her soul is melted by the Beloved's mysterious speech, as if to say: "I sleep, &amp; my heart is awake."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at subjecit, hanc brevi adfore ex domo quæstoris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051654Z">
        <seg>but he added that this would soon come from the house of the quaestor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at subrisit Virgo, monuitque, hujuscemodi cruces inter seculares aulæ pompas, &amp; assidua mundi commercia virere non posse: vetuit tamen emarcidum arbusculæ cadaver projici, jussitque ad se remitti propediem resuscitandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054959Z">
        <seg>But the Virgin smiled and warned that such crosses could not flourish amid the secular pomps of the court and the constant commerce of the world. Yet she forbade the withered little tree to be cast away and ordered it to be brought back to her at once to be revived.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at succedanei, quique ex occasione dumtaxat pœnitentiæ Sacramentalis, interdum studiosius intimas ejus animæ latebras pervestigarunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050030Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050030Z">
        <seg>but successors, and each one, only on occasion of the Sacrament of Penance, have at times more diligently searched the innermost recesses of his soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at superabat tum pia commiseratio, quæ negare justa solatia ægris nesciebat, tum humilis obedientia, quam Rosa Mariæ de Usategui, velut secundæ matri, deberi agnoscebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061451Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061451Z">
        <seg>But above all shone a pious compassion, which could not deny the sick just consolations, and a humble obedience, which she acknowledged she owed to Rosa Maria de Usategui, as to a second mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at sustine: nam in semetipsam tota spinosa fuit, quod ex varia corporis sui castigatione, proxima quatuor capitula fuse docebunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040930Z">
        <seg>But bear with me: for she was all thorns in herself, which the next four chapters will teach at length, through the varied discipline of her own body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tum vel maxime vexabant lacertos, quoties aut aliquid de terra levandum, aut brachia sursum extendenda erant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041814Z">
        <seg>But then their muscles ached the most whenever they had to lift something from the ground or stretch their arms upward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tunc ei importune occurrebat animæ suæ immortalitas, quam nulla pœnarum vis interimere, nullus infernus extinguere sufficeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045559Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045559Z">
        <seg>But then the immortality of his soul importunately occurred to him, which no power of punishments could kill, nor any hell could extinguish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at tunc nondum ei licebat sine matre adire ecclesiam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055653Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055653Z">
        <seg>But at that time, he was not yet allowed to go to church without his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at una certus, &lt;s2&gt;[&amp; juvenem, qui libidinoso animo ad se venerat,]&lt;/s2&gt; nuptias sperari non posse, quærebat occasionem, qua saltem oculos liberius pasceret Rosæ dulcissima venustate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060611Z">
        <seg>But certain of one thing, [and of the young man who had come to him with a lustful mind,] that a marriage could not be hoped for, he sought an opportunity by which he might at least more freely feast his eyes on Rosa's most sweet beauty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>at videbantur fugisse terreni flores, velut indigni, qui Rosam coronarent jam cælestem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042415Z">
        <seg>But the earthly flowers seemed to have fled, as if unworthy to crown the now heavenly Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>atque utinam sic operer, uti debeo!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062012Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062012Z">
        <seg>And may I work like this, as I ought!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen longe majus fuit, a via, quam elegerat, numquam postea ullius noxæ gravioris admissione per totam vitam exerrasse, quod ut iidem sui confessarii concorditer asseruerunt, ita patulum fiet ex iis, quæ sequuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035043Z">
        <seg>However, it was by far a greater thing that, from the path he had chosen, he never afterward deviated in the admission of any more serious fault throughout his entire life, which, as his own confessors unanimously affirmed, will become clear from what follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen quotquot illic adstabant reliqui, certissime illius intuitu, præsentia, solatio fruituros.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062709Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062709Z">
        <seg>However, the others who were there would certainly enjoy his gaze, presence, and comfort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen subjecit, inter hos favores arctissimæ unionis, se sibi videri immobiliter radicatam in Deo, securam in ejus amicitia, confirmatam in gratia, sentire donum quoddam inexplicabile, fundamentale, solidum, quo reputabat se impeccabilem, ut ille qui fidenter aiebat: “Quis nos separabit a charitate Christi?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050526Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050526Z">
        <seg>However, she added that amid these favors of the most perfect union she seemed to herself to be immovably rooted in God, secure in His friendship, confirmed in grace, and to feel a certain inexplicable, fundamental, and solid gift by which she considered herself impeccable, like the one who confidently said, "Who will separate us from the love of Christ?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen te superstite, te vivo &amp; oculato teste, monasterium, quod dixi, exstruetur, habitabitur, florebit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062644Z">
        <seg>However, with you alive and as an eyewitness, the monastery that I mentioned will be built, inhabited, and flourish.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>attamen ultra interrogata, ne quid responderet, cautelam, &amp; parsimoniam verbis suis appendit in trutina.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040847Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040847Z">
        <seg>However, when questioned further, he was cautious and sparing in his words, weighing his replies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Examen apotheoseos istius diu durasse, colligi potest ex nota superiori ad caput primum littera&lt;/e0&gt; i, &lt;e1&gt;in qua diximus, quo anno venerabilisantistes ille catalogo Beatorum &amp; Sanctorum inscriptus fuerit.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040422Z">
        <seg>It can be gathered from the above note to chapter one, letter i, that the examination of this apotheosis lasted a long time, in which we said in what year that venerable antipope was entered in the catalog of the Blessed and Saints.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;πράσινος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;idem est, quod Latine&lt;/e2&gt; viridis &lt;e3&gt;a colore porri, quod Græce&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;πράσον&lt;/s4&gt; &lt;e5&gt;appellatur.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060332Z">
        <seg>Greek πράσινος is the same as the Latin viridis, from the color of leek, which is called πράσον.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἐπιγλωττὶς&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;(quasi Latine dicas&lt;/e2&gt; superlingua) &lt;e3&gt;est membrana cartilaginosa rotunditatis oblongæ, in interiori gutturis parte collocata, &amp; exoritur a radice linguæ, quæ Græce&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;γλῶττα&lt;/s4&gt; &lt;e5&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043119Z">
        <seg>b Greek&lt;/e0&gt; ἐπιγλωττὶς&lt;/s1&gt; (as if you were to say in Latin&lt;/e2&gt; super-tongue) It is a cartilaginous membrane of an oblong shape, located in the inner part of the throat, and it arises from the root of the tongue, which in Greek is called glōtta.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hic in metropolitanam Limensis urbis cathedram S. Thuribio successit, &amp; sacras&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;reliquias solenniter ad honorificentiorem locum transferri permisit, ut infra videbimus&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054159Z">
        <seg>Here he succeeded St. Turibius as Metropolitan of the See of Lima, and permitted the sacred relics of St. Rose to be solemnly transferred to a more honorable place, as we shall see below.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hujus viri elogium apud Joannem Nadasinostrum in Anno dierum memorabilium Societatis Jesu ad diem 29 Martii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042446Z">
        <seg>An eulogy of this man is found in John Nadasynski's *Annus Dierum Memorabilium Societatis Iesu*, on March 29, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Hujus viri elogium apud Joannem Nadasinostrum in Anno dierum memorabilium Societatis Jesu ad diem 29 Martii pag. 174 legi potest.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T001036Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T001036Z">
        <seg>An eulogy of this man can be read in John Nadasynski's *Annus Dierum Memorabilium Societatis Iesu* on March 29, page 174.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Mare pacificum novam Hispaniam &amp; Peruam alluit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035102Z">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;The Pacific Ocean washes New Spain and Peru.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Nomen Græcum&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κατάσκοποσ&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;Latine significat&lt;/e2&gt; speculatorem, &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; exploratorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040940Z">
        <seg>The Greek name κατάσκοποσ&lt;/e0&gt; in Latin means speculator, or explorer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non invenio in Peruvia locum&lt;/e0&gt; Canta &lt;e1&gt;appellatum, nisi forte sit&lt;/e1&gt; Guanta &lt;e2&gt;pagus Indorum, de quo Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T045930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035849Z">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;I do not find in Peru a place called&lt;/e0&gt; Canta, &lt;e1&gt;unless perhaps it is the Indian village&lt;/e1&gt; Guanta, &lt;e2&gt;about which John Latius writes in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non satis assequor, cur Hansenius rosascognominet&lt;/e0&gt; Hyblæas, &lt;e1&gt;nisi forsan hoc epitheto meliores aut magis odoriferas designare voluerit: nam scarabæi vel cantharides odore cujuslibet rosæ exstinguuntur, ut apud Ulyssem Aldrovandum in Opere de Insectis lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044725Z">
        <seg>I do not fully understand why Hansen calls the Hyblæan roses by that name, unless perhaps he meant to designate with this epithet those that are more beautiful or more fragrant: for scarabs or cantharides are driven away by the scent of any rose, as Ulysses Aldrovandus notes in his Work on Insects, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Non tantum in Hispania est celebris istius nominis imago Deiparæ, sed etiam in urbe Limana, sicuti apud Franciscum de Echave superius citatum in Opere Hispanico de S. Turibio pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044002Z">
        <seg>Not only in Spain is there a famous image of the Virgin of that name, but also in the city of Lima, as stated by Francisco de Echave in the aforementioned Spanish work on St. Turibius, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Per&lt;/e0&gt; vocabula emphatica &lt;e1&gt;intelliguntur verba valde significantia vel efficaciter expressiva, quemadmodum colligimus ex figura rhetorica, quæ Græce dicitur&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἔμφασις&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;&amp; est expressio cum energia, quando verbis subest tacita significantia, quæ pronuntiatione vel gestibus indicatur&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053626Z">
        <seg>Through emphatic words, we understand words that are very significant or effectively expressive, as we gather from the rhetorical figure, which in Greek is called ἔμφασις. &amp; is expression with energy, when there is an underlying tacit significance to the words, which is indicated by pronunciation or gestures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Sanctus Franciscus Solanus concionem de spirituali animæ ruina instituerat, quam populus Limanus de materiali &amp; subita urbis suæ ruina perperam interpretabatur, ut in memoratis Actis ejus cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042000Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042000Z">
        <seg>Saint Francis Solanus had delivered a sermon on the spiritual ruin of the soul, which the people of Lima mistakenly interpreted as referring to the material and sudden ruin of their city, as related in the aforementioned Acts of his life, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Suspicor, eumdem locum indicari a Joanne Latio, dum lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054707Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054707Z">
        <seg>I suspect that the same passage is cited by John Latius, while in the book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;e0&gt;Verbum Græcum&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;γαργαρίζω&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;proprie significat liquorem ore haustum sæpe ad gurgulionem seu guttur revocare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045403Z">
        <seg>The Greek word gargarizo properly means to draw a liquid into the mouth and often to draw it back into the throat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b &lt;s0&gt;Κρᾶσις&lt;/s0&gt; &lt;e1&gt;apud medicos Græcos idem est quod&lt;/e1&gt; temperamentum, &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; temperies, &lt;e3&gt;id est, proportio quædam primarum qualitatum existentium in elementis, ex quibus corpus mixtum &amp; conflatum est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045915Z">
        <seg>Mixture among the Greek physicians is the same as temperament, or temperance, that is, a certain proportion of the primary qualities existing in the elements, from which the body is mixed and composed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Amphibolon, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀμφίβολον&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;hic significat sermonem ambiguum, ab adverbio&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἀμφὶ&lt;/s3&gt; utrimque, &lt;e4&gt;&amp; verbo&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;βάλλω&lt;/s5&gt; jacio &lt;e6&gt;vel&lt;/e6&gt; ferio, &lt;e7&gt;quasi&lt;/e7&gt; utrimque feriens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062353Z">
        <seg>b Amphibolon, Greek&lt;/e0&gt; amphibolon&lt;/s1&gt;, here it means an ambiguous speech, from the adverb ἀμφὶ on both sides, and the verb βάλλω to throw or strike, as if striking on both sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Carcinoma &lt;e0&gt;est species cancri, de cujus periculo &amp; remediis obvii medici consuli possunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061624Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061624Z">
        <seg>Carcinoma is a type of cancer, about whose risks and treatments one can consult a doctor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Chile &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; Cile &lt;e1&gt;est ampla Americæ regio, per quadringentas leucas Gallicas a Septemtrione in Meridiem extensa, quæ terminatur a Septemtrione Peruvia, ab Occidente mari Pacifico, a Meridie Magellanica regione, &amp; ab Oriente Tucumania.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060911Z">
        <seg>Chile or Cile is a vast region of America, extending four hundred French leagues from north to south, which is bordered on the north by Peru, on the west by the Pacific Ocean, on the south by the Magellanic region, and on the east by Tucumania.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Inquisitio &lt;e0&gt;est tribunal in America, Lusitania, Hispania &amp; aliis quibusdam regionibus usitatum, verosimiliter ab&lt;/e0&gt; inquirendo &lt;e1&gt;dictum, quia administratores hujus sacri officii in hæreticos, hypocritas, aliosque sacrilegos inquirunt&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052922Z">
        <seg>The Inquisition is a tribunal used in America, Portugal, Spain, and certain other regions, probably so called from inquiring, because the administrators of this sacred office inquire into heretics, hypocrites, and other sacrileges.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Ischias est arthritis coxendicis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041411Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041411Z">
        <seg>b Sciatica is arthritis of the hip.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Libanotis, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;λιβανοτὶς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est frutex spirans odorem thuris, a&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;λιβανωτὸς&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quo apud Græcos&lt;/e4&gt; thus &lt;e5&gt;significatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055507Z">
        <seg>Libanotis, Greek&lt;/e0&gt; libanotis&lt;/s1&gt;, is a shrub that gives off the scent of incense, from&lt;/e2&gt; libanotis&lt;/s3&gt;, by which incense is signified among the Greeks.&lt;/e5&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Pararius &lt;e0&gt;est ille, qui stipulationibus aut contractibus faciendis intervenit, &amp; quem poëta Martialis&lt;/e0&gt; proxenetam &lt;e1&gt;vocat a nomine Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;προξενήτης&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est, licitator, mediator, conciliator, sive interpres duorum inter se stipulantium, aut spondentium, aliumve contractum ineuntium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052302Z">
        <seg>b A proxenetas is one who intervenes in making stipulations or contracts, and whom the poet Martial calls by the Greek name προξενήτης, that is, a negotiator, mediator, conciliator, or interpreter for two who are stipulating with each other, or pledging, or entering into another contract.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>b Truxillum &lt;e0&gt;est urbs Americæ in parte septemtrionali Peruviæ, episcopalis sub archiepiscopo Limensi, quæ ab aliis ejusdem nominis oppidis distinguitur, ut in Annotatis ad Vitam S. Francisci Solani tomo 5 Julii pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050743Z">
        <seg>Trujillo is a city of America in the northern part of Peru, a bishopric under the Archbishop of Lima, which is distinguished from other towns of the same name, as in the annotations to the Life of St. Francis Solano, volume 5 of July, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>benedictus sit Deus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054633Z">
        <seg>Blessed be God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>bibitura vero, non frigidam sed calentem sorbebat lympham, metuens in aqua subgelida tantillum invenire deliciarum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041355Z">
        <seg>But she drank not a cold, but a warm draught, fearing to find any pleasure in the ice-cold water.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>bina insuper pulvinaria in speciem imposuit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042530Z">
        <seg>He also placed two cushions on the seat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>brachium viri Gundisalvus obstipum reperit ac inflexibile, &lt;s2&gt;[ac immobilis hæreret.]&lt;/s2&gt; dexteram algido sudore madidam, ut in mortuis solet, digiros circa anulum ita contortos, implexos, nodoseque incurvos, ut vi ac mora opus fuerit, qua sensim explicarentur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044700Z">
        <seg>He found the man Gundisalvus's arm stiff and inflexible, [and frozen in place.] His right hand was drenched in cold sweat, as is common in the dead, its fingers twisted, entwined, and knotted around a ring so that it took force and time to straighten them out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>brevi effectum manibus contrectandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054453Z">
        <seg>A brief, hands-on effect.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Ab Hispanis passim&lt;/e0&gt; beata &lt;e1&gt;vocatur femina, quæ sub modesto habitu vivit, &amp; mundo renuntiasse censetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040425Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040425Z">
        <seg>She is called blessed by the Spaniards everywhere, who lives in modest attire and is considered to have renounced the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Collector Vitæ hoc loco alludit ad sequens Ovidianum carmen:&lt;/e0&gt; Aprilem memorant ab aperto tempore dictum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203246Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035111Z">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;The Collector of Life alludes here to the following Ovidian poem:&lt;/e0&gt; "They call April 'the open season.'"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Cum nusquam hoc nomen in masculino genere invenerim, propter oscitantiam typothetæ dubito, an non potius&lt;/e0&gt; pulpas, &lt;e1&gt;quam&lt;/e1&gt; pulpos &lt;e2&gt;hic sit legendum: nam&lt;/e2&gt; pulpa &lt;e3&gt;est pars carnosa in animali, quam medici&lt;/e3&gt; musculum, &lt;e4&gt;Græci&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;μῦς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;ob similitudinem, quam cum mure excoriato habet, ut Gorræus in opere jam sæpius citato pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054202Z">
        <seg>c Since I have found this name nowhere in the masculine gender, I doubt, because of the typographer's oversight, whether it is not rather pulpas, than pulpos, that should be read here: for pulp &lt;e3&gt;is the fleshy part in an animal, which the doctors&lt;/e3&gt; call muscle, &lt;e4&gt;the Greeks&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;μῦς&lt;/s5&gt; &lt;e6&gt;on account of the resemblance it bears to a skinned mouse, as Gorræus in the work already cited several times, p.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Daniel Sennertus tom.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045924Z">
        <seg>Daniel Sennert, vol.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Eruditus lector observet, caput illud, quod hic citatur&lt;/e0&gt; quintum, &lt;e1&gt;apud nos esse&lt;/e1&gt; quartum, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; sic deinceps editionem nostram uno Capite Hansenianam præcedere, quia duo priora Vitæ Capita nimis brevia in unum conflavimus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035904Z">
        <seg>The learned reader should note that the chapter cited here as the fifth is the fourth in our edition, and so on, our edition preceding the Hansenian by one chapter, because we have combined the two preceding chapters of the Vita, which were too brief, into one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Ex&lt;/e0&gt; crasi, &lt;e1&gt;id est, temperie vel indole materna, sicut hanc vocem Græcam superius explicui&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062356Z">
        <seg>c Ex&lt;/e0&gt; crasi, that is, by maternal temperament or disposition, as I explained this Greek word above&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Hic eximius favor apud nos tomo 3 Aprilis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051514Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051514Z">
        <seg>Here is an exceptional favor among us, on April 3, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Iste Gregorius Lopez cognominatur&lt;/e0&gt; Mexicanus &lt;e1&gt;habitatione, ni fallor, cum Nicolaus Antonius in recentiore Bibliotheca Hispanica tomo 1, pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042006Z">
        <seg>This Gregory Lopez is nicknamed the Mexican, a resident, if I am not mistaken, as Nicholas Antonio states in the more recent Library of Hispanic Literature, volume 1, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Iste Gregorius Lopez cognominatur&lt;/e0&gt; Mexicanus &lt;e1&gt;habitatione, ni fallor, cum Nicolaus Antonius in recentiore Bibliotheca Hispanica tomo 1, pag. 417 de illo scribat sequentia:&lt;/e1&gt; Gregorius Lopez, cujus patria in obscuro est (alii Matritensem existimant, natumque anno MDXXXII, alii Lusitanum, ut Georgius Cardosus in Hagiologio suo XIII Martii pag. 164, qui ortum ait in oppido Linhares Beiræ provinciæ) cum ex Hispania in Septemtrionalem Americæ plagam, Mexici nempe regnum transfretasset, insigni apud eas gentes vixit morum innocentiæ ac sanctitatis fama &lt;e2&gt;&amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000930Z">
        <seg>This Gregory Lopez is nicknamed Mexican, a resident, if I am not mistaken, with Nicholas Antonio in the more recent Library of Spain, volume 1, page. 417 writes the following about him:&lt;/e1&gt; Gregory Lopez, whose nationality is obscure (some consider him a native of Madrid, born in 1532, others a Portuguese, as George Cardoso does in his *Hagiologio* on March 13, p. 164, who says that he was born in the town of Linhares in the province of Beira) when he crossed from Spain to the northern part of the Americas, namely the kingdom of Mexico, he lived among those peoples with a remarkable reputation for innocence and sanctity of character &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Istud medicamen dicitur&lt;/e0&gt; non anareticum, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt;, non selectum, &lt;e2&gt;quod epitheton ex verbo Græco&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἀναιρέω&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, eligo &lt;e5&gt;vel&lt;/e5&gt; seligo &lt;e6&gt;componitur, ni fallor.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041423Z">
        <seg>c This medicine is called&lt;/e0&gt; not anaretic, that is&lt;/e1&gt;, not selective, which epithet is from the Greek verb&lt;/e2&gt; ἀναιρέω&lt;/s3&gt;, that is&lt;/e4&gt;, to choose or&lt;/e5&gt; to select is formed, if I am not mistaken.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Laudatus Echardus tomo 2 Bibliothecæ Prædicatorum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045416Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045416Z">
        <seg>Praised be Echard, in volume 2 of the Library of Preachers, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Quanto gaudio spirituali vir iste delibutus fuerit, dum acutam&lt;/e0&gt; S. Rosæ &lt;e1&gt;coronam contrectaret, apud nos superius num.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044740Z">
        <seg>How spiritual joy this man must have been filled with while handling the sharp crown of St. Rose, as we noted above.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Sunt celebres Ægypti solitudines, quas olim sancti anachoretæ incoluerunt.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044008Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044008Z">
        <seg>There are the famous deserts of Egypt, which were once inhabited by holy anchorites.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Vocem&lt;/e0&gt; pluderones &lt;e1&gt;apud scriptores Latinos &amp; Græcos frustra quæsivi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061634Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061634Z">
        <seg>I searched in vain for the word pluderones among Latin and Greek writers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c &lt;e0&gt;Vox&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄγαλμα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;pure Græca est, quam necdum apud Latinos usitatam legi, &amp; quæ&lt;/e2&gt; simulacrum &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; ornamentum &lt;e4&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054720Z">
        <seg>Vox ἄγαλμα&lt;/s1&gt; is purely Greek, which I have not yet found in use among the Latins, and which&lt;/e2&gt; signifies a statue or&lt;/e3&gt; an ornament .</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Angina &lt;e0&gt;sic dicta, quod angat guttur viamque spiritui præcludat, est genus morbi acutissimi, quo fauces anguntur, ita ut ægre respiretur&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052925Z">
        <seg>Angina, so called because it constricts the throat and blocks the passage of breath, is a very acute form of disease in which the throat is constricted, so that one can barely breathe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Anteroticis, &lt;e0&gt;id est, quasi&lt;/e0&gt; redamatoriis, &lt;e1&gt;a verbo composito&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἀντεράω&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;vel&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἀντερῶ&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod significat&lt;/e5&gt; redamo &lt;e6&gt;vel&lt;/e6&gt; vicissim amo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053629Z">
        <seg>Anterotics, that is, quasi-redamatories, from the compound verb ἀντεράω&lt;/s2&gt; or&lt;/e3&gt; ἀντερῶ&lt;/s4&gt;, which means to love back or to love in return.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Anthropophagæ &lt;e0&gt;vocantur illæ nationes, quæ carnibus humanis vescuntur, ex origine Græca&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄνθρωπος&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;id est&lt;/e2&gt;, homo, &lt;e3&gt;ac verbo&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;φάγω&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;quod est&lt;/e5&gt; comedo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060921Z">
        <seg>c Cannibals are called those nations that feed on human flesh, from the Greek origin ἄνθρωπος, that is, man, and the verb φάγω, which is to eat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Arequipa &lt;e0&gt;est urbs meridionalis Americæ in regno Peruviæ episcopalis sub metropoli Limana, quæ distat a mari 12 leucis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050749Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050749Z">
        <seg>Arequipa is a southern city of America in the kingdom of Peru, a bishopric under the metropolitan see of Lima, which is 12 leagues from the sea.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Batavi &lt;e0&gt;sunt veteris Germaniæ populi, qui nunc vulgo vocantur&lt;/e0&gt; Hollandi, &lt;e1&gt;&amp; quos biographus hoc loco designat&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060335Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060335Z">
        <seg>The Batavi are a people of ancient Germany, who are now commonly called the Dutch, and whom the biographer designates here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Bronchus &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;βρόγχος&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;est corpus quoddam cartilagineum Galeno secundum asperam arteriam, quod sic nominatur, quia maxima ex parte constat cartilaginibus, quas Græci&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;βρόγχια&lt;/s3&gt; &lt;e4&gt;appellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043125Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043125Z">
        <seg>Bronchus (Greek βρόγχος) is, according to Galen, a certain cartilaginous body on the trachea, so named because it consists for the most part of cartilages, which the Greeks call bronchia.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Pataco &lt;e0&gt;est species monetæ, Hispanis vulgo dicta&lt;/e0&gt; patagon &lt;e1&gt;vel&lt;/e1&gt; real de a ocho, &lt;e2&gt;quia æquivalet octo solidis argenteis, quos ipsi&lt;/e2&gt; reales de plata &lt;e3&gt;appellant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055513Z">
        <seg>Pataco is a type of coin, commonly called by the Spaniards patagon or real de a ocho, because it is equivalent to eight silver solids, which they themselves call reales de plata.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>c Symmysta &lt;e0&gt;proprie in lingua Latina est&lt;/e0&gt; consacerdos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052308Z">
        <seg>Symmysta properly in Latin is&lt;/e0&gt; a fellow priest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>caligabat trepida, recogitans judiciorum Dei abyssum multam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061807Z">
        <seg>She trembled in fear, reflecting on the great abyss of God's judgments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044111Z">
        <seg>chapter</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cathedraticus theologiæ, quam primus invexit regno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050144Z">
        <seg>a professor of theology, whom he was the first to introduce to the kingdom;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>causabatur se citra confessarii permissionem nil præsumpturam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053957Z">
        <seg>She claimed that she would do nothing without the confessor's permission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>causam, cur levi ex tactu sanguis eruperit, sciscitatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042245Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042245Z">
        <seg>He asks the reason why blood has burst forth from a slight touch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cedo igitur pio &amp; ignito hortatui tuo, sequar invitantem ad meliora Deum, tu illum mihi propitium fac reddas, ut, quod hodie in me cœpit, perficiat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060710Z">
        <seg>I yield, therefore, to your pious and fiery exhortation; I will follow the God who invites me to better things. May you make Him propitious to me, so that He may complete what has today begun in me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>certe nec levissima dissimulabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053306Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053306Z">
        <seg>She certainly did not conceal even the slightest thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>certus sum, quia neque mors, &amp;c.” Verumtamen hic protestabatur Virgo, numquam se ulli mortalium hoc dicere ausam, &amp; ne nunc quidem id dicturam fuisse, nisi coëgisset rigor præsentis examinis, &amp; si forte errasset in phrasi locutionis, quam necessitas omnia efferendi expresserat, censuram correctionis demisse flagitabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050529Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050529Z">
        <seg>"I am certain, for neither death, etc." Nevertheless, here the Virgin protested that she had never dared to say this to any mortal, nor would she have said it even now, had not the rigor of the present examination compelled her; and if she had perhaps erred in the phrasing of her words, which necessity had forced her to utter, she humbly begged for the censure of correction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cervicali infarsit lanam, at non solam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042747Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042747Z">
        <seg>He stuffed the wool into the neck, but not by itself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cessantique foris persecutioni, agminatim corporis ægritudines succedebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052603Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052603Z">
        <seg>And as they ceased from persecution abroad, one sickness after another fell upon the body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cetera complures, qui visuri essent, digito monstravit personas, quæ illic Deo famulaturæ essent, (ac inter has matrem propriam) designavit, numerum diffinivit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062446Z">
        <seg>He then pointed out with his finger several others who were to be there to serve God (among them his own mother), and he set the number.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>charitas delicata non est, nec proximum fastidit, memor, nos omnes ex eodem esse luto ac cœno;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061320Z">
        <seg>Charity is not delicate, nor does it despise its neighbor, remembering that we are all made of the same clay and mire.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>chirurgi, tumidis ac liventibus indiciis territi, judicarunt, aut Virginem deinceps mancam ea parte remansuram, aut saltem curationis laborem admodum prolixum incertumque fore, periculum longe minus fuisse intercessurum, si ossa in partes diffracta crepuissent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053945Z">
        <seg>The surgeons, alarmed by the swollen and bruised indications, judged that either the Virgin would remain lame in that part, or at least the labor of the cure would be exceedingly long and uncertain, and that the danger would have been far less had the bones cracked, breaking into pieces.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>circumspiciebat singularum artificum marmora jam elaborata, absoluta, seposita, quorum nulli ad perfectionem exactissimam quidquam deerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044222Z">
        <seg>He surveyed the already finished, completed, and set-aside marbles of the individual artisans, in none of which anything was lacking to the most exacting perfection.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>coloris, odoris, tactus, aliarumque circumstantiarum experimenta neutiquam fallere, naturæ artisque industrias tali prodigio longe abesse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053818Z">
        <seg>that experiments of color, smell, touch, and other circumstances can in no way be deceived, and that the industries of nature and art are far from such a prodigy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>conata tamen dissimulare quod erat, caute in secretius cubiculum se subduxit, coronam festina seposuit, cruorem quasi ludibunda abstersit, impositisque panniculis, qui sauciæ plagæ stillicidium imbiberent, caput solitis velamentis confestim operuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042235Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042235Z">
        <seg>However, attempting to conceal what she was, she cautiously withdrew to a more secluded chamber, hastily set aside her crown, wiped away the blood as if in jest, and, placing cloths to soak up the trickle from her wound, she immediately covered her head with her usual veils.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>concordi omnium peritorum sententiæ obstinate refragandum non esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062808Z">
        <seg>it should not be stubbornly resisted, according to the unanimous opinion of all experts;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>concurrebant ardens contemplandi studium &amp; activa gubernandi dexteritas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050132Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050132Z">
        <seg>A burning zeal for contemplation and an active skill in governing came together.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>conferebat anteriora postremis, &amp; mire invicem consonare deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053929Z">
        <seg>He compared the former with the latter and found them to harmonize wonderfully with one another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>confestim illi concentu lenissimo erumpebant in bombos, sparsique in gyros varie miscebant arguta murmura, phalange tam ordinata, ac in se flexibus aptissimis retorta, ut credidisses aut chorum aut choreas esse, quibus ratio dux præsideret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043832Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043832Z">
        <seg>Immediately they burst forth in the softest harmony into booms, and scattered in circles they wove various witty murmurs, the phalanx so orderly and twisted into itself with the most graceful curves that you would have thought it was either a chorus or a dance led by reason.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>confestim omnis dolor evanuit, infirmum somnus, qui omnino diffugerat, placide corripit, mox altum obdormiscens (Virgine interim cum comitibus domum reversa) integre sanus evigilavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061506Z">
        <seg>Immediately all pain vanished, and a gentle sleep, which had completely fled, peacefully seized the invalid; soon he fell into a deep slumber (while the Virgin, having returned home with her companions, had awakened). He awoke fully recovered.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>confuso venatu turbabant omnia, nec sperari quietiora poterant, cum fœdis ac lucifugis bestiis nox ipsa locique faveret conditio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052023Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052023Z">
        <seg>The confused hunt disrupted everything, and nothing quieter could be hoped for, since the foul, light-fearing beasts were favored by night itself and the nature of the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cor in varias deliberationes repente dilatatum erigebatur totum ad cælestia, quasi pulsu citatiori vociferans: “Domine quid me vis facere”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062945Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062945Z">
        <seg>His heart, suddenly expanded in various deliberations, was lifted entirely to heavenly things, as if, with a quicker pulse, crying out: "Lord, what do you want me to do?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>costasque obsiderent ligna, ne laxaretur phalanx.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042617Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042617Z">
        <seg>and they would wedge the sides with logs, so that the phalanx would not be loosened.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>crudele ac Deo palam ingratum fore, quidquid ulterius tentaretur ad oppugnandam Rosæ abstinentiam, a qua tam perspicue illius vita pendebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041129Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041129Z">
        <seg>It would be cruel and manifestly ungrateful to God to attempt anything further to assail the fast of Rose, from which his life so clearly depended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui Rosa ornamentum, ex tractabili ficulneæ medulla, complusculos cælestes genios ordine aptissimo circumposuit, &amp; crucis basi accubuam sanctæ Mariæ Magdalenæ icunculam perite adjecit, sicque renatam, ornatam, pampinatam remisit confessarius proreginæ, miranti, libanotim suam sub Rosæ manibus tam celeriter, tam amœne, tam plene revixisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055005Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055005Z">
        <seg>to whom Rosa, an ornament crafted from the pliable pith of a fig tree, had most fittingly arranged a number of heavenly genii in a circle, and had skillfully added a small image of Saint Mary Magdalene reclining at the base of the cross, thus the queen's confessor returned her, reborn, adorned, and flourishing, to the astonished regent, her own Lebanon had so quickly, so charmingly, so fully come back to life under Rose's hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui Rosa: “Vix (inquit) in oratorio ad preces coram sacra imagine me composueram, quando attractu prævalido sensi, redire ad sua loca distortos nervos, detumescere pulpos&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;*&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;c&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;, distringi musculos, nec tamen prodire statim volui, donec cælesti medico grates ex debito persolvissem: in his nonnihil me distinui: tolle jam gossipium, tolle fascias, quia in tuto sunt omnia”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054022Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054022Z">
        <seg>to whom Rosa said: "I had scarcely (she said) composed myself in the oratory for prayer before the sacred image, when by a powerful attraction I felt the twisted nerves return to their places, the flesh swell&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;**2* I was disfigured, my muscles strained, and yet I did not immediately want to come out, until I had duly offered thanks to the heavenly physician: in this I somewhat delayed myself. Now remove the gossamer, take off the bandages, for everything is safe."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui bono tam ineptæ præstigiæ, si libuit matris oculos deludere, at ludere cum propriis oculis quale nefas, quantum periculum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043426Z">
        <seg>What good is such a foolish trick, if it pleased you to deceive your mother's eyes? But to play with your own eyes—what a crime, what a danger?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cui mox frater: “Hem (inquit) soror mea, si te deturpati capillitii tui injuria tam serio affligit, scito, cultos puellarum cincinnos inferni rudentes esse, quibus illaqueantur juvenum incautæ animæ, ut in gehennam æternam detrahantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034953Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034953Z">
        <seg>To whom his brother soon said, "Behold, my sister, if the offense of your disfigured hair afflicts you so seriously, know that the styled ringlets of maidens are the snares of hell, by which the unwary souls of young men are entangled and dragged down into eternal gehenna."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum Mariana ancilla e vestigio Filiam secuta, de vulnere interrogat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042242Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042242Z">
        <seg>When Mariana, the maid, followed the Daughter from the track, she asked about the wound.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum ecce repentina totius corporis lassitudo, mox tremor &amp; deliquium prostravit miseram, subitumque pectori ac pulmonibus asthma incubuit, pressit, ac respirandi facultatem interclusit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041048Z">
        <seg>when suddenly a total bodily weakness, soon followed by a tremor and fainting, struck the poor woman down, and a sudden asthma descended upon her chest and lungs, pressing down and cutting off her ability to breathe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum ex iis papilionibus unus, solo nigro alboque colore pulcherrime striatus, leni, sed minime fortuito, allapsu Virginem festive circumvolitavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040156Z">
        <seg>when one of those butterflies, most beautifully striped in black and white, fluttered playfully around the Virgin with a gentle, yet by no means accidental, flight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum inferne sat prohiberent subjecti asseres, nequa excideret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042613Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042613Z">
        <seg>since the subjects below were firmly holding the beams, lest anything fall out;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum saxo rediens Mariana audit sonoro crepitu seram sponte dissilientem, laxatur sensim catena, deducitur, abstrahitur tota;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041739Z">
        <seg>Returning with the rock, Mariana hears the gate with a loud creak spring open of its own accord; the chain slowly slackens, she is drawn out, carried off whole.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cum sciret, vicissim in ejus virgineo corculo se solum tranquillissime habitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051251Z">
        <seg>Knowing that, in turn, he alone dwelt most peacefully in her maidenly heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque Laurentio novis ardebat ignibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040227Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040227Z">
        <seg>and burned with new fires for Laurentio;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque accedentes angeli pararent libram attollere, rursum intercessit Christus, velut omnipotenti suo brachio dignam functionem accuratius peracturus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052804Z">
        <seg>and as the angels drew near to lift the balance, Christ interceded again, as though to more accurately perform the worthy function with his own omnipotent arm.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque de more sub seram noctem pallidula inde prodiisset, quæstori &amp; conjugi subtrepide fassa est, minimum abfuisse, quin exiguum id, quod ex avicula gustaverat, morte lueret, præ dira pectoris obstructione, quæ miseram prope suffocarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041119Z">
        <seg>And when, as was her custom, she came out a little pale late in the evening, she told her quæstor and husband in a trembling voice that she had come very near dying; for from the small amount she had tasted of the bird, she had suffered a dreadful obstruction of the chest that had nearly suffocated her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque nihil aut olidum aut oleaceum posset deprehendere, lustratis diligenter omnibus, edixit, supernaturale sibi omnino videri, quod in imagine gerebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053815Z">
        <seg>And since he could detect nothing either foul or oily, having carefully examined everything, he declared that what was taking place in the image seemed entirely supernatural to him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cumque tam serio ista &amp; sentiret &amp; diceret, ferre non poterat, quod ejus innocentiæ gnari talia credere recusarent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040546Z">
        <seg>And though he felt and said these things so earnestly, he could not bear that those who knew his innocence would refuse to believe such things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cupiebat voluntas amare, sed congelata obtorpuerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045512Z">
        <seg>The will to love desired, but it had grown numb and frozen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>cæcutiebat mens confusa, oberrabat, palpabat, impingebat orbata lumine;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045627Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045627Z">
        <seg>The mind, blinded, was confused, wandering, groping, and bumping about, deprived of light.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Designatur Dominica in Albis, cujus Missa inchoatur his verbis:&lt;/e0&gt; Quasi modo geniti infantes &lt;e1&gt;&amp;c.&lt;/e1&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054726Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054726Z">
        <seg>Designated as Low Sunday, whose Mass begins with these words: As newborn infants &amp;c.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;In Commentario prævio ad Vitam S. Dominici tomo 1 Augusti pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045422Z">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;In the Preliminary Commentary to the Life of St. Dominic, Volume 1, August, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 20 Aprilis Acta hujus sanctæ Agnetis illustrarunt, ubi tomo secundo istius mensis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035114Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors illustrated the Acts of this holy Agnes on April 20, in the second volume of that month, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 20 Aprilis Acta hujus sanctæ Agnetis illustrarunt, ubi tomo secundo istius mensis pag. 796 miraculum de floribus in loco orationis ejus cælitus inventis refertur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260405T000834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260405T000834Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors, on April 20, illustrated the Acts of this holy Agnes, where in the second volume of that month, page 796, a miracle concerning flowers found from heaven at the site of her prayer is recounted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Omnia pretiosa in supellectile vel vestitu dicta sunt&lt;/e0&gt; Attalica, &lt;e1&gt;ab Attalo Pergami rege, qui pecunia &amp; splendida supellectile abundabat, ut Plinius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055520Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055520Z">
        <seg>All things precious in furniture or clothing are called Attalic, from Attalus, king of Pergamon, who abounded in money and splendid furniture, as Pliny says.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d &lt;e0&gt;Significationem hujus appellationis in variis lexicis frustra quæsivi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052314Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052314Z">
        <seg>I searched in vain for the meaning of this appellation in various dictionaries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Aphorismus, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀφορισμὸς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est sententia, quæ omnes rei proprietates brevissimis verbis complectitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060924Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060924Z">
        <seg>An aphorism, from the Greek ἀφορισμὸς, is a statement that embraces all the properties of a thing in the briefest terms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Archithalassus, &lt;e0&gt;id est&lt;/e0&gt;, princeps maris, &lt;e1&gt;quod nomen ex&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;ἀρκὴ&lt;/s2&gt; principatus, &lt;e3&gt;&amp;&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;θάλασσα&lt;/s4&gt; mare &lt;e5&gt;compositum est, &amp; quo hic præfectus illius classis indicatur&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060338Z">
        <seg>d Archithalassus, that is&lt;/e0&gt;, prince of the sea, a name composed of&lt;/e1&gt; ἀρκὴ&lt;/s2&gt; (principality) and&lt;/e3&gt; θάλασσα&lt;/s4&gt; (sea), and by which the prefect of that fleet is designated&lt;/e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Catapotia &lt;e0&gt;proprie sunt&lt;/e0&gt; pilulæ &lt;e1&gt;a nomine Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;κατάποσις&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt;, deglutitio: &lt;e4&gt;teste enim Gorræo in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045940Z">
        <seg>Catapotia are properly pills from the Greek name kataposis, that is, deglutition: as Gorræus testifies in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Cusco &lt;e0&gt;erat urbs primaria Americæ ante adventum Hispanorum, &amp; sedes regum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050755Z">
        <seg>Cusco was the primary city of the Americas before the arrival of the Spaniards, and the seat of the kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Cynanche, (&lt;e0&gt;scribitur a Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κυνάγχη&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;&amp; interdum&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνάγχη&lt;/s3&gt;) &lt;e4&gt;differt ab angina in quibusdam circumstantiis, quas curiosus hujusmodi rerum lector apud Gorræum in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052928Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052928Z">
        <seg>d Cynanche, (&lt;e0&gt;written by the Greeks&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κυνάγχη&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;&amp; sometimes&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συνάγχη&lt;/s3&gt;) &lt;e4&gt;differs from angina in certain circumstances, which the curious reader of such matters may find in Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Guamanga &lt;e0&gt;est episcopalis civitas Americæ meridionalis inter Limam ad occasum &amp; Cuscum ad ortum sita, &amp; nonaginta leucis utrimque distans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053632Z">
        <seg>Guamanga is a episcopal city of South America, situated between Lima to the west and Cusco to the east, and ninety leagues distant from each.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Mulati &lt;e0&gt;ab Hispanis vocantur ii, qui ex parentibus Africanis &amp; Indis mixtim nati sunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061640Z">
        <seg>Mulattoes are those who are born of mixed African and Indian parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Ozimum &lt;e0&gt;est planta jam passim per Europamnota, de qua Calepinus in Dictionario octolingui sic scribit:&lt;/e0&gt; Ozimum item regia herba sive basilica, aut basilicum a Liguribus vocata, fragrantissimi &amp; vehementissimi odoris, unde nomen accepit, nempe a verbo &lt;s1&gt;ὄζω&lt;/s1&gt;, quod est redoleo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051530Z">
        <seg>Ozimum is a plant now common throughout Europe, about which Calepinus in his Octilingual Dictionary writes as follows: "Ozimum, also called the royal herb or basilica, or basilicum as the Ligurians call it, has a most fragrant and vehement odor, from which it received its name, namely from the verb ὄζω, which means 'to smell.'"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Paraphernalia &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; parapherna &lt;e1&gt;in jure dicuntur ea, quæ sibi propria habet uxor, a&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;παρὰ&lt;/s2&gt; præter, &lt;e3&gt;&amp;&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;φέρνη&lt;/s4&gt; dos, &lt;e5&gt;quasi diceres&lt;/e5&gt; præter-dotalia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062359Z">
        <seg>Paraphernalia, or parapherna, are in law those things which a wife holds as her own, from *pará* (beside) and *phérne* (dowry), as if one were to say, "things beside the dowry."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Polygonus, &lt;e0&gt;id est&lt;/e0&gt;, multos angulos habens &lt;e1&gt;ab adjectivo Græco&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;πολύγωνος&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;quod componitur ex adverbio&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;πολὺ&lt;/s4&gt;, &lt;e5&gt;id est&lt;/e5&gt;, multum, &lt;e6&gt;&amp; voce&lt;/e6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;γωνία&lt;/s7&gt;, &lt;e8&gt;quæ&lt;/e8&gt; angulum &lt;e9&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043131Z">
        <seg>from Polygonus, that is, having many angles from the Greek adjective polygonous, which is composed of the adverb πολὺ, that is, much, and the word γωνία, which means angle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Pæan &lt;e0&gt;est carmen, quod in signum victoriæ plerumque canitur, ut colligimus ex hoc hemistichio Virgiliano&lt;/e0&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040438Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040438Z">
        <seg>A paean is a song, which is usually sung as a sign of victory, as we gather from this hemistich of Virgil.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>d Æsculapius &lt;e0&gt;apud veteres erat deus sanitatis, aut medicinæ, de quo poëtæ multa fabulantur, quæ non sunt hujus loci&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041433Z">
        <seg>d. Asclepius was, among the ancients, a god of health or medicine, about whom the poets tell many fables, which are not relevant here.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>de Lorenzana, qui adstans eidem jam moribundæ, dum pro extremo viatico sacram Hostiam in ecstasi sensim deglutiebat, hæc pauca subjecit: &lt;s2&gt;[omnes in se effectus hujus cælestis Cibi,]&lt;/s2&gt; “Filia, nunc fruere Sponso tuo, ac sola cum solo deliciare, quia vere dulcissimus est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055916Z">
        <seg>de Lorenzana, who was present with the same one, now dying, while she was slowly swallowing the sacred Host as her last viaticum in ecstasy, added these few words: "Daughter, now enjoy your Spouse, and delight alone with the Alone, for He is truly most sweet;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinceps (ut ego) immunis eris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043931Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043931Z">
        <seg>From then on, you will be immune (like me).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde &amp; temporis spatium, quo dormiret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051316Z">
        <seg>and then a space of time in which to sleep;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deinde provinciæ S. Joannis Baptistæ Vicarius generalis, tandem provincialis &amp; visitator;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050154Z">
        <seg>then Vicar General of the province of St. John the Baptist, and finally provincial and visitor;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>demum objurgare, percellere, cædere innocentem, quod rudis, incompta, &amp; incomposita supino ac nimio mundi muliebris neglectu abiret a seculi genio, quod infra coæqualium sortem vilissima singularitate descenderet, quod spem opulenti splendidique matrimonii, quo ipsam natura formæque præstantia vocabat, ultro hypocrisi abjectissima corrumperet, sordideque præfocaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035415Z">
        <seg>finally to rebuke, to terrify, to slaughter the innocent, because she was crude, uncouth, and unkempt, departing from the spirit of the age with a wanton and excessive neglect of feminine adornment; because she descended to the vilest singularity, below the lot of her peers; because she would of her own accord corrupt the hope of a wealthy and splendid marriage, to which nature and the excellence of her form were calling her, with the most abject hypocrisy, and sordidly stifled it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique excusante Rosa, &amp; nequidquam deprecante, suspicacior vittam solvi, caput nudari imperat: quod ut vidit in orbem undique immaniter dispunctum, facile conjecit, unde hæc signa tam cruento inessent zodiaco.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042248Z">
        <seg>Finally, with Rosa excusing herself and pleading in vain, the more suspicious one ordered her to loosen her veil and bare her head. When he saw it, gaping in a monstrous circle on every side, he easily guessed whence these marks so bloody were in the zodiac.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique finge animo, quidquid obstaculorum potes concipere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062641Z">
        <seg>Finally, imagine whatever obstacles you can conceive of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique in tanti sudoris mercedem ac solatium sat fore, si vel unicum apud infideles infantem per Baptismi januam ad superos transmitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060415Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060415Z">
        <seg>Finally, it would be enough reward and consolation for so much sweat, if he were to send even a single infant among the infidels to the upper world through the gate of Baptism.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique in utriusque figuræ labiis, genis, oculis quoddam velut arcanæ affabilitatis horologium inesse, signorumque congruam diversitatem nulla linguarum peritia explicabilem, humana tamen locutione longe certiorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054403Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054403Z">
        <seg>Finally, on the lips, cheeks, and eyes of both figures there seemed to be a kind of clock of mysterious affability, and a corresponding diversity of signs, inexplicable by any knowledge of languages, yet far more certain than human speech.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique miro Dei beneficio rediisse ad cor, jamque in dicta civitate, resumpto sacro schemate, sub religiosa clausura inter sanctimoniales degere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053418Z">
        <seg>Finally, by a marvelous benefit of God, having returned to her heart, she now lives in the aforementioned city, having resumed her sacred habit and dwelling in religious enclosure among the nuns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique omne aliud dissertationis argumentum, quantumvis diversissimum, comi dexteritate huc inflectere, ut amor &amp; amabilitas Dei in thema reliquorum discursuum succederet, quo obtento, mirabile erat audire Virginem, alias taciturnam, verbis aptissimis, at vehementer ignitis, ratiocinantem de debito charitatis, de affabili bonitate Dei dignantis amari, de pulchritudine majestatis tam dignæ amari: tantum hic Rosæ non deerant vocabula emphatica&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053204Z">
        <seg>finally, to bend every other argument of the dissertation, however diverse, by graceful dexterity to this point, so that the love and loveliness of God might become the theme of the remaining discourses; which once achieved, it was marvelous to hear the Virgin, otherwise taciturn, reasoning with the most fitting, yet vehemently fiery, words about the debt of charity, about the affable goodness of God worthy to be loved, of the beauty of a majesty so worthy of being loved: it was only that here the Rose did not lack emphatic words&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;b&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique ordinato agmine tunc immigrarunt alta Dei æstimatio, filialis timor Numinis, cura salutis, abominatio peccati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035009Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035009Z">
        <seg>Finally, with an orderly procession, they then immigrated: a high estimation of God, filial fear of the Deity, concern for salvation, and the abhorrence of sin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique rationi, non contumaciæ in his parendum esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062814Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062814Z">
        <seg>Finally, that these are to be obeyed for the sake of reason, not out of stubbornness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique sciebat, inde se neutiquam surrecturam absque omnium membrorum gelido stupore, ac velut ex ossifragio, coxis, humeris, cruribus, brachiis miserrime divaricatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042642Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042642Z">
        <seg>for she knew that she would by no means rise from it without a frozen stupor in all her limbs, and as if from a fracture of the hips, shoulders, legs, and arms, miserably torn apart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique sinerent eam, quæ nil boni ageret, saltem mala pati, corpusque affligere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042936Z">
        <seg>Finally, they would allow her, who did no good, to at least suffer evil and afflict her body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique subverendum esse, ne nimia formido graviori periculo implicet desolatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060834Z">
        <seg>Finally, it must be overturned, lest excessive fear involve the desolate one in a more serious danger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>denique toti domui una hæc speciosa bestiola in deliciis erat, gaudebantque omnes hanc a matrefamilias nutriri &amp; asservari in spem sobolis, quando crevisset: nam sperabatur illi simillimos fore pullos, quos procrearet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061528Z">
        <seg>Finally, this one charming little creature was the delight of the entire household, and everyone was pleased that the matriarch was nurturing and keeping it in hopes of offspring when it grew up; for it was hoped that the chicks it would hatch would resemble it very closely.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>deplorans jacturam pretiosi temporis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043457Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043457Z">
        <seg>Lamenting the loss of precious time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>detecta erat venerabilis illa Salvatoris effigies, cui ad utrumque latus binæ ardebant candelæ in altari, ac tertia procul inde lucebat seorsim in mensula.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053700Z">
        <seg>The venerable image of the Savior had been uncovered, to either side of which two candles burned on the altar, and a third shone separately on a small table some distance away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dic nostro Creatori:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051614Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051614Z">
        <seg>say to our Creator:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dixisses arcam potius aut cistam grandiusculam, nisi modica ad latus fenestella esse domunculam clamitasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043643Z">
        <seg>You would have called it a chest rather than a box, or a rather large chest, if a small window on the side hadn't proclaimed it to be a little house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dolebat superbus, illic se non timeri ab imbelli juvencula, imo ea solitudine ultro ad monomachiam se provocari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051917Z">
        <seg>He was angry and proud that she, a timid young woman, did not fear him, but rather, in that solitude, was voluntarily challenging him to single combat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dolere se ob notabilem defectum, qui festivitatem ex parte obscuraturus erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053455Z">
        <seg>to lament a notable defect that was to have partially obscured the festival;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>doluit, suum nomen in tenera nepte sic obliterari, ac sere despici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034708Z">
        <seg>He grieved that his name was thus erased from his tender granddaughter and almost despised.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec ferocius materno compulsa imperio obtemperavit, munusque invisum admisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035337Z">
        <seg>Until, more fiercely compelled by her mother's command, she obeyed and accepted the hated task.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec fortuito casu manum cervicali incuriose imponens contrectavit hostem in latebra, juncos deprehendit, extraxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042756Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042756Z">
        <seg>Until, by chance, carelessly placing his hand on the neck, he seized the enemy in ambush, discovered the rushes, and pulled them out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec impavida, sed temeritatis pertæsa Virgo, exclamavit ad Sponsum, “Ne tradas bestiis animas confitentes tibi”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051939Z">
        <seg>Until the fearless, but disgusted with rashness, Virgin exclaimed to the Spouse, "Do not hand over the souls that confess to you to the beasts."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>donec tandem (incertum quibus indiciis) rescivit confessarius, qui continuo præcepit Virgini, catenam, ut erat, sine mora sibi transmitteret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041752Z">
        <seg>Until at last the confessor, by some uncertain means, learned of it, and immediately ordered the virgin to send him the chain, as it was, without delay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>duabus ac interdum tribus horis uno defixa loco cælumque respiciens, his vocibus immorabatur, eo calore &amp; impetu, ut audientium corda ad amorem &amp; compunctionem ignara commoverit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053223Z">
        <seg>For two and sometimes three hours, she would remain fixed in one spot, gazing at the sky, and linger over these words with such warmth and impetus that she moved the hearts of her listeners to a love and contrition they had never known.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dum nebat, texebat, dum sericos acu pingebat flores, dum aliis colloquebatur, dum comedebat, legebat, ambulabat, in templo, in horto, domi, foris, in platea, in cellula, ubique obversabatur interno obtutui cominus (ac velut in speculo) amabilis illa Majestas, cui serviunt angeli, cujus pulchritudinem sol &amp; luna mirantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044833Z">
        <seg>while she was spinning, weaving, painting flowers on silk with a needle, talking with others, eating, reading, walking, in the temple, in the garden, at home, outside, in the street, in a cell, everywhere she turned, that lovely Majesty, whom angels serve and whose beauty the sun and moon admire, was close to her inward gaze (as if in a mirror).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dumque agebat omnia, &lt;s2&gt;[febrique concionatoris in se translata,]&lt;/s2&gt; nihil se egisse querebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053443Z">
        <seg>And while he was doing everything, he complained that he had done nothing, having taken on the duties of a public speaker.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>dumque verbis humillimis enarrat successum, visa est (ut quondam Stephanus) vultu prorsus angelico aliquamdiu radiare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051047Z">
        <seg>And as she recounted the success in the humblest of words, she seemed (like Stephen of old) to glow for a while with a wholly angelic countenance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Antiqui&lt;/e0&gt; veredos &lt;e1&gt;appellabant equos velocissimi cursus, quibus in venatione utebantur, aut ipsas rhedas, quas equi tales vehebant, ut alii malunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052324Z">
        <seg>The ancients called veredos the very fastest racehorses, which they used for hunting, or the chariots themselves that such horses drew, as others prefer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Gorræus in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T072813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T072813Z">
        <seg>Furthermore, Gorræus in the medical definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Hæc herba Indica, jam per Europam passim nota &amp; usitata, primo nomen accepit ab Americana ejusdem nominis insula, in qua potissimumcrescebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060933Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060933Z">
        <seg>This Indian herb, now widely known and used throughout Europe, was first named after the American island of the same name, on which it grew most abundantly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Joannes de Laet lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T074440Z">
        <seg>g John of Laet, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Suspicor, esse eamdem speciem arboris, quam Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043137Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043137Z">
        <seg>I suspect it is the same species of tree that John Latius mentions in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;Teste Baudrando&lt;/e0&gt;, Portus dives &lt;e1&gt;est insula ampla Americæ Septemtrionalis, quæ ab Hispanis&lt;/e1&gt; la isla de Puerto rico, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; ab incolis&lt;/e2&gt; Boriquen &lt;e3&gt;appellatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T231511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035127Z">
        <seg>e &lt;e0&gt;According to Baudrand&lt;/e0&gt;, the rich port &lt;e1&gt;is a large island of North America, which is called by the Spaniards&lt;/e1&gt; la isla de Puerto Rico, &lt;e2&gt;and by the inhabitants&lt;/e2&gt; Boriquen&lt;e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Antidora, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt;&lt;s1&gt;ἀντίδωρα&lt;/s1&gt;,&lt;e2&gt;sunt remunerationes, quæ pro dono alicui facto redduntur, ab&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;ἀντί&lt;/s3&gt; contra, &lt;e4&gt;&amp;&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;δῶρον&lt;/s5&gt; donum, &lt;e6&gt;quasi&lt;/e6&gt; contra-donum, &lt;e7&gt;seu donum dono relatum&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062402Z">
        <seg>Antidora, Greek&lt;/e0&gt;ἀντίδωρα&lt;/s1&gt;,are returns, which are given in return for a gift made to someone, from&lt;/e2&gt; ἀντί&lt;/s3&gt; against, &amp;&lt;/e4&gt; δῶρον&lt;/s5&gt; gift, as if&lt;/e6&gt; a counter-gift, or a gift in return for a gift&lt;/e7&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Asthma, &lt;e0&gt;Græcis&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἄσθμα&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;est obstructio pulmonis, frequentem &amp; difficilem sine febre respirationem inducens, qualem hi habent, qui velociter cucurrerunt, ut Gorræus in Opere mox citato pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052934Z">
        <seg>Asthma, from the Greeks ἄσθμα, is an obstruction of the lungs, causing frequent and difficult breathing without fever, such as that experienced by those who have run quickly, as Gorræus states in the work cited below, p.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Caryophyllum &lt;e0&gt;est flos odoriferus, quasi&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;καρύου&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;φύλλον&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;id est&lt;/e3&gt;, nucis folium &lt;e4&gt;appellatum, qui a Gallis&lt;/e4&gt; giroflee, &lt;e5&gt;&amp; ab Hispanis&lt;/e5&gt; sanamunda &lt;e6&gt;nominatur&lt;/e6&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055532Z">
        <seg>from the Caryophyllum is a fragrant flower, as if&lt;/e0&gt; καρύου&lt;/s1&gt; leaf&lt;/s2&gt;, that is, the leaf of the nut, called giroflee by the French, and sanamunda by the Spaniards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Dicasterium &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;δικαστήριον&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;pro foro judiciali proprie sumitur a verbo Græco&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;δικάζω&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;id est&lt;/e4&gt;, judico.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054729Z">
        <seg>A dicastery (from the Greek δικαστήριον) is, in the proper sense, a judicial forum, taken from the Greek verb δικάζω, meaning "to judge."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Emplastrum, &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἔμπλαστρος&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;teste Gorræo, est&lt;/e2&gt; medicamentum in massam coactum, quod præmollitum vulneribus imponitur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061646Z">
        <seg>An emplaster, from the Greek ἔμπλαστρος, according to Gorræus, is a medicine pressed into a mass, which, when softened, is applied to wounds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Gaspacho, &lt;e0&gt;vel potius&lt;/e0&gt; gazpacho &lt;e1&gt;ab Hispanis vocatur jusculum sive pulmentarium ex oleo, aceto, sale, pane tosto, aliisque rebus confectum, ut Franciscus Sobrinus in Dictionario suo Hispano-Gallico ad hanc vocem Gallice exponit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041436Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041436Z">
        <seg>From Gaspacho, or rather gazpacho, which the Spaniards call a broth or porridge made from oil, vinegar, salt, toasted bread, and other ingredients, as Francisco Sobrino explains in his Spanish-French Dictionary under this word.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Hieratica &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἱερατικὴ&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;teste Plinio lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050808Z">
        <seg>from Hieratic Greek&lt;/e0&gt; hieratike&lt;/s1&gt;, as attested by Pliny, book.&lt;/e2&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>e Phlebotomia &lt;e0&gt;est venæ sectio, vel sanguinis detractio, incisa vena.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045949Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045949Z">
        <seg>Phlebotomy is the incision of a vein, or the drawing of blood from an incised vein.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eamdem transacto festo devestiendam in domo Gundisalvi quæstoris exspectabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055334Z">
        <seg>The same, after the festival was over, awaited its dismantling in the house of Gundisalvus the treasurer.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego vero de pastu cogito, nec solicitor, quid retribuam Domino pro omnibus, quæ retribuit mihi”!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045041Z">
        <seg>As for me, I am thinking about my food, and I am not concerned about what I shall repay the Lord for all that He has repaid me!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego vitam æternam do eis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061925Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061925Z">
        <seg>I give them eternal life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ego, quæ tam fidelem, solicitum, fortem comitem, non ad latus, sed in corde circumfero, timebo in posterum a timore nocturno?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062101Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062101Z">
        <seg>Will I, who carry such a faithful, caring, and brave companion not at my side but in my heart, ever again be afraid of the night?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ejusdem instituti Religiosum, a medicis pridie derelictum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060828Z">
        <seg>A Religious of the same institute, abandoned by the doctors the day before;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ejusque Mater sanctissima prægrandi sua pietate suppleat defectus meos, &amp; ausui det veniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054636Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054636Z">
        <seg>and may His most holy Mother, by her preeminent piety, make up for my shortcomings and grant me forgiveness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>empyrei favores delicati sunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051418Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051418Z">
        <seg>The favors of the heavens are delicate;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>en XVI patacones, quos penes me reperi, his utere ad seraphicæ imaginis ornatum, si opus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055052Z">
        <seg>Use the sixteen coins I found on me to decorate the seraphic image, if necessary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>en, quam splendide profitetur vultu, se cupediis ab aliquo dapsiliter hodie refectam!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040800Z">
        <seg>Behold, how splendidly she shows on her face that she has been sumptuously feasted on cakes by someone today!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eo anhelabat, hanc unice deperibat siderea indoles, nec facile a cælestium flammularum nocturno obtutu avelli poterat mens cælo nata: fatebatur, cæli siderumque diutina contemplatione se instaurari ac refici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043714Z">
        <seg>Her soul yearned for it, her starry nature longed for it alone, and the mind, born of the heavens, could not easily be torn away from the nightly gaze of celestial flames: she confessed that she was renewed and restored by the long contemplation of the heavens and the stars.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eoque privilegio gavisa est Rosæ mater.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062852Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062852Z">
        <seg>And Rosæ's mother enjoyed that privilege.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex altera parte, quas Rosa in sublime sparserat, hærebant in aëre, ibidem in conspicuam sanctæ crucis figuram aptissime coibant, eidemque cruci a succedentibus rosis decorus per circuitum formabatur limbus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062533Z">
        <seg>On the other hand, the roses that had been scattered aloft hung in the air, fittingly coming together there into a conspicuous figure of the holy cross, and a decorative border was formed around that same cross by the roses that followed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex creta insula advecta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052913Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052913Z">
        <seg>Brought from the island of Crete.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ex quo feroces incolæ, excusso regis &amp; religionis jugo, redierant ad pristinæ gentilitatis vomitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060357Z">
        <seg>since the fierce inhabitants, having shaken off the yoke of the king and of religion, had returned to the vomit of their former paganism.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>exaggerabat difficultatem operis, famem, sitim, ferarum pericula, æstum, lassitudines, exilia, demum &amp; venena, quibus gens illa truculentissima minimeque hospitalis in exteros fidei præcones sævire solebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060422Z">
        <seg>He exaggerated the difficulty of the work, the hunger, the thirst, the dangers from wild beasts, the heat, the fatigue, the exiles, and finally the poisons, with which that most cruel and inhospitable people used to rage against the preachers of the faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>exaruerat cordis fundus, distabuerat vigor, nulloque vitali motu, nullius affectus sapore divinis afficiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045633Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045633Z">
        <seg>The depths of his heart had grown parched, his vigor was gone, and with no vital motion, he was touched by divine affections, tasting them with no other emotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>excogitavit ergo &amp; amans Rosa, methodum ac rationem, qua Christum indumento, spiritualibus laciniis operose contexto, devotissime amiciret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053533Z">
        <seg>So then the loving Rose devised a method and a way by which she might most devoutly clothe Christ with a garment, laboriously woven from spiritual threads.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>eximiam corporis habitudinem sacco pannoso deformare, publicos conspectus fugere, &amp;, ne hominum placeret obtutibus, domi latere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035942Z">
        <seg>to deform her exceptional figure with a shapeless sack, to shun public view, and, so as not to please people's gaze, to hide at home;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>experire, si quid vales;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052057Z">
        <seg>Try it, if you're any good.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>extra hunc paradisum nusquam ei bene esse poterat, adeo ut domi abiret in proverbium: “Hortum adeas, si Rosulam quæris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043302Z">
        <seg>Nowhere outside this paradise could he be happy, so much so that at home the proverb went: "Go to the garden if you seek a rose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Bollandus noster ad diem 25 Januarii, sive tomo 2 istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045955Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045955Z">
        <seg>Our Bollandus to the 25th of January, or volume 2 of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Dum Hansenius hic asserit, sanctam Virginem&lt;/e0&gt; metrico sui idiomatis rhythmo &lt;e1&gt;angelum allocutam esse, satis indicat, hanc allocutionem versibus Hispanicis compositam fuisse: nam&lt;/e1&gt; S. Rosa &lt;e2&gt;idiomate Hispanico utebatur, utpote ex Hispanis oriunda, quamvis Limæ nata&lt;/e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052336Z">
        <seg>While Hansen here asserts that the Holy Virgin spoke to the angel in the metrical rhythm of her own idiom, he sufficiently indicates that this speech was composed in Spanish verses: for St. Rose used the Spanish language, being of Spanish origin, although born in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Hanc vocem apud scriptores Latinos non reperi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055535Z">
        <seg>I did not find this word among Latin writers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;In supradicta civitatis Limensis ichnographia vicus S. Dominici &amp; nosocomium sancti Spiritus, quod pro nautis ægrotantibus exstructum est, non longe ab invicem dissita assignantur.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T233006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035148Z">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;In the aforementioned plan of the city of Lima, the village of St. Dominic and the Hospital of the Holy Spirit, which was built for sick sailors, are assigned not far from each other.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Joannes de Laet jam aliquoties citatus lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041442Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041442Z">
        <seg>John de Laet, already cited several times, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Majores nostri ad diem 7 Januarii, sive tomo 1 istius mensis a pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043202Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043202Z">
        <seg>Our ancestors up to January 7, or volume 1 of that month, from page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Nomen Græcum&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;εὐτραπελία&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;idem fere significat apud Græcos, quod apud Latinos&lt;/e2&gt; urbanitas,lepor, festivitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050823Z">
        <seg>A Greek word, *eutrapelia*, means almost the same to the Greeks as *urbanitas, lepore,* and *festivitas* to the Latins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Tubus ex creta confectus est instrumentum, quo fumus tabaci bibitur, sicut Europæi æque acIndi jam norunt.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060958Z">
        <seg>A clay pipe is an instrument for drinking tobacco smoke, as Europeans as well as Indians already know.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f &lt;e0&gt;Tum in negotiis ecclesiasticis, tum in secularibus&lt;/e0&gt; chartophylax &lt;e1&gt;est custos chartarum seu præfectus scriniorum, qui privilegia &amp; similia asservat&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061655Z">
        <seg>In both ecclesiastical and secular affairs, the chartophylax is the keeper of the charters or prefect of the archives, who preserves privileges and the like.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Epidemia &lt;e0&gt;est morbus populariter vagans vel inter populum sparsus, ab&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἐπὶ&lt;/s1&gt; in, &lt;e2&gt;&amp;&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;δῆμος&lt;/s3&gt; populus, &lt;e4&gt;quasi&lt;/e4&gt; in populo versans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054735Z">
        <seg>An epidemic is a disease that spreads widely or is scattered among the people, from epi, "upon," and demos, "people," as if moving among the people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>f Pleuritis, &lt;e0&gt;quam Plinius&lt;/e0&gt; lateris compunctionem &lt;e1&gt;vocat, est inflammatio membranæ costas succingentis, quam Græci&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;πλευρὰν&lt;/s2&gt; &lt;e3&gt;appellant, cum febri acuta, dolore pungente, respirationis difficultate &amp; tussi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052941Z">
        <seg>Pleuritis, which Pliny calls a compunction of the side, is an inflammation of the membrane surrounding the ribs, which the Greeks call pleura, with acute fever, a stabbing pain, difficulty breathing, and a cough.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>famulare huic Domino, cole, venerare, ama hunc Regem gloriæ, qui sine intermissione ex pusilla icone, tam grandia in nos dona congerit, paratus majora &amp; plura effundere, modo velimus capere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054131Z">
        <seg>Serve this Lord, cultivate, venerate, love this King of glory, who without ceasing from a small icon pours upon us such great gifts, ready to pour out greater and more, if only we are willing to receive them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fassa est, modico illo tempore, quo cum S. Catharina Senensi de curanda sua dextera supplex egerat, simul in intimos cordis sui recessus largum insolitæ suavitatis rorem depluisse, qui etiam inde exciderit in corpus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055455Z">
        <seg>She confessed that during that brief time when she had humbly petitioned St. Catherine of Siena to heal her right hand, a generous dew of unusual sweetness had simultaneously descended into the innermost recesses of her heart, which had even from there trickled into her body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fecit tamen quod Rosa petiit, scalaque applicita, coronam haud sine labore circumdedit collo imaginis, ac scalam removit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043537Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless did what Rosa asked, and, placing the ladder, he, not without some effort, placed the crown around the image's neck and removed the ladder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>femina ab illo die sedatior ac mitior, visa est induisse tractabile pacis ingenium, tolerabat corde quietissimo domesticos tribulos ac molestias, &amp;, si repentino aliquo flamine succendebatur bilis, hanc sola recordatione mansuetissimæ Virginis, mira facilitate sopiebat, brevique eo pervenit in patientiæ studio, ut jam ultro a Deo plus ac plus adversitatum postularet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060735Z">
        <seg>From that day on, the woman was more tranquil and gentle, seeming to have put on a tractable disposition of peace. She bore the domestic thorns and troubles of her household with a very quiet heart, and if any sudden gust of anger was kindled, she would quench it by the mere remembrance of the most meek Virgin with remarkable ease. In a short time she reached such a degree of patience that she would now of her own accord ask God for more and more adversity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>festinet, cælos inclinet,</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051629Z">
        <seg>Let him hurry, let him incline the heavens,</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>filiæque pulvinar obtrusit lana vacuum, sed cum jussu, ut illud farciret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042731Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042731Z">
        <seg>and stuffed the daughter's pillow with wool, but at her command, to stuff it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>frequenter ab illa benedictionem supernæ dulcedinis emanare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054050Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054050Z">
        <seg>Frequently, a blessing of heavenly sweetness emanates from her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fuga seculi &amp; usus negotiorum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050138Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050138Z">
        <seg>Flight from the world &amp; the practice of business.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>fugite, declinate lubrica infelicissimæ æternitatis præcipitia, qui unico eoque incertissimo fugacis vitæ momento distatis a gehenna;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060530Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060530Z">
        <seg>Flee, you who stand on the slippery precipices of the most wretched eternity, separated from hell by a single, most uncertain moment of fleeting life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>futuræ ibidem fabricæ plantam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;*&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; ac systema in tabella delineavit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062452Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062452Z">
        <seg>He drew the plant and system of the future factory there on a tablet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Hoc nomen ad inflexionem Latinam detortum est, &amp; componitur ex præpositione Græca&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;ἀντὶ&lt;/s1&gt; contra, &lt;e2&gt;&amp; voce&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;βάρος&lt;/s3&gt; pondus, &lt;e4&gt;quod Galli vernacule dicunt&lt;/e4&gt; contre-poids, &lt;e5&gt;quasi&lt;/e5&gt; contra-pondus, &lt;e6&gt;quod Latini proprie&lt;/e6&gt; libramen &lt;e7&gt;vel&lt;/e7&gt; libramentum &lt;e8&gt;appelant&lt;/e8&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050848Z">
        <seg>This name was borrowed for the Latin inflection, and is composed of the Greek prefix ἀντὶ, meaning "against," and the word βάρος, meaning "weight," which the French call contre-poids. as if&lt;/e5&gt; counter-weight, which the Latins properly&lt;/e6&gt; call libramen or libramentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Joannes de Laet lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052339Z">
        <seg>g John of Laet, book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;Pentecosten ab Hispanis vernacule&lt;/e0&gt; Pascua del Espiritu santo &lt;e1&gt;communiter appellari audivi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T233105Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035151Z">
        <seg>g &lt;e0&gt;I have heard that Pentecost is commonly called&lt;/e0&gt; Pascua del Espiritu Santo &lt;e1&gt;by the Spaniards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Arthritis &lt;e0&gt;a Latinis vocatur&lt;/e0&gt; morbus articularis, &lt;e1&gt;cujus species peculiares sunt ischias, podagra, chiragra, &amp; alii morbi hujusmodi, ut apud Gorræum in Definitionibus medicis pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055545Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055545Z">
        <seg>Arthritis is called a Latin disease of the joints, whose particular species are sciatica, gout, chiragra, and other diseases of this kind, as in Gorræus in his Medical Definitions, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Hæmorrhagia &lt;e0&gt;Græce&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;αἱμοῤῥαγία&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;est copiosus immoderatusque sanguinis effluxus, ab&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;αἷμα&lt;/s3&gt; sanguis, &lt;e4&gt;&amp;&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;ῥέω&lt;/s5&gt; fluo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061001Z">
        <seg>Hemorrhage from Greek&lt;/e0&gt; αἱμοῤῥαγία&lt;/s1&gt; is an abundant and excessive flow of blood, from&lt;/e2&gt; αἷμα&lt;/s3&gt; blood, &amp;&lt;/e4&gt; ῥέω&lt;/s5&gt; to flow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Magnes &lt;e0&gt;a Græcis dicitur&lt;/e0&gt; siderites, &lt;e1&gt;estque lapis occulta naturæ vi ferrum ad se trahens, a voce Græca&lt;/e1&gt; &lt;s2&gt;σίδηρον&lt;/s2&gt;, &lt;e3&gt;qua ferrum significatur&lt;/e3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054742Z">
        <seg>A magnet is called a siderite by the Greeks, and is a stone that draws iron to itself by a hidden natural force, from the Greek word σίδηρον, which signifies iron.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Pericardion &lt;e0&gt;est tunica crassa, dura, a cordis basi nata &amp; ipsum undique ambiens, quampropterea vulgus&lt;/e0&gt; capsulam cordis &lt;e1&gt;appellat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052950Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052950Z">
        <seg>The pericardium is a thick, tough sac arising from the base of the heart and surrounding it on all sides; for this reason, the common people call it the heart's capsule.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>g Quito &lt;e0&gt;est provincia &amp; urbs Americæ, in qua est conventus juridicus &amp; episcopalis cathedra, quemadmodum Joannes Latius lib.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061659Z">
        <seg>Quito is a province and city of America, in which there is a legal assembly and a episcopal see, as John Latius states in his book.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>gaudebat, se teneri in dextera Salvatoris velut rosam electam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061916Z">
        <seg>He rejoiced to be held in the Savior's right hand like a chosen rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>geniculata in Rosariano divæ Virginis sacello.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044241Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044241Z">
        <seg>Kneel in the chapel of the Virgin Mary's Rosary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>gravius dolebat, si replicarent, ex mera humilitate similia dici;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040549Z">
        <seg>He would have been more distressed if they had replied that such things were said out of mere humility.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Adjectivum&lt;/e0&gt; sympathetica &lt;e1&gt;provenit a nomine appellativo&lt;/e1&gt; sympathia, &lt;e2&gt;quod a præpositione&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;συν&lt;/s3&gt; cum, &lt;e4&gt;&amp;&lt;/e4&gt; &lt;s5&gt;πάθεια&lt;/s5&gt; passio &lt;e6&gt;componitur, &amp; verbum de verbo&lt;/e6&gt; compassionem &lt;e7&gt;vel&lt;/e7&gt; condolentiam &lt;e8&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054745Z">
        <seg>The adjective sympathetic&lt;/e0&gt; comes from the noun&lt;/e1&gt; sympathy&lt;/e1&gt;, which is composed of the prefix συν&lt;/s3&gt; with, &amp;&lt;/e4&gt; πάθεια&lt;/s5&gt; passion, and literally means&lt;/e6&gt; compassion or sympathy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Laudatus Gorræus pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055551Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055551Z">
        <seg>Praised be Gorræus, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Non dubito, quin hic potus sanctæ Virgini nostræ, ex jejuniis ac pœnitentiis attenuatæ, profuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052358Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052358Z">
        <seg>I have no doubt that this drink of our holy Virgin, weakened by fasts and penances, has been beneficial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Per&lt;/e0&gt; febres ephimeras &lt;e1&gt;vel potius&lt;/e1&gt; ephemeras &lt;e2&gt;intelliguntur&lt;/e2&gt; diurnæ &lt;e3&gt;vel potius&lt;/e3&gt; ephemeras &lt;e4&gt;intelliguntur&lt;/e4&gt; diurnæ &lt;e5&gt;vel quotidianæ, a radice&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;ἐπὶ&lt;/s6&gt; per &lt;e7&gt;&amp;&lt;/e7&gt; &lt;s8&gt;ἡμέρα&lt;/s8&gt; dies, &lt;e9&gt;quasi per diem durans&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T202419Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052956Z">
        <seg>By intermittent fevers, diurnal, or rather ephemeral, are understood diurnal, or rather ephemeral, are understood diurnal, or rather ephemeral, are understood diurnal, or daily, from the root ἐπὶ&lt;/e5&gt; through &amp;&lt;/e7&gt; ἡμέρα&lt;/s8&gt; day, as if lasting through the day&lt;/e9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Veneroni in Lexico suo Italico testatur, Pentecosten ab Italis vernacule&lt;/e0&gt; Pasqua rosata &lt;e1&gt;vel&lt;/e1&gt; Pasqua di Maggio &lt;e2&gt;vocari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000148Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035201Z">
        <seg>h &lt;e0&gt;Veneroni, in his Italian Lexicon, testifies that Pentecost is vernacularly called by Italians&lt;/e0&gt; Pasqua rosata &lt;e1&gt;or&lt;/e1&gt; Pasqua di Maggio&lt;e2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>h Circitor &lt;e0&gt;vel&lt;/e0&gt; circuitor &lt;e1&gt;olim a præfecto Romæ constitui solebat, ut urbem circuiret vel custodibus ejus invigilaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061705Z">
        <seg>A watchman or patroller was formerly appointed by the prefect of Rome to patrol the city or to keep watch over its guards.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hac una meditatione acutissimos quosque hujus temporis dolores obtundi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052655Z">
        <seg>With this single meditation, to dull the sharpest pains of this time.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc ardentibus ocellis jugiter adurebat, omnique gestu, motu, flexu, protestabatur amorem&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050926Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050926Z">
        <seg>He constantly burned her with his ardent eyes, and with every gesture, movement, and turn, he proclaimed his love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc carbones desolatorii coxerunt in internæ derelictionis pœnali sartagine.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045444Z">
        <seg>They cooked these desolating coals in the punitive frying pan of internal abandonment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc deperibat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054115Z">
        <seg>was perishing for this;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hanc prompsit, ut in sacello SS. Rosarii imaginem divæ Virginis speciosus hic torques ornaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043522Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043522Z">
        <seg>He presented this so that in the chapel of the Holy Rosary, this splendid torque might adorn the image of the Blessed Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>has autem Rosa jacere humi, &amp; commeantium pedibus neglectim proculcari non ferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054856Z">
        <seg>But Rosa could not bear to see them lying on the ground and being carelessly trampled by the feet of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hi fuere undecim;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044049Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044049Z">
        <seg>These were eleven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic Rosæ matrem ea donavit: sic intra pauculos dies Maria de Oliva pro unico suo pallio, quod Rosa egenæ Montoyæ dederat, tria recepit nova ac meliora, didicitque animosius fidere tum vaticinio Filiæ, tum providi Numinis largitati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061144Z">
        <seg>Here Rosa gave her mother this gift: thus, within a few days, Maria de Oliva received three new and better cloaks in exchange for her only cloak, which Rosa had given to the needy woman of Montoya, and she learned to trust more boldly both the prophecy of the Daughter and the providence of the generous Deity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic Virginem obiter salutans, simul involutum strophiolo argentum porrexit, jubens illo præsentem necessitatem parentum sublevari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062254Z">
        <seg>Greeting the Virgin in passing, he at the same time offered a silver coin wrapped in a small cloth, ordering that it be used to relieve the present need of the parents.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic aptissime per varia pietatis exercitia dispensabat horas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043658Z">
        <seg>He most fittingly spent his hours on various exercises of piety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic ille inter amica, ut fit, colloquia, delapsus ad varia, cœpit narrare perfunctorie, quæ Rosæ (tunc iisdem ædibus cohabitantis) mirabilem vitæ sanctimoniam, castigationem corporis, operumque &amp; morum præstantiam commendabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040602Z">
        <seg>Here he, among his friends, as is customary, straying into various topics, began to recount perfunctorily those things which extolled to Rosa (then living in the same house) the marvelous sanctity of life, the discipline of the body, and the excellence of her deeds and manners.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic liberrimo contemplationis usu ascensiones in corde sua disponebat, propemodum ignara, in corpore an extra corpus degeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043701Z">
        <seg>She arranged her ascensions in her heart with the freest exercise of contemplation, almost unaware whether she was in the body or outside it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic partem affectivam increati Amoris decidua flamma igniri &amp; candescere, atque in palato voluntatis felicem beatæ fruitionis prægustum inchoari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050327Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050327Z">
        <seg>Here the affective part of uncreated Love is kindled and made bright by a fleeting flame, and on the palate of the will a happy foretaste of blessed fruition is begun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic solum abundabat fluida ejus facundia, hic ei inardescebat spiritus, acuebatur vox, fulgurabant oculi, succendebantur præcordia, nemoque præsentium dubitare poterat, ex abundantia cordis os loqui.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053207Z">
        <seg>Here his fertile eloquence flowed abundantly; his spirit burned within him, his voice sharpened, his eyes flashed, his heart was kindled, and no one present could doubt that his mouth spoke from the abundance of his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hic totos hærebat dies addita majori noctis parte;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043655Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043655Z">
        <seg>He would stay here all day, adding the greater part of the night.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc demum ad alias, quæ paulatim fundabantur, sacras ædes tota Sacramentorum administratio promanavit, ac velut ex uno augustissimæ cælorum Reginæ parthenico roseto, ver nascentis fidei in omnem circuitum floride se diffudit&lt;s2&gt;&lt;a3&gt;a&lt;/a3&gt;&lt;/s2&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054233Z">
        <seg>From this, at last, the entire administration of the Sacraments flowed forth to the other sacred edifices that were gradually being founded, and, as if from a single, most august, virgin rose-garden of the Queen of Heaven, the spring of the nascent faith spread itself in a flourishing bloom in every direction.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc importunitate creditoris, inde necessitatibus domus, &amp; cumprimis ægri mariti, sensim impar.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062247Z">
        <seg>Thus, from the creditor's importunity on one hand, and the needs of the household on the other, and especially of the sick husband, she was gradually becoming inadequate.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc radiare continuo in animæ suæ fundo præsentiam serenæ, amabilis, propitiæ Divinitatis, hanc certo se illic deprehendere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050305Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050305Z">
        <seg>from this, to continually radiate into the depths of his soul the presence of a serene, loving, and propitious Divinity, and to perceive it there for certain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hinc tantopere urebatur, sicubi privatim, aut publice quidquam committeretur, quod Dei honorem vel in minimo violaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053303Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053303Z">
        <seg>He was so zealous that he would not tolerate anything being done, either privately or publicly, that would in the slightest violate the honor of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his denique totum collucere cubiculum, imminere palam incendium: at iis procul abjectis, mox ardor, flamma, cruciatus evanuit, &amp; Rosa miræ suavitatis refrigerio cælitus perfusa dulcissime conquievit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035359Z">
        <seg>At last the entire room seemed to glow, and an open fire loomed: but when these were cast far away, the heat, the flame, and the torment vanished, and Rosa, bathed from heaven in a wondrously sweet coolness, rested most sweetly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his faciem &amp; manus obsidere festinabat importuna societas: repellebatur unus, succedebant quaterni, &amp; plerumque ex insidiis etiam incautorum sanguinem hauriebant, relicto pruritus ac tuberculi vestigio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043807Z">
        <seg>An importunate horde rushed to besiege his face and hands: one was repelled, four would take his place, and they would often draw blood from the unwary from ambush, leaving behind a trace of itching and pustules.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his in partem retractis, gaudebat moribunda, quod se lecticæ tabulis sentiret occipite humerisque acclinem, mirificeque contenta, quod crucifixo Sponso qualitercumque commoreretur in duro ligno, felicem spiritum paulo post in manus Creatoris placidissime exhalavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043007Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043007Z">
        <seg>When these were set aside, the dying woman rejoiced that she felt herself leaning on the planks of the bier against her head and shoulders, and was wonderfully content that she would dwell with her crucified Spouse in the hard wood. A little later, she peacefully breathed out her happy spirit into the hands of the Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his inermem jactando, trudendo, premendo miserrime concutiebat, ut jam singula Virgini ossa viderentur commolita, luxatæ scapulæ, nervi distracti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052122Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052122Z">
        <seg>He was miserably shaking her, throwing her, thrusting her, and pressing her, so that each of the Virgin's bones seemed crushed, her shoulder blades dislocated, her nerves torn;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his intererat usque ad meridiem tanta quiete ac silentio, quanto nec lapis in suo defigitur centro.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060019Z">
        <seg>It remained like this until noon, with such peace and silence that not even a stone held fast in its center.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his pro acu malleus &amp; scalprum, pro lana durissimi lapides &amp; saxa erant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044210Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044210Z">
        <seg>In place of a needle, there was a hammer and a chisel; instead of wool, there were the hardest stones and rocks.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his pulvinum implevit, impleto indormivit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042738Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042738Z">
        <seg>He filled this pillow, and fell asleep on it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his regerebat, utique sibi, quæ sola se nosset, fidem habendam esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040552Z">
        <seg>He was guided by this: that he should trust himself, who alone knew himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his sine culcitra paleave simplex ac detritum peripetasma instravit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042911Z">
        <seg>He laid a simple and worn-out mat, without a cushion, for them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his tundendis, æquandis, &amp; ad normam affabre conquadrandis instabant totæ, secabant, excavabant, lævigabant, defricabant, &amp;, ut ferramenta citius durior lapis admitteret, hunc affatim emolliebant crebro suarum lachrymarum stillicidio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044213Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044213Z">
        <seg>They were occupied with pounding, leveling, and skillfully squaring these to a standard; they cut, hollowed, smoothed, and scraped, and, so that the tool would take a harder stone more quickly, they thoroughly softened it with the frequent trickle of their tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>his velut fiduciariis intimorum conceptuum indicibus excitari in anima sua quamdam attentionem luminosam, qua sine discursu penetret limpideque percipiat singula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054400Z">
        <seg>to be stirred in his soul by these, as though by intimate fiduciaries of his innermost thoughts, a certain luminous attention, by which, without discursive reasoning, he may clearly perceive each thing;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hispidum, ex crassioribus equorum setis prædense contextum, vix non loricæ gravitatem æquabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041842Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041842Z">
        <seg>The coarse, plaited from the coarser horsehair, scarcely equaled the weight of a cuirass.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc Puellam jam grandiusculam frequentius delumbare solebat, præsertim quando comperta fuit, exemplo S. Catharinæ Senensis, rutilam sibimet cæsariem præscidisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052506Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052506Z">
        <seg>He used to frequently tickle the now-older girl, especially when it was discovered that, following the example of St. Catherine of Siena, she had cut a red lock of hair for herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc age, obsecro, hoc unicum stude &amp; conare, ut quam plurimas flagitiosas animas vorticibus eximas, ut ab ima vitiorum charybdi perituros evoces, &amp; ad salutaris pœnitentiæ securum litus educas”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060601Z">
        <seg>Do this, I beg you, strive for this one thing: to rescue as many wretched souls as possible from the whirlpools, to snatch from the very Charybdis of vices those who are about to perish, and to bring them to the safe shore of salutary repentance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hoc sanctæ patientiæ mercimonium est, &amp; utile fœnus: nemo quereretur de cruce, si hanc tantorum thesaurorum agnoscerent legitimam stateram”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052838Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052838Z">
        <seg>This is the merchandise of holy patience, and a useful interest: no one would complain of the cross, if they recognized this as the legitimate scale for such great treasures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>horam, qua mane surgeret, præscripserunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051319Z">
        <seg>They prescribed the hour at which he should rise in the morning.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>horum examina &amp; agmina immigrabant Rosæ tugurio, præsertim, quando vel per diem siccantes radii solis, aut sub vesperum serenæ noctis algores tenuissimis corpusculis imminebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043751Z">
        <seg>These swarms and troops would migrate to Rose's hut, especially when either the sun's drying rays by day, or the chills of a clear evening under a serene night, threatened their tiny bodies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hos Rosa digitis induit, opus arripuit, cœpitque sedulitate liberrima gemmas, torques, monilia suo, quo adhærebant, ordine disnectere, fila ligaminum apte dissuere, nodos incidere, ornatum per partes concinne deponere, imaginem nudare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055408Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055408Z">
        <seg>Rosa put on these gloves, seized the task, and with the utmost diligence began to unloose the gems, torcs, and bracelets from their setting, deftly to undo the threads of the fastenings, to cut the knots, to lay out the adornment in its proper parts, and to bare the image.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huc toto ferebatur amoris pondere, adeo ut horis integris vix (aut forte ne vix quidem) nictu unico cilia dimisisse, multo minus faciem vel ad momentum ab ara deflexisse notata sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060025Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060025Z">
        <seg>She was carried here, almost overwhelmed by the weight of her love, so that for entire hours she barely—or perhaps not even barely—let her eyelids flutter once, much less is it recorded that she ever turned her face away from the altar, even for a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic adversus somni insidias modicum id capillitii, quod obtegendæ coronæ in fronte residuum superfuerat, implectebat strictissime, eoque tormento accersebat vigiliam, recitabat preculas, somnolentiam strangulabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043100Z">
        <seg>Against the snares of sleep, he would most tightly bind that little tuft of hair which had remained on his forehead to cover his crown, and with that torment he would summon wakefulness, recite prayers, and strangle drowsiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic arridebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050919Z">
        <seg>He smiled at him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic assidebat immobilis, hanc dilapso frequentiori populo cominus tenerrimis basiis obterebat, in hanc eminus acclavabat oculos, nec alio vel ad momentum respicere poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054844Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054844Z">
        <seg>He sat motionless beside her; when the crowd thinned, he showered her with the most tender kisses; he fixed his eyes on her from a distance and could not look away, not even for a moment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic festum diem agebat Rosa, non sic lætatura, si totius Americæ sceptrum sibi, imperiumque sorte vel dono obtigisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053430Z">
        <seg>Rosa was celebrating this festive day, not that she would have been any happier if she had been granted the scepter and empire of all America by lot or as a gift.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic inferne spinea corona circumjacet, superne tres clavi infixi prominent, foliorum ambitum quasi filatim pendula flagella interstingunt, medium sinum acervatim implent granula eximii saporis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041253Z">
        <seg>Below, a crown of thorns surrounds it; above, three nails are fixed and protrude. The surrounding leaves are interspersed with thread-like, pendulous tendrils. The center of the sinus is filled in heaps with granules of exquisite flavor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic intranti una ex adstantibus feminis bona fide dixit: “Opportune venis, o Pater;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053340Z">
        <seg>As he entered, one of the women present said to him in all sincerity: "You have come at the right time, O Father;"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic mittebat crebra ex imo pectore suspiria;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054112Z">
        <seg>He sent her frequent sighs from the bottom of his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic protendebat breves ulnas velut amplexum offerens;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050922Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050922Z">
        <seg>He stretched out his forearms toward him, as if offering a embrace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>huic solemne est, quotannis ter in publici circuitus festivam processionem sublimi gestatorio efferre conspicuam sanctæ suæ tutelaris statuam, coronis, floribus, multoque gemmarum fulgore ac pretio decenter adornatam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055020Z">
        <seg>It is the custom here to carry on a festive procession three times a year around the public circuit the conspicuous statue of their patron saint, suitably adorned with crowns, flowers, and much glitter and preciousness of gems.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus liberalitati diffidere mihi jam non licet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062340Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062340Z">
        <seg>I can no longer doubt this generosity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus ministerium &amp; curam, utpote in familiari domo permissu matris, sibi Rosa assumpserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061336Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061336Z">
        <seg>Rosa had taken upon herself the care and service of this one, as she was in the family home with her mother's permission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus pharmacopœiæ se commendat, medicinam corroborativam petit, impetrat, nec aliunde, quam ex illo pretioso myrothecio vulnerati lateris, ubi quondam S. Catharina Senensis avide suxerat&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051115Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051115Z">
        <seg>He commends himself to this apothecary, asks for a strengthening medicine, and obtains it, and from no other source than that precious myrrh-bearing ointment of the wounded side, from which St. Catherine of Siena once eagerly sucked.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hujus providam curam mihi assumo: tu de reliquis, ut magis volueris, disponito”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061909Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061909Z">
        <seg>I will take charge of this with careful foresight; you may arrange the rest as you see fit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc Hispani, sed magis Hispanæ, ita depereunt, ut postquam semel illi assueverunt, se sine ullo vivere posse negent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052352Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052352Z">
        <seg>The Spaniards, but especially the Spanish women, are so infatuated with him that once they have grown accustomed to him, they deny that they can live without him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc generosum, præclarum, sublimem studiis suis finem scopumque præfigerent, ut residuis Americæ plagis, quidquid idololatriæ usquam supererat, profligent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060440Z">
        <seg>that they would set this generous, excellent, and sublime endeavor a goal and an end, so that with the remaining plagues of America, they would drive out whatever was left of idolatry anywhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hunc sibi dolorem videri prorsus intolerabilem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054143Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054143Z">
        <seg>this pain seemed to him to be utterly unbearable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc Limæ nata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T214204Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211808Z">
        <seg>she, born in Lima;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc Rosam absque alio magistro edocuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052252Z">
        <seg>She had taught Rosa without another teacher.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc circa gallicinium evigilans, vidit per tenebras frequentia micare lumina in cubiculo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053018Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053018Z">
        <seg>Waking around the cock's crow, she saw in the darkness a multitude of eyes sparkling in the room.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc enim lectulo suo clam, ad fundendas preces, in pavimentum sese demiserat, &amp; ubi maxime in obscuro se latere credebat, prodebatur lucidis igniculis, per os oculosque inter orandum evibratis, testante prodigio, quantum incendium fervebat intus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053024Z">
        <seg>For she had secretly let herself down from her bed onto the floor to pour out her prayers, and where she thought she was hiding most securely in the darkness, she was betrayed by bright sparks, shaken from her mouth and eyes as she prayed, a prodigy that testified to how fierce a fire raged within.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>hæc ignoto percusa gaudio, vertit oculos in parvulum, ipsumque similiter arridentem sibi conspexit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044315Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044315Z">
        <seg>Struck with joy by this unknown thing, she turned her eyes to the little boy and saw him smiling at her in the same way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Ad hanc celeberrimam Deiparæ imaginem fit magnus populi concursus, ut ipse vidi, quando illam prope Matritum veneratus sum.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054751Z">
        <seg>A great crowd of people gathers at this most celebrated image of the Virgin Mary, as I myself saw when I venerated it near Madrid.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Castellus pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055600Z">
        <seg>Castle page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis archiepiscopus Limanus die 23 Martii anno 1606 obiit, quem Innocentius XI Pontifex anno 1679 primum Beatis, ac deinde Benedictus XIII Sanctis adscripsit, ut apud illustrissimum Fontaninum in Codice Canonizationum pag.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035207Z">
        <seg>This venerable Archbishop of Lima died on March 23, 1606, whom Pope Innocent XI in 1679 first enrolled among the Blessed, and then Pope Benedict XIII among the Saints, as is found in the Most Illustrious Fontanini's Codex Canonizationum, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;Hic venerabilis archiepiscopus Limanus die 23 Martii anno 1606 obiit, quem Innocentius XI Pontifex anno 1679 primum Beatis, ac deinde Benedictus XIII Sanctis adscripsit, ut apud illustrissimum Fontaninum in Codice Canonizationum pag. 578 &amp; sequentibus videre est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000308Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T165635Z">
        <seg>i &lt;e0&gt;This venerable Archbishop of Lima died on March 23, 1606, whom Pope Innocent XI in 1679 first enrolled among the Blessed, and then Pope Benedict XIII among the Saints, as is found in the Most Illustrious Fontanini's Codex Canonizationum, page.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i Paralysis (&lt;e0&gt;a verbo Græco&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;παραλύω&lt;/s1&gt; resolvo &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; debilito) &lt;e3&gt;est nervorum resolutio, cum nervi &amp; corporis partes omnino sensus &amp; motus expertes sunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052959Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052959Z">
        <seg>Paralysis (from the Greek word paralýō, to loosen or weaken) is the relaxation of the nerves, when the nerves and parts of the body are completely devoid of sensation and movement.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>i Potosium &lt;e0&gt;est urbs Americæ in regno Peruano ad radices montis Potosii, &amp; argenti-fodinis inclyta, cujus situm, sterile solum, divitias ac fodinas proxime citatus Latius lib.&lt;/e0&gt; XI &lt;e1&gt;Indiæ occidentalis cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052404Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052404Z">
        <seg>Potosi is a city in the Kingdom of Peru in America, at the foot of the mountain of the same name, and renowned for its silver mines; its location, barren soil, wealth, and mines are described in the aforementioned book, Book XI, of the Western Indies, chapter.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ibi domesticis se subducebat tumultibus, jocis, fabulis, uni sibi cæloque vacans;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043258Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043258Z">
        <seg>there he withdrew from the household's turmoil, games, and stories, devoted to himself and to the heavens.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ibi mox liberrimo ejulatu, ac lacrymis suam amarissime sortem incusans, verberabat innoxium pectus, despuebat laudationis suæ cramben, caligabat pudore sui;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040608Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040608Z">
        <seg>There, with a most free outcry, and with tears bitterly blaming his own lot, he beat his innocent breast, despised the dregs of his own praise, and was blinded by his own shame.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ibique vicinæ arboris frontem occupabat, quasi dato signo eruptura in cantum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045220Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045220Z">
        <seg>And there, the neighbor's tree stood at the front, as if about to burst into song at a given signal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ideo frequenti hujus gratissimæ compellationis auditu velle se sui tum nominis tum officii sæpiuscule admoneri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034807Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034807Z">
        <seg>Therefore, by frequently hearing this most pleasant address, he wishes to be reminded from time to time of both his name and his office.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ideoque se intima animi sui sensa, uti vellet, apposite efferre non posse, ruditati suæ ne nomen quidem, si quod proprium extaret, hujus intuitivæ orationis innotuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050318Z">
        <seg>Therefore, he could not aptly express the innermost feelings of his soul; his ignorance was not even aware of a name, if any existed, for this intuitive mode of speech.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>idque perspicuum satis indicium erat, Deo sic placitum esse, in asperrimi lecti tolerantiam electam Rosam dilecto suo Sponso concrucifigi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042716Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042716Z">
        <seg>And this was a clear enough sign that it was God's will for Rosa, chosen for her tolerance of the harshest bed, to be crucified with her beloved Spouse.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>igitur ex nunc tranquillo animo esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061813Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061813Z">
        <seg>Therefore, be of a calm mind from now on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illa confestim rapta in mentis excessum, perspicue intellexit, quod animo dudum agitarat, nempe divinum id esse responsum de tertio habitu Prædicatorii Ordinis tandem assumendo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040159Z">
        <seg>She was immediately seized by ecstasy and clearly understood what she had long pondered in her mind: that it was a divine response concerning the third habit of the Order of Preachers, which was finally to be assumed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illaque fortius instante, promisit, se in singulas hebdomadas peculiariter certum diem selecturam, quo ipsam Deo commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062917Z">
        <seg>And with greater insistence, she promised that she would select a specific day each week on which she would commend herself to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ille ex insula Hispanorum oriundus, cui a Portu divite&lt;a5&gt;e&lt;/a5&gt; nomen est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203312Z">
        <seg>he, a native of the island of the Spaniards, who is named from the Port of Divite&lt;a5&gt;e&lt;/a5&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ille ex insula Hispanorum oriundus, cui a Portu divite&lt;s10&gt;&lt;a11&gt;e&lt;/a11&gt;&lt;/s10&gt; nomen est;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T193911Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211805Z">
        <seg>he, a native of the island of the Spaniards, who is named from the Port of Divite&lt;s10&gt;&lt;a11&gt;e&lt;/a11&gt;&lt;/s10&gt;;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic Ordinis sui habitum, illic Jesulum Sponsum nacta, divelli a loco non poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051301Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051301Z">
        <seg>Having there received the habit of her Order, and there espoused to Jesus, she could not be moved from the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic lacrymose procubua misericordiæ Matri angorem suum exposuit, remedium poposcit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034733Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034733Z">
        <seg>There, in tears, he prostrated himself before the Mother of Mercy, laid bare his anguish, and asked for a remedy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic pro Christi corpore corpus meum vulneribus objiciam, nec cedam, donec impiorum bipennibus undique perfossa cadam, quinimo precabor hostes, ne uno ictu repente me conficiant, sed lenta crudelitate articulatim corpusculum hoc totum deartuent, inque modicissima frustula partes singulas comminuant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060233Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060233Z">
        <seg>There I will lay my body, a substitute for Christ's, open to wounds, and I will not yield, until I fall, pierced on all sides by the two-edged weapons of the wicked. Nay, I will even pray my enemies not to finish me off with a single blow, but to dismember this little body of mine limb by limb with slow cruelty, and to crush each part into the smallest fragments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illic videbatur morosius &amp; amorosius occupari: nam instar apis favo construendo intentæ, vertebatur in breves gyros, quasi penicillo quidpiam delineans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044117Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044117Z">
        <seg>There she seemed to be occupied more slowly and lovingly: for, like a bee intent on building a honeycomb, she turned in short circles, as if sketching something with a brush.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illo se mirifice posthac delectari, mulceri, colliquescere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034755Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034755Z">
        <seg>to be wonderfully delighted, pleased, and enraptured by it from then on.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illuc tu velaberis, ibi solemniter profiteberis, ibi Religiosa, vitæ reliquum consummabis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062827Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062827Z">
        <seg>"There you will be veiled, there you will solemnly profess, there, as a Religious, you will consummate the rest of your life."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>illum ipsum, in quo omnes thesauri, fidejussorem habeo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062337Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062337Z">
        <seg>I have him as a surety for all the treasures.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>immergitur anima divinæ bonitatis immenso pelago, &amp; metamorphosi ineffabili e se desciscens, transformatur in Dilectum, fitque unum cum illo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050519Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050519Z">
        <seg>The soul is immersed in the immense ocean of divine goodness, and by an ineffable metamorphosis, detaching from itself, is transformed into the Beloved, and becomes one with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>immorabatur tantisper lineolis, ubicumque prædulce Jesu nomen occurrisset: nam e singulis horum characterum apicibus quasi igniculos &amp; amorosos aculeos in corde sentiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050907Z">
        <seg>He lingered for a while over the little lines wherever the sweetest name of Jesus appeared; for from the tips of each of those characters he felt as if tiny sparks and amorous thorns were piercing his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo &amp; alia quæcumque vegetioris succi esculenta se admissuram negabat, ita visum esse Numini, cui ipsæmet naturæ leges famulantur ad nutum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041135Z">
        <seg>Indeed, whatever other food of more vigorous sap she would not admit, so it seemed to the Deity, to whose will the very laws of nature themselves obey.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo communem &amp; publicum illic tam jumentorum, quam hominum quaquaversus commeantium, frequentari transitum: proinde serio metuebat, flosculos tam decoros, tam eximias terræ gemmulas, prætereuntium vestigiis improvide conculcari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062554Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062554Z">
        <seg>Rather, the common and public passage there, frequented by both beasts and men traveling in every direction, was used by all; and so he was seriously afraid that the so-beautiful little flowers, the earth's most exquisite gems, would be thoughtlessly trampled by the feet of passersby.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo pararium&lt;s7&gt;&lt;a8&gt;b&lt;/a8&gt;&lt;/s7&gt;, symmistam&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;c&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;, dinomilum&lt;s11&gt;&lt;a12&gt;d&lt;/a12&gt;&lt;/s11&gt;, ut etiam hinc adverteret, quam dulciter Christus apposuerat erga eam cor suum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051601Z">
        <seg>rather, the paramour, the companion, the champion, so that even from this she might notice how sweetly Christ had set his heart upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>imo si quid videbatur recta incurrere in pupillas, non attrahebat nictu cilia, non claudebat palpebras, non declinabat vultum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044858Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044858Z">
        <seg>in fact, if anything seemed to be heading straight for her pupils, she wouldn't bat her eyelashes, close her eyelids, or turn her face away;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>impavida, nihilumque turbata respondit, oppido se lætari tam grato nuntio, solum mirari se, cur metuerint citius exspectanti indicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061848Z">
        <seg>Fearless and unperturbed, she replied that she was delighted with the town by such a welcome message, and only wondered why they had feared to inform the expectant sooner.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>importune, quod repentinæ ægritudinis id genus esset, curque dissimularet, rogabat: &amp; quo minus Filia, quid pateretur, eloqui poterat, eo vehementius miseram urgebat mater, instabat, adjurabat, quasi internum tormentum alio tormento investigatura.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045735Z">
        <seg>She importunately asked what kind of sudden illness it was and why she was hiding it; and the less her daughter could tell what she was suffering, the more vehemently the mother urged the poor girl, pressing her, insisting, and swearing, as if she would probe one inner torment with another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in brevi hac veste expeditior conscendam superiorem aræ crepidinem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060229Z">
        <seg>In this short garment, I will more easily climb to the upper ledge of the altar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in ea divinam misericordiam secretius operari sua magnalia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054047Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054047Z">
        <seg>in it, to work its divine mercy more secretly, its great wonders;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in eo quam longinquus Virgini Deus, tam procul ipsa sibi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045502Z">
        <seg>The farther she is from the Virgin God, the farther she is from herself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in hoc quam plurimas præstantis sanctimoniæ rosas certatim commigraturas, ut in cruciformem arctioris vitæ regulam coaptatæ, e sublimi perfectionis gradu terram despiciant, ipsisque hic mundus crucifixus sit, &amp; ipsæ mundo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062542Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062542Z">
        <seg>that in this place the most numerous roses of outstanding holiness may strive to settle, so that, fitted into the cruciform rule of a stricter life, they may look down upon the earth from the lofty height of perfection, and that this world may be crucified to them, and they to the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in lemma, quod Christus ipse dictarat, &amp; ore proprio gratiose expresserat: sciebat, illud humanitus non potuisse aut fratri suo, aut ulli mortalium innotescere, altioris id instinctus fuisse dictamen;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044458Z">
        <seg>in the lemma, which Christ himself had dictated and graciously expressed with his own mouth, he knew that it could not have been known to his brother, or to any mortal, by human means, but that it was the dictate of a higher instinct.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>in sancta veritate protestor tibi, o Rosa, tuum erit hanc curam suscipere, ut præsto sit cochlear, dum a vesperis redeo” Rediit, quæsivit, circumspexit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035654Z">
        <seg>"I swear to you in holy truth, O Rosa, it will be your duty to see that a spoon is ready by the time I return from the evening service." She returned, searched, and looked around.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inculcare supremam obligationem diligendi Numen, quod tantopere nos dilexit &amp; diligit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053158Z">
        <seg>to inculcate the supreme obligation to love God, who so greatly loved us and loves us;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde notabiliter in cor suum descendere calores &amp; æstus perjucundos, quos explicare non possit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050302Z">
        <seg>from which, remarkably, descend into his heart most delightful heats and heats, which he cannot explain;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inde querula suspiriorum dulcedine accusabat violentiam gaudiorum, quæ felicis Rosæ anulus ingerebat: “O (inquit) amores!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044625Z">
        <seg>Then the querulous one, with the sweetness of sighs, accused the violence of the joys that the happy Rose's ring was bringing: "O, loves!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inquit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051229Z">
        <seg>he said</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>instabat, utique decentius in celebri Excalceatarum virginum parthenone, quam foris inter ultimas Tertiarias militaturam Deo, vacaturam spiritui, inhæsuram contemplationi.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040252Z">
        <seg>It was imminent, and of course more fitting in the famous parthenon of the Barefoot Virgins than out in the open, where she was to become a Tertiary for God, devote herself to the spirit, and immerse herself in contemplation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>insuper a summo mane præcedentis feriæ quintæ (cui nomen a Cœna Domini) continuo usque ad istius feriæ sextæ meridiem immota perstiterat in eodem S. Dominici templo, vigilias agens coram sacrosancta Hostia, tunc de more in S. sepulchri arculam solemniter illata, nec bolum aut aquæ guttulam toto illo triginta circiter horarum intervallo sumpserat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040751Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040751Z">
        <seg>Moreover, from early morning on the preceding Thursday (named from the Lord's Supper), it had remained motionless in the same St. until noon on this Friday. Dominic, in the temple of the same S. Dominic, had kept vigil before the most sacred Host, then, according to custom, solemnly placed in the little shrine of the Holy Sepulchre, and had taken neither a morsel nor a drop of water during that entire interval of some thirty hours.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>interea clarescere in corde suo placidam altioris sphæræ serenitatem, pacem, temperiem nulla comparatione explicabilem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055901Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, a placid serenity of a higher sphere, a peace and temper, inexpressible by any comparison, was growing clear in his heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>interjecit se blandiuscule Rosa, patris iram propitiatura;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042226Z">
        <seg>Rosa interjected herself rather sweetly, to appease her father's anger.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>inæstimabilis sibi videbatur illorum felicitas, quibus datum fuerat ultima gutta sanguinis obsignare fidei characterem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060307Z">
        <seg>Inestimable seemed to him the happiness of those who had been given the final drop of blood to seal the character of faith.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ipsa legumina, flores, frutices, corymbos agitatione varia quodammodo animari, in placidam hymnidici murmuris harmoniam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045149Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045149Z">
        <seg>The very legumes, flowers, shrubs, and clusters, through varied agitation, are somehow enlivened into a placid harmony of hymeneal murmuring.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ipse auriga circumspectabat trepidus, qua parte tutius in terram descenderet, seseque in pedes conjiceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062151Z">
        <seg>The charioteer himself was anxiously looking around, seeking a safer spot to descend to the ground and set himself on his feet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>is enim secretarium Joannem de Tineo, tunc e portu Callaoensi&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; Limam reversum, negotiorum causa convenerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053753Z">
        <seg>For he had summoned the secretary, John de Tineo, who had then returned to Lima from the port of Callao, on business.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>is vero dum Filiam sibi obstantem impetuose dimovet, fortuito manus errore, impegit leviter in caput Virginis, ea parte, qua tecta corona sæviebat: mox improvisum ictum tres sanguinis rivi secuti, frontem innocentis Filiæ decursim purpurarunt, manifesto satis indicio, qualis herinaceus sub candido ejus tegmine clam nidularetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042229Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042229Z">
        <seg>But as he forcefully pushed aside his daughter who stood in his way, he accidentally, by a slip of the hand, struck the Virgin's head lightly, on the part where the hidden crown was raging. Immediately, three streams of blood followed the unexpected blow, running down the innocent Daughter's forehead and staining it purple, a clear enough sign of what kind of porcupine was secretly nesting under its white covering.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>istique morbi, qui prosternendo cuivis robustissimo sat erant singuli, plerumque bini, terni, quaterni simul, ex diversis oppositisque causis, in languidulam irruebant, mirantibus interdum medicis, ubinam in exsucco, arido, exhaustoque corpusculo tam diversi ardores incendium fomitemque reperirent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052618Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052618Z">
        <seg>And these diseases, which were each strong enough to prostrate anyone very robust, would often strike the invalid in pairs, threes, or fours at once, from various and opposing causes, to the astonishment of the physicians, who wondered where in that dry, parched, and exhausted little body such diverse fires could find fuel and kindling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ita quod sentiebat jam in animæ suæ fundo, magnum aliquod secum agi, nec suum procul abesse Lapicidam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044328Z">
        <seg>so that he already felt deep in his soul that something great was being accomplished within him, and that his Lapicida was not far from him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ita viderentur ex vultu B.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051437Z">
        <seg>they would thus appear from B.'s face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>itaque cum illo se fidenter in viam conjecit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051815Z">
        <seg>And so, he confidently set out on the road with him.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item 33 trientes rosarii Mariani, 33 dies jejuniorum, &amp; tria millia verberum, in venerationem suæ ætatis, quam vixit in terris.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053607Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053607Z">
        <seg>Also, 33 Hail Marys, 33 days of fasting, and three thousand lashes, in veneration of the age she lived on earth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item Conventus Limensis Prior;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050151Z">
        <seg>also the Prior of the Convent of Lima;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>item possessiones, latifundia, servitia, proventus annui &amp; instructissima totius abundantiæ œconomia, sic quod inclytum hoc monasterium intra primam annorum quadragenam tot cæli benedictionibus perpetim irrigatum, exemplo, fama, splendore in eum profecerit gradum, quo possit grandioribus præcipuisque totius Europæ monasteriis æquiparari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062433Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062433Z">
        <seg>Likewise, with possessions, latifundia, services, annual revenues, and the most fully developed economy of abundance, so that this illustrious monastery, within the first forty years, perpetually irrigated by so many heavenly blessings, by example, fame, and splendor has advanced to such a degree that it can be equated with the grandest and most principal monasteries of all Europe.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam enim Rosa vocari te jusserat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053344Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053344Z">
        <seg>For Rosa had already ordered you to be called.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jam venis arteriisque omnibus bulliebat Virgini sanguis effundi avidus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060251Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060251Z">
        <seg>Already the blood, eager to be spilled, was surging through all the veins and arteries of the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>jamque stillæ ad ipsum usque marginem ligneum, qui picturam includebat, defluxerant, ac novæ identidem guttæ a fronte, maxillis, oculis succrescebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053802Z">
        <seg>And already the drops had trickled down to the very edge of the wooden frame that enclosed the painting, and new drops were constantly forming from the forehead, cheeks, and eyes.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>juveniliter agis, &amp; doleo vices tuas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060645Z">
        <seg>You act like a youth, and I pity your lot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;Biographus hoc loco alludit ad vocem Græcam&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;εὐφροσύνα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;vel Dorice&lt;/e2&gt; &lt;s3&gt;αὐφροσύνα&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;quæ&lt;/e4&gt; lætitiam &lt;e5&gt;significat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000434Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035220Z">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;The biographer here alludes to the Greek word&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;εὐφροσύνα&lt;/s1&gt; &lt;e2&gt;or, in Doric&lt;/e2&gt;, &lt;s3&gt;αὐφροσύνα&lt;/s3&gt;, &lt;e4&gt;which means&lt;/e4&gt; joy&lt;e5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k &lt;e0&gt;Teste eodem Gorræo pag, 214&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;κατάπλασμα&lt;/s1&gt; est medicamentum molle &amp; consistentia mediocre, quod repellendo, concoquendo, digerendo, resolvendo, laxando, vacuando, detergendo, calefaciendo, siccando, dolorique sedando adhibetur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055619Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055619Z">
        <seg>According to the same Gorræo (p. 214), a cataplasm is a soft, moderately consistent medicine that is applied to repel, digest, digest and dissolve, loosen, evacuate, cleanse, warm, dry, and soothe pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k Cerberus &lt;e0&gt;triceps canis &amp; custos inferni, quemadmodum poëtæ fabulantur, hic accipitur pro ipso dæmone, ut ex sensu patet&lt;/e0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052410Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052410Z">
        <seg>Cerberus, the three-headed dog and guardian of the underworld, as the poets fabulize, is here taken for the very demon, as the sense makes clear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>k Sirius &lt;e0&gt;est astrum seu signum zodiaci, quod a Latinis&lt;/e0&gt; canicula &lt;e1&gt;appellatur, &amp; magnum calorem efficit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054754Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054754Z">
        <seg>The star Sirius, which the Latins call the Dog Star, is a star or sign of the zodiac that produces great heat.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>l &lt;e0&gt;Cum Mercurius a Græcis vocetur&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Ἑρμῆς&lt;/s1&gt;, &lt;e2&gt;ex illo nomine Græco hic adjectivum&lt;/e2&gt; Hermeticus &lt;e3&gt;Latina inflexione compositum videtur, ita ut&lt;/e3&gt; hermeticus &lt;e4&gt;idem sit ac&lt;/e4&gt; mercurialis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T000640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035226Z">
        <seg>l &lt;e0&gt;Since Mercury is called&lt;/e0&gt; &lt;s1&gt;Ἑρμῆς&lt;/s1&gt; by the Greeks, &lt;e2&gt;the adjective&lt;/e2&gt; Hermeticus, &lt;e3&gt;formed from that Greek name with a Latin inflection, appears to have been composed in this way, so that&lt;/e3&gt; hermeticus &lt;e4&gt;is the same as&lt;/e4&gt; mercurial.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>l Pulluli &lt;e0&gt;inter alias significationes accipitur pro stolonibus, qui ex arborum radicibus enascuntur, a verbo&lt;/e0&gt; pullulare, &lt;e1&gt;quod est stolones aut germen emittere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054803Z">
        <seg>Suckers, among other meanings, is taken for the shoots that grow from the roots of trees, from the verb to sucker, which is to emit shoots or a sprout.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>la</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T211659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211659Z">
        <seg>The Latin</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>laborabat memoria e multis aliquam saltem præteritorum favorum imaginem menti adducere, nec poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045515Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045515Z">
        <seg>His memory struggled to bring to mind even a single image of past favors, and it could not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>languebant artus, velut sub sarcina;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041907Z">
        <seg>The limbs languished, as if under a burden;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>laudabatque Patrem luminum, qui tanta, sapientibus ac prudentibus abscondita, humilibus, parvulis, indoctis revelabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050637Z">
        <seg>and praised the Father of lights, who revealed such great things, hidden from the wise and prudent, to the humble, little ones, and the uneducated.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>liquescebat in dulci jubilo, novaque Tertiaria reputabat se plus, quam nomine, beatam&lt;a0&gt;c&lt;/a0&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203505Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203505Z">
        <seg>She was dissolved in sweet joy, and the new Tertiary considered herself blessed, not in name only, but in reality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>liquescebat in dulci jubilo, novaque Tertiaria reputabat se plus, quam nomine, beatam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040230Z">
        <seg>She was dissolved in sweet joy, and the new Tertiary considered herself blessed, not in name only, but in reality.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>locum prædixit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062449Z">
        <seg>He predicted the place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lucellum qualecumque illud matri afferebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035802Z">
        <seg>He brought whatever little he had to his mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lumina inusitata, excelsa, pretiosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044535Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044535Z">
        <seg>Unusual, lofty, precious lights;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lætaberis, imo tuopte experimento probabis, quod dico”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062347Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062347Z">
        <seg>You will rejoice; indeed, you will prove for yourself what I say.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>lætumque Pæna canentes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040441Z">
        <seg>and singing a joyful paean.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>m &lt;e0&gt;Biographus Deiparam hic vocat cælestem&lt;/e0&gt; anthophoram, &lt;e1&gt;id est&lt;/e1&gt;, floriferam &lt;e2&gt;vel&lt;/e2&gt; flores ferentem, &lt;e3&gt;ab&lt;/e3&gt; &lt;s4&gt;ἄνθος&lt;/s4&gt; flos, &lt;e5&gt;&amp; participio&lt;/e5&gt; &lt;s6&gt;φορὸς&lt;/s6&gt; ferens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054810Z">
        <seg>The biographer here calls the Virgin Mary a celestial flower-bearer, that is, flower-bearing or bearing flowers, from ἄνθος, flower, and the participle φορὸς, bearing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>m Phaseolus &lt;e0&gt;aut&lt;/e0&gt; faseolus, &lt;e1&gt;ac etiam&lt;/e1&gt; faselus &lt;e2&gt;est genus leguminis, quo lexicographi pisa vel fabas intelligi volunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260319T001407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035232Z">
        <seg>m Phaseolus, &lt;e0&gt;or&lt;/e0&gt; bean, &lt;e1&gt;and also&lt;/e1&gt; faselus, &lt;e2&gt;is a genus of legume which lexicographers understand to mean peas or beans.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>magister F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050020Z">
        <seg>Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>magistro F.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044942Z">
        <seg>To Master F.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>magna es, sublimis es, gloriosa, præfulgida, incomparabilis es in triumphali Sanctarum agmine.” Permulta similia ructabat identidem corde pleno, affectu suavissimo, at corpore prorsus immobili: nam intimus ille jubilus, ut linguam &amp; os sedentis laxaverat, ita reliqua membra ligarat, adeo quod nec valebat de sella surgere, nec brachia aut crura stupore obrigida movere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044600Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044600Z">
        <seg>You are great, you are sublime, glorious, resplendent, incomparable in the triumphal procession of the Saints." He repeatedly bellowed many similar things from a heart full of the sweetest feeling, yet his body remained completely motionless: for that innermost jubilation, having loosened the tongue and mouth of the seated man, had bound the rest of his limbs, so that he could neither rise from his seat nor move his arms or legs, which were stiffened by stupor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>male locari tot scholarum labores, tot noctes insomnes, tot disputationum clamorosas raucedines, si tantis parta sudoribus eruditio proximorum saluti, fideique propagationi non impendatur, si ad metam inertis otii, ad vanitatem plausumque inanis pompæ, vel in temporario laureatæ frontis honore prætumide conquiescat, oscitet, desideat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060456Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060456Z">
        <seg>It is a pity to squander so many scholars' labors, so many sleepless nights, the hoarse clamors of so many disputations, if the learning won by such sweat is not devoted to the salvation of others and the propagation of the faith; if it rests, puffed up, at the goal of idle leisure, in the vanity and applause of empty pomp, or in the temporary honor of a laurel on its brow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maluisset frequentius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055650Z">
        <seg>He would have preferred more often.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mappas &amp; pallas altarium, operimenta calicum bombycina, &amp; quidquid textile ad sacram hanc supellectilem spectare poterat, omni pietatis ingenio, arte, elegantia conficiebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060112Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060112Z">
        <seg>Altar cloths and veils, silk covers for chalices, and anything that could pertain to this sacred furniture were made with all skill, art, and elegance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>matronam vero novo sibi beneficio implicuit, ipsamque tandem, non in lucrum unicæ per quot hebdomades diei, sed plene in omnium suorum exercitiorum amicam communionem, &amp; participium, arcano Dei impulsu stimulata, admisit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062929Z">
        <seg>But he drew the matron into a new benefit for himself, and at last admitted her, not to the profit of a single day for a few weeks, but fully into the friendly communion and participation of all his exercises, stimulated by a secret impulse of God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime cum ingens illustrium personarum multitudo ad concionem (de qua fructus eximii sperabantur) fuisset invitata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053458Z">
        <seg>especially since a great multitude of distinguished persons had been invited to the assembly (from which exceptional results were hoped).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime postquam is, qui in se ceperat exorare Madriti facultates regias novæ fundationi necessarias, Limam vacuus rediit, ne spem quidem permissionis umquam obtinendæ secum apportans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062718Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062718Z">
        <seg>especially after he, who had undertaken to solicit from Madrid the royal faculties necessary for the new foundation, returned to Lima empty-handed, not even bringing with him the hope of ever obtaining permission.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime quod sciret, ipsum non jam obstinatione voluntaria, aut obedientiæ contemptu, sed sola infirmitate resistendi violento ac annoso vitio, sic obbrutuisse in absurditate intemperantiæ omnium vecordissimæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060800Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060800Z">
        <seg>especially since he knew that he had not fallen into this by willful obstinacy or contempt for obedience, but only through his weakness in resisting a violent and long-standing vice, and had thus been so brutalized by the most wanton intemperance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>maxime, quod experimentis jam didicerat, vix aliter se importunas procorum molestias, parentum, fratrum, domesticorum adsultum, mundique immundi conspectum evadere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040035Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040035Z">
        <seg>Above all, she had already learned from experience that she could scarcely escape the importunate annoyances of suitors, the advances of parents, brothers, and household servants, and the sight of the filthy world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>me infelicem, quæ illo nunc careo!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050941Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050941Z">
        <seg>Wretched that I am, for now I am without him!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>meminerat, de quibus ille promiserat Joan.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061919Z">
        <seg>He remembered the things about which he had promised Joan.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>memineris, a Domino te constitutum piscatorem hominum, robustius tibi laxandum est rete in capturam peccatorum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060558Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060558Z">
        <seg>Remember that you were appointed by the Lord as a fisherman of men; you must cast your net more vigorously to capture sinners.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mihi nolo impedimento sint panni luctaturæ ac morituræ pro Sacramento;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060226Z">
        <seg>I do not want the cloths of the wrestling and dying for the Sacrament to be a hindrance to me.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mirabantur omnes, &amp; præcipue chirurgi, qui pridie metuerant oleum &amp; operam perdi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054029Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054029Z">
        <seg>All were amazed, and especially the surgeons, who the day before had feared that the oil and their labor would be lost.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mirari se, cur Deus nondum horribili abysso demerserit infelicem mundum, qui tantam ferebat peccatricem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040537Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040537Z">
        <seg>He marvels that God has not yet plunged the wretched world, which bore so great a sinner, into a horrible abyss.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>miscebant secretissima invicem colloquia, hærebant in mutuis vultibus, tamquam rerum aliarum aut obliti, aut incurii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051158Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051158Z">
        <seg>They exchanged the most secret conversations with one another, and stared into each other's faces, as if oblivious or indifferent to all other things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mortalitate hæreditaria cuncti putrescimus, nec est, qui suæ corruptionis non circumferat sentinam: itaque, mater, parum putato, quod tunicam obiter maculavi improvida: longe fœdius ipsum Redemptoris vultum rheumata, sputa, salivæ turpium carnificum, pro nostris sceleribus deformarunt”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061324Z">
        <seg>By hereditary mortality we all rot, and there is no one who does not carry about the stench of his own corruption: therefore, mother, do not think it a small thing that I inadvertently stained my tunic; far fouler still, the face of the Redeemer itself was disfigured by tears, spittle, and the salivations of vile executioners, on account of our sins.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox panno, ne quid in labiis residuum notari posset, os abstersit, ac pelvim elutam, defricatam, nitidam reportavit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061413Z">
        <seg>He then wiped his mouth with a cloth so that no trace of residue might be noticed, and brought back the basin, washed, scrubbed, and shining.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>mox pavide circumspiciens, advertit, non aliunde esse, quam a facie orantis in angulo Rosæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053021Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053021Z">
        <seg>Soon, looking around in fear, he noticed that it came from no other place than the face of the one praying in the corner of the Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>multisque flexibus sinuosam absolvens oden, Virgini cedebat vicem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045310Z">
        <seg>Completing an ode winding through many bends, she yielded the turn to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam &amp; ruboris incendium, &amp; doloris acrimoniam, crebra stillicidia tacite affirmabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043413Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043413Z">
        <seg>For both the blush of shame and the acrimony of pain were frequently affirmed by silent trickles.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam Rosæ a frequenti Communione adeo increvit pascentis Sacramenti esuries, ut paulum adultiori sacra Synaxis ter in singulas hebdomadas fuerit permittenda.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055659Z">
        <seg>For Rose's thirst for the Sacrament of Pastoral Care grew so great from frequent Communion that she was permitted the sacred Synaxis three times each week, once she was a little older.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam Virgo gliscentem sui nominis celebritatem aversata, dolebat ex candido indumento aliis feminis internosci in publico, eminus se venientem monstrari digitis, notari oculis, laudari a prætereuntibus, ipsique S. Catharinæ Senensi nonnumquam ab obviis comparari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040342Z">
        <seg>For the Virgin, averse to the growing fame of her name, was distressed that from her white habit she was recognized in public by other women, that from afar people would point their fingers at her as she approached, that she was noted by their eyes, praised by passersby, and that she herself was sometimes compared to St. Catherine of Siena by those she met.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ad aræ crepidinem defixis humi genibus, &lt;s2&gt;[tandem amplectitur.]&lt;/s2&gt; inter orandum placidissimo animi deliquio sensim diriguit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040351Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040351Z">
        <seg>For, with his knees fixed on the ground before the altar, he finally embraced it. While praying, he was gradually overcome by a most peaceful faintness of mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ad aræ crepidinem defixis humi genibus, [tandem amplectitur.] inter orandum placidissimo animi deliquio sensim diriguit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203511Z">
        <seg>For, with his knees fixed on the ground before the altar, he finally embraced it. While praying, he was gradually overcome by a most peaceful faintness of mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam aliunde quoque innotuit hujus prodigii conspicua frequentia, ut illud Virgo nec celare conata sit, nec negare potuerit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045205Z">
        <seg>For the conspicuous frequency of this prodigy became known from other sources as well, so that the Virgin neither tried to conceal it nor could deny it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam annis superioribus in palmaram distributione (utpote Filia Ordinis) præterita numquam fuerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044247Z">
        <seg>For in the previous years, in the distribution of palm branches (as a Daughter of the Order), she had never been omitted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam divinum est mysterium hoc, quod cernitis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041144Z">
        <seg>For what you see is a divine mystery.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ex iis non ultra quinque sumebat sextis feriis, adjecto solo vervecini fellis haustu, ut hac lautitie quinque plagarum Christi acerbitatem præsentius ruminaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041318Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041318Z">
        <seg>For he would take no more than five of them on the six days of fasting, adding only a drink of kid's wool, so that by this austerity he might more keenly contemplate the bitterness of the five wounds of Christ.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam exigui temporis interlapsu, vir illis in ædibus numquam antea visus, nec aliunde cuiquam illic domesticorum notus, intravit, Rosæ matrem poposcit, eidem quadraginta libras argenteas pro novo pallio coëmendo porrexit, abiit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061131Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061131Z">
        <seg>For a short time later, a man, never before seen in those houses and unknown to any of the household, entered, asked for Rosa's mother, offered her forty silver pounds to buy a new cloak, and left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam impeditus erat P.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053833Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053833Z">
        <seg>for P. was impeded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam interea, dum tu miracula narrabas absentis imaginis de Atocha, hæc præsens edebat signa inusitatæ lætitiæ, movebat in nos columbinos suos ocellos, attollebatur novo corpulentiæ typo extra telæ superficiem, velut inde ad nos exitura cum sopito Filiolo, spargebat arcanas dulcedines, renitebat, radiabat augustior, nunc parvulo suo, nunc nobis materne adblandiens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054543Z">
        <seg>for in the meantime, while you were narrating the miracles of the absent image of Atocha, this one, present, was giving signs of unusual joy, moving her dovelike eyes toward us, rising on a new kind of corpulence beyond the surface of the canvas, as if about to come forth from it to us with her slumbering little Son, scattering secret sweetnesses, shining, radiating with greater majesty, now to her little one, now caressing us maternally.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam nullus deinceps ibi culex Catharinam punxit aut suxit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043912Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043912Z">
        <seg>For from then on, no mosquito there ever bit or sucked Catherine.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam quæ tanto suffragio dignabatur novitiam suam excelsa supra sidera, hic operabatur ut felix cæli porta.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054332Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054332Z">
        <seg>For she who was honored with such a vote, her high station above the stars, here served as a blessed gate of heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam ubi ceteræ proles, pro garrulo rudis ætatis genio, aut sordes loquebantur, quas nondum intelligebant, aut cantilenas amatorias aliunde forsan auditas innoxie repetebant, plorabat Rosa, fugiebat, puerile flagitium deferebat matri, querebatur offendi Deum, quodque alia ratione castigare satis non poterat, in se ipsa lachrymis amarissimis vindicabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053325Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053325Z">
        <seg>for where were the other offspring, for the garrulous genius of a rude age, or they spoke of filth they did not yet understand, or innocently repeated amorous ditties perhaps overheard elsewhere, Rosa would weep, flee, report the childish disgrace to her mother, complain that God was offended, and whatever she could not otherwise punish sufficiently, she avenged on herself with the bitterest tears.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nam vix ambulare ac fari cœperat, cum quotidie, ob muliebrem aviæ, matrisque de nomine Rosæ controversiam, innocens Puella utriusque persecutioni se vidit miserrime implicitam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052448Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052448Z">
        <seg>For she had scarcely begun to walk and speak when, every day, because of a dispute between her grandmother and her mother over the name Rose, the innocent girl found herself most wretchedly entangled in the persecution of both.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>naturam ei omnino divinam esse, attamen bene se observasse, quod gratia res sit alia a Deo, tametsi Dei imaginem mirabiliter contineat, animamque constituat deiformem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052848Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052848Z">
        <seg>that its nature is altogether divine, yet he took good care to observe that grace is something other than God, even though it wonderfully contains the image of God and constitutes the soul deiform.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ne miremur lucis angelos umbræ speciem assumpsisse, dum gerebant officium.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051828Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051828Z">
        <seg>Let us not be surprised that the angels of light assumed the appearance of shadows while carrying out their duty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec alio oblectari se posse, quam quod experimentali hac certitudine, sentiret intra se Deum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050309Z">
        <seg>and could find no other delight than that, by this certainty born of experience, he felt God within himself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec alio posthac nomine te compellabo”: quasi cum Præcursoris matre protestaretur his Ambrosii verbis: “Habet vocabulum suum, quod agnovimus, non quod elegimus”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034702Z">
        <seg>"nor will I henceforth call you by that name": as if he were protesting with the mother of the Forerunner with these words of Ambrose: "He has his own name, which we recognized, not which we chose."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec aliud dolebat, quam quod cæteri non æque, ut ipsa, totam monasterii imaginem in vivo radio præsentem habebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062740Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062740Z">
        <seg>She grieved for nothing else, but that the others did not, as she did, have the entire image of the monastery present in living color.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec aliud moratus, illico abscessit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062257Z">
        <seg>Without further delay, he left at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec de illo aliud audierat, quam significari mysterium impenetrabile, nec temere ulli mortalium perscrutandum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061826Z">
        <seg>He had heard nothing else about it, except that it was a sign of an impenetrable mystery, not to be rashly probed by any mortal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec deerant, quibus Rosa videretur, præ nimio in seraphicam matrem studio, insanire;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062715Z">
        <seg>Nor were there lacking those who thought that Rosa, from her excessive devotion to the seraphic mother, was insane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec defuit strato tam nobili dignum cervical;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042623Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042623Z">
        <seg>Nor was a pillow worthy of such a noble bed lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec enim tunc (uti hodie) Limæ amplitudo usque ad illa ulteriora trans fluvium spatia procreverat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061156Z">
        <seg>For at that time (as it is today), the size of Lima had not yet grown to those more distant spaces beyond the river.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec forte alius est, qui diuturnam infirmi patientiam aut citius abigat, aut voracius absumat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052631Z">
        <seg>Nor is there perhaps another who either more quickly drives away a long-standing patient's endurance or more voraciously consumes it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec gemitu ac levi suspirio cruciatus suos accusantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052640Z">
        <seg>nor accusing his torments with a groan and a faint sigh.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec in tota ipsius vita, vel semel ei obrepserit fœda aliqua cogitatio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044055Z">
        <seg>Nor in his entire life did a single foul thought ever creep into his mind.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec loco, ubi feria quinta genua fixerat, surgebat, donec postera die Parasceves divinus ille Thesaurus (cum quo semper erat cor suum) transferretur ritu solito ad tabernaculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060044Z">
        <seg>Nor did he rise from the place where he had knelt on the Thursday, until the next day, Good Friday, that divine Treasure (with whom his heart was ever) was transferred in the usual rite to the tabernacle.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec molli spernis adesse Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053248Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053248Z">
        <seg>Nor do you spurn being soft to Rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec mora, fructum exigit, memor fortasse ex Canticis, quod ait Sponsa: Fructus ejus dulcis gutturi meo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051019Z">
        <seg>And without delay, it demands its fruit, perhaps recalling from the Songs what the Bride says: "His fruit is sweet to my throat."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec peregrina tot ænigmatum involucra cogitatu sat capaci adæquabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045701Z">
        <seg>Nor did the foreign wrappers of so many riddles match a mind sufficiently capable of thought.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec perfunctorie, sed ea solicitudine examinis, eo contritionis stimulo, eo gemituum ac lachrymarum apparatu, acsi per hebdomadas &amp; menses utroque Sacramento penitus caruisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055721Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055721Z">
        <seg>not perfunctorily, but with the earnestness of an examination, the spur of contrition, and a display of groans and tears, as if he had been entirely deprived of both Sacraments for weeks and months.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec se illi sustinendo parem umquam fuisse, nisi quatenus prodigiose manu Omnipotentis conservabatur in vita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045822Z">
        <seg>nor was he ever equal to sustaining himself, except insofar as he was miraculously preserved alive by the hand of the Almighty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec se, diutius solito ibi fortasse moraturam, evocaret ad cœnam, donec ultro ipsa prodiret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052042Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052042Z">
        <seg>nor would he summon her to dinner, perhaps staying there longer than usual, until she herself came out of her own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec segnius interea pergebat sui vultus roseum decorem jejuniis deterere;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035939Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035939Z">
        <seg>Meanwhile, she was no less diligent in using fasts to wear away the rosy beauty of her face.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec sibi videbatur totum seraphicæ magistræ induisse habitum, nisi &amp; spineam induisset illius coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042111Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042111Z">
        <seg>It did not seem to her that she had fully assumed the habit of the seraphic mistress, unless she had also taken up her crown of thorns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec solum istis, sed &amp; iis, quæ procul celebrabantur in dissita S. Augustini ecclesia, quæ non minus quatuor aut quinque platearum anfractu seu interjectu distabat a Rosæ domicilio.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043736Z">
        <seg>not only those, but also those that were celebrated far off in the distant church of St. Augustine, which was no less than four or five blocks away by winding streets from Rose's home.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec solum terrere venerat, sed &amp; nocere, si posset: nam (ut vidit ab immota se despici) rabido morsu pertentabat virgineum illud corpusculum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051930Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051930Z">
        <seg>He had come not only to frighten, but also to harm, if he could: for (seeing that she looked down on him from her immobility) he gnawed at that virgin body with a rabid bite.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen Rosæ nomen tunc ei impositum, sed Isabellæ sive Elizabeth;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034640Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034640Z">
        <seg>However, her name at that time was not Rose, but Isabella or Elizabeth.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen impedimentum, quo lubens volens tenebatur, austeræ matri audebat detegere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040004Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040004Z">
        <seg>Yet she did not dare to reveal to her austere mother the impediment that held her against her will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec tamen, in tanto humillimæ contritionis apparatu, sufficiens materia semper adfuit, in quam tuto proferri posset sacramentalis absolutionis sententia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040645Z">
        <seg>Yet even in such a display of the most humble contrition, there was not always sufficient matter in which the sentence of sacramental absolution could be safely pronounced.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec totam illius videri staturam, sed solo vultu, &amp; pectore tenus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050547Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050547Z">
        <seg>not his entire stature could be seen, but only his face and up to his chest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec toto cautissimæ suæ modestiæ velo sufficiebat intimos erga illam fervores abscondere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054118Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054118Z">
        <seg>Not even her most cautious veil of modesty was enough to hide her innermost passions for her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec ulla malignantis auræ intemperies, non horrida obscuritas, non frigora, non turbines ventorum, insuaves erant Virgini, undequaque suo corpusculo quidquid incommodorum poterat accersenti.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041646Z">
        <seg>Neither the malice of the air, nor the dreadful darkness, nor the cold, nor the whirlwinds of the winds were unpleasant to the Virgin, who from every side drew upon her little body whatever discomfort she could.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec umquam ei facundia defuit, qua sequelam virtutis, fugamque vitiorum efficaciter persuaderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060741Z">
        <seg>He was never at a loss for the eloquence to effectively persuade his audience of the pursuit of virtue and the avoidance of vice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec umquam formidinis sensum expresserant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052150Z">
        <seg>They had never expressed a sense of fear.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec verba erant, sed tonitrua: nam repente mens illa capacissima simul immensum concepit horrorem inferni, simul apprehendit vastitatem æternitatis, simul divinam attentius reverita majestatem ponderabat secum omnis peccati fœdam infelicitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035002Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035002Z">
        <seg>There were no words, but thunder: for suddenly that most capacious mind conceived at once the immense horror of hell, grasped the vastness of eternity, and, more attentively revering the divine majesty, weighed within itself the foul misfortune of all sin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nec, nisi voti compos, humo se attollebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040518Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040518Z">
        <seg>He would not rise from the ground unless he had fulfilled his vow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>necdum scio congrue te amare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053043Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053043Z">
        <seg>I don't yet know how to love you properly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>negavit ægra se posse: verum, audito, quod ipse paterfamilias jusserat, se subditam recordata, paruit, sumpsit, exhausit, tenui voce adjiciens: Ite, nuntiate Gundisalvo domino meo, me, quod revera non poteram, ipso jubente potuisse, nec in ipsa mortis janua me fore immemorem, quid hero suo debeat subdita.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040717Z">
        <seg>The sick woman refused, saying she could not; but when she heard that her master himself had commanded, she remembered her duty and obeyed. She took it, drank it down, and in a faint voice added: "Go, tell my lord Gundisalvus that I, what I truly could not, was able to do at his command, and that even at the very gates of death I was not forgetful of what a subject owes her hero."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe Regina SS. Rosarii supplici Rosæ gratiam (quam optabat) spoponderat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043556Z">
        <seg>For the Queen of the Holy Rosary had promised the supplicant Rose the grace (which she desired).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe duplicavit lanam, arreptoque bacillo, tantam ejus copiam non tam ingessit, quam impressit cervicali, ut hoc suberis tandem duritiem exæquarit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042815Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042815Z">
        <seg>Indeed, she doubled the wool, and, seizing a rod, she pressed so much of it onto the collar that this cork eventually matched its hardness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe idipsum olim seraphica sua mater ac magistra optaverat, &amp; tandem inter hos ardores increpabat seipsam, quod non arderet satis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054150Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054150Z">
        <seg>For her seraphic mother and teacher had once desired the very same thing, and at last, amid these ardors, she would reproach herself for not burning enough.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe qui super alios clericos &amp; Ordinum Fratres supremum habuerunt in ordinandis &amp; instituendis Americanis ecclesiis &amp; monasteriis officialatum, jurisdictionem &amp; spiritualem principatum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054658Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054658Z">
        <seg>namely, those who held supreme authority over other clerics and brothers of the Orders in the ordaining and establishing of American churches and monasteries, with officialdom, jurisdiction, and spiritual primacy;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe tria ligna tortuosa, indolata, nodosa tabulis imposuit, ac inter commissuras tenuioribus paxillis firmavit sub stragulo, ne cederent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042549Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042549Z">
        <seg>He placed three winding, unseasoned, and knotty logs on the planks and, between the joints, secured them with thinner pegs under a blanket so they wouldn't give way.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe ut interea sub hoc divino Sole fortius incalesceret devotum pectus, &amp; vivifica Sacramenti conjunctione vegetius foveretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055929Z">
        <seg>so that in the meantime the devoted heart might grow stronger under this divine Sun, and be more vigorously nourished by the life-giving union of the Sacrament.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nempe vario &amp; ipse examine scrutabatur Virginis spiritum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050702Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he too was scrutinizing the Virgin's spirit with a varied examination.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque animus illi deerat tentandi etiam his majora, ni prudentes confessarii vetuissent, qui identidem, interposito moderaminis præcepto, discretam virium rationem habendam occlamitabant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041932Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041932Z">
        <seg>Nor was he lacking in the spirit to attempt even greater things, had not prudent confessors forbidden it, who repeatedly, with the precept of moderation interposed, cried out that a discreet use of one's strength should be made.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque converti ad corporea phantasmata, sed imaginibus vacuum, spiritualis formæ luminosa puritate imprægnari: hic Deum immediato, intimoque illapsu implere sacrarium fidelis animæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050324Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050324Z">
        <seg>nor be turned to corporeal phantasms, but be imbued with the luminous purity of spiritual form by images: here to fill the sanctuary of the faithful soul with God by an immediate and intimate influx;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque defuere ex iis, qui rogarint Dominum, ut ab hujus terroris atroci tormento ipsos demum immunes vellet, libenter se quodvis aliud pœnarum genus subituros”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050423Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050423Z">
        <seg>Nor were there wanting among those who had begged the Lord to grant them immunity from this atrocious terror, who readily declared themselves willing to undergo any other kind of punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque enim illa animi sui profunda demissio permittebat, quod usque ad singularem sponsæ dignitatem, tantæque celsitudinis titulum auderet ultro aspirare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044027Z">
        <seg>For his deep self-deprecation would not allow him to dare, even to the singular dignity of a bride and the title of such exalted rank, to aspire of his own accord.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque incassum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035818Z">
        <seg>Nor in vain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque jam Rosa privatim inter familiares, sed Jonas inter Ninivitas videretur reipsa ordiri pœnitentiæ concionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060543Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060543Z">
        <seg>Nor would Rosa now be seen privately among the faithful, but Jonas among the Ninevites, actually beginning a sermon of repentance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque me præteriit: siquidem &amp; mihi, ad pondus concessæ adversitatis, gazam inæstimabilem superfluentium gratiarum admensus est.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052810Z">
        <seg>Nor did he pass me by: for to me, in proportion to the weight of the adversity I was granted, he has measured out an inestimable supply of abundant grace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>neque putare se, ullum cujuscumque partis aut membri superesse cruciatum, quem ipsa non senserit;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052705Z">
        <seg>nor to think that there is any suffering of any kind or part that she herself has not felt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ni prompte strinxeris, elabuntur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051422Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051422Z">
        <seg>If you don't squeeze them promptly, they will slip away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ni totum, quod exquiro, dixeris, nec ego te, nec tu me, quantum sat est, intelliges, &amp; figurarum (quas partim explicui, partim explicaturus sum) ænigmata sensumque integre non assequeris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050501Z">
        <seg>If you do not say everything that I seek, neither you will understand me, nor I you, as much as is sufficient, and you will not fully grasp the riddles and meaning of the figures (which I have partly explained, and partly shall explain).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihil in moribus aut motibus apparuisse tetricum vel subtriste, quod acidam saperet austeritatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040921Z">
        <seg>nothing in his manners or movements appeared grim or gloomy, which would have a sour austerity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihilominus reperit solicita carnis suæ domitrix, quo &amp; illis propriam dispensaret cruciatuum portionem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041916Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041916Z">
        <seg>Nevertheless, the mistress of her own flesh found a way to dispense to them their own share of torments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nihilominus semper e cælo sibi adfuisse præsidium &amp; robur, quo universa imperturbate sustineret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052708Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052708Z">
        <seg>nevertheless, he always had a heavenly protection and strength to sustain all things unperturbed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum ab avia materna Isabella de Herrera, cui inter lustrales aquas videbatur is honor deberi humano more.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034643Z">
        <seg>namely, from his maternal grandmother Isabella de Herrera, to whom that honor seemed to be owed in the human manner amid the lustral waters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum procul inter secretiores ædium porticus ambulabat Rosa, &amp; cum ipsa Jesus in statura octennis pueri, sed amictus jam lumine sicut vestimento.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051152Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051152Z">
        <seg>Indeed, Rosa was walking far off among the more secluded porticoes of the buildings, and with her was Jesus, in stature like an eight-year-old boy, but already his radiance shone like clothing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum sentiens cor suum concalescere, dum illius aspectui inhærebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053654Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053654Z">
        <seg>Indeed, feeling his heart grow warm as he gazed upon her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nimirum sex ex Ordine Prædicatorum, quinque ex Societate Jesu, singulique seorsim (velut uno ore, ac sub fide juramenti) testati sunt, Virgineum Rosæ candorem ea semper nituisse puritate, ut neque veniali macula ullius lasciviæ umquam fuerit attaminata;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044052Z">
        <seg>namely, six from the Order of Preachers, five from the Society of Jesus, and each one separately (as with one voice, and under oath) testified that the Virgin of the Rose had always shone with such purity that she was never stained by the venial spot of any lasciviousness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nobiscum ille habitare dignabitur, certus esto.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054100Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054100Z">
        <seg>Rest assured, he will deign to live with us.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non istud, sed illud factum aut dictum fuisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053334Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053334Z">
        <seg>that it was not this, but that which was done or said.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non languendum circa inutiles vocularum controversias;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060453Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060453Z">
        <seg>not to languish over useless controversies of words;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non nimis hærendum esse in subtilitatum apicibus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060450Z">
        <seg>one should not be too hung up on subtle points.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non quod deessent nocendi vires: nam idem a Francisca impetu obliquo resiliens, aliam corripuit feminam, eique cum togæ laciniis intimam subuculam raptim succendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055312Z">
        <seg>Not that he lacked the power to do harm: for the same blow, rebounding from Francisca's oblique thrust, struck another woman, and with the folds of his toga he swiftly set fire to the inner tunic beneath her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>non unico ore, sed poris omnibus clamasse in imagine Redemptorem: “Redamate amantem vos”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053920Z">
        <seg>He cried out, not with a single mouth but with all his pores, in the image of the Redeemer: "Redeem the one who loves you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nondum primæ infantiæ cunas reliquerat, quando rosarum speciem, nomen, spinas induit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052426Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052426Z">
        <seg>She had not yet left the cradle of her earliest infancy when she took on the appearance, the name, and the thorns of a rose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>noster hic labor, &amp; cura esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055417Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055417Z">
        <seg>This is our labor, and take care of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>notabat angulos, flexus confines;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062734Z">
        <seg>He noted the corners, the bends, the boundaries.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nova semper inveniens, quæ miraretur &amp; approbaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050625Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050625Z">
        <seg>always finding new things that she would marvel at and approve of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>noverant puellam, ad suas tam atroces &amp; crebras injurias patientissimam, ea sola tolerare nequaquam posse, quibus vel tenuiter Deum offendi existimaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053319Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053319Z">
        <seg>They knew the girl, who was so patient with her own atrocious and frequent injuries, could by no means endure alone those by which she would have thought God was even slightly offended.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>numerabat vices familia, &amp; nonnumquam quindecies cantasse brevi horarii guadrantis spatio, deprehendit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061556Z">
        <seg>He counted the family's turns and discovered that they had sometimes sung fifteen times in the short span of a quarter hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>numquam auditum sermonem jactantiæ, fastus, ostentationis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040918Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040918Z">
        <seg>never a word of boasting, arrogance, or ostentation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>numquam sibi obrepsisse impatientiæ motum, quo vel latum pilum discesserit a resignata conformitate ac amussi divinæ voluntatis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052711Z">
        <seg>that he had never allowed a movement of impatience to creep in, by which even a hair's breadth would have departed from the resigned conformity and submission to the divine will.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nunc experiar;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055244Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055244Z">
        <seg>Now I will try.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>nunc instare severius, ut saltem formosam (qua tunc rutilabat adhuc) cæsariem erigeret calamistris, ut bysso, fimbriis, cæteroque cultu nonnihil sese ampullaret, fuco ac linimentis roseam oris pulchritudinem gratiose accenderet, gestu, habitu, illecebris nativi decoris juvaret honestatem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035412Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035412Z">
        <seg>now to press more strictly, so that she would at least set her beautiful hair (which was still then in its natural curl) with curling-irons, so that with linen, fringes, and with the rest of her adornment would embellish herself somewhat, graciously set off the rosy beauty of her lips with rouge and ointments, and with her bearing, her dress, and the charms of her natural grace would enhance her dignity;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o animæ æstuantis vincula tenacissima, nexus perjucundi, amplexus ineluctabiles!” Gundisalvus commiserantis &amp; gratulantis affectui medius, rogabat num placeret anulum (unde hæc lætitiæ tormenta erant) manumittere, ut illo sedilis &amp; gaudii se carcere expediret?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044644Z">
        <seg>"O bonds of a burning soul, most tenacious, most delightful ties, inescapable embraces!" Gundersalvus, caught between pity and congratulation, asked whether it would please him to release the ring (from which these torments of joy arose), so that he might free himself from its prison of repose and delight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o deliciæ!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044641Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044641Z">
        <seg>Oh, what a delight!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o felicem animam, quæ penes se illum nunc distinet!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050944Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050944Z">
        <seg>O happy soul, who now holds him with her!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o festinate reverti ad hunc propitium Redemptorem, quem si nunc spernitis, nulla supererit in inferno redemptio”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060536Z">
        <seg>Oh, hurry back to this merciful Redeemer, for if you now despise him, there will be no redemption left for you in hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o ignes!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044638Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044638Z">
        <seg>Oh, fires!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>o quæ flammulæ!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044628Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044628Z">
        <seg>Oh, what little flames!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>obliquavit opus, flores absolvit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035536Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035536Z">
        <seg>He twisted the work, and completed the flowers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>oculisque cum gemitu in cælum elevatis, “O (inquit) bone Jesu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060626Z">
        <seg>and, with eyes raised to heaven and a groan, said, "O good Jesus!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>omni hominum ordini, summis, mediis, infimis charus &amp; admirandus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050200Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050200Z">
        <seg>Beloved and admired by all classes of people: the highest, the middle, and the lowest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>optabat sibi obtigisse sexum, facundiam, vires, quibus universo terrarum orbi solum Jesu Christi amorem persuaderet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054146Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054146Z">
        <seg>he wished that he had been granted sex, eloquence, and strength, by which he would persuade the entire world of the love of Jesus Christ alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>parvoque labore grabatum, uti genitrix permiserat, absolvit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042533Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042533Z">
        <seg>And with little effort, he finished the bed, as his mother had permitted.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>peculiare tamen quotannis, ad normam Dominici instituti, septem illos continuos menses observabat, qui festo exaltatæ S. Crucis, &amp; Paschati Resurrectionis interfluunt, Ordinis jejunio consecrati.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041312Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041312Z">
        <seg>However, he observed annually, according to the rule of the Dominican institute, those seven consecutive months which lie between the Feast of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross and Easter of the Resurrection, consecrated to the Order's fast.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>per illa te obtestor, periclitanti Franciscæ salutem impetra”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055250Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055250Z">
        <seg>I implore you by these, to secure the safety of the endangered Francisca.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>peractoque circuitu, recta sacellum Rosarianum (suum portum) repetiit: ibi ad pedes Deiparæ Virginis cor suum effudit per undantes lachrymas, seipsam reprehendens, si forte palmam benedictam, aut nimis ambitiose concupierat, aut per inertiam non quæsierat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044300Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044300Z">
        <seg>And having completed the circuit, she returned straight to the Rosary chapel (her haven): there at the feet of the Virgin Mother she poured out her heart through streaming tears, reproaching herself that she had either too ambitiously coveted the blessed palm, or through inertia had not sought it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>perrexitque, ut ante, jactare in sublime rosas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062523Z">
        <seg>And he went on, as before, to toss roses high into the air.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pete, ut more suo iisdem nunc bonis te repleat, quibus solebat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055919Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055919Z">
        <seg>Ask that, in his usual way, he now fill you with the same good things he used to.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>placuitque ipsius dulci bonitati, conferre mihi vires extraordinarias, ac mire vivaces, quibus acie irretorta liberrime &amp; prolixe intuerer magnificentissimum Regem meum in toto decore suo: non enim, uti alias, ex obliquo, aut solo capite ac pectore tenus, sed integrum, ac totum cernebam a vertice ad plantas usque, &amp; ex directo facie ad faciem”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052736Z">
        <seg>and it pleased His sweet goodness to grant me extraordinary and wonderfully vivid powers, by which, with an unturned gaze, I might most freely and at length behold my most magnificent King in all His splendor: for not, as at other times, from the side, or only from the head and chest down, but I saw him whole, and entire from the top of his head to the soles of his feet, &amp; face to face."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>plerumque animo libero &amp; quietissimo perrexisse in Deum, at interdum quoque cum imbecillitate corpusculi, cum somnolentia &amp; distractionibus luctandum fuisse, verum ab illo tempore in posterum expeditius se orare, eo quod inter initia sentiret mirabiliter a Deo introrsum attrahi totam animam cum singulis potentiis, gustuque indicibili intellectum, voluntatem, memoriam, divinæ pulchritudini sic intus affigi, ut nec sponte se phantasmatum intercursu divellere, nec occupationibus sensuum externorum distrahi possit a complexu, &amp; admiratione præsentis in anima dulcissimi Numinis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050253Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050253Z">
        <seg>that he had generally advanced toward God with a free and very quiet mind, but that at times he had also to struggle with the frailty of his little body, with drowsiness, and with distractions; yet from that time forward he prayed more expeditiously, because in the beginning he had felt himself wonderfully drawn inwardly by God, with his whole soul and all its powers, and with an unspeakable taste, he perceived that his intellect, will, and memory were so affixed to the divine beauty within that he could neither of his own accord tear himself away through the interposition of phantasms, nor be distracted from this union by the occupations of the external senses, but remained in the admiration of the most sweetest Deity present in the soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>postea irrogabunt præsenti Sponso meo”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060239Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060239Z">
        <seg>They will later ask my current sponsor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>postremum infallibilis ex alto succursus in quibusvis tum necessitatibus, tum repentinis periculis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061752Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061752Z">
        <seg>ultimately, infallible help from on high in whatever needs or sudden dangers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>primum hoc ordine evenit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040056Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040056Z">
        <seg>First, this happened in this order.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>primum incruenti Sacrificii mystam nominatim expressit: quid multa?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062455Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062455Z">
        <seg>First, he explicitly named the initiate of the bloodless Sacrifice: what more?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prius tamen eorum numerum inire voluit, reperitque acuminata frustula parum abesse a trecentis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042902Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042902Z">
        <seg>However, he first wanted to count them, and found that the sharp fragments were just short of three hundred.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>procurrebant decuriatim, saltitabant, pugnabant, sursum deorsum per muros, armaria, tegulas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052020Z">
        <seg>they ran about in squads, leaped, fought, up and down over the walls, cupboards, and tiles;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prodiit Virgo quam lassitudine anhela, tam vultu hilaris, ac ultra morem lætissima;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052141Z">
        <seg>The Virgin appeared, breathless with fatigue, yet her face was cheerful and she was exceedingly joyful, more so than is customary.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde &amp; hoc inter alia complura condonandum esse ardori suorum affectuum, maxime si verum foret, quod aliunde obscura persuasione acceperat, ex sacris indulgentiarum thesauris aliquod repositum esse lucrum fidelibus, quicumque hoc simplicitatis religioso obsequio sanctæ cruci reverentiam exhiberent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054934Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054934Z">
        <seg>Therefore, this, like many other things, was to be forgiven on account of the fervor of his affections, especially if it were true, as he had received by some obscure persuasion, that from the sacred treasuries of indulgences a certain profit had been reserved for the faithful, whoever should show reverence to the holy cross in this simple religious observance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde cessa scrutari, nec inutili conatu me, teque lassaveris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040834Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040834Z">
        <seg>Therefore, stop scrutinizing, and do not weary yourself and me with a useless effort.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde cum quadam vice obtrusisset Filiæ gestandam calanticam aureo sericoque reticulo interfulgidam, una præceptum addidit, ut obediendi religionem injiceret obluctaturæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035424Z">
        <seg>So when she had once forced upon her daughter a calantike to be worn, glittering with gold and silk netting, she added a precept to instill in her a duty of obedience to counter her resistance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde novo jubilo subsultans, in dulcissimo prodigio ludentem Sponsi manum tacite adorabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044501Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044501Z">
        <seg>Thus, leaping with a new joy, she silently adored the hand of the Bridegroom playing in the sweetest wonder.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proinde si aliquando suspectum sibi fuerat Rosæ epitheton, jam illud in tuto esse, utpote divinitus approbatum;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034804Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034804Z">
        <seg>Therefore, if the epithet Rosa had ever seemed suspect to him, it was now safe, being, as it were, divinely approved.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>promissum monasterium citius in Utopia&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;a&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; aut spatiis imaginariis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062818Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062818Z">
        <seg>the promised monastery, more quickly in Utopia or in imaginary spaces;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>proscenium istud solius fuisse divini Amoris, reciprocos hominum amores invitantis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053917Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053917Z">
        <seg>that this stage was the sole property of divine Love, inviting the reciprocal loves of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>prosiliit ferocissimus hostis gigantea mole, qua contremuit cella, nil tremente Rosa;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052113Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052113Z">
        <seg>The fiercest enemy leaped forth with a gigantic bulk, which made the cell tremble, but Rosa trembled not.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>præcipue ab obitu, quando in mortuali feretro Rosæ facies liberius videnda omnibus patuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051446Z">
        <seg>especially after her death, when in her coffin Rose's face was more freely exposed to all.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>præsertim quod mira perspicuitate conceptus sublimes &amp; altissimos propriis terminis nosset explicare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050045Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050045Z">
        <seg>especially because, with remarkable clarity of conception, he could explain sublime and most lofty ideas in his own terms.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pudebat Crucifixi sponsam, vel per momentum extra crucem ac passionem reperiri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052556Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052556Z">
        <seg>The Spouse of the Crucified was ashamed to be found, even for a moment, apart from the cross and passion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pudebat sub spinoso Capite se membrum videri delicatum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042053Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042053Z">
        <seg>He was ashamed to appear a delicate member under the thorny Head.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>pœna erat quicumque motus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041904Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041904Z">
        <seg>Any movement was a punishment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qua gratanter accepta, subintulit: “Hæc Rosa tu es;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061906Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061906Z">
        <seg>Upon gratefully receiving it, she added: "This rose is you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qua lata, dimisit ancillam, præcipiens, ne cui domesticorum diceret, ubinam Rosa lateret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052038Z">
        <seg>She let the maid go, ordering her not to tell any of the household where Rosa was hiding.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam Limæ constituendum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062821Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062821Z">
        <seg>to be established in Lima.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam chara sint tibi stigmata nostri Redemptoris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055247Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055247Z">
        <seg>How dear to you are the stigmata of our Redeemer;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam denique attenta ac sedula, ut domesticas illorum præverteret offensas, placaret animos, mitigaret afflictiones, singulæ propemodum testantur paginæ in jurato Processu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035737Z">
        <seg>how attentive and diligent she was, in order to forestall their domestic quarrels, soothe their spirits, and mitigate their afflictions, is testified by nearly every page of the sworn deposition.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam longa nimis est patientia tua!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060629Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060629Z">
        <seg>How exceedingly long your patience is!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam officiosa, ut iisdem ex morbo decubuis ministraret, adsisteret, pharmaca, ceteraque necessaria compararet;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035734Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035734Z">
        <seg>how officious she was, ministering to them from their sickbeds, staying by their side, and procuring medicines and other necessities;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quam torrent suaviter, quam repunt tenere per medullas!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044635Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044635Z">
        <seg>How sweetly the torrent flows, how it rushes through the very marrow!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quamquam diligens perquisitio utrobique, tum materna, tum quæstoris, in domo non defuerit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042948Z">
        <seg>Although a diligent search, both by the mother and by the quaestor, has not been lacking in the house.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quando vel maxime humana prudentia (plerumque inidonea spirituum ponderatrix) importune Virginem ad alias vocationum semitas divertere conabatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035929Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035929Z">
        <seg>when human prudence (often an inept judge of spirits) was most insistently trying to divert the Virgin to other paths of calling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quare domestico necessitudinis jure audebat verecundam ultro compellare dicendo: “Hodie, o Rosa, ut video, rursum pluerunt favores”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054342Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054342Z">
        <seg>Therefore, by virtue of their domestic relationship, he dared to address the shy woman directly, saying, "Today, O Rosa, as I see it, the favors have rained down again."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quem eo tandem reduxit, ut horis omnino duabus contentus esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043020Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043020Z">
        <seg>which he eventually reduced to the point that he was content with only two hours altogether.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quem, ut sibi a D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044523Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044523Z">
        <seg>whom, as it were, from God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui eum non diligunt, bonitatem ignorant”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053151Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053151Z">
        <seg>Those who do not love him, do not know goodness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui fervores!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044632Z">
        <seg>What fervor!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>qui vero ex Hispanis &amp; Americanis&lt;/e0&gt; Mestizi &lt;e1&gt;nominantur, ut in auctiore Cangii Glossario ad has voces fusius videre est&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061643Z">
        <seg>who are called Mestizos from the Spaniards and Americans, as can be seen more fully in the later edition of Cangii's Glossary under these words.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia donum consilii mirifice in tanto viro eminere, sentiebant omnes.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050206Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050206Z">
        <seg>For everyone could see that the gift of counsel shone wonderfully in such a man.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia es qui es, &amp; quia te amo super omnia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053102Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053102Z">
        <seg>Because you are who you are, and because I love you above all things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia inter lanam extimumque pannum, ubi facies decumbentis excipienda erat, tortiles juncos clam occuluit, quos veteris sportæ fragmento in hunc usum detraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042750Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042750Z">
        <seg>because between the wool and the outer cloth, where the face of the recliner was to be supported, he secretly hid some rolled rushes, which he had taken from an old basket for this purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia inter ludos.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034938Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034938Z">
        <seg>because between games</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quia prototypo nulla aut devotior aut similior.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055027Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055027Z">
        <seg>For no prototype is more devoted or more similar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quibus quidem notis distinguitur &lt;s6&gt;ἀπὸ&lt;/s6&gt; &lt;s7&gt;τῆς&lt;/s7&gt; &lt;s8&gt;μελικηριδος&lt;/s8&gt;, quæ rotundior, humilior, latior, molliorque est, &amp; facilius, ut digitis cedit, sic postea recurrit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055616Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055616Z">
        <seg>By which characteristics it is distinguished from the *melichridus*, which is rounder, lower, broader, and softer, and more easily, as it yields to the fingers, so it recurves afterward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quid ultra imperem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042837Z">
        <seg>What more can I command?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quidquid de saluberrimi exercitii facilitate jactabat Virgo, meras ipse hyperbolas, femineæque simplicitatis fabulas interpretabatur: nec tamen destitit Rosa docere fratrem, qua methodo ac ratione, inter medias seculi occupationes, animum teneret in oratione defixum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045106Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045106Z">
        <seg>Whatever the Virgin boasted about the ease of the most salutary exercise, he himself interpreted as mere hyperboles and fables of feminine simplicity; yet Rosa did not cease to teach her brother by what method and with what reasoning he should keep his mind fixed in prayer amid the occupations of the world.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; aliis suadebat, clarum sereni firmamenti aspectum inter potiora, quæ spiritum elevant allectamenta, posse numerari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043717Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043717Z">
        <seg>Indeed, he also urged others that the clear sight of the serene firmament could be counted among the chief things that uplift the spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; eo tandem processit ars atque industria, ut qui dormituræ lectulus, idem &amp; equuleus esset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042502Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042502Z">
        <seg>Indeed, art and industry have advanced to such a degree that the little bed for sleeping is also a pillory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin &amp; postea ejusdem Seraphicæ virginis imitatione sibimet capillos ad vivam usque cutem, inscia matre, præscidit, ne quid superesset funium, quibus contra juratum virginitatis propositum aut traheret, aut traheretur ad nuptias: jam enim satis didicerat ex ludicris, luti inquinamenta suo capillitio quandoque etiam serio posse insidiari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035031Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035031Z">
        <seg>She even, later, by imitating that same Seraphic virgin, cut her hair down to the very skin, her mother unaware, so that no tuft would remain by which, contrary to her sworn vow of virginity, she might be either drawn or dragged to marriage: for she had already learned well enough from her playtime that the filth of mud could sometimes seriously threaten her tresses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quin flagitabat superaddi sputa, calces, verbera, nihilque omitti, quod extremi contemptus saperet vilitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040512Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040512Z">
        <seg>He was even demanding that spittle, kicks, and beatings be added, and that nothing be omitted which smacked of the vilest contempt.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quinimo te, o salus, o gaudium animæ meæ, te, inquam, opto amare quantum tu ipse, mi Deus, te amas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053114Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053114Z">
        <seg>Rather, I desire you, O health, O joy of my soul; you, I say, I desire to love as much as you yourself, my God, love yourself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quinimo, hac ipsa nocte, in honorem SS. Trinitatis una hæc virgula” (digito monstrabat surculum nulla fœcunditatis nota tunc usquam turgidum) “tria nobis caryophylla protrudet”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055141Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055141Z">
        <seg>"Rather, this very night, in honor of the Holy Trinity, this very little twig" (he pointed to a shoot, then entirely bare of any sign of fertility) "will produce three carobs for us."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quisnam aut Virginis languorem illi dominæ aut certum adventum emissi mancipii nuntiasset Rosæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051732Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051732Z">
        <seg>Who could have told Rosa of either the lady's fainting spell or the certain arrival of the freed slave?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo &lt;b0&gt;Rosa a S. Maria&lt;/b0&gt; in posterum cognominaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T213212Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203449Z">
        <seg>by which &lt;b0&gt;Rose of St. Mary&lt;/b0&gt; would be nicknamed in the future.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo &lt;b3&gt;Rosa a S. Maria&lt;/b3&gt; in posterum cognominaretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034742Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034742Z">
        <seg>by which &lt;b3&gt;Rose of St. Mary&lt;/b3&gt; would be nicknamed in the future.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo loco templum, quo situ porta, officinæ, porticus, dormitoria forent disponenda;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062737Z">
        <seg>where the temple, and in what location the gate, workshops, portico, and dormitories were to be arranged.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo sensu Persius&lt;/e0&gt; costam montis &lt;e1&gt;appellavit&lt;/e1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060908Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060908Z">
        <seg>In what sense Persius called the ridge of the mountain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quo ut pervenit, unico semper angulo immota consedit, nullo abrepta famosæ curiositatis spectaculo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035948Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035948Z">
        <seg>Wherever she arrived, she always sat motionless in a single corner, never carried away by the spectacle of the famed curiosity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod audisset, malignum [spiritum] aliquando per pupam (fortasse idolum fuerit) garritum edidisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043230Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043230Z">
        <seg>that he had heard an evil spirit at one time utter a cackle through a puppet (perhaps it was an idol).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod contra tot prudentum arbitria plus sola ipsa sapere velit, quam oporteat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062805Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062805Z">
        <seg>that it would want to be wiser than is fitting, against the judgment of so many prudent people, by relying on its own wisdom alone.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod si licentia in experimentum virtutis negabatur, plerumque in aliud triduum fraudabat sitim, non prius bibitura quam mater annueret: imo &amp; questa est, ab ea sibi hanc veniam non crebrius denegari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035728Z">
        <seg>For if permission to test her strength was denied, she would usually stave off her thirst for another three days, not drinking until her mother gave her leave. Indeed, she complains that her mother does not deny her this permission very often.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod tamen ille nec dixerat, nec somniarat&lt;a1&gt;b&lt;/a1&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203338Z">
        <seg>which, however, he had neither said nor dreamed of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod tamen ille nec dixerat, nec somniarat&lt;s5&gt;&lt;a6&gt;b&lt;/a6&gt;&lt;/s5&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041550Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041550Z">
        <seg>which, however, he had neither said nor dreamed of.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod tibi jam satis supra demonstravimus testibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054701Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054701Z">
        <seg>which we have already demonstrated to you sufficiently above with testimonies.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod vero id ibidem aliis Rosa communicare potuerit, singulare fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043940Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043940Z">
        <seg>That she was able to share it with others there, Rosa, was a unique thing.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quod ægerrime paterentur domesticum hunc thesaurum quotidiano suo ac suorum conspectui, devotioni, gaudio subduci: præsertim, quia pluries a Rosa didicerant, gratiosam hanc Salvatoris effigiem toti domui salutarem esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054044Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054044Z">
        <seg>that they would suffer grievously to have this domestic treasure removed from their daily sight, devotion, and joy; especially since they had learned from Rosa on several occasions that this gracious image of the Savior was salutary for the entire household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quodque interrogantibus responsum dabat, simul idioma erat mentis in Deum elevatæ: exempli gratia, si cum aliis feminis hortum intranti, operosius laudabatur viridarii amœnitas, respondebat comiter: “Venustus ac pulcher est hortus, Deus illi flores adaugeat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045006Z">
        <seg>She would answer everyone who asked, and at the same time her speech was a sign of a mind lifted up to God: for example, if, upon entering the garden with other women, the beauty of the green space was more lavishly praised, she would politely reply, "The garden is lovely and beautiful; may God increase its flowers."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæ postea a consciis prodigii interrogata, quidnam putarit hoc aërio rosarum suppendiculo portendi, respondit candore impavido, his præsignificari futurum apud Limenses suos insigne sanctæ Catharinæ Senensis monasterium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062539Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062539Z">
        <seg>When later questioned by those aware of the prodigy as to what she thought this aerial rosary pendant portended, she replied with unflinching candor that it was a foretaste of the future, distinguished monastery of Saint Catherine of Siena among her fellow Limeños.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæ, quod pridie dixerat, simplicissime confirmavit, totam rei gestæ seriem grato animo in suæ seraphicæ magistræ favorem, &amp; gratiam refundens.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055445Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055445Z">
        <seg>She most simply confirmed what she had said the day before, gratefully giving thanks for the entire series of events in favor of her seraphic mistress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæque ut in ceteris, ita celebrius in his duobus nec primam similem visa est, nec habere sequentem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044033Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044033Z">
        <seg>And each, as in the others, so more famously in these two, seemed neither the first of its kind nor to have a successor.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quærebat per ambulacra infirmas præ ceteris horridas, magisque porriginosas, his mira alacritate componebat lectulum, eluebat vasa, parabat edulia, neque tam vile, tetricum, abjectum obsequium commodari infirmis poterat, quod non ipsa ultro prensaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061302Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061302Z">
        <seg>She would seek out the sick along the corridors—those most dreadful and protracted of all—and with remarkable alacrity she would make their beds, wash their vessels, and prepare their food. Nor could she render them any mean, grim, or base service that she did not herself willingly undertake.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quærebat, pulsabat, gemebat spiritus contribulatus, nec audiebatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045630Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045630Z">
        <seg>he cried out, he knocked, he groaned with a broken spirit, and was not heard;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæsitum ubique &amp; diu, geminatur cum quæstione anxietas, turbantur omnia, inventum nihil.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035644Z">
        <seg>Seeking everywhere and for a long time, anxiety doubles with the search, everything is disturbed, nothing is found.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>quæst.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050836Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050836Z">
        <seg>question</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>recedite a viis pessimis, quibus pecudum instar ducimini ad macellum stygii lanionis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060527Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060527Z">
        <seg>Depart from the worst of paths, by which you are led like cattle to the slaughterhouse of a Stygian butcher;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>repente quæ plumbum fuerat, se plumam sensit, nemine adminiculante surrexit, domum repetiit, matri ingenue rem, ut erat, detexit, proque monastica aliud clausuræ genus (quod cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040140Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040140Z">
        <seg>Suddenly she who had been lead felt herself a feather; unassisted she rose, returned home, ingenuously revealed the matter to her mother just as it was, and in exchange for the monastic life chose another form of enclosure (which chapter…</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>resiccata valde tenuia, impense acria, calidaque.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043153Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043153Z">
        <seg>Very finely dried, intensely pungent, and hot.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>respondebat ad interrogata seriatim, apposite, ordinate: conversabatur expedite, disponebat agenda, agebat disposita, facilitate, attentione, promptitudine, qua solent, qui unice negotiis exterioribus intendunt.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044839Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044839Z">
        <seg>He answered questions in sequence, appropriately and in order; he conducted himself expeditiously, arranged his agenda, and carried out his plans with facility, attentiveness, and promptness—qualities typical of those who devote themselves exclusively to external affairs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>respondit illa, se brevi illo, quo coram imagine orarat, spatio sensisse reducem in articulos vigorem, illosque detumuisse statim violento spiraculo, ut solet aëre plena vesica, dum premitur, sicque una cum tumore omnem pariter evanuisse dolorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055439Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055439Z">
        <seg>She replied that in the short time she had been praying before the image, she felt the strength return to her joints, and that they immediately swelled up again with a violent puff, as a bladder full of air does when it is pressed, and that in this way all the pain vanished along with the swelling.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>restituor quasi numquam excidissem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050510Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050510Z">
        <seg>I am restored as if I had never fallen away.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rogasti me, &amp; ecce ad solitas preces te voco: surge (inquam) Filiola mea, surge, jam hora sonuit”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051400Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051400Z">
        <seg>You asked me, and behold I call you to the usual prayers: arise, my little daughter, arise, for the hour has already sounded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rosis porro liliisque significari puras ac Deo dilectas Limensium virginum animas, quæ quidem hinc inde per urbem sine ordine sparsim habitabant, &amp; suo tempore in cælum claustrale fore congregandas, ubi tutissimæ sint ab omni secularis calcanei protritu, solique æterno Numini fragrent odorem suavitatis, donec inde translatæ in Sponsi paradisum, eidem nectantur in immarcescibilem gloriæ coronam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062601Z">
        <seg>that the roses and lilies signify the pure and God-beloved souls of the virgins of Lima, who indeed dwelt scattered here and there throughout the city without order, and were to be gathered in due time into the cloistered heaven, where they will be safest from all the wear and tear of the secular world, and will breathe for the eternal Deity alone the fragrance of sweetness, until, translated from there into the Paradise of the Spouse, they are bound to Him in an unfading crown of glory.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>rursum guttulis guttæ, rivis rivuli successere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053846Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053846Z">
        <seg>Again, drops succeeded drops, and rivulets succeeded rivulets.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sanguinem, qui verbera sequeretur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041504Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041504Z">
        <seg>blood, which would follow the beatings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sat erat in his, aut plane elinguem fieri, aut compendio protestari, nil esse jucunditatis, lætitiæ, jubili, in istoc mundo inferiori, quod vel umbratenus assimiletur gaudio istius pretiosi Convivii, ubi infinita animæ fames incidit in pastum Deo dignum, in cibum filiorum, in mamillam Verbi incarnati, quod solum esurientes replet bonis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055907Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055907Z">
        <seg>it was enough in these things, or to be made completely mute, or to protest in short that there is no sweetness, joy, or jubilation in this lower world that can even remotely be likened to the joy of that precious Banquet, where the infinite hunger of the soul falls upon a meal worthy of God, upon the food of the sons, upon the breast of the Incarnate Word, which alone fills the hungry with good things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sciant, absque pondere afflictionum ad gratiæ cumulos non perveniri: agnoscant, juxta ærumnarum incrementa augeri pariter charismatum mensuram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052820Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052820Z">
        <seg>Let them know that without the weight of afflictions one does not attain the fullness of grace; let them acknowledge that, as the hardships increase, the measure of charisms increases accordingly.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>scio quid ille mihi, tametsi indignæ, pollicitus sit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062024Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062024Z">
        <seg>I know what he promised me, even though it was unworthy.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>se quidem refloridis oblati Carmeli desertis mirifice &amp; capi &amp; affici, verumtamen solius divinæ, non humanæ inspirationis proprium id opus ac munus esse: Spiritum ubi vult spirare, aptam regularis status tum electionem, tum commutationem non esse volentis, neque currentis, sed miserentis Dei;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040317Z">
        <seg>that indeed the Carmelite deserts, offered to the reflowering, are wonderfully both to be taken and to be affected, yet that this work and office is proper to divine, not human, inspiration: that the Spirit blows where He wills, and that the apt choice and change of the regular state is not of the will of the one who wills, nor of the one who runs, but of the merciful God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>se vero de suopte nomine hoc insuper adjectum ei velle auctarium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034739Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034739Z">
        <seg>but he himself, of his own accord, wanted to add this further to his reward.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>secreti hujus beneficium, quia concessit Dominus, non auferat minister”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040837Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040837Z">
        <seg>"Let the minister not take away the benefit of this secret, because the Lord has granted it."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed &amp; illa insolubiliter videbatur adglutinata: conabatur semel iterumve fortunatus homo se excutere de sedili, disjungere tibias, aut brachia saltem attrahere, nec quidquam horum poterat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044610Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044610Z">
        <seg>but she too seemed fastened in an insoluble way: the lucky man tried again and again to wrench himself from the seat, to pull his thighs apart, or at least to draw in his arms, but he could do none of it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Parvuli incessus gravior erat, quam puerorum esse soleat, majestas inerat, &amp; quacumque fixisset pedem, subitaneo lumine resplendebat pavimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051201Z">
        <seg>But the Parvuli's gait was more stately than is usual for children; there was a majesty to it, and wherever he placed his foot, the floor would suddenly gleam with light.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Rosam nec terreri, nec timidiuscule silere permittebat ipsa supernæ revelationis firmissima certitudo, adeo ut, quod verbis idoneis adstruere satis non poterat, expressis in assula notis architectonicis conaretur evidentius adumbrare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062725Z">
        <seg>But the very firmest certainty of the heavenly revelation did not permit Rosa to be either terrified or to fall silent in a timid manner, so much so that, since she could not adequately prove it with words, she attempted to more clearly outline it with the architectural marks inscribed on the tablet.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed Virgini pro affectus magnitudine videbantur pauca: fascem cupierat, non fasciculum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044935Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044935Z">
        <seg>But to a virgin, the magnitude of the affection seemed small: she had desired a bundle, not a sheaf.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed addito jussu, ne injussa illas a se revelleret, aut quoquomodo submoveret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035508Z">
        <seg>but with the added order that he should not, without permission, remove them from himself or in any way move them.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed aliter assueta præscriptum horæ punctum seu dormiendo seu evigilando attingere non potuit, idque sat fuit, ut vehementi scrupulo torqueretur, velut rea violatæ obedientiæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035715Z">
        <seg>But being accustomed to a different routine, she could not meet the appointed hour, whether by sleeping or by staying awake, and that alone was enough to torment her with a severe pang of conscience, as if she were guilty of disobedience.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed audita non fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045748Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045748Z">
        <seg>but it was not heard.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed blandum, candidum, ac pene in angelum lucis transfiguratum: nam species illi humana erat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052156Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052156Z">
        <seg>but gentle, fair, and almost transfigured into an angel of light: for his appearance was human.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed cavebant singuli, ne Rosæ insiderent, ac velut ex condicto suæ parcebant hospitæ.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043801Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043801Z">
        <seg>But each one was careful not to step on the roses, and as if by arrangement, they spared their hostess.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed consopitus erat omnis affectuum calor, obtusa omnis ratio, omnem undique pietatis sensum profundissimus obsederat lethargus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045546Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045546Z">
        <seg>But all warmth of feeling was extinguished, all reason dulled; a profound lethargy had seized every sense of piety.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed continuo revolvebatur in eamdem parœmiam sobria liquescentis animulæ ebrietas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044359Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044359Z">
        <seg>but the sobriety of the melting little soul was continually turned into the same proverb: drunkenness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed culicum insolentiam non ferens, unum, qui sanguine jam turgebat, manu occidit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043854Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043854Z">
        <seg>But, unable to bear the mosquito's insolence, he killed one that was already swollen with blood with his hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed de hoc infra.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042626Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042626Z">
        <seg>but more on this below.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed eo vespere nec spes residua fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055135Z">
        <seg>But that evening, there was no hope left.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed euntem occupavit cælitus immissa cogitatio, quæ deinceps omnem exarmavit formidinem, docuit fiduciam, infudit securitatem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062052Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062052Z">
        <seg>but a thought sent down from heaven seized him as he went, which thenceforth disarmed all fear, taught him confidence, and instilled security.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed explicare manum, implexosque exerere digitos nequaquam potuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044650Z">
        <seg>But he could by no means straighten his hand and stretch out his fingers.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed formosa, decens, urbana.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052159Z">
        <seg>but beautiful, proper, and urbane.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed generosa Discipula pluris faciebat magistræ dolorosa stigmata, quam delicata solatia;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052553Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052553Z">
        <seg>but the noble Disciple valued the painful stigmata of her teacher more than delicate consolations.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed huic graviter respondit Rosa: “Nolim haberi tam delicata, ut pro Sponsi mei ornatu unicæ noctis labor aut nimius mihi sit aut molestus: quæ enim tam socors aut otiosa fuerit nupta, cui durum sit noctem impendere ornamento &amp; cultui, quo maritus eget, ut postridie decenter in publicum prodeat”?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060130Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060130Z">
        <seg>But Rosa answered him sharply: "I do not wish to be so delicate that the labor of my groom's adornment for the one night should be too great or troublesome for me. For what bride could be so idle or unconcerned that she would find it hard to spend the night on the adornment and grooming her husband requires, so that the next day she might appear in public decently?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed incassum, quia contumax vitium, triginta &amp; trium annorum possessione ac mora, nimium invaluerat, nec videbatur aliud pereunti superesse remedium, nisi quod Rosa post tot aliorum hortamenta, consilia, invectivas, post tot Superiorum correptiones, minas, clamores, suam exereret ardentioris facundiæ mirabilem suadam, hominique loqueretur ad cor, &amp; ejus saltem animæ salutem omnipotenti Numinis dexteræ commendaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060753Z">
        <seg>but in vain, for the stubborn vice, by thirty-three years of possession and delay, had grown too strong, and no other remedy seemed to remain for the perishing man, except that Rosa, after so many exhortations, counsels, and invectives from others, after so many rebukes, threats, and clamors from her superiors, would exercise her marvelous, more ardent eloquence, speak to the man's heart, and commend at least the salvation of his soul to the hand of the Almighty.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed incassum: vix enim dies perpauci effluxerant, cum crucifer cespes apud proreginam funditus exaruit, manifesto indicio, propriam sibi defuisse crucis &amp; Calvariæ hortulanam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054952Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054952Z">
        <seg>but in vain: for scarcely a few days had passed when the crusader's tuft of grass at the queen's prow withered up completely, a clear sign that its own gardener of the Cross and Calvary had been lacking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed insuper iidem in decantandis Deo laudibus strenue pro modulo suo me juvant”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043822Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043822Z">
        <seg>but moreover, the same people energetically help me, to the best of their ability, in praising God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed interim cavebat, &amp; (quoad potuit) obstabat, ne in talium epularum quantulamcumque communionem aut sympathiam admitteretur miserum corpus, quod soli passioni &amp; cruciatui destinarat: &amp;, si eam Rex introducebat in cellaria sua, ipsa suum corpus jubebat foris stare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041947Z">
        <seg>But in the meantime she was on her guard and, as far as she could, kept herself from being admitted to any partaking or sympathy in such feasting—her wretched body, which she had devoted solely to suffering and torment. And if the King brought her into his cellars, she herself ordered her body to remain outside.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ipsam confessarii jam spiritu grandem repererant, quando tam tenellæ suæ ætati permiserunt accubitum in sacro Convivio, quod præbet delicias regibus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055644Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055644Z">
        <seg>But the confessors themselves had already found her great in spirit, when they permitted so tender an age to recline at the sacred Banquet, which provides delights for kings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed latius extenditur, nempe usque ad terras Australes, novam Guineam &amp; archipelagum sancti Lazari versus mare Indicum, sicut Baudrandus affirmat.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203207Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035108Z">
        <seg>but extends more broadly, namely to the Southern lands, New Guinea &amp; the Archipelago of St. Lazarus toward the Indian Ocean, as Baudrandus affirms.&lt;/e0&gt;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mater sonticum&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;c&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; morbum suspicata (præsertim quod eadem nonnullorum confessariorum erat seu crisis, seu imperita credulitas) medicum advocat, ejusque curæ inutili suam, tot undique tribulis vexatam, Rosam commendat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045741Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045741Z">
        <seg>But the mother, suspecting a contagious disease (especially since it was the same malady that had afflicted some of the confessors, whether a crisis or uninformed credulity), calls for a doctor and entrusts her own daughter, Rosa—who was being beset on all sides by troubles—to his useless care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed medicus vi crescentis morbi perterritus mandarat pridie, ne infans ab ægra lac sugeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055222Z">
        <seg>But the physician, terrified by the increasing severity of the disease, had ordered the day before that the infant should not suckle from the sick woman.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed mox fatebatur, se actum agere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045803Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045803Z">
        <seg>But he soon admitted that he was putting on an act.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed nec illa, nec istæ apparebant sub lineamento, quo solita analogia repræsentarent Creatorem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045531Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045531Z">
        <seg>But neither those nor these appeared under the outline by which the usual analogy represented the Creator.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed neque illis displicere merito queat meæ devotionis simplicitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054921Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054921Z">
        <seg>But neither can they justly take offense at the simplicity of my devotion.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed noluit Deus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041702Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041702Z">
        <seg>but God did not will it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed non tardavit festinus ad adjuvandum Dominus, quin cor illud contritum &amp; humiliatum opportune recrearet, blando affatu sic eam alloquens: “Filia, ego neminem condemno, nisi volentem condemnari;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061810Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061810Z">
        <seg>But the Lord did not delay to hasten to help; rather, He opportunely comforted that contrite and humbled heart, speaking to her with gentle words: "Daughter, I condemn no one, unless one is willing to be condemned;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed numquam in talem incidit interpretem, qui melius ipsa intimam favi hujus dulcedinem aut experimento nosset, aut aptis indiciis explicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061736Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061736Z">
        <seg>but he never came across an interpreter who could better know the innermost sweetness of this honey by experience, or explain it with fitting indications.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ob reverentiam matris illic præsentis ad singula non descendit, contenta dixisse generatim, se a domesticis, ob vitæ singularitatem, varias molestias, insultus, vexationes tolerasse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050355Z">
        <seg>But out of respect for her mother, who was present, she did not go into specifics, content to say in general that she had endured various annoyances, insults, and vexations from her household staff because of the singularity of her life.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed occurrit eunti visibilis Christus, blandeque affatus Virginem;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051223Z">
        <seg>but Christ appeared to the one who was going, and gently spoke to the Virgin.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed pampini, quia amarissimi: his decoctis vescebatur libentius, quod &amp; succi amaritudine, &amp; floris sui cognatione, aptius Parasceven, quam prandium repræsentarent.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041259Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041259Z">
        <seg>but the shoots, because they were most bitter: boiled, they were eaten more willingly, since both the bitterness of their juice and their kinship to their flower made them a more fitting representation of the Passion than of a mere meal.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed passim avulsione opus fuit, quam cutis &amp; cruor sequebantur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041743Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041743Z">
        <seg>but everywhere avulsion was necessary, which was followed by skin and blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed pene in singulis hærebat verbis, causata vocabulorum inopiam, nec aliter de iis fari noverat, quam quod ineffabiles dictitaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055855Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055855Z">
        <seg>But he was at a loss for words in almost every sentence, caused by a scarcity of words, and he knew no other way to speak of them than to call them ineffable.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed perdifficilis ingestio os parvulum diductione cruciabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052435Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052435Z">
        <seg>But the extremely difficult feeding tormented the little one's mouth with dislocation.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed properandum ad solidiora.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053257Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053257Z">
        <seg>But we must hurry to more solid matters.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed reipsa urebant, torrebant, assabant utramque manum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035350Z">
        <seg>But in reality, they burned, scorched, and roasted both hands.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed sera nox erat, qua nec medicum advocare, nec domesticorum ullum ad opem importune lubebat accersere: agnoscebat electuario vel sorbitiuncula se indigere, qua utcumque stomacho succurreret, ac vires instauraret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051059Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051059Z">
        <seg>But it was late at night, when he could neither call for a doctor nor was he inclined to importunately summon any of his household for help: he knew he needed an electuary or a little drink, by which he could somehow soothe his stomach and restore his strength.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed simul difficillima taliter amanti, cui felicius videtur non esse, quam non amare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050730Z">
        <seg>but at the same time it is exceedingly difficult for one who loves so deeply, for whom it seems happier not to exist than not to love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed tamen sexum inermem, qui cum Rosa in templo substiterat, sic perculit, ut propemodum exanimaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060205Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060205Z">
        <seg>But nevertheless, he struck the unarmed sex, who had remained in the temple with Rosa, so that he nearly killed her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed tunc festinus &amp; amans venerat, tamquam Virginem illic sponsam sibi quæsiturus.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044145Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044145Z">
        <seg>but then he had come, hastening and in love, as though to seek the Virgin there as his bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed ut vidit evadere se non posse, ingenue candideque fassa est (quantum per congruorum vocabulorum penuriam potuit) magnalia Dei, quæ sibi post tristissimam ærumnam obtingebant, ut in momento se videbat repositam in unione, qua exciderat, aut se excidisse putarat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045856Z">
        <seg>But when she saw that she could not escape, she ingenuously and candidly confessed (as best she could, for lack of fitting words) the great works of God that had befallen her after her most wretched suffering, seeing herself in an instant restored to the union from which she had fallen, or had thought she had fallen.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sed vivis, &amp; altissimis experimentis exacte didicerat, suisque principiis, regulis, &amp; capitibus methodice digestam, egregie callebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050055Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050055Z">
        <seg>but he was alive, and had learned it exactly through the most profound experiments, and was an expert in it, having digested it methodically according to his own principles, rules, and headings.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sentiebat afflicta totum a catena malum esse, velut quæ per extritam pellem hinc inde carni nervisque profunda incumbebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041711Z">
        <seg>She felt that the entire chain was afflicting her, like one that, through worn leather, pressed deeply here and there into the flesh and nerves.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sentio avidos liberrimi amoris impetus, ut cum rapidi amnes disruptis obicibus, non fluendo, sed tuendo, sese evolvunt per cataractam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050513Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050513Z">
        <seg>I feel the ravenous impulses of the freest love, as when swift rivers, their banks broken, not by flowing but by holding back, rush through the cataract.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>serpebant dulciter per omnes animæ suæ venas ignotæ flammulæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062942Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062942Z">
        <seg>Unknown little flames were sweetly creeping through all the veins of her soul.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sese nihilominus esse paratissimam suere ac dissuere, quæcumque juberet mater.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035548Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035548Z">
        <seg>She nevertheless declared herself most ready to sew or to undo whatever her mother commanded.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si qua transeuntem observasset, gestiebat animo illic os oculosque affricare, ubi Rosa vestigium fixerat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050715Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050715Z">
        <seg>If she had observed a passerby, she would have been eager to press her mouth and eyes there, where Rosa had left her trace.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si quoquo modo sibi licuisset prædicatoris fungi officio, utique se opertam horrido cilicio, nudipedem, squallidam interdiu noctuque per omnes Limensium vicos, &amp; compita circumlaturam crucifixi Redemptoris imaginem, &amp; ubique per fora, per trivia, per angiportus identidem clamore lugubri vociferaturam: “Resipiscite, o resipiscite, peccatores;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060524Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060524Z">
        <seg>if it had in any way been permitted for her to fulfill the office of a preacher, she would surely have wrapped herself in a horrid hair-shirt, barefoot, filthy, by day and by night through all the streets of Limensium, carrying the image of the crucified Redeemer on a crossroads, and everywhere in the marketplaces, at the crossroads, and in the alleys she would repeatedly cry out with a lugubrious cry: "Repent, O repent, sinners;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>si sternutatione exonerabatur miserum caput, aut alia fortius excreandi importuna necessitas vultum orisque habitum violentius commovebat?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042136Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042136Z">
        <seg>Was his wretched head relieved by a sneeze, or was some other, more urgent need to cough violently distorting his face and the expression of his mouth?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sibi denique alios superne impulsus non obtigisse hactenus, præter eos, quos habitu protestabatur;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040320Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040320Z">
        <seg>that he had not, in fact, received any other divine impulses so far, apart from those he professed by his conduct;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sibique videbatur plumbeo magis, quam cannabaceo stromate subuculata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041910Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041910Z">
        <seg>And she seemed to herself to be swathed in a leaden rather than a hemp mattress.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sic Rosam vel a prima infantia docuit solitarios angulos domi quærere, mundique commercia devitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043218Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043218Z">
        <seg>Thus, from early infancy, she taught Rosa to seek solitary corners at home and to avoid worldly commerce.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sic tamen ut neutri, ad quid ea hora vocarentur, &lt;s2&gt;[severe examinarunt.]&lt;/s2&gt; indicaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053824Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053824Z">
        <seg>Yet so that neither of them would indicate what they were called for at that hour.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque &amp; iste fluctus, ne fluctuaret obedientia, sponte detumuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035437Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035437Z">
        <seg>And so this wave, so that obedience would not waver, voluntarily subsided.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque Virgo, cum sua saturrima inedia usque in vesperam, subinde in sequentem usque diem perseverabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055957Z">
        <seg>Thus the Virgin, with her most complete fast, persevered until evening, and often until the following day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque corallinum illud sponsionis vinculum ab exsoluta Matre in se transtulerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043602Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043602Z">
        <seg>Thus that coral pledge-bond had been transferred to it from the discharged Mother.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque fidens divinæ providentiæ, reperit, quod in singulos menses clam Joannæ adferret in exactam solutionem hospitii, pacta dumtaxat silentii fidem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061221Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061221Z">
        <seg>Thus, trusting in divine providence, he discovered that each month he had been secretly bringing to Joanna the exact payment for her lodging, having only agreed to keep faith with her silence.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque munitus demum salutaribus Sacramentis, conceptaque ingenti salutis fiducia, tranquillum, placidum, serenum spiritum reddidit Creatori, pactus ante cum Petro, quod tam ipsum, quam Rosam a morte inviseret, si eam sibi facultatem Dominus indulgeret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060849Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060849Z">
        <seg>Thus fortified at last with the salutary Sacraments, and filled with great confidence of salvation, he returned his spirit to the Creator, tranquil, placid, and serene, having previously made a pact with Peter that he would visit both himself and Rosa in death, if the Lord would grant him that opportunity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque videbatur purgatorii magis, quam inferni, ærumnam sustinere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045620Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045620Z">
        <seg>Thus it seemed to endure the torments of purgatory rather than of hell.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sicque, vocato somno, exanguia membra deinceps composuit ad soporem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042914Z">
        <seg>And so, sleep calling, he laid his pale limbs down to rest.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>simulque ad aliquam hujus sacram imaginem (quæcumque illa esset toti regioni celebrior, ac de fama beneficiorum illustrior) publici refugii perennem tesseram communi voto collectam firmissime stabiliri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054252Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054252Z">
        <seg>and at the same time to establish most firmly a perpetual public refuge, collected by a common vow from some sacred image of it (whichever was most celebrated throughout the region and most renowned for the fame of its benefits).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>singularem, obstipam, ac sui ipsius carnificem proclamitans.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041154Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041154Z">
        <seg>proclaiming a singular, speechless, and self-wounding one.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem instabat propinquus dies, quo illi habendus erat solemnis ad populum sermo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053449Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053449Z">
        <seg>For the day was at hand on which he was to deliver his formal address to the people.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem intractabile viderant brachium, &amp; in omnem medelam contumax, ut non vererentur tamquam de incurabili ambigue mussitare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054032Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054032Z">
        <seg>For they had seen the arm as intractable and stubborn against all treatment, so that they did not hesitate to murmur ambiguously about an incurable case.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem ipsamet adjuvit Rosam instrumenta suæ mortificationis humiliter occultantem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040856Z">
        <seg>For she herself helped Rosa to humbly conceal the instruments of her mortification.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>siquidem, Augustino teste, quod corpori est anima, hoc amanti animæ Deus, illique minor est pœna ab animando, quam ab amando desistere aut repelli.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045645Z">
        <seg>For, as Augustine testifies, as the soul is to the body, so is God to the soul that loves Him; and for that soul, it is a lesser punishment to cease to live than to cease to love or be repelled from love.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive ad Isabellæ nomen respondisset vocantibus, irata mater expediebat virgidemiam, cædebat immeritam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052454Z">
        <seg>Whether she answered to the name of Isabella when called, the angry mother would unleash her virginity, slaughtering the undeserving.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive ad lentam curam migraret tantisper in civitatem, deerant peregrinæ &amp; inopi sumptus, quibus semestre hospitium conduceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061159Z">
        <seg>Whether he moved to the city for slow treatment, the funds were lacking for a six-month stay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive erecta stabat, capiti vertigo ponderosissima obtinniebat languidam somnolentiæ cantilenam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043038Z">
        <seg>Whether standing erect, a most ponderous dizziness held sway over her head, a languid lullaby of drowsiness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sive pansis brachiis procubuisset in terram, adgliscebat soporiferi corporis suave blandimentum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043041Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043041Z">
        <seg>Whether he had fallen to the ground with arms outstretched, he clung to the sweet caress of the soporific body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sola Rosa sentiebat (sed obscurius adhuc) clamantem eminus Sponsum: Præbe mibi cor tuum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044126Z">
        <seg>Only Rosa felt (but even more obscurely) the Spouse crying out from afar: Give me your heart.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sonoreque ac libere in prætereuntem jocati, “En (inquiunt) monialem opipare habitam!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040757Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040757Z">
        <seg>and loudly and freely, joking to a passerby, "Behold (they say) a nun richly dressed!"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>statim Virgo præ ingentis fiduciæ alacritate se ipsa robustior, animose respondit: “Ego vero Sponsum habeo, qui prægrandia &amp; rara dare potest, neque pusilla aut mediocria dumtaxat per diffidentiam ab illo speranda sunt”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061841Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061841Z">
        <seg>Immediately the Virgin, animated by great confidence, responded boldly: "I, however, have a Spouse who can give things that are exceedingly great and rare, and not merely small or mediocre things should be expected from him through lack of faith."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>statimque suspicata id quod erat, Filiam interrogavit, cur eo loci id abscondisset, aut cui bono?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041222Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041222Z">
        <seg>Immediately suspecting what it was, she asked her daughter why she had hidden it there, or to what purpose.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>stude cautius ambulare in semitis mandatorum Dei, ne pereas: en, quidquid coneris tegere, non latuit Sponsum meum intentio tua”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060700Z">
        <seg>Strive to walk more carefully in the paths of God's commandments, lest you perish: behold, whatever you try to cover up, your intention has not escaped my Spouse."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sub hoc Rosa effertur Gundisalvi domo ad tumulum, ut quæ viva seraphicam magistram ad vivum expresserat gestamine senticoso, eamdem &amp; mortua repræsentaret evidentius sub communi utrique corona.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042421Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042421Z">
        <seg>Under this rose, the body of Gundisalvi is carried from the house to the tomb, so that she who, while alive, had vividly portrayed the seraphic teacher in her thorny bearing might, even in death, represent her more clearly under the common crown.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>subitisque &amp; præcocibus incrementis, excelsa contemplationis peritia eo maturuit ac pertigit, ut circa duodecimum ætatis annum deprehensa fuerit jam conscendisse illum orationis gradum, quem unitivum mystica theologia appellat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044808Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044808Z">
        <seg>Through sudden and precocious growths, her lofty expertise in contemplation matured and reached such a point that, by about the twelfth year of her age, it was perceived to have already ascended to that degree of prayer which mystical theology calls the unitive.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>subjungebat tamen: “Vide, num amari dignus fit ille admirabilis mundi artifex;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045208Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045208Z">
        <seg>He nevertheless added: "See whether that admirable artisan of the world becomes worthy of being loved."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sublimi apud Deum gratia polleat necesse est: utinam animæ suæ tam lucidæ ac flagrantis intimum statum nosse liceat”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055826Z">
        <seg>He must be endowed with sublime grace before God; may he be permitted to know the innermost state of his soul, so bright and burning.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>succedant alia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054323Z">
        <seg>Let others succeed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sufficere tot eventuum tragica specimina in testimonium promptæ ultra vires obedientiæ;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041126Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041126Z">
        <seg>to suffice as so many tragic examples of events in testimony to ready obedience beyond one's powers;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sufficiat hoc postremum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040710Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040710Z">
        <seg>Let this last one suffice.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>suo idiomate GASPACHOS&lt;a0&gt;e&lt;/a0&gt; appellitabat hoc genus embammatis, quia frigidum sumebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T203330Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203330Z">
        <seg>In its own dialect, it was called GASPACHOS, because it was eaten cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>suo idiomate GASPACHOS&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;e&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; appellitabat hoc genus embammatis, quia frigidum sumebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041237Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041237Z">
        <seg>In its own dialect, it was called GASPACHOS, because it was eaten cold.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>supernos attactus præpotentes, dulces, delicatos;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044532Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044532Z">
        <seg>The most powerful, sweet, and delicate touches above;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>surge: nam instat hora”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051338Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051338Z">
        <seg>Arise: for the hour is at hand.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpe cum gemitu dicebat familiari D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060310Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060310Z">
        <seg>He often said with a groan to his familiar, "Sir."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpissime permolesta ischias utrique coxendici graviter incumbebat, non segnius interim costas vellicante pleuritide&lt;s9&gt;&lt;a10&gt;f&lt;/a10&gt;&lt;/s9&gt;.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052612Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052612Z">
        <seg>Very often, a severely troublesome sciatica pressed heavily on both hips, not less so, meanwhile, was the pleurisy tearing at the ribs.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>sæpius &amp; Virginem Matrem, decoram, amabilem, dulciterque familiarem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050538Z">
        <seg>often also the Virgin Mother, beautiful, lovely, and sweetly familiar.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tandem ausu furibundo, quam discerpere non poterat, raptabat per solum, trahebat, versabat, ceu lacerum aliquod peripsema;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051936Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051936Z">
        <seg>At last, with a furious boldness, it snatched what it could not tear apart, dragging it across the ground, pulling it, turning it, as if it were some tattered rag.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tandem innuebat ancillæ, sine strepitu pondus auferret, ne forte superveniens mater furtivæ devotionis turbaret scenam, aditumque similia iterandi præcluderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041631Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041631Z">
        <seg>She finally motioned to the maidservant to remove the weight without a sound, lest the mother, arriving unexpectedly, disturb the secret devotion and block any chance to repeat it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tanta erat verborum proportio, gravitas, ac mensura, sine parergo, sine circuitu, sine confusione ac superfluitate.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050643Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050643Z">
        <seg>There was such a proportion, gravity, and measure to the words, without digression, without circumlocution, without confusion and superfluity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tantum interrogavit, unde id nosset: Respondit ille, se Lima Potosium usque, vallante angelica custodia, feliciter aut calcasse, aut declinasse obvia quæque pericula;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051856Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051856Z">
        <seg>He only asked how he knew this. He replied that he had, with the protection of an angelic guard, either successfully braved or successfully avoided every danger all the way from Lima to Potosí.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tantumque abfuit, quod ulla hos terrores mitigarit assuetudo, ut pene adauxerit: nam ubi postridie Virgo relabebatur in idem chaos, denuo sub eadem luridæ æternitatis larva recurrebant horridi pavores, nec mœrenti suggerebat ligata memoria, quod inde pridie emersisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045611Z">
        <seg>So far was it from any habit softening these terrors that it almost increased them: for when the next day the Virgin relapsed into the same chaos, the hideous terrors would again rush in under the same lurid mask of eternity, and the bound memory offered the dying woman no comfort that she had emerged from it the day before.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>taxare Virginem tamquam aut chimeris delusam, aut affectui pertinaciter indulgentem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062753Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062753Z">
        <seg>To judge the Virgin as either a fool for chimeras or one who stubbornly indulges her affections.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>te quidem videre ante meum obitum desideravi, idque solum dixi, nil ultra”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053350Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053350Z">
        <seg>I did indeed desire to see you before my death, and I said only that, nothing more.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tempestive aderunt vires, ut possit, tametsi non sine conditione alteri onerosa”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053508Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053508Z">
        <seg>Help will arrive in time, so that he can, though not without a condition burdensome to another.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tibi (inquam) tibi, amabilissime Jesu, qui tanto humani generis amore bullis &amp; æstuas”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053728Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053728Z">
        <seg>to you (I say) to you, most loving Jesus, who with so much love for the human race boils and burns.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tibi omnium affectuum sudent balsama;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053725Z">
        <seg>All affections sweat balsam for you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tibi sese enervet omne pectus piæ violentia charitatis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053722Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053722Z">
        <seg>May every breast be enervated in you by the pious violence of charity.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>totum hoc, quidquid fuerat miraculi, eo dumtaxat spectare ac tendere, ut Salvator, quam enixe cupiat amari ab omnibus, ipsomet sudorifero vultu, æstuque præter ordinem luculento manifestaret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053914Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053914Z">
        <seg>all of this, whatever miracle it was, served only to show that the Savior, who so earnestly desires to be loved by all, was revealed by his very own sweat-stained face and unusually brilliant radiance;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>triumphabat, sed in cruce: nimirum prostabat in Virginis cubiculo par humanæ staturæ crux lignea, e cujus brachiis utrimque prominebant clavi fortes ac solidi, ut sufficerent corpori sustinendo.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043048Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043048Z">
        <seg>He triumphed, but on the cross: for in the Virgin's chamber lay a wooden cross of human stature, from whose arms on either side protruded strong, solid nails, sufficient to support a body.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu Domina, tu palma exaltata in Cades, me ramo ditabis immarcescibili”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044309Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044309Z">
        <seg>O Lady, you exalted palm in Cadiz, you will bless me with an unfading branch.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu mihi sponsa esto”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044343Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044343Z">
        <seg>Be my bride.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu vero ea præfidentia loqueris, acsi in tantæ fabricæ sumptuosa impendia tibi in promptu jam essent bis centies mille libræ argenti”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T062328Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T062328Z">
        <seg>But you speak with such confidence, as if you already had two hundred thousand pounds of silver on hand for the lavish expenses of such a grand undertaking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tu vero, nobilis Vincenti, ignosce, &amp; locum da veritati, quam tibi dictura sum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060632Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060632Z">
        <seg>But you, noble Vincent, forgive me, and give a hearing to the truth I am about to tell you.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tua res agitur: si quid dissimulaveris, si quid celaveris in examine, Dei beneficium negasti;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050458Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050458Z">
        <seg>It is your own affair: if you have concealed anything, if you have hidden anything in the examination, you have denied God's benefit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tua sum, tuam me profiteor, tua ero”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044353Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044353Z">
        <seg>I am yours, I profess myself yours, I will be yours.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>turbanda alias, &amp; turbatura, si rescivisset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045729Z">
        <seg>to be stirred up otherwise, and a commotion, if he had found out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>tuus?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050430Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050430Z">
        <seg>yours?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde &amp; pro modico aquæ haustu, flexis poplitibus, a Gundisalvo (si forte aderat) libandi facultatem sibi exorabat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040704Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040704Z">
        <seg>From which, for a small drink of water, he would, with knees bent, beg Gundisalvo (if he happened to be there) for the opportunity to taste.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde aliquamdiu hanc secum eodem in lectulo accubare jussit per noctem, ut arte artem deluderet.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042511Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042511Z">
        <seg>So he ordered her to lie with him in the same little bed for a while at night, so that by art he might defeat the art.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde ancilla Mariana in opem sibi advocanda fuit: hæc vero post Virginis obitum rem totam, ut gesta fuerat, manifestavit, quam Rosæ humilitas (ut alia majora &amp; plurima) de more occultasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040729Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040729Z">
        <seg>Hence a Marian servant had to be summoned to her aid; she, however, after the Virgin's death, revealed the entire matter, as it had transpired, which Rose's humility (like many other greater things) had, by custom, concealed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde cuidam matronæ post annos complures familiariter interroganti, quomodo lectum adeo horribilem, [ac] arduum potuerit vel nocte unica tolerare, respondit cum lepore (uti solebat) &amp; gratia, talem se ex eo accubitu dolorem capere, quantus utique sufficere posset in satisfactionem pro quavis amicissima persona Deo offerendam.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042645Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042645Z">
        <seg>When a certain matron, after several years, familiarly asked how she could have endured such a horrible and arduous bed even for a single night, she replied with her usual wit and grace that she derived from that couch a pain sufficient, in itself, to atone for any very dear friend offered to God.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde digressis aliis, secreto extorsit a Virgine, cur interpellasset, utique mysterium subesse, quod rescire sua intererat, omnino se scire id velle.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054534Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054534Z">
        <seg>After the others had gone off, he secretly forced the Virgin to tell him why she had interrupted him, insisting that there must be a mystery behind it which he was determined to uncover.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde ex tunc constituit parens infantem placidissimam deinceps, cum exeundum foret, secum non exportare.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034823Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034823Z">
        <seg>Henceforth, the parent resolved not to take the very placid infant with them when they had to go out.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde humillima Rosæ modestia, grave, serium, perque intervalla repetitum examen suæ vocationis ac spiritus, recusare non præsumpsit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050014Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050014Z">
        <seg>Hence, the most humble Rose did not presume to refuse the grave, serious, and repeated examination of her calling and spirit, at intervals.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde in horto umbrosas platanos muro proximas conspicata, locum elegit, ubi auxiliante Ferdinando fratre, oratoriolum ab omni domesticorum prospectu semotum efformaret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043243Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043243Z">
        <seg>Spotting some shady plane trees in the garden close to the wall, she chose a spot where, with the help of her brother Ferdinand, she could build a small oratory, hidden from all view of the household.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde initio grossioris panni incommodum volumen in cervical adhibuit, deinde paulatim crudum laterem, denique petram polygonam&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;d&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; &amp; undique scabram.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042725Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042725Z">
        <seg>Thus, at first he applied a bulky roll of coarser cloth to the neck, then gradually a raw side, and finally a polygonal stone, rough on all sides.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde jugiter apud confessarios lamentabatur, ingratam se esse tam suavi Numinis in se bonitati, nescire se modum apte colendi tam excelsam amicitiam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053440Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053440Z">
        <seg>Hence she constantly lamented to the confessors that she was unworthy of the sweetness of the Divine goodness in her, and did not know how to properly cultivate such an exalted friendship.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde nunc prece, nunc lachrymis ambiebat matrem obsecrando, ne secum se abduceret.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043317Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043317Z">
        <seg>So she now besought her mother with prayer and with tears, not to take her away with her.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde per totam deinceps vitam securissima erat, se jugiter in protectione Dei cæli commorari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061720Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061720Z">
        <seg>Hence, for the rest of her life, she was most secure, always dwelling in the protection of God of heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde placando huic jurgio nec annorum lustrum satis fuit, donec tandem controversiæ finem imposuit Sacramentale Chrisma, quo sanctissimus Limensium archipræsul Turibius&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt; in parochia loci Quivi, dum Rosæ illiniebat confirmationis signaculum, omisso baptismali nomine, Rosam pronuntiare maluit, cujus fortasse jam tunc fragrantiam senserat in spiritu, quo plenus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260318T212109Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260318T203314Z">
        <seg>so that to settle this dispute not even a five-year period was sufficient, until at last the Sacramental Chrism put an end to the controversy, when the most holy Archbishop of Lima, Turibius&lt;a0&gt;i&lt;/a0&gt;, in the parish of Quivi, while anointing Rosa with the sign of confirmation, omitted her baptismal name, He preferred to pronounce Rosa, whose fragrance he had perhaps already then perceived in the spirit with which he was filled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde placando huic jurgio nec annorum lustrum satis fuit, donec tandem controversiæ finem imposuit Sacramentale Chrisma, quo sanctissimus Limensium archipræsul Turibius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt; in parochia loci Quivi, dum Rosæ illiniebat confirmationis signaculum, omisso baptismali nomine, Rosam pronuntiare maluit, cujus fortasse jam tunc fragrantiam senserat in spiritu, quo plenus erat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034711Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034711Z">
        <seg>so that to settle this dispute not even a five-year period was sufficient, until at last the Sacramental Chrism put an end to the controversy, when the most holy Archbishop of Lima, Turibius&lt;s3&gt;&lt;a4&gt;i&lt;/a4&gt;&lt;/s3&gt;, in the parish of Quivi, while anointing Rosa with the sign of confirmation, omitted her baptismal name, He preferred to pronounce Rosa, whose fragrance he had perhaps already then perceived in the spirit with which he was filled.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde postremis vitæ annis, quos in Gundisalvi quæstoris domo exegit, in humili sella collecto corpusculo, sedentaria noctes suas transegit, capite paulisper reclinato ad columnam lecticæ, in qua Gundisalvi minorennes filiolæ acquiescebant.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042920Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042920Z">
        <seg>Thus, in the last years of her life, which she spent in the house of the treasurer Gundisalvi, she passed her nights in a humble chair, her small frame gathered in, her head for a moment resting against the column of the bed in which Gundisalvi's minor daughters lay.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde prodigiis opus fuit, quibus a longe præpararetur ad mysterium, erigeretur ad consensum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044104Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044104Z">
        <seg>Hence, prodigies were necessary, by which he would be prepared from afar for the mystery and raised to consent.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde renes &amp; latera ferri algor in atrocissimi doloris consensum attraxerat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041714Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041714Z">
        <seg>From which the cold of iron had drawn the kidneys and flanks into the consensus of the most atrocious pain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde rogabat Sponsum, dignaretur potius ipsam deducere per vias ordinarias suorum electorum, nec ultra permitteret sic amantem dehiscere ad ima profundi, ubi non est substantia.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045639Z">
        <seg>Therefore she begged the Bridegroom to deign rather to lead her along the ordinary paths of his elect, and not to permit the one so in love to fall into the depths of the abyss, where there is no substance.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde sic demum patuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041705Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041705Z">
        <seg>Thus it was finally revealed.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unde tam victam esse, quam vicisse, par gaudium fuit miranti æternam Sapientiam, quæ ludens in orbe terrarum, protestatur delicias suas esse cum filiis hominum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051037Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051037Z">
        <seg>For the one who marvels at the eternal Wisdom, it was as great a joy to have been conquered as to have conquered, for she, playing on the face of the earth, proclaims that her delight is with the children of men.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>unica hæc, &amp; vera est scala paradisi, nec præter crucem alia superest, qua in cælum ascendatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052826Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052826Z">
        <seg>This alone is the true ladder of paradise, and there is no other way to ascend to heaven except through the cross.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut denique Matris suavissimæ prævalidis gemitibus cesserat exarmata ira Filii;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054447Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054447Z">
        <seg>so that at last the Son's anger was disarmed by the most sweet Mother's most powerful groans.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut dum in me excarnificanda occupabuntur, differant saltem injurias, quas eheu!</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060236Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060236Z">
        <seg>so that while they are busy dismembering me, they may at least postpone the injuries, which alas!</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut in horreum Domini copiosos Indorum manipulos comportent;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060443Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060443Z">
        <seg>to bring into the Lord's barn the abundant sheaves of the Indians;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut iste jam placatior, benigno arrisu Matrem, deinde &amp; Rosam indulgentissime respexerit: exinde gratiam in tuto jam esse, ne dubitaret confessarius;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054450Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054450Z">
        <seg>so that this one, now more placated, would look upon the Mother with a kindly smile, and then upon Rosa with the utmost indulgence; from that point on, the confessor should have no doubt that grace was now safely secure.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut jam neque digitorum ullus moveri, multo minus forficem, aut quidvis aliud tenere posset, vel apprehendere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055346Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055346Z">
        <seg>so that he could no longer move any of his fingers, much less hold a pair of scissors or anything else, or grasp it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut millenas animarum chiliades ab æternæ damnationis voragine eripiant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060446Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060446Z">
        <seg>to rescue thousands upon thousands of souls from the vortex of eternal damnation;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut nemo dubitarit, magnum aliquid inter illas tenebras gestum esse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052144Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052144Z">
        <seg>So that no one would doubt that something great had taken place amid those darknesses.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut pateret evidentius, uno utramque spiritu excelluisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060006Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060006Z">
        <seg>so that it would be more evident that he had excelled in both by a single spirit.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut putasses cæcam esse, quando vel maxime aquila erat, acie irretorta suum admirans solem.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044901Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044901Z">
        <seg>You would have thought it blind, when it was at its most eagle-eyed, gazing in wonder at its own sun.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut repente sentiebat totam simul animam collucere &amp; inflammari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045859Z">
        <seg>He suddenly felt his entire soul shining and aflame all at once.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut tremuerat afflictum cor suum, percipiendo alternos Matris Filiique affatus, quibus illa gratiam, hic justitiam ac pœnas urgebat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054444Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054444Z">
        <seg>as his afflicted heart trembled, perceiving the alternating words of the Mother and the Son, by which she urged grace, and he justice and punishments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>ut tristissima domum redierat post insolitam hanc repulsam: deinde, ut postridie multis demum lachrymis Dei Matrem flexerat ad intercessionem, negante Filio placari se posse illi cœtui tam obstinate discordi;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054441Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054441Z">
        <seg>having returned home in the deepest sorrow after this unusual rebuff; then, the next day, having at last moved the Mother of God to intercede with many tears, since the Son refused to be appeased toward that assembly so stubbornly at odds.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utere prout lubet, fac inde, quæ vis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061038Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061038Z">
        <seg>Use it as you please; do whatever you want with it.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>uterque stirpe non ignobili, sed opibus permodicis, nisi quod ingentis opulentiæ loco fuerit tali filiola cælitus ditari.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260316T214719Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260316T211811Z">
        <seg>both were of noble birth, but of very modest means, unless it was their great fortune to have such a little daughter blessed from heaven.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utique experta, quam sint Sponsi ubera meliora vino, quam fragrantia unguentis optimis.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051124Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051124Z">
        <seg>Indeed, she has experienced how the bridegroom's breasts are better than wine, and more fragrant than the finest ointments.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utique numquam id affirmatura, nisi experimento suo comprobasset.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044957Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044957Z">
        <seg>She would certainly never have affirmed it, unless she had proven it by her own experiment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utpote secura, Deum non permissurum, quod consonis horum calculis evocaretur ab humilioris instituti electa semita, quam Seraphica magistra de Senis felicissime præcalcarat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040307Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040307Z">
        <seg>being secure, she would not permit God to be drawn by the calculations of these men from the chosen path of a humbler institution, which the Seraphic Mother of Siena had most happily trodden.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque cruciatum cruciatu simul pelleret &amp; vindicaret, pugnum crebrius impegit capiti, ubi densioribus aculeis spinea corona (de qua infra cap.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040611Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040611Z">
        <seg>And so, to drive out and avenge the torment with torment, he struck his head more frequently with his fist, where the crown of thorns was denser with its spikes (of which, below, chapter).</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque focum accenderet, ardentem aliunde titionem fuerat mutuata.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045019Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045019Z">
        <seg>And so that she could light the fire, she had borrowed a burning brand from elsewhere.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utque terrores duplicaret, fecit eam experiri contactu, quam pilosis, hispidis, ericinis lacertis esset;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052119Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052119Z">
        <seg>And to double the terrors, he made her feel by touch how hairy, rough, and bristly the lizards were.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>utrimque velut in vivo ac animato libro se responsa distincte legere multo significantius, quam si mutis characteribus descripta vel picta decerneret;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054357Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054357Z">
        <seg>to read the answers distinctly for himself in a living and animated book on both sides, which was far more meaningful than if he were to discern them from characters that were merely written or painted;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vaporabat habitaculum cordis sui pretiosis odoribus ignitarum aspirationum, totamque omnium suarum potentiarum ac virium familiam colligebat in obsequium chari hospitis postridie ad ipsam diversuri.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T055730Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T055730Z">
        <seg>It exhaled the precious scents of its heart's ignited aspirations, and gathered the whole family of its powers and strengths into obedience to its dear guest, who was to take up lodging there the next day.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>varios distinguere titulos, modos, stimulos hujus dilectionis;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053201Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053201Z">
        <seg>to distinguish the various titles, modes, and incentives of this love;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vel unde in quæstoris domo procul hinc dissita innotuisse potuit, te subitanea debilitate languidam isto medicamine indigere?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051700Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051700Z">
        <seg>Or from where, in the questor's house so far away from here, could it have become known that you, stricken by a sudden weakness, needed that medicine?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vel, comparatione suorum scelerum, permodicum esse, quod patiebatur.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052646Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052646Z">
        <seg>or, in comparison to his own crimes, what he was suffering was very slight.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>veni delicata, veni tenellula, experire melius, disce plenius, num te deceat sic fastidire miseram &amp; coæqualem tuam, in qua non minus, quam in te ipsa, splendet imago tui Creatoris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061407Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061407Z">
        <seg>Come, delicate one, come, tender one, experience better, learn more fully, whether it is fitting for you to so disdain your wretched and fellow human being, in whom the image of your Creator shines no less than in yourself.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>venit præfixa dies, &amp; rheda cum D.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043355Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043355Z">
        <seg>The appointed day came, and the carriage with D.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verebantur censorium Rosæ adhuc parvulæ supercilium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053316Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053316Z">
        <seg>They feared Rosa's censor still rested on her little eyebrow.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum &amp; hi velut in magnæ perplexitatis negotio videbantur trepidare, nec certi quidpiam resolvebant;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042713Z">
        <seg>But they too seemed to be flustered as if in a matter of great perplexity, and they did not resolve on anything certain.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum Potosio Truxillum eunti adversa fuisse omnia, ita ut aliud suspicari nequiverit, quam Rosam ab oratione promissa, &amp; angelum suum a tutela commissa interea destitisse.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051859Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051859Z">
        <seg>Indeed, everything was against Potosi as he went to Trujillo, so that he could not help but suspect that Rosa had gone back on her promise and had in the meantime abandoned her angel entrusted to his care.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum de hac nobis ad compendium sufficiat concors depositio confessariorum, quotquot generalem Virginis exomologesim exceperunt;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044046Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044046Z">
        <seg>But for this, the unanimous testimony of the confessors, as many as have received the general exomologesis of the Virgin, shall suffice for us in short.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum dum exspectat, dum agoni se præparat, dum socias exemplo, gestu, hortatu invitat, nuntiatur hostem longius abire a litore;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060254Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060254Z">
        <seg>But while she waits, while she prepares herself for the struggle, while she invites her companions by example, gesture, and encouragement, word is brought that the enemy is moving farther from the shore.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verum non nisi post Rosæ obitum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061937Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061937Z">
        <seg>But only after Rose's death.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>verumtamen altius mota, in certitudinis experimentum, cælitus arrham quasi pignoratitiam exspectavit &amp; obtinuit, quæ talis fuit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T043516Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T043516Z">
        <seg>However, moved to a higher level into the experiment of certainty, it awaited and obtained from heaven a kind of pledge-like down payment, which was as follows.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vide, an laudari, coli, adorari mereatur a nobis illa æterna Majestas, cui terrenum hoc germen, rudes trunci, cuncta vegetabilia modo, quo possunt, laudis canticum adsusurrant, pronæ venerationis tributum pendunt”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045211Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045211Z">
        <seg>See if that eternal Majesty deserves to be praised, honored, and adored by us, to whom this earthly seed, the rudimentary trunks, and all plants, in whatever way they can, whisper a song of praise and pay homage with bowed heads.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>videbatur jam assecuta, dum in exangui facie, præter obscurum pallorem, &amp; maciem cavernosam nihil conspicuum apparebat.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040735Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040735Z">
        <seg>She seemed already to have regained consciousness, while on her bloodless face, apart from a dull pallor and a cavernous emaciation, nothing was visible.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vilissimoque diabolo pudendam imbecillitatem exprobravit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051958Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051958Z">
        <seg>and reproached the vilest devil with shameful weakness.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vis ingenue tibi dicam, ad quid veneris?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060639Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060639Z">
        <seg>Shall I tell you frankly what you've come for?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vis perspicacis ingenii &amp; alta judicii maturitas;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050135Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050135Z">
        <seg>Sharpness of insight and deep maturity of judgment.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vivendum sibi, moriendumque esse sub charo S. Catharinæ Senensis schemate ac magisterio, adfore tamen aliquando tempus, quo hujus nommis atque instituti novum Limæ exsurgat monasterium;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040323Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040323Z">
        <seg>that they must live and die under the dear habit and teaching of St. Catherine of Siena, yet that a time will come when a new monastery of this name and institute will arise in Lima;</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vix intrarant, cum ad solitum Rosæ invitatorium, vidit certatim incurvari arbores, adeo quod summitatum cacumina terram attingebant, lenteque verrebant solum, velut profundæ adorationis solemnissimo ritu.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045159Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045159Z">
        <seg>They had scarcely entered when, at Rose's usual invitation, she saw the trees bend in a rush, so that their very tops touched the ground and slowly swept the earth, as in the most solemn rite of profound adoration.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vix temperatum a verbere.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T042802Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T042802Z">
        <seg>Barely restrained from striking.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>vixque oratorium ingressi, ubi uxor cum Joanne de Benavides &amp; Petro Chiandro familiaribus taciti expectabant, viderunt, non sine ictu pietatis effigiem undequaque velut roris antelucani guttulis confertim madidam &amp; obductam;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053759Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053759Z">
        <seg>and having scarcely entered the oratory, where his wife, with John de Benavides and Peter Chiandro, relatives, was waiting in silence, they saw, not without a pang of piety, the effigy, from every side, as if thickly drenched and coated with drops of pre-dawn dew.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æquiores, fumantis melancholiæ, seu atræ bilis esse paroxysmos interpretabantur, quodque cerebrum inedia ac vigiliis attenuatum nimis, pateret horridis luridorum phantasmatum incursibus, ex adustione vitiati splenis, aut fœculenti sanguinis corruptione.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T045713Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T045713Z">
        <seg>They interpreted paroxysms as more moderate attacks of smoky melancholy, or black bile, and held that the brain, too greatly weakened by fasting and sleeplessness, lay open to the horrid incursions of lurid phantoms, arising from a scorched spleen or the corruption of foul blood.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æstuabat impar oneri, gemebat, obluctabatur, donec interdum victa premeretur usque ad solum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T041637Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T041637Z">
        <seg>It groaned under the unequal burden, it struggled, it resisted, until at times, overcome, it was pressed down to the ground.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>Æstuabat, arctabatur, jam palmæ oblita Virgo, pro qua successerat Lilium convallium tam speciosum, tam pretiosum.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044424Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044424Z">
        <seg>It was scorched and withered; the Virgin, long since forgotten, for whom the lily of the valleys—so beautiful, so precious—had taken its place.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>æque in rebus tum Ecclesiæ, tum reipublicæ versatissimus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050157Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050157Z">
        <seg>equally well-versed in the affairs of both the Church and the state.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ad agonem (inquit) me succingo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T060223Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T060223Z">
        <seg>"I gird myself for the contest," he says.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Advertito (inquit) o Rosa, hic tergiversandi ac silendi locum non esse;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050454Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050454Z">
        <seg>"I tell you," he said, "O Rosa, there is no room here for equivocation and silence."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ah, &lt;s2&gt;[venerabatur, ac etiam exprimebat in plantis,]&lt;/s2&gt; frater, nimio dolore crucior, dum video, contemptim jacere solo, &amp; calcaneis proteri qualemcumque rudissimum typum illius adorandæ crucis, in qua innocens Agnus Dei, suæmet vitæ inæstimabili pretio nos redemit.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054915Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054915Z">
        <seg>Ah, brother, I am tormented by excessive pain as I see the most rudimentary form of that adored cross—the one on which the innocent Lamb of God redeemed us with the inestimable price of his own life—contemptuously lying on the ground and being trampled underfoot by my heels.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Apponis erga eum cor tuum” Job.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051552Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051552Z">
        <seg>"Lay your heart on him." Job.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Canta, (inquit) mi pulle, canta, ne moriaris”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061541Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061541Z">
        <seg>"Sing, my little one," he said, "sing, lest you die."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Dum in ima caliginosæ derelictionis voragine absorptam me puto &amp; lugeo, en repente, in ipso pristinæ unionis meridie, velut in Sponsi brachiis me deprehendo;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050507Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050507Z">
        <seg>While I think and lament myself absorbed in the deepest vortex of gloomy desolation, behold, suddenly, at the very noon of our former union, I find myself as it were in the arms of the Bridegroom.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Imo (ait) mysterium suberat;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054540Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054540Z">
        <seg>"Indeed," he said, "a mystery lay beneath."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Malebam (inquiebat) obsequi, debito adjungere lucrum explicitæ obedientiæ, &amp; materni respectus humile tributum una cum penso dependere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T035601Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T035601Z">
        <seg>"I would rather," he said, "obey, add the profit of explicit obedience to my duty, and, together with my stipend, pay a humble tribute of filial respect."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Mihi (inquiens) tu deinceps Rosa diceris, &amp; eris;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T034659Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T034659Z">
        <seg>"You shall be called Rosa from now on, and you shall be."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Ne quidquam dubites, ait, mi pater (nam ipsum &amp; uxorem parentum nomine honorare consueverat) ne, inquam, timueris: non enim hujuscemodi ostenta nobis indulsit benignus iste Salvator, tamquam propediem his ædibus abiturus;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054057Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054057Z">
        <seg>"Do not doubt anything," he said, "my father" (for he was accustomed to honor his parents by calling them by their names), "do not be afraid, I say: for this kind Savior has not granted us such displays as if he were about to leave this house any day now."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Perge, (inquiens) o mater, perge de hoc argumento plura, quæ nosti, disserere”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054528Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054528Z">
        <seg>"Go on, mother, go on and discuss more about this matter that you know."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Promitto, inquit, tibi, me recta ad matrem hinc redituram, ædesque paternas, quoad jusseris, habituram pro asceterio”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040134Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040134Z">
        <seg>"I promise you," she said, "that I will return straight from here to my mother and, as long as you command, will regard the family home as my ascetic cell."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Putasne (inquit) decere Virginem, toties in publico ad levandas paleas explicare faciei operimentum?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T054903Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T054903Z">
        <seg>"Do you think," he said, "it is fitting for a virgin to uncover her face so often in public to relieve the burdens?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quid doles?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T051226Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T051226Z">
        <seg>What do you ache?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quid putas, ad ea mihi regereret seraphica mater mea?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052538Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052538Z">
        <seg>What do you think, would my seraphic mother have me return to those things?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quid video?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T053737Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T053737Z">
        <seg>What do I see?</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Quorsum (inquit) hæc tuimet incuria?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T061311Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T061311Z">
        <seg>"What is the reason for this negligence of yours?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Suspensa (inquit) in unitivo quietissimæ contemplationis lumine, vidi fulgorem admirabilis excellentiæ, decoris, ac majestatis, quem sine termino quaquaversus diffuderat omnipræsens ac subtilis immensitas.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T052724Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T052724Z">
        <seg>"Suspended (he said) in the unitive light of the most tranquil contemplation, I saw the radiance of admirable excellence, beauty, and majesty, which the ever-present and subtle immensity had diffused without limit in every direction."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Teneritudo (inquit) robusti gaudii super omne gaudium imaginabile, cognatio filiationis divinæ, interior renovatio vetustatis in ipsa animæ essentia, impletio sinuum voluntatis, vita &amp; alacritas omnium affectuum, firma, sancta, lepida, &amp; demum undequaque ineffabilis”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050606Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050606Z">
        <seg>"Tenderness," he says, "of a robust joy beyond all imaginable delight, kinship with divine sonship, an inner renewal of antiquity in the very essence of the soul, the fulfillment of the desires of the will, life and alacrity in all affections, firm, holy, graceful, and, in short, ineffable in every respect."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Tibi (inquit) Rosula videtur, quæ coram Deo Rosa est, ac grandis quidem?</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050650Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050650Z">
        <seg>"Do you see Rosula," he said, "who before God is a Rose, and indeed a great one?"</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>“Vide (inquit) o Rosa, eximium favorem, quo te Filius meus dignatus est”.</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T044414Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T044414Z">
        <seg>"See (he said) O Rose, the extraordinary favor with which my Son has honored you."</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>”Cumque amor poësim doceat, Rosa pariter has querelas familiari metro illigarat, hoc fere sensu:</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T050947Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T050947Z">
        <seg>And since love teaches poetry, Rosa likewise had woven these complaints into a familiar meter, with this approximate meaning:</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
    <tu>
      <tuv lang="la">
        <seg>…Sane gavisa est Rosa, ut se vidit circumamictam varietate;</seg>
      </tuv>
      <tuv lang="en-US" changeid="alan" changedate="20260317T040224Z" creationid="alan" creationdate="20260317T040224Z">
        <seg>…Indeed, Rosa rejoiced to see herself surrounded by a variety of things.</seg>
      </tuv>
    </tu>
<!-- Alternative translations -->
  </body>
</tmx>
